"""
Comprehensive Ingredient Database - Single Source of Truth
This ensures consistency between product search and ingredient check pages
"""

COSMETIC_CATEGORIES = {
    "Skincare", "Hair Care", "Cosmetics", "Personal Care", "Baby Care", "Oral Care", "Household"
}

# Factual "what is it" descriptions shown in the Check Ingredient page
# Keys match the pattern keys in classify_ingredient (lowercased)
INGREDIENT_DESCRIPTIONS = {
    # --- Preservatives ---
    'triclosan': 'An antimicrobial agent first used in hospitals in the 1960s, later added to soaps, toothpastes and body washes. It was one of the most widely used antibacterial additives in consumer products for over 40 years.',
    'triclocarban': 'An antimicrobial compound (TCC) used in bar soaps and personal care products to kill bacteria and fungi. Structurally similar to triclosan, it was a common ingredient in antibacterial bar soaps.',
    'sodium benzoate': 'The sodium salt of benzoic acid (E211), used as a preservative in acidic foods, beverages and cosmetics. It occurs naturally in small amounts in cranberries, prunes and cinnamon. If combined with Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) or citric acid, it can potentially produce benzene, a known carcinogen, although this is more common in beverages than in topical products. While generally safe within approved limits (0.5% in cosmetics), higher concentrations are more likely to cause irritant reactions.',
    'e211': 'E211 is the EU code for sodium benzoate, a widely used preservative in soft drinks, pickles, condiments and fruit juices that prevents the growth of bacteria, yeast and mould.',
    'sodium metabisulphite': """Sodium metabisulfite (E223/INS 223), also written sodium pyrosulfite, is a white crystalline sulfite salt (Na₂S₂O₅) that releases sulfur dioxide (SO₂) when dissolved in water or exposed to moisture. One of the most versatile sulfite preservatives — functions simultaneously as an antioxidant (prevents enzymatic and non-enzymatic browning), antimicrobial (inhibits bacteria, yeast and mould), and reducing agent. Used in: dried fruits (prevents colour loss), wine and beer (prevents oxidation and wild yeast/bacteria), potato products (prevents post-cut browning), fruit juices, seafood and pharmaceutical preparations. Active agent is SO₂/bisulfite ions (HSO₃⁻) at food pH, which react with carbonyl compounds (blocking browning reactions) and form addition compounds with acetaldehydes in wine. Health concerns: sulfites are a recognised asthmatic trigger in 3–10% of people with asthma — can cause bronchospasm, urticaria, anaphylaxis in sensitive individuals; sulphite-sensitive asthmatics should strictly avoid. Mandatory label declaration required in India (FSSAI), EU and globally when >10 mg/kg SO₂ equivalent. ADI: 0.7 mg/kg body weight/day as SO₂ equivalent (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E223), FDA approved at category-specific limits.""",
    'e223': 'E223 is the EU code for sodium metabisulphite, a sulfite preservative used to preserve colour and freshness in dried fruits, wine, fruit juices and processed foods.',
    'sulfur dioxide': """Sulfur dioxide (SO₂ / E220/INS 220) is one of the oldest known food preservatives, used in winemaking since ancient Rome and in drying fruits for thousands of years. At food-processing conditions it exists primarily as bisulfite (HSO₃⁻) and sulfite (SO₃²⁻) ions in solution. Chemical functions: (1) antimicrobial — inhibits bacteria, wild yeast and moulds that cause spoilage; critical in wine for controlling wild fermentation and preserving post-bottling shelf life; (2) antioxidant — reacts with oxygen and reactive carbonyl compounds, preventing enzymatic and non-enzymatic browning (Maillard reactions, oxidation of polyphenols); (3) reducing agent — protects colour and Vitamin C in fruit products. Used in: wines and grape products (most important application globally), dried fruits (sultanas, apricots, figs — prevents brown discolouration), fruit juices and cordials, vegetable preparations, some vinegars and fermented beverages. Health — critical concern: sulfite hypersensitivity affects approximately 3–10% of people with asthma; reactions range from bronchoconstriction and rhinitis to urticaria, angioedema and life-threatening anaphylaxis in severe cases. Asthmatic individuals and those with sulfite sensitivity must strictly avoid SO₂-containing products. Mandatory label declaration required in India (FSSAI), EU, USA and Australia when SO₂ content exceeds 10 mg/kg. ADI: 0–0.7 mg/kg body weight/day as SO₂ equivalent (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI (INS 220), EU (E220), FDA and CODEX at category-specific limits. Non-asthmatic individuals tolerate it well at approved food levels.""",
    'e220': 'E220 is the EU code for sulfur dioxide, one of the oldest preservatives used in winemaking and food processing to prevent spoilage, oxidation and maintain colour.',
    'sodium nitrite': """Sodium nitrite (NaNO₂ / E250/INS 250) is an inorganic salt used since the 1920s as the primary fast-acting curing agent in processed meats — bacon, ham, hot dogs, sausages, corned beef and luncheon meats. Mechanism: nitrite ions (NO₂⁻) react with myoglobin in meat muscle to form nitrosomyoglobin, which on cooking converts to the heat-stable pink-red compound nitrosohemochrome — giving cured meats their characteristic colour. Critically, sodium nitrite inhibits growth of Clostridium botulinum spores in anaerobic, low-acid meat environments, preventing botulism. Health concern — nitrosamines: under high-heat cooking (frying bacon at high temperatures) or in the acidic stomach environment, nitrite can react with secondary amines from amino acid degradation to form N-nitrosamines — IARC Group 2A probable human carcinogens. Sodium ascorbate (Vitamin C) is routinely co-added to cured meats to inhibit nitrosamine formation. IARC classifies processed meat consumption (not sodium nitrite specifically) as Group 1 carcinogenic. Regulatory position: the botulism prevention benefit outweighs the risk at controlled levels — FSSAI, EU (E250) and CODEX strictly control its use (typically ≤80–150 mg/kg). Not permitted in infant food. WHO recommends limiting processed meat intake.""",
    'e250': 'E250 is the EU code for sodium nitrite, the curing salt used in processed meats to prevent botulism, fix the characteristic pink colour and extend shelf life.',
    'sodium nitrate': """Sodium nitrate (NaNO₃ / E251/INS 251) is an inorganic salt used as a curing agent in preserved and dried meats — particularly long-cured products such as dry-cured ham, salami, pepperoni and hard sausages. Unlike the fast-acting sodium nitrite (E250), sodium nitrate acts as a slow-release nitrite reservoir: endogenous bacteria in the meat matrix (Micrococcaceae, Staphylococci) enzymatically reduce nitrate to nitrite over days to weeks during curing, providing sustained preservation during extended drying and ageing periods. The nitrite formed then reacts with myoglobin to produce the stable pinkish-red colour (nitrosomyoglobin, converted to nitrosohemochrome on heating) characteristic of cured meats, and inhibits Clostridium botulinum. Naturally present: sodium nitrate occurs naturally in many vegetables — spinach (~250 mg/100g), celery (~200 mg/100g), lettuce, rocket and beetroot can contain higher nitrate levels than regulated limits in cured meats. The body reduces some dietary nitrate to nitrite via salivary bacteria, then to nitric oxide (a vasodilator with cardiovascular benefits). Health concern: nitrite derived from nitrate can react with secondary amines in the meat matrix or during high-heat cooking to form nitrosamines — IARC Group 2A probable carcinogens. WHO recommends limiting processed meat consumption. Sodium ascorbate is co-added to many cured meats to inhibit nitrosamine formation. Not recommended for infants. FSSAI, EU (E251) and CODEX approved at strictly controlled limits (typically ≤200 mg/kg).""",
    'e251': 'E251 is the EU code for sodium nitrate, a curing salt used in processed and preserved meats that converts to nitrite during curing.',
    'methylparaben': 'A paraben ester preservative widely used since the 1920s in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and foods to prevent mould and bacterial growth. One of the most commonly used preservatives in personal care products worldwide.',
    'propylparaben': """Propylparaben (propyl 4-hydroxybenzoate / E216 in food) is a paraben ester preservative — formed by esterification of p-hydroxybenzoic acid with n-propanol. Used since the 1920s in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and food as a broad-spectrum antimicrobial effective against fungi and gram-positive bacteria. Longer alkyl chain (propyl vs methyl) makes it more lipophilic and approximately 4 times more potent as an antimicrobial than methylparaben, but also less water-soluble — usually used in combination with methylparaben (methylparaben handles the aqueous phase; propylparaben handles the oil phase). Found in: creams, lotions, makeup, toothpastes, pharmaceuticals and some food products. Metabolised by ester hydrolysis to p-hydroxybenzoic acid (a normal plant polyphenol metabolite) and propanol — both rapidly cleared from the body. Controversy: parabens are weak estrogen mimics (xenoestrogens) — propylparaben and butylparaben have measurably higher estrogenic activity than methylparaben in in vitro studies (at very high concentrations). A 2004 study (Darbre et al.) detected parabens in breast tumour tissue, triggering widespread concern. However, detection ≠ causation; breast tissue is exposed to many environmental compounds. EFSA and SCCS systematic reviews have concluded that methylparaben and ethylparaben pose no risk; propylparaben and butylparaben require more caution. The EU Cosmetics Regulation limits propylparaben to 0.14% individually (0.19% combined with butylparaben); banned in rinse-off products for children under 3 years. FDA classifies it GRAS at ≤0.1% in food. In India, food use is regulated by FSSAI (E216 in food additives); cosmetic use via BIS. Consumer choice: people wishing to avoid endocrine-disrupting chemicals can seek paraben-free products — numerous effective alternatives exist.""",
    'butylparaben': 'The longest-chain of the common parabens, used as a preservative in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals. More potent than shorter-chain parabens but also the most tightly regulated member of the family.',
    'sodium lauryl sulfate': """Sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS / sodium dodecyl sulfate / SDS) is an anionic surfactant derived from lauryl alcohol — obtained from coconut oil or palm kernel oil (or petrochemically) — that is sulfated with sulfur trioxide or chlorosulfuric acid and neutralised with sodium hydroxide. SLS has been used in cosmetics since the 1930s and is among the most extensively studied cosmetic ingredients. It is a strong, effective surfactant and detergent with powerful foaming, emulsifying and grease-cutting action. Used in: shampoos, body washes, toothpastes (improves foam spread and cleansing; also acts as emulsifier for flavour oils), liquid hand soaps, facial cleansers, shaving foams and household cleaning products. Mechanism: SLS disrupts lipid bilayers and denatures proteins at the skin surface — this is the basis of both its cleansing power and its irritation potential. At high concentrations and with prolonged contact, SLS significantly disrupts the stratum corneum (skin barrier), causing transepidermal water loss, erythema, dryness and irritation — more so than SLES or other milder surfactants. The Draize eye irritation test (used to assess cosmetic safety) historically used SLS as the positive control. Canker sores (aphthous ulcers): some clinical evidence suggests SLS in toothpastes may worsen recurrent mouth ulcers; switching to SLS-free toothpaste reduces ulcer frequency in susceptible individuals. Carcinogenicity: SLS is not carcinogenic — the persistent internet myth that SLS causes cancer has been thoroughly debunked; FDA classifies it as GRAS in appropriate food applications. No genotoxicity. In India: widely present in lower-cost personal care products; FSSAI and BIS standards permit its use. Not considered a sensitiser at diluted cosmetic use concentrations, but a known primary irritant at high concentrations or with repeated exposure on compromised skin. People with eczema, rosacea or very sensitive skin should prefer surfactant-free or SLES-based products.""",
    'sodium lauryl sulphate': """Sodium lauryl sulphate (UK/Indian spelling — same compound as sodium lauryl sulfate / SLS). An anionic surfactant produced by sulfation of lauryl alcohol (coconut or palm-derived) and neutralisation with sodium hydroxide. One of the strongest and most cost-effective cleansing surfactants, responsible for abundant foam and efficient removal of oil and debris. Used widely in budget and standard-tier shampoos, body washes, toothpastes and hand soaps. More irritating than sodium laureth sulphate (SLES) due to its smaller molecular size and stronger protein-denaturing ability — disrupts the skin barrier and can cause dryness, tightness and irritation with repeated use, especially on sensitive skin, eczema-prone skin or around the mouth (may exacerbate aphthous ulcers). Not a sensitiser (does not cause immunological allergic reactions at standard cosmetic concentrations) — its effects are primary irritation. Not carcinogenic (the cancer claims circulating online are false). Not genotoxic. Safe for use in rinse-off cosmetics at standard concentrations according to FDA, EU Cosmetics Regulation, FSSAI and Cosmetic Ingredient Review (CIR). However, for individuals with sensitive skin, eczema, recurrent mouth ulcers (canker sores) or damaged skin barriers, SLS-free alternatives are strongly recommended. Approved globally. Vegan; derived from plant oils.""",
    # --- Artificial colours ---
    'tartrazine': """Tartrazine (FD&C Yellow 5 / E102/INS 102 / Yellow 5) is a synthetic monoazo dye — trisodium (4E)-5-oxo-1-(4-sulfonatophenyl)-4-[(4-sulfonatophenyl)hydrazinylidene]-3H-pyrazole-3-carboxylate — derived from coal tar/petroleum chemistry. Produces a vivid lemon-yellow colour. One of the earliest synthetic food dyes; first synthesised in the 1880s by German chemist Levinstein; approved by FDA in 1916. Chemical classification: monoazo dye (single azo bond -N=N-) in the pyrazolone subclass. Applications: carbonated soft drinks, cordials, flavoured milk drinks, confectionery, marzipan, ice cream, mustard, pickles, some breakfast cereals, canned vegetables, pharmaceutical tablets and topical cosmetics (nail polish, soap). One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes identified in the McCann et al. 2007 (The Lancet) double-blind randomised trial as contributing to hyperactivity and attention deficits in mixed populations of 3-year-old and 8–9-year-old children when consumed in combination with sodium benzoate. EU EFSA reviewed this evidence and required the EU-wide advisory label on foods containing any of the Southampton Six: "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Significant safety concern: tartrazine is one of the most common causes of urticaria, angioedema and rhinitis among synthetic food dyes — prevalence of hypersensitivity estimated at 1:10,000 in the general population, higher in aspirin-intolerant individuals (cross-reactivity through azo dye mechanism). Asthmatics, aspirin-sensitive individuals and those with eczema are at elevated risk. Metabolised by gut bacteria and intestinal azo reductase to sulfanilic acid and 3-carboxy-4-aminopyrazol — neither of these metabolites has shown mutagenicity in standard Ames tests at food-relevant concentrations. FDA requires tartrazine to be listed by name (not just 'colour' or 'artificial colour') on food and cosmetic labels — a notable exception to generic colour labelling. FSSAI similarly requires individual colour declaration. ADI: 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). Banned in some formulations in Norway and Austria; EU requires warning label; approved by FDA (with named declaration), FSSAI, EU and CODEX at specified limits. Not banned globally, but declining use due to clean-label preferences.""",
    'e102': 'E102 is the EU code for tartrazine, a synthetic yellow food dye used in sweets, soft drinks, crisps, breakfast cereals and medicines to add a bright yellow colour.',
    'sunset yellow': """Sunset Yellow FCF (FD&C Yellow 6 / E110/INS 110) is a synthetic monoazo dye — disodium 6-hydroxy-5-[(4-sulfophenyl)azo]-2-naphthalenesulfonate — derived from petroleum. Produces a bright orange-yellow colour. Widely used in orange-flavoured soft drinks, mango drinks, jelly, jam, sweets, snacks, some breakfast cereals, tinned baked beans (UK), marmalade and many pharmaceutical preparations. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes shown in the 2007 McCann/Lancet study to contribute to hyperactivity in children when consumed in combination; EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Also approved by FDA (USA) and FSSAI (India) at specified limits. Can trigger allergic reactions — urticaria, angioedema, rhinitis — particularly in aspirin-sensitive individuals via cross-reactivity with salicylates. Some individuals with nickel sensitivity may also react. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). EFSA found no genotoxicity at the ADI; some older animal mutagenicity data were not confirmed in later studies. In India, commonly found in orange-coloured beverages, mango drinks, boondi laddoo coloring and some namkeen seasonings. FSSAI permits 70–200 mg/kg depending on product category.""",
    'e110': 'E110 is the EU code for Sunset Yellow FCF, a synthetic orange food dye used in snacks, jelly, drinks and medicines. Also known as FD&C Yellow 6 in North America.',
    'allura red': """Allura Red AC (FD&C Red 40 / E129/INS 129) is a synthetic monoazo dye — disodium 6-hydroxy-5-[(2-methoxy-5-methyl-4-sulfonylphenyl)azo]-2-naphthalenesulfonate — developed in the 1970s as a replacement for Amaranth (E123, banned in USA). The most widely used red food colouring in the world. Used in confectionery, soft drinks, strawberry-flavoured products, snack foods, gelatins, ice cream, fruit spreads and many packaged foods and medicines. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes; EU mandates the "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children" advisory label. A 2023 study (Holton & Bhatt) suggested allura red may affect gut serotonin signalling, potentially linking it to intestinal inflammation — though this is preliminary animal research. FDA considers it safe at approved levels; periodic consumer advocacy pressure to ban it continues. Approved by FDA (USA), EU (E129) and FSSAI (India). Can provoke reactions in aspirin-sensitive individuals, asthmatics and those with other azo dye sensitivities. ADI: 7 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2015). EFSA found no genotoxicity or carcinogenicity at the ADI. In India, ubiquitous in red-coloured snacks, beverages, confectionery and pickles. FSSAI permits 70–200 mg/kg in various food categories.""",
    'red 40': """Red 40 (Allura Red AC / FD&C Red 40 / E129/INS 129) is a synthetic azo dye and the highest volume food colour used in the United States, approved by FDA in 1971 to replace the then-banned Amaranth dye. Produces a bright cherry-red colour used in beverages, sports drinks, cereals, candy, dairy products, baked goods, snacks and pharmaceuticals. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes — the EU requires the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." In the USA, advocacy groups including the Center for Science in the Public Interest have long petitioned FDA to ban or require warning labels on Red 40; as of 2024, FDA has not acted but state-level legislation in California (AB 2316, signed 2024) requires school food notification disclosures. Can trigger reactions in aspirin-sensitive and salicylate-sensitive individuals. ADI: 7 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2015). A 2023 cell/animal study raised concerns about gut barrier effects, but human evidence at food-additive doses remains limited. Approved in India (FSSAI) under the name Allura Red/E129. FSSAI permits use in beverages, confectionery, desserts and snacks at 70–200 mg/kg.""",
    'e129': 'E129 is the EU code for Allura Red AC, a synthetic red food dye used in sweets, soft drinks and snacks. Known as Red 40 or FD&C Red 40 in North America.',
    'ponceau 4r': """Ponceau 4R (Cochineal Red A / E124/INS 124) is a synthetic azo dye — trisodium (8Z)-7-oxo-8-[(4-sulfonato-1-naphthyl)hydrazinylidene]naphthalene-1,3,5-trisulfonate — derived from petroleum. Produces a vivid scarlet-red colour. Used in fruit-flavoured jelly, desserts, tinned strawberries, some confectionery, glace cherries, salami, and some beverages and medicines. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes in the 2007 McCann/Lancet study showing hyperactivity associations in children; EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Banned in USA (not FDA-approved for food use), Norway and Finland. Permitted in EU (E124) and India (FSSAI) at specified limits. Widely used in Indian confectionery, jelly, flavoured yoghurt and cordial drinks. Can trigger urticaria, rhinitis and bronchospasm in aspirin-sensitive individuals — same cross-reactivity mechanism as other azo dyes. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). EFSA found no evidence of genotoxicity at the ADI. FSSAI permits ponceau 4R at 70–200 mg/kg depending on food category. Use has declined in premium/clean-label products, replaced by carmine (E120) or paprika extract.""",
    'e124': 'E124 is the EU code for Ponceau 4R (Cochineal Red A), a synthetic red food dye used in strawberry desserts, tinned cherries and some fizzy drinks.',
    'carmoisine': """Carmoisine (Azorubine / E122/INS 122) is a synthetic azo dye — disodium 4-hydroxy-3-(4-sulfonato-1-naphthylazo)naphthalene-1-sulfonate — derived from coal tar/petroleum. Produces a deep reddish-crimson colour. Used in jams, jellies, marmalades, candied fruits, desserts, some alcoholic drinks and confectionery. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes identified in a 2007 double-blind randomised trial (McCann et al., The Lancet) as contributing to increased hyperactivity in children when consumed in combination with sodium benzoate. As a result, the EU requires the warning label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children" on foods containing carmoisine. Banned in USA, Japan, Australia, Canada and Norway. Permitted in EU (with warning), India (FSSAI) and many Asian markets. Can trigger reactions in aspirin-sensitive individuals (azo dye cross-reactivity with salicylates). Metabolised by gut bacteria to aromatic amines; some azo dyes have mutagenic metabolites though carmoisine itself has not shown carcinogenicity in animal studies at approved doses. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2012). Indian food safety regulations permit use at 100–200 mg/kg in specific product categories.""",
    'e122': 'E122 is the EU code for Carmoisine (Azorubine), a synthetic crimson-red food dye used in sweets, jellies, soft drinks and some medicines.',
    'brilliant blue': """Brilliant Blue FCF (FD&C Blue 1 / E133/INS 133) is a synthetic triarylmethane dye — disodium 2-[[4-[ethyl-[(3-sulfophenyl)methyl]amino]phenyl]-(4-[ethyl-[(3-sulfophenyl)methyl]azaniumylidene)cyclohexa-2,5-dien-1-ylidene]methyl]benzenesulfonate. Produces a vivid sky-blue to turquoise colour. Used in ice cream, sweets, beverages, canned peas, cheese, some cereals and pharmaceutical preparations. Also used in gel electrophoresis as Coomassie Blue. Not one of the Southampton Six (not an azo dye) — does not require the EU hyperactivity advisory label. Restricted or not approved in Austria, Belgium, France, Germany, Greece, Norway, Spain and Switzerland despite EU technically permitting it. Approved by FDA (USA, GRAS since 1929) and FSSAI (India). Some documented cases of anaphylactic reactions to FD&C Blue 1 after intravenous administration (used medically as a colourimetric marker); oral food exposure at normal use levels has not been associated with anaphylaxis. In rare cases has been associated with contact urticaria. ADI: 12.5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2010). EFSA found no genotoxicity or carcinogenicity at the ADI. FSSAI permits use at 70–200 mg/kg in relevant food categories. Also used widely in cosmetics and personal care products.""",
    'e133': 'E133 is the EU code for Brilliant Blue FCF (FD&C Blue 1), a synthetic blue food and cosmetic dye used in sweets, ice cream, cereals and sports drinks.',
    'indigo carmine': """Indigo carmine (FD&C Blue 2 / E132/INS 132 / indigotine / 5,5'-indigodisulfonic acid disodium salt) is a synthetic dye based on the classical indigo chromophore structure, modified with two sulfonate groups to make it water-soluble. Natural indigo (indigotin, from Indigofera tinctoria) has been used for millennia as a textile dye — indigo carmine is the sulfonated, water-soluble derivative approved for food and pharmaceutical use. Chemical structure: a fused bicyclic framework (two pyrrole rings connected via a double bond, with two indole-2,3-dione carbonyl groups) — the sulfonation at positions 5 and 5' provides water solubility while retaining the characteristic blue colour (absorption maximum ~610 nm). Not an azo dye — therefore not subject to the EU hyperactivity advisory labelling requirement applicable to the Southampton Six azo dyes. Applications: confectionery (blue/purple shades in boiled sweets, ice cream, jellies, candy coatings), baked goods, non-alcoholic beverages, alcoholic beverages (liqueurs), pharmaceutical tablets and capsules, cosmetics and personal care products. Medical use: indigo carmine is used intravenously or topically in surgical procedures — as a chromoendoscopy agent (to enhance visualisation of gastrointestinal mucosa), to demarcate tissue margins and to confirm ureter patency during gynaecological surgery. This clinical use provides additional human safety data. Safety: generally well-tolerated at food use concentrations. Rare cases of hypotension and allergic reactions reported from IV use (surgical doses), not from food ingestion. Not one of the Southampton Six; no confirmed hyperactivity link. No mutagenicity or genotoxicity concerns. ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E132), FSSAI and CODEX approved at specified limits. Suitable for vegetarians and vegans (fully synthetic).""",
    'e132': 'E132 is the EU code for Indigo Carmine (FD&C Blue 2), a synthetic blue food dye used in confectionery, pharmaceuticals and as a diagnostic dye in medicine.',
    'erythrosine': """Erythrosine (FD&C Red 3 / E127/INS 127) is a xanthene-based synthetic dye — disodium 2-(6-hydroxy-2,4,5,7-tetraiodo-3-oxo-3H-xanthen-9-yl)benzoate — containing four iodine atoms per molecule. Produces a vivid cherry-pink to cherry-red colour. Used in maraschino cherries, canned fruit cocktail cherries, tinned strawberries, some confectionery and decorations, pharmaceutical tablets and capsules. Due to its iodine content (approximately 577 mg iodine per gram of erythrosine), high intake could theoretically contribute to iodine excess — a relevant consideration in populations with iodine-sensitive thyroid conditions. Animal studies showed thyroid follicular cell tumours at very high sustained doses (thought to be a rat-specific mechanism linked to thyroid hormone feedback); as a result, FDA banned erythrosine from ingested cosmetics in 1990 but has not banned it from food and pharmaceuticals (a contested regulatory position). Not approved by EU for food use (only in cocktail cherries and maraschino cherries within very strict limits); EU phased out most uses. Permitted in India (FSSAI) and USA at specified limits. ADI: 0.1 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA — very conservative). People with thyroid disorders should note iodine content. Not one of the Southampton Six; no EU hyperactivity warning.""",
    'e127': 'E127 is the EU code for Erythrosine (FD&C Red 3), a synthetic pink food dye used in cocktail cherries, canned fruit and pharmaceutical tablets.',
    'quinoline yellow': """Quinoline Yellow (E104/INS 104) is a mixture of disulfonated quinoline-derived dyes — primarily disodium (Z)-2-(2-oxoindolin-3-ylidene)quinoline-6,8-disulfonate — a synthetic coal-tar dye that produces a dull greenish-yellow to khaki-yellow shade. The mixture nature of the commercial product (a mixture of mono- and disulfonated quinoline derivatives in varying proportions) makes standardisation challenging and has contributed to its regulatory scrutiny. Applications: snack foods, confectionery, some soft drinks, smoked fish, scotch eggs (traditional UK use), pharmaceutical capsules, tablets and syrups and cosmetics. One of the 'Southampton Six' synthetic dyes included in the 2007 McCann/Lancet study showing hyperactivity associations in children; EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Banned in USA (FDA has not approved it for food use), Norway, Japan and Australia. Permitted in EU (E104) and India (FSSAI). Chemical safety concerns: quinoline itself (the unmodified base compound) is genotoxic and a possible carcinogen; whether traces of quinoline in the commercial food-grade dye constitute a health risk at food use concentrations has been debated. EFSA (2009) re-evaluation concluded no evidence of genotoxicity of quinoline yellow at the ADI; however, impurity control is important. ADI: 0.5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009 — relatively conservative compared to most dyes). FSSAI permits at 70–200 mg/kg in specified food categories. Parents of children with ADHD or hyperactivity concerns may wish to avoid it. Suitable for vegans (fully synthetic).""",
    'e104': 'E104 is the EU code for Quinoline Yellow, a synthetic yellow food and pharmaceutical dye used to give medicines and some foods a distinctive yellow-green shade.',
    'brown ht': """Brown HT (E155/INS 155) is a synthetic bisazo food dye — disodium 4,4′-[(2-hydroxyl-3,6-disulfonato-1,4-phenylene)bis(azo)]bis[(3-methylphenyl)azo]... (complex azo structure) — derived from coal tar/petroleum. Produces a warm brown colour used primarily to colour chocolate-flavoured products including cakes, biscuits, chocolate desserts, pastries and some beverages. 'HT' stands for 'high temperature' stability, reflecting its ability to retain colour during baking. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes implicated in the 2007 McCann/Lancet study on hyperactivity in children. EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Banned in USA (not FDA-approved for food), Canada, Japan, Australia and Belgium. Permitted in EU (E155) and India (FSSAI). Can provoke reactions in aspirin-intolerant individuals and asthmatics. ADI: 1.5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA). EFSA 2010 re-evaluation: no mutagenicity or genotoxicity at the ADI. In India, used in chocolate-flavour coatings, bakery products and soft drinks at permitted limits of 100–200 mg/kg. Increasingly replaced by natural colours (cocoa powder, plain caramel E150a) in clean-label products.""",
    'e155': 'E155 is the EU code for Brown HT, a synthetic food dye used to colour chocolate cakes, biscuits and desserts. The HT stands for "high temperature" stability.',
    'patent blue v': """Patent Blue V (E131/INS 131) is a synthetic sulfonated triarylmethane dye — calcium di[α-(4-(diethylamino)phenyl)-2,5-disulfobenzyl-idene]cyclohexa-2,5-dien-1-ylidene]diethylammonium salt — that produces a vivid blue-violet colour. Used as a food colour in sweets, confectionery, some bakery icings, beverages, and as a colourant in alcohol-based liqueurs. Also used as a diagnostic dye in medicine: injected to map sentinel lymph nodes in breast cancer and melanoma surgery (identifies first lymph node draining a tumour). Interestingly, this dual use means its toxicology is well studied in human clinical settings as well as food safety contexts. Not approved by FDA for food use in USA. Permitted in EU (E131) and India (FSSAI). Several clinical case reports of anaphylactic reactions to Patent Blue V, primarily from its use as a surgical tracer — reactions at doses used as food colours are much rarer. Cross-reactivity with lidocaine (related sulfonated dye structure) has been reported. ADI: 15 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2013). EFSA found no genotoxicity or carcinogenicity concerns at the ADI. Those with a history of allergy to sulfonated dyes or procedures involving Patent Blue V injections should avoid food products containing it. Not one of the Southampton Six; no EU hyperactivity warning required.""",
    'e131': 'E131 is the EU code for Patent Blue V, a synthetic triarylmethane dye used in food, pharmaceutical and cosmetic applications as a blue colorant.',
    'azorubine': """Azorubine (E122/INS 122) is the same compound as Carmoisine — a synthetic coal-tar-derived azo dye producing a deep reddish-crimson colour. Chemically it is a disulfonate azo dye. Used in jams, alcoholic beverages, sweets, desserts and some medicines. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes linked to hyperactivity in children in the 2007 Lancet McCann study — EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children" on products containing azorubine. Not approved in USA, Canada, Japan, Australia or Norway. Approved in EU (E122 with warning), India (FSSAI) and many other countries. People with aspirin sensitivity should avoid it — cross-reactivity between azo dyes and salicylates is well documented. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA). At food use concentrations, no evidence of carcinogenicity; EFSA 2012 re-evaluation found no genotoxicity concerns at permitted doses. Used in India in glucose biscuit frostings, coloured confectionery and carbonated drinks.""",
    'e951': 'E951 is the EU code for aspartame, an artificial sweetener approximately 200 times sweeter than sugar, widely used in diet drinks, chewing gum and sugar-free foods since FDA approval in 1981.',
    'e950': 'E950 is the EU code for acesulfame potassium (Ace-K), an artificial sweetener about 200 times sweeter than sugar, commonly blended with aspartame in diet beverages and sugar-free products.',
    # --- Flavor enhancers ---
    'disodium guanylate': 'A flavour enhancer (E627) made from dried fish or seaweed, used to intensify savoury umami flavours in processed foods. Almost always used in combination with MSG and disodium inosinate.',
    'e627': 'E627 is the EU code for disodium guanylate, a nucleotide-based flavour enhancer extracted from dried fish or yeast that amplifies the savoury taste of foods.',
    'disodium inosinate': 'A flavour enhancer (E631) derived from meat or fish, used to boost the savoury umami taste in soups, crisps and instant noodles. Typically used alongside MSG and disodium guanylate.',
    'e631': 'E631 is the EU code for disodium inosinate, a nucleotide flavour enhancer derived from meat or sardines, commonly found in instant noodles, crisps and savoury snacks.',
    # --- Acids ---
    'phosphoric acid': """Phosphoric acid (orthophosphoric acid / H₃PO₄ / E338/INS 338) is a clear, colourless, non-volatile inorganic mineral acid — one of the few inorganic (non-organic) acids permitted in food. Produced by dissolving phosphorus pentoxide (from burning elemental phosphorus) in water (thermal process) or by treating phosphate rock with sulfuric acid (wet process); food-grade acid is purified to remove heavy metals. Distinctive flavour: imparts a sharp, tangy, clean sourness — unlike the fruity tartness of citric acid or the more complex flavour of malic acid. Phosphoric acid provides the characteristic "bite" of cola beverages (Coca-Cola, Pepsi) at concentrations of ~0.05–0.08% w/v. Functions: (1) acidulant — primary acidifier in cola-type carbonated soft drinks, imparting a pH of ~2.5–3.5; (2) pH regulator — in processed cheese emulsification salts; (3) flavouring — contributes the specific tartness profile unique to cola flavour; (4) technical role in phosphate salt production (sodium phosphates, potassium phosphates, calcium phosphates for food use). Health concerns: (a) Dental erosion: phosphoric acid is strongly erosive to dental enamel; regular consumption of cola drinks (pH ~2.5) is one of the most significant dietary risk factors for dental erosion and erosive tooth wear. (b) Bone density: phosphoric acid and inorganic phosphate additives increase serum phosphate; the calcium-phosphate balance is important for bone metabolism. Some epidemiological studies link high cola consumption to lower bone mineral density (especially in women), though causality (via phosphoric acid specifically vs. displacement of calcium-containing beverages) is debated. (c) Kidney: chronic high phosphate intake from food additives is associated with faster CKD progression. (d) Calcium absorption: high dietary phosphate can reduce intestinal calcium absorption. JECFA ADI: not specified (considered safe at food use levels). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E338), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX at specified limits.""",
    'e338': 'E338 is the EU code for phosphoric acid, an inorganic acid used as an acidulant, pH regulator and flavour agent in cola beverages, processed cheese and baked goods.',
    'caramel colour': """Caramel colour (E150a–E150d / INS 150a–150d) is produced by controlled heat treatment of food-grade carbohydrates — sucrose, glucose, high-fructose corn syrup, malt syrup, molasses or starch hydrolysates — under carefully controlled temperature, time, pH and chemical conditions. It is one of the most produced and widely used food additives globally, with an annual production in excess of 200,000 metric tonnes. Four regulatory classes defined by processing agents: (1) Class I — Plain Caramel (E150a/INS 150a): heated without any acids, alkalis, sulfite or ammonium compounds; pure thermal caramelisation; used in whisky, brandy, rum, vinegar, bread, biscuits and confectionery; cleanest caramel class with no processing chemical by-products. (2) Class II — Caustic Sulfite Caramel (E150b/INS 150b): heated with sulfite compounds; used in cognac, certain spirits and vinegars; less common. (3) Class III — Ammonia Caramel (E150c/INS 150c): heated with ammonia compounds; produces 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI) and 2-methylimidazole (2-MEI) as processing by-products; used in beer (gives Guinness-type colour), soy sauce, gravies and some confectionery. (4) Class IV — Sulfite Ammonia Caramel (E150d/INS 150d): heated with both sulfite and ammonium compounds; produces the highest 4-MEI levels; the most widely used class globally — found in Coca-Cola, Pepsi and most commercial cola drinks, dark soy sauce, baked goods, breakfast cereals, soups and instant noodle seasonings. The 4-MEI controversy: 4-methylimidazole is classified by IARC as Group 2B ('possibly carcinogenic to humans') based on lung adenoma induction in female B6C3F1 mice — a highly susceptible strain prone to spontaneous lung tumours. Human epidemiological data on 4-MEI at dietary levels are insufficient to establish cancer risk. The US National Toxicology Program confirmed the mouse finding (2007). California's Proposition 65 programme required a cancer warning label if product consumption delivers >29 micrograms 4-MEI/day — prompting Coca-Cola and PepsiCo to reformulate their products in California to reduce 4-MEI content (reducing the ammonia-process caramel level). FDA and FSSAI maintain that caramel colours are safe at approved levels; EFSA restricts E150d in soft drinks to 1,000 mg/L. Nutritionally: caramel colour contributes no meaningful vitamins, minerals or beneficial compounds. The colours can mask other quality indicators of food. ADI: not specified by JECFA for Class I and II; Class III ADI 200 mg/kg bw/day; Class IV ADI 200 mg/kg bw/day (JECFA). Health-conscious consumers and those wishing to minimise 4-MEI exposure should prefer beverages and foods using plain caramel (E150a) or other natural brown colours. FSSAI, EU and Codex approved for specified uses.""",
    'caramel color': """Caramel colour (E150) is produced by controlled heat treatment of carbohydrates (sugars, glucose syrup, corn starch hydrolysates) under specific conditions. One of the oldest and most widely used food colourings globally. Four distinct classes based on processing chemicals: Class I (plain caramel / E150a): heated without reactants — used in whisky, baked goods, confectionery; safest class. Class II (caustic sulfite caramel / E150b): heated with sulfite — used in spirits, cognac. Class III (ammonia caramel / E150c): heated with ammonia — used in beer, soy sauce; contains small amounts of 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI). Class IV (sulfite ammonia caramel / E150d): heated with both sulfite and ammonia — used in cola drinks, soy sauce, baked goods; the most common class, highest 4-MEI content. 4-MEI concern: California's Prop 65 requires cancer warnings on products contributing >29 mcg 4-MEI/day; IARC classifies 4-MEI as Group 2B (possibly carcinogenic) based on mouse lung tumour data. This prompted Coca-Cola and PepsiCo to reformulate in some markets to reduce 4-MEI. FSSAI and FDA permit caramel colour at specified limits with 4-MEI controls. Plain caramel (E150a) has no 4-MEI concern. ADI: not specified for E150a; Class IV has specific limits in some jurisdictions.""",
    'e150': 'E150 is the EU code for caramel colouring, one of the most widely produced food additives globally, made by heating carbohydrates and used in cola drinks, beer, biscuits, bread and soy sauce.',
    # --- Personal care preservatives ---
    'methylchloroisothiazolinone': 'A synthetic preservative (MCI/CMIT) used in rinse-off personal care products like shampoos, conditioners and body washes to prevent microbial growth. Usually paired with methylisothiazolinone (MIT).',
    'methylisothiazolinone': 'A synthetic preservative (MI/MIT) used in personal care products and household cleaners to prevent mould and bacterial contamination. Part of the isothiazolinone family of biocides, first introduced in the 1970s.',
    'dmdm hydantoin': """DMDM hydantoin (1,3-bis(hydroxymethyl)-5,5-dimethylhydantoin) is a synthetic formaldehyde-releasing preservative used in shampoos, conditioners, skin lotions, body washes and cosmetics. Produced by reacting 5,5-dimethylhydantoin with formaldehyde. Releases free formaldehyde proportionally to temperature and pH — higher temperature and lower pH accelerate release. Typical use concentration: 0.1–0.6%. Effective antimicrobial against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria and some fungi. EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex V) permits up to 1% with mandatory formaldehyde label statement if free formaldehyde >0.05%. Health concerns: as a formaldehyde donor, it is one of the most frequently identified contact sensitisers in patch test studies. Allergic contact dermatitis (ACD) reactions have been widely reported; individuals sensitised to formaldehyde will likely react to DMDM hydantoin even at low concentrations. In 2021–2022, a wave of class-action lawsuits was filed in the USA against manufacturers of hair care products containing DMDM hydantoin, citing scalp irritation and hair loss — although a direct causal link between DMDM hydantoin and hair loss has not been definitively established in clinical studies, sensitised scalp dermatitis can cause telogen effluvium. Avoid on sensitive skin, in children's products and if formaldehyde allergy is known.""",
    'imidazolidinyl urea': """A synthetic formaldehyde-releasing preservative (INCI: Imidazolidinyl Urea) used in cosmetics, lotions, shampoos and personal care products since the 1950s — one of the earliest synthetic preservatives developed for personal care. Chemically a condensation product of allantoin and formaldehyde; it releases free formaldehyde slowly in aqueous solution to maintain antimicrobial efficacy. Effective against a broad range of bacteria (including Gram-negative organisms) and yeasts at concentrations of 0.1–0.3%. Often used in combination with parabens for enhanced spectrum. EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex V): maximum 0.6%; mandatory formaldehyde disclosure if free formaldehyde exceeds 0.05% — the label must state "contains formaldehyde." Recognised as the most common preservative sensitiser in North American contact dermatitis studies (NACDG). Repeated skin exposure can induce allergic contact dermatitis; patch testing should be performed if eczema is suspected to be product-related. Sensitised individuals may also cross-react with other formaldehyde releasers (DMDM hydantoin, diazolidinyl urea, quaternium-15). As awareness of formaldehyde sensitisation has grown, many formulators have switched to alternative preservation systems. Avoid on damaged, inflamed or infant skin.""",
    'diazolidinyl urea': """A synthetic preservative (INCI: Diazolidinyl Urea) used in cosmetics, shampoos and personal care products since the 1980s. Chemically a cyclic urea derivative that works by slowly releasing formaldehyde (a primary biocide) into the product matrix — the free formaldehyde diffuses into microbial cells and crosslinks proteins, killing bacteria and fungi. Typically used at 0.1–0.5% concentration, often paired with parabens or iodopropynyl butylcarbamate for broader spectrum protection. Health concerns: as a formaldehyde releaser, it carries the same sensitisation potential as formaldehyde itself — repeated skin exposure can induce allergic contact dermatitis, even at low concentrations. The EU Cosmetics Regulation permits it at max 0.5% (rinse-off) and 0.3% (leave-on) with mandatory formaldehyde disclosure if free formaldehyde exceeds 0.05%. Listed as an allergen of concern by dermatology patch-test panels (NACDG, ESCD). Formaldehyde is classified as a Group 1 human carcinogen by IARC (primarily through inhalation in occupational settings); topical exposure risk at cosmetic concentrations is considered low but sensitisation is a real concern. Avoid in products for infants, damaged or eczema-prone skin.""",
    'quaternium-15': """Quaternium-15 (INCI: Quaternium-15 / chemical name: 1-(3-chloroallyl)-3,5,7-triaza-1-azoniaadamantane chloride) is a synthetic preservative and one of the most potent formaldehyde-releasing preservatives (FRP) used in cosmetics and personal care products. It belongs to the hexamethylenetetramine group of preservatives — on hydrolysis in aqueous products, it releases free formaldehyde, which is the actual antimicrobial agent. Quaternium-15 releases more formaldehyde per unit weight than any other commonly used cosmetic FRP — approximately twice as much as DMDM hydantoin and significantly more than imidazolidinyl urea or diazolidinyl urea. Used in: shampoos, conditioners, body washes, baby wipes, lotions, sunscreens and liquid hand soaps. Effective against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, fungi and yeast at concentrations of 0.05–0.2%. Health concerns: formaldehyde (from its hydrolysis) is classified by IARC as a Group 1 human carcinogen based on strong evidence for nasopharyngeal cancer and leukaemia from occupational inhalation. At cosmetic use concentrations, inhaled formaldehyde from opened products is low. The primary cosmetic risk is sensitisation — quaternium-15 is consistently the most commonly found contact allergen from preservatives in patch test studies (NACDG, ESCD); allergic contact dermatitis from quaternium-15 is common, and once sensitised, patients react even to trace amounts. People with formaldehyde allergy (a contact allergen) will react to quaternium-15 and other FRPs. EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex V): permitted at max 0.2% with mandatory label statement "contains formaldehyde" when free formaldehyde level exceeds 0.05%. Despite this, many European formulators have phased it out. No such mandatory restrictions in USA (FDA); India (BIS/FSSAI cosmetic standards are aligned to EU requirements increasingly). Avoid in products for infants, damaged skin, eczema-prone individuals or known formaldehyde allergy. Better alternatives: phenoxyethanol, caprylyl glycol, sodium benzoate/potassium sorbate combinations, ethylhexylglycerin.""",
    'phenyl mercuric': """Phenyl mercuric compounds (phenylmercuric acetate / phenylmercuric nitrate / phenylmercuric borate) are organomercury preservatives — in which mercury is bonded directly to a phenyl (benzene) ring. These compounds were historically used as antimicrobial preservatives in: ophthalmic preparations (eye drops, contact lens solutions), nasal sprays, injectable pharmaceuticals, topical ointments and some cosmetics, particularly where the Gram-negative organism Pseudomonas aeruginosa (a serious eye infection pathogen) needed control. Mercury is a well-established neurotoxin and nephrotoxin. Organic mercury compounds (including methylmercury and phenylmercury) are absorbed far more efficiently through biological membranes than inorganic mercury salts. Phenylmercuric compounds applied to the eye or mucous membranes can be absorbed systemically. Regular use can contribute to mercury accumulation in the kidneys and nervous system. Regulatory status: banned in cosmetics in the EU (Annex II — prohibited); FDA prohibited thimerosal (ethylmercury thiosalicylate) and phenylmercuric compounds from over-the-counter drug products. Most countries globally have banned or severely restricted phenylmercuric compounds in cosmetics and personal care products. In India, FSSAI/drug standards prohibit mercury-based preservatives in cosmetics. In some lower-regulation markets, illegal or counterfeit products (especially skin-lightening creams and eye drops) may still contain mercury compounds — consumers should check for banned ingredients. Safer alternatives (thimerosal-free ophthalmic preservatives including sodium perborate, polyquaternium-1 and stabilised oxychloro complexes) are now standard.""",
    'formaldehyde': """Formaldehyde (methanal, CH₂O) is the simplest aldehyde — a highly reactive colourless gas at room temperature, typically used as a 37–40% aqueous solution (formalin). Produced endogenously by all living organisms as a metabolic intermediate in one-carbon metabolism; normal blood formaldehyde levels are approximately 0.1 mM. Industrial production is via catalytic oxidation of methanol. In cosmetics and personal care: not typically added directly, but is released by formaldehyde-releasing preservatives (DMDM hydantoin, imidazolidinyl urea, diazolidinyl urea, quaternium-15, bronopol). EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex V) permits formaldehyde as a nail hardener at 5% max, and as a preservative at 0.2% max in rinse-off products (0.1% in oral products) — with mandatory label warning "contains formaldehyde." In food: naturally present in many foods (fruit, vegetables, fish, meat) at levels of 1–500 mg/kg — endogenous formaldehyde from metabolism vastly exceeds dietary intake. IARC classifies formaldehyde as a Group 1 carcinogen based on strong evidence for nasopharyngeal cancer and leukaemia from high-level occupational inhalation exposure (embalmers, pathologists, anatomy lab workers). Topical cosmetic exposure at low concentrations poses a primarily sensitisation rather than carcinogenicity risk for consumers. However, dermal sensitisation to formaldehyde is common and can be severe.""",
    'bronopol': 'A synthetic broad-spectrum preservative (2-bromo-2-nitropropane-1,3-diol) used in personal care products, cosmetics and industrial water systems to prevent bacterial and fungal growth.',
    # --- Sweeteners ---
    'aspartame': """Aspartame (E951/INS 951 / APM) is a dipeptide methyl ester sweetener — methyl ester of L-aspartyl-L-phenylalanine — approximately 180–200 times sweeter than sucrose. Discovered in 1965 by chemist James Schlatter at G.D. Searle & Co. while working on an ulcer medication. Chemical synthesis: condensation of L-aspartic acid with L-phenylalanine methyl ester. Upon ingestion, it is rapidly hydrolysed in the GI tract to three components: aspartic acid (an amino acid found in all foods), phenylalanine (an essential amino acid found in all proteins) and a very small amount of methanol (also released from many fruits, vegetables and fermented beverages in far larger quantities — tomato juice contains 15–21 mg/100 mL methanol; a can of diet drink provides ~20 mg). Aspartame itself is not absorbed intact. Caloric value: 4 kcal/gram (same as protein) but used in such small amounts (40–50 mg per can of diet drink) that it contributes essentially zero calories. Applications: diet soft drinks, sugar-free chewing gum, tabletop sweeteners (Equal, NutraSweet), low-calorie yoghurt, pharmaceuticals (paediatric medicine, flavoured vitamins), protein supplements. Not heat-stable — degrades at temperatures >70°C, so not suitable for baking unless specifically encapsulated. Critical safety concern: MANDATORY contraindication in phenylketonuria (PKU) — a rare inherited metabolic disorder where phenylalanine cannot be metabolised, leading to neurotoxic accumulation; FSSAI mandates all products containing aspartame carry the warning "Contains Phenylalanine" and "Not Suitable for Phenylketonurics." IARC July 2023 classified aspartame as Group 2B (possibly carcinogenic to humans) based primarily on limited evidence from observational studies on hepatocellular carcinoma — JECFA simultaneously maintained the ADI at 40 mg/kg body weight/day, concluding current evidence is insufficient to change the safety assessment. The Group 2B classification is the lowest "hazard" category. Reported side effects from sensitive individuals include headaches and mood changes — not confirmed in controlled studies. No proven links to cancer in humans at typical consumption levels. ADI: 40 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA/EFSA). FDA, EU (E951), FSSAI and CODEX approved. Suitable for diabetics; not for PKU patients.""",
    'acesulfame potassium': """Acesulfame potassium (Ace-K / acesulfame-K / E950/INS 950) is a synthetic, non-nutritive sweetener. The potassium salt of 6-methyl-1,2,3-oxathiazine-4(3H)-one 2,2-dioxide — discovered in 1967 by German chemist Karl Clauss at Hoechst AG during laboratory work. About 200 times sweeter than sucrose; available pure sweetness with a slightly bitter metallic aftertaste at higher concentrations, which is why it is almost always blended with aspartame (E951) or sucralose (E955) to achieve a more sugar-like profile. Exceptional heat and pH stability — stable through pasteurisation, UHT processing and baking up to 225°C without degradation, a key commercial advantage over aspartame. Completely absorbed from the GI tract but not metabolised — excreted unchanged in urine, providing zero calories and zero glycaemic index. Applications: carbonated diet drinks, low-calorie juices, sugar-free baked goods, confectionery, tabletop sweeteners, dairy desserts, protein supplements, pharmaceuticals (masking bitterness of medications) and oral care products. Extensive safety testing: no mutagenicity in Ames test; no carcinogenicity at doses up to 3% of diet in long-term rat and mouse studies; no reproductive or developmental toxicity. Does not cause dental caries. Compatible with diabetic diets. FSSAI mandates "contains sweetener" labelling. ADI: 15 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2003) — typical exposure well below. FDA (1988), EU (E950), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'acesulfame-k': """Acesulfame potassium (Ace-K / E950 / INS 950) is a synthetic sweetener discovered in 1967 by Karl Clauss at Hoechst AG. Chemically it is the potassium salt of 6-methyl-1,2,3-oxathiazine-4(3H)-one 2,2-dioxide. About 200 times sweeter than sucrose. Notable for exceptional heat stability — does not break down during baking or pasteurisation, unlike aspartame. Not metabolised by the body; excreted unchanged in urine, contributing zero calories and zero glycaemic impact. Commonly blended with aspartame or sucralose to mask its slightly bitter aftertaste and achieve a more sugar-like flavour profile. Approved by FDA (1988), EU (E950), FSSAI and CODEX. ADI: 15 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA, 2003) — typical consumer exposure is well below this. Suitable for diabetics; tooth-friendly. No confirmed safety concerns in human studies at regulatory-approved levels. Some older animal studies at very high doses showed thyroid effects; not considered relevant at food use levels. Does not require PKU labelling.""",
    # --- Antioxidant preservatives ---
    'butylated hydroxyanisole': """Butylated hydroxyanisole (BHA / E320/INS 320) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant — a mixture of two isomeric tert-butylated methoxyphenols: 2-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol (the major isomer, ~85–90%) and 3-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol (~10–15%). White or off-white waxy solid with a characteristic mild phenolic odour. Produced industrially by alkylating p-methoxyphenol with isobutylene in the presence of an acid catalyst. One of the oldest approved synthetic food antioxidants, in use since the 1940s. Mechanism: BHA donates a hydrogen atom from its phenolic hydroxyl group to lipid peroxyl radicals (LOO•), forming a stable BHA radical that does not continue the oxidative chain — thereby preventing rancidity in fats and oils. Highly fat-soluble; effective at concentrations of 20–200 mg/kg in the fat phase. Functions and applications: protects animal fats (lard, tallow), vegetable oils, fried snack foods (potato chips, namkeen), breakfast cereals, biscuits, chewing gum, dried meat, instant noodle seasoning oils, cosmetics and pharmaceutical preparations from oxidative rancidity and off-flavour development. Approved food use levels (FSSAI, EU E320, FDA GRAS): typically 100–200 mg/kg in the fat or oil. Often used in combination with BHT (E321) — their antioxidant effects are additive at sub-optimal levels; combined limits apply. Regulatory concerns: IARC classifies BHA as Group 2B (possibly carcinogenic to humans) based on: (1) forestomach tumours (papillomas and carcinomas) in rats and hamsters at high chronic doses — the rodent forestomach is a distinct anatomical structure absent in humans, which complicates extrapolation; (2) absence of convincing positive epidemiological data in humans; (3) some in vitro evidence of genotoxicity at high concentrations. US National Toxicology Program classified BHA as 'reasonably anticipated to be a human carcinogen' (15th Report on Carcinogens, 2021). Japan has banned BHA in food use in some categories. California's Proposition 65 lists BHA as a carcinogen. EU (E320) restricts its use to specific food categories and sets maximum limits. Natural alternatives (tocopherols / Vitamin E, rosemary extract E392) are increasingly preferred. JECFA ADI: 0.5 mg/kg body weight/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E320, with restrictions), FDA (GRAS at specified levels) and CODEX.""",
    'bha': """BHA (butylated hydroxyanisole / E320/INS 320) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant — a mixture of two isomers: 2-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol (major) and 3-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol. Produced by alkylating p-methoxyphenol with isobutylene. Approved for use since the 1940s. Used to prevent oxidative rancidity in edible fats and oils, fried snacks, cereals, chewing gum, butter and cosmetics; also used to stabilise packaging materials. Highly fat-soluble, effective at very low concentrations (50–200 ppm). Mechanism: donates a hydrogen atom to lipid free radicals, breaking the oxidative chain reaction. Health concerns: BHA is listed as a possible human carcinogen (IARC Group 2B) based on forestomach tumours in rats and hamsters at very high doses — the rodent forestomach is an anatomical site humans do not possess, making direct extrapolation difficult. US National Toxicology Program listed it as "reasonably anticipated to be a human carcinogen" in its 15th Report on Carcinogens (2021). Also has weak oestrogenic activity. Japan banned BHA in fats and oils for infant foods. FSSAI, EU (E320) and FDA permit BHA at specified limits (typically 100–200 mg/kg in fats). ADI: 0.5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA).""",
    'butylated hydroxytoluene': """Butylated hydroxytoluene (BHT / E321/INS 321 / 2,6-di-tert-butyl-4-methylphenol / dibutyl methylphenol) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant that is chemically closely related to BHA. A white, waxy crystalline solid with a faint characteristic odour. Produced by alkylation of p-cresol (4-methylphenol) with isobutylene in the presence of an acid catalyst — simpler and cheaper to manufacture than BHA. Mechanism: BHT donates a hydrogen atom from its phenolic hydroxyl to lipid peroxyl radicals (LOO•), stopping the oxidative chain reaction and preventing rancidity. Highly lipophilic; soluble in fats and oils; effective at 20–200 mg/kg in the fat phase. Functions: antioxidant preservative in edible oils, animal fats, fried snacks (potato chips, bhujia, namkeen), breakfast cereals, chewing gum, biscuits, dried fruits, instant noodle seasoning oils, cosmetics (hair care, skin care, lipsticks) and packaging materials (antioxidant-impregnated food contact plastics). May be found in food via migration from packaging. Often used with BHA (E320) — combined limits apply. Regulatory concerns: IARC classifies BHT as Group 3 (not classifiable as carcinogenic to humans) — animal study findings have been inconsistent: some studies showed liver and lung tumour promotion in specific rodent strains; other studies showed tumour-inhibiting effects. This inconsistency explains the lower regulatory concern compared to BHA. However, BHT has shown: (1) liver microsomal enzyme induction (cytochrome P450 induction) in rodents at moderate doses; (2) thyroid hormone disruption (dose-dependent reductions in serum T4/T3) in chronic rat studies; (3) some evidence of reproductive toxicity (reduced litter size, birth weight) in high-dose rodent studies; (4) blood coagulation impairment at high doses. Evidence of endocrine disruption at human dietary exposure levels is not established. California Proposition 65: listed as a chemical known to cause reproductive toxicity. Japan: restricted in specific food applications. EU (E321): approved with specific maximum limits. FDA (GRAS): approved at ≤0.02% of fat or oil content. FSSAI: permits BHT as antioxidant in edible oils up to 0.02%. JECFA ADI: 0.3 mg/kg body weight/day. Clean-label trend: manufacturers increasingly replacing BHT with natural antioxidants (mixed tocopherols, rosemary extract) to meet consumer expectations for 'free-from' synthetic antioxidants.""",
    'bht': """BHT (butylated hydroxytoluene / 2,6-di-tert-butyl-4-methylphenol / E321/INS 321) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant chemically related to BHA. Produced by alkylating p-cresol with isobutylene. Highly lipophilic white solid used since the 1940s to protect edible fats, oils, cereals, potato chips, chewing gum and cosmetics (creams, lotions) from oxidative rancidity. Fat-soluble; bioaccumulates to a limited extent in adipose tissue. Metabolised by cytochrome P450 enzymes to quinone methide intermediates. Health concerns: IARC classifies BHT as Group 3 (not classifiable as carcinogenic) — some rodent studies showed tumour promotion at very high doses, but other studies showed anticarcinogenic effects, making the overall picture inconsistent. Some research suggests BHT may act as an endocrine disruptor at high doses, though evidence is not considered conclusive. At doses around 500 mg/kg body weight in animals, liver enzyme induction and thyroid effects were observed — far above food exposure levels. BHT can also trigger hyperactivity reactions in some children sensitive to food additives, per older Feingold hypothesis observations. FSSAI, EU (E321), FDA (GRAS) approved at specified limits (generally 100–200 mg/kg in fat). ADI: 0.3 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA).""",
    'tert-butylhydroquinone': """Tert-butylhydroquinone (TBHQ / E319/INS 319) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant — a monoalkyl ether of hydroquinone, specifically 2-(1,1-dimethylethyl)-1,4-benzenediol. One of the most effective antioxidants for protecting polyunsaturated fats and oils: particularly used in frying oils (vegetable, cotton seed, soybean), instant noodles, crackers, chips, microwave popcorn and fast-food applications. Mechanism: donates a hydrogen atom to peroxy radicals generated during lipid oxidation, terminating the free radical chain reaction. Provides superior protection at high frying temperatures compared to BHA or BHT. Maximum permitted levels: typically 100–200 mg/kg of fat in food (EU, FDA, FSSAI, CODEX). ADI: 0–0.7 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Health concerns: at high doses (above 1 g/kg in animal studies), TBHQ caused forestomach hyperplasia and neoplasia in rats; at very high doses (human poisoning cases), it caused nausea, vomiting and delirium. Animal immunology studies at supra-normal doses showed impaired vaccine response. Not classifiable as a human carcinogen at food use levels (IARC Group 3). A 2020 Environmental Working Group report raised concerns about immune system effects. Considered safe by FSSAI, FDA and EFSA at approved food levels. Should not be used with BHA or BHT (combined antioxidant limits apply).""",
    'tbhq': 'TBHQ (tert-butylhydroquinone) is a synthetic antioxidant preservative found in cooking oils, chips, instant noodles and crackers to slow rancidity and extend shelf life.',
    # --- Banned additives ---
    'potassium bromate': """Potassium bromate (KBrO₃ / INS 924) is a powerful oxidising agent — the potassium salt of bromic acid — that was widely used as a bread improver and flour treatment agent in commercial baking from the early 20th century until bans took effect. In baking: potassium bromate strengthens gluten network in wheat dough by oxidising sulfhydryl groups (-SH) on gluten proteins to form disulfide bonds (-SS-), producing a tighter, more extensible dough that retains gas better during fermentation. This results in better oven spring, a higher rise, finer crumb structure and more uniform loaf. The bromate is theoretically reduced to bromide (KBr — harmless salt) during the baking process at high temperatures, but studies repeatedly show that residual bromate remains in the finished bread, especially in under-baked products, thick loaves and when used at higher concentrations. Carcinogenicity: IARC classified potassium bromate as Group 2B — possibly carcinogenic to humans — based on strong evidence of renal cell adenocarcinomas, renal tubular cell tumours, mesotheliomas and follicular cell thyroid tumours in rats and mice. The bromate ion is both a genotoxicant (DNA-damaging) and a carcinogen. As a result, potassium bromate has been banned as a food additive by: EU, UK, Canada, Australia, China, Brazil, Sri Lanka, Nigeria, Japan, and many other countries. In India: BANNED by FSSAI under Schedule 1 of the Food Safety and Standards (Prohibition and Restrictions on Sales) Regulations. FSSAI classifies potassium bromate as a prohibited substance in bread and bakery products; penalties apply to manufacturers using it. Despite the ban, enforcement challenges mean it may still be found in some Indian bakeries — particularly smaller or unlicensed producers. USA: the FDA has not formally banned potassium bromate but the FDA and California (Prop 65) have expressed concerns; California requires warning labels on bread made with it. Consumers should avoid products labelling it or 'INS 924'; ascorbic acid (E300) is a safe and effective alternative oxidising improver.""",
    'azodicarbonamide': """Azodicarbonamide (ADA / biurea / diazenedicarboxamide / INS 927a) is a synthetic chemical used as a flour bleaching agent and dough conditioner. It is a yellow-orange crystalline solid best known in India and internationally as the "yoga mat chemical" — as it is also used as a blowing agent to manufacture foam rubber, PVC rubber gaskets and yoga mats. In food: ADA oxidises flour carotenoids (bleaching agents) and reacts with gluten proteins (dough conditioner), improving dough strength, elasticity and machinability. It works rapidly — during dough mixing rather than fermentation — allowing bread to rise faster in high-speed commercial production. This allows manufacturers to process more bread with less flour resting time. The primary breakdown product concern: ADA decomposes during baking primarily to biurea (considered safe) and semicarbazide. Semicarbazide (SEM) has been found in finished bread in multiple studies and has been shown to be genotoxic and possibly carcinogenic in animal studies (tumours at high doses in rats). ADA can also react with yeast-derived compounds to form urethane (ethyl carbamate), a probable carcinogen (IARC Group 2A). Regulatory status: BANNED in the EU, UK, Australia, Singapore, China, India and most of Asia. FSSAI: azodicarbonamide is NOT listed as a permitted food additive under FSSAI regulations — it is effectively banned for use in India. In USA: approved by FDA at up to 45 ppm in flour — Subway famously removed it from their bread after a 2014 public petition. Subway's removal demonstrated that commercial bread can be made without ADA using alternative flour improvers (ascorbic acid, enzymes, DATEM). Indian consumers should note that breads made with imported premix or by international fast food chains operating in India should comply with Indian (FSSAI) standards, which prohibit ADA.""",
    'brominated vegetable oil': """Brominated vegetable oil (BVO) is produced by reacting soybean or corn oil with elemental bromine, incorporating bromine atoms into the triglyceride molecules. The increased density (bromine makes it heavier) allows BVO to form stable emulsions with citrus flavour oils (limonene, other terpenes) in water-based beverages — without BVO, the lighter flavour oils would float to the surface. Used for decades in citrus-flavoured soft drinks and sports drinks in the USA, India and some other markets. Bromine is a heavy halogen that accumulates in fatty tissues; repeated consumption at high intake has been associated in case reports with skin rashes, memory problems, nerve damage and excess bromide in the blood (bromism). Long-term studies in animals showed thyroid and cardiac effects at high doses. Regulatory status: FDA revoked its GRAS status and issued a final rule banning BVO from US food in July 2024 (effective within 2 years). PepsiCo and Coca-Cola voluntarily removed BVO from products including Mountain Dew and Fanta in 2014. Still permitted under FSSAI regulations in India; EFSA in EU had not approved it. Quillaja extract (E999) and modified food starch (E1450) are now used as safer alternatives.""",
    # --- Talc ---
    'talcum': 'Talcum is a finely ground mineral powder (hydrated magnesium silicate) derived from natural talc deposits. Used in baby powders, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and industrial applications for its absorbent and lubricating properties.',
    'talc': 'Talc is a naturally occurring clay mineral (magnesium silicate) mined worldwide. Ground into a fine powder, it is used as a filler, lubricant and anti-caking agent in cosmetics, food supplements and pharmaceuticals.',
    # --- CI colorants (commonly questioned) ---
    'ci 47000': """CI 47000 (Quinoline Yellow / E104 / INS 104 / D&C Yellow 10 in cosmetics) is a synthetic greenish-yellow dye derived from quinoline (a coal tar-derived heterocyclic aromatic compound), produced by sulfonation of 2-(2-quinolyl)indanedione. It is NOT an azo dye (unlike most other synthetic food dyes) — it is a quinophthalone dye. Food use: used to colour beverages, confectionery, ice cream, bakery products and medicines in a greenish-yellow colour. Geographic restrictions: banned in USA (FDA has not approved it for food use — it has limited approval in cosmetics as D&C Yellow 10), banned in Canada, Japan, Norway and Australia. Permitted in EU (E104) and India (FSSAI) at regulated limits. Health concerns: included in the 2007 Southampton study (McCann et al., Lancet) that found a mixture of azo dyes + sodium benzoate increased hyperactive behaviour in children — quinoline yellow was one of the six dyes tested. EU mandatory warning: products containing E104 must carry the label statement 'may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children.' This label requirement has led many UK and European manufacturers to voluntarily remove E104 and replace with natural alternatives (turmeric, beta-carotene). Additional concern: cross-reactivity with aspirin in aspirin-sensitive individuals (salicylate sensitivity); potential skin and respiratory sensitisation in susceptible individuals. In cosmetics (D&C Yellow 10): permitted in rinse-off cosmetics in the USA; used in soaps, hair products and some lipsticks. Patch test may be advisable for those with history of contact dermatitis. ADI: 0.5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). Generally declining commercial use as manufacturers move to natural colours. FSSAI and EU permitted at specified limits.""",
    'ci 19140': """CI 19140 (Tartrazine / E102 / FD&C Yellow No. 5 / INS 102 / Lemon Yellow / Acid Yellow 23) is one of the most widely used and extensively studied synthetic yellow azo food dyes. Produced by diazotisation of sulphanilic acid and coupling with 1-(4-sulphophenyl)-3-carboxy-5-pyrazolone — a multi-step organic synthesis from petroleum/coal tar precursors. Produces vivid lemon-yellow colours. Used in: beverages, confectionery, ice cream, desserts, canned vegetables and fruit, baked goods, breakfast cereals, certain medications (tablets, syrups), cosmetics and personal care products. One of the Six Southampton Six: the landmark 2007 McCann et al. study (Lancet, double-blind randomised crossover trial in 3- and 8-year-old children) found a mixture of food dyes including tartrazine combined with sodium benzoate caused a significant increase in hyperactive behaviour — this was not a placebo-controlled study of tartrazine alone but of a six-dye mixture. EU mandatory warning label: all EU products containing E102 must carry 'may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children' on packaging. This requirement has driven significant reformulation away from E102 by UK and EU manufacturers. Allergenicity: tartrazine is one of the most commonly reported allergens among food dyes — documented causes of urticaria, angioedema, rhinitis, asthma and very rarely anaphylaxis in susceptible individuals. Cross-reactivity with aspirin is documented (aspirin-sensitive individuals may react to tartrazine through related mechanisms). India: tartrazine is widely used in Indian food and beverages (yellow sweets, kesar-flavoured products, various beverages) — FSSAI permits it. US FDA: permitted for use with mandatory declaration by name on labels. Australia/NZ: permitted. No mandatory warning label in India. People with asthma, aspirin sensitivity or ADHD may benefit from avoiding tartrazine. JECFA ADI: 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day. In cosmetics: used in hair dyes, lipsticks and makeup to achieve yellow-green shades.""",
    'ci 15985': """CI 15985 (Sunset Yellow FCF / E110 / FD&C Yellow No. 6 / INS 110 / Orange Yellow S / Food Yellow 3) is a synthetic monoazo dye producing bright orange-yellow colours. Produced by diazotisation of sulphanilic acid coupled with 2-naphthol-6-sulfonate. One of the original FD&C (Food, Drug and Cosmetic) certified colours in the USA since 1929. Used in: beverages (soft drinks, sports drinks, orange-flavoured drinks), confectionery, jelly babies, ice cream, tinned fish, sauces, soups, bread mixes and medicines. Produces characteristic orange colour in many popular products. One of the Southampton Six: included in the McCann 2007 Lancet study; EU requires the hyperactivity advisory label on all products containing E110. Bans: Norway and Finland banned E110 on precautionary grounds. The EU permitted it pending the results of the Southampton study and subsequent review; EFSA re-evaluated it in 2009 and found the ADI adequate but affirmed the hyperactivity label requirement. US FDA: approved with mandatory name declaration on labels. India (FSSAI): permitted at specified limits. Allergenicity: sunset yellow can cause allergic reactions including urticaria, angioedema and rhinitis in susceptible individuals; documented cross-reactivity with aspirin sensitivity. Potential to cause gastrointestinal symptoms at higher exposures in some individuals. In cosmetics: used in hair dyes and some colour cosmetics to produce orange shades. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). Declining use in UK/EU as manufacturers switch to natural alternatives (beta-carotene, paprika extract, turmeric) to avoid the hyperactivity advisory label. Still widely used in many markets including India.""",
    'ci 16035': """CI 16035 (Allura Red AC / E129 / FD&C Red No. 40 / INS 129 / Red 40 / Food Red 17) is a synthetic monoazo dye producing a bright red to orange-red colour. Produced by coupling diazotised 5-amino-4-methoxybenzene-sulfonate with 6-hydroxy-2-naphthalene-sulfonate. The most widely used red food dye in the USA and one of the most used worldwide — used in Coca-Cola's signature red packaging, Red 40 being the number one synthetic food colour by volume consumed in the USA. Used in: soft drinks (including many Fanta, Mountain Dew and energy drink varieties), candies (Skittles, M&Ms, red gummy bears), breakfast cereals (Froot Loops), popsicles, dairy desserts, maraschino cherries, sausage casings, some medications and many Indian confectionery products. One of the Southampton Six: E129 was included in the 2007 McCann et al. study; EU mandates the hyperactivity advisory label. Allura Red replaced Amaranth (E123 / Red 2, banned in USA 1976) and Ponceau 4R (E124) in many USA applications when those were restricted. Bans: banned in Denmark, Belgium, France (historically), Switzerland, Sweden, Austria, Norway. US FDA: approved (FD&C Red No. 40); the single most consumed certified dye in the USA. India (FSSAI): permitted. 2023 animal research: a 2023 study (Yan & Bhura et al., Nature Communications) found that high Allura Red intake in mice activated serotonin production in the gut, disrupted gut barrier function and exacerbated colitis — generating widespread media attention. The concentrations used exceeded typical human dietary exposure, and human relevance remains uncertain. Allergenicity: may cause urticaria and cross-react in aspirin-sensitive individuals. ADI: 7 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2009). In cosmetics: widely used red dye in lipsticks, blushes, eye shadows and nail products (may be listed as CI 16035 or Allura Red).""",
    'ci 42090': """CI 42090 (Brilliant Blue FCF / E133 / FD&C Blue No. 1 / INS 133) is a synthetic triarylmethane dye producing a bright blue colour. Unlike the azo dyes (which contain the -N=N- chromophore), brilliant blue belongs to the triphenylmethane family and is produced by condensation of 2-(4-formylphenyl)-N,N-diethyl-benzenesulphonamide with aniline, then oxidation. Produces a uniquely vivid greenish-blue colour. Used in: blue sports drinks (Gatorade), certain candies and confectionery, some ice creams, gelatine products, processed cheese, certain canned peas (to enhance green colour perception when combined with yellow), blue frosting and decorations, medications and dietary supplements. Combined with tartrazine (E102/CI 19140) to produce green colours. Bans: historically banned in Belgium, France, Germany, Greece, Italy, Spain, Switzerland and other EU countries — though EU harmonised food colour regulations now permit E133 across the EU at specified limits. Japan permitted. Allergenicity: may cause urticaria and asthma in susceptible individuals; some cross-reactivity with aspirin sensitivity. Animal toxicology: some chromosomal damage has been observed in in vitro animal cell culture studies at high concentrations — not considered genotoxic at food-use levels by regulatory bodies. NOT included in the Southampton Six — it was not tested in the 2007 McCann study. EU does NOT require the hyperactivity advisory label for E133 (only the Southampton Six require it). ADI: 6 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2010). Approved by FDA (FD&C Blue No. 1, GRAS), EU (E133), FSSAI and CODEX at specified limits. In cosmetics: one of the few approved blue dyes for cosmetic use — used in eye shadow, mascara, shampoos and some soap colourings. Patch test sensitivity can occur in some individuals.""",
    # --- Sugars and sweeteners ---
    'sugar': """Sucrose (table sugar / cane sugar / E-number not typically assigned as a refined food) is a disaccharide composed of one glucose and one fructose unit linked by an alpha-1,beta-2 glycosidic bond. Extracted from sugar cane (Saccharum officinarum, ~80% of global production) or sugar beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. vulgaris) by pressing, juice clarification, evaporation, crystallisation and centrifugation. Provides exactly 4 kcal/g. Glycaemic index of pure sucrose: ~65. In the body, sucrase (intestinal brush-border enzyme) cleaves sucrose to glucose and fructose, which are absorbed and metabolised separately — glucose via insulin-dependent GLUT4 pathways in tissues, fructose almost entirely in the liver via fructokinase. Health concerns: excess free sugar intake is strongly linked to dental caries (oral bacteria ferment sucrose to lactic acid, eroding enamel), obesity, type 2 diabetes, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (via hepatic fructose metabolism) and cardiovascular disease. WHO recommends limiting free sugars to <10% of total energy (~50 g/day for a 2,000-kcal diet), with additional benefit at <5%. FSSAI mandates declaration of added sugars on nutrition labels. Not a food additive requiring an INS number — a basic food ingredient. Safe in moderate amounts; concerning in excess for metabolic and dental health.""",
    'high fructose corn syrup': 'A liquid sweetener made by enzymatically converting glucose in corn starch to fructose. Developed in the 1960s and widely used in North American soft drinks, condiments and processed foods from the 1970s onwards.',
    'glucose syrup': 'A refined liquid sweetener produced by hydrolysing starch (from corn, wheat or potato) into shorter glucose chains. Used in confectionery, baked goods and soft drinks to add sweetness, moisture retention and smooth texture.',
    'invert sugar': """Invert sugar (inverted sugar syrup) is a mixture of equal parts glucose and fructose produced by hydrolysing sucrose — cleaving the glycosidic bond using the enzyme invertase (sucrase), dilute acid, or heat. Called "invert" because the optical rotation of the mixture (−19.8°) is opposite to that of sucrose (+66.5°). It is sweeter than sucrose (~1.1× sweetness) because fructose is released as a free sugar. Highly hygroscopic — actively draws moisture from the air and retains it in the product. Key functional properties: (1) moisture retention — keeps baked goods soft for longer by binding water; (2) crystallisation inhibition — the mixture of two monosaccharides disrupts sucrose crystal packing, essential for fudge, fondant and soft-centre chocolates; (3) freezing point depression — used in ice cream for smoother texture. Nutritionally identical to sucrose after digestion (sucrase converts sucrose to the same molecules). High fructose fraction raises the same hepatic lipogenesis concerns as HFCS at high intake. Not suitable for diabetics in quantity. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'maltodextrin': """Maltodextrin is a highly processed carbohydrate ingredient produced from starch — corn (maize), wheat, potato or tapioca — by partial enzymatic or acid hydrolysis. The starch chains are broken down to shorter glucose polymers characterised by their Dextrose Equivalent (DE), typically DE 3–20; unlike glucose syrup (DE >20), maltodextrin is less sweet than sugar and dissolves readily in water to form a bland, slightly sweet, white powder. It is not a full hydrolysis product — the glucose chains are medium-length oligomers, not single glucose molecules. Glycaemic index: approximately 85–110 — paradoxically higher than table sugar (GI 65) despite appearing to be a starch ingredient, because the short chain length allows rapid digestion and absorption. Functions: bulking agent and filler (adds physical bulk without excessive sweetness), carrier for spray-dried flavours, vitamins and extracts, texture modifier, coating agent, encapsulant, prevents caking and improves flowability in powdered products. Used widely in: protein powders and meal replacements (provides carbohydrate energy), instant beverage mixes, snack seasonings, infant formula (as a carbohydrate energy source — specifically regulated by FSSAI and CODEX for infant foods), soups, sauces, confectionery, pharmaceutical tablet coatings and excipients. Health considerations: despite being a processed starch, maltodextrin raises blood glucose rapidly — people with diabetes, insulin resistance or metabolic syndrome should be aware of its high GI. Regular high intake may negatively affect gut microbiome diversity according to some in vitro studies, though clinical evidence is limited. No allergen risk from corn-derived maltodextrin; wheat-derived maltodextrin is a potential gluten source — EU allergen labelling requires declaration of wheat as an allergen even for derived ingredients unless gluten has been removed. FSSAI, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX permit maltodextrin as a food ingredient.""",
    'corn syrup solids': """Corn syrup solids (CSS) are produced by evaporating most of the water from corn (maize) syrup — a solution of glucose oligomers and polymers obtained by enzymatic hydrolysis of corn starch — until a dry or semi-dry powder (≤10% moisture) is achieved. Characterised by their dextrose equivalent (DE): typically DE 20–36, indicating the degree of starch hydrolysis. At DE 36, about 36% of the carbohydrates are reducing sugars (primarily glucose); the remainder are longer maltooligosaccharide and dextrin chains. Properties: readily dissolves in water to reconstitute a sweet syrup; provides bulk, mild sweetness, browning (Maillard reaction), viscosity and film-forming properties; absorbs moisture and delays staling in baked goods. Used in: non-dairy coffee creamers (provides bulk and sweetness to the powdered matrix), infant formula (carbohydrate energy source — carefully regulated), instant beverage mixes, confectionery coatings, baked goods and snack seasonings. Nutritionally: primarily digestible carbohydrate with high glycaemic index — rapidly raises blood glucose. No nutritional benefit beyond energy compared to other carbohydrate sources. Not a significant allergen (gluten protein is removed during starch processing), though individuals with very severe corn allergy should exercise caution. Suitable as a food ingredient; not a concentrated sugar in the same way as sucrose but provides significant caloric energy. FSSAI, FDA and CODEX recognised as safe at food use levels.""",
    # --- Sweeteners (moderate) ---
    'sucralose': """Sucralose (1,6-dichloro-1,6-dideoxy-β-D-fructofuranosyl-4-chloro-4-deoxy-α-D-galactopyranoside / E955/INS 955) is a non-caloric chlorinated disaccharide sweetener discovered in 1976 at Queen Elizabeth College, London by Shashikant Phadnis and Leslie Hough during research on chlorinated sugars. Produced by selectively replacing three hydroxyl groups on the sucrose molecule with chlorine atoms, creating a highly stable, intensely sweet compound approximately 600 times sweeter than sucrose. The strategic placement of chlorine atoms prevents recognition by intestinal digestive enzymes — sucralose passes through the GI tract essentially undigested and unabsorbed (about 85% excreted in faeces unchanged; ~15% is absorbed but excreted in urine without significant metabolism). Contributes essentially zero calories. Excellent heat stability — stable up to ~130°C, making it suitable for baking unlike aspartame — though at very high temperatures it can undergo some decomposition. Applications: diet carbonated beverages (Pepsi Max, Sprite Zero, Coke Zero variants), sugar-free confectionery, reduced-calorie baked goods, protein supplements, pharmaceutical preparations, tabletop sweeteners (Splenda), oral hygiene products and diabetic foods. Health debate: long considered one of the safest non-nutritive sweeteners, sucralose has faced increasing scrutiny — (1) Gut microbiome: animal and some human studies suggest it may alter the composition and function of the gut microbiome at high doses; clinical significance at typical dietary exposure is debated; (2) Insulin response: some studies report altered glucose and insulin responses in specific metabolic contexts; (3) Sucralose-6-acetate: a metabolite/manufacturing contaminant identified in some studies to be genotoxic in vitro — regulators are reviewing this; (4) EFSA and FDA have maintained safety assessments at current ADI. Not confirmed to be carcinogenic. Does not cause dental caries; compatible with diabetic diets; halal/kosher compatible (no pork or alcohol). ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2017; JECFA). FDA (1998), EU (E955), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e955': 'E955 is the EU code for sucralose, a zero-calorie artificial sweetener about 600 times sweeter than table sugar, used in diet drinks, sugar-free sweets and baked goods.',
    'saccharin': """Saccharin (E954/INS 954 / o-benzoic sulfimide / sodium saccharin) is the world's oldest artificial sweetener, discovered in 1879 by Constantin Fahlberg at Johns Hopkins University during coal tar research — the story goes that he noticed his hands tasted sweet after working with o-sulfobenzoic acid in the lab. It is a sulfonyl amide compound — the cyclic anhydride of o-sulfamylbenzoic acid — about 300–400 times sweeter than sucrose with a characteristic bitter, metallic aftertaste at higher concentrations, largely masked by blending with cyclamate or acesulfame-K. Not metabolised at all — absorbed and excreted in urine completely unchanged; contributes zero calories. Exceptional stability: stable to heat, pH extremes and prolonged storage. Applications: diet soft drinks, tabletop sweeteners (Sweet & Low, Hermesetas), diabetic and pharmaceutical preparations, some toothpastes, cosmetics, industrial applications. History of controversy: in 1977, the FDA proposed a ban after studies showed high-dose saccharin caused bladder tumours in male rats. The mechanism involved a rat-specific urinary protein (α2u-globulin) not present in humans — the resulting urinary crystals caused chronic bladder irritation and tumour promotion; this mechanism is now well-established as rodent-specific. The ban was not enacted — Congress required warning labels (removed in 2000 after the US Department of Health and Human Services delisted saccharin from its carcinogen list). Canada removed saccharin from its prohibited list in 2014 after 37 years. IARC now classifies it as Group 3 (not classifiable as to carcinogenicity to humans). Special regulatory concern in India: FSSAI mandates labels on saccharin-containing foods must state "Not Recommended for Children" and "Contains Saccharin." ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). FDA, EU (E954), FSSAI and CODEX approved with mandatory labelling requirements.""",
    'e954': 'E954 is the EU code for saccharin, the world\'s first artificial sweetener (discovered 1879), used in diet soft drinks, sugar-free confectionery and pharmaceutical products.',
    # --- Fragrance ---
    'fragrance': 'A complex proprietary mixture of aromatic chemicals used to give personal care products their distinctive scent. A single "fragrance" entry on a label may represent dozens or even hundreds of individual chemical ingredients.',
    'perfume': 'A mixture of natural and synthetic aromatic compounds used to create a pleasant scent, listed as a single ingredient to protect proprietary formulas. May contain essential oils, synthetic musks and fixatives.',
    'parfum': """Parfum (EU/International INCI term) or Fragrance (US INCI term) is a broad ingredient category on cosmetic labels that may represent a single fragrance chemical or a complex proprietary blend of dozens to hundreds of individual aromatic molecules — natural essential oil components, synthetic aroma chemicals, fixatives, solvents, preservatives and stabilisers. Cosmetic regulations worldwide allow the entire blend to be declared simply as 'parfum' or 'fragrance' without disclosing individual components, to protect trade secrets. This makes parfum one of the most opaque cosmetic ingredients from a consumer safety perspective. Composition: a typical fine fragrance or cosmetic fragrance blend may contain 10–300 individual compounds. These include terpenes (limonene, linalool, geraniol, citronellol, eugenol — from natural essential oils or synthesised); synthetic musks (including nitromusks like musk ketone, polycyclic musks, macrocyclic musks); aldehydes; esters; phenols; and solvents (often ethanol or diethyl phthalate). Health concerns: fragrance ingredients are the #1 cause of allergic contact dermatitis from cosmetics worldwide. The EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex III) lists 26 specific fragrance allergens that must be individually declared on the label when their concentration exceeds 0.001% in leave-on products and 0.01% in rinse-off products — common allergens include linalool, limonene, citronellol, cinnamal, eugenol, geraniol, benzyl alcohol, isoeugenol, oakmoss. The SCCS (EU Scientific Committee) has recommended expanding the declared allergen list to 80+ compounds. Who should be cautious: anyone with sensitive skin, asthma, eczema, or known fragrance allergy should choose fragrance-free products. Pregnant women and infants are recommended to avoid heavily fragranced cosmetics. In India, FSSAI and BIS cosmetic standards follow EU allergen disclosure requirements increasingly. 'Unscented' products may still contain masking fragrances — 'fragrance-free' is the safer label claim.""",
    'artificial flavor': """Artificial flavour (artificial flavoring / synthetic flavouring) is a broad ingredient category covering any flavouring agent synthesised chemically rather than extracted from a natural source — even if chemically identical to a naturally occurring molecule. A single "artificial flavor" declaration on a label may represent one or multiple synthetic aroma chemicals whose exact identities are not disclosed, protected as proprietary trade secrets. Common compound classes: esters (ethyl butyrate — pineapple; isoamyl acetate — banana), aldehydes (benzaldehyde — almond/cherry), ketones, pyrazines (roasted notes), furanones and thiazoles. In India, FSSAI requires artificial flavours to be labelled but does not mandate disclosure of constituent chemicals — this is the same standard used by FDA, EU (flavour regulation EC 1334/2008) and CODEX. Notable safety concern: diacetyl (butter flavour), used heavily in microwave popcorn, caused bronchiolitis obliterans ("popcorn lung") in factory workers with chronic high-level occupational exposure. Consumer risk from eating popcorn is negligible. Most synthetic flavour molecules in approved commercial use have been reviewed by the Flavor and Extract Manufacturers Association (FEMA) GRAS program and EFSA, and are considered safe at typical food use concentrations. People preferring to avoid synthetics should look for labels stating "no artificial colours or flavours." FSSAI regulates flavouring substances under the Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations.""",
    'artificial flavour': """Artificial flavour (artificial flavouring / synthetic flavour) is a broad ingredient category on food labels covering any flavouring agent produced by chemical synthesis rather than extracted from a natural source — even if the molecule is chemically identical to one found in nature. A single "artificial flavour" declaration may represent one or dozens of synthetic aroma chemicals whose identities are not disclosed to consumers, protected as proprietary trade secrets. Common classes of artificial flavour compounds include: esters (e.g., ethyl butyrate — pineapple note; isoamyl acetate — banana note), aldehydes (e.g., benzaldehyde — almond/cherry), ketones (e.g., 2-heptanone), lactones, pyrazines (roasted notes), thiazoles and furanones. Manufacturing transparency: in India, FSSAI requires that artificial flavours be listed on labels but does not require disclosure of constituent chemicals; the same applies under FDA, EU and CODEX standards — the generic declaration is legally sufficient. Key concerns: (1) Diacetyl (2,3-butanedione) — a synthetic butter flavour used in microwave popcorn. Occupational exposure caused "popcorn lung" (bronchiolitis obliterans) in factory workers; consumer risk from eating popcorn is negligible but the compound has been replaced in most products by acetoin. (2) Propylene glycol diester — used as a solvent carrier for flavours; safe. (3) Artificial vanilla (ethyl vanillin/vanillin) — widely used and safe at food concentrations. (4) Some older synthetic flavours were found to be toxic or carcinogenic and removed — regulatory review processes exist in the EU (EFSA), FDA and FSSAI to screen flavour substances. Not all artificial flavours are unsafe; the vast majority of those in commercial use have been reviewed and cleared as safe at typical food use concentrations. People who prefer to avoid synthetics for personal, dietary or religious reasons should seek products labelled "no artificial colours or flavours."  FSSAI regulates flavouring substances under Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations.""",
    # --- MSG ---
    'monosodium glutamate': """Monosodium glutamate (MSG / E621/INS 621) is the sodium salt of glutamic acid — a non-essential amino acid that occurs abundantly as a natural free amino acid in tomatoes (~0.25 g/100g), parmesan cheese (~1.2 g/100g), soy sauce, mushrooms, miso and many fermented foods. Discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda from kombu seaweed extract; he named the unique taste it provides "umami" (the fifth basic taste alongside sweet, sour, salty and bitter). Commercially produced by bacterial fermentation of sugarcane molasses or starch using Corynebacterium glutamicum — a fermentation process similar to those used to produce vitamins and amino acids. Provides ~4 kcal/g but is used in such small amounts (0.1–0.5% of food) that it contributes negligible calories. The body handles MSG and naturally occurring glutamate identically. Extensive safety history: FDA classifies MSG as GRAS; WHO, EFSA and FSSAI consider it safe. "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" (headaches, flushing attributed to MSG) has not been reproduced in rigorous double-blind studies — the nocebo effect and high sodium content are more likely explanations. FSSAI permits MSG (INS 621) in several food categories with an ADI of "not specified" (JECFA — effectively unlimited at food use levels). People wishing to avoid it can look for "no added MSG" labelling.""",
    'msg': """MSG (monosodium glutamate / E621/INS 621) is the sodium salt of glutamic acid — an amino acid naturally abundant in parmesan cheese, tomatoes, soy sauce and mushrooms. Produced commercially by microbial fermentation of sugarcane molasses. Used as a flavour enhancer that activates umami taste receptors (TAS1R1/TAS1R3), amplifying savoury notes even at concentrations of 0.1–0.5%. The body metabolises MSG glutamate identically to the glutamate from any dietary protein source — there is no physiological distinction. One of the most studied food additives: FDA classifies it GRAS; WHO/JECFA sets ADI as "not specified" (safe at all food use levels); EFSA and FSSAI approve its use. The "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" claim that MSG causes headaches and flushing in sensitive individuals has not been reproducibly confirmed in double-blind, placebo-controlled studies — it is considered scientifically unsubstantiated. MSG is lower in sodium than table salt (~12% sodium vs ~39% for NaCl), and using MSG to enhance flavour can allow overall sodium reduction in a dish. Not recommended for infant formula. People following low-glutamate diets (rare, for specific medical conditions) should avoid.""",
    'e621': 'E621 is the EU code for monosodium glutamate (MSG), a flavour enhancer produced by fermentation of starch or sugar that amplifies the savoury umami taste in foods.',
    # --- Fiber additives ---
    'inulin': """Inulin is a naturally occurring fructan polysaccharide — a chain of fructose units (typically 2–60, with an average degree of polymerisation of ~10) terminated by one glucose unit, connected by β-2,1 glycosidic bonds. Found naturally at high concentrations in chicory root (Cichorium intybus, 35–48% dry weight — the primary commercial source), Jerusalem artichoke (~16–20%), dahlia tubers (~12–19%), garlic (9–16%), onions (2–6%), leeks, asparagus and wheat (1–4%). Commercial production: hot water extraction from chicory roots followed by membrane filtration, ion exchange and spray-drying. Available as: native inulin (average DP ~10–12) and long-chain inulin / HP-inulin (hydrolysis-purified, average DP ~23, stiffer gel, stronger prebiotic effect). Physicochemical properties: slightly sweet (approximately 10% sweetness of sucrose), soluble in warm water; forms heat-irreversible gels at concentrations >15%, providing creamy, fat-like texture — used as a partial fat replacer in low-fat dairy products, spreads and baked goods. Prebiotic function: β-2,1 bonds are not hydrolysed by human digestive enzymes — inulin reaches the colon intact, where Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus species selectively ferment it, producing short-chain fatty acids (butyrate, propionate, acetate) and CO₂. This bifidogenic effect is one of the best-documented prebiotic mechanisms. Clinical evidence: inulin supplementation (5–10 g/day) consistently increases Bifidobacterium in the colon; reduces putrefactive bacteria; may improve stool frequency and consistency; reduces serum triglycerides in some studies; modestly reduces glycaemic response. Calcium absorption: inulin fermentation produces a mildly acidic colonic environment that improves calcium solubility and absorption — of particular relevance to bone health. In infants: galacto-oligosaccharides (GOS) + long-chain fructo-oligosaccharides (lcFOS) mixtures (similar to inulin) are added to infant formula to mimic breast milk prebiotic effects. Side effects: dose-dependent flatulence and bloating are the main adverse effects — typically manageable with gradual introduction. People with fructose malabsorption or FODMAP sensitivities should be cautious. Not suitable for people with known inulin/chicory allergy (rare). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS, 2018 for long-chain inulin), EU permitted, FSSAI approved as a food ingredient.""",
    'polydextrose': """Polydextrose (E1200/INS 1200) is a synthetic soluble dietary fibre produced by vacuum thermal polymerisation of glucose with a small proportion (~10%) of sorbitol and citric acid (as catalyst) at high temperature (~100–160°C), creating a randomly branched polymer of glucose with predominantly 1,6- and some 1,4-glucosidic linkages, with sorbitol at many of the terminal positions and citrate ester groups scattered throughout the backbone. Average molecular weight ~2000 Da (DP ~12). Caloric value: 1 kcal/g (European designation) or approximately 1 kcal/g (FDA) — because it is only partially fermented in the colon (about 50% fermented by colonic bacteria to short-chain fatty acids; 50% excreted unchanged). Physical properties: white to pale yellow powder; soluble in water to form low-viscosity solutions; no significant thickening effect at typical concentrations; mild sweetness (~1/10th of sucrose). Functions in food: (1) low-calorie bulking agent and sucrose/fat replacer — provides body, mouthfeel and structure in reduced-calorie products without significant caloric contribution; used in low-calorie baked goods, confectionery, dairy desserts, salad dressings, beverages and ice cream; (2) prebiotic dietary fibre — fermented by Bifidobacterium and other commensal bacteria; (3) humectant — retains moisture in bakery products. Regulatory fibre status: FDA approved polydextrose as a dietary fibre for label declaration purposes (2018), after extensive review of fermentability and physiological benefits; similar EU and FSSAI position. Health benefits: contributes to daily fibre intake (recommended 25–38 g/day); modest prebiotic effects; reduces energy density of foods. Laxative effect: high doses (>50 g/day) may cause soft stools; well-tolerated at normal food use concentrations (typically 1–15 g per serving). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E1200), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    # --- Oils and fats ---
    'palm oil': """Palm oil is a semi-solid vegetable fat extracted from the orange-red mesocarp (pulp) of the fruit of the oil palm tree (Elaeis guineensis), native to West Africa and now cultivated predominantly in Indonesia and Malaysia (accounting for ~85% of global supply). It is the world's most produced and traded vegetable oil. Chemical composition: approximately 44% palmitic acid (saturated C16:0), 39% oleic acid (monounsaturated C18:1), 10% linoleic acid (polyunsaturated C18:2) and 5% stearic acid — one of the most saturated of all vegetable oils. Also very rich in carotenoids (responsible for its characteristic red-orange colour in unrefined form) and Vitamin E tocotrienols. Refined, bleached and deodorised (RBD) palm oil is colourless and flavour-neutral. Functional advantages in food: naturally semi-solid at room temperature (~35–40°C melting point), providing texture without hydrogenation and thus without trans fats — widely used as a trans-fat-free alternative in biscuits, instant noodles, chocolate, margarine and commercial frying fats. Health concern: high saturated fat content, particularly palmitic acid, raises LDL cholesterol and is associated with increased cardiovascular disease risk at high intakes. Environmental concern: palm oil expansion has driven tropical deforestation in Indonesia and Malaysia, with severe biodiversity loss. RSPO (Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil) certification addresses this. FSSAI permits refined palm oil as an edible vegetable oil. People with cardiovascular risk should monitor saturated fat intake from palm oil sources.""",
    'palmolein': """Palmolein (palm olein) is the liquid fraction of palm oil, obtained by fractional crystallisation (fractionation) of refined palm oil at controlled temperatures (around 15–20°C). When palm oil is cooled, it partially solidifies — the solid fraction (palm stearin, rich in saturated palmitic and stearic acids) separates from the liquid fraction (palm olein, relatively enriched in oleic acid). Palm olein contains approximately: 40% palmitic acid (C16:0), 44% oleic acid (C18:1), 10% linoleic acid (C18:2) — a higher proportion of monounsaturated oleic acid than crude palm oil. This composition gives palmolein a lower melting point and better fluidity at room temperature than palm stearin, making it suitable as a liquid cooking oil. Widely used in Indian cooking, South and Southeast Asian food manufacturing, and commercial deep frying (similar high smoke point to crude palm oil, ~220°C, with better clarity and oxidative stability than many vegetable oils). Environmental and sustainability concerns: palm oil and its fractions have been linked to deforestation of tropical rainforest habitats in Indonesia and Malaysia, with significant biodiversity and carbon emission impacts. The RSPO (Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil) certification attempts to address this. Nutritionally: the palmitic acid content is high enough that regular consumption contributes to saturated fat intake. FSSAI permits palmolein as a refined vegetable oil. Generally safe at normal dietary levels.""",
    'hydrogenated': """Hydrogenated fat is produced by a chemical process called catalytic hydrogenation: liquid vegetable oil is reacted with hydrogen gas at high temperature (150–200°C) and pressure in the presence of a nickel catalyst. Under full hydrogenation conditions, most of the carbon-carbon double bonds in unsaturated fatty acids are saturated — converting liquid oil into a solid fat with a higher melting point, better stability against oxidative rancidity and longer shelf life. Fully hydrogenated vegetable oils (no remaining double bonds) contain negligible trans fats — the product is essentially a saturated fat similar in hardness to palm stearin. Used in: confectionery fats, bakery shortenings, margarine and specialty fats for food manufacturing. Fully hydrogenated fat is distinct from partially hydrogenated fat (which is the source of harmful trans fatty acids). FSSAI has mandated that trans fat content in partially hydrogenated oils used in Indian foods must not exceed 2% since 2021 (WHO elimination target). Consumers should distinguish between "fully hydrogenated" (minimal trans fat, high saturated fat) and "partially hydrogenated" (contains trans fats) on ingredient labels — both are functionally similar food fats but with different health implications.""",
    'partially hydrogenated': """Partially hydrogenated vegetable oil (PHVO) is produced by incomplete catalytic hydrogenation of liquid vegetable oil — hydrogen is added but the reaction is stopped before full saturation, leaving residual double bonds. During this incomplete reaction, many cis double bonds are converted to trans configuration, generating trans fatty acids (TFAs, primarily elaidic acid — trans-octadecenoic acid). These trans fats provide the desired semi-solid texture (for spreadability in margarine, flakiness in pastries, creaminess in fried snacks) but with a critical health consequence: TFAs raise LDL cholesterol and simultaneously lower HDL cholesterol — the worst lipid profile effect of any dietary fat, double the cardiovascular risk of saturated fat. Multiple meta-analyses confirm that industrial TFAs from partially hydrogenated oils significantly increase coronary heart disease risk and myocardial infarction mortality. Global regulatory response has been decisive: the USA FDA determined that partially hydrogenated oils are no longer GRAS (2015), with a final compliance date of 2018. WHO launched its REPLACE initiative in 2018 to eliminate industrial trans fats globally by 2023. FSSAI restricted trans fats in vanaspati, bakery shortening and margarine to ≤2% of total fat since 2021. India had previously permitted up to 10% TFAs. Despite progress, legacy products and small-scale manufacturers may still use PHVO. Consumers should actively avoid ingredients listing 'partially hydrogenated' on any food label.""",
    # --- Emulsifiers and stabilizers ---
    'soy lecithin': """Soy lecithin (E322 / soya lecithin) is the most commercially produced form of food-grade lecithin, extracted from soybean oil during the degumming step of soy oil refining: water is added to crude soybean oil to hydrate and precipitate the phospholipids, which are then separated by centrifugation, dried and sold as soy lecithin. It is a by-product of soybean oil production — one of the most abundant and inexpensive phospholipid sources available. Composition: a complex mixture of phospholipids (phosphatidylcholine ~35%, phosphatidylethanolamine ~20%, phosphatidylinositol ~20%), neutral lipids and small amounts of other components. Soy lecithin may be de-oiled (with residual oil removed to give a free-flowing powder) or fluid/standard (with residual oil, giving a paste). GMO consideration: most commercial soybeans are genetically modified (>90% in the USA and globally); soy lecithin is typically derived from GMO soy unless specifically labelled non-GMO or organic. The phospholipid fraction of soy lecithin contains negligible soy protein — processing removes virtually all protein — so the GMO protein is absent in the final lecithin ingredient. Functions in food: emulsifier (oil-water stabilisation in chocolate, margarine, salad dressings, instant foods); dough strengthener in bread and baked goods; release agent in baking; viscosity reducer in chocolate. Nutritional benefit: a source of dietary choline (an essential nutrient). Allergen considerations: soy is a major allergen (among 8 in USA, 14 in EU). The soy protein is largely absent in refined lecithin — EFSA and FDA acknowledge that highly refined soy oil (and by extension, refined lecithin) typically does not cause allergic reactions in soy-allergic individuals. However, FSSAI and EU require 'soya lecithin' to be declared on labels given soy's allergen status. People with very severe soy allergy should consider sunflower lecithin alternatives. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E322), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'mono and diglycerides': """Mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids (MDG / E471/INS 471) are the most widely used food emulsifiers by volume globally. Chemically they are partial esters of glycerol with fatty acids: a monoglyceride has one fatty acid chain esterified to one hydroxyl of the glycerol backbone; a diglyceride has two. Commercial grades typically contain ~40–50% monoglycerides, ~40–50% diglycerides and ~10–20% triglycerides (regular fats) and free fatty acids — produced by interesterification or glycerolysis of refined edible oils or animal fats with glycerol, using alkaline catalyst or lipase enzymes. Source matters: may be derived from vegetable oils (palm, soybean, rapeseed — vegan/vegetarian acceptable) or from animal fats (tallow, lard — not acceptable to vegans, vegetarians or those following halal/kosher practices without specific certification). Trans fatty acid concern: MDG produced from partially hydrogenated oils contain trans fatty acids; most modern manufacturers now use non-hydrogenated source fats, but legacy products may still contain trans-containing MDG — not distinguishable from the label. Also: EFSA (2017) identified glycidol fatty acid esters (GFAEs) as a 2A probable carcinogen concern in refined palm oil products; MDG from palm oil processing can contain trace GFAEs — industry response has been to improve refining processes. Food functions: dough conditioner — starch complexation by monoglycerides delays staling in bread and baked goods (amylose-lipid complex formation reduces retrogradation); ice cream stabiliser — prevents ice crystal growth; margarine and spread emulsification; chocolate texture; whipping cream stabiliser. ADI: not specified (JECFA — largely metabolised as normal dietary fats). FSSAI, EU (E471), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'polyglycerol polyricinoleate': """Polyglycerol polyricinoleate (PGPR / E476/INS 476) is a synthetic emulsifier produced by esterifying polyglycerol (a polymer of glycerol, typically 3–6 glycerol units) with polymerised ricinoleic acid derived from castor bean oil (Ricinus communis). The result is a viscous, amber-coloured oil with unusually powerful emulsifying properties — particularly effective at reducing viscosity and improving flow of fat-based systems (water-in-oil interface modification). Primary application: chocolate manufacturing — the chocolate industry uses PGPR to reduce the apparent viscosity of melted chocolate, particularly at chocolate manufacturers where tight control of flow properties ('yield value') during enrobing, moulding and tempering is critical. PGPR specifically addresses the yield stress (initial resistance to flow) of chocolate, complementing cocoa butter's role in plastic viscosity; together, PGPR + lecithin provide a synergistic viscosity reduction. This allows manufacturers to reduce cocoa butter content (a very expensive ingredient) — typically 3–5 g PGPR per kg chocolate can replace 20–30 g cocoa butter per kg, a significant cost reduction in industrial chocolate. Also used in peanut butter (as an oil-stabilising emulsifier) and some baked goods and spreads. Castor bean safety: while the castor bean plant is the source of the deadly protein ricin, the industrial processing of castor oil (to extract ricinoleic acid) completely destroys any ricin; no ricin is present in food-grade castor oil or PGPR. Health: PGPR is partially absorbed and metabolised as a fat; JECFA (2012) and EFSA (2017) both completed safety evaluations — ADI raised from 7.5 to 25 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2017). FDA (GRAS in chocolate), EU (E476), FSSAI and CODEX approved. No allergen concerns (castor bean protein is absent).""",
    'ammonium phosphatides': """Ammonium phosphatides (E442/INS 442) are a mixture of ammonium salts of phosphatidic acids derived by enzymatic phosphorylation of mono- and diglycerides from rapeseed (canola) oil. Chemically distinct from lecithin (which consists of phosphatidylcholines); ammonium phosphatides have a simpler headgroup structure that confers specific rheological properties in chocolate. Primary application: chocolate and cocoa products, where they reduce plastic viscosity and yield value of molten chocolate more effectively than equivalent amounts of soy lecithin. This allows manufacturers to reduce cocoa butter content (a cost saving) while maintaining flow properties and mouthfeel. Typical use level: 0.1–0.2% in chocolate. Unlike soy lecithin, ammonium phosphatides are soy-free — an important consideration for soy-allergic consumers. Derived from rapeseed oil — rapeseed/canola is not a common food allergen. Metabolised to glycerol, fatty acids, phosphoric acid and ammonium — all normal food constituents. EU (E442), FSSAI and CODEX approved. ADI: 0–30 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Generally safe; no known sensitisation or toxicological concerns at food use levels. Vegan-friendly and halal-compatible when derived from plant sources.""",
    'carrageenan': """Carrageenan (E407/INS 407) is a family of high-molecular-weight sulphated polysaccharides extracted from several species of red edible seaweeds: primarily Chondrus crispus (Irish moss), Gigartina species, Eucheuma denticulatum and Kappaphycus alvarezii. Used in Ireland and Britain for centuries as a food thickener — Irish moss jelly (blancmange) predates industrial carrageenan by hundreds of years. Three main commercial types with distinct gelling properties: (1) Kappa-carrageenan — contains one sulphate group per disaccharide; forms firm, brittle gels in the presence of potassium ions; used in dairy gels, restructured meat and pet food; (2) Iota-carrageenan — contains two sulphate groups; forms soft, elastic gels with calcium ions; used in dairy products and cosmetics; (3) Lambda-carrageenan — three sulphate groups; does not gel but is an effective thickener and stabiliser in cold systems. Chemical structure: alternating 3-linked β-D-galactose and 4-linked 3,6-anhydro-D-galactose backbone with sulphate half-esters at various positions — a high-density anionic polymer. Applications: dairy products (chocolate milk, coffee cream, ice cream — prevents whey separation), plant-based milks (oat, almond, soy — provides creamy texture), processed deli meats (water-binding and sliceability), salad dressings, infant formula, pet food, cosmetics and pharmaceutical tablet coatings. Health controversy: degraded carrageenan (poligeenan / degraded carrageenan, produced by acid hydrolysis at low molecular weight) is NOT a food additive — it has been shown to cause intestinal inflammation in animal models and is IARC Group 2B (possibly carcinogenic). Food-grade carrageenan is high-molecular-weight, intact polymer — NOT the same as degraded carrageenan. However: some researchers (notably Dr Joanne Tobacman) have argued that stomach acid can degrade food-grade carrageenan to lower-MW fractions that may act as inflammatory promoters. Multiple systematic reviews and EFSA (2018) and JECFA evaluations maintain that food-grade carrageenan does not cause intestinal damage at food use concentrations. In infants: due to unresolved concerns, several countries advise against carrageenan in infant formula — EU restricts its use in infant formula to 0.3 g/L. The US National Organic Program moved to remove it from organic certified products in 2016 (later reversed). Sensitive populations — people with inflammatory bowel disease — may choose to avoid it. ADI: not specified (JECFA — for food-grade carrageenan). FSSAI, EU (E407), FDA and CODEX approved with restrictions in infant formula.""",
    # --- Surfactants (personal care) ---
    'sodium laureth sulfate': """Sodium laureth sulfate (SLES / sodium lauryl ether sulfate) is an anionic surfactant derived from lauryl alcohol (from coconut oil or palm kernel oil) by ethoxylation followed by sulfation. The ethoxylation step adds 2–3 ethylene oxide units to the lauryl chain, which reduces the skin irritation potential compared to non-ethoxylated SLS. SLES is the dominant surfactant in shampoos, body washes, liquid hand soaps, toothpastes and bubble baths worldwide. Function: creates rich lather; effectively removes oil, sebum, dirt and product residue; works well across a range of water hardnesses. Mildness vs SLS: SLES is significantly less irritating to skin and mucous membranes than SLS because the ether oxygen groups reduce binding to skin proteins. However, SLES still disrupts the skin lipid barrier with repeated use — frequent washing with high-SLES products can cause dryness, especially in people with sensitive or dry skin conditions. Concerns: (1) 1,4-Dioxane contamination — the ethoxylation process can leave trace 1,4-dioxane (a probable human carcinogen, IARC Group 2B). The FDA advises minimising it in cosmetics; the EU restricts it to <10 ppm. Manufacturers can remove it through vacuum stripping after synthesis. (2) Scalp or skin irritation with damaged or sensitised skin. Not a sensitiser (does not cause allergic reactions at normal use levels). Not carcinogenic itself. FSSAI-permitted in cosmetics. Safe for the general population in rinse-off use.""",
    'sodium laureth sulphate': """Sodium laureth sulphate (UK/Indian spelling — same compound as sodium laureth sulfate / SLES). An anionic surfactant produced by ethoxylation of lauryl alcohol (coconut/palm-derived) followed by sulfation. Ethoxylation with 2–3 ethylene oxide units makes it significantly milder than sodium lauryl sulphate (SLS) by reducing affinity for skin proteins. One of the most widely used cosmetic surfactants globally — found in virtually every commercial shampoo, body wash and shower gel. Creates abundant foam and efficiently removes oil and grime. Mildness: well tolerated by most people in rinse-off use; skin contact time is brief. Frequent high-concentration use (daily shampooing, multiple daily washes) may dry out skin or scalp over time by disrupting the lipid barrier. Key concern: ethoxylation can leave trace 1,4-dioxane (a probable human carcinogen, IARC Group 2B) in the ingredient. Reputable manufacturers remove this via vacuum stripping — consumers can look for transparency on 1,4-dioxane testing. EU limits 1,4-dioxane in cosmetics at <10 ppm. SLES itself is not classified as carcinogenic. Approved in India (BIS/FSSAI cosmetic standards), EU, USA and globally. Vegan; derived from plant oils. Not a skin sensitiser at normal use concentrations. People with eczema, psoriasis or a damaged scalp barrier should consider surfactant-free or SLES-free alternatives.""",
    'ammonium laureth sulfate': """Ammonium laureth sulfate (ALES) is the ammonium salt form of the SLES family — produced by the same ethoxylation-sulfation route on lauryl alcohol (coconut/palm-derived) but neutralised with ammonium hydroxide instead of sodium hydroxide. Properties: anionic surfactant with very similar cleansing, foaming and mildness profile to SLES. Slightly more acidic final pH compared to SLES formulations — this is advantageous in hair products where a lower pH (closer to hair's natural acidic pH ~3.67) helps smooth and close the cuticle, increasing shine and reducing frizz. Used in: shampoos (especially those targeting smooth, shiny or colour-protected hair), body washes and bubble baths. Often chosen over SLES in formulations where a lower pH profile is desired. The ammonium counterion is metabolised normally in the body — contributes to a faint ammonia note in concentrated raw form, undetectable in finished consumer products. Like SLES, ethoxylation can introduce trace 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B probable carcinogen) — manufacturers should use vacuum stripping to remove it; EU limits at <10 ppm. ALES itself is not carcinogenic. Not a sensitiser at normal cosmetic use concentrations. Well tolerated by most skin types in rinse-off use. Approved in EU, USA, India and globally. Vegan (plant-derived surfactant backbone). Caution for people with eczema, scalp sensitivity or compromised skin barrier — consider gentler surfactant alternatives.""",
    'cocamidopropyl betaine': 'A mild amphoteric surfactant derived from coconut oil, used in shampoos, body washes and baby products. Added to reduce irritation from stronger surfactants and to improve conditioning and lather.',
    # --- Preservatives (milder) ---
    'phenoxyethanol': 'A synthetic preservative and solvent used in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals since the 1950s to prevent microbial growth. Found in skincare, haircare, eye cosmetics and baby products.',
    'benzyl alcohol': 'A naturally occurring aromatic alcohol found in jasmine, hyacinth and ylang-ylang, also produced synthetically. Used as a preservative, solvent and fragrance ingredient in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals.',
    'potassium sorbate': 'The potassium salt of sorbic acid (E202), a natural preservative originally isolated from rowan berries. Used to inhibit mould and yeast in cheese, wine, dried fruits, baked goods and cosmetics.',
    'e202': 'E202 is the EU code for potassium sorbate, a widely used food and cosmetic preservative that inhibits the growth of mould, yeast and some bacteria at low concentrations.',
    'e407': 'E407 is the EU code for carrageenan, a natural thickener and stabiliser extracted from red seaweed, used in dairy products, meat products and plant-based alternatives.',
    # --- Petroleum-derived ---
    'mineral oil': 'A highly refined petroleum-derived oil used as a moisturiser and emollient in cosmetics and as a laxative in medicines. Also used as a food-grade lubricant on processing machinery and as a coating on some foods.',
    'petrolatum': 'Petroleum jelly (e.g., Vaseline), a semi-solid mixture of hydrocarbons refined from petroleum. Used as a skin protectant and occlusive moisturiser since 1872 and as a base for ointments.',
    'paraffinum liquidum': """Paraffinum liquidum (INCI name for liquid paraffin / white mineral oil) is a highly refined, purified mixture of saturated aliphatic hydrocarbons (paraffins) derived from petroleum — essentially the liquid form of petroleum jelly. It is produced by vacuum distillation and hydrotreatment/hydrorefining of crude petroleum, removing aromatic compounds, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) and other impurities. The degree of refining is critical for safety. Types: cosmetic/pharmaceutical grade (highly refined, low aromatic content) vs. industrial/technical grade (less refined, higher PAH content). In cosmetics: a classic and highly effective occlusive emollient — it sits on top of the skin surface forming a thin layer that prevents transepidermal water loss (TEWL). Used in cold creams, baby oils, lip balms, hair oils, ointments, moisturisers, cleansing milks and barrier creams. It does not penetrate significantly into the skin or get absorbed into the body from topical application. In food: food-grade white mineral oil (E905b) is approved as a surface treatment agent on some fruits and vegetables and as a release agent in baking. Health concerns: (1) Refined mineral oil (cosmetic/food grade): IARC Group 3 — not classifiable as carcinogen. (2) Untreated/mildly treated mineral oils: IARC Group 1 carcinogens (occupational skin cancer risk from heavy industrial exposure — NOT from cosmetic use). (3) Oral consumption: mineral oil used as a laxative can impair absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) with chronic use. (4) Contamination of food via packaging migration: MOSH (mineral oil saturated hydrocarbons) and MOAH (aromatic hydrocarbons) migrating from recycled cardboard into food — ongoing EU regulatory concern. Cosmetic grade paraffinum liquidum on intact skin: well-tolerated, non-comedogenic at low concentrations. Some people report it feels heavy or greasy on skin. Not sensitising or allergenic. Not vegan (though not animal-derived — derived from petroleum; some vegans object on environmental grounds).""",
    'paraffin wax': 'A petroleum-derived solid wax used as a coating and glazing agent in food (E905) and as an occlusive ingredient in cosmetics. Also widely used in candles and as a waterproofing material.',
    # --- PEG compounds ---
    'peg-': 'PEG (polyethylene glycol) compounds are petroleum-derived polymers used as emollients, emulsifiers, thickeners and surfactants in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals. The number after PEG indicates the average molecular weight.',
    'polyethylene glycol': """Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is a family of synthetic water-soluble polymers made by polymerising ethylene oxide — the same starting material as 1,4-dioxane (a probable human carcinogen). The number after PEG indicates average molecular weight (e.g., PEG-4 = low MW, PEG-100 = high MW). Manufactured by ethoxylation — a process that can leave trace 1,4-dioxane and ethylene oxide residues. Low-MW PEGs (PEG-4 to PEG-32): used as emollients, humectants and solvents; small molecular size allows some skin penetration. High-MW PEGs (PEG-100+): used as thickeners, emulsifiers and film-formers; too large to penetrate intact skin appreciably. Functions: humectant (attracts water to skin), emollient, emulsifier (e.g., PEG-100 stearate in cream formulas), texture modifier. Health concerns: (1) 1,4-dioxane contamination — trace levels detected in ethoxylated cosmetic ingredients including PEGs; FDA advises manufacturers to remove it but does not mandate testing; the EU restricts to <10 ppm in cosmetics. (2) Skin penetration enhancement — PEGs can temporarily increase skin permeability, potentially increasing absorption of co-applied ingredients; concern when used on damaged or broken skin. (3) Low-MW PEG ingestion (oral laxatives) — safe and approved as a pharmaceutical laxative. Cosmetic use on intact skin: generally considered safe at standard concentrations by FDA, EU and FSSAI. On damaged or eczematous skin, caution is recommended — use on wounds or severely broken skin should be avoided as systemic absorption increases. Not a sensitiser at normal cosmetic concentrations. Vegan; not animal-derived.""",
    # --- Denatured alcohols ---
    'alcohol denat': """Alcohol denat. (alcohol denatured / denatured alcohol / denatured ethanol) is ethyl alcohol (ethanol) that has been deliberately made unfit for human consumption by adding denaturants — specific compounds regulated by government tax authorities to exempt the alcohol from beverage taxation while permitting industrial and cosmetic use. Common denaturants used in cosmetic-grade denatured alcohol include: denatonium benzoate (the most bitter substance known — bittering agent), t-butyl alcohol, isopropanol, acetone, diethyl phthalate and methyl ethyl ketone (MEK). The specific denaturant formula is typically identified by an SDA (Specially Denatured Alcohol) number (e.g., SDA 40-B is denatured with denatonium benzoate and t-butyl alcohol — the most widely used cosmetic denaturant). EU cosmetic formulas use "Alcohol denat." as the INCI name. Functions in cosmetics: identical to undenatured ethanol — solvent (dissolves active ingredients, fragrance compounds and preservatives), antiseptic (broad-spectrum antimicrobial at 60–80%), astringent (reduces oiliness, tightens appearance of pores), quick-drying carrier (leaves actives on skin without water residue in toners, setting sprays), penetration enhancer (transiently increases skin permeability). The specific denaturant used matters for safety: the EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex IV lists permitted denaturants for cosmetics; methanol (toxic) is NOT permitted in cosmetic alcohol denat. Safety: at cosmetic use concentrations, alcohol denat. is generally safe for most adults; caution for damaged skin, eczema-prone skin or in concentrated leave-on formulations. Children's exposure to high-ethanol hand sanitisers should be supervised (accidental ingestion risk). Alcohol content on cosmetic labels is required in many jurisdictions.""",
    'denatured alcohol': """Denatured alcohol (alcohol denat. / SD alcohol) is ethanol (ethyl alcohol, the same compound found in alcoholic drinks) that has been deliberately rendered unfit for human consumption by adding denaturants — chemical agents that make it unpalatable, toxic or undrinkable, exempting it from beverage alcohol taxation. Common denaturants: denatonium benzoate (the bitterest known substance), methanol (toxic; used in industrial applications — not approved in cosmetics), isopropanol, acetone, diethyl phthalate, t-butyl alcohol and camphor. In cosmetics: pure ethanol and SD (specially denatured) alcohols are widely used as solvents (dissolve many active ingredients), antiseptics (kill bacteria and viruses on skin contact), astringents (temporarily tighten pores and reduce oiliness), quick-drying carriers (in sprays, toners and setting sprays — evaporates rapidly leaving actives on skin) and as penetration enhancers that temporarily increase skin permeability. Common in: face toners, hand sanitisers, setting sprays, hair sprays, antiperspirants, aftershaves, perfumes. Health considerations: alcohol is drying to skin with repeated high-concentration use — disrupts the skin's lipid barrier and stratum corneum, leading to dryness, irritation and potential sensitivity over time. This is especially problematic for people with sensitive skin, rosacea, eczema or dry skin conditions. The EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex IV lists some denaturants permitted in cosmetics; the specific denaturant used matters — SDA 40-B (denatured with denatonium and t-butyl alcohol) is a common cosmetic denaturant. At low concentrations in leave-on products (e.g., serums with <20% alcohol) impact on skin barrier is minimal for most people. Not a carcinogen in cosmetic use. The denaturant does not change the fundamental solvent/antiseptic properties of the ethanol.""",
    'sd alcohol': """SD alcohol (specially denatured alcohol) is a form of denatured ethanol produced under formulas specified by regulatory authorities (in the USA, the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau / TTB) for specific industrial and cosmetic uses. "SD" indicates the specific denaturant formula used — for example, SD Alcohol 40 (also written SDA 40-B) is the most widely used cosmetic grade, denatured with denatonium benzoate (extremely bitter) and t-butanol, making it safe for external cosmetic use while being unfit for drinking. The denaturant number (40, 39-C, 23-H, etc.) encodes the specific formula. Functions identically to undenatured ethanol in cosmetics: solvent (dissolves fragrance compounds, emollients and actives), antiseptic/antimicrobial, quick-drying carrier (evaporates rapidly), astringent, penetration enhancer and viscosity modifier. Widely used in: perfumes and colognes, hair sprays, toners, astringents, hand sanitisers, antiperspirants, aftershaves, setting sprays and cosmetic preparations. In the EU cosmetics framework, the equivalent is "Alcohol denat." — same concept, different notation. Safety at cosmetic use concentrations: generally safe for adults on intact skin in rinse-off and most leave-on products; may be drying with very frequent use on sensitive skin; children should not have prolonged exposure to high-alcohol products (absorption through mucous membranes). The specific denaturant (denatonium, t-butanol) at trace cosmetic concentrations is not a toxicological concern but is not suitable for internal use. FSSAI and BIS cosmetic standards permit SD alcohol in cosmetics. The denaturant type should not include methanol (toxic — some counterfeit products have caused methanol poisoning).""",
    # --- Allergens ---
    'lanolin': 'A natural waxy substance produced by the sebaceous glands of wool-bearing animals, mainly sheep. Extracted during wool processing, it is used as a skin softener and emollient in lip balms, creams and nipple creams.',
    'wool wax': """Wool wax (lanolin / Adeps lanae) is the natural sebaceous wax secreted by sebaceous glands of wool-bearing sheep (primarily Merino breeds) to condition and waterproof their wool. Extracted from raw wool during the scouring (washing) process — the washwater is centrifuged to recover the wax, which is then refined. Composition: complex mixture of wax esters (~97%) — primarily esters of long-chain fatty alcohols (lanolin alcohols — lanosterol, cholesterol, agnosterol) and long-chain fatty acids (hydroxy acids and straight-chain acids). Unlike plant waxes, lanolin closely resembles human sebum in its chemical composition — this is why it is uniquely effective as a skin emollient. In cosmetics: occlusive emollient (creates a moisture-retaining barrier on skin); one of the most effective emollients for extremely dry skin, cracked heels, dry lips and nursing nipples; used in creams, lip balms, ointments, hair conditioners and pharmaceutical preparations. Lanolin USP is used in topical pharmaceutical preparations. Allergen concern: lanolin allergy (contact dermatitis) has been widely discussed — historically quoted at 1–5% of eczema patients on patch testing; however, many older studies used impure lanolin containing allergens that have since been removed from pharmaceutical-grade lanolin. Modern purified wool wax/lanolin has a much lower sensitisation rate. Still avoid on broken or eczematous skin if lanolin sensitivity is suspected.""",
    # --- Silicones (cyclic) ---
    'cyclomethicone': """Cyclomethicone is a trade/INCI term for a blend of cyclic siloxane compounds — primarily cyclopentasiloxane (D5), cyclotetrasiloxane (D4) and cyclohexasiloxane (D6) — used in cosmetics as volatile, lightweight silicone carriers. These are organosilicon compounds (silicone ring structures) that evaporate completely from skin and hair after application, leaving behind other non-volatile actives. Properties: extremely low viscosity, silky smooth feel, excellent spreadability, odourless, colourless, non-greasy, and leave no residue after evaporation. Used in: hair conditioners and serums (smooth cuticle, reduce frizz, deliver non-volatile silicones), foundations, primers and BB creams (silky application, improve spreadability), moisturisers (lightweight carrier), deodorant sticks and antiperspirants, sunscreens (improve texture), dry hair oils. Regulatory concerns: D4 (octamethylcyclotetrasiloxane) has been classified by the EU as a persistent, bioaccumulative and toxic (PBT) substance and as a substance of very high concern (SVHC) — restricted to <0.1% in rinse-off cosmetics. D5 (decamethylcyclopentasiloxane) is also under restriction in the EU (<0.1% in rinse-off cosmetics since 2020) due to environmental persistence and bioaccumulation, though endocrine disruption studies in humans have not confirmed harm at cosmetic exposure levels. D6 has lower regulatory restriction. Canada has also restricted D4 and D5 in products. In India and USA, no specific restrictions as of 2024, but the EU restrictions are considered global best practice. Environmental concern: D4 and D5 are released to the environment via rinse-off products (shampoos, body washes) and are detected in rivers and fish. Not significantly absorbed through intact human skin. Generally not a sensitiser.""",
    'cyclopentasiloxane': 'A cyclic silicone (D5) widely used in haircare and skincare as a lightweight conditioning agent that evaporates after application, leaving no residue and imparting silky smoothness.',
    'cyclohexasiloxane': """Cyclohexasiloxane (D6 / dodecamethylcyclohexasiloxane) is a cyclic polydimethylsiloxane compound with a six-membered siloxane ring — the largest of the main cyclic silicones used in cosmetics. Like its smaller siblings D4 and D5, cyclohexasiloxane is a volatile cyclic silicone that evaporates from skin after application. It is used as a lightweight carrier and conditioning agent in hair and skin care products. Being a larger molecule than D4/D5, D6 evaporates more slowly and has lower vapour pressure, which means it stays on skin slightly longer before evaporating. Found in: hair serums, smoothing products, facial primers, foundations, body lotions and leave-on conditioners. Properties: extremely smooth, non-greasy skin feel; excellent carrier for other actives; leaves no noticeable residue. Regulatory status: unlike D4 and D5, cyclohexasiloxane (D6) has NOT been classified as a substance of very high concern (SVHC) in the EU — it does not meet PBT (persistent, bioaccumulative, toxic) criteria to the same degree. The EU's 2020 restriction on cyclic silicones in rinse-off cosmetics targeted D4 and D5 (<0.1%), not D6. However, regulatory scrutiny of cyclic silicones as a class continues; periodic review updates may include D6. Not restricted in India (FSSAI), USA (FDA). Poorly absorbed through intact skin at cosmetic concentrations. No sensitisation risk. Environmental persistence is lower than D4/D5 but some bioaccumulation potential exists.""",
    'amodimethicone': """Amodimethicone is an amino-functional silicone — a modified polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) polymer where some methyl groups on the silicone backbone have been replaced with aminopropyl groups (-CH₂-CH₂-CH₂-NH₂ or similar amine-terminated side chains). These amine groups carry a positive charge in acidic to neutral pH conditions (i.e., at the pH of hair and skin). This is the key property that makes amodimethicone uniquely effective compared to standard dimethicone: the positive charge attracts it to the negatively charged surface of damaged or chemically processed hair (hair damage exposes more negative sites), so amodimethicone deposits preferentially on areas that need conditioning most, rather than coating uniformly and causing build-up on healthy hair. Used in: hair conditioners (rinse-off and leave-on), hair masks, heat protectants, smoothing serums, split-end treatments and shampoos. Benefits: reduces combing friction (slip), reduces frizz, repairs damaged cuticle appearance, adds shine, protects against heat damage, improves manageability. Substantivity: unlike dimethicone which can build up on hair with repeated use, amodimethicone's selective deposition means less build-up tendency on undamaged hair. Skin safety: well tolerated on scalp and skin; not a known sensitiser. Some users with fine or oily hair find even functional silicones contribute to product build-up over time — a clarifying shampoo resolves this. Not absorbed through skin in meaningful amounts. Approved in cosmetics globally; no known regulatory restrictions. Vegan; not animal-derived.""",
    # --- Retinoids ---
    'retinol': 'The alcohol form of Vitamin A (a retinoid), used in anti-ageing skincare since the 1980s to increase cell turnover, stimulate collagen production and improve fine lines, wrinkles and uneven skin tone.',
    'retinyl palmitate': """Retinyl palmitate is an ester of retinol (Vitamin A alcohol) and palmitic acid (a 16-carbon saturated fatty acid) — formed by combining these two components. It is the most common vitamin A ester used in cosmetics and nutritional supplements. In the body and on the skin, enzymes hydrolyse retinyl palmitate to free retinol, which is then oxidised to retinaldehyde and further to retinoic acid (the biologically active form). The ester form is more stable and less irritating than retinol itself, making it suitable for sensitive skin formulations, daytime products and products for beginners to retinoid-based skincare. Used in: moisturisers and anti-ageing creams (gentlest retinoid, slower acting than retinol), sunscreens (often added as an antioxidant — this use has become controversial), lip balms, eye creams, baby care products and nutritional supplements (Vitamin A fortification). Anti-ageing efficacy: retinyl palmitate does provide retinoid-like skin benefits (stimulate cell turnover, improve fine lines, even skin tone) but is significantly less potent than retinol or tretinoin and shows effects only with prolonged use. Controversy: a 2012 study by the US National Toxicology Program (NTP) found that mice exposed to retinyl palmitate in a cream applied to skin and then exposed to UV light developed more skin tumours — raising concern that retinyl palmitate in sunscreens could be photocarcinogenic. This has not been confirmed in human studies and FDA/EU have not restricted its use in sunscreens. The Environmental Working Group (EWG) has cautioned against retinyl palmitate in sunscreens for this reason; this remains scientifically debated. Pregnancy: all vitamin A derivatives (retinoids) at high systemic doses are teratogenic. Topical retinyl palmitate at normal cosmetic concentrations absorbs minimally and has not been shown to cause teratogenic effects in humans, but pregnant women are conventionally advised to avoid retinoid-containing cosmetics as a precaution — especially avoid oral high-dose vitamin A supplements in first trimester. FSSAI permits retinyl palmitate as a Vitamin A source in fortified foods. EU Cosmetics Regulation requires a maximum concentration and pregnancy warning for retinol and its esters in leave-on products.""",
    'tretinoin': 'The acid form of Vitamin A (all-trans retinoic acid), a prescription-only topical retinoid used to treat acne, photodamage and wrinkles. Regarded as the gold standard topical anti-ageing agent.',
    'hydroxypinacolone retinoate': 'A newer ester of retinoic acid used in over-the-counter cosmetics as a gentler alternative to retinol, designed to deliver anti-ageing benefits with reportedly less irritation and dryness.',
    'retinal': 'Retinaldehyde, an intermediate form of Vitamin A between retinol and retinoic acid, used in high-performance anti-ageing skincare. Converts to retinoic acid on the skin more readily than retinol.',
    # --- Silicones ---
    'dimethiconol': 'A high-molecular-weight silicone polymer used in hair conditioners and styling products to add shine, smooth the cuticle and improve detangling. Unlike cyclic silicones, it is non-volatile and remains on the hair.',
    'dimethicone': 'The most widely used silicone in cosmetics, a linear polydimethylsiloxane polymer. Creates a smooth, silky feel on skin and hair, acts as a skin protectant and is used in everything from moisturisers to wound dressings.',
    # --- CI cosmetic colorants (worth knowing) ---
    'ci 26100': """CI 26100 (Solvent Red 23 / D&C Red No. 17 / Sudan III) is a synthetic azo dye used exclusively in cosmetics — it is NOT approved for food use. Chemically it is an oil-soluble azo dye with a characteristic deep reddish-brown tone. Primarily used in: lipsticks (to create warm red to orange shades), nail lacquers, blushes, lip glosses, cream and powder eyeshadows, mascaras and hair colourants. Oil-solubility makes it effective in oily and waxy cosmetic matrices (lipstick bases, balms, crayons). Regulatory status: permitted in cosmetics in EU (Annex IV of Cosmetics Regulation), USA (as D&C Red No. 17, with restrictions — not permitted in area of the eye or on lips in some applications — check FDA labelling), India (CDSCO cosmetics list) and most other markets. NOT approved for food use. Safety: azo dyes can theoretically be reduced by azo reductases in the gut or on the skin to aromatic amines, some of which may have mutagenic properties; however, at cosmetic application levels where skin absorption is limited, regulatory authorities consider CI 26100 safe for cosmetic use. Patch testing is advisable for individuals with known sensitivity to azo dyes, sulfonamides or aspirin. Some case reports of contact allergy. The EU requires cosmetic products to list this as CI 26100 on ingredient labels — useful for identification. As a lipstick dye, a small fraction may be ingested via lip contact; safety data cover this route of exposure. No known mutagenic or carcinogenic concern at current cosmetic use levels. Fragrance-free formulations sometimes include this as a pure colourant with minimal sensitisation potential beyond the dye itself.""",
    'ci 61565': """CI 61565 (D&C Green No. 6 / Solvent Green 3 / Vat Green 1) is a synthetic anthraquinone dye — it belongs to the anthraquinone family rather than the azo dye family, meaning it lacks the -N=N- chromophore and does not release the aromatic amines that are a concern with azo dyes. Produces green to blueish-green shades. Used exclusively in cosmetics — not approved for food. Found in: lipsticks (creates green to teal shades and, when blended with red or yellow dyes, various mixed tones), eye cosmetics (eyeshadow, eyeliner, mascara), hair dyes, soaps and skin care products with green colouring. Regulatory status: approved in USA as D&C Green No. 6 for external use (drugs and cosmetics) including some eye area applications; permitted in EU under Annex IV (CI 61565); permitted in India for cosmetics. The anthraquinone family generally has lower skin sensitisation potential than azo dyes — the chemical structure does not generate aromatic amine metabolites. Patch test reactions have been reported in some individuals. Limited systemic absorption from normal topical cosmetic use. No evidence of carcinogenicity or mutagenicity at cosmetic use concentrations. When listed on a cosmetic label as CI 61565 it can be identified as this particular green shade. Natural alternatives (chlorophyllin copper complex, spirulina extract) are used in some 'natural' cosmetic lines instead. Generally considered safe for cosmetic use by EU and USA regulatory bodies.""",
    'ci 17200': """CI 17200 (D&C Red No. 33 / Acid Red 33 / Naphthalene Red) is a synthetic monoazo dye producing deep red to maroon colours. Used in both cosmetics and some pharmaceutical applications (though not food-grade). Found in: lipsticks, lip glosses, blushes, hair dyes, nail polishes, eyeshadows, some temporary hair colour products, and certain pharmaceutical tablet coatings and oral medications (where coloured tablets aid identification). In hair dyes: CI 17200 is used in semi-permanent and direct dye formulations as a red colour base. Regulatory status: approved in EU as Annex IV entry (CI 17200); approved in USA as D&C Red No. 33 for external use including drugs (not for food); permitted in India in cosmetics and some pharmaceuticals. Restrictions in some countries — not universally permitted. Azo dye concerns: like other azo dyes, CI 17200 can theoretically be reduced to aromatic amines by intestinal or skin bacteria; at cosmetic application levels on intact skin, regulatory authorities consider this risk low. However, some aromatic amines from azo reduction may be of concern particularly for hair dye use (long-duration scalp contact, potential for systemic absorption through scalp). Patch testing is recommended before hair dye use — it is estimated that 5–10% of individuals who regularly use oxidative hair dye develop contact dermatitis. Cross-reactivity with other azo dyes and para-phenylenediamine (PPD) is possible. For lip use, some ingestion occurs — safety data include this route. EU mandates listing on cosmetic products as CI 17200, enabling consumer identification.""",
    'ci 15510': """CI 15510 (Acid Orange 7 / Orange II / D&C Orange No. 4 / Sodium Orange / 4-[(2-hydroxynaphthyl)azo]benzenesulfonic acid sodium salt) is a synthetic monoazo acid dye producing bright orange to orange-yellow colours. It is an anionic water-soluble dye. Historical significance: one of the earliest synthetic azo dyes, first synthesised in 1876; it has been studied extensively for over 140 years. Used in: cosmetics (lipsticks, blushes, lip glosses, hair dyes, some colour cosmetics — listed as CI 15510), hair colouring products (semi-permanent and direct dyes), pharmaceutical oral medications and topical products (tablet coatings, eye drops — some restricted applications), and historically in textiles and industrial dyeing. In cosmetics: approved in EU under Annex IV (CI 15510) for general cosmetic use; permitted in USA as D&C Orange No. 4 for external use (including approved oral and ophthalmological drug use under FDA 21 CFR 74.1254); permitted in India. Azo dye mechanism of concern: CI 15510 can be bacterially reduced to sulphanilic acid and beta-naphthylamine — beta-naphthylamine is a known human carcinogen (IARC Group 1) and bladder carcinogen. At cosmetic exposure levels via skin application, the systemic dose of any released aromatic amine is considered negligible by regulatory bodies. However, for prolonged scalp application in hair dyes, the systemic absorption potential and therefore the cumulative aromatic amine exposure is higher — a higher concern in this application compared to rinse-off cosmetics. EU Directive 2003/15/EC: all azo dyes must not release the prohibited amines listed in Annex II above permitted concentrations (1 mg/kg or 5 mg/kg depending on product type). Manufacturers must test CI 15510-containing products for amine release. Contact allergy has been documented in patch test studies. People with known azo dye sensitivity or aspirin sensitivity should perform patch testing. Not permitted as a food colour.""",
    'ci 45410': 'CI 45410 (Acid Red 92 / D&C Red No. 27) is a synthetic fluorescein dye used to add red or pink colour to lip products and other cosmetics.',
    # --- Titanium Dioxide ---
    'titanium dioxide': 'A naturally occurring white mineral (E171) mined from ilmenite or rutile ore. Used as a bright white pigment in paints, sunscreens, toothpastes, tablets and food products since the early 20th century.',
    # --- Chelating agents ---
    'tetrasodium edta': """Tetrasodium EDTA (tetrasodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate / tetrasodium salt of EDTA) is the fully neutralised, strongly alkaline sodium salt of EDTA — a synthetic aminopolycarboxylate chelating agent. Functions identically to other EDTA salts as a sequestrant: it forms stable, water-soluble complexes with divalent and trivalent metal cations (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺/³⁺, Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺) by coordinating through its four carboxylate groups and two amine nitrogens. In cosmetics: the primary use is chelating calcium and magnesium from hard water in shampoos, conditioners, body washes and liquid soap formulations — preventing metal ion precipitation with surfactants that would reduce lathering and increase soap scum, and preventing metal-catalysed oxidation of product ingredients. Also used as a preservative enhancer by chelating calcium from bacterial and fungal cell membranes, increasing permeability to other antimicrobial preservatives. Unlike calcium disodium EDTA (E385 in food), tetrasodium EDTA is not the food-grade additive — it is primarily a cosmetic and personal care ingredient. In cosmetics: considered safe at standard use concentrations (EU Cosmetics Regulation allows it; FDA considers it safe for cosmetic use); minimal skin penetration; not a sensitiser or irritant at permitted concentrations. Environmental concern: EDTA is very persistent in wastewater treatment (resists biodegradation) and can mobilise heavy metals from sediments when discharged into waterways — an environmental concern prompting the cosmetics industry to seek biodegradable alternatives such as tetrasodium etidronate, tetrasodium glutamate diacetate (GLDA) and MGDA. Not the same compound as tetrasodium etidronate.""",
    'disodium edta': """Disodium EDTA (disodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate / Na₂EDTA / disodium salt of EDTA) is a partially neutralised, water-soluble chelating agent — the disodium salt of ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid, with two of the four carboxylate groups neutralised with sodium. This leaves two carboxylate groups as acidic (pKa ~2.7 and 6.2), giving aqueous solutions a slightly acidic to neutral pH. Functions: sequestrant — forms stable, soluble complexes with divalent and trivalent metal ions (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺/³⁺, Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Cd²⁺); used to chelate metal ions in cosmetics, food, pharmaceuticals and industrial applications. In cosmetics and personal care (most common use): prevents metal-catalysed oxidation of fragrance compounds, actives and coloured ingredients (extends shelf life); improves surfactant performance in hard water (similar to tetrasodium EDTA); used in shampoos, conditioners, body washes, facial cleansers, moisturisers, hair dyes and sunscreens. In food: calcium disodium EDTA (E385) is the approved food-grade chelating preservative, not disodium EDTA — however, disodium EDTA is used in some food formulations and pharmaceutical preparations where its acidity profile is useful. In pharmaceuticals: used in ophthalmic preparations (chelates calcium from bacterial cell membranes, acting as a preservative synergist), IV chelation therapy for heavy metal poisoning, and as an anticoagulant in laboratory blood collection tubes. Safety: similar to calcium disodium EDTA — poorly absorbed (<5%); excreted unchanged in urine; can chelate essential minerals at high doses but not at cosmetic or food use concentrations. Environmental persistence: like all EDTA forms, biodegrades very slowly in the environment (see tetrasodium EDTA entry). Approved in EU and FDA for cosmetic use at standard concentrations.""",
    'tetrasodium etidronate': """Tetrasodium etidronate (tetrasodium 1-hydroxyethane-1,1-diphosphonate / HEDP tetrasodium salt) is a chelating agent — the fully neutralised, water-soluble tetrasodium salt of etidronic acid (also known as HEDP or hydroxyethylidene diphosphonic acid). It is a bisphosphonate chelator that binds strongly to divalent and trivalent metal ions (calcium, magnesium, iron, copper, manganese) through its two phosphonate groups and a hydroxyl group. Used as an alternative to EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) in cosmetic formulations where EDTA may be undesirable — particularly in rinse-off products (shampoos, conditioners, body washes, toothpastes) and oral care products. Functions: (1) Sequestrant — binds hard water calcium and magnesium ions that could precipitate with soap/surfactant anions, improving lathering and rinsing in hard water. (2) Stabiliser — prevents metal ion-catalysed oxidation of fragrance and active ingredients, extending product shelf life. (3) Preservative booster — chelating calcium ions from bacterial cell membranes increases permeability and potentiates antimicrobial preservatives. Why preferred over EDTA? Etidronate biodegrades more readily than EDTA in wastewater treatment and in the environment — EDTA is a persistent chelator that mobilises heavy metals in rivers and soil; tetrasodium etidronate is considered a greener alternative. Skin safety: not a sensitiser; not irritating at cosmetic use concentrations. Minimal skin penetration. Not carcinogenic. Bisphosphonate drug class (given orally or IV in osteoporosis treatment) at pharmacological doses affects bone metabolism — but cosmetic/topical tetrasodium etidronate concentrations are far below any systemic threshold. Approved in EU cosmetics Annex (with restrictions in oral hygiene to limited concentrations), FDA-GRAS in appropriate uses, FSSAI-compatible.""",
    # --- Mild acids ---
    'citric acid': """Citric acid (2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid / E330/INS 330) is a naturally occurring tricarboxylic acid found abundantly in citrus fruits (lemon juice ~48 g/L, lime juice ~50 g/L, orange juice ~12 g/L) and is an intermediate in the Krebs (tricarboxylic acid) cycle in every aerobic organism on Earth. Commercially produced almost entirely by submerged fermentation of molasses or glucose-fructose syrups using Aspergillus niger moulds — a highly efficient process developed in the early 20th century. The world's most widely used food acidulant, with global production exceeding 2 million tonnes per year. Chemical properties: pKa values 3.13, 4.76, 6.40 (triprotic acid); mildly sour, clean taste without harshness; water-soluble; weakly chelates metal ions (Ca²⁺, Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺). Food functions: (1) acidulant/pH regulator — controls acidity and tartness in carbonated beverages, fruit drinks, squashes, jams, jellies, confectionery, canned vegetables and dairy desserts; (2) preservative — reduces water activity and creates unfavourable pH for microbial growth, extending shelf life; (3) antioxidant synergist — chelates pro-oxidant metal ions that catalyse rancidity, potentiating the effect of BHA, BHT and tocopherols; (4) flavour enhancer — enhances fruit and berry flavour profiles by interacting with taste receptors; (5) gel formation — activates pectin in jams at low pH. Cosmetic function: pH adjuster (the most common cosmetic pH buffer), chelating agent, mild exfoliant (alpha-hydroxy acid at high concentrations). Safety: metabolised via the Krebs cycle to CO₂ and water. No accumulation; no toxicity at food use levels. Dental erosion risk: prolonged exposure of tooth enamel to acidic foods/drinks (including citric acid-containing products) can cause dental erosion — recommended to consume acidic beverages with meals rather than sipping continuously. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E330), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    # --- Colorants ---
    'beta carotene': """Beta-carotene (β-carotene / E160a/INS 160a / provitamin A) is a tetraterpene carotenoid pigment responsible for the orange colour of carrots, pumpkin, sweet potato, mango and many other fruits and vegetables. Chemically it is a C₄₀ polyene — a long conjugated chain of alternating single and double bonds with cyclohexene rings at each end (β-ionone rings) — its chromophore (11 conjugated double bonds) absorbs visible light in the blue-green region (~450–480 nm), resulting in the vivid orange colour. Produced commercially: (1) natural extract — from carrots, red palm oil, Dunaliella salina algae (a major commercial microalgal source); (2) synthetic — chemical synthesis using isoprenoid building blocks; (3) fermentation — Blakeslea trispora fungal fermentation. Applied as a yellow-to-orange food colourant in: margarine and butter (colour standardisation), cheese, juices, soft drinks, baked goods, confectionery, breakfast cereals, soups and plant-based dairy alternatives. Nutritional function — provitamin A activity: beta-carotene is enzymatically cleaved by beta-carotene-15,15'-dioxygenase (BCO1) in the intestinal mucosa and liver to form two molecules of retinal (vitamin A aldehyde), subsequently reduced to retinol (Vitamin A). Vitamin A is essential for: visual pigment formation (retinal in rhodopsin — night vision), immune function, epithelial cell differentiation and reproduction. Beta-carotene from food is converted on demand — avoiding the risk of vitamin A toxicity that occurs with preformed retinol supplementation. Antioxidant: quenches singlet oxygen and peroxy radicals — part of the body's antioxidant network. Critical safety note: beta-carotene SUPPLEMENTATION in high doses (20–30 mg/day from supplements) has been shown to INCREASE lung cancer risk in current smokers and asbestos workers (ATBC trial, 1994; CARET trial, 1996). The mechanism is unclear but may involve pro-oxidant effects of high-dose beta-carotene in an oxidative stress environment. Food-level intakes and standard food-additive colouring doses do not reach supplemental dose levels and do not carry this risk. No concerns from food use. ADI: not specified as colour (JECFA — used at food use concentrations); EFSA has evaluated safety of synthetic beta-carotene. FDA (GRAS), EU (E160a), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'caramel': """Caramel (E150/INS 150) is a natural brown colour and flavour produced by the controlled thermal decomposition (caramelisation) of food-grade sugars — sucrose, glucose, invert sugar or their mixtures — at temperatures of 120–220°C, with or without catalysts (acids, alkalis, or ammonium and sulfite compounds). The process breaks the glycosidic bonds, dehydrates sugars and polymerises the fragments into a complex mixture of hundreds of coloured compounds (furans, pyruvaldehyde, maltol, hydroxymethylfurfural) and melanoidins responsible for the characteristic brown colour and caramel aroma. Four classes with different production methods and applications: Class I (E150a — plain caramel, no additives); Class II (E150b — caustic sulphite process, with SO₂); Class III (E150c — ammonia caramel, darker colour, common in soy sauce and dark beers); Class IV (E150d — sulphite-ammonia caramel, the most widely used — in cola drinks, soy sauce, instant noodles). The most significant safety concern is with Class IV: the reaction of sugars with ammonium compounds produces 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI), which IARC classifies as Group 2B (possibly carcinogenic to humans, based on rodent data). California's Prop 65 requires warning labels for 4-MEI above 29 mcg/day. EFSA and FDA have reviewed the evidence and consider Class IV safe at current permitted food use levels, but exposure should be minimised. ADI varies by class. FSSAI, EU (E150) and CODEX permit caramel colours; specific limits depend on the class and food category.""",
    # --- Thickeners ---
    'guar gum': """Guar gum (E412/INS 412 / guaran) is a natural galactomannan polysaccharide derived from the endosperm of the guar bean (Cyamopsis tetragonoloba), an annual legume cultivated almost entirely in the semi-arid regions of India (Rajasthan, Gujarat, Haryana) and Pakistan — India produces approximately 80% of global supply, making this a strategically important Indian crop. The guar seed is split and the endosperm (whitish germ) is separated from the hull and germ, then ground to produce guar gum powder. Chemical structure: linear chain of β-1,4-linked mannose units with α-1,6-linked galactose side chains (mannose:galactose ratio approximately 2:1). The galactose side chains prevent the mannan backbone from aligning and forming crystalline structures, enabling full water solubility and hydration. One of the highest-viscosity natural gums per unit weight — 0.5% solution has viscosity exceeding most other gums at equivalent concentration. Functions in food: (1) thickener and stabiliser — in ice cream (controls ice crystal size, body and meltdown), dairy products, salad dressings and sauces; (2) binder and texture agent in gluten-free bread (partially compensates for the absence of gluten by providing network structure); (3) water-holding agent in processed meats; (4) cloud stabiliser in fruit beverages. Pharmaceutical: used as a tablet disintegrant and controlled-release matrix former. Dietary fibre: guar gum is a viscous soluble dietary fibre — when consumed it forms a thick gel in the stomach, slowing gastric emptying and glucose absorption, which is useful for managing postprandial glucose spikes. Clinical doses of guar gum (10–30 g/day) have been shown to reduce LDL cholesterol and improve glycaemic control. FDA allows a health claim for soluble fibre and cardiovascular disease risk reduction. Not digestible — fermented by colonic bacteria to short-chain fatty acids. Allergen: guar gum is derived from legumes — rare but confirmed cases of IgE-mediated allergy (including occupational asthma in food manufacturing workers and anaphylaxis in sensitive individuals) have been documented. Guar gum is listed as a potential allergen in some jurisdictions. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E412), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'xanthan gum': """Xanthan gum (E415/INS 415) is a high-molecular-weight exopolysaccharide produced by aerobic fermentation of glucose, sucrose or other carbohydrates by the gram-negative bacterium Xanthomonas campestris. Discovered by Allene Rosalind Jeanes at the USDA in the 1950s and commercialised by Kelco Company. Chemical structure: primary backbone of β-1,4-linked glucose (cellulose-like), with trisaccharide side chains (mannose-glucuronic acid-mannose) every two glucose units; about 50% of the mannose units carry pyruvate groups, and many carry acetate groups. The side chains prevent intermolecular aggregation, enabling complete water solubility and very high solution viscosity. Xanthan solutions are highly pseudoplastic (shear-thinning) — at rest the solution is thick (high viscosity) but under shear (pumping, stirring, squeezing from a bottle) it becomes fluid; when shear stops, viscosity is rapidly restored. This property is uniquely valuable for food applications. Functions: (1) thickener — salad dressings (provides stable, pourable-yet-cling texture), sauces, soups, gravies; (2) stabiliser — prevents phase separation and sedimentation in emulsions and suspensions (fruit juices, beverage syrups); (3) gluten-free bread — one of the most effective gelling/binding agents for gluten-free formulations, providing the dough network structure and gas retention otherwise provided by gluten; (4) freeze-thaw stabiliser — withstands freezing and thawing without texture changes; (5) acid and salt stable — functions effectively across pH 1–13 and in high salt environments (unlike many other hydrocolloids). Used in: salad dressings, sauces, gravies, soups, beverages, ice cream, gluten-free bread, dairy products and cosmetics (gel texturisers, cleansers). Cosmetic use: see cosmetics category entry. Allergen note: produced from fermentation on a carbohydrate substrate — the fermentation substrate may include gluten-containing grains (wheat, corn or soy); the final xanthan gum product is purified, but extremely sensitive coeliac individuals occasionally report reactions. Most producers can provide certificate of gluten testing (<20 ppm). No significant toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E415), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    # --- Humectants ---
    'propylene glycol': 'A synthetic, odourless viscous liquid derived from petroleum or bio-based sources. Used as a humectant, solvent and stabiliser in food, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and as an aircraft de-icing fluid.',
    'glycerin': """Glycerin (glycerol / propane-1,2,3-triol / E422/INS 422) is a three-carbon polyol with three hydroxyl groups and is one of the most biologically fundamental small molecules — the structural backbone of all triglycerides (dietary fats), phospholipids and glycolipids. A colourless, odourless, viscous, hygroscopic liquid with a distinctly sweet taste (~60% sweetness of sucrose). Produced commercially as a by-product of soap making (fat saponification), biodiesel production (transesterification of vegetable oils), or by fermentation. In food: functions as a humectant (absorbs and retains moisture to keep baked goods, confectionery and dried fruits soft); solvent (dissolves colours, flavours and extracts); plasticiser in confectionery coatings; low-GI sweetener with minimal glycaemic impact (4.3 kcal/g but absorbed slowly and converted to glucose via gluconeogenesis, not directly digested to glucose). In cosmetics and skincare: one of the most effective humectants available — draws water from the dermis and ambient air into the stratum corneum, improving skin hydration; works synergistically with occlusives (oils, waxes) that prevent moisture loss. Found in virtually every moisturiser, serum, cleanser and hand cream. In pharmaceuticals: used as a solvent for medications, in cough syrups, ear drops, and as a suppository excipient. Generally very safe. Very high oral doses (>1 g/kg body weight) can have a mild laxative or osmotic effect but this is not relevant at food-use concentrations. At cosmetic-use concentrations it is non-comedogenic and safe for all skin types. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E422), FSSAI and CODEX approved. Vegan when derived from plant oils.""",
    'sorbitol': 'A naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in apples, pears and plums, also produced commercially from glucose. Used as a low-calorie sweetener, humectant and laxative in foods, cosmetics and pharmaceutical preparations.',
    # --- Caffeine ---
    'caffeine': """Caffeine (1,3,7-trimethylxanthine) is a purine alkaloid and central nervous system stimulant found naturally in: coffee beans (~1–2% by weight), tea leaves (~2–4%), cacao seeds/dark chocolate (~0.07%), guarana berries (~3.5%), yerba mate and kola nuts. Also produced synthetically (used in energy drinks, pharmaceuticals and as a cosmetic active). The world's most widely consumed psychoactive compound — an estimated 85% of the global adult population consumes caffeine daily. Mechanism: caffeine is a non-selective adenosine receptor antagonist — it blocks adenosine A1 and A2A receptors in the brain. Adenosine normally promotes drowsiness by inhibiting neural activity; caffeine blocks this, increasing alertness, reducing fatigue perception and improving concentration. Secondary effects: increases dopamine and norepinephrine release (mood, motivation), stimulates cardiac output, increases metabolic rate and raises blood pressure transiently. Physiological effects and doses: 40–200 mg: improved alertness, concentration, reduced fatigue; 200–400 mg: peak cognitive effects; >400 mg/day: anxiety, tremors, insomnia, tachycardia in sensitive individuals; >600 mg/day: associated with adverse effects. Safe daily intake for most adults: 400 mg/day (EFSA, FDA, Health Canada); pregnant women: <200 mg/day (WHO, EFSA). In food: used in energy drinks (80 mg/250 mL can), cola drinks (~30–40 mg/355 mL), tea (~40–80 mg/cup) and coffee (~80–100 mg/espresso). FSSAI requires caffeine to be declared on energy drink labels. Dependence: physical caffeine dependence (withdrawal headaches, fatigue, irritability) develops readily with regular use but is clinically mild. Interaction with medications: CYP1A2 inhibitors (fluvoxamine, some antibiotics) can markedly increase caffeine blood levels; adenosine-based cardiac medications may be affected. Children and adolescents should limit caffeine intake. Not recommended for infants.""",
    # --- Alcohol ---
    'alcohol': """Alcohol in food, cosmetic and pharmaceutical contexts refers to ethyl alcohol (ethanol / ethyl alcohol / C₂H₅OH / CAS 64-17-5) — a two-carbon primary alcohol produced by the anaerobic fermentation of sugars by Saccharomyces cerevisiae and other yeast species. Distinct from other alcohols: methanol (wood alcohol — toxic), isopropanol (rubbing alcohol — toxic for consumption), propylene glycol and glycerol (all used in food/cosmetics). In food: ethanol is the intoxicating compound in alcoholic beverages; it is also used as a solvent for vanilla extract, food flavourings and food-grade preservative at concentrations above 20% ABV. In cosmetics and personal care: alcohol functions as a solvent (dissolves many hydrophilic and lipophilic active ingredients), antiseptic (denatures microbial proteins — effective against bacteria, viruses and fungi at 60–80% concentrations), astringent (temporarily reduces oiliness by dehydrating stratum corneum), quick-drying carrier (evaporates rapidly from skin surface in toners, setting sprays, hand gels), and penetration enhancer (transiently increases skin permeability). Common in: toners, hand sanitisers, aftershaves, perfumes, antiperspirants, setting sprays. At high concentrations in leave-on products, ethanol can damage the skin barrier over time — disrupting lipid bilayers, reducing ceramide content and increasing transepidermal water loss. Islamic dietary law (halal): ethanol from non-khamr fermentation in extremely small amounts in food flavourings is accepted by most Islamic scholars; alcohol as a beverage (khamr) is forbidden. FSSAI regulates alcoholic beverage standards; cosmetic use of alcohol is permitted under BIS cosmetic standards.""",
    'ethanol': """Ethanol (ethyl alcohol / C₂H₅OH / grain alcohol) is a short-chain primary alcohol produced by yeast fermentation of carbohydrates (glucose, sucrose, lactose, starch) via the glycolytic pathway — the same process responsible for all alcoholic beverages. Also produced synthetically by ethylene hydration (petroleum-derived ethylene + steam over phosphoric acid catalyst). In food and beverages: the psychoactive component of beer, wine, spirits and fermented foods; also used as a solvent carrier in natural food flavour extracts (vanilla extract, almond extract); present as a trace fermentation by-product in bread, yoghurt and other fermented foods; used as a food-grade preservative. In cosmetics and personal care: ethanol and its denatured forms (alcohol denat., SD alcohol) are among the most widely used cosmetic ingredients — function as solvents, antiseptics, quick-drying carriers and astringents; used in perfumes, toners, hand sanitisers, hair sprays, antiperspirants and aftershaves. Skin effects at high concentrations: ethanol can impair the skin barrier by dissolving surface lipids, but at typical cosmetic concentrations (5–20% in leave-on products) the effect is generally minimal for most skin types; people with dry, sensitive or eczema-prone skin may experience more dryness. Metabolism: ethanol is metabolised primarily by hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH) — producing acetaldehyde (a toxic intermediate, IARC Group 1 carcinogen) then acetic acid. Chronic excessive ethanol consumption causes liver damage, liver cirrhosis, various cancers (oropharyngeal, oesophageal, hepatic, colorectal, breast), cardiovascular disease and neurological damage. IARC classifies ethanol in alcoholic beverages as Group 1 carcinogen. No safe level of alcoholic beverage consumption for cancer risk. FSSAI regulates ethanol in beverages and cosmetics separately.""",
    # --- Salts ---
    'salt': """Salt (sodium chloride / NaCl / common salt / table salt) is the most ancient and widely used food seasoning and preservative in human history, found in seawater (~35 g/L) and mined as rock salt (halite) from geological deposits worldwide. Chemically it is an ionic compound: Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions packed in a face-centred cubic crystal lattice. Essential physiology: sodium regulates extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure; chloride is the primary anion in blood plasma; the sodium-potassium ATPase pump (the most energy-consuming process in most animal cells) depends on Na⁺ gradients for nerve impulse transmission, muscle contraction, nutrient absorption (glucose and amino acids are co-transported with Na⁺ in the small intestine) and kidney filtration. Functions in food: seasoning (enhances flavour perception at <0.5%, above which perception is salty); preservative (high salt lowers water activity, inhibiting microbial growth in cured meats, cheese, pickles, salted fish); fermentation regulator (in cheese, bread, sauerkraut, kimchi — salt controls which bacteria dominate); texture agent in bread (strengthens gluten network); and important in emulsification. Health concern: excess sodium intake is strongly and consistently associated with hypertension (high blood pressure), stroke and cardiovascular mortality. WHO recommends <5 g salt per day (<2 g sodium); global average intake is 9–12 g/day. The National Institute of Nutrition (India) recommends <5 g/day. Iodized salt is the vehicle for iodine fortification in India — critical for preventing iodine deficiency disorders (goitre, cretinism). Those with hypertension, heart disease or kidney disease should follow medical advice on sodium restriction. FSSAI specifies standards for iodised, free-flowing and mineral salt types.""",
    'sodium chloride': """Sodium chloride (NaCl / E common salt) is the chemical name for what is universally known as table salt — an ionic compound consisting of positively charged sodium ions (Na⁺) and negatively charged chloride ions (Cl⁻) in a 1:1 ratio, forming a characteristic cubic crystal lattice with a melting point of 801°C. Occurs naturally in seawater (~3.5% by weight), underground deposits (rock salt/halite), and many biological fluids. Produced commercially by solar evaporation of seawater, solution mining, or underground mining of rock salt. Nutritional role: both sodium and chloride are essential macrominerals. Sodium is the primary cation in extracellular fluid, regulating blood volume, pressure and osmolality; chloride is the primary extracellular anion, essential for gastric acid (HCl) secretion. The sodium-potassium ATPase pump uses Na⁺ gradients to drive cellular processes including nerve signal propagation, muscle contraction and active nutrient absorption. Food functions: flavour enhancement, preservation (antimicrobial via water activity reduction), texture modification, fermentation control and protein denaturation. Health consideration: WHO recommends limiting sodium intake to <2,000 mg/day (equivalent to <5 g salt). Excess dietary sodium is the leading modifiable risk factor for hypertension and stroke globally. FSSAI mandates iodisation of salt sold for direct consumption to address widespread iodine deficiency in India. Used in food, cosmetics (toner, exfoliant), pharmaceuticals (IV normal saline), dentistry and industry.""",
    # --- Acids (milder) ---
    'lactic acid': """Lactic acid (E270/INS 270 / 2-hydroxypropanoic acid / L-lactic acid / D-lactic acid) is a naturally occurring organic acid produced by: (1) anaerobic glycolysis in human and animal muscles during exercise (the burn sensation); (2) fermentation of carbohydrates by lactic acid bacteria (LAB — Lactobacillus, Streptococcus, Leuconostoc) — the acidifying agent responsible for the characteristic sourness of yoghurt, cheese, kefir, sauerkraut, kimchi, sourdough bread, pickled vegetables, wine, beer and cured meats. Commercially produced almost entirely by bacterial fermentation of carbohydrate feedstocks (glucose, lactose, sucrose, starch hydrolysates). Both L-lactic acid (natural biological form) and DL-racemic mixture are used commercially in food. Chemical nature: a three-carbon alpha-hydroxy acid with a carboxylic acid and a hydroxyl group on the same carbon; mildly sour taste with lower acidity than citric acid per gram. Food functions: (1) acidulant/flavouring — provides gentle, smooth tartness in pickles, beverages, confectionery, dairy products and salad dressings; (2) preservative — inhibits growth of moulds and undesirable bacteria at low pH; (3) pH regulator — buffering in meat processing, brewing and fermented products; (4) texture agent — in processed meats (calcium lactate added for texture and preservation); (5) fermented food production — lactobacillus strains are the central producers in food fermentation. As a cosmetic/skincare active (alpha-hydroxy acid): accelerates skin cell turnover by breaking the corneodesmosomes holding dead skin cells to the surface; improves skin texture, fades hyperpigmentation and reduces fine lines. Gentler than glycolic acid (one carbon smaller AHA). Used in chemical peels, moisturisers, toners and exfoliants. Metabolism: rapidly absorbed and enters the Krebs cycle; L-lactic acid is metabolised completely; large amounts of D-lactic acid may accumulate in individuals with short bowel syndrome. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E270), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'malic acid': """Malic acid (E296/INS 296 / L-malic acid / 2-hydroxybutanedioic acid) is a dicarboxylic organic acid naturally present in high concentrations in apples (up to 1%), pears, cherries, grapes and many other fruits — it is the primary acid responsible for the tartness of green apples and unripe fruit. Commercial production is primarily by chemical synthesis (hydration of maleic anhydride, petroleum-derived) or by microbial fermentation; L-malic acid is the natural isomer. Functions in food: acidulant (adds clean, smooth tartness to beverages, confectionery and bakery products — often preferred over citric acid for its lingering flavour); pH adjuster; flavour enhancer (enhances fruit flavour notes); sequestrant (chelates metal ions). Used in: hard candy and gummy confectionery (malic acid coating gives sour candies their intense tartness), beverages (sports drinks, fruit drinks), baked goods, powdered drink mixes and some sauces. Also used in oral care products (reduces dental plaque adhesion) and skincare as a mild alpha hydroxy acid (AHA) exfoliant. Metabolised via the Krebs cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle) — a normal intermediate in cellular energy metabolism. Provides 2.4 kcal/g. Completely safe at food use levels; no genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E296), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. No significant allergen concerns.""",
    # --- Vitamins ---
    'ascorbic acid': """Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C / E300/INS 300 / L-ascorbic acid) is an essential water-soluble vitamin and powerful reducing agent found abundantly in citrus fruits, kiwi, guava (~200 mg/100g — one of the richest sources), amla (Indian gooseberry), bell peppers, strawberries and broccoli. Humans lack the enzyme L-gulonolactone oxidase needed to synthesise Vitamin C endogenously — we must obtain it entirely from diet. Biochemical roles: cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases (essential for collagen synthesis — collagen cannot form without Vitamin C; deficiency causes scurvy); cofactor for carnitine biosynthesis, neurotransmitter synthesis (norepinephrine), and tyrosine catabolism; reduces dietary non-haem iron (Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺) in the GI tract, dramatically improving iron absorption; regenerates alpha-tocopherol (Vitamin E) from its oxidised radical form in a synergistic antioxidant network. As a food additive (E300): prevents enzymatic browning in cut fruits and vegetables; protects against oxidative rancidity in fats and beverages; added to cured meats (as sodium ascorbate) to inhibit nitrosamine formation from nitrite; used as a flour improver in bread baking. As a cosmetic active: inhibits tyrosinase (the enzyme that produces melanin), reducing hyperpigmentation; stimulates collagen synthesis in the dermis; scavenges UV-generated free radicals. Deficiency symptoms: scurvy (bleeding gums, poor wound healing, fatigue). RDA: 65–90 mg/day (adults); ICMR recommendation for India: 40 mg/day. Toxicity: very high supplemental doses (>2 g/day) may cause osmotic diarrhoea and increase oxalate kidney stone risk in susceptible individuals. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA GRAS; FSSAI, EU (E300) and CODEX approved.""",
    'tocopherol': """Tocopherol (Vitamin E) is the collective term for a family of four related fat-soluble antioxidants — alpha-, beta-, gamma- and delta-tocopherol — found naturally in nuts, seeds, vegetable oils and leafy vegetables. Alpha-tocopherol (E307) has the highest biological Vitamin E activity; gamma-tocopherol (found in soybean and corn oils) is the most abundant form in the North American diet. Chemical nature: chromanol ring (a phenol hydroxyl group capable of donating hydrogen atoms to free radicals) with a phytyl side chain providing membrane anchoring. Antioxidant mechanism: the phenolic hydroxyl donates a hydrogen atom to lipid peroxy radicals in cell membranes and LDL particles, terminating the oxidative chain reaction; the tocopheryl radical formed is regenerated by Vitamin C. As a food additive (E306–E309): used to prevent oxidative rancidity in edible oils, fats, margarines, infant formula, baby foods and nut butters. Both natural mixed tocopherols (E306, extracted from vegetable oils) and individual synthetic forms (alpha-tocopherol, E307) are used. As a nutrient: RDA is 15 mg/day; deficiency (rare, seen in fat-malabsorption conditions) causes peripheral neuropathy and haemolytic anaemia. Very high supplemental doses (>400 IU/day) have shown increased all-cause mortality in some meta-analyses. As a cosmetic active: skin-conditioning, antioxidant protection, wound healing support. FSSAI, EU (E306–E309), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'vitamin': 'An essential organic micronutrient that the body requires in small amounts. Added to food products for fortification, to replace vitamins lost during processing or to supplement dietary intake.',
    'mineral': 'An inorganic element required by the body in small amounts for functions including bone formation, enzyme activity and fluid regulation. Added to foods for fortification purposes.',
    # --- Proteins ---
    'whey': """Whey is the liquid co-product obtained during cheese or casein manufacture when milk proteins are coagulated — either by rennet enzymes or acid — and the curd is separated. It constitutes roughly 80–90% of the total milk volume and contains approximately 6% total solids. Whey proteins make up ~20% of total milk protein and include: beta-lactoglobulin (~50% of whey protein, not present in human milk), alpha-lactalbumin (~25%), immunoglobulins (~10%), serum albumin, lactoferrin and other bioactive peptides. Dried or concentrated forms: whey protein concentrate (WPC, 35–80% protein), whey protein isolate (WPI, ≥90% protein — most lactose and fat removed), and hydrolysed whey protein (partially pre-digested). Whey proteins are "fast-digesting" — amino acids appear in the bloodstream within 60–90 minutes of ingestion — making them widely used in sports nutrition for post-exercise muscle protein synthesis. PDCAAS (Protein Digestibility Corrected Amino Acid Score) = 1.0 — the highest possible score; all essential amino acids are present in ideal proportions. Rich in leucine (~11%), a branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) that directly activates mTORC1, the primary anabolic signalling pathway in muscle. Applications: whey powder in baked goods (improves browning and texture), infant formula (whey:casein ratio adjusted to approximate human milk), protein supplements, protein bars, processed cheese, confectionery and hospital nutrition formulas. Lactose content varies: WPC retains significant lactose; WPI contains minimal lactose (<1%) and is generally tolerated by mildly lactose-intolerant individuals. Allergen — CRITICAL: whey is a milk-derived protein and is a major food allergen (one of top 14 in EU; top 8 in USA). FSSAI mandates mandatory allergen declaration. Cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA) in infants requires complete avoidance. Distinct from lactose intolerance — CMPA is an immune-mediated protein allergy.""",
    'casein': 'The dominant protein in cow\'s milk, making up about 80% of total milk protein. Used as a slow-digesting protein supplement and as a food ingredient in processed cheese, adhesives and pharmaceutical tablets.',
    'lactose': """Lactose (milk sugar / 4-O-β-D-galactopyranosyl-D-glucose) is the primary carbohydrate in mammalian milk — present at approximately 4.5–5% in cow's milk and 6.9% in human breast milk. It is a disaccharide consisting of galactose and glucose joined by a β-1,4-glycosidic bond, hydrolysed in the small intestine by the enzyme lactase (lactase-phlorizin hydrolase / LPH), expressed on the brush border of intestinal enterocytes. Lactase activity is highest in infancy (adapted to a milk-based diet) and genetically declines in the majority of the world's adults — a condition called lactase non-persistence or primary lactase deficiency (PLD): approximately 65–70% of the global adult population has reduced lactase activity, varying from ~25% in Northern Europe to ~90–100% in Southeast Asia and most of sub-Saharan Africa and ~65–70% in India. When lactose reaches the colon undigested (because lactase is absent), colonic bacteria ferment it, producing hydrogen gas, CO₂ and short-chain fatty acids — causing bloating, flatulence, abdominal cramping and diarrhoea (lactose intolerance symptoms). Severity varies: many lactase-non-persistent individuals can tolerate small amounts of lactose (especially yogurt, where bacteria partially pre-digest lactose, and aged cheeses with minimal lactose). Commercial uses: lactose crystallises easily into a stable white powder — widely used as an excipient (filler, binder and flow agent) in pharmaceutical tablets and capsules; as a mild sweetener (0.2× sweetness of sucrose); as a browning agent (Maillard reactions with proteins) in baked goods; in infant formula (to match human milk carbohydrate content); and as a carrier in spray-dried dairy flavours. Produced commercially from cheese whey permeate (the filtrate from whey protein concentration). Allergen note: lactose itself is not allergenic (it is a carbohydrate, not a protein) — lactose intolerance is an enzyme deficiency, not an allergy. Cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA) is a separate IgE-mediated condition. Lactose can contain trace milk proteins if not highly purified — individuals with severe CMPA should verify purity of pharmaceutical lactose. FSSAI and global food regulations permit lactose as a food ingredient and excipient.""",
    # --- Starches ---
    'starch': """Starch is the primary storage carbohydrate in plants — a polysaccharide composed of glucose units linked in two structural forms: amylose (linear, α-1,4 linkages, ~20–30% of most starches) and amylopectin (highly branched, α-1,4 and α-1,6 linkages, ~70–80%). Major commercial sources: maize (corn), wheat, potato, tapioca (cassava) and rice. Starch granules are insoluble in cold water; when heated with water they swell, absorb water and eventually rupture (gelatinisation, typically at 60–85°C depending on source), releasing starch chains and forming a viscous paste. On cooling, the starch paste sets to a gel or thickened consistency. Retrogradation: over time, amylose and amylopectin chains re-associate, causing gels to stiffen (syneresis — water expulsion); this is the cause of bread staling. Native starch from different sources has distinctly different properties: potato starch gives very high viscosity; waxy maize starch (no amylose) gives clear, cohesive pastes; wheat starch produces a firmer gel. Uses: thickener in soups, sauces, gravies, custards, pie fillings; coating agent (confectionery panning); binder in processed meats; base for glucose and dextrose production; substrate for modified starch production. High GI when cooked. Completely digestible to glucose — primary energy carbohydrate. A vital food component globally. Generally recognised as safe; no regulatory restrictions.""",
    'modified starch': """Modified starch (INS 1400–1442 range) is the broad category name for starches (from corn, wheat, potato, tapioca, rice or waxy varieties) that have been physically, enzymatically or chemically treated to alter functional properties beyond those of native starch. Native starch granules break down ('cook out'), become viscous when hot, then retrograde (re-crystallise) on cooling, causing syneresis (weeping of liquid). Modified starches overcome these limitations for specific food processing needs. Main modification types: (1) Cross-linking (E1411–E1414 — distarch phosphate, acetylated distarch phosphate) — introduction of chemical bridges between starch chains strengthens the granule structure, providing stability under heat, acid and mechanical shear; used in canned soups, pie fillings and baby foods; (2) Stabilisation (E1420–E1422 — acetylated starches) — introduction of acetyl or hydroxypropyl groups prevents retrogradation and maintains smooth texture after freeze-thaw cycles; used in frozen foods, salad dressings; (3) Oxidised starch (E1404) — chemical oxidation introduces carbonyl and carboxyl groups, reduces molecular weight, improves film-forming for coating applications, increases whiteness; (4) Enzyme-converted starches — enzymatic partial hydrolysis modifies viscosity and solubility. All approved modified starches are GRAS (FDA), EU (E1404–E1442) and CODEX/FSSAI approved. Not absorbed intact — hydrolysed in the GI tract like regular starch (digestible forms) or function as resistant starch (RS type) providing dietary fibre (some modified forms). No significant allergen concerns (the starch protein is removed during processing; wheat-derived modified starch may contain trace gluten — relevant for severe coeliac disease). Chemical residues from modification processes are at trace levels within regulatory limits. No carcinogenicity or mutagenicity concerns at food use levels.""",
    # --- Raising agents ---
    'sodium bicarbonate': """Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃ / baking soda / bicarbonate of soda / E500ii/INS 500ii) is an alkaline white crystalline salt that is one of the most widely used leavening agents in baking. Mined as nahcolite mineral or produced commercially by the Solvay process (CO₂ + NH₃ + brine → NaHCO₃ + NH₄Cl) or by the simpler trona refining process. Leavening chemistry: when sodium bicarbonate contacts an acid (buttermilk, lemon juice, vinegar, curd, brown sugar, honey, cocoa powder or cream of tartar) in an aqueous medium, it reacts rapidly to release CO₂ gas: NaHCO₃ + H⁺ → Na⁺ + H₂O + CO₂. The CO₂ bubbles expand during baking, producing the light, porous structure of cakes, breads and biscuits. Without sufficient acid, heating alone decomposes NaHCO₃ at ~50°C (though meaningful reaction requires higher oven temperatures): 2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂; the residual Na₂CO₃ leaves a soapy, bitter taste. In Indian cooking: used to leaven bhatura (puffed deep-fried bread), naan, chakli, halwa and idli/dosa batters supplementing natural fermentation. As an antacid: reacts with gastric hydrochloric acid to form NaCl + H₂O + CO₂, neutralising acid rapidly. Also used in sports nutrition (sodium bicarbonate loading at ~0.3 g/kg before high-intensity exercise buffers lactic acid, improving anaerobic performance). Sodium content: ~12% sodium by weight — one teaspoon (~5 g) provides ~600 mg sodium; relevant for people restricting dietary sodium. FSSAI, EU (E500), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified. Safe at normal food use levels.""",
    'ammonium bicarbonate': """Ammonium bicarbonate (E503ii/INS 503ii / NH₄HCO₃ / hartshorn salt / powdered hartshorn / baker's ammonia) is an inorganic salt leavening agent with a distinct characteristic: when heated above approximately 35–60°C, it decomposes completely and rapidly into three volatile products — ammonia gas (NH₃), carbon dioxide gas (CO₂) and water vapour (H₂O). Because all three decomposition products are gaseous, no leavening acid residue remains in the finished baked product — unlike sodium bicarbonate or baking powder which leave sodium salts behind. This zero-residue property makes ammonium bicarbonate particularly well-suited for thin, low-moisture baked goods where ammonia can fully escape before or during baking and where alkaline sodium residues would affect flavour. Applications: thin, crispy biscuits and crackers (digestive biscuits, cream crackers, shortbread), some shortbread cookies, certain thin-crust pastries, flat wafers, and some flat breads in which the entire product thickness allows ammonia to fully diffuse and escape during baking. In thicker baked goods (e.g., regular bread or large cakes), using ammonium bicarbonate would result in ammonia entrapment inside the product, leaving a distinct, unpleasant ammonia flavour and odour — therefore it is NOT used in thick baked goods. Historical note: before baking powder became widely available in the 19th century, hartshorn salt (derived by dry distillation of deer antler — hence "hartshorn") was the primary leavening agent in European baking, particularly in Nordic and German traditional biscuits (pepparkakor, springerle). Responsible for the exceptionally crispy texture of some traditional European biscuits. Ammonia volatilises fully during proper baking and is not present in the finished product. ADI: not specified (JECFA — residue-free leavening). FSSAI, EU (E503), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'sodium carbonate': """Sodium carbonate (E500i/INS 500i / soda ash / washing soda / Na₂CO₃) is an inorganic alkaline salt produced commercially by the Solvay process (reaction of brine and limestone). A strong pH adjuster providing alkaline conditions. Distinct from sodium bicarbonate (baking soda, E500ii) — sodium carbonate is more strongly alkaline (pH ~11.6 in solution vs ~8.3 for sodium bicarbonate). Food uses: leavening agent (raises pH and generates CO₂ when heated); pH adjuster in cocoa powder processing — 'Dutch process' cocoa is treated with sodium carbonate to reduce acidity, darken colour and mellow bitterness; traditional ingredient in Chinese alkaline noodles (lye water gives ramen its characteristic yellow colour, chewy texture and alkaline flavour); used in pretzel and bagel solutions (along with sodium hydroxide) to achieve their characteristic brown crust and chewy texture; pH regulator in some beverages. In homemade cooking: used as an ingredient in certain Indian snacks. Provides sodium — relevant for those restricting dietary sodium. Highly alkaline in pure form; food-grade use at low concentrations is safe (neutralised by food acids during processing or dilution). No significant toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E500i), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'potassium carbonate': """Potassium carbonate (E501i/INS 501i / K₂CO₃ / pearl ash) is an alkaline inorganic salt with ancient uses in food preparation — it was produced historically by leaching wood ash with water and evaporating the resulting solution (the original 'potash'). As a food additive, it is produced by the reaction of potassium hydroxide with CO₂ or by electrolysis processes. Strongly alkaline (pH ~11.6 in solution), similar to sodium carbonate but providing potassium instead of sodium. Food uses: leavening agent in baked goods — particularly traditional German honey cake (Lebkuchen) and some Chinese pastries; alkaline treatment of cocoa powder (Dutch process, often used alongside sodium carbonate); Chinese alkaline noodles and ramen (lye water provides yellow colour, chewy texture and distinctive alkaline taste); stabiliser in some cocoa and coffee products; and acidity regulator. Advantage over sodium carbonate: provides potassium instead of sodium, useful in sodium-reduced formulations. Potassium is an essential mineral; dietary potassium contributes to cardiovascular health. However, individuals with chronic kidney disease (CKD) must strictly limit potassium intake — the kidneys regulate potassium excretion, and impaired kidneys allow dangerous potassium accumulation (hyperkalaemia). At food use concentrations, potassium contribution from this additive is generally modest. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E501i), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    # --- Anticaking ---
    'silicon dioxide': """Silicon dioxide (SiO₂ / E551/INS 551 / silica) is one of the most abundant naturally occurring minerals on Earth — the primary constituent of sand (quartz), diatomite and many rocks. For food use, food-grade synthetic amorphous silicon dioxide (rather than crystalline quartz) is manufactured by gas-phase hydrolysis of silicon tetrachloride (fumed silica) or wet precipitation from sodium silicate solutions (precipitated silica), producing a highly porous, very fine white powder with enormous surface area (50–800 m²/g). This large surface area enables the primary food function — anti-caking: particles of silicon dioxide physically insert between powder particles (salt, spices, cocoa powder, milk powder, soup mixes, baking powder, nutritional supplement powders), absorbing moisture and preventing the electrostatic and capillary water bridging that causes caking, especially in humid tropical climates like India. Also used as a carrier for liquid flavours and colours, allowing them to be converted to free-flowing powders for industrial use. Safety — critical distinction: crystalline silica (quartz dust from mining/construction) is an IARC Group 1 carcinogen causing silicosis and lung cancer from occupational inhalation exposure. Food-grade and cosmetic-grade silica is amorphous — entirely different morphology; no fibrous particles; no carcinogenicity concerns at food use levels. 2018 EFSA nano-silica concern: EFSA flagged food-grade E551 nano-particles as a concern (some silica particles are in the nano range <100 nm); EFSA concluded the available data are insufficient to establish an ADI for nano-SiO₂ — a precautionary review is ongoing. Traditional coarser-particle silicon dioxide is not the subject of this concern. At approved food additive use levels (typically 1–2% of powdered products), silicon dioxide is not absorbed in meaningful amounts; any absorbed silicic acid is excreted in urine. ADI: not specified (JECFA for food-grade). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E551), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    # --- Flavor enhancers (natural) ---
    'yeast extract': """Yeast extract is a concentrated flavour ingredient produced from the intracellular contents of Saccharomyces cerevisiae (baker's or brewer's yeast) or Candida utilis. Production: yeast cells are disrupted — either by autolysis (self-digestion using the yeast's own enzymes, triggered by heat at 45–55°C) or by plasmolysis (salt-induced osmotic rupture) — then the soluble cell contents (amino acids, peptides, nucleotides, B vitamins) are separated from the insoluble cell walls and concentrated by evaporation or spray-drying. Chemical nature: a complex mixture of free amino acids (particularly glutamic acid ~5–20% of dry weight), short peptides, 5'-nucleotides (IMP, GMP — which amplify umami synergistically), B vitamins (thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, B6, folate) and minerals. Rich natural source of umami flavour — used in soups, stocks, gravies, instant noodles, bouillon cubes, savoury seasonings, snack coatings and ready-to-eat meals to enhance flavour depth. Generally considered natural and clean-label. People with gout or hyperuricaemia should moderate intake (nucleotide content raises uric acid). Contains naturally occurring glutamate — similar umami effect to MSG. May not be vegan if grown on animal-derived media (most commercial grades use plant-based substrates). FSSAI and internationally approved as a food ingredient. No ADI specified.""",
    'hydrolysed': 'A protein that has been broken down into amino acids and shorter peptide chains by hydrolysis using acid, base or enzymes. Used as a natural-source flavour enhancer in soups, sauces and processed meat products.',
    # --- Natural colorants ---
    'annatto': """Annatto (E160b/INS 160b) is a natural orange-yellow food colour derived from the seed coat (aril) of the achiote tree (Bixa orellana), native to tropical America and now cultivated widely in India, Brazil and Africa. Contains two carotenoid pigments: bixin (fat-soluble, 80% of pigment) and norbixin (water-soluble, the saponified form). Bixin-based preparations colour fat-containing foods (butter, margarine, cheese rinds); norbixin-based preparations colour water-based products (beverages, sauces, processed cheese). Produces a warm yellow to deep orange colour. Used in cheeses (Cheddar, Red Leicester, Colby), butter, margarine, custard powder, rice dishes, snacks, smoked fish and some beverages. In Indian cooking: used in some traditional preparations for its rich yellow-orange colour. Health note: annatto is one of the more commonly reported natural food colour allergens — case reports of anaphylaxis, urticaria, angioedema and IBS-like symptoms. Individuals with reactions to artificial dyes do not necessarily react to annatto (it is not an azo dye), but those with tree nut or seed allergies should note that the achiote seed is the source. ADI: 0–6 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA for bixin and norbixin combined). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160b), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'paprika': """Paprika is a spice and natural food colorant produced from dried and ground sweet or mildly hot red peppers (Capsicum annuum). The colour comes from carotenoid pigments — primarily capsanthin and capsorubin (red), plus beta-carotene and zeaxanthin (yellow-orange) — collectively termed paprika oleoresin (E160c/INS 160c) when extracted and concentrated as a food-grade colour ingredient. Nutritional composition: rich in beta-carotene (provitamin A), Vitamin C, Vitamin E, Vitamin B6, potassium and iron. The carotenoids in paprika are powerful fat-soluble antioxidants. Heat units: sweet paprika (~0 Scoville units); smoked Spanish paprika (pimentón de La Vera); hot paprika (higher capsaicin content). Used as a spice for flavouring and as a natural red-orange colour in: processed meats, sausages (Hungarian salami), spice blends, snack seasonings, cheese products, sauces, soups and bakery products. Oleoresin paprika (E160c) is the concentrated extract — fat-soluble, providing intense colour to fats, oils, cheese and dairy products. Health benefits: the carotenoids and vitamin C content support antioxidant status; capsaicin (in hot paprika) has anti-inflammatory properties. Allergen: rare — paprika/capsicum allergy exists but is not among the 14 major regulated allergens. Safe at culinary and additive concentrations. FSSAI permits paprika and paprika oleoresin (E160c) as natural food colours. ADI: 24 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA for paprika oleoresin/capsanthin-capsorubin).""",
    'beetroot': """Beetroot (Beta vulgaris var. rubra) is a root vegetable and one of the most nutritionally dense plant foods consumed in India. Unlike many other natural food colourings, whole beetroot is primarily a whole food, not a processed additive. Nutritional profile: very rich in folate (Vitamin B9 — critical for DNA synthesis, cell division and prevention of neural tube defects; 37% RDA per 100g cooked), dietary fibre (2.8 g/100g), vitamin C, manganese and potassium. Uniquely high in inorganic nitrate (~250–300 mg/100g fresh beetroot) and betaine — both with distinct health roles. Inorganic nitrate pathway: dietary nitrate from beetroot is converted by oral bacteria (in saliva, via nitrate reductase) to nitrite, then absorbed and converted in the body to nitric oxide (NO) — a potent vasodilator. Clinical trials show that 500 mL beetroot juice (200 mg nitrate) reduces systolic blood pressure by ~4–5 mmHg, improves exercise economy (allows muscles to function with less oxygen), and enhances athletic endurance performance. Multiple randomised controlled trials confirm these benefits. Betaine: a methyl donor derived from choline; reduces plasma homocysteine (an independent cardiovascular risk factor); liver protectant. Pigments: betalains (primarily betanin and isobetanin — red/violet betacyanins) are potent antioxidants. Beeturia: approximately 10–14% of the population excretes red-pink urine after eating beetroot (beeturia) — this is a harmless genetic variation in how betanin is metabolised, not an indicator of bleeding; it can alarm people unfamiliar with it. When used as a food colour extract (E162/INS 162 / beetroot red / betanin), see the beetroot red entry for stability and food use details. In India, beetroot is a popular vegetable used in salads (particularly in kachumber), raita, paratha, halwa, soup and as a natural food colour in sweets. Also used as a natural colourant in ice cream, health beverages and plant-based meat products. Generally very safe for most people. Rare: may cause kidney stone risk in individuals susceptible to oxalate stones (beetroot is relatively high in oxalic acid at ~600 mg/100g). People on blood thinners should moderate very high-nitrate juice intake (monitor BP). No known allergenicity.""",
    # --- Waxes ---
    'shellac': 'A natural resin secreted by the female lac bug (Kerria lacca) on trees in India and Thailand. Used as a glazing agent (E904) on confectionery, fresh fruit and pharmaceutical tablets to add shine and reduce moisture loss.',
    'beeswax': 'A natural wax produced by honey bees to build honeycomb. Used as a glazing and coating agent (E901) in confectionery and cheese, and widely used in cosmetics as a thickener, emulsifier and emollient.',
    'carnauba wax': """Carnauba wax (E903/INS 903 / Cera carnauba) is harvested from the leaf surfaces of the carnauba palm (Copernicia prunifera / Copernicia cerifera), a tall palm tree native to and cultivated almost exclusively in northeastern Brazil (Ceará, Piauí, Maranhão states). The dried leaves are beaten to release the wax coating, which is then refined from a grey to bright yellow to white powder. The hardest natural wax available (melting point 82–86°C; Mohs hardness sufficient to withstand polishing without melting from friction). Composition: primarily wax esters of long-chain fatty acids and alcohols, with some free acids and hydrocarbons. In food (E903): glazing agent — applied to confectionery (sweets, chocolate-covered nuts), chewing gum, fresh fruit and some baked goods to provide shine, reduce moisture loss and improve handling. Vegan and vegetarian friendly — pure plant wax. In cosmetics: used in lipsticks (provides structure, high melting point and glossy finish), mascara, eyeshadow, eyeliner, foundations and many colour cosmetics; component of car wax, shoe polish and floor polish. Provides a hard, glossy film. Extremely low sensitisation potential — contact allergy is very rare. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E903), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Considered one of the safest glazing agents; no toxicological concerns at food use levels. Biodegradable.""",
    # --- Herbal / generally recognised ---
    'neem': """Neem (Azadirachta indica / nimba in Sanskrit / Indian lilac) is a fast-growing evergreen tree of the Meliaceae family native to the Indian subcontinent, now cultivated throughout tropical and subtropical Asia, Africa and the Americas. Recognised for over 2,000 years in Ayurvedic medicine as a "sarva roga nivarini" (universal healer). Every part of the neem tree is medicinal — leaves, seeds, bark, roots and oil. Key phytochemicals: azadirachtin (the principal terpenoid limonoid — potent insect antifeedant and insecticidal agent), nimbin, nimbidin, nimbolide, quercetin, beta-sitosterol, gedunin and a wide array of limonoids. Properties: (1) Antimicrobial — broad-spectrum antibacterial (effective against S. aureus, E. coli, P. aeruginosa) and antifungal (effective against Candida species) — used in oral care products (toothpastes, mouthwashes), soaps and antibacterial formulations; (2) Anti-inflammatory — nimbidin and quercetin inhibit prostaglandin synthesis; (3) Insecticidal — azadirachtin disrupts insect hormone systems; neem is a key ingredient in organic pest control; (4) Antiviral — some evidence for activity against HSV and dengue virus; (5) Antiseptic wound-healing; (6) Dandruff treatment — antifungal activity effective against Malassezia. Used in: toothpastes (Dabur, Vicco, Patanjali), soaps, shampoos, face washes, hair oils, skin creams and Ayurvedic formulations. Generally safe in topical and oral care use. Neem oil is contraindicated in pregnancy (potential abortifacient effects at high systemic doses). Neem seed oil must not be given orally to young children — cases of neem oil poisoning with encephalopathy have been reported in infants. AYUSH Ministry and FSSAI regulate neem-containing products. Organic farming use extensively permitted.""",
    'neem extract': """Neem extract is a concentrated preparation derived from the leaves, bark, seeds or other parts of the neem tree (Azadirachta indica), using water extraction (aqueous extract), ethanol extraction or supercritical CO₂ extraction. The composition depends on the plant part and extraction method: leaf extract (rich in nimbin, quercetin, azadirachtin, beta-sitosterol); seed kernel extract (highest azadirachtin content); bark extract (rich in nimbin and bitter tannins). Commercial neem extracts are standardised for specific bioactive content — for example, neem leaf extract standardised for nimbidin or quercetin. Uses in personal care and food supplements: (1) Oral care — antibacterial and anti-inflammatory in toothpastes, mouthwashes and chewing gums; prevents dental plaque (biofilm inhibition); clinical evidence supports comparable plaque reduction to chlorhexidine mouthwash at 15 days; (2) Skin care — antibacterial and anti-acne (reduces P. acnes growth, reduces sebum production); anti-inflammatory for eczema and psoriasis; (3) Hair care — antifungal for dandruff (anti-Malassezia activity), scalp anti-inflammatory; (4) Dietary supplements — immunity support, blood glucose management (emerging evidence). See the neem entry for full phytochemical and safety details. Generally safe in topical use and oral care products. Oral supplementation at high doses: animal studies show hepatotoxicity with large doses of neem seed extract — human safety data for oral supplements at high doses is limited. FSSAI and AYUSH Ministry regulate use limits.""",
    'neem oil': """Neem oil is a vegetable oil cold-pressed or expeller-pressed from the seeds (kernels) of the neem tree (Azadirachta indica). It has a distinctive, strong pungent odour (from sulfurous compounds) and a brownish-yellow colour. Chemical composition: fatty acids (~50% oleic acid, ~15–20% palmitic acid, ~15% stearic acid, ~15% linoleic acid), azadirachtin and related limonoids (concentrated in seeds), nimbin, nimbidin and other terpenoids. The high azadirachtin content makes neem oil one of nature's most powerful natural insecticides and insect repellents. Applications: (1) Agriculture and pest control — organic-certified insecticide and nematicide; approved for use in organic farming in India, EU and USA; disrupts insect moulting and reproduction; (2) Personal care — used in soaps, shampoos and body lotions for antimicrobial, antifungal and insect-repelling properties; used in antidandruff shampoos (anti-Malassezia); (3) Ayurvedic medicine — anti-inflammatory topically for eczema, psoriasis and skin infections; scalp oil for hair and scalp health; (4) Wood preservation. Safety: topical use of diluted neem oil is generally safe; it is commonly diluted 1–5% in a carrier oil for skin application. Direct oral ingestion of neem oil is extremely dangerous for infants and young children — multiple case reports of toxic encephalopathy, metabolic acidosis and coma following neem oil ingestion by infants (treated as a remedy for stomach ailments in some traditional practices); FSSAI and paediatric bodies warn against oral neem oil in young children. Contraindicated in pregnancy (potential abortifacient). Adult oral supplementation at high doses may cause hepatotoxicity. Topical use in appropriate dilutions is safe for adults.""",
    'tulsi': """Tulsi (holy basil / Ocimum tenuiflorum / Ocimum sanctum) is a sacred aromatic plant of the Lamiaceae family, native to the Indian subcontinent and found in most Hindu households as a sacred plant. One of the most revered Ayurvedic herbs — described in texts going back over 3,000 years as a rasayana, adaptogen, immunomodulator and spiritual herb. Three main varieties used: Krishna tulsi (dark green to purple, most pungent), Rama tulsi (green leaves, milder) and Vana tulsi (wild tulsi). Key phytochemicals: eugenol (~70–75% of essential oil — the primary bioactive, with potent COX inhibitory and antimicrobial properties), ursolic acid, rosmarinic acid, beta-caryophyllene, luteolin, apigenin, and the volatile terpenoids ocimene and linalool. Documented properties: (1) Adaptogenic — reduces cortisol and stress biomarkers in clinical trials; (2) Antimicrobial — eugenol effective against bacteria, fungi and some viruses; (3) Anti-inflammatory — COX-1/2 inhibition; (4) Immunomodulatory — enhances NK cell activity and T-cell proliferation; (5) Antidiabetic — reduces fasting and postprandial blood glucose in clinical studies; (6) Respiratory — expectorant, bronchodilator and anti-tussive; used in Ayurvedic respiratory preparations; (7) Antioxidant — rosmarinic acid and ursolic acid protect against oxidative stress; (8) Oral health — antimicrobial against dental pathogens. Used in: herbal teas (tulsi tea — one of India's most popular herbal beverages), dietary supplements, immunity formulas, oral care products, cosmetics (anti-inflammatory skincare), hair care. Regulated by AYUSH Ministry for Ayurvedic preparations; FSSAI permits in herbal beverages. Generally safe at culinary and supplement doses. Eugenol in large amounts may have blood-thinning effects — caution with anticoagulants.""",
    'aloe vera': 'A succulent plant native to the Arabian Peninsula, used medicinally for over 6,000 years. The clear gel from its leaves is used in skincare, cosmetics, beverages and medicines for its soothing and moisturising properties.',
    'aloe': """Aloe (from Aloe vera / Aloe barbadensis Miller) encompasses multiple ingredient forms derived from the aloe plant: the inner leaf gel (clear, water-like), the whole leaf extract (includes both inner gel and the bitter latex layer containing aloin), dried powder and concentrated extracts. The aloe plant has been used medicinally for over 6,000 years across ancient Egypt, India, China and the Mediterranean. Active compounds: acemannan (immunostimulatory and wound-healing polysaccharide), barbaloin/aloin (anthraquinone laxative — present in latex, should be absent or minimal in food and skin products), aloe-emodin, vitamins, enzymes and salicylates. In cosmetics: soothing, anti-inflammatory, hydrating and wound-healing properties are well supported; used in after-sun products, moisturisers, face masks, hair care, shaving preparations and baby products. In food/beverages: aloe vera juice is popular in India as a health drink; FSSAI permits the use of aloe vera in foods with specified maximum limits for aloin (the anthraquinone that is potentially carcinogenic in high doses). WHO and IARC safety findings: whole leaf extract (containing aloin/barbaloin) classified as Group 2B possibly carcinogenic in rodent studies; inner leaf gel (properly decolourised, aloin <10 ppm) classified Group 3 — not classifiable as carcinogen. Check that beverages and supplements use 'inner leaf gel' and confirm low aloin content. Generally safe topically; very rare allergy (patch testing recommended for those with latex sensitivity — some cross-reactivity possible).""",
    'turmeric extract': """Turmeric extract is a concentrated preparation derived from the dried rhizome of Curcuma longa, standardised for its curcuminoid content — primarily curcumin (the principal bioactive) along with demethoxycurcumin and bisdemethoxycurcumin. Commercial extracts typically contain 95% curcuminoids, compared to 2–5% in whole turmeric powder. The extraction process uses ethanol or supercritical CO₂ (for solvent-free "clean" extracts). Used in: dietary supplements (anti-inflammatory, joint health, liver support), functional foods and as a natural yellow food colour (E100/INS 100). Curcumin's poor oral bioavailability — it is rapidly metabolised in the gut wall and liver and has very low water solubility — has driven formulation innovation: piperine-enhanced formulas (black pepper extract increases bioavailability 20-fold by inhibiting CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein efflux), lecithin-based phytosomes (Meriva), nanotechnology-based dispersions and BCM-95 (with essential oils). Clinical evidence: strongest for osteoarthritis symptom relief, IBD, and metabolic syndrome (multiple RCTs). Cautions: antiplatelet properties at high doses — caution with blood thinners (warfarin, aspirin); may increase bile secretion — avoid in bile duct obstruction and gallstones; high doses not recommended in pregnancy. AYUSH Ministry regulates turmeric extract in Ayurvedic products; FSSAI permits curcumin (E100) as a food colour.""",
    'turmeric': """Turmeric is a bright yellow spice derived from the dried and powdered rhizome of Curcuma longa, a perennial plant of the ginger family (Zingiberaceae) native to and cultivated predominantly in South Asia — India accounts for approximately 80% of global production. The rhizomes are boiled, dried and ground to produce the familiar yellow powder. Chemical composition: 2–5% curcuminoids (curcumin, demethoxycurcumin, bisdemethoxycurcumin), 1–4% volatile essential oils (turmerone, zingiberene), starch and fibre. Curcumin gives the characteristic yellow-orange colour (E100/INS 100) and is responsible for most studied pharmacological effects. Function in food and cooking: flavour and colour in curry spice blends, mustard, pickles, dairy products and rice dishes; used as a natural food colour; traditional preservative (antimicrobial). In cosmetics: anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial and antioxidant properties are used in face masks, skin-brightening preparations and hair oils in Ayurvedic beauty formulations. Health effects: well-documented anti-inflammatory activity (NF-κB inhibition), antioxidant properties, and potential benefits in osteoarthritis, IBD and metabolic syndrome. Bioavailability is limited — co-consumption with black pepper (piperine) or fat improves absorption. Safe at culinary doses; high-dose supplements require caution with blood thinners and in pregnancy. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient and food colour (E100).""",
    'haldi': """Haldi is the Hindi/Urdu name for turmeric (Curcuma longa), a perennial rhizomatous herb of the ginger family (Zingiberaceae) originating in the Indian subcontinent, where it has been cultivated and used for at least 4,000 years. India produces approximately 80% of the world's turmeric, with Erode (Tamil Nadu) known as the "Turmeric City." Composition: the dried rhizome contains 2–5% curcuminoids (curcumin, demethoxycurcumin and bisdemethoxycurcumin — collectively called curcumin), 1–4% volatile essential oil (turmerone, zingiberene, curlone), starch, fibre, proteins and minerals. Curcumin is responsible for turmeric's vivid yellow-orange colour and most of its pharmacological activity. In food: used as a spice (flavour and colour), food colour (E100/INS 100 — curcumin/turmeric), preservative (antimicrobial) and in Ayurvedic medicine. In cosmetics: anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial and antioxidant properties make it popular in face masks, skin brightening creams, anti-acne formulations and hair care in India. Health benefits (studied): curcumin has demonstrated anti-inflammatory (NF-κB inhibition), antioxidant, antifungal, antibacterial and possible anticancer activity in cell culture and animal models. Clinical evidence in humans is promising for osteoarthritis, IBD and metabolic syndrome, but poor bioavailability limits systemic effect (curcumin is rapidly metabolised and poorly absorbed — piperine from black pepper increases bioavailability 20-fold). Caution: turmeric and curcumin supplements in high doses can inhibit platelet aggregation (anticoagulant effect) — caution with blood-thinning medications. May increase bile production — contraindicated in bile duct obstruction and should be used cautiously with gallstones. Not recommended in high supplemental doses during pregnancy. FSSAI permits curcumin (E100) as a food colour across many product categories. Generally recognised as safe as a spice at culinary amounts.""",
    'ashwagandha': """Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera / winter cherry / Indian ginseng) is a small shrub of the Solanaceae family native to the Indian subcontinent, the Mediterranean and North Africa, used in Ayurvedic medicine for over 3,000 years as a primary rasayana (rejuvenating) and medhya (brain-tonic) herb. The root (and to a lesser extent the leaves and berries) is the medicinal part. Key phytochemicals: withanolides (steroidal lactones — withanolide A, withaferin A — the main adaptogenic and anti-inflammatory compounds), alkaloids (somniferine, somnine, anaferine), saponins, sitoindosides and iron. Pharmacological effects: adaptogen — modulates the HPA (hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal) axis, reducing cortisol and stress biomarkers; anxiolytic and anti-depressant effects in clinical trials; improves sleep onset and quality; cognitive function and memory enhancement (particularly in stressed individuals); muscle strength and endurance (multiple RCTs show significant improvement in VO2 max and muscle recovery); thyroid stimulation (modestly raises T3/T4 — relevant for hypothyroid individuals); testosterone and fertility support in men (several clinical trials). Regulatory: AYUSH Ministry of India recognises ashwagandha under the traditional medicine category; supplements are widely sold. Many Indian products (including protein powders, immunity supplements and stress-relief formulations) include ashwagandha extract. Cautions: may interact with thyroid medications; contraindicated in pregnancy (uterotonic effects); autoimmune conditions (may stimulate immune activity); and during surgery (possible anaesthesia interactions). Rare cases of hepatotoxicity (liver injury) with high-dose supplements have been reported — dose and quality of extract matter. Generally safe at traditional supplement doses (300–600 mg KSM-66 or Sensoril extract/day).""",
    'aloe vera gel': """Aloe vera gel (Aloe barbadensis leaf gel / aloe vera inner leaf gel) is the clear mucilaginous gel obtained from the central parenchyma (inner leaf pulp) of Aloe barbadensis Miller leaves. Distinct from aloe latex (the yellow-brown exudate from sub-epidermal cells just below the leaf skin, which contains potent laxative anthraquinones including aloin) — properly extracted gel should be essentially free of aloin. Composition: over 98% water; the remaining solids include polysaccharides (acemannan — a beta-1,4-acetylated mannan that is the primary bioactive compound), glycoproteins, vitamins C, E and B vitamins, minerals, amino acids, salicylic acid, sterols and enzymes. Acemannan has documented wound-healing, anti-inflammatory and immune-modulating properties. Cosmetic uses: moisturiser (high water content provides immediate hydration); wound healing accelerator (acemannan stimulates fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis); anti-inflammatory (reduces redness, burning and irritation in sunburn, eczema and minor wounds); film-former (dries to a light, flexible film in hair gels); widely used in after-sun products, face gels, hair care, shaving gels and baby care. In beverages: aloe vera juice drinks are popular in India — must use properly decolourised (aloin-removed) inner leaf gel; WHO has raised safety concerns about carcinogenic potential of improperly processed aloe (high aloin content); properly processed products are safe. IARC classified whole leaf extract (containing aloin) as Group 2B possible carcinogen; inner leaf gel is Group 3 (not classifiable). ADI established for inner leaf gel products. FSSAI permits in beverages at specified aloin limits.""",
    'coconut oil': """Coconut oil is a plant fat extracted from the dried kernel (copra) or fresh meat of mature coconuts (Cocos nucifera). Two main commercial forms: refined coconut oil (from dried copra — bleached, deodorised, neutral flavour) and virgin or cold-pressed coconut oil (from fresh coconut meat — retains characteristic coconut flavour and aroma and more polyphenols). Fatty acid composition is highly unusual for a plant oil: ~92% saturated fatty acids — primarily lauric acid (~49%, C12:0), myristic acid (~18%), palmitic acid (~9%) and caprylic/capric acids (medium-chain triglycerides, MCTs, ~15%). This extremely high saturated fat content makes it solid below ~24°C. Lauric acid consideration: lauric acid raises both LDL and HDL cholesterol, with the net cardiovascular impact debated — some research suggests it is less atherogenic than palmitic acid; others treat it like other saturated fats. WHO, American Heart Association and FSSAI guidelines continue to recommend limiting saturated fat intake, which includes coconut oil. MCT content: approximately 15% of coconut oil is medium-chain triglycerides (C8–C10), absorbed differently from long-chain fats and rapidly converted to ketones — basis for coconut oil's popularity in ketogenic diets. Claims of 'superfood' status are not supported by strong clinical evidence. In cosmetics: a widely used emollient, carrier oil and hair conditioning agent — reduces protein loss in hair due to its small molecular size allowing penetration into the hair shaft. FSSAI permits refined coconut oil as a standard edible vegetable oil.""",
    'olive oil': """Olive oil is a vegetable oil extracted from the fruit of the olive tree (Olea europaea), a cornerstone of Mediterranean cuisine for over 5,000 years. Grades: extra virgin (cold-pressed, unrefined, <0.8% free acidity, highest quality and polyphenol content), virgin (cold-pressed, slightly higher acidity), refined olive oil (chemically or thermally refined, lighter flavour), and olive pomace oil (extracted from the residue using solvents). Fatty acid composition: approximately 73% oleic acid (monounsaturated C18:1), 14% palmitic acid (saturated), 11% linoleic acid (omega-6) — one of the highest monounsaturated fat contents of common vegetable oils. Beyond fats, extra virgin olive oil contains significant polyphenols (oleocanthal, oleuropein, hydroxytyrosol) with potent anti-inflammatory, antioxidant and cardioprotective properties — oleocanthal inhibits COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes in a mechanism similar to ibuprofen. Health evidence: the Mediterranean diet, of which olive oil is the primary fat, has the strongest evidence base of any dietary pattern for cardiovascular disease reduction. PREDIMED trial (7,477 high-risk adults) demonstrated extra virgin olive oil supplementation reduced major cardiovascular events by ~30%. Polyphenol content is highest in extra virgin olive oil and decreases with refining. Used in cooking, salad dressings, marinades and cosmetics (skin emollient, hair conditioner). High smoke point of refined olive oil (~240°C) makes it suitable for cooking. FSSAI permits imported olive oil as a food ingredient.""",
    'honey': """Honey is a natural viscous sweetener produced by honeybees (Apis mellifera and related species) from flower nectar and plant secretions — nectar is collected and repeatedly enzymatically processed and concentrated in the hive by evaporation to <20% water content. Composition: approximately 38% fructose, 31% glucose, 17% water, 7.2% maltose, and small amounts of sucrose, higher sugars, vitamins (B vitamins, Vitamin C in small amounts), minerals (potassium, calcium, magnesium, iron), enzymes (diastase/amylase, invertase, glucose oxidase), organic acids (gluconic acid from glucose oxidase reaction), and polyphenols (flavonoids, phenolic acids). The glucose oxidase enzyme converts some glucose to hydrogen peroxide and gluconic acid, contributing to honey's antimicrobial properties. Glycaemic index: approximately 50–65 (varies by floral source and fructose:glucose ratio) — lower than white sugar (GI ~65) but still a high-GI food in significant quantities. Functional properties in food: natural humectant (retains moisture in baked goods, extending shelf life), natural preservative (low water activity and hydrogen peroxide), flavouring, browning agent (hygroscopic sugars promote Maillard reaction). In cosmetics: emollient, humectant, wound-healing agent (medical-grade manuka honey has evidence for wound care). Infant safety: raw honey should NEVER be given to infants under 12 months — it may contain Clostridium botulinum spores causing infant botulism; adult immune systems neutralise these, but infant gastrointestinal systems cannot. Diabetics should treat honey as a sugar equivalent. FSSAI regulates honey quality standards including permissible adulteration limits.""",
    'salicylic acid': 'A naturally occurring beta hydroxy acid (BHA) found in willow bark and other plants. Used as a keratolytic and acne treatment in skincare and as a food preservative in some applications.',
    'hyaluronic acid': 'A natural polysaccharide (glycosaminoglycan) found in the skin, eyes and joint fluid of all vertebrates. Used extensively in skincare for its exceptional ability to bind and retain moisture.',
    'ceramide': 'A class of lipid molecules that are the primary component of the skin\'s stratum corneum (outer barrier layer). Used in skincare products to reinforce the skin barrier and prevent moisture loss.',
    'collagen': 'The most abundant structural protein in the human body, found in skin, tendons, bones and cartilage. Used in skincare products and dietary supplements for skin elasticity and wound healing.',
    # --- CI iron oxides (generally recognised) ---
    'ci 77491': 'CI 77491 is the Colour Index number for Iron Oxide Red, a naturally occurring mineral pigment widely used in cosmetics, face makeup and some foods as a safe, stable red colourant.',
    'ci 77492': 'CI 77492 is the Colour Index number for Iron Oxide Yellow, a naturally occurring mineral pigment used in cosmetics and some food colourants as a safe yellow or buff colourant.',
    'ci 77499': 'CI 77499 is the Colour Index number for Iron Oxide Black, a naturally occurring mineral pigment used in cosmetics like mascara, eyeliner and nail products as a stable black colourant.',
    'iron oxide': """Iron oxides (E172/INS 172) are inorganic mineral pigments — naturally occurring forms of iron and oxygen — used as food and cosmetic colourants in three main forms: red (Fe₂O₃, haematite), yellow (FeOOH, goethite) and black (Fe₃O₄, magnetite). Commercially produced by controlled precipitation of iron salts (ferrous or ferric sulfate) under controlled pH and temperature conditions. The synthetic iron oxides used as food additives are chemically identical to the natural mineral forms but are produced synthetically to ensure purity and absence of heavy metal contaminants. Uses in food: colouring the outer coating of olives, tablet coatings on pharmaceutical supplements, confectionery sugar coatings, food colour in some surface treatments. Uses in cosmetics: the most widely used inorganic pigments in makeup (foundation, blush, eyeshadow, lipstick) and sunscreen formulations. Iron oxides also act as physical UV light absorbers in cosmetics and provide some HEV (high-energy visible) light protection. All three forms are non-toxic, non-allergenic, chemically inert and do not penetrate the skin barrier — iron oxides are considered among the safest pigments available. No ADI specified (JECFA — safe at food use levels). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E172), FDA (cosmetics and limited food uses) and CODEX. Small contribution of dietary iron (negligible at use concentrations).""",

    # ── INS / E Numbered Food Additives ────────────────────────────────────────
    # Colours
    'curcumin': """Curcumin (E100/INS 100) is the primary polyphenol pigment of turmeric (Curcuma longa), a member of the ginger family cultivated in South Asia for over 4,000 years. As a food colour it is extracted from the dried rhizome using solvents; the extract contains curcumin (60–70%), demethoxycurcumin and bisdemethoxycurcumin. Produces vivid yellow to orange-yellow colours stable in acidic to neutral environments but sensitive to light and alkaline pH (turns red-brown above pH 8). Used to colour mustard, butter, margarine, dairy products, confectionery, snack seasonings, rice dishes and non-alcoholic beverages. Curcumin is highly lipophilic — dissolved in fat or oil for food applications; water-dispersible forms use emulsification with polysorbates or lecithin. As a bioactive: curcumin has been intensively studied (>16,000 papers) for anti-inflammatory, antioxidant and anticancer properties in cell and animal models; however its low oral bioavailability (poor water solubility, rapid metabolism) limits translation to human therapeutic applications — clinical trials have been largely inconclusive. As a food colour, the doses ingested are far below studied therapeutic doses. No significant toxicity; not genotoxic. ADI: 0–3 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E100), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Natural and plant-derived.""",
    'e100': 'E100/INS 100 is curcumin, the natural yellow pigment extracted from turmeric (Curcuma longa). Approved as a food colorant by FSSAI, EU and CODEX at specified limits in food products.',
    'riboflavin': """Riboflavin (Vitamin B2 / E101/INS 101) is an essential water-soluble B vitamin and a natural yellow-green fluorescent pigment found naturally in dairy products, eggs, meat, fish, green leafy vegetables, almonds and fortified cereals. Chemically it is 7,8-dimethyl-10-ribitylisoalloxazine — a tricyclic isoalloxazine ring attached to a ribitol sugar chain. Biochemical role: the precursor to two critical coenzymes — flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD) — both essential for mitochondrial electron transport (oxidative phosphorylation), fatty acid beta-oxidation, amino acid catabolism, and the activity of several antioxidant enzymes including glutathione reductase. Also required for Vitamin B6 and folate metabolism. Deficiency (ariboflavinosis): sore throat, inflamed tongue (glossitis), angular stomatitis (cracked mouth corners), dermatitis and anaemia — associated with poor nutrition, particularly in low-income populations. Indian relevance: riboflavin deficiency is documented in rural Indian populations on primarily cereal-based diets. RDA: 1.1–1.3 mg/day (adults). As a food colour (E101): provides an intense yellow colour to beverages, confectionery, pasta and dairy products. Produced by microbial fermentation (Ashbya gossypii) or chemically synthesised. Safe and beneficial as both a nutrient and a colour. Excess riboflavin is excreted in urine (gives characteristic bright yellow colour). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E101), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e101': 'E101/INS 101 is riboflavin (Vitamin B2), used as a yellow food colorant and nutritional supplement. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Safe and beneficial as an essential vitamin.',
    'carmine': """Carmine (E120/INS 120 / Natural Red 4 / CI 75470 / carminic acid) is a vivid crimson-red pigment produced from the dried bodies of female cochineal insects (Dactylopius coccus), scale insects that feed on Opuntia cacti, cultivated primarily in Peru, the Canary Islands and Mexico. The active colouring agent is carminic acid (C22H20O13, a hydroxyanthraquinone glucoside) — typically comprising 17–24% of the dried insect body weight. Processing: dried, powdered insects are extracted with water or dilute acid; the extract is acidified and the carminic acid precipitated with aluminium ions to form the lake pigment "carmine" or "aluminium carmine" used in food colouring. Produces hues from orange-red at low pH to violet-red at higher pH. Used in: yoghurt, confectionery, beverages, processed meats, ice cream, cosmetics (lipsticks, eye shadows, blushers) and pharmaceuticals. Allergen concern: carmine is one of the most well-documented natural colour allergens — documented to cause urticaria, angioedema, rhinitis, asthma and anaphylaxis in sensitised individuals. Reactions are IgE-mediated. FSSAI and EU regulations require carmine to be identified by name "cochineal" or "carminic acid" or "carmine" on ingredient labels (not hidden as "natural colour"). Vegan and vegetarian status: carmine is of insect origin — not acceptable to most vegans, many vegetarians and those following Jain dietary practices. Also not acceptable in some Islamic (halal) and Jewish (kosher) dietary frameworks depending on interpretation. ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E120), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'cochineal': """Cochineal refers to both the dried bodies of female cochineal insects (Dactylopius coccus) and the extract derived from them containing carminic acid (E120/INS 120). Cochineal insects are scale insects that feed parasitically on Opuntia (prickly pear cactus) and are cultivated primarily in Peru, Mexico and the Canary Islands. The female insects accumulate high concentrations of carminic acid (17–24% of dry weight) as a deterrent against predators — this acid is the basis for one of humanity's oldest red dyes, used by Aztec and Mesoamerican civilisations centuries before European contact, and brought to Europe by Spanish conquistadors in the 16th century. Chemical nature of carminic acid: a hydroxyanthraquinone glucoside (C22H20O13); produces vivid crimson-red when complexed with aluminium (carmine lake), orange-red in acidic conditions, violet in alkaline conditions. Uses in food: yoghurt, berry drinks, processed meats, confectionery, gelatine desserts — where an intense natural red is required. As a cosmetic colour: widely used in lipstick, eye shadow, blusher and nail polish. As E120, cochineal extract must be clearly labelled — not hidden as "natural colour" — because it is a documented allergen causing IgE-mediated urticaria, angioedema and anaphylaxis in sensitised individuals. Not suitable for vegans, most vegetarians, Jain dietary practitioners or those following strict halal/kosher diets. ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E120), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'e120': 'E120/INS 120 is carmine (cochineal extract), a natural red dye derived from cochineal insects. Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA. Vegetarians and vegans should note it is animal-derived; some individuals are allergic.',
    'e160b': 'E160b/INS 160b is annatto extract (bixin/norbixin), a natural yellow-orange colorant from the seeds of the achiote tree. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in cheese, butter, snacks and beverages.',
    'e162': 'E162/INS 162 is betanin (beetroot red), the natural crimson-red pigment extracted from beetroot (Beta vulgaris). Approved by FSSAI and EU as a natural food colorant; the coloured urine or stools from beetroot are harmless.',
    'betanin': """Betanin (E162/INS 162 / beetroot red) is the primary red pigment found in red beetroot (Beta vulgaris var. rubra) — a betacyanin (a subclass of betalains) — chemically it is 1-(2-(2,6-dicarboxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydropyridyl)ethylidene)-(S)-5,6-dihydroindole-2-carboxylate. The purple-red colour extracted from beetroot juice concentrate or dried beetroot powder. Colour is pH-sensitive: vivid red-violet at neutral pH; transitions to yellow at pH <3.5 and blue-violet at pH >7. Heat-sensitive (degrades during cooking) and light-sensitive — limitations that make formulation challenging for heat-processed foods. Used in yoghurt, dairy desserts, ice cream, confectionery, fruit preparations and beverages. Safe and naturally derived; no synthetic dyes or petroleum derivatives involved. A benign, commonly observed phenomenon: eating significant amounts of beetroot can cause beeturia (red urine) and red stools in some individuals — this is a harmless metabolic variation (partial metabolism of betalains), not evidence of toxicity. No ADI needed (JECFA) — considered safe. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E162), FDA (GRAS for certain applications) and CODEX. An antioxidant in its own right — betanin has free radical scavenging activity.""",
    'e163': 'E163/INS 163 is anthocyanins, the natural blue-violet-red pigments found in berries, red cabbage, grapes and hibiscus flowers. Approved by FSSAI and EU as natural food colorants with antioxidant properties.',
    'anthocyanins': """Anthocyanins (E163/INS 163) are a family of over 700 natural water-soluble flavonoid pigments responsible for the red, purple and blue colours of berries (blueberries, strawberries, blackcurrants, elderberries), red grapes, red cabbage, purple corn, hibiscus and many other plants. Chemically: glycosides of anthocyanidins (the common aglycones being cyanidin, pelargonidin, delphinidin, malvidin, peonidin and petunidin). Colour is strongly pH-dependent: red at pH <3, purple/mauve at neutral, blue/green at alkaline pH — this limits their stability in alkaline foods and at neutral pH. Commercial sources: grape skin extract (from wine by-products), elderberry, red cabbage, black carrot and purple sweet potato. Used in beverages, fruit preparations, yoghurt, confectionery, jams and dairy products. Functional beyond colour: extensively studied antioxidants with free radical scavenging, anti-inflammatory, cardiovascular-protective and potential neuroprotective effects; epidemiological studies link higher dietary anthocyanin intake with lower cardiovascular disease risk and better cognitive function with aging. No safety concerns at food use or supplement doses — no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E163), FDA (GRAS for natural fruit/vegetable extracts) and CODEX. Natural, plant-derived and generally considered highly safe.""",
    'e172': 'E172/INS 172 is iron oxides and hydroxides, naturally occurring mineral pigments in red, yellow and black forms. Approved by FDA and EU as safe colorants in cosmetics, food colouring and pharmaceutical tablet coatings.',
    # Sorbates
    'sorbic acid': """Sorbic acid (E200/INS 200 / (2E,4E)-hexa-2,4-dienoic acid) is a naturally occurring polyunsaturated six-carbon fatty acid first isolated in 1859 from the unripe berries of the rowan tree (Sorbus aucuparia) by A.W. von Hofmann. It is the parent compound of the sorbate family of preservatives (potassium sorbate E202, sodium sorbate E201, calcium sorbate E203) and is the most widely used antimicrobial food preservative globally by volume. Chemical nature: a trans,trans-conjugated diene carboxylic acid — the diene structure is key to its antimicrobial activity and biological metabolism. Mechanism: the undissociated free acid (predominant below pH 6.5) penetrates microbial cell membranes, where it inhibits key fungal and bacterial enzymes — particularly fumarase, succinate dehydrogenase and SH-containing enzyme systems in the electron transport chain — disrupting energy metabolism. Highly effective against moulds and yeasts; less so against bacteria (especially gram-negative). Does not affect human cells at food use concentrations. Applications: cheese (surface treatment and incorporation — inhibits surface mould), bread and bakery products, dried fruits, wines and beverages, fruit products, condiments and margarines. Typical use level: 0.05–0.2% (as sorbic acid equivalents). Metabolism: sorbic acid is metabolised by normal fatty acid beta-oxidation pathways — broken down to CO₂ and water; it is effectively just a short unsaturated fatty acid from a metabolic standpoint. No accumulation; no mutagenicity; no carcinogenicity at food use levels. Very low potential for sensitisation compared to benzoates. Rare cases of contact dermatitis in occupational settings (bakers) have been reported. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). FDA GRAS; approved by FSSAI, EU (E200) and CODEX. One of the safest food preservatives available.""",
    'e200': 'E200/INS 200 is sorbic acid, the most widely used mould inhibitor in food products. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in cheeses, baked goods, dried fruits and beverages at specified limits.',
    'sodium sorbate': """The sodium salt of sorbic acid (E201/INS 201). While potassium sorbate (E202) dominates commercial use due to better water solubility and stability, sodium sorbate is used when the sodium form is preferred for formulation or cost reasons. Chemically identical mode of action to sorbic acid: the undissociated free acid that forms in mildly acidic conditions (pH <6.5) inhibits mould and yeast enzymes, preventing microbial growth. Used in cheese coatings, beverages, baked goods and fruit products. Rapidly metabolised by normal fatty acid beta-oxidation — no accumulation in body tissues. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day (as sorbic acid equivalents, JECFA). CODEX approved. Approved by FSSAI at category-specific limits. Considered one of the safest antimicrobial preservatives available. Those following low-sodium diets should note sodium content (approx. 14% by weight), though at typical use levels (0.05–0.2% in food) sodium contribution is negligible. No carcinogenicity, genotoxicity or reproductive toxicity concerns established at food-use concentrations.""",
    'e201': 'E201/INS 201 is sodium sorbate, a sorbic acid salt used as a mould and yeast inhibitor in food products. CODEX approved at specified limits.',
    # Benzoates
    'benzoic acid': """Benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH / E210/INS 210) is a simple aromatic carboxylic acid found naturally at low concentrations in cranberries (~300 mg/kg), prunes, cinnamon, cloves and some fermented foods such as yoghurt and cheese. Commercially produced by toluene oxidation (liquid-phase catalytic oxidation with air or by Dow's decarboxylation of phthalic anhydride). The parent compound of the benzoate preservative family (E210–E213). Antimicrobial mechanism: the undissociated benzoic acid molecule (predominant below pH 4.5; pKa = 4.2) readily permeates the lipid bilayer of microbial cell membranes; once inside the neutral-pH cytoplasm it dissociates, acidifying the cell interior. This forces the microorganism to expend ATP driving proton-pumping ATPases to maintain intracellular pH homeostasis, depleting cellular energy and inhibiting growth. Most effective against yeasts and moulds in acidic products (soft drinks, fruit juices, pickles, sauces, jams). Applications: carbonated soft drinks, squashes, fruit juices, marinated fish (bokkoms), pickles, soy sauce and condiments. Critical safety concern: in beverages containing both benzoic acid and ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) under acidic conditions and heat, traces of benzene (a known human carcinogen, IARC Group 1) can form via decarboxylation driven by transition metal catalysis. Several countries have detected benzene in soft drinks above WHO drinking water guideline (10 µg/L). Manufacturers have addressed this by replacing benzoic acid with sorbate or reducing ascorbate in such formulations. Health: some people with aspirin hypersensitivity (cross-reactive with salicylates) experience reactions; benzoate sensitivity is reported in chronic urticaria patients. Involved in the Southampton additive mix linked to hyperactivity in children. Metabolised to hippuric acid (benzoylglycine) and excreted in urine; depletes glycine in genetically predisposed individuals (urea cycle disorders). ADI: 0–5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E210), FDA and CODEX approved at category-specific limits.""",
    'e210': 'E210/INS 210 is benzoic acid, a food preservative effective in acidic products. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Can form trace benzene with ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) in acidic beverages — a concern at high concentrations.',
    'calcium benzoate': """The calcium salt of benzoic acid (E213/INS 213), composed of two benzoate anions per calcium cation. Used as an antimicrobial preservative in acidic foods (jams, beverages, sauces, pickles) at concentrations typically ≤0.1%. Mechanism: undissociated benzoic acid (which predominates at low pH < 4.5) diffuses across microbial membranes and disrupts intracellular pH homeostasis, inhibiting bacteria, yeast and mould. Effective only in acidic environments — inactive near neutral pH. Like sodium benzoate, it can react with ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) in acidic beverages to form trace amounts of benzene (a Group 1 carcinogen). Regulatory limits exist to minimise this risk; manufacturers are advised against co-formulating high ascorbic acid with benzoates in beverages. FSSAI permits benzoates at 250–750 mg/kg in various food categories. EU and CODEX approved at specified limits. Part of the 'Southampton Six' concern group — the Southampton study (2007) linked benzoate-containing mixtures with hyperactivity in children, triggering EU mandatory advisory labels. Those with aspirin sensitivity may react to benzoates.""",
    'e213': 'E213/INS 213 is calcium benzoate, a calcium salt preservative used in acidic foods and beverages. Similar concerns as sodium benzoate at high concentrations with Vitamin C.',
    # Propionates
    'propionic acid': """Propionic acid (E280/INS 280 / propanoic acid / CH₃CH₂COOH) is a naturally occurring short-chain saturated fatty acid — the simplest carboxylic acid with three carbons — produced naturally during fermentation by Propionibacterium species in Swiss and Emmental cheese (0.5–1%), generated in the human colon by gut bacteria during dietary fibre fermentation, and present in small amounts in many fermented dairy products. Produced commercially by catalytic carbonylation of ethylene with CO and steam (Reppe process) or by fermentation using Propionibacterium. Antimicrobial mechanism: the undissociated free acid (which predominates at pH below 5.5) permeates microbial membranes, disrupts membrane potential and inhibits key metabolic enzymes including fumarate reductase and pyruvate carboxylase — halting ATP production and cell wall synthesis. Highly effective against moulds and rope-forming bacteria in bread, less effective against yeasts (allowing yeast leavening to proceed normally). Applications: primary use is in bread and bakery products (0.1–0.3% of flour weight) to prevent mould and Bacillus subtilis rope; also used in processed cheese, animal feed and as a grain preservative. Metabolism: absorbed rapidly from the gut; enters the propionate catabolism pathway (methylmalonyl-CoA → succinyl-CoA) as a normal Krebs cycle intermediate — effectively metabolised as a dietary fatty acid. Essentially non-toxic at food use levels. Provides some energy (~4.9 kcal/g). Some susceptible individuals report migraine headaches linked to propionate — the mechanism may involve histamine or tyramine interactions. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E280), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Naturally present in human biology.""",
    'e280': 'E280/INS 280 is propionic acid, a naturally occurring short-chain fatty acid used as a mould inhibitor in bread. Naturally present in some fermented dairy products. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'sodium propionate': """Sodium propionate (E281/INS 281) is the sodium salt of propionic acid, a short-chain saturated fatty acid that occurs naturally in Swiss cheese (up to 1% by weight) as a fermentation product of Propionibacterium freudenreichii, and in small amounts in the human gut as a product of dietary fibre fermentation. Produced commercially by neutralising propionic acid with sodium hydroxide or sodium carbonate. Mechanism of antimicrobial action: propionic acid (the undissociated free acid, which predominates at pH below ~5.5) enters fungal and bacterial cells, disrupts membrane transport systems, acidifies the intracellular pH and inhibits key metabolic enzymes including fumarase and beta-oxidation — effectively halting growth of bread moulds (Rhizopus, Aspergillus, Penicillium) and rope-causing bacteria (Bacillus subtilis / Bacillus mesentericus). Does not inhibit yeast at food use concentrations, so does not affect yeast-leavened bread rising. Typical bread use level: 0.1–0.3% of flour weight. Used in: bread, buns, rolls, flour tortillas, crumpets, cake mixes, some pastries and some dairy products. Metabolised via the methylmalonyl-CoA pathway to succinyl-CoA — a normal intermediate in the Krebs cycle. No accumulation; effectively a normal dietary fatty acid at food use concentrations. Provides dietary sodium — relevant for hypertensive individuals at high intake levels, though concentrations in bread are modest. Some case reports and small studies suggest propionate may trigger migraine headaches in susceptible individuals; mechanism may involve histamine release. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E281), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX at specified limits.""",
    'e281': 'E281/INS 281 is sodium propionate, a bread preservative preventing mould growth. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX at specified limits in bakery products.',
    'calcium propionate': """The calcium salt of propionic acid (E282/INS 282) and the world's most widely used bread preservative. Produced by neutralising propionic acid with calcium hydroxide. A white, free-flowing powder freely soluble in water. Mechanism: propionic acid (produced when calcium propionate dissociates in the slightly acidic dough environment) inhibits fungal growth by disrupting membrane transport and enzymatic activity — particularly effective against bread mould (Rhizopus, Aspergillus) and the ropy bacteria Bacillus mesentericus. Effective at pH 4.5–6.5, ideal for bread. Typical use level: 0.1–0.3% of flour weight. Preferred over sodium propionate where sodium reduction is desirable, and also provides a small dietary calcium supplement. FSSAI, EU (E282), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. A small number of case reports and double-blind studies suggest calcium propionate may trigger migraine in susceptible individuals. Some animal studies at high doses showed behavioural effects but these are not replicated at food use levels. Workers in bakeries with high airborne propionate dust exposure may experience occupational contact dermatitis.""",
    'e282': 'E282/INS 282 is calcium propionate, the most widely used preservative in bread and baked goods to prevent mould. FSSAI, EU and FDA approved at specified limits.',
    # Antioxidants
    'sodium ascorbate': 'The sodium salt of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C / E301/INS 301), used as an antioxidant preservative and a more stable, less acidic form of Vitamin C in food and pharmaceutical products.',
    'e301': 'E301/INS 301 is sodium ascorbate (Vitamin C sodium salt), an antioxidant preservative and Vitamin C source approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'calcium ascorbate': 'The calcium salt of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C / E302/INS 302), providing both antioxidant protection and dietary calcium. Used as a food additive and dietary supplement.',
    'e302': 'E302/INS 302 is calcium ascorbate (Vitamin C calcium salt), a buffered, non-acidic antioxidant and calcium supplement approved by FSSAI and CODEX.',
    'ascorbyl palmitate': """A fat-soluble ester of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) and palmitic acid (E304/INS 304), produced by esterification of the 6-hydroxyl of ascorbic acid with palmitic acid. Unlike water-soluble vitamin C, ascorbyl palmitate dissolves in oils and fats, allowing it to protect lipid-based foods from oxidative rancidity. Mechanism: locates at oil-water interfaces; the ascorbate moiety donates electrons to lipid peroxy radicals and quenches singlet oxygen, halting the oxidative chain. Used in cooking oils, margarines, infant formula, bakery products and powdered soups at typical levels of 50–200 mg/kg. Also used in cosmetic products as an oil-phase antioxidant and skin-conditioning agent (the palmitic acid moiety is a natural skin lipid). Stability note: ascorbyl palmitate is a more stable delivery form than ascorbic acid in fatty matrices, but is itself susceptible to hydrolysis in high water activity products. Provides some but not full Vitamin C nutritional activity (partially cleaved to free ascorbic acid in the gut). No significant toxicological concerns — it is hydrolysed to ascorbic acid and palmitic acid, both normal food constituents. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E304), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; not animal-derived.""",
    'e304': 'E304/INS 304 is ascorbyl palmitate, a fat-soluble form of Vitamin C used as an antioxidant to protect oils, fats and fat-containing foods from oxidative rancidity. FSSAI, EU and CODEX approved.',
    'propyl gallate': """Propyl gallate (3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoic acid propyl ester / E310/INS 310) is a synthetic antioxidant manufactured by esterifying gallic acid (a naturally occurring plant polyphenol found in oak bark, tea and gall nuts) with n-propanol. Approved since the 1940s and one of the oldest synthetic food antioxidants still in widespread use. Mechanism: the trihydroxyphenol (galloyl) group efficiently scavenges free radicals, particularly chain-propagating peroxy radicals generated during lipid oxidation; it also chelates transition metal ions (Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺) that catalyse the Fenton reaction, providing dual antioxidant protection. Applications: used in edible fats and oils, lard, shortening, chewing gum base, snack foods, dried meat products and cosmetic lipid preparations, typically at 50–200 mg/kg. Often used in synergistic combination with BHA and/or BHT, with citric acid added as a chelating synergist. Technical limitation: propyl gallate forms a dark-blue-grey complex with iron ions — a processing concern when iron equipment or iron-containing ingredients are present. Safety: JECFA ADI of 1.4 mg/kg body weight/day. EU (E310) and FSSAI permit its use in fats and oils at specified limits. NOT permitted in infant or baby food. Some animal studies at high doses showed thyroid effects, but JECFA considers it safe at food use levels. Potential concern: hydrolysis to gallic acid and propanol in the GI tract; gallic acid itself has both antioxidant and weak oestrogenic activity. Consumers with aspirin sensitivity may occasionally react, as propyl gallate has a structure similar to salicylate-adjacent compounds.""",
    'e310': 'E310/INS 310 is propyl gallate, a synthetic antioxidant used in fats, oils and baked goods. Approved by FSSAI and CODEX at specified limits, though under review in some jurisdictions for potential safety concerns.',
    # Thickeners & Stabilisers
    'e401': 'E401/INS 401 is sodium alginate, a natural polysaccharide thickener extracted from brown seaweed. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in dairy, bakery and processed foods.',
    'e402': 'E402/INS 402 is potassium alginate, a natural seaweed-derived thickener and stabiliser approved by CODEX and EU for food use.',
    'potassium alginate': 'A natural thickener and gel-former (E402/INS 402) extracted from brown seaweed, the potassium salt of alginic acid. Used in food and pharmaceutical applications.',
    'e404': 'E404/INS 404 is calcium alginate, a calcium salt of alginic acid extracted from brown seaweed. Used as a thickener, stabiliser and gelling agent in processed foods. CODEX and EU approved.',
    'calcium alginate': 'The calcium salt of alginic acid (E404/INS 404), extracted from brown seaweed. Forms a firm gel in the presence of calcium ions. Used in restructured foods and as an encapsulation material.',
    'agar': """Agar (E406/INS 406 / agar-agar) is a natural polysaccharide extracted from the cell walls of red algae (primarily Gelidium, Gracilaria and Pterocladia species) by hot water extraction and freeze-drying. A mixture of agarose (the gelling fraction — linear alternating 3,6-anhydro-L-galactose and D-galactose units) and agaropectin (the non-gelling sulfonated fraction). Unique gelling properties: agar gels form at 32–40°C and melt at 85–95°C (a 50°C temperature hysteresis), making agar gels stable at body temperature — unlike gelatine, agar does not melt in the mouth. Produces firm, brittle, clear gels at concentrations as low as 0.5%. Used for centuries in East and Southeast Asian cooking (Japanese kanten, Indonesian and Indian agar sweets, Chinese herbal jellies). As a food additive: gelling agent, thickener and stabiliser in confectionery, jellies, dairy desserts, ice cream, processed cheese, canned meat and as a vegan/vegetarian gelatine substitute. Also the standard culture medium in microbiology labs. Non-digestible dietary fibre; agar is used medicinally as a gentle bulk laxative. Very well tolerated; no significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E406), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Suitable for vegetarians and vegans. Kosher and halal.""",
    'e406': 'E406/INS 406 is agar, a natural red algae-derived gelling agent and vegetarian alternative to gelatine. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in desserts, confectionery and dairy products.',
    'locust bean gum': """Locust bean gum (LBG / carob bean gum / E410/INS 410) is extracted from the endosperm of carob tree (Ceratonia siliqua) seeds. The carob tree is an evergreen native to the Mediterranean basin; its seed pods have been used as food for over 4,000 years. LBG is a galactomannan polysaccharide — a backbone of mannose units with galactose side chains at a mannose:galactose ratio of approximately 4:1. The lower galactose substitution (compared to guar gum, ratio ~2:1) means LBG does not fully hydrate in cold water; heating is required for full viscosity development. LBG forms synergistic gels with kappa-carrageenan and xanthan gum: the galactose-free mannose segments of LBG interdigitate with the helical structure of carrageenan/xanthan, producing a gel at concentrations where neither alone would gel. Widely used in: ice cream (controls ice crystal growth; prevents coarse icy texture on thawing), cheese (controls syneresis), processed meat (moisture retention), infant formula and salad dressings. Non-digestible soluble dietary fibre — slowly fermented by colonic bacteria; prebiotic effect. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E410), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups including infants. Vegan.""",
    'carob bean gum': """Carob bean gum (E410/INS 410) is an alternative name for locust bean gum, derived from the dried and powdered endosperm of carob tree (Ceratonia siliqua) seeds. A galactomannan polysaccharide with a mannose:galactose ratio of ~4:1, giving it intermediate thickening properties between guar gum and konjac glucomannan. Requires heating (75–85°C) for full solubilisation and viscosity development, unlike guar gum which hydrates in cold water. Key application: synergistic gelling with xanthan gum and carrageenan — widely exploited in ice cream to control ice crystal formation, prevent graininess and improve meltdown resistance. Also used in cheese, processed meats, infant formula, sauces and bakery products. A non-digestible dietary fibre fermented by colonic microbiota — contributes to prebiotic effects. Safe for infants (widely used in infant formula to thicken formula for reflux management). No significant allergen concerns. No ADI needed (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E410), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e410': 'E410/INS 410 is locust bean gum (carob bean gum), a natural thickener from carob seeds. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in dairy products, sauces and processed meats.',
    'gum arabic': """Gum arabic (E414/INS 414 / acacia gum) is a complex natural polysaccharide exudate collected from the trunks and branches of Acacia senegal and Acacia seyal trees in the Sahel region of sub-Saharan Africa (Sudan, Chad, Nigeria, Senegal), where it forms as a natural protection against mechanical injury. One of the oldest food additives used by humans — records of its use in ancient Egypt date back 4,000 years. Chemically it is a highly branched arabinogalactan (a polysaccharide of D-galactose, L-arabinose, D-glucuronic acid and L-rhamnose), with some arabinogalactan-protein conjugates that contribute to its unique emulsifying properties unusual for a polysaccharide. Food functions: emulsifier (stabilises flavour-oil emulsions in beverages), glazing agent (hard confectionery coating), film former (sugar panning), encapsulant (spray-dried flavours), and a low-viscosity thickener. Also a soluble dietary fibre / prebiotic — fermented by colonic bacteria to produce short-chain fatty acids (butyrate, propionate, acetate). Prebiotic trials show gum arabic feeding at 10 g/day increases Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus populations and reduces BMI in obese individuals. No known allergenic potential except for rare occupational asthma in production workers. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E414), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe and highly beneficial dietary fibre.""",
    'acacia': 'Gum arabic from acacia trees (E414/INS 414), a natural water-soluble gum used as a thickener, emulsifier, encapsulating agent and prebiotic fibre in confectionery, beverages and food supplements.',
    'e414': 'E414/INS 414 is gum arabic (acacia gum), a natural thickener, stabiliser and encapsulant from acacia trees. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Also a prebiotic dietary fibre with fermentation studies showing gut health benefits.',
    'gellan gum': 'A natural microbial polysaccharide (E418/INS 418) produced by the bacterium Sphingomonas elodea. Used as a thickener, gelling agent and suspension stabiliser in beverages, desserts and dairy products.',
    'e418': 'E418/INS 418 is gellan gum, a fermentation-derived thickener and gelling agent. Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA for use in food products including beverages and dairy.',
    'pectin': 'A natural structural polysaccharide (E440/INS 440) found in the cell walls of fruits, especially citrus peel and apple pomace. The most widely used natural gelling agent in jams, jellies and confectionery. Also a soluble dietary fibre with prebiotic properties.',
    'e440': 'E440/INS 440 is pectin, a natural fruit-derived thickener and gelling agent used in jams, jellies, yoghurts and confectionery. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. A natural dietary fibre with no known health concerns.',
    'microcrystalline cellulose': 'Refined, purified cellulose (E460/INS 460) produced from wood pulp or cotton by partial acid hydrolysis. Used as a texturiser, anticaking agent, binder in tablets and as a fat replacer in reduced-calorie foods.',
    'e460': 'E460/INS 460 is microcrystalline cellulose (MCC), a refined cellulose used as a texturiser, bulking agent and tablet binder. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Safe, indigestible structural carbohydrate.',
    'methyl cellulose': 'A modified cellulose (E461/INS 461) that forms a gel when heated and dissolves when cooled — the opposite of most gels. Used as a thickener, emulsifier and film-forming agent in food and pharmaceuticals.',
    'e461': 'E461/INS 461 is methyl cellulose, a thermoreversible gelling agent and thickener approved by EU and CODEX. Used in dietary supplements and food products.',
    'hydroxypropyl methylcellulose': 'A cellulose ether (E464/INS 464) used as a film-forming agent, thickener and vegetarian capsule material in pharmaceuticals and food products.',
    'e464': 'E464/INS 464 is hydroxypropyl methylcellulose (HPMC), used as a thickener and film-forming agent in food, and as a vegetarian/vegan capsule shell in supplements.',
    'cmc': """Carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC / sodium carboxymethyl cellulose / cellulose gum / E466/INS 466) is a water-soluble semi-synthetic cellulose ether produced by reacting cellulose (from wood pulp or cotton linters) with sodium monochloroacetate in the presence of sodium hydroxide (alkali activation). The chloroacetate groups react with the free hydroxyl groups on the cellulose backbone, substituting carboxymethyl (-CH₂COO⁻Na⁺) groups at position C2, C3 or C6. The degree of substitution (DS) — average number of hydroxyl groups per glucose unit substituted, typically 0.6–0.9 — determines solubility and viscosity characteristics. CMC solutions are pseudo-plastic (shear-thinning). Applications: ice cream and frozen desserts — controls ice crystal size, improves body, prevents whey separation on thawing; dairy products — stabilises protein particles in chocolate milk; beverages — stabilises juices and reduces sedimentation; gluten-free bread and baked goods — acts as a network-forming agent similar to xanthan gum; salad dressings; processed meats and fish products (water-holding); pet food. Pharmaceutical: tablet binder and disintegrant; laxative (Citrucel contains methylcellulose; some CMC products used as bulk laxatives). Cosmetics: thickener in lotions, toothpaste, surgical lubricants. Non-digestible — not absorbed; passes through the GI tract as dietary fibre. No caloric contribution. Fermented to some extent by colonic bacteria — produces short-chain fatty acids. Rodent studies at very high dietary proportions (5–10% of diet) showed intestinal inflammation and microbiome disruption (Chassaing et al., 2015) — but these concentrations far exceed food-additive exposure levels for humans. At normal food use concentrations, no adverse effects in human studies. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E466), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'carboxymethyl cellulose': """Sodium carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC / E466/INS 466) — see the 'cmc' entry for full scientific description. A semi-synthetic, water-soluble cellulose derivative (cellulose gum) used universally as a thickener, stabiliser, water-binding agent and ice crystal controller in ice cream, frozen desserts, dairy products, beverages, baked goods and pharmaceutical preparations. Non-digestible dietary fibre; not absorbed. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E466), FSSAI and CODEX approved. Safe at food use levels.""",
    'sodium carboxymethyl cellulose': """Sodium carboxymethyl cellulose (Na-CMC / cellulose gum / E466/INS 466) is a water-soluble anionic cellulose ether produced by reacting alkaline cellulose (from wood pulp) with sodium monochloroacetate. The degree of substitution (DS, typically 0.6–0.95 for food grades) determines viscosity and solubility. Produces highly viscous, pseudoplastic aqueous solutions (shear-thinning: viscous at rest, flows more easily under shear). Functions: thickener, stabiliser, water binder and texture modifier in a wide range of food products. Uniquely effective at preventing ice cream syneresis (water expulsion from ice cream); widely used in ice cream, frozen desserts, dairy beverages, bakery products, gluten-free bread (moisture retention), condiments, infant formula and beverages. Also a major excipient in pharmaceutical tablets and suspensions. A high-DS-CMC is used in detergents. Clinically: at doses of 5–30 g/day, CMC has laxative and cholesterol-lowering effects as a soluble fibre. A 2021 study (Viennois et al., Gastroenterology) found CMC consumed at high doses (comparable to estimated high dietary intakes) altered the gut microbiome and promoted intestinal inflammation in some individuals — this finding remains under active regulatory review and does not reflect typical food-additive exposure. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E466), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e466': 'E466/INS 466 is sodium carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC), a cellulose-derived thickener and stabiliser approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in ice cream, beverages, sauces and bakery products.',
    # Emulsifiers
    'datem': """DATEM (diacetyl tartaric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids / E472e/INS 472e) is a synthetic emulsifier produced by reacting mono- and diglycerides with diacetyl tartaric acid (itself produced from tartaric acid and acetic anhydride). An amphiphilic molecule — the fatty acid portion is lipophilic and the tartrate-acetate headgroup is hydrophilic, allowing DATEM to sit at the fat-water interface. In bread: DATEM is uniquely effective at strengthening the gluten network. It intercalates between gluten protein strands, forming hydrogen bonds and displacing gliadins to allow glutenins to form longer, stronger networks — this translates into improved dough machinability, increased loaf volume, finer crumb structure and improved moisture retention. Used at 0.2–0.5% of flour weight in commercial bread, rolls, wraps and laminated pastry. Also used in coffee creamers and margarine. FSSAI, EU (E472e), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified. The tartaric acid and acetic acid components are metabolised normally. May be derived from animal fat (tallow) or vegetable fat depending on the manufacturer — relevant for vegan and halal considerations. Generally considered safe; no significant adverse effects at food use levels.""",
    'e472e': 'E472e/INS 472e is DATEM, a synthetic emulsifier used in bread and baked goods to strengthen gluten and improve loaf volume. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA and CODEX at specified limits.',
    'sodium stearoyl lactylate': """Sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL / E481/INS 481) is an anionic emulsifier produced by esterification of stearic acid (a C18 saturated fatty acid) with lactic acid — typically as a lactyl lactate oligomer — then neutralised with sodium hydroxide. A versatile dough conditioner and emulsifier for the baking industry. In bread: SSL interacts with both gluten proteins (strengthening the gluten network) and starch (forming inclusion complexes with amylose to retard staling). This dual action improves loaf volume, crumb fineness, dough machinability and shelf life. In non-dairy whipped toppings and coffee creamers: acts as an aerating and fat-structuring emulsifier. In pasta and noodles: improves texture. Typical use in bread: 0.1–0.5% of flour weight. Metabolised to stearic acid and lactic acid — both normal dietary constituents. Stearic acid is a neutral saturated fat with minimal LDL-raising effect. Lactic acid is a normal metabolite. FSSAI, EU (E481), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified (JECFA considers it safe). May be derived from animal (tallow) or vegetable fat — relevant for vegan, vegetarian and halal consumers (animal-derived versions exist). Generally very well tolerated; no known sensitisation.""",
    'ssl': """SSL (sodium stearoyl lactylate / E481/INS 481) is a widely used bread and baked goods emulsifier produced by reacting stearic acid with lactic acid and neutralising with sodium. Used at 0.1–0.5% of flour weight. Functions as a dough strengthener — it forms a lipid-protein complex with gluten that increases elasticity and gas retention, resulting in improved loaf volume. Also complexes with amylose starch to slow staling. Used in industrial bread, burger buns, tortillas, pastries and some dairy-free whipped products. Metabolised to stearic acid and lactic acid — both normal food components. Safe for diabetics. May be animal-fat or vegetable-fat derived; vegans and those observing halal or kosher dietary requirements should check source. FSSAI, EU (E481), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified. Very well studied and considered safe at all normal food use levels.""",
    'e481': 'E481/INS 481 is sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL), an emulsifier and dough conditioner used in bread, pastry and other baked goods. Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA at specified limits.',
    'calcium stearoyl lactylate': 'A calcium-based emulsifier (E482/INS 482) similar to sodium stearoyl lactylate, produced by reacting stearic acid with lactic acid. Used as a dough conditioner and emulsifier in bakery products.',
    'e482': 'E482/INS 482 is calcium stearoyl lactylate (CSL), an emulsifier used in baked goods. Approved by EU and CODEX at specified limits.',
    # Sweeteners
    'mannitol': 'A naturally occurring sugar alcohol (E421/INS 421) found in mushrooms, seaweed and various fruits. Used as a low-calorie sweetener, bulking agent and anticaking agent. Has a tooth-friendly property as it is not fermented by oral bacteria.',
    'e421': 'E421/INS 421 is mannitol, a natural sugar alcohol from mushrooms and fruits. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX as a sweetener and bulking agent. Laxative effect at doses above 20g/day.',
    'isomalt': """Isomalt (E953/INS 953 / palatinit) is a sugar alcohol produced from sucrose in a two-step industrial process: first, sucrose isomerase rearranges sucrose to isomaltulose (palatinose — glucose linked to fructose differently than in sucrose), then catalytic hydrogenation reduces the isomaltulose to isomalt. The final product is a mixture of approximately equal parts alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-1,6-sorbitol (GPS) and alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-1,6-mannitol (GPM). Sweetness: approximately 45–65% that of sucrose. Caloric value: 2 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g for sucrose). Key property — very low hygroscopicity: unlike sorbitol, isomalt absorbs almost no moisture from ambient air, making it ideal for hard boiled candies, panned confectionery and sugar sculptures that stay clear and non-sticky without special humidity control. Low glycaemic index — suitable for diabetics. Dental friendly — poorly metabolised by Streptococcus mutans. Digestive tolerance: laxative threshold approximately 25–30 g/day; above this, osmotic water retention in the colon causes flatulence, bloating and diarrhoea. EU mandatory labelling: "Excessive consumption may have a laxative effect." ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E953), FDA and CODEX approved.""",
    'e953': 'E953/INS 953 is isomalt, a sucrose-derived sugar alcohol used in sugar-free confectionery, chocolates and chewing gum. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Laxative effect at high doses.',
    'steviol glycosides': """Steviol glycosides (E960/INS 960) are the intensely sweet compounds extracted from leaves of the stevia plant (Stevia rebaudiana Bertoni), a perennial herb native to Paraguay and Brazil. Key glycosides include rebaudioside A (Reb A, ~200–250× sweeter than sucrose — most commonly used), stevioside (~100–150×, with slight bitterness), and high-purity Reb M and D (up to 350× sweetness, cleanest flavour). They pass through the small intestine unabsorbed; colonic bacteria cleave them to steviol aglycone, which is absorbed and excreted in urine as steviol glucuronide — zero caloric contribution. Does not raise blood glucose or insulin — validated for use by diabetics and in weight management. Produced commercially by hot-water extraction and purification from dried stevia leaves. Extensive safety data: JECFA/EFSA ADI of 4 mg/kg body weight/day as steviol equivalents; no carcinogenicity, genotoxicity or reproductive toxicity. FSSAI, EU (E960), FDA (GRAS for certain glycosides) and CODEX approved. Minor aftertaste (liquorice or bitter note) in some products; blending with erythritol masks this. Not the same as unprocessed stevia leaf powder (which is not approved under E960 regulations).""",
    'stevia': """Stevia (E960/INS 960) is the common name for the sweetener derived from Stevia rebaudiana leaves — specifically its purified steviol glycoside extracts. The stevia plant has been used by the Guaraní people of South America for centuries as a sweetener. The sweetening compounds (primarily rebaudioside A and stevioside) are 200–350 times sweeter than sucrose. Zero caloric value — steviol glycosides are not metabolised by human digestive enzymes; they pass to the colon where bacteria cleave them to steviol, which is absorbed and excreted unchanged in urine as a glucuronide conjugate. No effect on blood glucose or insulin secretion — genuinely suitable for diabetics and those following low-carbohydrate diets. Safety: the ADI is 4 mg/kg body weight/day as steviol equivalents (JECFA). Over two decades of food safety studies confirm no carcinogenicity, genotoxicity or reproductive/developmental toxicity at human food consumption levels. FSSAI permits steviol glycosides in India; EU (E960) and FDA (GRAS) approved. Slight aftertaste (bitter or liquorice note) in some formulations, especially at high concentrations or with stevioside-dominant extracts — commercial products often blend with erythritol or other sweeteners for a cleaner flavour.""",
    'e960': 'E960/INS 960 is steviol glycosides (stevia extract), a natural zero-calorie sweetener approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA and CODEX. 200-350 times sweeter than sugar with no glycaemic impact.',
    'erythritol': """A sugar alcohol (E968/INS 968) produced commercially by fermenting glucose (usually from corn or wheat starch) with osmophilic yeasts such as Moniliella pollinis. Occurs naturally in small amounts in grapes, pears, melons and fermented foods like sake and soy sauce. Chemically it is a four-carbon polyol (2R,3S-butane-1,2,3,4-tetraol). Because human cells lack the enzyme erythritol dehydrogenase, roughly 90% is absorbed in the small intestine and excreted unchanged in urine — this is why it causes virtually no digestive distress unlike sorbitol or mannitol. Provides about 0.2 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g for sugar). 60–70% as sweet as sucrose. Does not raise blood glucose or insulin — suitable for diabetics and keto diets. Does not feed oral bacteria — genuinely tooth-friendly. A 2023 observational study (NMR metabolomics) linked high blood erythritol to elevated cardiovascular risk, but the relationship may be confounded as the body also endogenously produces erythritol from glucose via the pentose phosphate pathway. Regulatory status: FSSAI permitted; EU approved (E968, 2008); FDA GRAS. ADI: not specified. Caution: very large amounts (>50g) may still cause osmotic diarrhoea in some individuals.""",
    'e968': 'E968/INS 968 is erythritol, a fermentation-derived sugar alcohol with zero calories and zero glycaemic impact. Better tolerated than other polyols. Approved by EU and CODEX.',
    'xylitol': 'A naturally occurring sugar alcohol (E967/INS 967) found in birch wood, plums, strawberries and many vegetables. Widely used as a tooth-friendly sweetener in chewing gum and dental products. About the same sweetness as sugar but with 40% fewer calories. Highly toxic to dogs.',
    'e967': 'E967/INS 967 is xylitol, a natural sugar alcohol sweetener with dental health benefits (prevents cavity-causing bacteria). Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA. Laxative at high doses. Toxic to dogs.',
    'neotame': """A synthetic high-intensity sweetener (E961/INS 961) derived from aspartame by adding a 3,3-dimethylbutyl group to the nitrogen of the aspartyl moiety. This structural modification makes it 7,000–13,000 times sweeter than sucrose — the most potent approved sweetener globally — and critically prevents significant hydrolysis to phenylalanine, so it does not require the mandatory PKU (phenylketonuria) warning that aspartame carries. Metabolism: rapidly absorbed, metabolised and excreted as neotame-N-monocarboxylic acid and minor metabolites; does not accumulate. Used in very small quantities (mg per kg food) in beverages, baked goods, chewing gum, dairy products and tabletop sweeteners. Zero calories, zero glycaemic impact. Approved by FDA (2002), EU (E961, 2010), CODEX and FSSAI. ADI set by JECFA at 2 mg/kg body weight/day — easily met since effective use concentrations are so low. No significant adverse effects identified in extensive animal toxicology. A suitable option for diabetics, keto dieters and PKU patients.""",
    'e961': 'E961/INS 961 is neotame, an ultra-high intensity artificial sweetener approved by FDA (2002), EU (2010) and CODEX. Unlike aspartame, it does not require PKU warnings.',
    # Anticaking & Mineral Salts
    'magnesium carbonate': """Magnesium carbonate (E504/INS 504) occurs naturally as the mineral magnesite (MgCO₃) and is also produced synthetically by reaction of magnesium salt solutions with sodium carbonate. Two common food-grade forms: magnesium carbonate (E504i) and magnesium hydrogen carbonate (E504ii). White, light powder with good moisture-absorbing properties. Functions in food: anti-caking agent (prevents clumping in table salt, confectionery powders, baking powder and dried food mixes); acidity regulator (weak alkalinity buffering); colour retention agent in some applications. As a dietary magnesium supplement: provides approximately 29% elemental magnesium by weight; moderate bioavailability compared to organic magnesium salts; converts to magnesium chloride in the stomach acid, releasing CO₂ (mild effervescence). Mild antacid properties. Athletes and gymnasts use chalk (a form of magnesium carbonate) on their hands to improve grip — not a food use, but indicates its inert nature. Magnesium is an essential mineral; deficiency is common in populations with low intake of whole grains, nuts and leafy greens. The anti-caking function contributes minor but real dietary magnesium. No genotoxicity or carcinogenicity concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E504), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e504': 'E504/INS 504 is magnesium carbonate, a natural mineral used as an anticaking agent and dietary magnesium supplement. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'potassium chloride': """Potassium chloride (KCl / E508/INS 508) is an ionic mineral salt consisting of potassium (K⁺) and chloride (Cl⁻) ions — structurally identical to sodium chloride (NaCl) but with potassium replacing sodium. Found naturally in seawater, mineral deposits (sylvite, carnallite) and most whole foods. Commercially mined from potash deposits in Canada (Saskatchewan), Belarus, Russia and Germany. Taste profile: distinctly different from NaCl — has a characteristic slightly bitter, metallic note at higher concentrations that limits its use as a pure sodium replacement; blended at 25–50% replacement of NaCl it substantially reduces sodium content while maintaining acceptability. Food functions: (1) reduced-sodium salt substitute — used in low-sodium foods to partially replace table salt and maintain saltiness; (2) acidity regulator and pH buffer; (3) gelling agent in processed foods; (4) mineral supplement providing dietary potassium. Nutritional role: potassium is the primary intracellular cation — essential for maintaining membrane potential, muscle contraction (including cardiac muscle), nerve function, fluid balance, and blood pressure regulation. Adequate potassium intake counteracts the blood pressure-raising effect of excess sodium (potassium promotes sodium excretion via the kidney). WHO recommends ≥3,510 mg potassium per day. Most Indians are deficient in potassium. Health consideration: people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) must restrict potassium intake — impaired kidneys cannot excrete excess potassium, risking dangerous hyperkalaemia (high blood potassium — can cause fatal cardiac arrhythmias). Also those taking potassium-sparing diuretics or ACE inhibitors should consult a doctor before using potassium chloride-based salt substitutes. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E508), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e508': 'E508/INS 508 is potassium chloride, a natural mineral salt used as a sodium substitute in low-sodium products and as a potassium supplement. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'calcium chloride': 'A mineral salt (E509/INS 509) used as a firming agent, anticaking agent and mineral supplement. Commonly used in cheese making (for curd firming), tofu production and as a preservative in canned vegetables.',
    'e509': 'E509/INS 509 is calcium chloride, a mineral firming agent used in cheese making, tofu production and canned vegetables. Provides dietary calcium. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'calcium sulfate': 'A naturally occurring mineral (E516/INS 516) also known as gypsum. Used as a firming agent in tofu, a calcium supplement and an acidity regulator. The primary ingredient in traditional tofu production.',
    'e516': 'E516/INS 516 is calcium sulfate (gypsum), a natural mineral used as a firming agent in tofu, bread and vegetable products. Provides dietary calcium. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'magnesium oxide': """Magnesium oxide (MgO / E530/INS 530) is produced by calcining (high-temperature burning) magnesium carbonate or magnesium hydroxide. A white, odourless, fine powder classified as a 'light' (low-density, high surface area, reactive) or 'heavy' (higher density, less reactive) grade. In food: used as an anti-caking agent in cocoa powder, table salt, confectionery powders and dried spices; also as a pH adjuster (alkalinity regulator) in some food processing applications. As a nutrient: magnesium oxide contains approximately 60% elemental magnesium by weight — the highest of any common magnesium supplement form. However, its bioavailability (absorption in the gut) is lower than magnesium citrate or magnesium glycinate due to poor solubility at physiological pH. As an antacid: reacts with gastric acid to form magnesium chloride, providing acid neutralisation (used in some antacid formulations). High doses have a laxative effect (osmotic laxative — draws water into the colon). Magnesium is an essential mineral required for over 300 enzyme reactions, muscle function and bone health; RDA is 310–420 mg/day. At food additive use levels, contribution to dietary magnesium is modest. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E530), FDA and CODEX. Generally safe; very high doses may cause diarrhoea.""",
    'e530': 'E530/INS 530 is magnesium oxide, a mineral used as an anticaking agent and magnesium supplement in food products. Approved by EU and CODEX.',
    'e551': 'E551/INS 551 is silicon dioxide, a naturally occurring mineral used as an anticaking agent in powdered foods, spices and salt. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Amorphous form is safe; crystalline silica (from different sources) is the occupational hazard.',
    # Flavour Enhancers (additional)
    'glutamic acid': """Glutamic acid (L-glutamic acid / E620 / INS 620) is a non-essential dicarboxylic amino acid and the most abundant excitatory neurotransmitter in the mammalian central nervous system, as well as the amino acid responsible for the umami (savoury) fifth basic taste. It is the free acid form of glutamate — at physiological pH (7.4), it exists predominantly as the glutamate anion, which is what binds to umami taste receptors (TAS1R1/TAS1R3 heterodimer and mGluR4). As a food additive (E620/INS 620), glutamic acid is the sodium-free form of MSG — it provides identical umami flavour without contributing sodium. Dietary sources: glutamic acid occurs naturally as a free (flavour-active) form in aged cheeses (Parmesan, ~1,200 mg/100g free glutamate), soy sauce (~1,700 mg/100g), miso paste, dried mushrooms (shiitake contain glutamate + guanylate synergy), tomatoes (ripe fresh tomatoes ~140 mg/100g), anchovies, bonito flakes (katsuobushi), and fermented fish sauces — the basis of umami in traditional cuisines globally. Produced commercially by microbial fermentation using Corynebacterium glutamicum (same process as MSG). Unlike MSG (monosodium glutamate), free glutamic acid does not contribute sodium — relevant for sodium-reduction formulations. Safety: glutamate is a naturally occurring amino acid that the body synthesises and metabolises normally. JECFA established an ADI of "not specified" — meaning no restriction needed at food use concentrations. The widely discussed "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" (headaches, flushing after consuming MSG-heavy food) has not been consistently reproducible in double-blind studies; regulatory agencies including EFSA, FDA and WHO consider MSG/glutamate safe. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E620), FDA and CODEX with quantitative limits in some categories.""",
    'e620': 'E620/INS 620 is L-glutamic acid, the free amino acid form of MSG naturally found in high-protein fermented foods. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX at specified limits. Same general safety profile as MSG.',
    'disodium ribonucleotides': """Disodium 5'-ribonucleotides (E635/INS 635) is a commercially blended flavour enhancer — a 1:1 mixture of disodium guanylate (E627/GMP) and disodium inosinate (E631/IMP). Both nucleotides work by positive allosteric modulation of the umami taste receptor TAS1R1/TAS1R3 in the presence of glutamate (MSG), amplifying its effect 7–15 fold. This synergistic combination allows manufacturers to achieve intense umami flavour while using significantly less MSG. Typical use level: 0.01–0.1% in the final product. Found in virtually all commercially flavoured crisps, instant noodles, bouillon cubes, savoury seasonings and processed snacks in India. FSSAI and EU (EC Regulation 1333/2008) permit up to 500 mg/kg in most processed food categories. Safety: ADI "not specified" (JECFA). Critical caution: both IMP and GMP are purines — metabolised to uric acid. People with gout, hyperuricaemia or urate kidney stones should strictly limit intake of nucleotide-containing foods and additives. Not recommended for infants and young children (immature purine metabolism). May not be vegetarian/vegan if derived from animal sources (fish, meat) — fermentation-derived grades are available but rarely disclosed on labels.""",
    'e635': 'E635/INS 635 is Disodium 5\'-ribonucleotides, a combined nucleotide flavour enhancer (E627 + E631 in 1:1 ratio) used in crisps, instant noodles and processed savoury foods. FSSAI (India) and EU limit use to 500 mg/kg in most processed food categories. Raises uric acid — avoid if you have gout or hyperuricaemia. Not recommended for infants and young children. Those with MSG sensitivity may also react.',
    # Modified Starches
    'modified starch': 'Starch that has been physically, enzymatically or chemically treated to alter its properties — including freeze-thaw stability, thickening power, clarity and resistance to heat and acid. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX at quantum satis in most food categories.',
    'acetylated distarch adipate': """Acetylated distarch adipate (E1422/INS 1422) is a dual-chemically modified food starch produced by treating native starch (typically corn, waxy corn, potato or tapioca) with two modifying agents: (1) adipic anhydride (or adipic acid-acetic anhydride mixed anhydride) — which introduces adipate diester crosslinks between adjacent starch chains, similar to but distinct from phosphate crosslinks in E1412; the adipate bridges are more flexible than phosphate bridges, providing shear stability while maintaining softer gel texture; (2) acetic anhydride (acetylation) — which introduces acetyl ester groups on the starch hydroxyl groups, reducing retrogradation and improving freeze-thaw stability. The combination of adipate crosslinking (stability against heat, acid and mechanical shear) and acetylation (freeze-thaw stability, reduced gelling) gives E1422 a versatile functional profile suitable for products requiring both processing stability and cold-storage performance. Applications: frozen foods requiring sauce stability through freeze-thaw cycles; ambient-temperature sauces and gravies requiring heat stability; acidic preparations (fruit yogurt, tomato-based sauces, acidic dressings) where both acid and shear stability are needed; dairy desserts and puddings; baby food formulations. Functions identically to E1442 (hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate) in many applications but with adipate crosslinking instead of phosphate crosslinking — the choice depends on processing conditions and regulatory preferences. In the body: hydrolysed by amylases to glucose, acetic acid and adipic acid — all normal dietary or metabolic components. No residual reagents at approved modification levels. EFSA reviewed in 2017 and set ADI as 'not specified' — confirming comprehensive safety. No allergen concerns (verify starch source for wheat/gluten if applicable). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1422), FDA (GRAS for specific applications) and CODEX.""",
    'e1422': 'E1422/INS 1422 is acetylated distarch adipate, a modified starch used in sauces, yoghurts, dressings and canned foods for its freeze-thaw and heat-acid stability. Approved by FSSAI, EU, CODEX and FDA.',
    'hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate': """Hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate (E1442/INS 1442) is the most widely used modified food starch globally — combining two chemical modifications for maximum functional versatility: (1) cross-linking via phosphate diester bonds between starch chains (improves heat stability, acid stability and shear resistance); (2) hydroxypropyl substitution (improves freeze-thaw stability, reduces retrogradation). This dual modification makes E1442 uniquely stable across: high-temperature processing (retorting, UHT), acidic conditions (tomato sauces, vinegar dressings), mechanical shearing (pumping, homogenisation), and freeze-thaw cycles. Used in: ketchup and tomato-based sauces, retorted baby foods, mayonnaise, salad dressings, frozen ready meals, dairy desserts, pie fillings and canned soups. The hydroxypropyl groups are not metabolised but are well tolerated; phosphate groups are hydrolysed to normal dietary phosphate. EFSA 2017 opinion: ADI 'not specified' — comprehensive safety established over decades of widespread use. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1442), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. One of the most comprehensively safety-tested food additives available.""",
    'e1442': 'E1442/INS 1442 is hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate, one of the most widely used food-grade modified starches. Approved by FSSAI, EU, CODEX and FDA. EFSA confirmed ADI "not specified" in 2017.',
    'starch sodium octenyl succinate': """Starch sodium octenyl succinate (OSA starch / E1450/INS 1450) is a lipophilically modified starch produced by reacting native starch (typically waxy maize or tapioca) with octenyl succinic anhydride (OSA). The OSA groups introduce hydrophobic octenyl chains onto the normally hydrophilic starch backbone — creating an amphiphilic molecule with both water-loving (starch) and oil-loving (octenyl chains) character. This unique combination makes OSA starch an effective Pickering emulsifier and encapsulant. Primary applications: spray-dried flavour encapsulation (protects volatile flavour oils from oxidation and evaporation during spray drying and storage), oil-in-water beverage emulsions (weighing agent with high HLB), salad dressings, powdered coffee creamers and infant formula oil emulsification. The degree of substitution (DS) is tightly regulated — maximum 3% octenyl succinate content per CODEX and JECFA. Hydrolysed in the gut to glucose and octenyl succinic acid (a normal dicarboxylic acid metabolised to CO₂ and water). EFSA 2017 opinion: ADI "not specified" — no safety concern. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1450), FDA (GRAS for specific uses including infant formula) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups including infants.""",
    'e1450': 'E1450/INS 1450 is starch sodium octenyl succinate, an emulsifying modified starch used in spray-dried flavours, powdered beverages and infant formula. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'distarch phosphate': """Distarch phosphate (E1412/INS 1412) is a cross-linked modified starch produced by treating native starch (maize, waxy maize, potato, wheat or tapioca) with phosphorus oxychloride (POCl₃) or sodium trimetaphosphate (STMP). Cross-linking introduces phosphate diester bridges between adjacent starch chains, significantly reinforcing the granular structure. Functional effects of cross-linking: (1) greatly improved resistance to heat — can withstand retorting at 121°C without excessive thinning; (2) improved acid stability — functional at pH 3–4 in fruit fillings and acidic sauces; (3) improved shear stability — withstands high-speed mixing, homogenisation and pumping; (4) reduces over-thickening ('peak viscosity clipping') and breakdown during processing. Unlike hydroxypropyl starches, distarch phosphate does not have improved freeze-thaw stability — it does retrograde and synerese on refrigeration. For freeze-thaw stability, cross-linking must be combined with hydroxypropylation (E1442). Used in: canned fruit pie fillings, retorted baby foods, acid-stable sauces, soup mixes and thickened beverages. Hydrolysed to glucose and small amounts of phosphate (normal dietary constituents). ADI: not specified (JECFA, EFSA 2017). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1412), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'e1412': 'E1412/INS 1412 is distarch phosphate, a cross-linked modified starch approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use in sauces, soups and processed foods.',
    'acetylated starch': """Acetylated starch (E1420/INS 1420 / starch acetate / monostarch acetate) is produced by treating native starch (from corn, wheat, potato or tapioca) with acetic anhydride or vinyl acetate under controlled alkaline conditions, introducing acetyl ester groups (–OCOCH₃) onto the hydroxyl groups of starch chains. The degree of substitution (DS) is tightly regulated — CODEX limits it to a maximum of 0.09 per anhydroglucose unit, ensuring a mild modification that improves functional properties without changing the digestibility of the starch significantly. Effects of acetylation: (1) reduced gelatinisation temperature — acetylated starch begins to thicken at lower temperatures than native starch; (2) reduced retrogradation (staling resistance) — the bulky acetyl groups physically prevent starch chains from re-associating and forming crystalline retrograded structures, producing pastes that remain smooth and clear during refrigeration; (3) improved paste clarity — gels are clearer than native starch pastes; (4) improved texture — softer, less brittle gels. Applications: thickener and stabiliser in salad dressings, fruit preparations, dairy desserts, bakery fillings, sauces and some Asian noodle products. Often combined with cross-linking (to produce acetylated distarch phosphate, E1414) for combined freeze-thaw stability and heat/acid resistance. In the body: hydrolysed by amylases to glucose and acetic acid (vinegar component) — completely safe. The degree of substitution approved means starch is substantially unchanged as a digestible carbohydrate. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1420), FDA (GRAS for specified applications) and CODEX.""",
    'e1420': 'E1420/INS 1420 is acetylated starch, a mildly modified food starch approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for use as a thickener and stabiliser.',
    # Antifoaming / Processing Aids
    'dimethyl polysiloxane': """Dimethylpolysiloxane (polydimethylsiloxane / PDMS / E900/INS 900) is a linear silicone polymer — alternating silicon and oxygen atoms in a chain with two methyl groups per silicon. A clear, viscous, chemically inert liquid with very low surface tension. The 'silicone' in silicone oil. Functions in food: antifoaming agent in edible frying oils, hydrogenated fats, fruit juices, soft drinks and wine — minute concentrations (2–10 mg/kg) dramatically reduce foaming during high-temperature frying and carbonated beverage processing by breaking surface tension. Used in McDonald's frying oil (famous due to the 2012 Refinery29 'McRib' article). Also used as a release agent in baking pans and as a processing aid in sugar production. Chemical inertness: PDMS does not react with food components, is not metabolised, not absorbed, and has no biological activity. Excreted unchanged in faeces. Approved maximum levels: EU and FDA permit up to 10 mg/kg in frying oils and fats; CODEX permits 10 mg/kg in edible fats and oils; some beverages permit up to 10 mg/kg. ADI: 0–1.5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA) — based on no-observed-effect levels in animal studies with very large safety margins. FSSAI, EU (E900), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. Chemically inert; no toxicological concerns at approved levels.""",
    'dimethylpolysiloxane': 'Same as dimethyl polysiloxane (E900/INS 900), a silicon-based antifoaming agent approved for use in cooking oils and some beverages at FDA GRAS and EU/CODEX permitted levels.',
    'e900': 'E900/INS 900 is dimethylpolysiloxane, a silicon antifoaming agent used in frying oils, cooking fats and some beverages. FDA GRAS; approved by EU and CODEX at ≤10mg/kg in frying oils.',
    # Acidity Regulators
    'acetic acid': """Acetic acid (CH₃COOH / E260/INS 260 / ethanoic acid) is the organic acid responsible for the characteristic sharp taste and pungent smell of vinegar. The world's most ancient food acidulant and preservative — vinegar has been used for at least 10,000 years. Commercial production: primarily by bacterial fermentation of ethanol using Acetobacter aceti or Gluconobacter species (converting wine, beer or dilute ethanol to acetic acid); also synthesised industrially by methanol carbonylation (Monsanto and Cativa processes). Natural occurrence: produced in the human body as acetyl-CoA from carbohydrate, fat and protein catabolism — acetic acid/acetate is a fundamental metabolic intermediate. Present naturally in fermented foods — wine, beer, sourdough bread, pickles and yoghurt. Food functions: (1) acidulant and pH regulator — reduces pH to inhibit microbial growth; (2) preservative — acetic acid is antimicrobial against bacteria, moulds and yeasts; particularly effective against Listeria and Salmonella; (3) flavouring — the sharp, sour note characteristic of vinegar; (4) dough conditioner in bread-making; (5) the basis of pickled foods globally (achaar in India). Typical vinegar = 4–8% acetic acid (w/v). Food additive use: glacial acetic acid (99.7%) or diluted solutions. Health: safe for the general population. Dental enamel erosion can occur with excessive direct consumption of undiluted vinegar (pH ~2.4). Some people with acid reflux or oesophageal conditions should use cautiously. Potentially beneficial effects on blood glucose and satiety have been reported in clinical studies. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA GRAS; FSSAI, EU (E260) and CODEX approved. No allergen concerns.""",
    'e260': 'E260/INS 260 is acetic acid (ethanoic acid), the active component of vinegar. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX as a preservative, acidulant and flavouring. One of the safest food acids in common use.',
    'sodium acetate': 'The sodium salt of acetic acid (E262i/INS 262i), used as an acidity regulator, preservative and mild salt-and-vinegar flavouring in bread, chips and savoury snacks.',
    'e262': 'E262/INS 262 is sodium diacetate (or acetates), an acidity regulator and mild preservative approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX in bakery and snack products.',
    'tartaric acid': 'A naturally occurring dicarboxylic acid found abundantly in grapes (E334/INS 334). One of the most common organic acids in wine, also produced industrially. Used as an acidulant in confectionery, baked goods and as the "sour" component of cream of tartar.',
    'e334': 'E334/INS 334 is L-tartaric acid, a natural grape-derived acidulant used in confectionery, baked goods and beverages. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. EFSA confirmed ADI of 30mg/kg body weight.',
    'cream of tartar': """Cream of tartar (potassium bitartrate / potassium hydrogen tartrate / E336i/INS 336i / KHC₄H₄O₆) is the acidic monobasic potassium salt of L-tartaric acid, deposited as crystalline tartar inside wine barrels during fermentation and cold-stabilisation of grape wine. Recovered from wine lees (argol), refined and milled to a fine powder. A naturally derived ingredient with no synthetic processing steps. Chemical role: with a pKa1 ~3.04, it functions as a leavening acid in baking — reacting with sodium bicarbonate to release CO₂ at oven temperature. The cream of tartar reaction is slower and more thermally activated than fast-acting leavening acids (like cream of tartar vs monocalcium phosphate), giving bakers more time to work with the batter before leavening occurs. Egg white stabilisation: adding a small amount (~1/8 tsp per 2 egg whites) before whipping lowers pH of the albumen from ~9 to ~7–8; at this lower pH, ovotransferrin denatures more readily, forming a more stable protein foam network that holds air longer and resists collapse. This is critical for meringues, soufflés, macarons and angel food cake. Sugar crystallisation inhibitor: added to fudge, fondant and candy to prevent sugar from crystallising prematurely by interfering with sucrose nucleation. Also prevents discolouration of boiled vegetables. Historical medical use: used as a purgative and to treat fever (potassium tartrate has mild osmotic laxative properties). Metabolism: tartaric acid is partially excreted unchanged in urine; remainder fermented by gut microbiota. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E336i), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. Suitable for vegetarians, vegans and gluten-free diets.""",
    'glucono delta-lactone': """Glucono delta-lactone (GDL / glucono-δ-lactone / D-glucono-1,5-lactone / E575/INS 575) is the cyclic inner ester (lactone) of D-gluconic acid — produced by controlled oxidation of glucose (from starch or glucose syrups) using glucose oxidase or by microbial fermentation, followed by dehydration. White crystalline powder with a mildly sweet initial taste that transitions to gentle sourness as it slowly hydrolyses in aqueous solution. The fundamental property that makes GDL uniquely valuable in food processing: it hydrolyses slowly and predictably in water to form D-gluconic acid, providing a gradual, controlled release of acidity over 15–90 minutes at room temperature (faster at higher temperatures). This slow, steady acidification enables precise pH control in applications where instant acidification would be harmful. Applications: (1) Tofu manufacture — the critical food use in Asia; GDL is dissolved in hot soy milk at ~80–90°C and poured into moulds; as it hydrolyses, the dropping pH gradually denatures soy proteins (particularly glycinin and beta-conglycinin), forming a uniformly smooth, silken curd; this produces silken tofu (the smooth, custard-like variety) with a more uniform, finer texture than calcium salt coagulants; (2) Cheese and dairy — slow acidification in cream cheese, fromage frais, quark, cultured dairy products; controlled acidification of milk without live cultures; (3) Fermented sausage / processed meats — GDL achieves the characteristic tang of fermented meats (salami, pepperoni) rapidly without requiring a bacterial culture starter or extended fermentation time; ensures uniform acidification throughout large-diameter sausages; (4) Leavening agent in baking — in baking powder formulations, GDL acts as a delayed-action acidulant; its gradual hydrolysis releases acid over time to react with sodium bicarbonate during resting and baking, producing CO₂ leavening with good volume and texture; (5) Acidity regulator — in canned vegetables, wines, beverages and pharmaceutical preparations. Safety: completely metabolised to gluconic acid and then via phosphogluconate pathway to CO₂ and water. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA — essentially a safe food chemical). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E575), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan and vegetarian suitable.""",
    'sodium citrate': """Sodium citrate (E331/INS 331 / trisodium citrate) is the trisodium salt of citric acid, produced by neutralising citric acid with sodium hydroxide or sodium carbonate. White crystalline powder with a mild, pleasant saline-sour taste. Multiple food functions: acidity regulator and pH buffer (maintains pH in the range 6.0–7.0 in beverages, processed cheese and pharmaceutical preparations); emulsifying salt in processed cheese — sodium citrate is one of the most important 'melting salts' in processed cheese (along with sodium phosphates and sodium tartrate); it sequesters calcium ions in casein micelles, disrupting the protein network and allowing fat to emulsify smoothly, producing the characteristic meltable, spreadable processed cheese texture; sequestrant (chelates metal ions that catalyse oxidation and rancidity); humectant in some applications. Widely used in: processed cheese slices and spreads, carbonated beverages (as a flavour enhancer and pH buffer), sports and energy drinks (provides electrolyte sodium and citrate), pharmaceutical preparations (antacid component) and dessert mixes. Sodium citrate is also used medically as an anticoagulant in blood collection tubes (chelates calcium needed for coagulation). Generally very safe — citrate is a normal metabolic intermediate (Krebs cycle). The sodium content is relevant for individuals monitoring sodium intake. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E331), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e331': 'E331/INS 331 is sodium citrate, used as an acidity regulator and emulsifying salt in processed cheese, beverages and pharmaceutical formulations. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'calcium citrate': """Calcium citrate (E333/INS 333 / tricalcium dicitrate / Ca₃(C₆H₅O₇)₂) is the calcium salt of citric acid, produced by neutralising citric acid with calcium hydroxide or calcium carbonate. One of the most important and well-studied dietary calcium supplement forms. In food: functions as a firming agent (calcium ions crosslink pectin chains in plant cell walls, maintaining firmness in canned vegetables, tomatoes, fresh-cut produce and bakery fillings), sequestrant (chelates pro-oxidant metal ions preventing discolouration and rancidity), acidity regulator and calcium fortification agent in beverages, dairy alternatives, infant formula and fortified foods. As a calcium supplement: calcium citrate stands out for its superior characteristics compared to calcium carbonate (the most common supplement form): (1) Absorption: calcium citrate is well-absorbed independently of stomach acid — absorption is approximately 27% compared to ~22% for calcium carbonate; crucially, calcium citrate does not require an acidic gastric environment for dissolution, making it the preferred form for: people taking proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) or H2 blockers (which reduce stomach acid); elderly individuals (who naturally produce less stomach acid); those who have had gastric surgery or bariatric surgery. (2) GI tolerance: calcium citrate is better tolerated than calcium carbonate — fewer reports of constipation, gas and bloating. (3) Food timing: unlike calcium carbonate (best absorbed with food), calcium citrate can be taken with or without food. (4) Kidney stone consideration: calcium citrate supplementation slightly increases urinary citrate, which inhibits calcium oxalate and uric acid stone formation — it may actually reduce kidney stone risk compared to calcium carbonate (a concern particularly for people with a history of kidney stones). Calcium content: ~21% elemental calcium by weight (lower than calcium carbonate at ~40%, meaning larger tablets are needed for equivalent dose). Typical adult supplement dose: 500–1,200 mg/day elemental calcium from citrate. Nutritional role: calcium is the most abundant mineral in the body; 99% in bones and teeth; remainder critical for muscle contraction, nerve transmission, blood coagulation and cellular signalling. India context: calcium deficiency is widespread, particularly among women, adolescents and the elderly; dairy consumption is variable and vegetarian diets may have lower calcium bioavailability from phytate-rich plant sources; calcium citrate supplements are widely used. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E333), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e333': 'E333/INS 333 is calcium citrate, a firming agent and highly bioavailable calcium supplement approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'calcium carbonate': """Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃ / E170/INS 170 / chalk / limestone / calcite / aragonite) is one of the most abundant minerals on Earth — the primary component of limestone, chalk, marble, shells and coral. For food use, it is produced by either milling of natural chalk or limestone, or by precipitation from calcium hydroxide and CO₂ (precipitated calcium carbonate / PCC), which has a finer, more uniform particle size. Multiple food functions: (1) white colour — a bright, neutral white pigment used in sugar coating of confectionery, chewing gum and pharmaceutical tablets; (2) anti-caking agent — absorbs moisture to keep powdered products free-flowing; (3) calcium fortification — used in calcium-fortified flour (mandatory in UK wheat flour), plant-based milks, breakfast cereals, bread and infant formula; (4) acidity regulator — mildly alkaline, neutralises excess acidity in food processing and in Dutch-process cocoa; (5) firming agent — in packaged fruit and vegetables; (6) releasing agent — prevents sticking in panned confectionery. As a calcium supplement: provides ~40% elemental calcium by weight — the highest of any common supplement form, making tablets compact. However, calcium carbonate requires an acidic stomach environment for dissolution and absorption — it should be taken with food (when stomach acid is stimulated). Bioavailability ~27–39%. Not ideal for people with achlorhydria (low stomach acid) or those taking PPIs — calcium citrate is better absorbed in these cases. High doses (>2.5 g elemental calcium/day) can cause hypercalcaemia. FSSAI mandates calcium carbonate for flour fortification. ADI: not specified (JECFA). EU (E170), FDA (GRAS), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e170': 'E170/INS 170 is calcium carbonate (chalk/limestone), a natural mineral used as a white colorant, calcium supplement, anticaking agent and pH regulator. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    'sodium hydroxide': """Sodium hydroxide (NaOH / lye / caustic soda / E524/INS 524) is the strongest common base used in food processing — a white, highly hygroscopic solid that dissolves exothermically in water to form a strongly alkaline solution (pH ~14 at 1% concentration). Manufactured by the chloralkali process: electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride (brine) solution generates Cl₂ gas at the anode and NaOH + H₂ at the cathode; food-grade sodium hydroxide undergoes multiple purification steps to remove chlorine, mercury and heavy metals. Despite its extreme caustic nature in pure form, food-grade sodium hydroxide has a long history of specific, precisely controlled food applications where it is entirely consumed by chemical reaction during processing: (1) Lye pretzels and bagels: dipping or spraying dough with 3–4% lye solution before baking creates the characteristic glossy, dark-brown crust and unique chewy-crispy texture; during baking, NaOH reacts with surface proteins and sugars (Maillard and saponification reactions), generating the brown pigmentation and distinctive alkaline flavour; virtually no free NaOH remains in the finished baked product. (2) Cured olives: traditional green olive curing uses lye solution to hydrolyse the intensely bitter glycoside oleuropein — without lye treatment, raw olives are inedibly bitter; NaOH penetrates the olive flesh and breaks down oleuropein, then is thoroughly washed out. (3) Ramen/alkaline noodles: adding sodium hydroxide or sodium carbonate to noodle dough produces the characteristic springy-chewy texture (gluten cross-linking), yellow colour (pH shift affects flavonoid pigments) and alkaline flavour of Chinese-style alkaline noodles, ramen and Japanese sōmen. (4) Hominy/nixtamal: traditional alkali cooking of maize (though calcium hydroxide is more common). (5) Cocoa processing: Dutch-process cocoa treats cocoa powder with alkali (sodium or potassium carbonate/hydroxide) to reduce acidity and deepen colour. Safety: food-grade NaOH is completely safe in finished food products because it has reacted completely during processing — the pH of finished products is tested. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI (E524), EU (E524), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e524': 'E524/INS 524 is sodium hydroxide (lye/caustic soda), a strong pH adjuster used in food processing for olives, pretzels and some Asian noodles. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX for food use.',
    'calcium hydroxide': """Calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)₂ / slaked lime / hydrated lime / pickling lime / E526/INS 526) is a mild, relatively insoluble inorganic base produced by the reaction of quicklime (calcium oxide, CaO) with water (slaking): CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + heat. Mildly alkaline (saturated solution pH ~12.4; much less caustic than sodium hydroxide). Fine white powder, slightly soluble in water. Among the most ancient and culturally significant food processing chemicals in human civilisation. Key food applications: (1) Nixtamalisation — the transformative traditional process of cooking maize (corn) with calcium hydroxide (cal/chuna) in alkaline water, dating back ~3,500 years in Mesoamerican civilisation. The NaOH partially hydrates the grain outer pericarp and gelatinises the starch; critically, alkaline hydrolysis releases niacin (Vitamin B3) from its bound form (niacytin/nicotinoyl polysaccharide) into free, bioavailable niacin — civilisations that depended on untreated corn without nixtamalisation developed pellagra (niacin deficiency disease). Nixtamalisation also increases bioavailability of calcium (from the lime), improves amino acid profile (increases lysine availability), modifies texture for masa dough (tortillas, tamales, tostadas) and reduces mycotoxin contamination. (2) Paan (betel) preparations: calcium hydroxide (chuna/choon) has been used for millennia in South and Southeast Asian betel leaf preparations (paan) — it raises pH in the mouth, enabling alkaloid absorption from betel nut; importantly, the combination of areca nut, tobacco and calcium hydroxide in paan is associated with oral submucous fibrosis and oral cancer risk. (3) Pickling: "Pickling lime" (calcium hydroxide solution) is used to firm cucumbers before pickling — calcium ions crosslink pectin in cell walls, maintaining crunch. (4) Firming agent: in canned tomatoes, fruit preparations and jams to prevent texture breakdown. (5) Sugar refining: used to clarify and purify sugar cane juice (carbonatation process). (6) Water treatment: raising water pH to reduce lead and copper pipe corrosion; used in municipal water treatment. Safety in food: completely safe at food use levels — the calcium hydroxide reacts during processing and the finished pH is controlled. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E526), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e526': 'E526/INS 526 is calcium hydroxide (slaked lime), used in nixtamalisation of corn, as a firming agent and acidity regulator. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',
    # Enzymes
    'amylase': """Amylase (E1100/INS 1100 / alpha-amylase) is a glycoside hydrolase enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch, releasing shorter oligosaccharides, maltose and glucose. Naturally present in: human saliva (salivary amylase, ptyalin — begins starch digestion in the mouth) and pancreatic fluid (pancreatic amylase — completes digestion in the small intestine); germinating grains (malt amylase); and in microorganisms such as Bacillus subtilis, Bacillus amyloliquefaciens and Aspergillus oryzae (the primary commercial food-grade sources). Types: alpha-amylase cleaves internal bonds randomly, producing short dextrins and maltooligosaccharides; beta-amylase cleaves from the non-reducing end, producing maltose; glucoamylase/amyloglucosidase cleaves terminal glucose units. Food industry applications: (1) Bread baking — added alpha-amylase (fungal amylase from A. oryzae) improves bread volume, crust colour (Maillard browning from released reducing sugars), crumb softness and anti-staling; also important in fermentation — yeast cannot ferment unbroken starch; (2) Glucose syrup production — amylase and glucoamylase used together to convert starch completely to glucose; (3) Beer brewing — malt amylase converts starch from malted barley to fermentable sugars during mashing; (4) Fruit juice clarification; (5) Animal feed enzyme additive. Used at very low concentrations as a processing aid — generally not declared on finished food labels. Regarded as GRAS (FDA), EU and FSSAI approved. No significant allergen concerns at food use levels (may rarely cause occupational asthma in bakery workers exposed to raw amylase dust).""",
    'e1100': 'E1100/INS 1100 is alpha-amylase, a natural enzyme used as a flour treatment agent in baking. Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA as GRAS. Produced from Bacillus subtilis or Aspergillus oryzae for food-grade use.',
    'lipase': """Lipase is a class of hydrolytic enzymes that catalyse the cleavage of ester bonds in triglycerides, releasing free fatty acids and glycerol (full hydrolysis to glycerol + 3 fatty acids) or partial glycerides (monoglycerides, diglycerides — partial hydrolysis). Naturally present in: human pancreatic juice (pancreatic lipase — essential for fat digestion in the small intestine), human gastric juice (gastric lipase), milk (lipoprotein lipase), most animal tissues, and various microorganisms and fungi. Commercial food-grade lipases are derived from: Candida rugosa, Aspergillus niger, Rhizopus oryzae, Mucor miehei and calf or lamb pre-gastric esterase (an animal-derived lipase). Food industry applications: (1) Cheese flavour development — lipases release short-chain fatty acids (butyric, caproic, caprylic acids) that create characteristic pungent flavour in traditional Italian cheeses (Pecorino, Romano, Provolone) and in accelerated ripening; (2) Bread baking — lyso-phospholipid production from wheat flour phospholipids improves dough structure, bread volume and crumb softness; (3) Interesterification of fats — enzyme-based (sn-1,3 selective lipases) rearrangement of fatty acids in fat blends to produce trans-free specialty fats; (4) Digestive enzyme supplements — used in supplements for those with pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis); (5) Biodiesel production. Used as processing aids at very low levels; generally not declared on finished food labels. GRAS (FDA), EU (E1104) and FSSAI approved. No significant safety concerns at food-grade use levels. Animal-derived lipases (from calf or lamb) are not vegetarian/vegan.""",
    'protease': """Protease (proteinase / peptidase) is the collective name for a large and diverse family of enzymes that catalyse the hydrolysis of peptide bonds in proteins, breaking them down into smaller peptides and ultimately free amino acids. Classified by mechanism: serine proteases (subtilisin, chymotrypsin, trypsin), cysteine proteases (papain, bromelain), aspartic proteases (pepsin, chymosin/rennet) and metalloproteases. Naturally present in: human gastric juice (pepsin — aspartic protease) and pancreatic fluid (trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase), most living organisms. Commercial food-grade proteases are derived from: Bacillus subtilis and B. licheniformis (bacterial subtilisins), Aspergillus oryzae and A. niger (fungal proteases), papaya (papain — E1101), pineapple (bromelain), kiwi (actinidin), and animal sources (pepsin, trypsin, rennet). Food industry applications: (1) Meat tenderisation — papain and bromelain from tropical fruits break down connective tissue collagen and myofibrillar proteins, tenderising tough cuts; used in commercial meat tenderisers; (2) Cheese making — rennet (chymosin, an aspartic protease from calf stomach or microbial fermentation) cleaves kappa-casein at a specific site, causing micelle aggregation and milk gelation — the first step in cheese making; (3) Bread baking — reduces dough mixing time, improves extensibility; (4) Beer haze removal — removes haze-forming proteins from beer (chill haze); (5) Soy sauce and fermented foods production; (6) Flavour development (glutamic acid release provides umami); (7) Digestive enzyme supplements. GRAS (FDA), EU and FSSAI approved. Generally safe at food use levels. Allergen: papain from papaya can cause allergy — rare.""",
    'lactase': """Lactase (beta-galactosidase / E1104 when listed, though enzyme processing aids often not numbered) is the digestive enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of lactose (milk sugar) into its two monosaccharide components: glucose and galactose, which can then be absorbed in the small intestine. Naturally produced in the small intestinal brush border epithelium (enterocytes) in infants and young children (essential for milk digestion); lactase activity typically declines after weaning in most of the world's population (lactase non-persistence — the genetically "normal" condition for most humans), causing lactose intolerance in adulthood. Indian prevalence: approximately 70–80% of adult Indians have lactase non-persistence — among the highest rates globally. Commercial production: food-grade lactase is produced by fermentation from Kluyveromyces lactis or Aspergillus oryzae. Food industry applications: (1) Lactose-free dairy products — lactase is added to milk, yoghurt, ice cream and other dairy products to pre-digest the lactose before consumption, producing lactose-free products (which taste slightly sweeter due to released glucose and galactose); (2) Whey processing — conversion of lactose in whey for incorporation into other foods; (3) Ice cream — improves scoopability (reduced crystallisation from hydrolysed sugars). As a digestive supplement: sold as "lactase enzyme" supplements (Lactaid, LactoJoy, etc.) — taken orally with dairy-containing meals to compensate for endogenous lactase deficiency, enabling lactose-intolerant individuals to digest dairy. Very safe; no significant adverse effects. GRAS (FDA), EU and FSSAI approved. Vegan lactase (Aspergillus-derived) is available.""",
    # Nutritional ingredients
    'lecithin': 'A natural complex mixture of phospholipids found in egg yolk, soya and sunflower seeds. One of the most widely used food emulsifiers, present in chocolate, bread, margarine and many processed foods. May be sourced from soy (E322), sunflower or egg.',
    'sunflower lecithin': """Sunflower lecithin (E322 / INS 322 — lecithins from sunflower) is a mixture of phospholipids extracted from sunflower seeds (Helianthus annuus) — the primary alternative to soy lecithin (the most widely used lecithin in food). Unlike soy lecithin, which is typically extracted using hexane solvent, sunflower lecithin is most often produced by cold-pressing sunflower seeds to expel the oil, then separating the phospholipid-rich gum fraction by centrifugation (a mechanical, solvent-free process) — making it appealing to "clean label" and organic food manufacturers. Composition: a complex mixture of phospholipids — phosphatidylcholine (~35%), phosphatidylethanolamine (~20%), phosphatidylinositol (~20%), phosphatidic acid and other minor phospholipids, along with neutral lipids and glycolipids. This phospholipid profile is similar to soy lecithin. Function in food: emulsifier (stabilises oil-in-water and water-in-oil emulsions); dispersing agent; wetting agent; anti-sticking agent. Applications: chocolate and confectionery (prevents bloom, improves flow), baked goods (extends shelf life, improves dough workability), protein supplements (improves mixability), margarine, infant formula and dietary supplement capsules. Allergen advantage: soy is one of the 14 major food allergens regulated globally — people with soy allergy must avoid soy lecithin. Sunflower lecithin is not derived from a major allergen plant and is safe for soy-allergic individuals. Note: sunflower lecithin can rarely cause reactions in people with very severe sunflower seed allergy (sunflower itself is not a regulated major allergen but reactions have been documented). Nutritional consideration: lecithins are a natural source of choline — an essential nutrient for liver function, brain health and fetal neural development. Typical food-additive concentrations provide a modest but measurable choline contribution. Not carcinogenic; not a sensitiser at normal use concentrations. Approved by EU (E322), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; non-GMO (unlike most soy lecithin which is from GMO soy).""",
    'vanillin': """Vanillin (4-hydroxy-3-methoxybenzaldehyde) is the primary flavour compound responsible for the characteristic aroma and taste of vanilla — originally extracted from vanilla bean pods (Vanilla planifolia) but now produced almost entirely by chemical synthesis or biotechnology. Natural vanillin from vanilla beans makes up <1% of global vanillin supply. Commercial vanillin is produced by: (1) chemical synthesis from guaiacol (methoxyphenol, a petroleum or lignin-derived compound) via glyoxylic acid condensation — the dominant route globally; (2) lignin oxidation from wood pulp (paper industry by-product); (3) microbial or enzymatic bioconversion of ferulic acid from rice bran or other plant sources (marketed as "natural" vanillin). Approximately 20,000 tonnes produced annually globally. Used as a flavour enhancer or substitute for natural vanilla in chocolate, baked goods, ice cream, beverages, confectionery and pharmaceuticals. Safety: extensive use since the 1870s; FDA GRAS, EU and CODEX approved. No significant allergen concerns. EFSA evaluated and confirmed safety at food use concentrations. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Not carcinogenic; no genotoxicity at food use levels. Suitable for vegans unless vanilla bean-derived. FSSAI permits as a flavouring substance.""",
    'ethyl vanillin': """Ethyl vanillin (3-ethoxy-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde) is a synthetic vanilla-type flavouring compound structurally similar to vanillin (which has a methoxy group) but with an ethoxy group at the 3-position of the benzaldehyde ring. This seemingly small structural difference increases potency dramatically: ethyl vanillin is approximately 3–4 times more intense in vanilla flavour than vanillin at equivalent concentrations. Not naturally found in vanilla beans — entirely synthetic. Produced by condensation of ethylguaiacol with glyoxylic acid. Widely used in: chocolate and cocoa products (provides intense vanilla note at very low dosage), confectionery, baked goods, ice cream, beverages and as a flavour enhancer in combination with vanillin. Lower cost per unit of flavour intensity makes it attractive for commercial food manufacturing. Safety: FDA GRAS, EU approved (as flavouring), FSSAI permitted. ADI: not specified (JECFA). No carcinogenicity or genotoxicity at food use concentrations. Not found in nature — strictly synthetic; relevant for consumers seeking "natural flavours only" products. Well tolerated; rare contact allergy cases reported in perfumery use (cosmetic application at high concentrations) but not a significant concern at food use levels.""",
    'dextrose': """Dextrose (D-glucose / dextrose monohydrate / grape sugar) is the monohydrate crystalline form of D-glucose — the primary circulating blood sugar in all mammals and the universal cellular fuel. Produced commercially by complete enzymatic hydrolysis of starch (primarily maize/corn, also wheat and cassava) using alpha-amylase and glucoamylase, yielding a solution of approximately 100% glucose (100 DE — Dextrose Equivalent), which is then purified by ion exchange and activated carbon, then crystallised as the stable monohydrate form. Sweetness: ~70–75% that of sucrose. Glycaemic Index: 100 (the reference standard). Applications: bakery fermentation substrate — dextrose is the preferred readily fermentable sugar for yeast in bread and fermented products; confectionery — controls sweetness, lowers water activity; cured and processed meats — provides substrate for curing bacteria and participates in Maillard browning; sports and energy products — immediately available blood glucose source; pharmaceutical — the standard IV fluid (5% dextrose solution) for energy and hydration, hypertonic dextrose for hypoglycaemia; rehydration formulas (ORS); anti-caking in salt substitutes. In cosmetics: used as a skin-conditioning humectant. Nutritionally, dextrose is pure glucose — the same as any other glucose source. High GI means rapid blood sugar spike; people with diabetes or insulin resistance should use caution. 'Dextrose' and 'glucose' are interchangeable terms on food labels — there is no meaningful nutritional distinction. FSSAI, FDA (GRAS), EU and CODEX recognised as safe food ingredient.""",
    'fructose': """Fructose (D-fructose / levulose / fruit sugar) is a six-carbon ketohexose monosaccharide and the sweetest naturally occurring carbohydrate — approximately 1.2–1.8 times sweeter than sucrose (sweetness varies with temperature: sweeter when cold, less sweet when warm, because of differences in the furanose/pyranose ring equilibrium). Found naturally in fruits (apples 6%, grapes 7%, pears 7–8%), honey (~38%) and most vegetables. The 'F' component of sucrose — after sucrase digestion, all sucrose yields equal amounts of glucose and fructose. Commercial food-grade fructose is produced: (1) as crystalline fructose (>99% pure fructose) by chromatographic separation from glucose in high-DE corn syrup; (2) as a component of HFCS (42 or 55% fructose); (3) by acid or enzyme hydrolysis of sucrose (invert sugar). Used in food as a sweetener because crystalline fructose is sweeter per gram than sucrose, allowing reduced sweetener quantity; also used in diabetic foods because fructose does not require insulin for cellular uptake (it is taken up by GLUT5 transporters in the intestine and liver independently of insulin). Metabolism — a critical distinction from glucose: fructose is absorbed by GLUT5 in the small intestine and transported via the portal vein almost entirely to the liver, where it is phosphorylated by fructokinase to fructose-1-phosphate — a step that bypasses the rate-limiting phosphofructokinase step in glycolysis, meaning fructose can flood glycolysis without normal regulatory constraints. This drives: (1) de novo lipogenesis (liver fat synthesis) — raising serum triglycerides and VLDL; (2) uric acid generation — the ATP consumed by fructokinase reaction generates AMP, which is degraded to uric acid; (3) hepatic insulin resistance — not from fructose itself but from the lipid accumulation and oxidative stress. Multiple clinical and epidemiological studies associate high added fructose intake (particularly from beverages) with NAFLD, hypertriglyceridaemia, hyperuricaemia/gout and metabolic syndrome. Whole fruit consumption, despite containing fructose, is not associated with these risks — because fruit fibre slows absorption and the amounts are far lower than from sweetened beverages. The same metabolic concerns apply to HFCS and sucrose at equivalent fructose content. WHO recommends limiting free sugars (including fructose) to <10% total energy. FSSAI permits crystalline fructose as food ingredient.""",
    'lactose': """Lactose is the principal carbohydrate of mammalian milk — a disaccharide of glucose and galactose joined by a β-1,4-glycosidic bond. See the primary lactose entry for full details. In brief: requires the intestinal enzyme lactase for digestion; approximately 65–70% of adults globally have reduced lactase activity (lactase non-persistence), making lactose a significant cause of bloating, gas and diarrhoea in this population. Severity varies with dose and fermented dairy products are often better tolerated. Used commercially as a pharmaceutical excipient (filler, binder), mild food sweetener, and Maillard browning agent. Produced from cheese whey permeate. Lactose intolerance is distinct from cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA) — lactose is a carbohydrate, not a protein, and does not cause immune-mediated reactions. Highly purified pharmaceutical-grade lactose is generally protein-free. FSSAI, CODEX and global regulations permit as a food and pharmaceutical ingredient.""",
    'casein': 'The primary milk protein (about 80% of cow\'s milk protein), present in milk as a suspension of micelles. A slow-digesting, high-quality complete protein. Used in cheese, dairy products and as a protein supplement. Dairy allergen — must be declared.',
    'whey': """Whey protein is the fast-digesting fraction of milk proteins (~20% of total milk protein), separated during cheese-making as the liquid whey stream. See the primary whey entry for full details. In brief: whey protein is the highest-quality protein by PDCAAS (score = 1.0), richest in leucine (~11%) among common proteins, and is digested rapidly (peak amino acids in blood within 60–90 minutes). Available as whey protein concentrate (WPC, 35–80% protein), whey protein isolate (WPI, ≥90% protein, minimal lactose), and hydrolysate. Widely used in sports nutrition, infant formula, baked goods and protein bars. Contains beta-lactoglobulin (major milk allergen), alpha-lactalbumin, immunoglobulins and lactoferrin. Allergen — CRITICAL: dairy allergen under FSSAI, EU and FDA regulations — mandatory declaration required. Cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA) patients must strictly avoid. People with lactose intolerance may tolerate WPI (very low lactose) but should check product specifications.""",
    'pea protein': 'A plant-based protein extracted from yellow split peas (Pisum sativum) by aqueous processing. Hypoallergenic and naturally free from the top 8 allergens. Used in plant-based meat alternatives, protein powders and dairy-free products.',
    'soy protein': """Soy protein is a complete plant protein extracted from defatted soybean flakes (Glycine max) after solvent removal of soybean oil. Available in three commercial forms: soy protein concentrate (SPC, ~65–70% protein, retains fibre), soy protein isolate (SPI, ≥90% protein, most fibre and fat removed — see separate entry) and textured/textured vegetable protein (TVP — soy protein concentrate or isolate extruded into meat-like fibrous structures). Amino acid profile: contains all nine essential amino acids; relatively high in lysine and relatively low in methionine compared to animal proteins — PDCAAS (Protein Digestibility Corrected Amino Acid Score) of soy protein is 1.0 (equivalent to egg or milk protein), making it the only plant protein classified as a complete protein by FDA. Used in: plant-based meat alternatives (burgers, nuggets, sausages), infant formula (as the only hypoallergenic milk-replacement protein base for cow's milk-allergic infants), protein powders and bars, processed meats (extender), dairy alternatives and tofu/tempeh. Isoflavone content: soy protein retains isoflavones (genistein, daidzein — phytoestrogens). At typical dietary levels, evidence does not support adverse hormonal effects; large supplemental doses warrant caution for thyroid medication users (take thyroid medication 4 hours apart from soy). Allergen — CRITICAL: soy is one of the 14 major food allergens in EU and 8 in USA; FSSAI mandates mandatory allergen declaration. Soy protein allergy is distinct from soy oil allergy (see soybean oil entry). FSSAI, FDA and EU recognise soy protein as a safe, high-quality plant protein ingredient.""",
    'rice protein': """Rice protein is a hypoallergenic, gluten-free plant protein extracted from brown or white rice (Oryza sativa) using enzymatic hydrolysis — typically a combination of amylases (to hydrolyse the starch) followed by proteases (to extract and concentrate the protein fraction). The resulting protein powder typically contains 75–85% protein by dry weight. Amino acid profile: contains all essential amino acids; relatively low in lysine (like most cereal proteins) but compensated when combined with legume proteins; higher in cysteine and methionine than soy protein. Digestibility is moderate (PDCAAS ~0.5–0.7) — enzymatic hydrolysis improves digestibility of rice protein compared to the raw grain protein. Hypoallergenicity is the primary advantage: rice is not among the top 8 (USA) or top 14 (EU) major food allergens, making rice protein an ideal choice for individuals with soy allergy, dairy allergy, gluten sensitivity and egg allergy — i.e., people who cannot tolerate the most common protein sources in supplements. Used in: protein powders and bars, infant formula alternatives, plant-based foods, bakery protein enrichment and vegan/vegetarian products. Functional properties: relatively neutral taste compared to pea or soy protein; slight grittiness is reduced in hydrolysed forms. Used in blends with pea protein to complement amino acid profiles. No major regulatory restrictions. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient. Generally very safe; no significant adverse effects documented.""",
    'oat bran': """Oat bran is the outer pericarp (bran layer) of the oat grain (Avena sativa), separated from the oat groat during milling. It is the most clinically validated dietary fibre ingredient for LDL cholesterol reduction. Chemical composition: exceptionally rich in mixed-linkage (1→3)(1→4)-beta-D-glucan — a soluble fibre that makes up approximately 5–10% of oat bran dry weight, compared to 3–4% in whole oat groats. Also contains 15–20% protein, 7–10% fat (mostly unsaturated), significant amounts of B vitamins (thiamine, pantothenic acid), iron, zinc, magnesium, phosphorus and phytochemicals (avenanthramides — polyphenols unique to oats with anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties). Mechanism of cholesterol reduction: oat beta-glucan forms a viscous gel in the small intestine that: (1) binds bile acids (cholesterol-derived) and prevents their reabsorption from the ileum — the liver compensates by converting more cholesterol to bile acids, lowering serum LDL; (2) slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, reducing postprandial glycaemic response. Regulatory health claims: FDA (USA) permits: "Soluble fibre from oat bran, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease." Requirement: ≥0.75 g beta-glucan per serving. EFSA (EU) permits a health claim: oat beta-glucan has been shown to lower/reduce blood cholesterol. Dose requirement: 3 g beta-glucan per day. Used in: high-fibre breakfast cereals, muffins, porridge, bread, crackers and dietary supplements. Suitable for diabetics (low GI). Contains gluten (oat avenin can trigger coeliac symptoms in some patients) — coeliac individuals should use certified gluten-free oat bran. No significant allergen concerns for the general population. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'psyllium husk': """Psyllium husk (Isabgol / Plantago ovata husk) is the outer seed coat (testa and perisperm) of the psyllium plant (Plantago ovata, family Plantaginaceae) — an annual herb cultivated almost exclusively in the semi-arid regions of Gujarat and Rajasthan, India (which supply approximately 85–90% of world production), making this a strategically important Indian crop. The husk is separated from the whole seed by milling and air-sifting. Chemical composition: approximately 85% dietary fibre, of which approximately 70% is a branched arabinoxylan — a highly water-binding, mucilaginous polysaccharide that absorbs 10–15 times its weight in water, forming a gel (mucilage). The mucilage is concentrated in the outer husk, while the inner seed contains starch and protein that are less relevant to its fibre properties. Mode of action: (1) Bulk laxative — in the colon, psyllium husk absorbs water, swells and forms a viscous mass that lubricates and propels stool; normalises stool consistency (prevents both constipation and diarrhoea — a unique bidirectional effect). (2) Cholesterol reduction — the gel binds bile acids in the small intestine, preventing their reabsorption; the liver compensates by synthesising new bile acids from cholesterol, reducing serum LDL. FDA has approved a health claim: "3 g or more of soluble fibre daily from psyllium, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease." FSSAI has accepted this as a significant health benefit. (3) Glycaemic control — slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, reducing postprandial blood glucose spikes; useful for type 2 diabetes management. (4) Prebiotic effect — bifidogenic fermentation in the colon (though less pronounced than with inulin). Clinical applications: irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) — psyllium husk (5–10 g/day with ≥250 mL water) is one of the best-evidenced treatments for IBS-related constipation; constipation treatment; dyslipidaemia management. Safety: psyllium husk is very safe but must always be consumed with adequate water (≥200 mL per dose); without sufficient water it can cause oesophageal or bowel obstruction, particularly in people with swallowing difficulties. Documented to cause IgE-mediated allergy (occupational: nurses and pharmacy staff who handle psyllium-containing medications; consumer allergy is uncommon but reported). ADI: not specified. FDA (GRAS), FSSAI and CODEX approved as a dietary fibre ingredient.""",
    'wheat bran': """Wheat bran is the multi-layered outer covering of the wheat kernel (Triticum aestivum) — the outermost pericarp, testa and aleurone layers — separated from the white endosperm during flour milling. It constitutes approximately 14–17% of the whole wheat grain by weight. Nutritional composition: exceptionally rich in dietary fibre (~40–48% total dietary fibre, predominantly insoluble cellulose, hemicellulose and arabinoxylans); significant protein (~16%); rich in B vitamins — particularly thiamine (B1), niacin (B3), B6, folate and pantothenic acid; excellent source of minerals — iron (~15 mg/100g, though bioavailability is reduced by phytate), magnesium (~450 mg/100g), zinc, phosphorus and manganese; and phytochemicals including ferulic acid (a potent antioxidant). The insoluble fibre (predominantly arabinoxylan) is distinct from soluble fibre — it does not form a viscous gel but adds bulk to the stool, speeds intestinal transit and feeds specific colonic bacteria (particularly fermenters of arabinoxylan). Health effects: (1) bowel regularity — the most well-evidenced benefit; insoluble fibre increases stool weight and frequency, reducing constipation; (2) colon cancer risk reduction — epidemiological evidence links high dietary fibre (including wheat bran) to reduced colorectal cancer risk, though mechanistic evidence is less definitive than for some other fibres; (3) fermentation — a proportion of wheat bran arabinoxylan is fermented by gut bacteria to short-chain fatty acids; (4) modestly reduces LDL cholesterol. Phytate consideration: wheat bran is very high in phytic acid (phytate) — a strong chelator of minerals. High wheat bran consumption can inhibit absorption of iron, zinc, calcium and magnesium from the same meal — relevant for individuals with marginal mineral status. Used in: high-fibre bread, breakfast cereals (All-Bran, bran flakes), muffins, chapati/roti blends and fibre supplements. Contains gluten — not suitable for coeliac disease. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'flaxseed': """Flaxseed (linseed / Linum usitatissimum / alsi) is the seed of the flax plant, one of the oldest cultivated crops, grown for both its seeds (nutritional and industrial) and its fibres (linen). Small, flat, brown or golden seeds with a mildly nutty flavour. Nutritional composition: rich in ALA (alpha-linolenic acid, C18:3 omega-3 fatty acid, ~22g/100g — among the highest plant sources; essential omega-3 precursor to EPA and DHA), lignans (~0.3 g/100g secoisolariciresinol diglucoside / SDG — the highest plant lignan concentration known, ~75–800 times more than other plant foods), soluble fibre (~7g/100g — mucilage polysaccharides forming a gel similar to psyllium), protein (~18g/100g, complete with all essential amino acids), magnesium, thiamine and Vitamin B6. Bioavailability consideration: whole flaxseeds have a hard outer coat that resists digestion — whole seeds often pass through the GI tract intact with minimal nutritional release. Ground/milled flaxseed (flaxseed meal) dramatically improves absorption of omega-3 fatty acids and lignans. Health effects: ALA is the plant precursor to EPA and DHA, though conversion efficiency is limited (~5–15%); lignans (SDG) are phytoestrogens with potential protective effects for hormone-sensitive conditions and documented antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties; soluble fibre contributes to cholesterol lowering and glycaemic control. Cyanogenic glycoside safety: raw flaxseed contains small amounts of linamarin and linustatin (cyanogenic glucosides); typical dietary quantities (1–2 tablespoons/day) are not a concern for healthy adults; excessive raw intake (>4 tablespoons/day) may theoretically release HCN. Cooking eliminates this. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient. Widely used in Indian functional foods and supplements.""",
    'chia seeds': """Chia seeds (Salvia hispanica) are tiny seeds (1–2 mm) from a flowering plant native to central Mexico and Guatemala, consumed since Aztec and Mayan times and now a globally popular functional food. Nutritional composition per 100g: ALA omega-3 fatty acids (~17g — approximately 65% of fat content), soluble dietary fibre (~11g — very high; mucilaginous arabinoxylan that absorbs 10–12x its weight in water), protein (~17g — moderate quality, complete with all essential amino acids), calcium (~630mg — one of the highest plant sources), phosphorus, magnesium, zinc, iron and antioxidants (quercetin, kaempferol, chlorogenic acid). Gel-forming property: when soaked in water, chia seeds absorb liquid to form a thick mucilaginous gel — exploited in beverages (chia water/drinks), puddings, as an egg replacer in vegan baking (1 tbsp chia + 3 tbsp water = "chia egg") and as a thickener. This gel-forming fibre also slows gastric emptying, smoothing the glycaemic response to co-consumed carbohydrates. Health evidence: ALA from chia contributes to omega-3 fatty acid intake (conversion to EPA/DHA limited — approximately 5–10%); soluble fibre supports LDL cholesterol reduction and gut microbiome; calcium is bioavailable; protein quality is good for a plant source. EFSA health claims: novel food status approved in EU for use in bread (as chia can replace up to 10% of wheat in bread formulations without affecting nutritional profile). No documented major allergens though rare reactions have been described. Oxalate content is moderate — relevant for individuals with kidney stone tendency. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient. Increasingly used in Indian functional foods, health drinks and protein bars.""",
    'canola oil': """Canola oil is a refined vegetable oil pressed and solvent-extracted from the seeds of low-erucic acid rapeseed cultivars (Brassica napus or Brassica rapa). The name "canola" originated in Canada from "Canadian oil, low acid." Standard rapeseed contains high levels of erucic acid (~40%) which caused cardiac lipidosis in animal studies; canola cultivars were developed by plant breeding to reduce erucic acid to <2% and glucosinolates to <30 µmol/g — making it safe for human consumption. Fatty acid profile: approximately 63% oleic acid (monounsaturated C18:1), 20% linoleic acid (omega-6), 9% alpha-linolenic acid (ALA, omega-3), 7% saturated fat — among the lowest saturated fat content of all cooking oils. This profile is close to the omega-3:omega-6 ratio recommended by health authorities (~1:2). Heat stability: high smoke point (~204°C refined) suitable for frying, sautéing and baking. Vitamin E (mainly gamma-tocopherol) content. Most commercial canola is from GMO crops in Canada and the US; non-GMO and organic variants are available. Health: the combination of low saturated fat, moderate omega-3 and high oleic acid content supports cardiovascular health; FDA permits a health claim for high-oleic canola oil and reduced heart disease risk. FSSAI permits canola oil as an edible vegetable oil. Widely used in Indian packaged food manufacturing.""",
    'sunflower oil': """Sunflower oil is a widely used vegetable oil extracted by pressing and solvent extraction from sunflower seeds (Helianthus annuus). Available in two main types based on oleic acid content: conventional high-linoleic sunflower oil (60–70% linoleic acid/omega-6, ~20% oleic acid) and high-oleic sunflower oil (75–90% oleic acid, developed for improved oxidative stability). Standard sunflower oil's fatty acid composition: approximately 65% linoleic acid (omega-6 polyunsaturated), 21% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 11% saturated (primarily palmitic and stearic). Very rich in Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) — one of the best dietary sources (~41 mg/100g). Used in: cooking, frying, food manufacturing (biscuits, chips, mayonnaise, margarine), salad dressings and cosmetics (skin emollient). Omega-6 consideration: Indian diets already tend to be high in omega-6 fatty acids — disproportionate omega-6 to omega-3 intake (current Indian average ~20:1 vs recommended ~4:1) is associated with a pro-inflammatory state and cardiovascular risk. High-oleic sunflower oil (marketed for better frying stability) has a better balance. Smoke point: ~230°C (refined) suitable for high-temperature cooking. Allergen: sunflower seed allergy exists but is uncommon and not among the 14 major regulated allergens (EU). Highly refined oil is unlikely to cause reactions in sunflower-sensitised individuals. FSSAI permits as a standard edible vegetable oil.""",
    'rapeseed oil': """Rapeseed oil is a vegetable oil extracted from the seeds of Brassica napus and Brassica rapa (rapeseed/rape plant), cultivated primarily in Europe, Canada and China. Traditional rapeseed oil contained high amounts of erucic acid (~40%) which caused cardiac concerns; modern food-grade rapeseed oil now typically refers to low-erucic acid rapeseed oil (erucic acid <2%), essentially the same as canola oil. European and Indian rapeseed oil may be sold as "rapeseed oil" or "canola oil" interchangeably when low-erucic acid varieties are used. Fatty acid profile of low-erucic rapeseed oil: ~63% oleic acid, ~20% linoleic acid (omega-6), ~9% ALA (omega-3), ~7% saturated fat — a healthful profile with good omega balance and high monounsaturated content. High smoke point (~204°C) makes it suitable for cooking. Rich in Vitamin E and plant sterols. Indian yellow mustard oil (from Brassica juncea) is chemically related but distinct, and contains higher erucic acid — Indian regulations permit erucic acid up to 2% in edible oils. When traditional (high-erucic) rapeseed oil appears in food labels, it is a concern; food-grade modern rapeseed oil in India meets FSSAI erucic acid limits. FSSAI permits rapeseed oil (low-erucic acid) as an edible oil.""",
    'sesame oil': """Sesame oil is a vegetable oil cold-pressed or solvent-extracted from sesame seeds (Sesamum indicum), one of the oldest oilseed crops in South Asia with cultivation dating back over 5,000 years. Two types: light/refined sesame oil (pale yellow, mild flavour, suitable for cooking) and dark toasted sesame oil (made from toasted seeds — intensely nutty aroma, used as a finishing flavour). Fatty acid composition: approximately 41% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 43% linoleic acid (omega-6), 14% saturated. Distinctive chemical feature: uniquely high content of two lignans — sesamin and sesamolin — which are metabolised to sesamol in the body. These lignans are potent antioxidants (particularly protective against lipid oxidation) and may have cholesterol-lowering, anti-inflammatory and antihypertensive properties; they also inhibit delta-5 desaturase, potentially modulating inflammatory eicosanoid production. Gamma-tocopherol content contributes additional antioxidant protection. High smoke point (~210°C) for refined oil. Traditional significance: widely used in South Indian, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada and Bengali cooking; in tempering (tadka); Ayurvedic abhyanga (oil massage); Makar Sankranti til sweets. Allergen: sesame is one of the 14 major food allergens regulated in the EU and India; the 9th most common food allergy in the USA (now among the 9 major allergens under FALCPA 2023). FSSAI mandates allergen declaration. People with sesame allergy must avoid. Not suitable for those with sesame sensitivity.""",
    'rice bran oil': """Rice bran oil (RBO) is extracted from the outer bran layer (aleurone and subaleurone) of rice grains (Oryza sativa) — a by-product of rice milling. It is one of the most uniquely nutritious Indian cooking oils. Chemical composition: approximately 38% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 35% linoleic acid (omega-6), 25% saturated fats (palmitic and stearic) plus 2–3% unsaponifiable fraction. The unsaponifiable fraction is nutritionally distinctive: oryzanol (1–2% of crude oil) — a mixture of ferulic acid esters of sterols and triterpene alcohols; gamma-oryzanol has documented LDL cholesterol-lowering, antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity in clinical trials; it also improves the oil's own oxidative stability. Also contains phytosterols, squalene, tocotrienols and tocopherols (particularly gamma-tocotrienol). High smoke point (~232°C) makes it ideal for Indian frying and deep-frying of snacks, puris and pakoras. Research: multiple clinical trials show RBO supplementation reduces serum total cholesterol and LDL by 15–20% compared to other vegetable oils. Japan and Korea have used RBO as a premium health oil for decades. Popular in commercial snack food frying in India. Allergen: rice is not a top-8 allergen but rice allergy exists. FSSAI permits rice bran oil as a standard edible vegetable oil. A particularly good choice for Indian high-temperature cooking given its stability and bioactive content.""",
    'groundnut oil': """Groundnut oil (peanut oil / Arachis oil) is a vegetable oil pressed or solvent-extracted from peanuts (Arachis hypogaea / groundnuts), one of India's most important oilseed crops. Fatty acid profile: approximately 48% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 32% linoleic acid (omega-6), 17% saturated (primarily palmitic and stearic) — a moderate to high oleic, omega-6 dominant oil. Distinctive chemical features: contains arachidic acid (C20:0) and small amounts of behenic acid — uncommon very long-chain saturated fatty acids that distinguish peanut oil from most other vegetable oils. Also contains phytosterols (beta-sitosterol), Vitamin E (alpha- and gamma-tocopherol) and resveratrol. Refined peanut oil has a high smoke point (~232°C), making it one of the best oils for high-temperature Indian frying (pakoras, puris, deep-fried snacks). Cold-pressed/expeller-pressed groundnut oil retains more flavour compounds and a characteristic nutty aroma — used in Rajasthani, Gujarati and South Indian cuisines. Allergen — CRITICAL: peanut/groundnut is one of the most prevalent and potentially severe food allergens globally, causing anaphylaxis in sensitised individuals. FSSAI mandates that any food product containing groundnut oil (especially cold-pressed or unrefined, which retains peanut proteins) must declare the allergen prominently on packaging. Highly refined peanut oil has most allergenic proteins removed and is generally considered safer for peanut-allergic individuals (FDA exempts highly refined peanut oil from mandatory peanut labelling), but cold-pressed groundnut oil retains allergenicity. Peanut-allergic individuals should avoid any groundnut oil unless confirmed protein-free.""",
    'mustard oil': """Mustard oil is a pungent, distinctive vegetable oil extracted by cold-pressing or expeller-pressing from the seeds of brown mustard (Brassica juncea), widely used in the cooking traditions of North India, Bengal, Odisha, Punjab and Nepal. Fatty acid composition: approximately 40% erucic acid (a very long-chain monounsaturated C22:1 fatty acid), 12% oleic acid, 12% linoleic acid (omega-6), 14% ALA (alpha-linolenic acid, omega-3) and 7% saturated fats. Pungent flavour: from allyl isothiocyanate — a volatile compound produced when myrosinase enzyme (released by crushing) hydrolyses the glucosinolate sinigrin. The characteristic sharp bite of mustard oil is due to this compound. Regulatory status: mustard oil with high erucic acid (>2%) is banned or restricted as a cooking oil in the USA, Canada and EU — based on animal studies showing cardiac lipidosis at very high erucic acid diets. However, FSSAI permits mustard oil (with erucic acid up to 2% of total fatty acids in edible oils, though traditional Brassica juncea oil exceeds this) for specific Indian uses under a long-standing exemption recognising traditional culinary use. WHO has not established evidence of harm at typical Indian dietary intakes. Nutritional positives: relatively good ALA (omega-3) content; selenium, Vitamin E and glucosinolate metabolites. Antimicrobial properties (allyl isothiocyanate is potent against foodborne pathogens — used as a natural preservative in pickles). Should not be used for cooking by those with a history of cardiovascular disease at high intake; traditional Indian culinary use in modest amounts is generally considered safe by FSSAI.""",
    'groundnut': 'The Indian term for peanut (Arachis hypogaea), one of the most important oilseed crops in India. Rich in protein and healthy monounsaturated fats. Peanut is one of the most common and severe food allergens globally.',
    'sea salt': """Sea salt is produced by the solar evaporation of seawater in shallow coastal pans (salterns / salt pans), a technique practised for over 6,000 years in India (particularly in Gujarat and Odisha, which produce most of India's sea salt), France, Portugal and along the Mediterranean. Unlike refined table salt (produced by vacuum pan evaporation and recrystallisation to near-pure NaCl), sea salt retains a small but measurable complement of trace minerals from seawater — typically magnesium (~0.05%), calcium (~0.04%), potassium (~0.02%), sulfate, and trace amounts of zinc, iron and iodine — along with natural moisture. These trace minerals are the basis for sea salt's slightly more complex, less harsh flavour compared to pure refined salt. However, from a nutritional perspective, sea salt provides essentially the same sodium content as table salt (~38–39% sodium by weight) and the trace minerals in typical serving sizes (1–2 g) are nutritionally insignificant compared to food sources. Important Indian regulatory context: unprocessed sea salt in India is generally not iodised — failure to use iodised salt significantly increases the risk of iodine deficiency disorders (goitre, hypothyroidism, cretinism, intellectual impairment in children). FSSAI mandates iodisation of salt sold for direct human consumption. Sea salt used as a premium ingredient in cooking or in cosmetics (body scrubs, exfoliants, bath salts) bypasses this requirement. Health considerations: identical to regular salt — WHO recommendation of <5 g total salt per day applies. The presence of microplastics in sea salt has been documented in studies (from ocean plastic pollution) — levels are very low but a growing environmental concern. Generally regarded as safe as a food ingredient and cosmetic ingredient.""",
    'dextrose monohydrate': """Dextrose monohydrate (D-glucose monohydrate / C₆H₁₂O₆·H₂O) is the stable crystalline form of glucose — the most fundamental monosaccharide in human metabolism and the universal cellular fuel. Produced by complete enzymatic hydrolysis of starch (primarily corn/maize) using alpha-amylase and glucoamylase, yielding 100% glucose, which is crystallised as the water-containing monohydrate form. Constitutes approximately 96% glucose and 4% water by weight. Sweetness: ~70–75% that of sucrose. Glycaemic Index: 100 — the international reference standard. Applications: bread and fermented bakery products (preferred yeast fermentation substrate, produces CO₂ and ethanol for leavening); cured and processed meats (fermentation substrate and browning agent); confectionery (lowers sweetness, reduces crystallisation); sports nutrition and rehydration products (rapidly absorbed via SGLT1 co-transport with sodium); pharmaceutical IV solutions (5% dextrose in water); oral rehydration therapy (ORS — glucose absorption drives water absorption in the gut). People with diabetes or insulin resistance must monitor intake carefully as it causes rapid blood glucose elevation. FSSAI, FDA (GRAS), EU and CODEX recognise as a safe food ingredient.""",

    # ── Cosmetic & Skincare Actives ────────────────────────────────────────────
    'niacinamide': 'Niacinamide (nicotinamide) is the amide form of Vitamin B3, a water-soluble vitamin found in meat, fish, eggs and green vegetables. Extensively studied in dermatology for its ability to reduce hyperpigmentation, minimise pore appearance, strengthen the skin barrier, reduce sebum production and improve fine lines. One of the best-tolerated cosmetic actives.',
    'nicotinamide': """Nicotinamide (niacinamide / nicotinic acid amide / pyridine-3-carboxamide) is the amide form of Vitamin B3 (niacin) — one of two principal interconvertible forms of this essential water-soluble vitamin, the other being nicotinic acid (niacin). Unlike nicotinic acid, nicotinamide does not cause the characteristic skin flushing (prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation) associated with niacin supplementation. Biochemical role: the precursor for the coenzymes NAD⁺ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) and NADP⁺ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate), which are essential electron carriers in hundreds of metabolic reactions including glycolysis, Krebs cycle, fatty acid synthesis and oxidation, and mitochondrial electron transport. Also a substrate for sirtuin enzymes (NAD⁺-dependent deacetylases involved in gene regulation, DNA repair and longevity pathways) and PARP enzymes (DNA damage repair). Deficiency: pellagra — characterised by the "4 Ds": Dermatitis, Diarrhoea, Dementia and Death if untreated. Historically common in populations relying on corn (maize) without nixtamalisation. RDA: 14–16 mg NE/day (adults). As a food supplement: added to fortified cereals, flour and other staple foods; used in multi-vitamin supplements. As a cosmetic active: nicotinamide/niacinamide is extensively studied and used in skincare — inhibits melanosome transfer from melanocytes to keratinocytes (brightens skin and reduces dark spots), strengthens the skin barrier (increases ceramide and fatty acid synthesis), reduces sebum production, has anti-inflammatory effects useful in acne and rosacea, and reduces fine lines and wrinkles in clinical studies. At 4–5% concentration in topical formulations, efficacy is well supported by RCTs. Generally excellent tolerability — suitable for sensitive skin. Approved by FSSAI as a food nutrient supplement; widely used in Indian skincare products.""",
    'sodium hyaluronate': 'The sodium salt of hyaluronic acid, the primary moisture-binding molecule in the skin. Lower molecular weight than hyaluronic acid, allowing deeper penetration into the stratum corneum. Used extensively in serums and moisturisers for intense hydration.',
    'kojic acid': 'A naturally occurring chelation agent produced during fermentation of rice (sake production) and by Aspergillus and Penicillium fungi. Used in skincare as a tyrosinase inhibitor to reduce melanin production. Approved in Japan (1%); EU Cosmetics Regulation evaluated at 1% in face care products.',
    'alpha arbutin': 'A biosynthetic glycoside of hydroquinone derived from bearberry (Arctostaphylos uva-ursi). The alpha form is more stable and effective than beta-arbutin. Used in skincare as a skin-brightening agent. EU Cosmetics Regulation permits alpha-arbutin at up to 2% in face care.',
    'arbutin': 'A natural glycoside of hydroquinone found in bearberry, blueberry and cranberry plants. Used in skincare to reduce dark spots by inhibiting tyrosinase. EU permits alpha-arbutin at 2% (face) and 0.5% (body).',
    'glycolic acid': 'The smallest alpha hydroxy acid (AHA), derived from sugar cane (E-glycolic acid / Hydroxyacetic acid). Used as a chemical exfoliant in skincare to dissolve dead skin cells, improve texture, stimulate collagen and treat hyperpigmentation. FDA and EU approved for cosmetic use at specified concentrations.',
    'azelaic acid': 'A naturally occurring saturated dicarboxylic acid found in wheat, rye and barley, also produced by Malassezia yeasts on the skin. Prescription-strength (15–20%) is approved by FDA for acne and rosacea. OTC concentrations (≤10%) are widely available in skincare.',
    'bakuchiol': 'A meroterpene phenol extracted from the seeds and leaves of Psoralea corylifolia (babchi plant), used in Ayurvedic medicine. Clinical studies show retinol-like anti-ageing effects (collagen synthesis, skin smoothing) with significantly lower irritation. Safe in pregnancy unlike retinol.',
    'squalane': 'A stable, saturated form of squalene (a natural lipid found in the skin\'s sebum and shark liver), derived by hydrogenating plant-sourced squalene (from sugarcane, olive or amaranth). A lightweight, non-comedogenic, skin-identical emollient. Very well tolerated.',
    'centella asiatica': 'A tropical medicinal herb (Cica / Gotu kola) used in Ayurvedic, Chinese and African traditional medicine. Its active compounds (madecassoside, asiaticoside, asiatic acid) have clinically demonstrated wound-healing, anti-inflammatory and collagen-stimulating properties.',
    'allantoin': 'A naturally occurring phytochemical found in comfrey root, burdock root and sugar beet. Used in skincare for its soothing, keratolytic (softening) and wound-healing properties. CIR Expert Panel confirmed it safe for cosmetic use. Very well tolerated, including on sensitive skin.',
    'bisabolol': 'A naturally occurring sesquiterpene alcohol first isolated from German chamomile (Matricaria chamomilla) and also produced synthetically. Widely used in skincare for anti-inflammatory, anti-irritant, soothing and skin-penetration-enhancing properties. CIR reviewed and confirmed safe.',
    'adenosine': 'A naturally occurring nucleoside found in all living organisms, a building block of DNA. Used in skincare for its anti-ageing effects. EU Cosmetics Regulation approved at 0.04% in face care for skin smoothing. Well tolerated in clinical studies.',
    'zinc oxide': 'A naturally occurring mineral compound of zinc and oxygen (ZnO). Used as a broad-spectrum physical (mineral) UV filter in sunscreens (reflects both UVA and UVB), as a soothing anti-inflammatory agent in skin creams and as a baby powder ingredient. FDA approved Category I UV filter.',
    'zinc pyrithione': 'A zinc complex of the pyrithione ion (ZPT), used as the primary antifungal and antibacterial agent in anti-dandruff shampoos since the 1960s. Effective against Malassezia furfur, the yeast associated with dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis. EU Cosmetics Regulation updated restrictions in 2021.',
    'madecassoside': 'A key active compound from Centella asiatica, clinically studied for its wound-healing, collagen-stimulating and anti-inflammatory effects. Used as a concentrated active in skincare products for sensitive and damaged skin.',
    'sea buckthorn oil': 'A nutrient-dense oil from the berries and seeds of Hippophae rhamnoides. Exceptionally rich in Vitamin C, Vitamin E, carotenoids (including beta-carotene) and rare omega-7 (palmitoleic acid). Used in skincare for anti-ageing and wound-healing properties.',
    'rosehip oil': 'A plant oil cold-pressed from the seeds of wild rose (Rosa canina) hips. Rich in linoleic acid, alpha-linolenic acid and naturally occurring trans-retinoic acid. Widely used in skincare for anti-ageing, scar reduction and brightening.',
    'octinoxate': 'Ethylhexyl methoxycinnamate (OMC), a UV-B absorbing organic UV filter and one of the most widely used chemical sunscreen actives globally. Under regulatory review in some jurisdictions for potential endocrine activity and coral reef toxicity.',
    'oxybenzone': 'Benzophenone-3, an organic UV-A and UV-B absorbing filter used in chemical sunscreens. Penetrates the skin and is detected in blood, urine and breast milk. Associated with endocrine disruption concerns and coral bleaching. Banned for reef protection in Hawaii, Palau and the US Virgin Islands.',
    'avobenzone': 'Butyl methoxydibenzoylmethane, the most widely used UVA-only organic UV filter globally. Photounstable without stabilisers (e.g., octocrylene, homosalate). EU and FDA approved at specified concentrations.',
    'octocrylene': """Octocrylene (INCI: Octocrylene / INN: octocrylene / chemical name: 2-ethylhexyl (2E)-3-(3-benzoylphenyl)propenoate or 2-ethylhexyl 2-cyano-3,3-diphenyl acrylate) is a UV absorber used in sunscreens to filter UVB radiation (peak absorption ~303 nm) and short-wave UVA2 radiation. In addition to its own UV-filtering action, it acts as a photostabiliser — absorbing the energy from unstable UV filters like avobenzone (which degrades rapidly in sunlight) and dissipating it safely as heat, dramatically extending the effective lifetime of the sunscreen. Used in: sunscreens (often formulated with avobenzone), daily moisturisers with SPF, lip balms and hair care with UV protection. Concerns: (1) Benzophenone formation — octocrylene degrades over time (especially in products that also contain avobenzone) to benzophenone, a potentially carcinogenic compound. A 2021 FDA study found benzophenone contamination in many sunscreen products containing octocrylene, prompting reformulation concerns. FDA classifies octocrylene as not GRAS/GRASE (insufficient systemic safety data to conclusively affirm or deny safety) — meaning more data are needed, not that it is confirmed unsafe. (2) Systemic absorption — octocrylene is absorbed through the skin and detected in blood, urine and breast milk with regular use, as documented in a 2020 FDA study. Systemic exposure levels and their health significance are under evaluation. (3) Photoallergy — cross-reacts with ketoprofen (an NSAID photoallergen), causing severe photoallergic contact dermatitis in ketoprofen-sensitised individuals. (4) Environmental concerns — octocrylene accumulates in coral polyp tissue (coral reef toxicity); Hawaii and several island jurisdictions have banned or restricted reef-toxic UV filters. Approved by EU at max 10% (photostabiliser/UV filter combination), USA (FDA's "GRASE" evaluation ongoing), India and globally at specified limits.""",
    'charcoal': """Activated charcoal (activated carbon) is a highly porous, black carbon material produced by heating carbon-rich organic materials — typically wood, coconut shells, bamboo, or peat — at 600–900°C in an inert atmosphere (carbonisation), followed by "activation" through steam treatment or chemical activation with zinc chloride or phosphoric acid at high temperatures. The activation process creates an enormous network of tiny pores, dramatically increasing the surface area (500–1500 m²/g — a teaspoon can have the surface area of a football field). This extensive surface area gives activated charcoal exceptional adsorptive capacity for organic molecules. Medical use (systemic): oral activated charcoal (50–100g in water) is the gold-standard emergency treatment for many oral poisonings and drug overdoses — it physically adsorbs many toxins in the GI tract, preventing systemic absorption. It must be administered within 1–2 hours of ingestion for maximum effectiveness. Medical activated charcoal is given in specific clinical settings, not for routine consumption. In food: activated charcoal is used as a black food colourant (E153/INS 153 — vegetable carbon) in gourmet food and beverages (black burger buns, black ice cream, charcoal cocktails). Critical safety concern: consuming activated charcoal alongside medications can significantly reduce their absorption — oral contraceptives, antiretrovirals, blood thinners and many other medications may lose efficacy if taken within 2 hours of charcoal. Activated charcoal food products should not be consumed regularly by people on prescription medications. In cosmetics: used in face masks, cleansers, toothpastes and hair care for its purported "detoxifying" and adsorptive properties; however, the skin is an effective barrier — topical activated charcoal cannot "draw out" deep pore toxins; it can adsorb surface sebum and impurities. Tooth-whitening: no clinical evidence that charcoal toothpaste whitens teeth; the abrasive particles may actually damage enamel with prolonged use. Generally safe for topical use. FSSAI and EU permit E153 as a food colour.""",

    # ── Indian Herbs & Ayurvedic Ingredients ───────────────────────────────────
    'amla': """Amla (Indian gooseberry / Phyllanthus emblica / Emblica officinalis), native to the Indian subcontinent, is one of Ayurveda's most revered medicinal plants — classified as a "rasayana" (rejuvenating agent) and used for over 3,000 years. The small, pale green-yellow sour fruit is nutritionally exceptional: one of the richest plant sources of Vitamin C (ascorbic acid 600–900 mg/100g fresh fruit — the heat-stable forms include emblicanins A and B bound to gallic acid), along with gallic acid, ellagic acid, chebulinic acid, quercetin, kaempferol, rutin and other polyphenols. The high tannin-complexed Vitamin C may be more stable to heat than free ascorbic acid. India produces approximately 800,000 tonnes/year of amla. Used in: Chyawanprash (the most widely consumed Ayurvedic formulation — amla is the principal ingredient), amla hair oils (promotes hair growth, reduces premature greying by DHT inhibition and antioxidant protection of hair follicles), amla juice and powder supplements, traditional pickles (murabba), candies (amla candy), and as a natural vitamin C supplement. Health benefits documented: potent antioxidant (one of the highest DPPH radical scavenging capacities), hepatoprotective (protects liver against toxins in animal models), hypolipidaemic (reduces LDL in clinical studies), immunomodulatory, anti-inflammatory, antidiabetic (alpha-glucosidase inhibition) and anti-platelet. AYUSH Ministry regulates amla-containing Ayurvedic products; FSSAI permits amla in food and supplements. Generally very safe at food and traditional supplement doses. High tannin content may reduce iron absorption at the same meal.""",
    'amalaki': """Amalaki is the Sanskrit name for amla or Indian gooseberry (Phyllanthus emblica syn. Emblica officinalis Gaertn.), one of the most revered herbs in Ayurvedic medicine and one of the three fruits of the triphala formulation. It is classified as a "rasayana" (rejuvenating herb) in classical Ayurvedic texts including the Charaka Samhita and Ashtanga Hridayam. The fresh fruit is one of the richest natural sources of Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its analogues) among botanical sources — with some studies reporting 600–900 mg per 100 g, though the heat-stable nature of amalaki's Vitamin C (possibly bound to tannins as emblicanin A and B) distinguishes it from synthetic ascorbic acid. Key phytochemicals: gallic acid, ellagic acid, chebulinic acid, emblicanins A and B, quercetin, kaempferol, punigluconin, pedunculagin, vitamin C, minerals (especially iron and calcium) and polyphenols. Traditional uses: digestive tonic and laxative (vibhitavi — cleanses the bowel), liver protectant, hair tonic (inhibits DHT, strengthens hair follicles), rejuvenative, anti-aging (antioxidant load), eye health (triphala eye wash), respiratory health. Modern research: antioxidant activity extremely well documented; hepatoprotective, anti-inflammatory (COX-2 inhibition), anti-platelet, hypolipidaemic, immunomodulatory and adaptogenic effects demonstrated in vitro and in animal models; human clinical evidence accumulating for dyslipidaemia, diabetes management and liver health. Used widely in Indian hair oils, health supplements, Chyawanprash (the classic Ayurvedic tonic where amalaki is the main ingredient), amla powder, amla juice and standardised extracts. AYUSH Ministry (India) regulates Ayurvedic products containing amalaki. Generally safe at food and traditional supplement doses; very high concentrated supplements may be contraindicated in anticoagulant therapy (antiplatelet effect).""",
    'brahmi': """Brahmi (Bacopa monnieri) is a creeping marsh herb of the Plantaginaceae family native to wetlands across India, Southeast Asia and the Americas. Used in Ayurvedic medicine for over 3,000 years as a primary medhya rasayana (brain tonic herb) — prescribed for memory, cognition and learning in classical texts including Charaka Samhita. Key phytochemicals: bacosides A and B (triterpenoid saponins — the primary active compounds standardised in commercial extracts), bacopaside compounds, alkaloids (brahmine), flavonoids (luteolin, apigenin) and phenylethanoids. Mechanism: bacosides enhance synaptic transmission by increasing acetylcholine synthesis and release; promote neuronal dendrite elongation and nerve impulse transmission rate; reduce amyloid beta aggregation; antioxidant and neuroprotective. Clinical evidence: multiple double-blind, placebo-controlled trials (primarily Keenmind and BacoMind formulations) demonstrate improved memory recall, processing speed and attention in healthy adults after 8–12 weeks of supplementation (typically 300–450 mg standardised extract/day). Evidence is also emerging for anxiety reduction and stress adaptation. Used in: cognitive supplements, memory enhancement products, hair oils (for scalp health) and Ayurvedic preparations. AYUSH Ministry of India regulates Brahmi-containing Ayurvedic products. Generally safe at therapeutic doses; high doses may cause GI discomfort (nausea, increased gut motility). May interact with anticholinergic medications. Not recommended in pregnancy (insufficient evidence).""",
    'bhringraj': """Bhringraj (Eclipta prostrata / Eclipta alba / Eclipta erecta) is an annual herb of the Asteraceae family, native to moist and swampy areas across India, China and Brazil. Known as "kesharaj" (Sanskrit: "ruler of hair") for its traditional use in hair care. Key phytochemicals: wedelolactone (a coumestan with hepatoprotective, antivenom and anti-inflammatory properties), ecliptine, eclalbasaponins, alpha-terthienyl (a polythienyl compound), flavonoids and sterols. Traditional uses in Ayurveda: (1) Hair care — used as a primary ingredient in hair oils (bhringraj oil, traditionally in sesame or coconut oil base) to promote hair growth, reduce hair fall, prevent premature greying and condition the scalp; (2) Liver health — wedelolactone has documented hepatoprotective activity against carbon tetrachloride and other liver toxins in animal models; used in Ayurvedic liver tonics; (3) Wound healing — anti-inflammatory and tissue-regenerating properties; (4) Anti-inflammatory and analgesic. Modern research: bhringraj extract has demonstrated 5-alpha reductase inhibition (reduces DHT, the hormone responsible for androgenic alopecia) in some in vitro studies — a mechanistic basis for traditional hair loss prevention claims. Hair growth promotion via follicular stimulation and melanin production support. Used in: bhringraj hair oils, shampoos, conditioners, scalp serums, Ayurvedic tonics and supplements. AYUSH Ministry regulates Ayurvedic products containing bhringraj. Generally safe at traditional and cosmetic use levels. Rare: may cause contact dermatitis in Asteraceae-sensitive individuals.""",
    'shikakai': """Shikakai (Acacia concinna / senegalia rugata) is a climbing shrub of the Fabaceae family native to tropical regions of South and Southeast Asia, cultivated primarily in India. The pods, bark and leaves are all traditionally used, but the pods are the primary commercial ingredient. The name "shikakai" comes from Hindi meaning "fruit for hair." Key active constituents: saponins (~30% in pods) — primarily shikakainin and some unique saponins from the Acacia genus — responsible for the natural lathering property; natural pH of shikakai is mildly acidic (~4.5–5.5), close to the natural pH of scalp and hair (pH ~3.67–4.7). Uses in hair care: used for centuries in India as a natural hair cleanser and conditioner — the saponins create a gentle, low-foaming lather that cleans hair without the harsh stripping effect of anionic sulfate surfactants (SLES, SLS); the mild acidic pH helps smooth the cuticle layer of hair, improving shine and reducing tangling; traditionally the pods are ground to a paste or boiled and strained. The low pH is particularly beneficial for chemically treated or dye-damaged hair. Also used as a scalp conditioner and to reduce dandruff and scalp irritation. Commercial use: dried pods, powder, liquid extracts and decoctions are used in shampoos, hair powders, herbal hair care formulations and Ayurvedic products. Generally very safe; low sensitisation potential. A biodegradable, plant-based surfactant alternative. AYUSH and FSSAI recognise in traditional product categories.""",
    'reetha': """Reetha (soapnut / aritha / ritha / Sapindus mukorossi / S. saponaria) is the dried fruit shell (pericarp) of the soapnut tree, native to the tropical and subtropical regions of India and Nepal. The genus name Sapindus is derived from "Sapo Indicus" (Indian soap). The dried shells (without seeds) are the commercial ingredient. Active constituents: saponins (~12–15% of dry weight — predominantly triterpenoid saponins including saponin, sapindoside A and B) — natural anionic surfactants that lower surface tension when mixed with water, creating a natural, biodegradable foam. The saponins are amphiphilic molecules (hydrophilic head + hydrophobic tail) functioning mechanistically like synthetic surfactants but derived from a natural source. Traditional and modern uses: (1) Hair cleansing — soaked reetha shells release a saponin-rich liquid used as a gentle shampoo base; removes dirt, sebum and pollution without harsh synthetic surfactants; (2) Laundry and general cleaning — used in Ayurvedic laundering of delicate textiles; (3) Jewelry cleaning; (4) Cosmetic formulations — liquid extracts incorporated in natural shampoos, body washes and face cleansers. Environmental advantage: reetha is fully biodegradable, non-toxic to aquatic organisms and produces no synthetic chemical waste — popular in eco-friendly personal care formulations. Generally well tolerated; rare cases of contact sensitivity in saponin-sensitive individuals. AYUSH Ministry and FSSAI recognise reetha in traditional product categories. Widely sold in Indian markets as a natural hair care ingredient.""",
    'soapnut': """Soapnut (Sapindus mukorossi / S. saponaria / Sapindus trifoliatus) is the same plant as reetha — the dried pericarp (outer shell) of the soapberry tree fruit. See the reetha entry for full botanical, chemical and functional details. "Soapnut" is the English common name; "reetha" or "aritha" is the Hindi/Indian name. The dried fruit shells contain 12–15% triterpenoid saponins (natural surfactants) that produce a gentle lather in water, used as a biodegradable, natural cleanser in hair care products, laundry formulations and general cleaning. The saponin content varies by species: S. mukorossi (Himalayan soapnut) has higher saponin content and better lathering than S. saponaria. Used in natural and organic personal care product formulations as a sustainable, plant-derived surfactant alternative to synthetic sulfate-based detergents. Generally hypoallergenic and biodegradable. Recognised by AYUSH Ministry and FSSAI in traditional cosmetic and food categories.""",
    'triphala': """Triphala (Sanskrit: tri = three, phala = fruits) is the most fundamental and widely prescribed polyherbal formulation in Ayurvedic medicine — a combination of equal parts of three dried fruits: amalaki (Emblica officinalis / amla), haritaki (Terminalia chebula) and bibhitaki (Terminalia bellirica). Classical Ayurvedic texts (Charaka Samhita, Sushruta Samhita, Ashtanga Hridayam) describe Triphala as a rasayana (rejuvenating), tridosha-balancing (balances vata, pitta and kapha) and "divya aushadhi" (excellent medicine). Key bioactive compounds: gallic acid, ellagic acid, chebulic acid, emblicanins A and B, chebulagic acid, chebulinic acid, bellericanin, beta-sitosterol, quercetin and numerous polyphenols from all three fruits — one of the highest polyphenol concentrations of any Ayurvedic formula. Health applications: (1) Digestive tonic — the best-studied indication; promotes bowel regularity (mild laxative), reduces constipation, reduces intestinal inflammation (IBD); (2) Eye health — triphala eye wash (boiled, strained and cooled triphala water as eye drops) used for conjunctivitis and eye irritation; (3) Oral health — triphala mouthwash has evidence for reducing dental plaque and gingival bleeding (RCTs showing comparable efficacy to chlorhexidine); (4) Anti-obesity and metabolic syndrome (emerging clinical evidence); (5) Immunomodulatory; (6) Skin care (anti-inflammatory, wound-healing). Safety: generally very safe at traditional doses; very high doses can cause diarrhoea (tannin content) and may interact with anticoagulants (anti-platelet effects of the component herbs). Not recommended in pregnancy (haritaki is contraindicated). Regulated by AYUSH Ministry (India) as a classical Ayurvedic formulation.""",
    'giloy': """Giloy (Tinospora cordifolia / guduchi / amrita) is a climbing shrub of the Menispermaceae family native to the Indian subcontinent. One of the most important herbs in classical Ayurvedic medicine — considered one of the "divya aushadhi" (divine medicines). The stems (and roots) are the medicinal parts. Key phytochemicals: alkaloids (berberine, palmatine, tinosporin, cordifoliside), diterpenoid lactones (tinosporon, tinosporide), glucosides, polysaccharides and bitter compounds (tinocordioside). Pharmacological activity: immunomodulatory (stimulates macrophage activation, NK cell activity, cytokine production), adaptogenic, antipyretic (widely used for dengue and malaria fever in India), anti-inflammatory (COX-2 inhibition, NF-κB pathway), antidiabetic (improves insulin sensitivity, inhibits alpha-glucosidase), hepatoprotective and antioxidant. Widely used in: Ayurvedic immunity supplements, kadha (herbal decoction), Giloy Ghana tablets, Amritarishta and COVID-19 immunity booster products during the pandemic. Safety concern: in 2021, multiple cases of severe acute liver injury (drug-induced liver injury, DILI) were attributed to giloy-containing Ayurvedic products in India. The CDSCO and AYUSH Ministry reviewed these cases. Concerns included: adulteration with the more toxic Tinospora crispa, or dose-dependent hepatotoxicity. Individuals with autoimmune conditions (lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, multiple sclerosis) should avoid — immune stimulation may worsen autoimmune disease. Not recommended during pregnancy. People with immunosuppressant medications should consult a physician.""",
    'guduchi': """Guduchi (Sanskrit: गुडूची) is another name for giloy (Tinospora cordifolia Willd. Miers), a climbing shrub of the family Menispermaceae native to India and the tropical regions of the Indian subcontinent. One of the most important herbs in classical Ayurvedic medicine — listed in the Charaka Samhita as one of the medhya rasayana (rejuvenating herbs beneficial for mind and immunity) and as the most important herb for treating fever (jwara). Common names: giloy (Hindi), guduchi (Sanskrit), amrita (Sanskrit — "divine nectar") and heart-leaved moonseed (English). Active phytochemicals: diterpenoid lactones (tinosporon, tinosporide, tinosporaside), alkaloids (berberine — a well-studied antimicrobial and antidiabetic compound), glucosides (tinocordiside), polysaccharides, glycosides and minerals. Berberine in guduchi explains significant pharmacological overlap with berberine research. Documented pharmacological activity: immunomodulatory (stimulates macrophage activation, natural killer cell activity), adaptogenic (reduces cortisol and stress), antipyretic (particularly for dengue and chikungunya fever — widely used in India during outbreaks), anti-inflammatory, antidiabetic (improves insulin sensitivity), hepatoprotective, antimicrobial and antioxidant. Used in: Ayurvedic tablets/kadha for immunity, fever, diabetes, arthritis and liver health; ayurvedic product Giloy Ghana, Guduchi Satva (starch extract), Amritarishta; included in many Indian immunity booster products. Safety concerns: a 2021 case series from India reported acute drug-induced liver injury (DILI) in patients taking various Ayurvedic products containing guduchi — multiple hepatology societies and government bodies investigated. The CDSCO (India) and AYUSH Ministry conducted reviews. Quality control and adulteration concerns exist; some products may contain Tinospora crispa (a related but more toxic species) or other adulterants. People with autoimmune conditions should use cautiously (immune stimulation may worsen autoimmune disease). Not recommended during pregnancy.""",
    'moringa': """Moringa oleifera (the drumstick tree / horseradish tree / "miracle tree") is a fast-growing tree native to the sub-Himalayan foothills of northwestern India, now cultivated across tropical and subtropical regions worldwide. The leaves are nutritionally exceptional — fresh leaves contain: Vitamin C (~51mg/100g), beta-carotene (provitamin A ~6780 mcg/100g), Vitamin E, B vitamins (riboflavin, B6, folate), calcium (~185mg/100g), potassium (~337mg/100g), iron (~4mg/100g) and all 9 essential amino acids — making it a rare vegetable with complete protein. The dried leaf powder is more concentrated (approximately 10x more nutrients per gram due to water removal) but heat-sensitive vitamins are reduced. All parts are used: leaves (food and supplement), seed pods (drumsticks — a common Indian vegetable), seeds (water purification — moringa seed cake coagulates turbid water), seed oil (Ben oil — cosmetic and edible), roots and bark (traditional medicine). Health evidence: clinical trials show moringa leaf powder reduces fasting blood glucose in type 2 diabetics; reduces LDL cholesterol; anti-inflammatory effects (quercetin, kaempferol, isothiocyanates — including 4-alpha-L-rhamnosyloxy benzyl isothiocyanate, specific to moringa); glucosinolate derivatives support liver detoxification. Used in: nutritional supplements, protein powders, dietary capsules, functional foods, cosmetics (anti-ageing, brightening). Generally very safe at food and supplement levels. The dried leaf powder is used as a nutritional supplement across India, particularly in maternal and child nutrition programmes. AYUSH Ministry regulates moringa-based Ayurvedic products. FSSAI permits in foods as a food ingredient.""",
    'karela': """Karela (bitter gourd / bitter melon / Momordica charantia) is a tropical vine of the Cucurbitaceae family, cultivated widely in India, South and Southeast Asia, Africa and the Caribbean. The fruits are distinctively bitter due to compounds including momordicin, charantin and other cucurbitane-type triterpenoids. Nutritional profile: rich in Vitamin C (~84mg/100g), Vitamin A (beta-carotene), folate, potassium, zinc, iron and dietary fibre. Very low in calories (~17 kcal/100g). Key bioactive compounds: charantin (steroidal glycoside mixture — documented hypoglycaemic activity); polypeptide-p (insulin-like peptide purified from karela seeds and tissues — lowers blood glucose in animal models); vicine (a pyrimidine nucleoside); momordicin; and diverse triterpenoids and flavonoids. Blood glucose lowering: karela is the most widely studied plant for type 2 diabetes management in traditional medicine globally. Multiple clinical trials have shown karela juice or extract reduces fasting blood glucose and HbA1c in patients with type 2 diabetes, though effect size varies significantly between studies. The mechanisms include: inhibition of intestinal glucose absorption (alpha-glucosidase inhibition), improved insulin sensitivity, enhanced glucose uptake in peripheral tissues and possible beta-cell stimulation. Used in: vegetable preparations (bitter karela sabzi, stuffed karela), karela juice (health supplement), Ayurvedic diabetic tablets and syrups, herbal supplements. Cautions: people on anti-diabetic medications should monitor blood glucose carefully when consuming karela extract — risk of additive hypoglycaemia; not recommended in pregnancy (uterotonic effects documented); vicine may trigger haemolytic crisis in G6PD-deficient individuals. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'methi': """Methi (fenugreek / Trigonella foenum-graecum) is an annual plant of the Fabaceae family, native to the Eastern Mediterranean and South Asia. One of the oldest known medicinal plants — seeds found in Egyptian tombs dating to 1500 BCE. Cultivated across India for both its seeds (used as a spice) and leaves (fenugreek leaves / methi leaves — fresh or dried as kasuri methi). Chemical composition of seeds: galactomannan (~45% — a soluble dietary fibre forming a thick gel; responsible for much of the hypoglycaemic and cholesterol-lowering activity); saponins (including diosgenin — a steroidal saponin used as a precursor in synthetic steroid hormone production); alkaloids (trigonelline — responsible for the characteristic bitter flavour and some of the glucose-lowering activity); 4-hydroxyisoleucine (an unusual amino acid with documented insulin-secretion stimulating activity). Health effects: (1) Blood sugar management — multiple RCTs show fenugreek seed powder (10–25g/day) significantly reduces fasting blood glucose and HbA1c in type 2 diabetics; reduces postprandial glucose spike via slowing gastric emptying and glucose absorption; (2) Cholesterol reduction — galactomannan reduces bile acid and cholesterol reabsorption; (3) Lactation support — traditionally used as a galactagogue (promotes breast milk production); some clinical evidence supports this; (4) Hair care — used in hair oils for strengthening and reducing hair fall; (5) Digestive health. Used as: a spice in Indian cooking (whole or ground seeds), kasuri methi (dried leaves), hair oil ingredient, diabetes supplement. People on anti-diabetic medications should monitor blood glucose carefully when taking fenugreek supplements. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient and spice.""",
    'mulethi': """Mulethi (licorice root / Glycyrrhiza glabra / yashtimadhu in Sanskrit) is the dried root of the licorice plant, a leguminous perennial native to southern Europe, the Middle East and Asia. One of the most ancient medicinal plants in Ayurvedic, Chinese, Unani and Western herbal traditions. Key phytochemicals: glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizinic acid — a triterpenoid saponin comprising ~2–14% of the root, responsible for the characteristic sweet taste — approximately 50x sweeter than sucrose); glabridin (the primary isoflavane — well-studied for skin brightening via tyrosinase inhibition, anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects); liquiritigenin, isoliquiritigenin, glycyrrhetinic acid (the aglycone of glycyrrhizin). Uses and effects: (1) Respiratory — mulethi is a cornerstone of Ayurvedic respiratory formulations (expectorant, antitussive, soothing for bronchial inflammation); (2) Oral care — antibacterial against oral pathogens (S. mutans), anti-inflammatory for gingival health; used in toothpastes, mouthwashes; (3) Skin brightening — glabridin inhibits tyrosinase enzyme (reduces melanin production), incorporated in skin brightening creams; anti-inflammatory reduces post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation; (4) Gastrointestinal — soothing for gastric mucosa; (5) Anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory. Safety concern: glycyrrhizin at high doses (>100mg/day) can cause pseudoaldosteronism — sodium and water retention, potassium loss, hypertension and hypokalaemia. Can interact with antihypertensive and cardiac medications. Deglycyrrhizinated licorice (DGL) supplements have the glycyrrhizin removed and avoid this side effect. FSSAI permits in Ayurvedic preparations and as a food flavouring. Not recommended at high doses during pregnancy or in hypertensive patients.""",
    'chandan': """Chandan (sandalwood / Santalum album) is a hemiparasitic tree native to the Eastern Ghats of peninsular India, Sri Lanka and parts of Indonesia. It is one of the world's most valued aromatic trees — the heartwood and essential oil (containing alpha- and beta-santalol as the primary fragrant compounds) have been used for over 4,000 years in religious ceremonies, Ayurvedic medicine, perfumery and skincare. India is the principal historic producer, though wild populations are now critically depleted (CITES Appendix II); most commercial supply comes from Australia and plantations. Key active compounds: alpha-santalol (~55%), beta-santalol (~25%), santyl acetate, and other sesquiterpene alcohols. Properties in cosmetics and medicine: (1) Anti-inflammatory — alpha-santalol inhibits NF-κB and COX-2 pathways; effective against skin inflammation, redness and acne; (2) Antimicrobial — active against Gram-positive bacteria and Candida species; (3) Skin brightening — reduces tyrosinase activity and melanin synthesis; used in fairness creams and skin tone-evening formulations; (4) Cooling and soothing — the cooling sensation of sandalwood paste on skin is a distinctive Indian traditional use; (5) Anti-ageing — promotes fibroblast activity and collagen synthesis in vitro; (6) Fragrance — one of the most valued base notes in perfumery (deep, warm, woody, creamy). Uses in: luxury skincare, face powders, face packs (chandan face pack), soaps, incense, and Ayurvedic preparations. High value leads to frequent adulteration in commercial products. AYUSH Ministry regulates Ayurvedic preparations; sandalwood oil is listed as a cosmetic ingredient. Generally safe; rare cases of contact allergy to sandalwood oil.""",
    'haritaki': """Haritaki (Terminalia chebula / Indian walnut / chebulic myrobalan) is a large deciduous tree of the Combretaceae family native to South Asia. Known as the "king of medicines" in Tibetan medicine, and one of the three fruits of Triphala. The dried fruit (without seed) is used medicinally. Key phytochemicals: chebulic acid, chebulinic acid, chebulagic acid, corilagin, gallic acid, ellagic acid, punicalagin, terflavin B and other hydrolysable tannins (one of the highest tannin concentrations among medicinal plants). Tannins give haritaki potent astringent, antioxidant and antimicrobial properties. Traditional uses: (1) Digestive tonic and mild laxative — used for constipation, improving digestion and bowel regularity; (2) Liver protector (hepatoprotective effects of tannins documented in animal models); (3) Anti-inflammatory and wound healing; (4) Antimicrobial — active against E. coli, S. aureus, Salmonella; (5) Oral health — antibacterial against oral pathogens; used in herbal toothpastes; (6) Rejuvenative (rasayana) — improves strength and immunity. Modern research: antioxidant activity is exceptional; chebulagic acid inhibits HIV integrase (potential antiviral interest); haritaki extracts inhibit alpha-glucosidase (antidiabetic interest); anti-inflammatory via COX-2 inhibition. Used in: Triphala formulations, haritaki churna (powder), digestive supplements, herbal oral care products. Cautions: contraindicated in pregnancy (potential uterotonic effects at high doses); the laxative effect at high doses can cause diarrhoea; high tannin content may reduce iron and nutrient absorption at the same meal. Regulated by AYUSH Ministry as a classical Ayurvedic ingredient.""",
    'bibhitaki': """Bibhitaki (Sanskrit: विभीतकी / Vibhitaki) is the Sanskrit name for Terminalia bellirica (Gaertn.) Roxb., a large deciduous tree of the Combretaceae family native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia. Known as Baheda (Hindi/Urdu), beleric myrobalan or belleric (English). One of the three fruits of the classical Ayurvedic formulation Triphala (with amalaki and haritaki), considered foundational to Ayurvedic therapeutic practice. The dried fruit (without seed) is the medicinal part used. Key phytochemicals: hydrolysable tannins (ellagitannins — gallic acid, ellagic acid, chebulagic acid, bellericanin, terniflorin, granatin B), phenolic acids, flavonoids, saponins (terpene glycosides), fatty acids (linolenic, palmitic, oleic), minerals and beta-sitosterol. Traditional uses: bronchial and respiratory health (expectorant and antitussive — one of the few Ayurvedic herbs specifically for the lungs and upper respiratory tract), digestive tonic, liver health, eye health (triphala eye wash for conjunctivitis), hair care (promotes growth, reduces dandruff in hair oils), mild laxative, anti-dysenteric and antimicrobial. Modern pharmacological research: antioxidant activity (tannins are potent free radical scavengers), hepatoprotective (protects liver against chemical-induced damage in animal models), antimicrobial against Staphylococcus aureus and other pathogens, antifungal, hypolipidaemic (reduces LDL cholesterol in rat models), antidiabetic (alpha-glucosidase inhibition) and anti-inflammatory (inhibits COX-2 pathway). Used in: Triphala powder/tablets, Bibhitak churna, ayurvedic cough syrups (Vasavaleha, Chyawanprash), hair oils and hair masks. Regulated by AYUSH Ministry (India) as part of classical Ayurvedic formulations. Generally safe at traditional doses. High tannin content means excessive intake may cause constipation (opposite of laxative effect at high doses), reduce absorption of iron and other minerals and interact with iron supplements.""",
    'shatavari': """Shatavari (Asparagus racemosus / hundred roots) is a climbing plant of the Asparagaceae family native to the Himalayas and other tropical regions of India and Sri Lanka. The name "shatavari" in Sanskrit means "she who possesses a hundred husbands" — reflecting its traditional role as the primary female reproductive tonic in Ayurveda. The tuberous roots are the medicinal part. Key phytochemicals: steroidal saponins (shatavarins I–V — the primary bioactive glycosides; shatavarin I is the most potent), isoflavonoids (8-methoxy-5,6,4'-trihydroxyisoflavone-7-O-beta-D-glucopyranosyl-(1→4)-beta-D-glucopyranoside), asparagamine (an alkaloid with immune-modulating effects), polysaccharides and mucilage. Documented pharmacological properties: (1) Galactagogue — Shatavari root and extract (500 mg–2g/day) has clinical evidence for increasing breast milk production in nursing mothers via prolactin-stimulating activity; (2) Adaptogen — modulates cortisol and HPA axis stress responses; (3) Immunomodulatory — enhances lymphocyte proliferation and macrophage activity; (4) Anti-inflammatory — COX-2 inhibition by shatavarins; (5) Female hormone balance — phytoestrogenic saponins may moderate menopausal symptoms and support menstrual regularity (preliminary evidence); (6) Antioxidant and neuroprotective. Traditional uses: female reproductive tonic, fertility support, perimenopause symptom relief, lactation enhancement, ulcer healing, anti-diarrhoeal and as a post-illness rejuvenative. Used in: Ayurvedic supplements, shatavari kalpa (traditional preparation), ghee-based formulations, and women's health products across India. AYUSH Ministry regulates shatavari-containing Ayurvedic products. Generally safe at traditional doses. May rarely cause allergic reactions in asparagus-sensitive individuals. Contraindicated in oestrogen-sensitive tumours due to phytoestrogenic activity (theoretical concern).""",
    'multani mitti': """Multani mitti (Fuller's earth / Calcium Montmorillonite clay) is a naturally occurring aluminium magnesium silicate clay mineral mined in India (particularly in Rajasthan and the Multan region — the name "multani mitti" means "earth from Multan," now in Pakistan). The primary mineral constituent is montmorillonite (a smectite clay: [Na,Ca]₀.₃(Al,Mg)₂Si₄O₁₀(OH)₂·nH₂O), a swelling clay with an extremely high surface area (600–800 m²/g) and strong adsorptive capacity. Physical properties: pale grey to cream-coloured powder; highly absorbent (absorbs 3–5 times its weight in oil/water); forms a smooth paste with water that dries to a film on skin. Cosmetic skin care properties: (1) Oil absorption — the most effective natural ingredient for absorbing excess sebum, making it ideal for oily and acne-prone skin (reduces shine and clogged pores); (2) Deep cleansing — the clay matrix physically adsorbs impurities, dead skin cells, bacteria and pollutants; (3) Mild astringent — temporarily tightens pores; (4) Cooling effect — evaporation of water absorbed by the clay from skin produces a soothing, cooling sensation; (5) Mild exfoliant — particle size gently removes dead surface cells. Uses: face masks (the most classic Indian beauty face pack ingredient), facial cleansers, body masks, scalp masks for oily scalp and dandruff, and clay-based skin care products. Widely used in Indian home beauty routines (multani mitti + rose water, multani mitti + milk, etc.). Caution: not recommended for dry or sensitive skin as it can cause excessive drying; should not be used for prolonged periods or in concentrated dry form; should be fully washed off before it dries completely on sensitive skin. Considered safe for cosmetic use; not a food ingredient. FSSAI/BIS cosmetic standards permit.""",
    'kaolin': """Kaolin (kaolinite / china clay / E559/INS 559 in food / cosmetic-grade kaolin) is a naturally occurring soft white clay mineral — hydrated aluminium silicate with the chemical formula Al₂Si₂O₅(OH)₄. Formed by weathering of aluminium silicate rocks (particularly feldspar) over millions of years. Major deposits in India (Rajasthan, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat), China, USA and UK. Physical properties: fine, white, soft powder; very low oil absorption; low cation exchange capacity; insoluble in water; neutral to slightly acidic pH; layered phyllosilicate crystal structure. In cosmetics (primary use): mild astringent and absorptive clay mineral used in face masks, clay-based cleansers, mattifying primers and loose powders; absorbs excess sebum (oil) and impurities from the skin surface; its fine particles provide a smooth, mattifying effect without stripping essential moisture — gentler than bentonite or fuller's earth, making it suitable for dry and sensitive skin; used in deodorant formulations as an oil absorber; in hair masks for oil control. In pharmaceuticals: used as a tablet filler, binder and excipient; historically used as a protective gastrointestinal adsorbent in kaolin-pectin anti-diarrhoeal preparations (Kaopectate) — adsorbs toxins, bacteria and irritants in the gut; largely replaced in Western markets but still used in some formulations. In food (E559): used as a coating/clarifying agent in wine production and some food processing; used to coat some fruits as a pest deterrent (kaolin-based particle film technology in organic orchid growing). Safety: non-toxic, not absorbed, not metabolised; passes through the GI tract inert. No carcinogenicity or reproductive toxicity at use levels. FSSAI permits as a food additive (E559); EU (E559); cosmetic use widely approved. Not the same as asbestos (though some natural deposits may be contaminated — commercial cosmetic-grade kaolin is tested and certified asbestos-free).""",
    'noni': """Noni (Morinda citrifolia / Indian mulberry / cheese fruit) is a small evergreen tree of the Rubiaceae family native to Southeast Asia, Polynesia and the Pacific Islands, now widely cultivated in tropical regions including parts of India. The fruit is distinctive — large, fleshy, bumpy, off-white to yellow fruit with a pungent, distinctively unpleasant odour when ripe (due to octanoic acid and other volatile short-chain fatty acids). Key phytochemicals: iridoids (deacetylasperulosidic acid, asperuloside — the primary bioactives), anthraquinones (damnacanthal, nordamnacanthal), polysaccharides (6-D-glucopyranose pentaacetate), flavonoids (quercetin, rutin), scopoletin (coumarin with anti-inflammatory, antifungal properties), proxeronine (proposed to yield xeronine via an enzymatic process — not confirmed by mainstream science) and fatty acid esters. Traditional uses (Polynesian, Hawaiian and Indian Ayurveda): pain relief, fever, infection treatment, digestive complaints and general tonification. Noni juice became globally popular in the 1990s with marketing claims for immunity, cancer prevention and vitality — many of these specific claims are not supported by peer-reviewed clinical evidence. Documented evidence: antioxidant activity is well-established; some anti-inflammatory effects via scopoletin and damnacanthal; anticancer cell-line activity (in vitro); limited clinical trial evidence for pain relief (fibromyalgia study, 2010); immunomodulatory effects in animal models. Safety concern: multiple cases of hepatotoxicity (liver injury) have been reported with Noni juice consumption, particularly in Europe — EU EFSA assessed commercially marketed Tahitian Noni juice as safe in 2006, but cases of acute liver injury in susceptible individuals continue to be reported. People with liver disease or those taking hepatotoxic medications should avoid. High potassium content — contraindicated in individuals with chronic kidney disease who must restrict potassium. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",

    # ── Common Grain & Flour Ingredients ─────────────────────────────────────
    'refined wheat flour': """Refined wheat flour (maida / white wheat flour) is produced by milling wheat grains (Triticum aestivum) and systematically removing the bran (outer layers) and germ (embryo), retaining only the starchy endosperm — approximately 72–76% of the original grain by weight. This refining process begins with wheat cleaning and tempering (adding water to toughen bran), followed by grinding through multiple roller mills and sifting to separate flour of different particle sizes and degrees of bran contamination. The resulting flour is very fine, white, smooth and high in starch (~75%), with lower protein content than whole wheat flour (10–12% protein — though sufficient for gluten network formation). Removal of bran and germ eliminates: most dietary fibre (bran contains ~40% fibre), vitamin B1 (thiamine), B2 (riboflavin), B3 (niacin), B6, folate, vitamin E, iron, magnesium, zinc, selenium and phytochemicals. Glycaemic index: 70–85 (high) — the starch, without bran's buffering effect, is rapidly digested to glucose. Gluten content: provides good gluten development — elastic, strong dough for bread, noodles, pastry and biscuits. In India, FSSAI mandates that refined wheat flour (maida) used in mass-manufactured bread and fortifiable products must be fortified with iron, folic acid, Vitamin B12, zinc and Vitamin B6 (wheat flour fortification standards). Health considerations: high GI, low in micronutrients and fibre — regular consumption in large amounts is associated with rapid blood sugar spikes, reduced satiety and contributes to insulin resistance in the context of overall high-refined-carbohydrate diets. People with coeliac disease or wheat allergy cannot consume. Preferred over whole wheat flour in some applications for texture reasons. FSSAI regulates as a standard food ingredient.""",
    'wheat flour': """Wheat flour is produced by grinding whole wheat grains (Triticum aestivum / T. durum) into a powder. The most widely consumed cereal flour globally — the foundation of bread, pasta, noodles, roti, chapati, paratha, biscuits, cakes and pastries across almost every culture. Composition varies by extraction rate: wholemeal flour (100% extraction — contains all bran, germ and endosperm) provides protein (~13%), fibre (~12%), B vitamins, iron, magnesium and zinc. White flour (72–76% extraction) is primarily starch and gluten protein. Wheat protein (gluten) consists of glutenin and gliadin — when hydrated and worked mechanically, they form the three-dimensional viscoelastic gluten network that gives dough elasticity, extensibility and the ability to trap CO₂ from fermentation or chemical leavening, producing light, airy bread and baked goods. Protein content varies by variety: hard wheat (12–14% protein) for bread; soft wheat (8–10% protein) for cakes and biscuits; durum wheat (semolina) for pasta. Glycaemic index: high for refined white flour (~70–85); lower for whole wheat flour (~50–60) due to fibre buffering. Health considerations: wheat flour is a major dietary staple providing significant calories, B vitamins and plant protein globally. Whole wheat flour is nutritionally superior to refined flour for micronutrients and fibre. Critical allergen: gluten-containing wheat is a major allergen requiring mandatory declaration on food labels under FSSAI, EU and US regulations. People with coeliac disease (autoimmune gluten intolerance) must strictly avoid wheat; non-coeliac wheat sensitivity (NCWS) and wheat allergy also require avoidance. FSSAI regulates wheat flour quality and fortification standards.""",
    'maida': """Maida (مائدہ / मैदा) is the Indian term for refined white wheat flour — produced by milling wheat and systematically removing the bran (outer husk layers) and wheat germ, retaining only the white starchy endosperm. Maida is the Indian equivalent of plain/all-purpose flour or cake flour in Western terminology, though typically even more finely milled. It has an extremely fine, smooth, brilliant-white texture and has been used in Indian cooking for centuries, arriving via the Central Asian and Mughal culinary tradition — the word "maida" comes from the Arabic/Urdu "ma'ida" (food). Nutritional profile: very high starch (~75%), moderate protein (~10–11%), negligible fibre (<1%), minimal vitamins and minerals — the refining process removes >80% of fibre, B vitamins, iron, magnesium and other micronutrients present in whole wheat. Glycaemic index: 70–90 — among the highest of any common food; rapidly digested to glucose, producing a sharp blood sugar spike. Gluten: forms a strong, extensible gluten network ideal for flaky pastry (samosa covering, paratha layers), leavened fried breads (bhatura, puri, naan), biscuits, noodles (sevai), pakora batter and Indian sweets using dough. FSSAI mandates fortification of commercial maida and flour products with iron (28 ppm), folic acid (75 mcg per 100g) and vitamin B12 as part of India's mandatory wheat flour fortification programme. Health debate: "maida is unhealthy" is a common claim in Indian nutrition discourse — the concern is its very low fibre content and high GI (contrasted with whole wheat atta). Regular heavy consumption in the context of already low-fibre, high-refined-carbohydrate Indian diets is a genuine concern for blood sugar management. As an occasional ingredient, not a major concern for healthy individuals. People with coeliac disease or wheat allergy cannot consume. FSSAI regulates quality standards.""",
    'wheat gluten': 'Wheat gluten is the natural protein network formed when wheat flour is hydrated and worked. It consists of glutenin and gliadin proteins and gives dough its elasticity and strength. Used as a protein supplement and dough strengthener in baked goods and noodles. Not suitable for coeliac or wheat-allergic individuals.',

    # ── Phosphates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'pentasodium triphosphate': """Pentasodium triphosphate (STPP / sodium tripolyphosphate / E451i/INS 451i / Na₅P₃O₁₀) is the pentasodium salt of triphosphoric acid — the most widely used polyphosphate food additive globally. A white, water-soluble crystalline powder (or granules) with strongly alkaline aqueous solution (pH ~9.7 at 1%). Functions in food: (1) Sequestrant/chelating agent — forms stable soluble complexes with divalent metal cations (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺), which inhibits metal-catalysed oxidation, fat rancidity and colour changes; (2) Moisture and water-retention agent — the dominant application in processed shrimp, prawn and seafood products: STPP treatment increases water-holding capacity of seafood proteins by altering protein charge and structure, resulting in higher product weight after processing and cooking (this is commercially significant but is controversial as it may constitute undisclosed water addition — FSSAI and CODEX set limits specifically for this); also used in processed meats (ham, sausages) for water binding; (3) Emulsifying salt — in processed cheese and dairy products, polyphosphates disrupt casein micelles enabling re-emulsification; (4) Noodle alkaliser — used in Chinese-style alkaline noodles at low concentrations, contributing to the characteristic chewy texture and slightly alkaline pH; (5) Acidity regulator. Phosphate intake concern: chronic high polyphosphate intake from processed foods (not single additive sources but cumulative from multiple products) contributes to elevated serum phosphate — a risk factor for cardiovascular calcification and mortality in CKD patients. Kidney-diseased individuals should minimise processed meat and seafood product intake with phosphate additives. ADI: group ADI for phosphates 70 mg/kg/day as phosphorus (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E451i) and CODEX approved at specified maximum use levels.""",
    'e451': 'E451/INS 451 is triphosphates (pentasodium or pentapotassium triphosphate), used as sequestrants and water-retention agents in processed foods. Approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Excess phosphate intake is a concern for individuals with kidney disease.',
    'sodium triphosphate': 'Sodium triphosphate (STPP / pentasodium triphosphate / E451i/INS 451i) is used as a sequestrant and moisture-retention agent in seafood, processed meats and noodles. FSSAI and CODEX approved at specified limits. Excess phosphate intake is a concern for individuals with kidney disease. See the pentasodium triphosphate entry for full details.',
    'triphosphate': 'Triphosphates (E451/INS 451 — pentasodium or pentapotassium triphosphate) are polyphosphate salts used as sequestrants, emulsifying salts and water-retention agents in processed and convenience foods. The pentasodium form (STPP) is most common. Approved by FSSAI and CODEX at specified limits. Excess phosphate from additive-heavy processed diets is a significant concern for individuals with chronic kidney disease. See the pentasodium triphosphate entry for full details.',

    # ── Iron Mineral ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'ferric pyrophosphate': """Ferric pyrophosphate (iron(III) pyrophosphate / ferric orthopyrophosphate / Fe₄(P₂O₇)₃) is a water-insoluble iron salt used as a dietary iron fortification ingredient, chosen specifically for applications where the organoleptic (taste, colour, appearance) impact of more reactive iron forms would be unacceptable. Chemical nature: an iron(III) [ferric] salt of pyrophosphoric acid — light yellowish-brown powder with very low water solubility (<1 mg/L at neutral pH). Bioavailability: relative bioavailability compared to ferrous sulfate (the reference standard, RBV = 100%) is approximately 45–85% depending on particle size and food matrix — micronised and encapsulated forms have better bioavailability. Lower reactivity with food components means minimal oxidation of food fats or colour changes. Used in: instant noodles, fortified cereals, flour, salt, sugar, rice and other fortification programmes where the pale colour of ferric pyrophosphate avoids the grey-blue discolouration that ferrous sulfate causes in some foods. India context: FSSAI's mandatory food fortification standards recommend ferric pyrophosphate as one of the acceptable iron sources for wheat flour fortification, rice fortification (extruded kernels) and double-fortified salt (DFS). WHO and the Global Alliance for Improved Nutrition (GAIN) recognise it as an effective vehicle for addressing iron deficiency in populations. Iron deficiency anaemia is India's most prevalent micronutrient deficiency. Caution: like all iron supplements, excessive iron intake should be avoided by individuals with haemochromatosis or iron overload conditions. FSSAI, WHO and CODEX approved for food fortification.""",
    'ferrous sulfate': """Ferrous sulfate (iron(II) sulfate / FeSO₄ / iron vitriol) is the most bioavailable inorganic iron supplement and food fortification compound. Chemical nature: a water-soluble iron(II) [ferrous] salt with 20.1% elemental iron by weight (anhydrous) or 20% as heptahydrate (FeSO₄·7H₂O — the common commercial form). Bioavailability: relative bioavailability of ~100% (the WHO reference standard for comparing all other iron fortification compounds); highly soluble in water and stomach acid; absorbed as Fe²⁺ ions in the duodenum and upper jejunum via the divalent metal transporter-1 (DMT-1). Food fortification: used in wheat flour, infant formula, ready-to-eat cereals, complementary foods and multi-micronutrient sachets to address iron deficiency — the most prevalent nutritional deficiency globally, affecting ~2 billion people. Iron deficiency anaemia is estimated to affect 50–60% of pregnant women and approximately 25% of children under 5 in India. FSSAI mandates iron fortification of wheat flour (28 ppm as ferrous sulfate), rice (14 ppm), and double-fortified salt. Pharmaceutical use: ferrous sulfate is the first-line treatment for iron deficiency anaemia — typically 150–200 mg elemental iron per day in three divided doses. Organoleptic challenges: ferrous sulfate is reactive — it can cause grey-blue discolouration in some fortified foods (rice, noodles), metallic taste, fat oxidation and rancidity in foods with unsaturated fat. Encapsulation (microencapsulation with lipid or hydroxypropyl methylcellulose) overcomes these issues. Side effects at therapeutic doses: nausea, constipation, dark stools — manageable by taking with food and gradually increasing dose. Contraindicated in haemochromatosis, sideroblastic anaemia. FSSAI, WHO and CODEX approved for food fortification.""",

    # ── Common Spices (Indian) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'onion powder': """Onion powder is produced by dehydrating and finely grinding mature onions (Allium cepa). The drying process (typically hot-air or freeze-drying) removes >95% of moisture while concentrating flavour and functional compounds. Organosulfur chemistry: fresh onion flavour compounds are generated by the alliinase enzyme acting on trans-S-(1-propenyl)-L-cysteine sulfoxide (PECSO) and propyl-cysteine sulfoxide, releasing propanethial S-oxide (the lachrymatory tear factor) and various sulfenic acids that rearrange to thiosulfinates, disulfides and other volatile compounds. Drying inactivates the enzyme, trapping the sulfur flavour compounds in a dehydrated form; the characteristic onion flavour is released on rehydration or during cooking. Nutritional composition per 100g: approximately 7g protein, 78g carbohydrate, fibre, quercetin and related flavonols (among the highest food sources), Vitamin C (reduced from fresh), B vitamins and potassium. Functional properties: mild antimicrobial (quercetin inhibits bacterial growth); quercetin is a potent antioxidant and anti-inflammatory flavonol well-studied for cardiovascular protection. Uses in food manufacturing: seasoning mixes, instant soups, bouillon cubes, noodle seasoning, snack coatings, processed meats, marinades and convenience foods. A versatile, shelf-stable alternative to fresh onion. No significant allergen concerns; onion intolerance (FODMAPs) exists — fructans in onion powder can trigger symptoms in IBS sufferers. FSSAI permits as a food spice/flavouring. Generally very safe.""",
    'garlic': """Garlic (Allium sativum) is one of the oldest and most widely used medicinal and culinary plants in human history — cultivated for over 7,000 years. An important crop in India (third-largest producer globally). The characteristic pungent flavour and most medicinal activity derive from organosulfur chemistry: intact garlic cloves contain alliin (S-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide); when garlic is crushed or chopped, the enzyme alliinase (compartmentally separated from its substrate) catalyses the rapid conversion of alliin to allicin (diallyl thiosulfinate) — the primary bioactive compound responsible for garlic's antimicrobial, antithrombotic and cardiovascular effects. Allicin is unstable — it further transforms to ajoene, diallyl disulfide (DADS), diallyl trisulfide (DATS) and other volatile organosulfur compounds. Rich in: vitamin B6, Vitamin C, manganese and selenium; also contains polyphenols (quercetin, allixin) and fructooligosaccharides (prebiotic fibre). Health effects: (1) Cardiovascular — clinical meta-analyses confirm modest but significant reductions in total cholesterol, LDL and blood pressure with garlic supplementation; (2) Antimicrobial — allicin is effective in vitro against a wide range of bacteria, fungi, viruses and even some drug-resistant organisms (MRSA); (3) Antithrombotic — inhibits platelet aggregation via ajoene and DATS; (4) Immunomodulatory — stimulates NK cell and macrophage activity; (5) Anticancer — epidemiological studies associate higher garlic intake with reduced gastric and colorectal cancer risk. Cautions: anticoagulant effect — caution before surgery and with blood-thinning medications. May cause GI discomfort in some individuals. FSSAI permits as a food spice. Safe and beneficial at culinary and supplement levels.""",
    'garlic powder': """Garlic powder is produced by drying garlic (Allium sativum) cloves (slice, dehydrate, grind) to remove >95% moisture, concentrating the flavour and bioactive compounds. The dehydration process inactivates alliinase enzyme, locking in the precursor alliin rather than allicin (which requires fresh crushing to form). When garlic powder rehydrates during cooking or in the stomach, partial enzymatic conversion to allicin and organosulfur compounds occurs. Flavour intensity: garlic powder (~1/8 teaspoon) substitutes for approximately one fresh garlic clove in cooking, though the flavour profile is slightly different (less pungent raw heat, more mellow and round flavour). Bioactive retention: while allicin itself is unstable in dried form, garlic powder retains alliin, S-allylcysteine (SAC), fructooligosaccharides and flavonoids. Research on garlic powder supplements confirms cardiovascular benefits (blood pressure, cholesterol) similar to fresh garlic, provided the powder is not overheated during processing. Uses in food manufacturing: ubiquitous in spice blends, seasoning mixes, garlic bread, instant noodles, processed meats, canned goods, sauces, marinades and convenience foods across India and globally. Shelf-stable and cost-effective. Anticoagulant caution: at high supplemental doses, garlic compounds inhibit platelet aggregation — caution before surgery and with anticoagulant medications. Generally safe at culinary food-use concentrations. FSSAI permits as a food flavouring/spice.""",
    'red chilli': """Red chilli (Capsicum annuum, Capsicum frutescens, Capsicum chinense) is one of the most widely consumed spices globally, a staple of Indian cuisine across all regions. Dried red chillies are the fully ripened, dried forms of the chilli fruit. Pungency chemistry: capsaicin and dihydrocapsaicin (capsaicinoids) are produced in glands on the placenta (the interior pale membrane). They activate the TRPV1 (transient receptor potential vanilloid 1) pain receptor, creating the burning sensation on mucous membranes. Scoville heat units (SHU) range from ~1,000 (mild Indian varieties) to ~100,000+ for very hot varieties. Nutritional value: rich in carotenoids (capsanthin, capsorubin — deep red pigments; beta-carotene, zeaxanthin), Vitamin C (drying reduces some Vitamin C but much is retained), Vitamin B6, Vitamin K, potassium and iron. Bioactive properties: capsaicin inhibits substance P (a neuropeptide involved in pain signalling) — basis for topical capsaicin creams for arthritis, neuropathic pain and post-herpetic neuralgia; anti-inflammatory; thermogenic (increases metabolic rate and fat oxidation — modestly; used in weight management supplements); antioxidant (carotenoid-based). GI effects: capsaicin at culinary levels stimulates gastric secretions and gut motility; may aggravate symptoms in people with gastric ulcers, GERD or IBS; can cause anal burning at high intake. Used as: a spice, a natural red food colour (E160c paprika/capsicum extract), and in topical pharmaceutical formulations. FSSAI permits both the spice and capsicum extract (E160c) as food colour. Safe at culinary levels for the general population.""",
    'red chilli powder': 'Red chilli powder is produced by grinding dried red chillies (Capsicum annuum) into a fine powder. It provides both heat (from capsaicin and related capsaicinoids) and red-orange colour (from capsanthin, capsorubin and beta-carotene). A staple seasoning in virtually all Indian regional cuisines. Rich in antioxidant carotenoids, Vitamin C and Vitamin B6. May irritate the GI tract in sensitive individuals or at very high intake. Safe at normal culinary levels. FSSAI regulates purity standards for chilli powder to prevent adulteration with Sudan dyes (illegal synthetic dyes). See the red chilli entry for full compositional and health details.',
    'ginger': """Ginger (Zingiber officinale) is a rhizomatous perennial of the Zingiberaceae family, native to Southeast Asia and cultivated in India for over 5,000 years. India is the world's largest producer. Both fresh root and dried powder are widely used. Chemical composition: pungent principles — gingerols (particularly [6]-gingerol — the main active compound in fresh ginger; structurally related to capsaicin and piperine, activating TRPV1 receptors); shogaols (dehydration products of gingerols formed during drying — [6]-shogaol; approximately twice as pungent and more bioavailable from dried ginger); paradols, zingerone and other pungent ketones. Volatile essential oil (~1–3%): zingiberene (~35%), bisabolene, and beta-sesquiphellandrene contributing to aroma. Also contains starch, dietary fibre, Vitamin B6, magnesium and potassium. Health effects — best evidence: (1) Anti-nausea — multiple systematic reviews confirm efficacy for nausea and vomiting in pregnancy, post-operative nausea, and chemotherapy-induced nausea (considered evidence-based by European guidelines); (2) Anti-inflammatory — gingerols inhibit COX-1, COX-2 and 5-LOX (leukotriene pathway) — studied for osteoarthritis, with moderate evidence for pain reduction; (3) Gastrointestinal — prokinetic effect (accelerates gastric emptying) useful for dyspepsia and gastric motility; (4) Antithrombotic — mild anti-platelet activity at high doses. Used in: cooking, ginger tea (adrak chai), confectionery, ginger ale, supplements, Ayurvedic preparations. Generally very safe; mild GI discomfort with large doses. Caution: anti-platelet effects at >4g/day — reduce before surgery; may reduce blood glucose (additive with anti-diabetic drugs). FSSAI permits as a food spice.""",
    'ginger powder': 'Dried and finely ground ginger (Zingiber officinale), used as a spice in cooking, baking and beverages. Drying converts gingerols to shogaols — the predominant pungent compound in dried ginger, which is more potent than gingerols. Retains anti-inflammatory (COX inhibition), anti-nausea and digestive properties. Widely used in masalas, chai, ginger biscuits and Ayurvedic preparations. Safe at culinary levels; caution at high supplemental doses (anti-platelet effect). FSSAI permits as a food spice. See the ginger entry for full details.',
    'aniseed': """Aniseed (Pimpinella anisum / anise) is the dried fruit (achene) of an annual flowering plant of the Apiaceae family, native to the Eastern Mediterranean and Southwest Asia, now cultivated in India, Egypt and Spain. Not to be confused with star anise (Illicium verum — a different plant from China producing similar flavour compounds). Key phytochemical: trans-anethole (~80–90% of essential oil) is the compound responsible for the characteristic sweet, liquorice-like flavour and aroma, and also confers antimicrobial, antifungal, antispasmodic and mild oestrogenic (phytoestrogen) activity. Also contains estragole (~2–5%), pseudoisoeugenol, anise ketone, flavonoids (quercetin, apigenin) and coumarins. Uses in food: used as a spice in Indian mukhwas (after-meal mouth freshener blends), anise-flavoured confectionery, breads, cookies (springerle), liqueurs (anisette, ouzo, arak, pastis), digestive herbal teas and flavouring for Indian sweets. Digestive properties: carminative (reduces intestinal gas/flatulence), mild antispasmodic (relaxes intestinal smooth muscle) — widely used in digestive preparations and as a post-meal breath freshener (similar to fennel seeds). Mild expectorant and antitussive in respiratory herbal formulations. Safety: safe at culinary amounts. The phytoestrogenic activity of anethole means very high supplemental doses are not recommended in pregnancy or for individuals with hormone-sensitive conditions. Estragole (a constituent) is a weak genotoxin at very high doses in animal models — not a concern at food spice levels. Rare Apiaceae allergy. FSSAI permits as a food spice.""",
    'black pepper': """Black pepper (Piper nigrum) is the fruit of a tropical climbing vine native to the Malabar coast of Kerala, India — historically the world's most traded spice ("the king of spices"). India remains one of the largest producers. Dried, unripe fruits are black pepper; fully ripe red fruits become white pepper when the outer pericarp is removed. Pungency: piperine (1-piperoyl piperidine) — an alkaloid amide responsible for the characteristic pungent heat (distinct mechanism from capsaicin: piperine activates TRPV1 receptor but less intensely; also inhibits P-glycoprotein efflux pump). Composition: piperine (~5–9%), piperin, chavicine (piperine isomers), essential oil (~3.5% — pinene, sabinene, beta-caryophyllene contributing to flavour), flavonoids (luteolin, quercetin) and starch. Bioavailability enhancement — scientifically important: piperine is a well-documented inhibitor of: (1) CYP3A4 enzyme in intestinal wall and liver (first-pass metabolism inhibition); (2) P-glycoprotein efflux pump in intestinal epithelium. This combination dramatically increases the bioavailability of many co-administered compounds. Most famously: piperine (20 mg) increases curcumin bioavailability 20-fold in humans; also increases bioavailability of many pharmaceutical drugs including some antibiotics, antiepileptics and anticancer drugs. This drug-food interaction effect means high-dose piperine supplements warrant caution for people on multiple medications. At culinary levels, the effect is minimal. Antimicrobial properties against a range of bacteria. Antioxidant via phenolic content. Promotes gastric acid secretion (improves digestion). Widely used as the universal table seasoning. FSSAI permits as a food spice. Considered safe at culinary and moderate supplemental levels.""",
    'clove': """Cloves (Syzygium aromaticum / Eugenia caryophyllus) are the dried aromatic flower buds of a tropical evergreen tree of the Myrtaceae family, native to the Maluku Islands (Spice Islands) of Indonesia, now widely cultivated in Madagascar, Tanzania, Sri Lanka and India. Among the world's most intensely aromatic spices. Key phytochemical: eugenol (~72–90% of clove essential oil; 14–20% of the whole clove by weight) — a phenylpropanoid compound with the highest antioxidant capacity of any spice per unit weight (ORAC value). Also contains: acetyleugenol, beta-caryophyllene, methyl salicylate, galloyl esters and flavonoids. Properties: (1) Antimicrobial — eugenol is a potent broad-spectrum antimicrobial and antifungal agent; clove oil is used as a natural food preservative; (2) Analgesic — eugenol is a COX-2 inhibitor (anti-inflammatory) and also acts as a mild local anaesthetic; clove oil has been used for toothache pain relief for centuries (confirmed effective by dental research); used in ZOE cement (zinc oxide eugenol — a dental cement); (3) Antioxidant — among the highest ORAC values of any tested food or spice; (4) Antidiabetic — clove extract inhibits alpha-glucosidase and improves insulin sensitivity in animal studies; (5) Antiviral — in vitro activity against HSV. Uses: Indian masalas, biryani, chai, pickles, oral care products (toothpastes, mouth fresheners), and clove cigarettes (kreteks — concerning because inhaling eugenol causes lung damage). At culinary levels: very safe. Clove oil: concentrated eugenol should be used with caution — can irritate mucous membranes; internal ingestion of concentrated clove oil is toxic; dilution required for topical use. FSSAI permits as a food spice.""",
    'cardamom': """Cardamom (Elettaria cardamomum / green cardamom / true cardamom) is an aromatic seed capsule of a perennial plant of the Zingiberaceae family, native to and predominantly cultivated in the Western Ghats of Kerala and Karnataka, India — called the "Queen of Spices." India produces the majority of world cardamom. The pods contain dark seeds with the essential oil. Key phytochemicals: essential oil (~3–7% of seed weight) dominated by 1,8-cineole (eucalyptol, ~40–60% — responsible for the cool, camphor-like undertone), alpha-terpinyl acetate (~35%), linalool, and alpha-terpineol. These terpenoids confer cardamom's distinctive complex aroma — a balance of spicy warmth and cooling eucalyptol. Also contains flavonoids and phenolic acids. Health properties: (1) Digestive — carminative (reduces gas and bloating); antispasmodic (relaxes intestinal smooth muscle); traditional remedy for nausea; (2) Antioxidant — phenolic content; (3) Antimicrobial — 1,8-cineole inhibits a range of bacteria including Staphylococcus aureus; (4) Blood pressure — preliminary clinical evidence that cardamom powder (3g/day) reduces blood pressure in newly diagnosed hypertension (small RCT, 2009); (5) Anti-inflammatory via terpenoid activity. Uses: Indian chai (masala tea), biryanis, kheer, lassi, mouth freshener (mukhwas), mithai, sweets, and Ayurvedic preparations. One of the world's most expensive spices after saffron and vanilla. Safe at culinary levels and moderate supplemental doses. No significant allergen concerns. FSSAI permits as a food spice.""",
    'nutmeg': """Nutmeg (Myristica fragrans) is the seed of a tropical tree native to the Banda Islands of Indonesia, now cultivated in India, Sri Lanka and Grenada. The seed (nutmeg) and its outer red aril covering (mace) are both used as separate spices with slightly different flavour profiles. Key compounds: volatile essential oil (~15% of weight) containing myristicin (~4–8%), safrole (~0.6%), elemicin, iso-elemicin, eugenol (~6%), and numerous terpene compounds (sabinene, alpha-pinene, beta-pinene, camphene). Flavour: warm, slightly sweet, subtly complex. Health properties at culinary doses: digestive (carminative and antispasmodic), antioxidant, antimicrobial, mild sedative (at pharmacological doses). Regulatory and safety note: nutmeg at culinary levels (small amount in cooking or beverages) is completely safe. At very high doses (1–2 whole nutmegs or >5g ground nutmeg in a single sitting), myristicin and elemicin are biotransformed to amphetamine analogues (MMDA — methylene-dioxy-methoxyamphetamine, and MSDA) in the liver, causing hallucinations, tachycardia, nausea, CNS depression and anxiety in a syndrome called "nutmeg intoxication." The minimum toxic dose is approximately 5–15g, with most cases reported from intentional ingestion for recreational effects. Myristicin and safrole are considered potentially toxic at high doses (not at food levels). Mace (the red aril) has a similar composition but is milder. FSSAI permits as a food spice at culinary use levels. Safe and widely used in Indian sweets (kheer, halwa, chai).""",
    'fenugreek': """Fenugreek (Trigonella foenum-graecum / methi) is a leguminous annual plant of the Fabaceae family native to the Indian subcontinent and Mediterranean region, one of the oldest cultivated plants in human history. See also the 'methi' entry for full phytochemical and health details. Both seeds and leaves are used in Indian cooking: seeds (whole, soaked, or ground) add a slightly bitter, maple-like flavour; leaves (fresh and dried kasuri methi) add a distinctive aromatic flavour. Chemical composition of seeds: galactomannan (~45% — the primary soluble dietary fibre and the compound responsible for blood glucose and cholesterol-lowering effects), saponins (diosgenin), alkaloids (trigonelline — a precursor of pyridine used in traditional medicine), 4-hydroxyisoleucine (an insulin secretagogue), flavonoids and volatile oil. FSSAI permits as a food spice. In Indian households, fenugreek seeds are a household remedy for diabetes management, with good clinical evidence supporting this traditional use. Also used in Ayurvedic hair care preparations. Safe at culinary levels. High supplemental doses may cause GI discomfort and hypoglycaemia when combined with antidiabetic medications.""",
    'coriander': """Coriander (Coriandrum sativum) is an annual herb of the Apiaceae family cultivated since antiquity across the Mediterranean, Middle East and Indian subcontinent — both the fresh leaves (called coriander or cilantro) and the dried ripe seeds (called coriander seeds or dhania) are used as distinct spice and flavouring ingredients with different chemical compositions. Seeds are the dominant form in Indian spice blends. Coriander seed composition: rich in linalool (~60–80% of essential oil), alpha-pinene, gamma-terpinene and camphor — producing the characteristic warm, citrusy, slightly woody aroma. Also contains flavonoids (quercetin, isoquercitrin), phenolic acids, fatty acids and minerals. Properties: (1) Carminative and digestive — linalool relaxes intestinal smooth muscle, reducing bloating and cramping; widely used in digestive spice blends (sambhar masala, panch phoron); (2) Antioxidant — polyphenol content; (3) Antimicrobial — essential oil active against E. coli, S. aureus and Salmonella; (4) Hypoglycaemic activity — coriander seed extract shown to increase insulin secretion and reduce blood glucose in animal studies; (5) Cholesterol-lowering effect demonstrated in rodent models (bile acid sequestration by saponins). Used in: virtually all Indian regional cooking (whole seeds, roasted and ground, as the base of spice blends like garam masala, sambhar masala, rasam powder, chole masala), pickles, chutneys, preserved meats and as a natural food flavouring. Allergen note: coriander (Apiaceae family) can cross-react with other Apiaceae plants (carrot, celery, fennel, aniseed, dill, parsley) in people with plant food allergy or pollen-food allergy syndrome — rare but documented. FSSAI permits as a food spice. Very widely consumed across India without adverse effects.""",

    # ── Flavour Enhancers (name variants) ────────────────────────────────────
    "disodium 5'-ribonucleotides": 'Disodium 5\'-ribonucleotides (E635/INS 635) is a combined nucleotide flavour enhancer — a 1:1 blend of disodium guanylate (E627) and disodium inosinate (E631). Used in instant noodles, crisps, savoury seasonings and processed foods to synergistically intensify umami flavour with MSG. FSSAI (India) permits up to 500 mg/kg in processed foods; EU sets 500 mg/kg in most snacks and seasonings (Regulation EC 1333/2008 Annex II); CODEX permits quantum satis in certain categories. Should be avoided by people with gout or hyperuricaemia — nucleotides raise uric acid levels. Not recommended for infants and young children. Those sensitive to MSG may also react to nucleotide enhancers.',
    "disodium 5' ribonucleotides": """Disodium 5'-ribonucleotides (E635/INS 635) — a 1:1 blend of disodium guanylate (E627/GMP) and disodium inosinate (E631/IMP). Used in savoury processed foods to synergistically intensify umami flavour with MSG. FSSAI and EU limit to 500 mg/kg in most processed food categories. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Raises uric acid via purine catabolism — avoid if you have gout, hyperuricaemia or urate kidney stones. Not recommended for infants and young children. See the primary 'disodium ribonucleotides' entry for full details.""",
    'disodium 5-ribonucleotides': """Disodium 5'-ribonucleotides (E635/INS 635) — a combined nucleotide flavour enhancer (E627 GMP + E631 IMP in 1:1 ratio). Found on Maggi, Top Ramen and many instant noodle and snack labels. FSSAI and EU limit to 500 mg/kg in most processed food categories. Raises uric acid — avoid if you have gout or hyperuricaemia. Not recommended for infants and young children. Source may be animal-derived (fish/meat) or fermentation-derived. See the primary 'disodium ribonucleotides' entry for full details.""",

    # ── Hydrolysed Proteins ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'hydrolysed groundnut protein': """Hydrolysed groundnut protein (HGP) is produced by breaking down peanut (groundnut / Arachis hypogaea) protein into free amino acids and short peptides using acid hydrolysis (hydrochloric acid), alkaline hydrolysis or enzymatic hydrolysis with food-grade proteases. The resulting ingredient is a rich source of free glutamic acid (glutamate) — the amino acid responsible for umami (savoury) taste — along with other free amino acids, short peptides and Maillard reaction compounds (from the hydrolysis process). Used in: instant noodle seasoning, soup powders, bouillon cubes, savoury seasonings, sauces, gravies and processed snack foods as a clean-label natural-source umami flavour enhancer and taste modifier. Acid hydrolysis at high temperature with fat-containing proteins can produce 3-MCPD (3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol) — a genotoxic and potentially carcinogenic process contaminant (IARC Group 2A). FSSAI and EU impose strict limits on 3-MCPD in hydrolysed vegetable/plant proteins; enzymatic hydrolysis avoids this concern. Allergen — CRITICAL: peanut/groundnut is one of the most severe food allergens globally, causing anaphylaxis. Hydrolysed groundnut protein retains allergenic potential — the hydrolysis breaks proteins into peptides, but IgE-reactive epitopes are often retained in partially hydrolysed forms. Any food product containing hydrolysed groundnut protein must prominently declare peanut as an allergen per FSSAI, EU and FDA regulations. Peanut-allergic individuals must strictly avoid.""",
    'hydrolysed vegetable protein': """Hydrolysed vegetable protein (HVP) is a flavour ingredient produced by hydrolysing plant-derived proteins — typically from defatted soy flour, wheat gluten, corn protein or groundnut — into free amino acids and short peptides. Hydrolysis is most commonly performed by acid hydrolysis (digesting with hydrochloric acid at elevated temperature and pressure), alkaline hydrolysis or enzymatic hydrolysis using food-grade proteases. The hydrolysis releases large amounts of free glutamic acid (glutamate) — the key umami-tasting amino acid — along with other amino acids, short peptides and Maillard reaction products that contribute roasted, savoury and meaty flavour notes. HVP is therefore used primarily as a natural-source umami flavour enhancer in: soups, stocks, instant noodles, seasonings, sauces, gravies, snack food coatings, processed meats and ready-to-eat meals. Safety concern — 3-MCPD: acid hydrolysis of protein in the presence of residual fat (glycerol from triglycerides) can produce 3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol (3-MCPD), a genotoxic and probable carcinogen (IARC Group 2A). EU regulations set maximum limits for 3-MCPD in HVP; FSSAI has adopted similar limits. Enzymatic HVP avoids this contaminant. Allergen: HVP source must be declared — soy-derived HVP must list soy; wheat-derived HVP must list wheat/gluten; peanut-derived HVP must list peanut — these are all major allergens. Glutamate content: functionally similar to added MSG; people with glutamate sensitivity may react. FSSAI permits HVP as a flavouring substance.""",
    'hydrolysed soy protein': """Hydrolysed soy protein (HSP) is produced by breaking down soy protein molecules into smaller peptides and free amino acids through either acid hydrolysis (treatment with hydrochloric acid at elevated temperatures) or enzymatic hydrolysis (using food-grade proteases). The hydrolysis releases glutamic acid, which creates an intense umami (savoury) flavour — similar in function to MSG but from a complex mixture of amino acids and peptides rather than pure glutamate. Used in: soups, sauces, gravies, seasonings, meat products, instant noodles, flavoured snacks and ready-to-eat meals as a flavour enhancer and umami booster. Acid hydrolysis concern: When protein is hydrolysed with hydrochloric acid at high temperature, 3-MCPD (3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol) can form as a by-product — particularly when fat (triglycerides) is present in the protein source. 3-MCPD is a genotoxic and potentially carcinogenic compound; IARC Group 2A (probably carcinogenic). EU regulations set strict limits on 3-MCPD in hydrolysed vegetable proteins. Enzymatic hydrolysis avoids this by-product. In India, FSSAI regulates HSP and requires compliance with limits on process contaminants. Allergen: soy is one of the 14 major food allergens regulated by EU, FDA, FSSAI and CODEX — any product containing hydrolysed soy protein must declare SOY on the label. People with soy allergy should avoid. Some individuals with soy intolerance may tolerate highly hydrolysed forms where soy proteins are broken down to peptides too small to be recognised by IgE antibodies — but this is not reliable; allergy-diagnosed individuals should avoid. Also a source of glutamate — people sensitive to MSG may react. Vegan; produced from soy (a legume). Halal and Kosher when properly certified.""",

    # ── Caramel Colour variants ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'caramel iv': """Caramel Colour Class IV (E150d / INS 150d), also called sulphite-ammonia caramel, is produced by heating food-grade carbohydrates (glucose, invert sugar, sucrose, corn syrup, molasses or starch hydrolysates) with both sulfite and ammonium compounds under controlled temperature and pressure. The use of both ammonia and sulfite reactants during manufacture distinguishes Class IV from Classes I (plain caramel), II (sulphite caramel) and III (ammonia caramel). Class IV produces the most intensely coloured caramel (very dark brown to black) with the highest tinctorial strength — the amount of colour per unit weight is greater than other classes. It is the most widely used caramel colour globally and the standard colouring in cola beverages (Pepsi, Coca-Cola), soy sauce, hoisin sauce, dark gravies, instant noodle seasonings and beer. 4-Methylimidazole (4-MEI): a process contaminant formed during the ammonia-based caramelisation reaction. IARC classified 4-MEI as a Group 2B possible carcinogen to humans (based on animal studies showing increased lung tumours in mice at very high doses). The California Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment (OEHHA) added 4-MEI to the Proposition 65 list (requiring cancer warning labels above 29 µg/day in California). Regulators disagree on risk at human dietary exposure: FDA and EFSA have reviewed the data and concluded that typical dietary exposures to 4-MEI from caramel colour IV do not pose a significant health risk; FDA stated that a consumer would have to drink more than 1,000 cans of cola per day to reach doses used in rodent studies. EFSA set a maximum limit for 4-MEI of 250 mg/kg in E150d. FSSAI permits caramel colour as per CODEX standards. For health-conscious consumers: those who regularly consume large quantities of cola drinks may wish to note the California warning; individuals with 4-MEI sensitivity or cancer concerns may prefer products with Class I caramel or other colorants.""",
    'caramel colour iv': """Caramel Colour Class IV (E150d / INS 150d) — see 'caramel iv' entry for full details. This is the sulphite-ammonia process caramel colour, the most widely used caramel colour globally. Produced by heating carbohydrates with both ammonium and sulfite compounds, yielding the deepest, most concentrated brown-black colour. Used in: cola soft drinks (primary cola colouring), soy sauce, dark beer, gravies, seasonings and savoury sauces. Contains 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI) as a process contaminant — IARC Group 2B possible carcinogen in animal studies; listed on California Proposition 65. Regulatory status: EFSA, FDA and FSSAI have concluded that typical dietary exposures from food are not a significant cancer risk in humans, as rodent study doses were thousands of times higher than human dietary exposure. EFSA maximum: 250 mg 4-MEI per kg of product. Large regular cola consumption represents the primary exposure route. FSSAI permits under CODEX standards.""",
    'e150d': """E150d / INS 150d — Caramel Colour Class IV (Sulphite-Ammonia Caramel). See 'caramel iv' entry for the full description. In brief: produced by heating carbohydrates with both ammonium and sulphite compounds — yields the most intensely dark caramel colour of all four classes. The most widely used caramel colour globally, found in cola drinks (Coca-Cola, Pepsi), soy sauce, dark beer, gravies and savoury seasonings. Contains 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI) as a process contaminant — IARC Group 2B possible carcinogen to humans (animal data); California Prop 65 listed (requires cancer warning above 29 µg/day in California products). EFSA, FDA and FSSAI conclude typical dietary exposure is not a significant cancer risk. EFSA maximum: 250 mg 4-MEI per kg of caramel colour. EU (Regulation 1333/2008) restricts maximum doses in soft drinks and other beverage categories. FSSAI permits as per CODEX GSFA standards.""",

    # ── Colorants E100–E199 (additions) ──────────────────────────────────────
    'e104': 'E104/INS 104 is Quinoline Yellow, a synthetic greenish-yellow food dye. Banned in USA, Australia and Japan; permitted in EU and India with mandatory "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children" warning. Used in smoked fish, ice creams and scotch eggs.',
    'quinoline yellow': """Quinoline Yellow (E104/INS 104 / CI 47005 / Acid Yellow 3) is a synthetic quinoline-derived monosulfonated or disulfonated dye producing a greenish-yellow colour. Originally derived from quinoline (a coal tar derivative), modern production uses chemical synthesis. Provides a distinctive greenish-yellow colour not easily matched by natural alternatives. Applications: scotch eggs, smoked fish, medications and pharmaceutical tablets, some confectionery and ice cream products in EU and Indian markets. Global regulatory status: Banned in USA (not approved by FDA), Norway and Australia as a food additive. EU (E104) permits it in specific food categories (smoked fish, scotch eggs, preserved cherries, some confectionery, beverages) but requires the "Southampton six" warning label: "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." FSSAI permits E104 in specified Indian food categories. The ADHD warning label requirement stems from the 2007 McCann et al. study (University of Southampton) showing a mixture of six synthetic food dyes (including Quinoline Yellow) plus sodium benzoate significantly increased hyperactivity in young children. EFSA's 2009 review concluded the evidence was not sufficient to determine which dyes caused the effect or whether it applied generally to children. As a synthetic azo-type-related dye, quinoline yellow is not associated with azo-dye cross-reactivity (it is a quinoline dye, not an azo dye), but it carries similar precautionary concerns. Consumers with children with ADHD or hyperactivity may prefer to avoid it. ADI: 0–0.5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA 2016 evaluation for the disulfonated form).""",
    'e123': 'E123/INS 123 is Amaranth, a dark red-violet synthetic azo dye. Banned in USA (since 1976) due to carcinogenicity concerns; permitted in EU for caviar and fish roe only. Not widely used in Indian packaged foods.',
    'amaranth dye': """Amaranth (E123/INS 123 / FD&C Red No. 2 — historical / CI 16185 / CI Acid Red 27) is a dark purplish-red synthetic azo dye — the trisodium salt of 3-hydroxy-4-((4-sulpho-1-naphthyl)azo)-2,7-naphthalenedisulphonic acid. Produces a deep red-violet colour. Despite its name, it has no relationship to the nutritious grain amaranth (Amaranthus spp.) — the shared name refers to the Greek word "amarantos" (unfading) describing the colour. Regulatory status — globally restricted: BANNED in the USA since 1976 (FDA revoked approval citing animal carcinogenicity studies); BANNED in Russia; BANNED in Canada as a food colour; EU (E123): permits only in caviar and fish roe as a colour. Not approved for use in most food applications in India by FSSAI; FSSAI's positive list of permitted food colours does not include E123. Toxicological basis for restrictions: rodent studies showed increased tumour incidence in rats fed high doses of amaranth dye. JECFA and EFSA have evaluated the dye — JECFA noted the evidence was inconclusive but the USA's revocation stands. The narrow permitted EU use (caviar only) reflects a very conservative position. As an azo dye, amaranth can potentially be reduced by intestinal bacteria to aromatic amines (the azo cleavage products), some of which are mutagenic — a class concern rather than specific to amaranth. ADI: not established in most jurisdictions. Consumers should avoid products with E123 or INS 123 where labelled; must be clearly distinguished from amaranth grain products which are nutritious and safe.""",
    'e140': 'E140/INS 140 is Chlorophylls and Chlorophyllins, natural green pigments extracted from plants. Used to colour foods, oils and beverages green. Completely natural and considered safe.',
    'chlorophyll': """Chlorophyll (E140/INS 140) is the primary photosynthetic pigment found in all green plants and algae — extracted from plant material (nettles, spinach, grass, alfalfa) for use as a natural green food colour. Two principal forms: chlorophyll a (blue-green) and chlorophyll b (yellow-green), both porphyrin ring structures with a central magnesium atom and a long phytol tail. Fat-soluble; used to colour oils, fats, confectionery coatings and fat-based products. Colour stability challenges: heat, acid and light can displace the central magnesium ion (pheophytisation), turning the vivid green to olive-brown. This instability in acidic foods and during heat processing limits applications where process stability is required — copper chlorophyll (E141) was developed to overcome this. The water-soluble sodium and potassium chlorophyllin salts (E140ii) offer better water-phase application but are produced by more extensive processing. As a dietary compound: chlorophyllin has antimutagenic properties in animal models. No significant safety concerns; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E140), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Fully natural and plant-derived. Generally safe; very rare cases of allergic contact reactions. Preferred over synthetic green dyes in clean-label products.""",
    'e141': 'E141/INS 141 is Copper Complexes of Chlorophylls, produced by replacing magnesium in chlorophyll with copper to give a stable bright green. Used in canned vegetables, peas and beverages. Generally safe.',
    'copper chlorophyll': """Copper chlorophyll (E141/INS 141 — copper complexes of chlorophylls and chlorophyllins) is produced by treating natural plant chlorophyll with copper salts, replacing the central magnesium ion in the porphyrin ring with copper. This metal substitution confers dramatically improved colour stability: the copper-chlorophyll complex resists acid, heat, light and oxygen far better than natural magnesium-chlorophyll, producing a stable, vivid olive-green to bright green colour. Two commercial forms: copper chlorophyll (fat-soluble) and copper chlorophyllin (water-soluble sodium/potassium salts). Used in: canned and processed green vegetables (peas, beans, spinach) to maintain attractive green colour through heat sterilisation; pickles; some confectionery coatings and mint products; beverages. The copper content in food products from this additive is typically very low and does not significantly contribute to dietary copper intake. Copper itself is an essential mineral (RDA ~0.9 mg/day), but high copper exposure is toxic — food-grade copper chlorophyll is used at concentrations where total copper from this source is far below safety thresholds. ADI: 15 mg/kg body weight/day for copper complexes of chlorophylls (EFSA, expressed as copper chlorophyllin). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E141), FDA (limited use in citrus-based dry beverage mixes) and CODEX at specified limits. Generally regarded as safe.""",
    'e142': 'E142/INS 142 is Green S (Lissamine Green), a synthetic green food dye. Not approved in USA or Canada; permitted in EU and some Asian countries. Used in tinned peas, mint jelly and desserts.',
    'green s': """Green S (Lissamine Green B / E142/INS 142) is a synthetic sulfonated triarylmethane food dye — not an azo dye — that produces a bright green colour. Used to colour tinned peas (to maintain their green appearance after heat processing), mint jelly, green confectionery, some desserts, and artificially flavoured mint sauces. A notable use is in combination with brilliant blue or tartrazine to achieve various shades of green in food products. Not approved by FDA for food use in USA, not permitted in Canada, Japan or Australia. Permitted in EU (E142) and India (FSSAI). A few case reports link green S to allergic reactions and urticaria; cross-reactivity with other sulfonated dyes is possible. Unlike azo dyes, it is not one of the Southampton Six and does not carry a mandatory EU hyperactivity warning. ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2015). EFSA 2015 re-evaluation found no evidence of genotoxicity or carcinogenicity at the ADI. FSSAI permits use in several food categories at limits of 100–200 mg/kg. Increasing consumer preference for natural greens has led to its partial replacement by chlorophyllin (E141) in some markets.""",
    'e150a': 'E150a/INS 150a is Plain Caramel, the simplest caramel colour made by heating carbohydrates without ammonium or sulfite compounds. Used in baked goods, syrups and confectionery. Does not contain 4-MEI.',
    'plain caramel': """Plain caramel (E150a/INS 150a / Caramel I) is produced by carefully controlled heat treatment of food-grade carbohydrates (sucrose, glucose, invert sugar, lactose) without the addition of acids, alkalis, sulfite or ammonia compounds. It is the simplest and most traditional form of caramel colour — essentially the same process as making caramel confectionery but optimised for colour rather than flavour. The brown colour develops through non-enzymatic browning reactions (thermal degradation of sugars, retro-aldolisation, dehydration and aldol condensation creating a complex mixture of melanoidins and furan compounds). Does not contain 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI) — the potentially carcinogenic by-product of ammonia-processed caramels (Classes III and IV). Produces a warm brown colour used in whisky, rum, brandy, other spirits, baked goods, confectionery, bread, syrups, vinegar and some sauces. Considered the cleanest and safest of the four caramel colour classes for use in products targeting health-conscious consumers. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E150a), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan — no animal-derived raw materials. Suitable for clean-label formulations. This is categorically different from the Caramel IV (E150d) in cola drinks that carries 4-MEI concerns.""",
    'e150b': 'E150b/INS 150b is Caustic Sulfite Caramel, made by heating carbohydrates with sulfite compounds. Used in some spirits and vinegars.',
    'e150c': 'E150c/INS 150c is Ammonia Caramel, made by heating carbohydrates with ammonium compounds. Contains some 4-MEI but less than Class IV. Used in beer, soy sauce and some confectionery.',
    'e151': 'E151/INS 151 is Brilliant Black BN (Black PN), a synthetic black azo dye. Not permitted in USA, Canada or Japan. Approved in EU; used in blackcurrant products, pastilles and some savoury foods.',
    'brilliant black': """Brilliant Black BN (Black PN / E151/INS 151) is a synthetic trisazo food dye — tetrasodium 4-acetamido-5-hydroxy-6-[7-sulfonato-4-(4-sulfonatophenylazo)-1-naphthylazo]naphthalene-1,7-disulfonate — derived from petroleum/coal tar. Produces a deep black to dark blue-black colour. Used in confectionery, some savoury snacks, liquorice products, salmon substitutes (to mimic beluga caviar colour) and some beverages. One of the 'Southampton Six' azo dyes included in the 2007 McCann study linking azo dye mixtures to hyperactivity in children; EU mandates the advisory label "may have an adverse effect on activity and attention in children." Banned in USA (not FDA-approved), Canada, Japan and Australia. Permitted in EU (E151) and India (FSSAI) at specified limits. Like other azo dyes, may cause reactions in aspirin-sensitive or salicylate-sensitive individuals. Cross-reactivity with asthma is documented. ADI: 1 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2010). EFSA's 2010 re-evaluation found no genotoxicity concerns at the ADI. In India, limited to specific product categories at concentrations up to 200 mg/kg. Not recommended for children's foods or products targeting children.""",
    'e153': 'E153/INS 153 is Vegetable Carbon (activated charcoal), a natural black pigment made from charred plant material. Used to colour confectionery, jelly and cheese rinds black. Generally safe.',
    'vegetable carbon': """Vegetable carbon (E153/INS 153 / activated vegetable charcoal) is produced by carbonising (charring) plant material — typically wood, coconut shells, peat or other vegetable matter — at high temperatures in a reduced-oxygen environment. The resulting black porous carbon material is then purified to remove hydrocarbons. Produces a deep, opaque jet-black colour. Used in: black confectionery and biscuits, cheese rind coatings (Morbier cheese), chocolate products, some liquorice products and savoury crackers. Also used as a charcoal-black pigment in cosmetics (eyeliner, mascara). The adsorbent properties of activated charcoal are relevant at higher (medicinal) doses — activated charcoal is used in hospital emergency medicine to adsorb ingested toxins in the gut. At food colouring levels, the amounts are small and adsorption of dietary nutrients is not a practical concern. However, consuming very large amounts of activated charcoal products (e.g., charcoal 'detox' drinks) alongside medications could theoretically reduce medication absorption — individuals on medications should space charcoal food product consumption away from medication times. Not approved by FDA as a food colour for general food use (approved for limited applications). Approved by EU (E153), FSSAI and CODEX. No ADI specified; generally safe at food use levels.""",
    'e160a': 'E160a/INS 160a is Beta-Carotene, the orange pigment found naturally in carrots, sweet potatoes and many fruits. Used as a food colour and Vitamin A precursor. Converts to Vitamin A in the body; safe and beneficial.',
    'e160b': 'E160b/INS 160b is Annatto extract (bixin/norbixin), a natural orange-yellow colour from the seeds of the achiote tree. Widely used in cheese, butter, snacks and cereals. Generally safe but may cause allergic reactions in sensitive individuals.',
    'paprika extract': """Paprika extract (E160c/INS 160c / oleoresin paprika) is a concentrated natural red-orange colourant and flavour ingredient obtained by solvent extraction of dried red peppers (Capsicum annuum). The primary colouring compounds are capsanthin (65% of carotenoids) and capsorubin, along with beta-carotene, zeaxanthin and other carotenoids. Capsanthin and capsorubin are xanthophyll carotenoids unique to red pepper — not found in other natural sources. Depending on extraction conditions, paprika extract may or may not retain capsaicin (the heat-giving compound); deodorised/demineralized grades used in food colouring are typically low in capsaicin. Produces warm red-orange colours with excellent fat solubility and good heat stability (more heat-stable than beta-carotene). Used to colour snack foods (crisps, namkeen), processed meats, sausages, spice blends, sauces, soups, dairy products and poultry. Also functions as a mild flavour contributor. Provides some provitamin A (beta-carotene fraction) and xanthophyll antioxidants. No significant sensitisation concerns; not an azo dye. No ADI set by JECFA (considered safe). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160c), FDA (GRAS as oleoresin paprika) and CODEX. Natural, plant-derived. Widely used in India in namkeen seasonings, masala snacks and processed meat products.""",
    'e160c': 'E160c/INS 160c is Paprika extract (capsanthin/capsorubin), a natural red-orange colour from dried capsicum peppers. Used in snack foods, processed meats and cheese. Safe and naturally derived.',
    'lycopene': """Lycopene (E160d/INS 160d) is an acyclic carotenoid (C₄₀H₅₆) — the pigment responsible for the red colour of tomatoes, watermelon, guava, pink grapefruit, papaya and rosehip. The most powerful singlet oxygen quencher among common carotenoids — approximately twice as effective as beta-carotene. Commercial lycopene is produced either by extraction from tomatoes (hexane extraction from tomato paste by-products) or by fermentation-based biosynthesis (Blakeslea trispora). As a food colour: used to give red colouration to juices, soups, sauces, confectionery and some meat products. Fat-soluble; better absorbed when consumed with dietary fat and when lycopene-containing cells are broken (cooked tomatoes provide more bioavailable lycopene than raw). As a dietary antioxidant: epidemiological studies associate higher lycopene intake with reduced risk of prostate cancer and cardiovascular disease. The evidence for prostate cancer risk reduction is particularly strong and has been cited in FDA authorised qualified health claims for tomato products. No conversion to Vitamin A (unlike beta-carotene). No significant toxicity; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160d), FDA (GRAS for tomato-derived lycopene) and CODEX. Natural, plant-derived.""",
    'e160d': 'E160d/INS 160d is Lycopene, a natural red carotenoid from tomatoes. Used as a food colour in juices, soups and sauces. Also an antioxidant studied for potential health benefits.',
    'lutein': """Lutein (E161b/INS 161b) is a xanthophyll carotenoid — a dihydroxy carotene (C₄₀H₅₆O₂) — found naturally in marigold flowers (Tagetes erecta), egg yolks, kale, spinach, corn and many other plants. Commercial food-grade lutein is primarily extracted from marigold flowers using ethanol or hexane. Produces yellow to orange-yellow colours. As a food colour, used in beverages, dairy products, confectionery, bakery products and infant formula. Unique among food carotenoids: lutein (and its isomer zeaxanthin) is selectively accumulated in the retina and macular region of the eye, where it functions as a blue light filter and antioxidant, protecting photoreceptors from oxidative damage. Strong epidemiological evidence and clinical trial data (AREDS2 study) support lutein/zeaxanthin supplementation for reducing progression of age-related macular degeneration (AMD). Absorption is enhanced by fat consumption. As a food additive colour: the amounts ingested are modest but contribute to dietary lutein intake. No safety concerns; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E161b), FDA (GRAS for specific applications) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups. Natural; plant-derived from marigold flowers.""",
    'e161b': 'E161b/INS 161b is Lutein, a natural yellow carotenoid used as a food colour. Extracted from marigold petals. Also important for eye health — accumulates in the retina.',
    'canthaxanthin': """Canthaxanthin (E161g/INS 161g) is a keto-carotenoid pigment (C₄₀H₅₂O₂) — a diketone of beta-carotene — that occurs naturally in chanterelle mushrooms, algae (Haematococcus pluvialis) and pink flamingo feathers. Commercially produced by chemical synthesis or by algal fermentation. Produces a vivid orange-red colour. Uses: food colouring in sauces, ketchup, soups, some confectionery; in aquaculture — added to salmon feed to give farmed Atlantic salmon its characteristic pink/orange flesh (wild salmon obtain this colouration from natural dietary astaxanthin and canthaxanthin). Human health concerns: high doses of canthaxanthin caused gold-crystal deposits in the retina (retinal crystalline inclusions / 'canthaxanthin retinopathy') in individuals who took high-dose canthaxanthin as a 'tanning pill' (a now-withdrawn use). The retinal deposits are generally reversible on cessation. This risk led to strict EU restrictions: EU banned canthaxanthin use as a tanning pill additive and set the ADI at 0.03 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA). FDA does not permit canthaxanthin as a colour for human food (except poultry feed). EU (E161g) and FSSAI permit limited food use at low levels. CODEX approved at specified limits. No carcinogenicity concerns at approved food doses. Individuals with retinal conditions should avoid high intake.""",
    'e161g': 'E161g/INS 161g is Canthaxanthin, an orange carotenoid colour. Used to colour certain foods and in aquaculture to give farmed salmon its pink colour. Generally safe at food use levels.',
    'e171': 'E171/INS 171 is Titanium Dioxide, a bright white pigment used in sweets, chewing gum, sauces and medicines. Banned as a food additive in the EU since 2022 due to genotoxicity concerns. Still permitted in India and the USA. Used in cosmetics and sunscreens as a UV filter.',
    'titanium dioxide': 'Titanium Dioxide (E171/INS 171) is a bright white food colour and opacity agent. Banned in the EU for food use since 2022 due to genotoxicity concerns. Still used in many countries including India in chewing gum, sweets and white sauces. Also a UV-blocking ingredient in sunscreens.',
    'e173': 'E173/INS 173 is Aluminium, a metallic silver food colour permitted only for decorating the surface of confectionery and cake decorations. Not for general food use. Aluminium accumulation in the body is a health concern at high exposures.',
    'e174': 'E174/INS 174 is Silver, a metallic food colour permitted only for decorating confectionery and chocolates. Used in edible silver leaf on sweets and cakes. Generally safe in tiny decorative amounts.',
    'e175': 'E175/INS 175 is Gold, a metallic food colour (edible gold leaf/gold powder) permitted only for decorating food surfaces. Chemically inert and safe in the tiny amounts used for decoration.',

    # ── Preservatives E200–E299 (additions) ──────────────────────────────────
    'sorbic acid': """Sorbic acid (E200/INS 200 / 2,4-hexadienoic acid / (E,E)-hexa-2,4-dienoic acid) is a naturally occurring unsaturated fatty acid (a C6 conjugated diene) first isolated from rowan berry oil (Sorbus aucuparia) in 1859 by August Wilhelm von Hofmann. While present in nature, commercial sorbic acid is produced entirely by chemical synthesis — primarily by condensation of ketene with crotonaldehyde (Reppe process), followed by isomerisation to the all-trans form. It is one of the most effective, well-tolerated and widely used food preservatives globally. Antimicrobial mechanism: the undissociated acid form (pKa 4.74) penetrates fungal and yeast cell membranes at low pH (effective below pH 6.5, optimal below 6.0); intracellularly it inhibits dehydrogenases and enzymes required for glycolysis and the tricarboxylic acid cycle, blocking energy metabolism and preventing spore germination and growth. Particularly effective against moulds and yeasts; moderate activity against bacteria. Applications: cheese (natural, processed, cottage cheese), baked goods, dried fruits, wine, soft drinks, fruit juices, vegetable margarines, confectionery, pickles and personal care products. Typically used as potassium sorbate (more soluble) or calcium sorbate rather than free sorbic acid (low water solubility). Metabolism: rapidly absorbed and metabolised via normal beta-oxidation of fatty acids to CO₂ and water — no bioaccumulation. Safety profile: ADI of 25 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). No carcinogenicity, mutagenicity or reproductive toxicity at any relevant dose. Rare occupational skin sensitisation in food workers. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E200), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e200': 'E200/INS 200 is Sorbic Acid, a natural preservative found in rowan berries. Used in cheese, baked goods, dried fruit and beverages to inhibit mould and yeast. One of the safest food preservatives.',
    'potassium sorbate': 'Potassium sorbate (E202/INS 202) is the potassium salt of sorbic acid, used as a preservative in a vast range of foods including cheese, wine, baked goods and personal care products. One of the most widely used and well-tolerated food preservatives.',
    'e202': 'E202/INS 202 is Potassium Sorbate, the salt form of sorbic acid. Extremely widely used to preserve cheese, bread, wine, dried fruits and beverages. Well-studied and generally regarded as safe.',
    'calcium sorbate': """The calcium salt of sorbic acid (E203/INS 203), formed by reacting sorbic acid with calcium hydroxide. Sorbic acid is a naturally occurring trans,trans-2,4-hexadienoic acid first isolated from rowan berry oil in 1859. Calcium sorbate is less hygroscopic than potassium sorbate and is occasionally preferred in applications where water activity management is important. Mechanism: at pH ≤6.5, undissociated sorbic acid disrupts the enzyme systems of yeasts and moulds (particularly inhibiting dehydrogenase enzymes), preventing their growth. Effective against a broad spectrum of moulds, yeasts and some bacteria. Used in cheese, baked goods, dried fruits, beverages and wine at typical levels of 0.05–0.2%. Generally regarded as one of the safest food preservatives — metabolised via normal fatty acid beta-oxidation to CO₂ and water. Rare cases of skin sensitisation reported in occupational settings (bakers, food workers). No known carcinogenicity or reproductive toxicity. ADI set by JECFA at 25 mg/kg body weight/day (as sorbic acid equivalents). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E203), CODEX. Provides a small amount of dietary calcium.""",
    'e203': 'E203/INS 203 is Calcium Sorbate, a preservative derived from sorbic acid. Used in some processed foods and beverages. Generally safe.',
    'potassium benzoate': """Potassium benzoate (E212/INS 212 / dipotassium dibenzoate) is the potassium salt of benzoic acid (a naturally occurring phenolic acid found in cranberries, prunes and cinnamon), produced by neutralising benzoic acid with potassium hydroxide or potassium carbonate. Less commonly used than sodium benzoate (E211) due to slightly lower water solubility and higher cost, but preferred in sodium-reduced formulations. Antimicrobial mechanism: at low pH (below 4.5), the free undissociated benzoic acid penetrates microbial cell membranes, disrupts membrane potential and inhibits glycolysis and the electron transport chain, preventing growth of bacteria, yeasts and moulds. Effective only in acidic products — becomes essentially inactive near neutral pH. Used in: carbonated soft drinks, fruit juices, cordials, squashes, syrups, jams, pickles, sauces and condiments. Benzene formation concern: when benzoates co-occur with ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) in acidic beverages containing trace transition metals (iron, copper), a Fenton-type reaction can produce benzene — IARC Group 1 known human carcinogen. FDA and EU have set guidance limiting benzene in beverages; manufacturers can prevent formation by using chelating agents (EDTA), controlling metal contamination, or avoiding the combination. Hyperactivity concern: the 2007 Southampton study identified sodium benzoate as part of a mixture associated with increased hyperactivity in children; the same concern extends to potassium benzoate. Some aspirin-sensitive individuals react to benzoates via cyclo-oxygenase cross-reactivity. JECFA ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (as benzoic acid). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E212), FDA and CODEX at specified limits.""",
    'e212': 'E212/INS 212 is Potassium Benzoate, a preservative used in soft drinks and acidic foods. Can potentially form benzene when combined with Vitamin C.',
    'calcium benzoate': """Calcium benzoate (E213/INS 213 / calcium dibenzoate / Ca(C₆H₅COO)₂) is the calcium salt of benzoic acid, a naturally occurring phenolic acid found in cranberries, plums, cinnamon and many fermented foods. Produced by neutralising benzoic acid with calcium hydroxide. Functions as an antimicrobial preservative — effective against yeasts, moulds and many bacteria at low pH (below 4.5–5.0), where the undissociated benzoic acid form can permeate microbial cell membranes and inhibit glycolysis and the electron transport chain. Used in beverages, sauces, jams, pickles, condiments and seafood products. Advantage over sodium benzoate: calcium benzoate provides calcium rather than sodium — useful in sodium-reduced formulations; however, less commonly used than sodium benzoate (E211) due to lower solubility. The benzene formation concern: when benzoates (sodium or calcium) co-occur with ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) in acidic beverages, trace amounts of benzene can form via a Fenton-type reaction catalysed by transition metal ions (iron, copper) — even at very low concentrations. Benzene is IARC Group 1 (known human carcinogen). Both benzoates and ascorbic acid are individually safe; the combination in certain beverages warrants attention. Manufacturers manage this by: using chelating agents (EDTA); avoiding direct mixing; or replacing benzoate with alternative preservatives. Hyperactivity concern: the 2007 McCann study (Southampton study) identified sodium benzoate as part of a mix associated with increased hyperactivity in children — while calcium benzoate was not specifically studied, the same benzoate moiety is involved. Some EU regulators have called for voluntary removal from children's foods. JECFA ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day for benzoic acid and benzoates (as benzoic acid). Approved by FSSAI (Annexure 3 of Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations), EU (E213), and CODEX. Not approved by FDA as a direct food additive (sodium benzoate is GRAS but calcium benzoate has limited US FDA status). People with aspirin sensitivity may also react to benzoates. Avoid in children's soft drinks and fruit beverages containing ascorbic acid.""",
    'e213': 'E213/INS 213 is Calcium Benzoate, a calcium-based preservative less common than sodium or potassium benzoate. Used in some jams and beverages.',
    'nisin': """Nisin (E234/INS 234) is a natural polycyclic antimicrobial peptide (bacteriocin) consisting of 34 amino acids, produced by the gram-positive bacterium Lactococcus lactis subsp. lactis through fermentation of glucose or lactose substrates. It is the first bacteriocin approved for food use and remains the most commercially significant. Classification: a lantibiotic (a class of bacteriocins that contain unusual amino acids — lanthionine and beta-methyllanthionine — generated by post-translational modification). First isolated from milk starter cultures in the 1920s; commercial food use approved since the 1960s. Mechanism: nisin binds to lipid II (a cell wall biosynthesis precursor) on gram-positive bacterial membranes with very high affinity, inhibiting cell wall synthesis and simultaneously forming pores in the membrane — a dual mechanism that makes resistance development exceptionally difficult. Spectrum: highly effective against gram-positive bacteria including Listeria monocytogenes, Clostridium botulinum (and other clostridia), Bacillus cereus, Staphylococcus aureus and many lactic acid bacteria. Not active against gram-negative bacteria (their outer membrane excludes nisin). Applications: processed cheese and cheese spreads (prevents Clostridium tyrobutyricum late blowing defect, extends shelf life), canned soups and vegetables (reduces spore outgrowth during mild heat treatment), pasteurised liquid egg, some beverages and dairy desserts. Metabolised by digestive proteases to amino acids — effectively digested like any food peptide. No accumulation. Does not affect the human gut microbiome at food-additive levels (primarily gram-negative bacteria dominate). Not known to induce resistance to clinically important antibiotics. ADI: not specified (JECFA — safe at food use levels). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E234), FDA and CODEX. Natural origin; derived from bacterial fermentation.""",
    'e234': 'E234/INS 234 is Nisin, a natural bacteriocin (antimicrobial peptide) produced by bacteria. Used to preserve processed cheeses and canned goods. Natural origin and generally regarded as safe.',
    'natamycin': """Natamycin (E235/INS 235 / pimaricin) is a naturally occurring polyene macrolide antifungal antibiotic produced by the soil bacterium Streptomyces natalensis (and related species), first isolated in Natal, South Africa in 1955. Chemically it is a large 26-membered lactone ring macrolide with a polyene chromophore and an amino sugar (mycosamine) — the same structural class as amphotericin B (a clinical antifungal). Mechanism: natamycin binds with high specificity to ergosterol, the dominant sterol in fungal cell membranes (absent in mammalian cell membranes which contain cholesterol instead). This binding disrupts membrane structure and permeability, killing fungi and yeasts without affecting bacteria or mammalian cells. Spectrum: active against all common food spoilage moulds (Aspergillus, Penicillium, Mucor, Rhizopus) and yeasts; not active against bacteria or food-fermenting lactic acid bacteria. Applications: applied as a surface spray, dip or coating on hard and semi-hard cheese rinds (prevents mould growth on the surface without penetrating the cheese interior); surface treatment of cured sausage casings; some wine and fruit juice applications. Typical use level: 1–20 mg/kg on cheese surface. Critical safety advantage: because natamycin is too large a molecule to be absorbed across the gut epithelium, it has minimal systemic exposure even if consumed on the food surface. No detected residues in the interior of properly treated cheese. Used in human medicine as a topical ophthalmic antifungal (eye drops for fungal keratitis). No known resistance development in food applications. ADI: 0–0.3 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E235), FDA (approved for cheese surface) and CODEX.""",
    'e235': 'E235/INS 235 is Natamycin (Pimaricin), a natural antifungal preservative applied to cheese rinds and sausage surfaces. Naturally derived and considered safe.',
    'potassium nitrite': """Potassium nitrite (KNO₂ / E249/INS 249) is an inorganic salt used as a curing agent in processed and preserved meats — functionally identical to sodium nitrite (E250) but providing potassium instead of sodium, which is relevant in sodium-reduced product formulations. Mechanism: nitrite ions (NO₂⁻) react with myoglobin in meat muscle to form nitrosomyoglobin, which on cooking converts to the heat-stable nitrosohemochrome — producing the characteristic pink-red colour of cured meats like bacon, ham, hot dogs and certain sausages. More critically, nitrite inhibits growth of Clostridium botulinum spores in anaerobic, low-acid meat environments — preventing botulism, a potentially fatal form of food poisoning. The inhibitory effect requires an acidic environment (pH below 6) and adequate nitrite concentration. Health concern — nitrosamines: nitrite can react with secondary amines derived from amino acid degradation in the meat matrix or under high-heat cooking conditions (frying, grilling bacon at very high temperatures) to form N-nitrosamines — compounds classified as IARC Group 2A probable human carcinogens. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid or sodium ascorbate) is routinely co-added to cured meats to inhibit nitrosamine formation by competing with amines for available nitrite. IARC has classified processed meat consumption (not nitrite specifically) as Group 1 carcinogenic. Regulatory position: FSSAI, EU (E249) and CODEX approve potassium nitrite at strictly controlled limits (≤80–150 mg/kg depending on product), as the botulism prevention benefit outweighs the risk at approved levels. Not permitted in food for infants. WHO recommends limiting processed meat intake.""",
    'e249': 'E249/INS 249 is Potassium Nitrite, a curing agent in processed meats that prevents botulism but can form carcinogenic nitrosamines under high heat.',
    'potassium nitrate': """Potassium nitrate (saltpetre / E252/INS 252, KNO₃) is an inorganic salt used since ancient times as a meat curing agent and food preservative. Unlike sodium nitrite (E250), which acts immediately, potassium nitrate is a slow-release curing agent — bacterial reduction by endogenous Micrococcaceae and Staphylococci enzymes in the meat matrix converts nitrate to nitrite over days to weeks, making it suitable for long-cured products like dry-cured ham, salami and hard sausage. Nitrite then reacts with myoglobin to form the stable, cooked pink colour characteristic of cured meats, and inhibits Clostridium botulinum growth. Naturally present in many vegetables (spinach, celery, beetroot) at levels comparable to or exceeding cured meat contributions. Health concern: nitrite (formed from nitrate) can react with secondary amines (from amino acid degradation) in the meat matrix and during high-heat cooking (frying, grilling) to form nitrosamines, classified as probable carcinogens (IARC Group 2A). Vitamin C (sodium ascorbate) is often co-added to cured meats to inhibit nitrosamine formation. FSSAI, EU (E252) and CODEX approved at strictly controlled limits. WHO recommends limiting processed meat consumption. Not recommended for infants.""",
    'e252': 'E252/INS 252 is Potassium Nitrate (saltpetre), a traditional meat curing agent. Slower-acting than nitrite; converts to nitrite during the curing process.',
    'lactic acid': """Lactic acid (CH₃CH(OH)COOH / E270/INS 270) is a two-carbon alpha-hydroxy acid that is one of the most ubiquitous organic acids in biology and food science. It exists in two stereoisomeric forms: L-(+)-lactic acid (the biologically prevalent form, identical to the compound produced in muscle during anaerobic exercise) and D-(-)-lactic acid (produced by some bacteria). Commercial production: predominantly via microbial fermentation of glucose or lactose by Lactobacillus species (L. delbrueckii, L. acidophilus, L. helveticus) — a process that mirrors its natural production in dairy fermentation. Naturally present in: yoghurt, cheese, sour cream, kefir, buttermilk (formed by Lactobacillus activity); sauerkraut, kimchi, pickles and other lacto-fermented vegetables; sourdough bread; beer and wine. Also produced endogenously in all human cells via anaerobic glycolysis and the Cori cycle. Food functions: (1) acidulant and pH regulator — mild, smooth acidic taste without sharpness; used in beverages, confectionery, sauces and bakery; (2) preservative — inhibits spoilage and pathogenic bacteria by lowering pH; (3) flavour compound — the primary characteristic acid of fermented dairy products; (4) meat acidification in fermented sausages; (5) calcium lactate formation in cheese. Cosmetic/skincare function: one of the most important alpha-hydroxy acids (AHAs) in dermatology — loosens desmosomal bonds between corneocytes in the stratum corneum, accelerating skin cell turnover; stimulates collagen synthesis; used in chemical peels (glycolic + lactic), anti-ageing creams and moisturisers (as a natural moisturising factor component); pKa 3.86. Health: L-lactic acid is metabolised via the Cori cycle in the liver (converted back to glucose) and by many tissues. No mutagenicity or carcinogenicity. D-lactic acid at high levels is poorly metabolised and can cause D-lactic acidosis in short-bowel syndrome patients — not a concern from normal food consumption. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA GRAS; EU (E270); FSSAI and CODEX approved. Very widely used dual food/cosmetic ingredient.""",
    'e270': 'E270/INS 270 is Lactic Acid, produced by fermentation of sugars. Found naturally in yoghurt, sauerkraut and pickles. Used as a preservative and flavouring. Safe and naturally derived.',
    'potassium propionate': """The potassium salt of propionic acid (E283/INS 283), used as a mould and rope inhibitor in bread, baked goods, tortillas and some processed cheese products. Propionic acid is a short-chain saturated fatty acid naturally produced in significant amounts during gut fermentation of dietary fibre, and occurs in Swiss cheese (up to 1%) as a natural fermentation product. Potassium propionate is preferred over calcium propionate when additional calcium in the formula is undesirable, and over sodium propionate for sodium reduction. Mechanism: propionic acid (released at bread-dough pH ~5–6) inhibits fungal growth by disrupting membrane transport and blocking key intracellular enzymes; particularly effective against Rhizopus stolonifer (bread mould) and Bacillus subtilis (rope in bread). Does not inhibit yeast fermentation at food use concentrations. FSSAI, EU (E283), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved at specified limits (typically 0.1–0.3% of flour weight). A minority of people experience migraine after propionate consumption — likely linked to biogenic amine interactions in susceptible individuals. Metabolised identically to naturally occurring propionate via the methylmalonyl-CoA pathway.""",
    'e283': 'E283/INS 283 is Potassium Propionate, a preservative used in bread to prevent mould. Generally safe; some individuals report migraine sensitivity.',
    'carbon dioxide': """Carbon dioxide (CO₂ / E290/INS 290) is a colourless, odourless gas and one of the most versatile food-grade gases used in food and beverage processing. It is naturally produced in fermentation (yeast fermentation in bread baking, beer and wine making), combustion and respiration, and commercially extracted from fermentation processes or captured from industrial sources, then purified to food-grade specifications. Food functions: (1) Carbonation — the primary use; CO₂ dissolves in water under pressure to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which provides the characteristic fizz, tang and mouthfeel of carbonated beverages (soft drinks, soda water, sparkling water, beer, sparkling wine); carbonation contributes to the tartness of fizzy drinks; CO₂ release on opening provides sensory stimulation; (2) Modified Atmosphere Packaging (MAP) — CO₂ is used as a component of MAP gas mixtures (often with nitrogen and oxygen) to inhibit bacterial and fungal growth, delaying spoilage and extending shelf life of fresh meat, fish, cheese, bakery products and fresh produce; CO₂ is bacteriostatic — it dissolves in product moisture to form carbonic acid, lowering pH and inhibiting aerobic bacteria and moulds; (3) Propellant gas in aerosol dispensers for whipped cream (cream chargers contain compressed CO₂ or nitrous oxide); (4) Quick-freezing — dry ice (solid CO₂, sublimation temperature -78.5°C) is used for rapid freezing and cold transport of perishable goods; (5) Supercritical CO₂ extraction — used in food-grade extraction of essential oils, hops, caffeine (decaffeination of coffee), spice oleoresins and natural colours without chemical solvent residues. At food-use concentrations, CO₂ is completely safe. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E290), FDA and CODEX approved.""",
    'e290': 'E290/INS 290 is Carbon Dioxide, the gas responsible for carbonation in fizzy drinks. Also used in packaging to preserve freshness. Safe and naturally occurring.',
    'malic acid': """Malic acid (E296/INS 296 / 2-hydroxysuccinic acid / 2-hydroxybutanedioic acid) is a naturally occurring dicarboxylic acid and a key intermediate in the Krebs (citric acid) cycle found in every aerobic organism. Particularly abundant in unripe apples (up to 9 g/L in apple juice — responsible for the characteristic green-apple tartness), sour cherries, grapes, rhubarb, quinces and kiwi. Commercially produced either by enzymatic conversion of fumaric acid (using fumarase enzyme) or by chemical synthesis via hydration of fumaric acid or maleic acid. Exists as two enantiomers: L-malic acid (naturally occurring, present in all fruit) and D-malic acid (synthetic) — food additives may use the racemic DL mixture (E296) or pure L-form. Properties: moderately sour with a smooth, persistent sourness that lingers longer on the palate than citric acid and has a less sharp initial punch; highly water soluble; enhances fruit flavours. Applications: confectionery (sour gummies, hard candies — provides the intense sour coating in sour sweets; widely used with citric acid for sour taste); carbonated and still fruit beverages (provides a rounded apple-fruit tartness); jams, jellies and preserves; baked goods; wine production (added to low-acid wines to adjust tartness and balance); dietary supplements and sports drinks. Metabolism: L-malic acid is a normal cellular metabolite and is fully metabolised via the Krebs cycle to CO₂ and water. The D-form (in synthetic DL-malic acid) is metabolised more slowly but is still metabolised completely — no accumulation. Malate supplementation is used in sports nutrition as it may support energy production during high-intensity exercise (component of citrate and malate shuttle). ADI: not specified (JECFA). Very safe. FSSAI, EU (E296), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e296': 'E296/INS 296 is Malic Acid, the tart acid found naturally in apples and grapes. Used to add tartness to sweets, beverages and jams. Safe and naturally derived.',
    'fumaric acid': """Fumaric acid (E297/INS 297 / trans-butenedioic acid) is an unsaturated dicarboxylic acid that is a key intermediate in the Krebs (citric acid) cycle — the universal metabolic pathway used by all aerobic organisms to generate cellular energy. Chemically it is the trans-isomer of maleic acid. Natural sources: formed in all human cells during the citric acid cycle from succinate via succinate dehydrogenase; also occurs in mushrooms (Agaricus bisporus) and Iceland moss (Cetraria islandica). Food-grade production: primarily by catalytic isomerisation of maleic acid (a by-product of phthalic anhydride manufacture) using isomerase enzymes or heat. Physical properties: white, crystalline, odourless powder; the strongest (highest acid power per gram) of the common food-approved acidulants — approximately 1.36 times more acidic than citric acid on an equivalent weight basis; poorly soluble in cold water (0.6 g/100 mL at 25°C) but dissolves well in hot water; non-hygroscopic, making it ideal for dry baking mixes and powdered beverages without caking. Food uses: leavening acid in baking powder (reacts with sodium bicarbonate to release CO₂); acidulant and tartness enhancer in carbonated beverages, hard candies, gummies, fruit-flavoured snacks and pie fillings; pH control in grape-based juices; fumigation of dried figs (traditional use in some regions). Pharmaceutical use: fumaric acid esters (dimethyl fumarate) are approved treatments for relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis and severe psoriasis — demonstrating potent anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective actions via Nrf2 pathway activation; this is an entirely different dose range (pharmaceutical) from food use. Health at food use levels: metabolised completely through the citric acid cycle; no accumulation; no mutagenicity. At very high food intake, mild GI irritation possible. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E297), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. Safe.""",
    'e297': 'E297/INS 297 is Fumaric Acid, a natural organic acid used as an acidulant in baked goods, soft drinks and jelly. Safe and naturally occurring.',

    # ── Antioxidants & Acidity Regulators E300–E399 (additions) ─────────────
    'ascorbic acid': """Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C / E300/INS 300) is an essential water-soluble vitamin and one of the most important natural antioxidants in the human diet, found richly in amla (Indian gooseberry ~600 mg/100g), guava (~200 mg/100g), kiwi, citrus fruits, strawberries and bell peppers. Chemically: a five-membered lactone ring with an enol-diol functional group at C2–C3 that readily donates electrons, making it an outstanding reducing agent. Humans must obtain Vitamin C entirely from diet (lack the enzyme L-gulonolactone oxidase). Biochemical functions: essential cofactor for collagen prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases (deficiency causes scurvy — bleeding gums, impaired wound healing); carnitine and norepinephrine synthesis; non-haem iron absorption enhancement; regenerates oxidised Vitamin E (tocopheryl radical → tocopherol) in synergistic antioxidant recycling. As food additive (E300): prevents enzymatic browning in cut produce, fruit juices and jams; protects against lipid oxidation and off-flavour development in beverages; added to cured meats alongside sodium nitrite to inhibit nitrosamine formation; used as a flour treatment agent in bread baking (oxidises thiol groups in gluten, strengthening dough). Safety: extremely safe at food use levels. Very high supplemental doses (>2 g/day) may cause osmotic diarrhoea, increased oxalate excretion (risk of calcium oxalate kidney stones in susceptible individuals). ADI: not specified (JECFA — considered safe at any food use level). ICMR RDA for India: 40 mg/day adults. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E300), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e300': 'E300/INS 300 is Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C), used as an antioxidant and preservative in foods. Essential vitamin and generally very safe.',
    'tocopherols': """Tocopherols (E306/INS 306) are the naturally occurring forms of Vitamin E — a family of four fat-soluble compounds (alpha-, beta-, gamma- and delta-tocopherol) biosynthesised by plants and abundant in vegetable oils (wheat germ oil ~150 mg/100g; sunflower oil ~56 mg/100g; soybean oil ~16 mg/100g), nuts, seeds and leafy vegetables. Chemical structure: a chromanol ring with a phenolic hydroxyl group attached to a 16-carbon phytyl side chain. The phenolic OH is the active antioxidant site — it donates a hydrogen atom to lipid peroxy radicals (LOO•) in cell membranes and fat matrices, interrupting the auto-oxidative chain reaction that causes rancidity and cellular oxidative damage. Alpha-tocopherol has the highest biological Vitamin E activity; gamma-tocopherol (predominant in soybean and corn oil) provides better protection against nitrogen dioxide free radicals. As food additive: used to prevent rancidity in edible oils, margarines, nut butters, infant formula, baby foods, breakfast cereals and fat-containing cosmetic preparations. "Mixed tocopherols" (E306) refers to natural concentrates extracted from vegetable oil deodorisation distillates during oil refining — a by-product of the refining process. More stable and regarded as more "natural" than individual synthetic tocopherols. As nutrient: RDA is 15 mg alpha-tocopherol equivalents/day (adults); deficiency (rare, seen in fat malabsorption conditions) causes peripheral neuropathy and haemolytic anaemia. High-dose supplementation (>400 IU/day) has shown potential harm in meta-analyses. At food additive use levels, far below supplemental doses. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E306–E309), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e306': 'E306/INS 306 is mixed Tocopherols (natural Vitamin E), used as an antioxidant in oils and fat-based foods. Naturally derived and considered safe.',
    'alpha tocopherol': """Alpha-tocopherol (E307/INS 307) is the biologically most active of the eight natural forms of Vitamin E (four tocopherols and four tocotrienols). Chemically it is 2,5,7,8-tetramethyl-2-(4,8,12-trimethyltridecyl)chroman-6-ol. As an antioxidant it is fat-soluble and preferentially incorporated into cell membrane phospholipid bilayers and LDL particles, where it donates hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxy radicals and terminates lipid oxidation chain reactions. Regenerated by Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) in a synergistic interaction. As a food additive: used to protect oils, margarines, infant formula, baby foods, nut butters and cosmetic oils from oxidation. Can be naturally extracted from vegetable oils (soya, sunflower, wheat germ) or synthesised as all-rac-alpha-tocopherol (the synthetic form has lower biological activity than natural RRR-alpha-tocopherol). As a nutrient: the recommended dietary allowance (RDA) is 15 mg/day for adults; deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy and haemolytic anaemia. Supplemental alpha-tocopherol at very high doses (>400 IU/day long-term) has shown increased all-cause mortality in some meta-analyses, though the evidence is debated. At food additive use levels (antioxidant function), doses are much lower than supplemental levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E307), FDA (GRAS) approved.""",
    'e307': 'E307/INS 307 is Alpha-Tocopherol (Vitamin E), a natural antioxidant used to prevent rancidity in oils and fat-containing products.',
    'e308': 'E308/INS 308 is Gamma-Tocopherol, a form of Vitamin E used as a food antioxidant. Naturally present in soybean and corn oils.',
    'e309': 'E309/INS 309 is Delta-Tocopherol, a form of Vitamin E found in soybean oil. Used as a food antioxidant.',
    'octyl gallate': """Octyl gallate (E311/INS 311) is the octyl ester of gallic acid (3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoic acid), a synthetic antioxidant in the gallate family. Produced by esterification of gallic acid (extracted from oak galls or gallnut) with 1-octanol. More lipophilic than propyl gallate (E310), making it better suited for protecting high-fat products. Used to prevent oxidative rancidity in edible fats and oils, chewing gum, margarine and cosmetics at typical concentrations of 50–100 mg/kg fat. Mechanism: the three hydroxyl groups on the galloyl ring donate hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxy radicals, quenching the oxidative chain reaction. Synergistic with citric acid (chelates pro-oxidant metal ions). Health concerns: gallates can cause allergic contact dermatitis and bronchospasm in aspirin-sensitive individuals (cross-reactivity with salicylates); reactions have been documented in atopic individuals. Some cases of oral mucosal reactions and contact stomatitis with propyl gallate — likely extends to octyl gallate. Not approved in the USA specifically as octyl gallate; approved in EU (E311), FSSAI and CODEX at specified limits. ADI: 0–0.5 mg/kg body weight/day as gallates combined (JECFA — applies to propyl, octyl and dodecyl gallates together).""",
    'e311': 'E311/INS 311 is Octyl Gallate, a synthetic antioxidant in oils and fats. May cause reactions in aspirin-sensitive people.',
    'dodecyl gallate': """Dodecyl gallate (lauryl gallate / E312/INS 312) is the dodecyl (C12) ester of gallic acid — the most lipophilic member of the food-approved gallate antioxidant family. Produced by esterification of gallic acid with dodecanol (lauryl alcohol). Excellent solubility in oils and fats; used to protect animal fats (lard, tallow), margarine and edible oils from oxidative rancidity. Typical use level: 50–200 mg/kg fat. Like other gallates, it acts by donating hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxy radicals, interrupting the free radical chain. Due to high lipophilicity, it distributes almost entirely in the fat phase, providing excellent in-situ protection of the most oxidation-susceptible matrix. Health concerns: contact sensitisation is the primary concern — dodecyl gallate has been identified as a skin sensitiser and is included in standard patch test series by dermatology organisations. May trigger allergic reactions in aspirin-sensitive individuals (gallic acid is structurally related to salicylates). Cases of systemic contact dermatitis from food additives have been documented with gallates. ADI: 0–0.5 mg/kg body weight/day for the total gallate group (propyl + octyl + dodecyl combined, JECFA). Approved by EU (E312) and CODEX; not specifically approved by FDA as a food additive in the USA.""",
    'e312': 'E312/INS 312 is Dodecyl Gallate (Lauryl Gallate), a synthetic antioxidant in fats. Potential skin sensitiser.',
    'erythorbic acid': """Erythorbic acid (D-isoascorbic acid / E315/INS 315) is the D-stereoisomer of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C is L-ascorbic acid) — a structural isomer with almost identical chemical antioxidant properties but only approximately 5% of the biological Vitamin C activity. Because the human body's L-specific transport proteins and enzyme cofactor binding sites do not efficiently recognise the D-form, erythorbic acid cannot substitute for Vitamin C nutritionally. Produced commercially by fermentation of sucrose or glucose, or by chemical isomerisation of L-ascorbic acid — generally lower cost than Vitamin C. Mechanism: like ascorbic acid, the enol-diol functional group on the lactone ring readily donates electrons to oxygen and free radicals, making it an effective reducing agent and oxygen scavenger. Applications: used in cured meats (bacon, ham, sausages, hot dogs) and deli products to accelerate and stabilise the nitrosomyoglobin colour reaction with sodium nitrite (reduces reaction time for pink cured colour to develop); scavenges residual oxygen in packaged meats; used in carbonated beverages and fruit drinks to prevent oxidative discolouration; applied to frozen fish and shellfish to prevent melanosis (black spot). Health: very safe in food quantities. Very large doses (>3 g/day, vastly exceeding food intake) may compete with Vitamin C for cellular transporters, potentially reducing tissue ascorbate levels — not relevant at food additive use levels. Not mutagenic or carcinogenic. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA GRAS; EU (E315); FSSAI and CODEX approved. Suitable for vegetarians and vegans.""",
    'e315': 'E315/INS 315 is Erythorbic Acid (Isoascorbic Acid), an antioxidant preservative used in meats and beverages. Does not have vitamin C activity. Generally safe.',
    'sodium erythorbate': """Sodium erythorbate (E316/INS 316 / sodium isoascorbate) is the sodium salt of erythorbic acid (D-isoascorbic acid), a stereoisomer of Vitamin C (L-ascorbic acid) with almost identical antioxidant chemistry but only about 5% of the biological Vitamin C activity. Produced by chemical synthesis from sucrose via fermentation by Penicillium notatum or by chemical isomerisation of sodium ascorbate. Chemical nature: a five-membered lactone ring with an enol diol group that readily donates electrons to oxygen and free radicals, making it an effective reducing agent and antioxidant. Primary food function: in cured and processed meats (bacon, hot dogs, sausages, corned beef, deli meats), sodium erythorbate serves two critical roles — (1) it rapidly reduces metmyoglobin back to myoglobin and then to nitric oxide myoglobin, accelerating the pink colour fixation reaction with sodium nitrite, so less nitrite is needed for the same colour effect; (2) it scavenges residual oxygen and acts as a reducing agent to prevent nitrosamine formation by reacting preferentially with nitrite under acidic conditions, reducing the reaction between nitrite and secondary amines. Also used in beverages, frozen fish and fruit products as an oxygen scavenger. Health: generally very safe. At high supplemental doses (>3 g/day), sodium erythorbate may competitively inhibit Vitamin C transport, potentially reducing Vitamin C tissue levels — not a concern at food additive use levels. No mutagenicity or carcinogenicity concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA GRAS; EU (E316); FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e316': 'E316/INS 316 is Sodium Erythorbate, an antioxidant used in cured meats and beverages to maintain colour and freshness.',
    'tbhq': """TBHQ (tertiary butylhydroquinone / E319/INS 319) is a synthetic monophenolic antioxidant derived from hydroquinone by tert-butylation — a petroleum-based industrial process. It is one of the most effective antioxidants for preventing oxidative rancidity in polyunsaturated fats. Mechanism: the phenolic hydroxyl group donates hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxy radicals (LOO•), breaking the auto-oxidation chain reaction; TBHQ is particularly effective in oils with high linoleic or linolenic acid content (soybean oil, sunflower oil, cottonseed oil) because these polyunsaturated fatty acids are most susceptible to oxidative degradation. Applications: used in cooking oils, palm shortening, frying fats, instant noodles, crackers, microwave popcorn, potato chips, animal feed fats and cosmetic lipid formulations. Typically used at 100–200 mg/kg fat content. Often combined with BHA, BHT or citric acid synergistically. Maximum permitted level in EU and many jurisdictions: 200 mg/kg in fat content. Safety concerns: at doses far above food use levels, TBHQ causes stomach tumours in rats — mechanism appears rodent-specific (forestomach hyperplasia, absent in humans). A 2020 EWG-highlighted study (Environmental Science & Technology Letters) suggested high-dose TBHQ in mice impaired T-cell function and worsened influenza outcomes — the dose was ~125× the ADI, making direct human relevance uncertain. JECFA ADI: 0–0.7 mg/kg body weight/day. NOT approved in EU for food use (banned in EU since 1990s, though permitted in cosmetics). Approved by FSSAI, FDA (GRAS), CODEX (at specified limits). Countries without EU-equivalent regulations (including India) still permit TBHQ widely in edible oils. Consumers can avoid it by choosing oils that use natural antioxidants (rosemary extract, mixed tocopherols).""",
    'e319': 'E319/INS 319 is TBHQ (Tertiary Butylhydroquinone), a synthetic antioxidant used in edible oils, instant noodles and crackers. Considered safe at approved doses; high doses are toxic.',
    'tertiary butylhydroquinone': """Tertiary butylhydroquinone (TBHQ / E319/INS 319) is the full chemical name for the synthetic antioxidant widely known by its acronym TBHQ. It is a tert-butyl substituted hydroquinone — a benzene-1,4-diol with a tert-butyl group at the 2-position. Synthesised by acid-catalysed alkylation of hydroquinone with isobutylene (derived from petroleum refining). TBHQ is the most widely used synthetic antioxidant in edible oils in India and much of Southeast Asia and the Americas. Mechanism: phenolic hydrogen donation to lipid peroxy radicals interrupts the propagation phase of lipid autoxidation, dramatically extending the shelf life of polyunsaturated fatty acid-containing products (sunflower, soybean, canola and palm olein oils). A single TBHQ molecule can scavenge two peroxy radicals. Applications: frying oils, instant noodles, crackers, snack foods, animal feed fats, cosmetics (lipstick, skin creams). Typical use level: 50–200 mg/kg of fat. Safety status: JECFA ADI of 0–0.7 mg/kg body weight/day. FSSAI (India), FDA (USA), CODEX Alimentarius and many countries permit TBHQ. However, the European Union does NOT permit TBHQ in food (prohibited under EU flavouring and antioxidant regulations) — making TBHQ one of the most notable regulatory divergences globally. At extreme overdose levels (far exceeding any food intake scenario), TBHQ is toxic; no carcinogenicity risk at food levels in humans. Recent animal immunology studies (2020) noted potential impairment of T-helper cell function at high doses — mechanistic significance for humans at food use levels is unclear and not established. Consumers choosing to avoid TBHQ should look for products using tocopherols (Vitamin E) or rosemary extract as antioxidants.""",
    'lecithin': """Lecithin (E322/INS 322 / phosphatidylcholine / soy lecithin / sunflower lecithin / egg lecithin) is a complex mixture of phospholipids — the most widely used food emulsifier in the world, occurring naturally in all living cells as a fundamental component of cell membranes. Major commercial sources: soybean (by far the largest global source — a by-product of soy oil refining); sunflower seed (the allergen-free, often non-GMO alternative); egg yolk (used in specialist applications); rapeseed/canola. Composition: a mixture of phospholipids — primarily phosphatidylcholine (PC, ~35%), phosphatidylethanolamine (PE, ~20%), phosphatidylinositol (PI, ~20%), phosphatidic acid and minor phospholipids — plus neutral lipids and glycolipids. Phospholipids are amphiphilic: they have a water-soluble phosphate-containing head group and fat-soluble fatty acid tail(s), enabling them to position at oil-water interfaces and stabilise emulsions. Emulsification mechanism: lecithin molecules concentrate at oil-water interfaces, reducing surface tension and forming a mechanical barrier that prevents oil droplets from coalescing. Food applications: chocolate (reduces viscosity and improves flow; prevents fat bloom; typically 0.2–0.5%); margarine and spreads; baked goods (dough strengthener, emulsifier for fats, extends shelf life); mayonnaise and salad dressings; infant formula; pasta; instant food mixes; protein supplements (improves dispersibility). Allergen note: soy lecithin is derived from soybeans (a major allergen) but typically contains minimal soy protein — the highly purified lecithin fraction usually contains <1 ppm soy protein, below levels that trigger reactions in most soy-allergic individuals; however, people with very severe soy allergy should exercise caution. Sunflower lecithin is soy-free and increasingly preferred for allergen-sensitive formulations. Nutritional benefit: lecithin is a dietary source of choline — an essential nutrient for liver function (VLDL synthesis and fat export), brain development and neurotransmitter synthesis (acetylcholine). ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E322), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan from plant sources.""",
    'e322': 'E322/INS 322 is Lecithin, a natural emulsifier from soy, sunflower or eggs. Widely used in chocolate, mayonnaise and baked goods. Safe and naturally occurring.',
    'soy lecithin': 'Soy lecithin (E322) is lecithin derived specifically from soybeans. Widely used as an emulsifier in chocolate, bread and margarine. Generally safe; those with severe soy allergies should be aware, though soy lecithin typically contains minimal soy protein.',
    'sunflower lecithin': """Sunflower lecithin (E322) is lecithin derived from sunflower seeds — the allergen-free, often non-GMO alternative to soy lecithin (E322). Both forms have the same emulsifying function and phospholipid composition (phosphatidylcholine, phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylinositol). Used in chocolate, baked goods, protein supplements, infant formula and margarine. See the main 'sunflower lecithin' entry for full chemistry, safety and regulatory details. Key advantages over soy lecithin: no soy allergen risk, non-GMO, often cold-pressed (solvent-free). Approved by EU (E322), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS). Safe.""",
    'sodium lactate': 'Sodium lactate (E325/INS 325) is the sodium salt of lactic acid, used as a humectant, acidity regulator and preservative in meat products and baked goods. Generally safe.',
    'e325': 'E325/INS 325 is Sodium Lactate, used as an acidity regulator and humectant in meats and baked goods. Safe and naturally derived from fermentation.',
    'potassium lactate': 'Potassium lactate (E326/INS 326) is the potassium salt of lactic acid used as a preservative and acidity regulator in meat and fish products. Safe.',
    'e326': 'E326/INS 326 is Potassium Lactate, a preservative from lactic acid used in meat and fish products.',
    'calcium lactate': 'Calcium lactate (E327/INS 327) is the calcium salt of lactic acid, used as an acidity regulator, firming agent and calcium supplement in foods. Also used to keep cut fruits firm.',
    'e327': 'E327/INS 327 is Calcium Lactate, an acidity regulator and calcium supplement used in fruits, vegetables and beverages.',
    'citric acid': """Citric acid (E330/INS 330 / 2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid) — see also the primary entry under Mild Acids. The world's most widely used food acidulant: a triprotic weak organic acid naturally present in citrus fruits (lemon juice ~48 g/L; lime juice ~50 g/L) and an essential intermediate in the Krebs cycle in every living cell. Commercially produced by Aspergillus niger fermentation of sugars from molasses or glucose syrups at a scale exceeding 2 million tonnes per year. Functions in this context: (1) acidity regulator — adjusts and stabilises pH in carbonated soft drinks, fruit beverages, squashes, confectionery, jams, jellies, ice cream and canned vegetables; (2) flavouring — imparts the characteristic tart, clean sourness of citrus fruits; (3) preservative — low pH inhibits microbial growth; (4) antioxidant synergist — chelates pro-oxidant iron and copper ions, enhancing the efficacy of antioxidants such as BHA, BHT, propyl gallate and tocopherols; (5) emulsification aid — helps stabilise oil-in-water emulsions; (6) acidity control in cheese manufacture (quick-acidification method for mozzarella). Pharmaceutical/cosmetic: pH adjuster, chelating agent, mild alpha-hydroxy acid exfoliant at higher concentrations. Safety: fully metabolised through the Krebs cycle to CO₂ and water; no bioaccumulation. At very high habitual intake of acidic beverages (fizzy drinks), dental erosion is the primary concern — acid attacks tooth enamel. Absolutely safe at any food use level internally. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E330), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e330': 'E330/INS 330 is Citric Acid, the tart acid found naturally in lemons and oranges. One of the most widely used food additives globally — in beverages, sweets, jams and as a pH adjuster.',
    'potassium citrates': """Potassium citrate (E332/INS 332 / tripotassium citrate) is the fully neutralised potassium salt of citric acid — a white, crystalline, hygroscopic powder with a mildly saline taste and alkaline reaction in solution (pH ~8.5 at 1%). Produced by neutralising citric acid with potassium hydroxide or potassium carbonate. Functions in food: (1) Acidity regulator — provides alkaline buffering, adjusting and stabilising pH in beverages, confectionery, dairy products and processed foods; (2) Emulsifying salt in processed cheese — like sodium citrate, potassium citrate chelates calcium ions in casein micelles, dispersing casein and creating the smooth, meltable texture of processed cheese slices; an advantage over sodium citrate for low-sodium processed cheese formulations; (3) Sequestrant — chelates divalent metals (iron, copper), preventing metal-catalysed rancidity and browning in beverages and canned goods; (4) Stabiliser in sports and electrolyte drinks — provides potassium as an essential electrolyte; the alkaline nature may buffer lactic acid, though this is modest. Medical applications — distinct and important: potassium citrate is a widely used pharmaceutical agent for kidney stone prevention and management: it alkalinises urine, which increases the solubility of uric acid and calcium oxalate, preventing crystallisation; it directly inhibits calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate crystal formation; used in uric acid kidney stones, calcium kidney stones and renal tubular acidosis. India has a high prevalence of kidney stones. Dietary potassium from potassium citrate at food additive levels may benefit those with borderline low potassium intake. CKD caution: potassium restriction is essential in advanced kidney disease — potassium citrate should not be consumed freely by CKD patients. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E332), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e332': 'E332/INS 332 is Potassium Citrate, used as an acidity regulator and emulsifying salt in cheese and beverages. Generally safe.',
    'sodium tartrate': """Sodium tartrate (E335/INS 335 / disodium L-tartrate) is the disodium salt of L-tartaric acid, a naturally occurring organic acid found primarily in grapes and many other fruits. Produced by neutralising tartaric acid with sodium hydroxide or sodium carbonate. White, crystalline powder with a slightly salty, acidic taste. Functions in food: acidity regulator and pH buffer; stabiliser in some confectionery and bakery applications; sequestrant (chelates metal ions). Less commonly used than its relatives cream of tartar (potassium bitartrate) or sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle salt). Also used as an emulsifying salt component in processed cheese formulations (alongside sodium citrate and sodium phosphates). Metabolised: L-tartaric acid is poorly metabolised by humans — about 40% is absorbed and excreted in urine as tartaric acid; the rest is fermented by gut microbiota. At food use levels, very low intake amounts. No significant toxicological concerns. Sodium content is relevant for those monitoring sodium intake, though at typical additive concentrations the contribution is negligible. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E335) and CODEX. FSSAI permits tartrates as food additives. Generally safe.""",
    'e335': 'E335/INS 335 is Sodium Tartrate, an acidity regulator used in jellies, jams and confectionery.',
    'potassium tartrate': """Potassium tartrate (dipotassium tartrate / E336ii/INS 336ii) is the dipotassium salt of L-tartaric acid — distinct from potassium bitartrate (cream of tartar / E336i, the monobasic salt). Both are natural by-products of wine fermentation: L-tartaric acid crystallises from wine as a potassium or calcium salt on the inside of wine barrels during fermentation and ageing; these deposits (wine lees or "argol") are collected and processed to recover tartrates commercially. L-tartaric acid is found almost exclusively in grapes among common fruits. Chemical properties: dipotassium tartrate has a mildly alkaline reaction in water (pH ~7.5 for 0.1% solution); solubility increases with temperature. Functions in food: used as an acidity regulator and pH buffer — particularly in baking (regulates leavening rate by controlling the acid-base equilibrium with sodium bicarbonate); emulsifying salt; sequestrant in cheese. Key distinction from cream of tartar (potassium bitartrate E336i): potassium bitartrate is acidic and reacts with bicarbonate directly as a leavening acid (CO₂ evolution); dipotassium tartrate acts more as a buffer. Both are used in baking powder formulations, jam setting and confectionery. Metabolism: about 40% of ingested tartaric acid is absorbed and excreted renally; the remainder is fermented by gut microbiota. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. Very large doses of tartrates (grams) can have a laxative effect via osmotic action. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E336), FSSAI and CODEX. Safe and naturally sourced.""",
    'cream of tartar': """Cream of tartar (potassium hydrogen tartrate / potassium bitartrate / E336i/INS 336i / KHC₄H₄O₆) is the acidic monobasic potassium salt of L-tartaric acid, naturally produced as a crystalline deposit (wine lees / argol) on the inside of wine barrels during the fermentation and cold-stabilisation of grape must. The name "cream of tartar" originated from the cream-coloured crystalline deposits; the word "tartar" derives from Medieval Latin "tartarum" used by alchemists. Recovered from wine deposits, purified, ground to a fine powder and sold as a pure food ingredient. Chemical properties: weakly acidic (pKa1 ~3.04; the second proton of the diacid is already released into the potassium salt); mildly soluble in water; solubility increases with temperature. Leavening function: reacts with sodium bicarbonate (baking soda) in the presence of water to release carbon dioxide gas (CO₂), providing leavening — the reaction is: KHC₄H₄O₆ + NaHCO₃ → KNaC₄H₄O₆ + H₂O + CO₂. Cream of tartar is a slow-acting leavening acid (activates more at oven temperature than at room temperature), which prevents premature CO₂ loss before the cake batter sets. Egg-white stabilisation: adding cream of tartar (~0.25 tsp per egg white) before whipping increases foam stability by lowering pH, denaturing ovotransferrin at a point that creates a more stable foam structure, and allowing the foam to be beaten to higher volume without collapse. Applications: meringues, angel food cake, snickerdoodles, baking powder formulations, fudge and fondant (prevents sugar crystallisation — the acid interferes with sucrose nucleation sites), stabilising whipped cream. Also used in silver cleaning (removes tarnish). Safety: tartaric acid is metabolised by gut bacteria and partially renally excreted. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E336i), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e336': 'E336/INS 336 is Potassium Tartrate (Cream of Tartar), a natural by-product of wine fermentation. Used in baking as a leavening aid and egg-white stabiliser.',
    'rochelle salt': """Rochelle salt (sodium potassium tartrate / E337/INS 337 / potassium sodium tartrate tetrahydrate / KNaC₄H₄O₆·4H₂O) is the double salt of tartaric acid with both sodium and potassium cations, named after the city of La Rochelle, France, where it was first prepared in the 17th century by Seignette — hence also known as 'Seignette salt.' The crystals are colourless, monoclinic and highly piezoelectric (they generate electric charge under mechanical pressure) — in fact, Rochelle salt was the most widely used piezoelectric material from the 1920s–1940s in microphones and phonograph pickups before synthetic piezoelectric ceramics replaced it. Food and pharmaceutical uses: in food, used as an acidity regulator, buffering agent and sequestrant — chelates divalent metal ions (calcium, magnesium) to prevent mineral-induced side reactions in processed foods; used in baking powder formulations to modulate leavening rate; pH buffering in beverages and confectionery. In pharmaceuticals: historically the key ingredient in Fehling's solution (used to test for reducing sugars like glucose) and Seidlitz powders (an effervescent purgative of sodium potassium tartrate + citric acid). Also used in Biuret reagent for protein detection. As a laxative: at doses of 10–15 g, sodium potassium tartrate draws water into the colon osmotically and stimulates peristalsis. Metabolism: poorly metabolised by humans (tartrate is not readily broken down by mammalian enzymes); excreted mainly unchanged in urine. Dietary tartrate at food use levels is safe. No significant allergen concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E337), FSSAI and CODEX. Safe at food use concentrations.""",
    'e337': 'E337/INS 337 is Sodium Potassium Tartrate (Rochelle Salt), used as an acidity regulator in baking. Safe.',
    'sodium phosphates': """Sodium phosphates (E339/INS 339) are a group of three sodium salts of phosphoric acid: monosodium phosphate (MSP / E339i / NaH₂PO₄ — acidic, pH ~4.4 at 1%), disodium phosphate (DSP / E339ii / Na₂HPO₄ — mildly alkaline, pH ~9.0 at 1%) and trisodium phosphate (TSP / E339iii / Na₃PO₄ — strongly alkaline). Each form has distinct acidity/alkalinity and functional properties in food processing. Food functions: (1) Emulsifying salts in processed cheese — disodium phosphate and other phosphate salts interact with casein proteins, disrupting micelle structure and allowing casein molecules to re-emulsify the fat into a homogeneous, meltable processed cheese product; (2) Leavening agents — MSP is used as a leavening acid in baking powder; (3) pH and buffering control in beverages, dairy products and meat products; (4) Sequestrant — chelates calcium and magnesium ions in dairy processing; (5) Water retention in processed meats — phosphates bind to meat proteins, increasing water-holding capacity and reducing cooking loss; (6) Cleaning agent (TSP — strong alkaline cleaner, not directly consumed); (7) Starch modification in cereals. Sodium contribution: sodium phosphates contribute significant dietary sodium in proportion to their use — relevant for consumers monitoring sodium intake. Phosphate safety — kidney disease: people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) must strictly limit phosphate additive intake; CKD impairs phosphate excretion, leading to hyperphosphataemia, which accelerates cardiovascular calcification and bone disease. Inorganic phosphate from additives is absorbed at ~90% efficiency vs ~50% for organic food phosphorus. ADI: see group ADI for phosphates (70 mg/kg/day as phosphorus, JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E339), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e339': 'E339/INS 339 is Sodium Phosphates, used in processed cheese, meat products and baked goods. Excessive phosphate intake from processed foods is a concern for kidney health.',
    'potassium phosphates': """Potassium phosphates (E340/INS 340) are a group of three potassium salts of phosphoric acid: monopotassium phosphate (MKP / E340i), dipotassium phosphate (DKP / E340ii) and tripotassium phosphate (TKP / E340iii). Each has a progressively higher pH (MKP is mildly acidic; TKP is strongly alkaline) offering a range of buffering and alkalinity options. Functions: acidity regulator and pH buffer across a wide range; emulsifying salt in processed cheese and coffee creamers (disperses casein proteins and fat, similar to sodium phosphates); sequestrant (chelates calcium and magnesium); leavening agent in baking powder (MKP, along with sodium bicarbonate, releases CO₂); nutrient supplement (potassium and phosphorus). Preferred over sodium phosphates where sodium reduction is important, providing potassium instead. Used in: processed cheese, coffee creamers, soft drinks, sports beverages (electrolyte source), processed meats, bakery products and pharmaceutical preparations. Health considerations: phosphate is a biologically essential mineral — essential for bone health, energy metabolism (ATP) and cell membranes. However, excessive inorganic phosphate from food additives is absorbed at ~90% efficiency (vs ~50% for organic food phosphate) and has been associated with cardiovascular disease progression and accelerated CKD in people with pre-existing kidney disease. CKD patients should minimise phosphate additive intake. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E340), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e340': 'E340/INS 340 is Potassium Phosphates, used as emulsifiers and acidity regulators in processed foods.',
    'calcium phosphates': """Calcium phosphates (E341/INS 341) are a group of inorganic calcium salts of phosphoric acid in three forms: monocalcium phosphate (MCP / E341i / Ca(H₂PO₄)₂ — acidic, most soluble), dicalcium phosphate (DCP / E341ii / CaHPO₄ — slightly acidic, moderately soluble) and tricalcium phosphate (TCP / E341iii / Ca₃(PO₄)₂ — nearly neutral, insoluble). Food functions vary significantly by form: (1) Monocalcium phosphate (MCP) — the primary leavening acid in baking powder and self-raising flour; reacts with sodium bicarbonate in two stages (fast-acting fraction reacts at room temperature during mixing; most reacts at oven temperature), releasing CO₂ for rise; also used as an acidulant in fruit beverages; (2) Dicalcium phosphate (DCP) — used as a calcium and phosphorus supplement in food fortification; anti-caking agent in powdered foods; dough conditioner; yeast nutrient; (3) Tricalcium phosphate (TCP) — anti-caking agent in salt, powdered sugar, confectionery and dietary supplement powders; provides structural calcium in food; nutritional calcium supplement in cereals, infant formula and dairy alternatives; used as a tablet filler and calcium supplement in pharmaceuticals. Calcium bioavailability: all three forms provide dietary calcium; DCP and TCP are less soluble but well absorbed in the acidic stomach environment; DCP has a relative bioavailability of approximately 85% compared to calcium carbonate. Phosphate safety note: inorganic phosphate from food additives is absorbed more efficiently (~90%) than organic food phosphorus (~50–60%), and chronic high phosphate intake is a concern for people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) due to impaired phosphate excretion. ADI: see group ADI for phosphates. FSSAI, EU (E341), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e341': 'E341/INS 341 is Calcium Phosphates, used as leavening agents in baking and as calcium supplements. Safe and nutritionally beneficial.',
    'adipic acid': 'Adipic acid (E355/INS 355) is an organic acid used as an acidity regulator and leavening agent in powdered beverages, baking mixes and confectionery. Generally safe.',
    'e355': 'E355/INS 355 is Adipic Acid, used as an acidity regulator and leavening aid in baking mixes and powdered drink mixes.',
    'succinic acid': 'Succinic acid (E363/INS 363) is a natural organic acid found in plant and animal tissues. Used as an acidity regulator and flavouring in powdered beverages and baked goods. Safe.',
    'e363': 'E363/INS 363 is Succinic Acid, a natural acidity regulator found in many plants. Used in beverages and baked goods.',
    'edta': """EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid / ethylene diamine tetraacetic acid) is one of the most powerful and versatile synthetic chelating agents known — a hexadentate ligand that forms stable, water-soluble complexes with virtually all divalent and trivalent metal cations (Fe²⁺/³⁺, Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺, Zn²⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Pb²⁺) by coordinating through its four carboxylate groups and two amine nitrogen atoms. The food-grade form used as an additive is calcium disodium EDTA (E385/INS 385), not free acid EDTA, as this formulation limits calcium chelation from the body. In food and cosmetics, EDTA functions as a sequestrant/chelating preservative: it binds trace metals that would otherwise catalyse lipid peroxidation and enzymatic browning, extending shelf life and preserving colour in: canned legumes, canned vegetables and seafood, mayonnaise and salad dressings, pickled products, soft drinks, processed dressings and sauces. Also prevents discolouration of cut potatoes and crustaceans. In cosmetics: EDTA is used to chelate metal ions in water that would otherwise deactivate preservatives, cause product discolouration and reduce fragrance longevity. In pharmaceuticals: stabilises metal-sensitive drugs; used in IV preparations. Safety: at approved food additive levels, calcium disodium EDTA is generally safe — poorly absorbed from the GI tract (<5% absorbed). At high pharmaceutical IV doses, EDTA can cause hypocalcaemia and renal damage. ADI: 2.5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA for calcium disodium EDTA). FSSAI, EU (E385) and FDA approved at specified limits in specific food categories.""",
    'calcium disodium edta': """Calcium disodium EDTA (calcium disodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate / E385/INS 385) is the specific food-grade form of EDTA approved as a sequestrant and preservative in food. The calcium-sodium salt formulation is deliberately chosen over the free acid or disodium EDTA form because the calcium in the molecule limits the extent to which EDTA depletes calcium from the body — an important safety consideration. Chemical function: a chelating agent that forms stable, soluble complexes with divalent and trivalent trace metal cations (particularly Fe²⁺/³⁺, Cu²⁺, and Mn²⁺) that catalyse oxidative reactions in food. By sequestering these pro-oxidant metals, calcium disodium EDTA: (1) inhibits lipid peroxidation (prevents rancidity); (2) prevents discolouration from metal-catalysed reactions; (3) inactivates enzymes that require metal cofactors; and (4) enhances the activity of conventional antioxidant preservatives. Used in: mayonnaise (the most common application — prevents metal-catalysed oxidation of oil), salad dressings, pickled cucumbers, canned fish and shellfish, canned legumes (prevents grey-blue discolouration from metal-phytate complexes), soft drinks with added ascorbic acid (prevents Fe-catalysed benzene formation from benzoic acid + ascorbic acid), margarines and processed dressings. Absorption and metabolism: <5% absorbed from GI tract; the small absorbed fraction is excreted unchanged in urine, taking chelated metals with it — this is why excessive EDTA intake could theoretically deplete essential minerals (calcium, zinc, iron). At approved food use concentrations, this is not a clinically significant concern. ADI: 2.5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E385), FDA and CODEX at specified limits and food categories.""",
    'e385': 'E385/INS 385 is Calcium Disodium EDTA, a chelating preservative used in mayonnaise, sauces and canned foods to maintain colour and shelf life. Safe at food use levels.',
    'rosemary extract': """Rosemary extract (E392/INS 392) is obtained from the leaves of Rosmarinus officinalis (Salvia rosmarinus) by solvent extraction or supercritical CO₂ extraction. The primary antioxidant compounds are diterpene phenols — carnosic acid and carnosol — along with rosmarinic acid and ursolic acid. Carnosic acid is highly unstable and self-oxidises to form carnosol and other antioxidant metabolites, functioning as a 'sacrificial antioxidant.' Mechanism: donates hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxy radicals; also chelates pro-oxidant metal ions (iron, copper) via phenolic hydroxyl groups; carnosic acid has one of the highest radical-scavenging capacities of any known natural food antioxidant. Used to protect edible oils, animal fats, meat products, soups, dressings and snack foods from oxidative rancidity, typically at 50–400 mg/kg. Increasingly replacing synthetic antioxidants (BHA, BHT) in clean-label formulations. EU authorised E392 in 2010. Approved by FSSAI and CODEX. ADI: not specified (considered safe at food use levels). Rosemary extract is also used in cosmetics as an antioxidant and fragrance. At high doses as a supplement it may affect platelet aggregation. The concentrated extract is not the same as culinary rosemary and should not be confused with herbal rosemary for direct consumption. Generally safe for most people at food-additive levels.""",
    'e392': 'E392/INS 392 is Rosemary Extracts, natural antioxidants used to preserve oils and fat-containing foods from rancidity.',

    # ── Emulsifiers & Stabilisers E400–E499 (additions) ─────────────────────
    'alginic acid': """Alginic acid (E400/INS 400 / algin) is a naturally occurring anionic polysaccharide found as a structural cell wall and intercellular matrix component in brown algae (Phaeophyta) — including Laminaria hyperborea, Macrocystis pyrifera (giant kelp) and Ascophyllum nodosum. It is the most abundant polysaccharide in brown seaweed (up to 40% of dry weight). Chemical structure: a linear copolymer of two uronic acid monomers — beta-D-mannuronic acid (M) and alpha-L-guluronic acid (G) — arranged in varying block patterns (MM-blocks, GG-blocks and MG-alternating blocks). The ratio and pattern of M and G blocks determines physical properties: high-G alginates (from cold-water seaweed like Laminaria hyperborea) form firm, brittle, thermostable gels; high-M alginates form softer, more elastic, freeze-thaw stable gels. In its acid form (alginic acid), it is practically insoluble in water — for food use, it is typically converted to the water-soluble sodium or calcium salt forms (E401 sodium alginate, E404 calcium alginate). However, alginic acid itself is used as a thickener in bakery products and as a swelling binder. It swells in contact with water, absorbing 200–300 times its weight in water. Health/functional benefits: alginic acid is a non-digestible soluble dietary fibre that forms viscous gels in the gut, slowing glucose absorption, reducing cholesterol reabsorption (bile acid binding) and supporting bowel regularity — documented prebiotic and cholesterol-lowering effects. Pharmaceutical use: in antireflux medications (alginic acid + sodium alginate + antacid forms a raft on top of stomach contents to block acid reflux). Completely non-toxic; not absorbed. No allergen concerns; vegan; sustainably sourced from seaweed. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E400), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'e400': 'E400/INS 400 is Alginic Acid, a natural seaweed-derived thickener and stabiliser used in ice cream and dairy products.',
    'sodium alginate': """Sodium alginate (E401/INS 401) is the sodium salt of alginic acid, a natural polysaccharide extracted from brown seaweed species (Laminaria hyperborea, Macrocystis pyrifera, Ascophyllum nodosum). Alginic acid is a linear copolymer of mannuronic acid (M) and guluronic acid (G) residues; the M:G ratio varies by seaweed species and affects gel properties — high-G alginates form firm, brittle gels; high-M alginates form softer, more elastic gels. Sodium alginate dissolves freely in water to form viscous solutions; gelation is induced by calcium ions (Ca²⁺), which crosslink adjacent guluronate sequences to form an 'egg-box' structure — this is exploited in spherification (molecular gastronomy), encapsulation technology and textile printing. Food uses: thickener and stabiliser in ice cream, salad dressings, bakery fillings, restructured fish and meat products; widely used in instant noodles in Japan (konnyaku); also used as a medical wound dressing and in pharmaceutical tablet coatings. Dietary fibre with prebiotic properties; alginates reduce cholesterol and slow glucose absorption. No significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E401), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; sustainably sourced from seaweed.""",
    'potassium alginate': """Potassium alginate (E402/INS 402) is the potassium salt of alginic acid derived from brown seaweed. Has similar physicochemical properties to sodium alginate but with potassium as the counterion. Used as a thickener, gelling agent and stabiliser in similar applications to sodium alginate — ice cream, dairy products, bakery products, salad dressings and pharmaceutical tablet coatings. In food applications requiring low sodium, potassium alginate offers a useful sodium-free alternative while maintaining functionality. Unlike sodium alginate, potassium alginate also gels in the presence of calcium ions. Provides a modest contribution of dietary potassium. A non-digestible soluble dietary fibre. No significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E402), CODEX and FSSAI. Generally safe; vegan; seaweed-derived.""",
    'ammonium alginate': """Ammonium alginate (E403/INS 403) is the ammonium salt of alginic acid extracted from brown seaweed. One of several alginate salt forms approved as food additives. While sodium alginate (E401) and potassium alginate (E402) are more commonly used, ammonium alginate offers useful properties in specific applications where neutral pH and water-binding are important. Used as a thickener, stabiliser and gelling agent in foods including ice cream, processed cheese and some bakery products. The ammonium cation is a normal body metabolite (liver converts it to urea via the urea cycle) and its contribution from food-additive concentrations is negligible. Non-digestible dietary fibre component. No significant safety concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). EU (E403) and CODEX approved. FSSAI permits alginates generally. Vegan; seaweed-derived.""",
    'e403': 'E403/INS 403 is Ammonium Alginate, a seaweed-derived thickener. Safe.',
    'calcium alginate': 'Calcium alginate (E404/INS 404) is a calcium salt of alginic acid used as a stabiliser and gel-forming agent in foods. Also used to form food capsules in molecular gastronomy.',
    'propylene glycol alginate': """Propylene glycol alginate (PGA / E405/INS 405) is produced by reacting alginic acid (extracted from brown seaweed) with propylene oxide to form partial propylene glycol esters of alginic acid. The propylene glycol ester groups interrupt the carboxylate groups on the alginate backbone, reducing calcium sensitivity (unlike sodium alginate, PGA does not gel or precipitate in the presence of calcium ions or at low pH), making PGA uniquely functional in acidic beverages, fruit drinks and salad dressings. Functions: emulsifier, thickener and stabiliser, particularly in acidic conditions where alginate itself would precipitate. Used in: salad dressings (acid-stable thickening), beer (foam stabilisation — unique ability), fruit beverages, ice cream and bakery creams. The degree of esterification varies and affects functionality. Metabolised: the propylene glycol ester bonds are hydrolysed in the gut, releasing alginic acid (non-digestible dietary fibre, excreted) and propylene glycol (metabolised to lactic acid). Propylene glycol is generally regarded as safe at food use levels. ADI: 70 mg/kg body weight/day for propylene glycol from all sources. EU (E405), CODEX and FSSAI approved. Not approved by FDA as a direct food additive at high concentrations, though GRAS for limited uses. Generally safe.""",
    'e405': 'E405/INS 405 is Propylene Glycol Alginate, a seaweed-derived emulsifier used in dressings and ice cream.',
    'pectin': 'Pectin (E440/INS 440) is a natural structural polysaccharide found in fruit cell walls, especially citrus peel and apple pomace. Widely used as a gelling agent in jams, jellies and marmalades. Also a soluble dietary fibre with benefits for gut health and cholesterol levels.',
    'e440': 'E440/INS 440 is Pectin, a natural plant fibre used as a gelling agent in jams and jellies. Derived from citrus peel or apple pomace. Safe and also a beneficial dietary fibre.',
    'microcrystalline cellulose': """Microcrystalline cellulose (MCC / E460/INS 460 / Avicel) is partially depolymerised alpha-cellulose produced by treating wood pulp (or purified cotton linter) with dilute mineral acid (typically hydrochloric acid) — this hydrolysis removes the disordered amorphous cellulose regions, leaving behind the highly ordered crystalline regions as stable, insoluble, fine white particles with very narrow particle size distribution. The resulting MCC is a white, odourless, tasteless powder with unique physical properties: compressibility (the crystalline cellulose particles bind together under pressure, making MCC an ideal tablet binder), high water absorption without dissolving, non-digestible by human amylases, free-flowing when dry but forms a stable gel suspension in water. Chemical nature: pure cellulose — a polymer of glucose with beta(1→4) linkages that human intestinal enzymes cannot break (unlike starch's alpha-linkages which amylase cleaves). Functions in food: (1) anti-caking and flow agent — in powdered foods, supplements, protein powders; (2) fat replacer — in low-fat dairy products and dressings, MCC suspensions mimic the mouthfeel of fat; (3) texture modifier — in ice cream, salad dressings, processed cheese; (4) dietary fibre — non-digestible, not absorbed; contributes to stool bulk; (5) excipient in pharmaceutical tablets — the most widely used tablet binder globally (compacted by direct compression without granulation). As dietary fibre: contributes to the daily fibre count (non-digestible) but has less prebiotic effect than soluble fibres. No significant allergen concerns. Extremely safe; EFSA, JECFA and FDA GRAS. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E460), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'methyl cellulose': """Methyl cellulose (MC / E461/INS 461 / methylcellulose) is a semi-synthetic cellulose derivative produced by treating purified wood-pulp cellulose with chloromethane to substitute methyl ether groups (–OCH₃) for hydroxyl groups. The degree of methylation (DS 1.4–2.0 per anhydroglucose unit) determines water solubility and gel properties. Most distinctive property — thermal gelation: unlike virtually all other food hydrocolloids (which dissolve in hot water and gel on cooling), methyl cellulose dissolves freely in cold water below 10°C but gels reversibly when warmed above 40–50°C. This hot-gel, cold-liquid behaviour is called thermoreversible gelation and is exploited in several food applications: (1) Fried food coatings: MC solution applied before frying gels on contact with hot oil, forming a barrier that reduces oil uptake and retains moisture — used in low-fat fried products; (2) Gluten-free and vegan meat analogues: the hot-gelation provides structural integrity to plant-based burgers and meat alternatives during cooking (the gel holds the patty together when hot), then releases to chewable texture when cooled; (3) Pharmaceutical controlled-release tablets — forms a viscous gel in the gut that controls drug release rate; (4) Bulking and thickening in low-calorie and high-fibre foods. Laxative function: methyl cellulose is not absorbed or metabolised — it adds insoluble bulk in the gut and draws water, providing a non-stimulant bulk laxative effect (marketed as Citrucel). Non-digestible; contributes to dietary fibre intake. No significant allergen concerns; vegan (plant cellulose); gluten-free. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E461), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e461': 'E461/INS 461 is Methyl Cellulose, a modified plant fibre used as a thickener. Gels when hot, liquefies when cold. Safe.',
    'hydroxypropyl cellulose': """Hydroxypropyl cellulose (HPC / E463/INS 463) is a non-ionic, water-soluble cellulose ether produced by reacting cellulose (from wood pulp or cotton) with propylene oxide. The hydroxypropyl groups make the normally insoluble cellulose freely soluble in cold water and alcohol, while retaining film-forming and thickening properties. Dissolves in cold water; precipitates (thermally gels) when heated above 40–45°C (opposite to methylcellulose's behaviour — HPC is unusual in that its thermogelling temperature is relevant in food processing). Food uses: thickener and stabiliser in emulsions, foams and gels; film-former for coating confectionery and supplements; stabiliser in low-fat dressings. Widely used in pharmaceuticals: tablet binder, film coat and controlled-release matrix former. The degree of hydroxypropyl substitution is regulated. Non-digestible — not absorbed; acts as dietary fibre. Completely inert in the gastrointestinal tract. No significant allergen or toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E463), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. FSSAI permits cellulose derivatives. Safe for all population groups.""",
    'e463': 'E463/INS 463 is Hydroxypropyl Cellulose, a modified fibre used as a thickener in foods and as a tablet coating.',
    'hydroxypropyl methylcellulose': """Hydroxypropyl methylcellulose (HPMC / hypromellose / E464/INS 464) is a semi-synthetic cellulose ether produced by first methylating wood-pulp cellulose (with chloromethane, as for methylcellulose) and then further reacting with propylene oxide to introduce hydroxypropyl ether groups. The two chemical modifications — methyl and hydroxypropyl — work synergistically: methyl groups provide thermoreversible gelling (hot gel, cold liquid) while hydroxypropyl groups improve cold-water solubility and modify the gelation temperature. Key property: HPMC dissolves in cold water and gels reversibly on heating — the same thermoreversible behaviour as pure methylcellulose (E461) but with better cold solubility and film-forming properties. Functions in food: (1) gluten-free baking — HPMC's hot-gel behaviour replicates the structural role of gluten: it forms a viscous, elastic network in gluten-free doughs that traps CO₂ bubbles during fermentation and baking, dramatically improving loaf volume, crumb structure and texture in gluten-free bread, compared to doughs without any hydrocolloid; (2) thickener and stabiliser in sauces, dressings and beverages; (3) fat replacer — the gelled network provides fat-like mouthfeel in reduced-fat products; (4) emulsifier and coating. Pharmaceutical uses: tablet film coating (vegetarian/vegan capsules as a gelatine substitute — widely used in Indian Ayurvedic supplements), ophthalmic lubricant (eye drops), controlled-release matrix former. HPMC capsules dissolve in the stomach independent of pH — suitable for most oral supplements. Non-digestible; not absorbed; acts as dietary fibre. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E464), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups including vegetarians and vegans.""",
    'hpmc': """HPMC (hydroxypropyl methylcellulose / E464/INS 464) is a semi-synthetic cellulose ether with two types of chemical modification: methyl groups (from dimethyl sulfate or methyl chloride treatment) and hydroxypropyl groups (from propylene oxide), producing a versatile, water-soluble polymer with unique properties. Unlike plain methylcellulose (E461), HPMC has improved cold-water solubility and better film-forming properties. Distinctive feature: thermally gels when heated and re-dissolves when cooled (opposite of most gels) — this thermoreversible behaviour is exploited in products that need to hold structure during hot processing but flow when cool. Used in: gluten-free bread and baked goods (HPMC mimics gluten by forming a viscous network that traps CO₂ bubbles during fermentation and baking, dramatically improving loaf volume in gluten-free formulations); sauces and dressings (thickener); pharmaceutical tablet coatings and capsule shells (vegetarian/vegan capsules as a gelatine substitute); eye drops and ophthalmic lubricants. HPMC capsules are widely used in India for Ayurvedic and herbal supplements — they are vegetarian and dissolve at appropriate gut pH. Non-digestible; not absorbed; acts as dietary fibre. No significant allergen concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E464), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups including vegetarians and vegans.""",
    'e464': 'E464/INS 464 is Hydroxypropyl Methylcellulose (HPMC), a modified cellulose thickener and emulsifier used in gluten-free baked goods and pharmaceutical coatings.',
    'mono and diglycerides': """Mono- and Diglycerides of Fatty Acids (E471/INS 471) — see the main 'mono and diglycerides' entry for full chemical description, mechanism and safety profile. These partial glycerol esters are the world's most used food emulsifiers: dough conditioners in bread (slowing staling via amylose-lipid complex formation), ice cream stabilisers (preventing ice crystal growth), margarine and spread emulsifiers, and texture agents in baked goods and chocolate. Safety note: sourced from vegetable or animal fats; may contain trans fatty acids if derived from partially hydrogenated oils (check with manufacturer for source specifics); trace glycidol fatty acid esters (GFAEs) possible from palm oil processing. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E471), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e471': 'E471/INS 471 is Mono- and Diglycerides of Fatty Acids, the world\'s most common food emulsifier. Used in bread, ice cream, margarine and chocolate. Generally safe.',
    'acetylated distarch phosphate': """Acetylated distarch phosphate (E1414/INS 1414) is a dual-chemically modified food starch produced by treating native starch (corn, waxy corn, potato or tapioca) with both phosphorus oxychloride or sodium trimetaphosphate (crosslinking agent) and acetic anhydride (acetylating agent). The two modifications deliver complementary functional improvements: (1) phosphate crosslinking — introduces phosphate diester bridges between adjacent starch chains, greatly stabilising the granule structure against high temperatures, acidic conditions (low pH) and mechanical shear — preventing the characteristic 'thinning' of starch pastes under these stresses; (2) acetylation — introduces acetyl groups on starch hydroxyl groups, which prevents starch chain reassociation (retrogradation) after gelatinisation — important for maintaining smooth texture during refrigeration and freeze-thaw cycles. Together, E1414 provides: heat and acid stability (retorting, acidic sauces); shear stability (industrial pumping, homogenisation); freeze-thaw stability (frozen food applications); smooth, stable pastes without syneresis (water separation) during storage; lower gelatinisation temperature. Applications: a very widely used food starch thickener globally — used in canned soups, retorted baby foods and infant foods, fruit pie fillings, tomato sauces, acid-containing condiments, refrigerated dairy desserts, yogurts, frozen gravies and sauces, salad dressings and instant noodle sauce sachets. Used at 1–5% w/w in finished products. Metabolised: amylases hydrolyse E1414 to glucose (the acetyl and phosphate groups are removed during hydrolysis), providing the same caloric contribution as regular starch. The chemical residues are at trace levels well within regulatory limits and are metabolically safe. EFSA reviewed and set ADI 'not specified' confirming extensive safety data. No allergen concerns unless derived from wheat (which would require allergen labelling). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1414), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e1414': 'E1414/INS 1414 is Acetylated Distarch Phosphate, a modified starch thickener used in soups and sauces. Safe.',
    'hydroxypropyl starch': """Hydroxypropyl starch (E1440/INS 1440) is produced by reacting native starch (corn, waxy corn, potato or tapioca) with propylene oxide, introducing hydroxypropyl ether substituents (–OCH₂CHOHCH₃) onto starch hydroxyl groups. The bulky, hydrophilic substituents physically prevent starch chains from re-associating after gelatinisation (retrogradation), providing: (1) exceptional freeze-thaw stability — the gold standard among modified starches for frozen meal sauces that must not separate on reheating; (2) reduced gelatinisation temperature; (3) clear, stable, non-gelling pastes even at refrigeration temperatures; (4) improved stability under moderate acidity. Used in: frozen soups and sauces, dairy desserts, fruit fillings, salad dressings, baby foods and infant formula. Propylene oxide (the reagent) is a carcinogen in its pure form, but is fully reacted and removed during processing — food-grade hydroxypropyl starch contains residual propylene oxide at or below detection limits (<1 ppm). Degree of substitution is regulated (maximum 0.2 per CODEX). Completely hydrolysed in the small intestine to glucose. ADI: not specified (JECFA, EFSA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1440), FDA (GRAS for certain applications) and CODEX.""",
    'e1440': 'E1440/INS 1440 is Hydroxypropyl Starch, a modified food starch thickener. Safe.',
    'hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate': """Hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate (E1442/INS 1442) is a dual-chemically modified food starch that combines two distinct chemical treatments: (1) phosphate crosslinking (same as distarch phosphate, E1412) — phosphate diester bridges between starch chains prevent breakdown under heat, shear and acidic processing; and (2) hydroxypropylation (same as hydroxypropyl starch, E1440) — hydroxypropyl ether groups on starch chains physically prevent retrogradation (starch chain re-association and gel syneresis). The combination of these two modifications creates the most functionally versatile and stable modified starch available: (a) extraordinary freeze-thaw stability — the gold standard for frozen food applications; sauces and gravies in frozen meals reconstitute perfectly upon thawing and reheating, without the water separation (syneresis), graininess or textural breakdown that would affect less-modified starches; (b) superior heat and acid stability from crosslinking; (c) stable clear gels without retrogradation during refrigeration; (d) excellent shear stability in continuous pumping, mixing and filling operations. Used extensively in: frozen soups and ready meals (critical thickener), ketchup, tomato sauce, mayonnaise, fruit pie fillings, baby food (where stability through sterilisation and refrigeration is critical), fruit yogurts, chocolate puddings and instant noodle sauce sachets. In India: ubiquitous in commercial ketchup brands, ready-to-eat curry sauces and frozen food products. Degree of hydroxypropyl substitution and phosphate crosslinking density are both regulated — CODEX limits hydroxypropyl substitution to DS 0.2 maximum and phosphate crosslink density to 0.014% residual phosphorus. In the body: like all modified food starches, E1442 is hydrolysed by salivary and pancreatic amylases to glucose and short oligosaccharides — essentially fully digestible (the chemical modifications do not prevent enzymatic hydrolysis significantly at food use substitution levels). Not a dietary fibre. No significant allergen concerns (check source starch for wheat/gluten). No residual propylene oxide (the hydroxypropylation reagent) at food-grade production standards. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1442), FDA (GRAS for waxy corn and other specified sources) and CODEX. Generally regarded as very safe.""",
    'starch sodium octenyl succinate': """Starch sodium octenyl succinate (E1450/INS 1450 / OSA starch / starch octenyl succinate) is produced by reacting native starch (typically waxy corn starch or tapioca starch) with octenyl succinic anhydride (OSA), introducing hydrophobic octenyl succinate ester groups onto the starch chains. This is the defining modification that makes OSA starch fundamentally different from all other food starches: the native starch is hydrophilic (water-loving), but the bulky, long-chain octenyl succinate groups introduce lipophilic (oil-loving) character. The resulting starch is amphiphilic — it has both hydrophilic (starch backbone) and hydrophobic (octenyl succinate chains) sections — essentially a natural polymer emulsifier. Primary functions: (1) emulsification — OSA starch adsorbs at oil-water interfaces just like lecithin and protein emulsifiers, forming a physical barrier around oil droplets and stabilising oil-in-water emulsions; used to create stable, cloudy beverage emulsions (cloud beverages, cloudy apple juice, orange-flavoured drinks); (2) flavour oil encapsulation — the most commercially important application; OSA starch is the primary wall material for spray-dried flavour encapsulates; the hydrophobic sections trap the flavour oil inside granules while the starch provides water-solubility; the dried granules reconstitute in water, providing controlled flavour release; used in instant noodle flavour sachets, beverage powders, bakery mixes, confectionery; (3) emulsification in salad dressings and mayonnaise; (4) thickening and stabilisation with emulsification in complex food systems. Degree of substitution (DS) is regulated: CODEX/EU/FSSAI limit the octenyl succinate content to 3% (maximum DS 0.12). At food use levels, OSA starch is digested and hydrolysed to glucose and octenyl succinic acid — both safe metabolites. Octenyl succinic acid is a short-chain dicarboxylic acid that is metabolised normally. No significant allergen concerns (verify source starch species for gluten). No significant toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1450), FDA (21 CFR 172.892, limited to waxy corn) and CODEX. Vegan; increasingly the preferred encapsulant and emulsifier in clean-label formulations as it is starch-derived and more natural-seeming than synthetic emulsifiers.""",
    'e1450': 'E1450/INS 1450 is Starch Sodium Octenyl Succinate, an emulsifying modified starch used in beverages and dressings.',

    # ── Acidity Regulators & Anti-caking E500–E599 (additions) ──────────────
    'ammonium bicarbonate': """Ammonium bicarbonate (NH₄HCO₃ / E503ii/INS 503ii / hartshorn salt / baker's ammonia) is an inorganic leavening agent with a history extending to the 18th century — it was originally derived by dry distillation of deer antlers (hence 'hartshorn'). Modern production by passing carbon dioxide and ammonia gas through water. Physical: white crystalline solid with a characteristic strong ammonia odour; highly soluble in water; decomposes easily at or above 36°C. Leavening mechanism: unlike sodium bicarbonate (which requires an acid to produce full CO₂ release), ammonium bicarbonate decomposes completely and without requiring any co-acid when subjected to oven heat: NH₄HCO₃ → NH₃ + H₂O + CO₂. All three decomposition products are gases that volatilise from the baked product — crucially, no solid residue remains, leaving the finished product neutral and clean-flavoured. This is a key advantage over sodium bicarbonate (which, without sufficient acid, leaves an alkaline, slightly soapy sodium carbonate residue). Applications: thin, crisp, flat baked products are the ideal use — digestive biscuits, ginger snaps, crackers, some Indian snacks (thin mathri, certain murukkus) and traditional European biscuits (German Lebkuchen, Scandinavian gingerbread, Speculoos). Inadequate baking (insufficient heat exposure or thick product geometry) can leave residual ammonia — detectable as an off-smell. Not suitable for thick cakes where ammonia cannot fully escape. Also known as baker's ammonia. Health: ammonium bicarbonate at food additive use levels is safe for all healthy populations — the ammonia produced is at very low levels and either escapes as vapour during baking or is metabolised via the urea cycle in the liver (ammonium is a normal hepatic substrate). No mutagenicity or carcinogenicity. Not suitable for individuals with very severe urea cycle disorders. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E503), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e503': 'E503/INS 503 is Ammonium Bicarbonate, a leavening agent used in biscuits and crackers. Decomposes completely during baking — no residue. Safe.',
    'ammonium carbonate': 'Ammonium carbonate (E503) is a leavening agent in baked goods that releases CO₂ and ammonia when heated. Used in traditional European biscuits (hartshorn).',
    'potassium chloride': """Potassium chloride (KCl / E508/INS 508) — see the primary 'potassium chloride' entry above for complete chemistry, physiology, health considerations and regulatory details. KCl is an ionic mineral salt used as a sodium-reduced salt substitute, flavour enhancer and acidity regulator. Provides the essential mineral potassium (an important dietary mineral for blood pressure regulation and cardiovascular health). Used in low-sodium and 'no added salt' labelled products. Critical caution: people with chronic kidney disease (CKD), those on potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone, amiloride), ACE inhibitors or ARBs must avoid excessive potassium chloride — impaired renal potassium excretion can cause potentially fatal hyperkalaemia (elevated blood potassium causing cardiac arrhythmias). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E508), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'calcium chloride': """Calcium chloride (CaCl₂ / E509/INS 509) is a highly soluble ionic calcium salt — a white, hygroscopic solid that dissolves exothermically in water, producing free calcium (Ca²⁺) and chloride (Cl⁻) ions. Produced commercially as a by-product of the Solvay process (soda ash production) or by reaction of calcium carbonate with hydrochloric acid. Multiple food functions: (1) Firming agent — Ca²⁺ ions cross-link pectin chains in fruit and vegetable cell walls (calcium pectate bridge), maintaining texture and firmness during canning, freezing and processing; used in canned tomatoes, peppers, pickled cucumbers, olives and other vegetables to prevent softening; (2) Coagulant in tofu production — added to hot soy milk to coagulate soy proteins, producing calcium-set tofu (nigari tofu); provides substantial dietary calcium to the tofu (calcium-set tofu has higher calcium than magnesium-set tofu); (3) Cheese-making — added to pasteurised milk that has been damaged during heat treatment to restore calcium ion balance, improving rennet coagulation efficiency and cheese firmness; (4) Brewing water adjustment — raises water hardness (calcium content) to desired brewing chemistry for ales and lagers; (5) Electrolyte in sports rehydration formulas. Dietary calcium contribution: provides bioavailable calcium at food-use concentrations. Excess calcium chloride at very high industrial concentrations is bitter, but at permitted food additive levels the contribution is well tolerated. Osmotic effects at very high oral doses are relevant for supplements but not for food additive use. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E509), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'magnesium carbonate': """Magnesium carbonate (E504/INS 504) is a naturally occurring inorganic salt occurring as the mineral magnesite (MgCO₃), also produced synthetically by reacting magnesium sulfate or magnesium chloride solutions with sodium carbonate. Two food-grade forms: anhydrous magnesium carbonate (E504i) and magnesium hydrogen carbonate / magnesium bicarbonate (E504ii). A white, lightweight, highly porous powder with excellent moisture-absorbing capacity and strong anti-caking properties. Functions in food: (1) anti-caking agent — absorbs moisture in table salt, icing sugar, confectionery powders, baking powder, cocoa and dried food mixes, preventing clumping and maintaining free-flowing properties even in humid tropical climates; (2) acidity regulator — mildly alkaline, can neutralise excess acidity in some food systems; (3) colour retention agent in green vegetables in some applications; (4) dietary magnesium supplement — provides approximately 28–29% elemental magnesium by weight; bioavailability is moderate (better in acidic stomach conditions). As a dietary mineral, magnesium is essential for over 300 enzyme-catalysed reactions including ATP synthesis, DNA and protein synthesis, neuromuscular transmission, bone mineralisation and blood glucose regulation. Deficiency is common in India — associated with hypertension, type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular risk. The anti-caking function in table salt provides a small but consistent daily magnesium contribution for salt users. Athletes and gymnasts use chalk (a form of magnesium carbonate, commercially called 'gym chalk') for grip — entirely different from food use. Mild laxative effect at very high supplemental doses. No significant toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E504), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e504': 'E504/INS 504 is magnesium carbonate, an anti-caking agent in salt and powdered foods and a dietary magnesium source. See magnesium carbonate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'calcium sulfate': """Calcium sulfate (CaSO₄ / E516/INS 516 / food-grade gypsum) is an inorganic calcium salt occurring naturally as the mineral gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O, dihydrate form) or anhydrite (anhydrous CaSO₄). In food, three forms are used: dihydrate (gypsum), hemihydrate ("plaster of Paris" — CaSO₄·0.5H₂O) and anhydrous. White, slightly soluble powder (solubility ~2 g/L) — much less soluble than calcium chloride, which is relevant for applications requiring slow calcium release. Food functions: (1) Firming and coagulating agent — used to set tofu and bean curd (calcium-set tofu with calcium sulfate has a slightly firmer, more crumbly texture than magnesium chloride nigari tofu); firms canned vegetables and fruits by cross-linking pectin; (2) Dough conditioner and flour treatment — calcium strengthens gluten networks; used as a yeast nutrient in bread making (yeast requires calcium for enzyme activity); (3) Acidity regulator — mildly alkaline buffer in baked goods; (4) Calcium fortification in beverages, cereals and infant formula — calcium sulfate is a food-grade calcium source with ~23% elemental calcium, used in calcium-fortified orange juices (the standard reference calcium supplement for food fortification, with bioavailability equivalent to milk calcium); (5) Anti-caking in some products. Bioavailability of calcium from calcium sulfate: well absorbed (~25–35%); considered equivalent to dairy calcium bioavailability by FDA (used as the standard for health claims on calcium-fortified orange juice). Provides meaningful dietary calcium. No significant toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E516), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'sodium hydroxide': """Sodium hydroxide (NaOH / E524/INS 524 / lye / caustic soda) — this entry covers the same compound as the primary sodium hydroxide entry in the food processing acids section. NaOH is a strong alkaline base used in controlled food processes: curing green olives (hydrolysing bitter oleuropein), baking lye pretzels and bagels (Maillard browning via surface alkalinisation), Japanese and Chinese alkaline noodles (ramen, kansui noodles — creating chewy gluten structure and yellow colour), Dutch-process cocoa (alkalising cocoa powder), and hominy/nixtamal corn preparation. In all these uses, the sodium hydroxide reacts completely during processing and is neutralised — finished food products contain no free caustic NaOH. Food-grade NaOH is highly purified and free of heavy metals. Regulatory: FSSAI (E524), EU (E524), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Safe in finished processed foods. The undissolved raw chemical is extremely dangerous to skin, eyes and mucosa — relevant only for handling precautions, not for the finished food product.""",
    'e524': 'E524/INS 524 is Sodium Hydroxide (Lye), used to treat olives, pretzels and bagels. Caustic in pure form but safe after processing neutralisation.',
    'calcium hydroxide': """Calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)₂ / E526/INS 526 / slaked lime / chuna / pickling lime) — cross-reference to the primary calcium hydroxide entry in the food processing section. Ca(OH)₂ is a mildly alkaline inorganic base (pH ~12.4 saturated solution) with essential and culturally significant food processing roles. Primary uses: nixtamalisation of maize (the ancient Mesoamerican process that releases bound niacin, prevents pellagra, improves calcium content and creates masa for tortillas and tamales); pickling of cucumbers (calcium crosslinks pectin, maintaining crispness); firming agent in canned tomatoes and fruit preparations; paan/betel quid preparation in South Asia (chuna raises oral pH for alkaloid absorption — the combination with areca nut and tobacco is associated with oral cancer risk). Also used in sugar refining (carbonatation clarification), water pH adjustment, and food-grade lime spraying on grain surfaces. Provides dietary calcium in all applications. Unlike sodium hydroxide, calcium hydroxide is less soluble (~0.17 g/100 mL water at 20°C) and less caustic, making it somewhat more forgiving in food handling. Regulatory: FSSAI (E526), EU (E526), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Safe at approved food use concentrations.""",
    'magnesium oxide': """Magnesium oxide (MgO / E530/INS 530) is produced by calcining (thermally decomposing) magnesium carbonate or magnesium hydroxide at high temperatures (700–1000°C for reactive 'light' MgO; >1500°C for less reactive 'dead-burned' MgO). Two commercial grades relevant to food: 'light' magnesium oxide (high surface area, fine particle size, highly reactive) and 'heavy' magnesium oxide (denser, less surface area, less reactive). In food: (1) anti-caking agent — used in cocoa powder, table salt, confectionery powders, dried milk and protein powders; prevents moisture-induced clumping; the very fine particle size of light MgO provides excellent anti-caking properties at low use concentrations; (2) pH adjuster / acidity regulator — neutralises excess acidity; (3) nutrient supplement — magnesium oxide contains the highest elemental magnesium content of any common magnesium supplement form (~60.3% Mg by weight); however, its bioavailability is relatively low compared to organic forms — in vivo studies show approximately 4–11% absorption from MgO vs 12–22% from magnesium citrate. The poor solubility at physiological pH (it requires stomach acid to convert to magnesium chloride for absorption) explains the lower bioavailability. Despite lower bioavailability per gram, the high elemental content means effective doses remain practical. As an antacid: reacts with gastric hydrochloric acid to form MgCl₂ + H₂O, providing reliable acid neutralisation; used in antacid formulations (Milk of Magnesia combines magnesium hydroxide, a hydrated form). Laxative effect: high doses draw water into the colon by osmotic action, useful therapeutically but a potential side effect at excessive intake. Essential nutrient: magnesium RDA is 310–420 mg/day; deficiency in India is common due to low consumption of whole grains, nuts and leafy greens. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E530), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'e530': 'E530/INS 530 is magnesium oxide, an anti-caking agent in cocoa and salt and a high-potency dietary magnesium source. See magnesium oxide entry for full details. Safe.',
    'silicon dioxide': 'Silicon dioxide (E551/INS 551), also called silica, is a natural anti-caking agent used to prevent clumping in powdered foods, salt, spices, dried milk and seasoning mixes. Naturally present in many foods. Generally safe.',
    'silica': """Silica (silicon dioxide / E551 / INS 551 when used in food) is the same compound in two very different contexts: (1) In food: an anti-caking agent in powdered products — see silicon dioxide food entry for full details. (2) In cosmetics: amorphous silica is used extensively as a mattifying agent, oil absorber, smooth-texture filler, and thickener in pressed powders, foundations, primers, setting powders, dry shampoos, sunscreens and skincare. Cosmetic-grade silica is amorphous (non-crystalline) — this distinction is critical for safety. Crystalline silica (quartz dust from occupational exposure) is IARC Group 1 carcinogen causing silicosis and lung cancer; amorphous silica used in cosmetics/food does not share this hazard. Fumed silica (pyrogenic silica, made by flame hydrolysis of silicon tetrachloride) and precipitated silica are the two main cosmetic forms — both amorphous. In skincare: absorbs sebum and provides a silky powdery texture; used in mattifying formulas. Some cosmetic silica preparations contain very fine particles approaching the nano range — EFSA (2018, 2021) flagged food-use nanoparticle silica as a concern, but cosmetic use via skin (poor penetration through intact stratum corneum) is considered low risk. Inhaled silica particles from dry cosmetics (setting powders, dry shampoos) applied as sprays could theoretically reach airways — most major brands use particle sizes too large for deep lung penetration. Generally considered safe in cosmetics at standard concentrations. No known sensitisation or allergen risk. Suitable for vegan and vegetarian products.""",
    'e551': 'E551/INS 551 is Silicon Dioxide (Silica), a natural anti-caking agent used in powdered foods, salt and spice blends to prevent clumping.',
    'calcium silicate': """Calcium silicate (E552/INS 552 / CaSiO₃) is an inorganic anti-caking agent produced by combining calcium oxide (lime) with silicon dioxide (silica) — a white, amorphous, highly porous powder with excellent moisture-absorbing capacity. Mechanism: the large surface area physically absorbs moisture from the surrounding air, preventing powder particles from forming bridges and clumping (caking) that would make them difficult to pour or dissolve. Used in: table salt (the most important application — prevents clumping in humid tropical climates like India), baking powder, powdered sugar, powdered food mixes, spice blends, coffee whitener and dietary supplement tablets. Also used as a food-grade release agent to prevent baked bread from sticking to baking tins and equipment. Bioavailability: calcium silicate is poorly soluble in water and is not a significant dietary calcium source (unlike calcium carbonate or calcium citrate). Any absorbed silica is excreted in urine as silicic acid. No significant systemic absorption at food use levels. No genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity, no reproductive toxicity concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E552), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. One of the safest anti-caking agents in use.""",
    'e552': 'E552/INS 552 is Calcium Silicate, an anti-caking agent used in powdered foods and salt.',
    'magnesium silicate': """Magnesium silicate (E553a/INS 553a) refers to synthetic amorphous magnesium silicate — a white powder produced by precipitation of magnesium salts in the presence of silicate ions, creating an amorphous (non-crystalline) structure distinct from food-grade talc (E553b, a crystalline Mg silicate) and from asbestos-form silicates. Used as an anti-caking agent in powdered foods including spice blends, baking powder, confectionery sugar coatings and powdered food mixes. Also used as an excipient in pharmaceutical tablet manufacturing. Functions by absorbing moisture and providing physical separation between particles. The amorphous structure means it lacks the fibre-like morphology of asbestiform minerals that makes those materials carcinogenic. Negligible bioavailability at food use levels — essentially passes through the GI tract unchanged. Not significantly absorbed; excreted in faeces. Provides some dietary magnesium but contribution at food-additive concentrations is negligible. No significant toxicological concerns for the amorphous synthetic form. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E553a), CODEX and FSSAI. Distinguished from E553b (talc) — though structurally related, their safety profiles and applications differ.""",
    'talc': 'Talc (E553b/INS 553b) is a mineral anti-caking and glazing agent. Food-grade talc is used on rice, confectionery and chewing gum. Safety concerns exist for cosmetic talc near reproductive organs; food-grade use is considered safe.',
    'e553b': 'E553b/INS 553b is Talc, a mineral used as an anti-caking agent and rice-glazing agent. Food-grade use is considered safe.',
    'sodium ferrocyanide': 'Sodium ferrocyanide (E535/INS 535) is an anti-caking agent added to table salt in tiny amounts to prevent clumping. Despite the "cyanide" in the name, the compound is very stable and the cyanide is firmly bound — safe at food additive levels.',
    'e535': 'E535/INS 535 is Sodium Ferrocyanide, an anti-caking agent used in table salt. Safe at food levels despite the misleading "cyanide" name — the cyanide is chemically bound.',

    # ── Flavour Enhancers E600–E699 (additions) ──────────────────────────────
    'glutamic acid': """Glutamic acid (E620/INS 620) — see primary glutamic acid entry for full details. The free acid form of glutamate, responsible for umami taste. The sodium-free alternative to MSG, delivering the same savoury flavour without sodium contribution. Found naturally at high levels in aged Parmesan (~1,200 mg/100g), soy sauce (~1,700 mg/100g), dried mushrooms and ripe tomatoes. Produced commercially by Corynebacterium glutamicum fermentation. As a food additive, used in savoury seasonings, soups, processed meats and snack foods where sodium reduction is desired. Safety: JECFA ADI 'not specified'. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E620) and CODEX. Well tolerated; the reported MSG sensitivity syndrome has not been confirmed in controlled double-blind trials.""",
    'e620': 'E620/INS 620 is Glutamic Acid, the free amino acid responsible for umami flavour. Naturally found in tomatoes, cheese and meat. Safe.',
    'monosodium glutamate': """Monosodium glutamate (MSG / E621/INS 621) — see the primary 'monosodium glutamate' entry above for full chemistry, discovery history, mechanism and safety data. The sodium salt of glutamic acid; used since 1908 to enhance umami flavour. Produced by fermentation of sugarcane molasses. Metabolised identically to food protein glutamate. FDA GRAS; WHO/JECFA ADI "not specified"; EFSA and FSSAI approved. "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" claims not confirmed in double-blind studies. Lower sodium than table salt — can help reduce overall dietary sodium when used for flavour enhancement.""",
    'msg': """MSG (Monosodium Glutamate / E621/INS 621) — see the primary 'msg' entry above for full details. One of the most studied food additives globally. FDA GRAS; WHO considers it safe; FSSAI permits in several food categories. Naturally occurring glutamate is metabolically identical to added MSG. Headache/flushing claims not confirmed in controlled trials. Used at 0.1–0.5% in foods. Not suitable for infant formula.""",
    'e621': 'E621/INS 621 is Monosodium Glutamate (MSG), the most widely used flavour enhancer in the world. Safe for the general population according to FDA, WHO, and EFSA. Naturally present in parmesan, mushrooms and tomatoes.',
    'disodium guanylate': """Disodium guanylate (E627/INS 627 / GMP / guanosine 5'-monophosphate disodium salt) is a nucleotide flavour enhancer — the disodium salt of guanylic acid, a purine ribonucleotide naturally abundant in dried shiitake mushrooms (~150 mg/100g), sardines (~180 mg/100g) and pork (~65 mg/100g). Commercially produced by enzymatic hydrolysis of yeast or fungi RNA, or by microbial fermentation. Mechanism: GMP binds allosterically to the TAS1R1/TAS1R3 umami receptor at a site separate from glutamate — at food concentrations it does not activate the receptor alone, but in the presence of glutamate (MSG) it amplifies receptor response 7–15 fold, enabling significant MSG dose reduction while maintaining or intensifying umami flavour. Almost always used in combination with disodium inosinate (E631) as "E635 disodium 5'-ribonucleotides." Typical use level: 0.01–0.05%. Used in: instant noodles, crisps, soups, sauces, seasonings and processed meats. Safety: FSSAI, EU (E627) and CODEX approved; ADI "not specified" (JECFA). Critical caution: GMP is metabolised to uric acid via purine catabolism — people with gout, hyperuricaemia or kidney stones (urate type) should restrict intake. Not recommended for infants and young children. Source may be animal-derived (fish) or microbial fermentation — relevant for vegetarians.""",
    'e627': 'E627/INS 627 is Disodium Guanylate (GMP), a flavour enhancer that works synergistically with MSG. Usually combined with MSG in instant foods. Derived from fish or yeast.',
    'disodium inosinate': """Disodium inosinate (E631/INS 631 / IMP / disodium 5'-inosinate / inosine-5'-monophosphate disodium salt) is the commercially used disodium salt of inosinic acid — a purine nucleotide naturally present in all living cells as an intermediate in purine biosynthesis. Naturally abundant in: pork, beef, chicken and fish (especially sardines, mackerel, tuna — 150–400 mg IMP per 100g raw fish); it is one of the main compounds responsible for the umami and meaty flavour of cooked meat and high-quality dashi (Japanese soup stock from katsuobushi/dried bonito). Commercially produced by: (1) enzymatic hydrolysis of yeast RNA followed by selective dephosphorylation and desalination; or (2) microbial fermentation using Brevibacterium ammoniagenes or Corynebacterium strains; or (3) extraction from animal-derived sources. Critical function — umami synergy with MSG: IMP binds to a separate site on the TAS1R1/TAS1R3 umami taste receptor in a positive allosteric modulation — it does not activate the receptor on its own at food concentrations, but when MSG (glutamate) is present, IMP amplifies the receptor's response to glutamate 7–15 fold at optimal concentrations. This allows manufacturers to significantly reduce MSG content while intensifying or maintaining umami intensity. Typically used at 0.01–0.05% in combination with MSG (in the optimum IMP:MSG ratio of approximately 1:10–1:20 by weight). Found in: virtually all commercially flavoured crisps (Lay's, Bingo, Uncle Chips), instant noodles (Maggi, Top Ramen, Yippee), soup powders, bouillon cubes, savoury seasonings, processed meats and ready meals in India. Often listed as E631, INS 631, or sometimes simply 'flavour enhancer.' Frequently combined with disodium guanylate (E627) — together they are often labelled as E635 (disodium 5'-ribonucleotides). Safety: IMP is metabolised via purine catabolism to uric acid — people with gout, hyperuricaemia, kidney stones (urate type) or Lesch-Nyhan syndrome should restrict intake of purine-rich foods and additives including IMP. Source concern: IMP from animal-derived sources (meat/fish) is not vegetarian; microbial fermentation-derived IMP is vegan — source is not always disclosed on labels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E631), FDA and CODEX. Restricted in infant and baby foods.""",
    'e631': 'E631/INS 631 is Disodium Inosinate (IMP), a meat/fish-derived flavour enhancer. Works with MSG to create a powerful umami boost.',
    'disodium ribonucleotides': """Disodium 5'-ribonucleotides (E635/INS 635) — see the primary 'disodium ribonucleotides' entry above for full chemistry, mechanism and safety data. A 1:1 blend of disodium inosinate (E631/IMP) and disodium guanylate (E627/GMP) used to synergistically amplify umami flavour alongside MSG. FSSAI and EU permit up to 500 mg/kg in most processed food categories. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Raises uric acid — avoid if you have gout or hyperuricaemia. Not suitable for infants and young children. May be animal-derived or fermentation-derived (source rarely disclosed).""",
    'e635': 'E635/INS 635 is Disodium 5\'-Ribonucleotides, a flavour enhancer blend of IMP and GMP. Common in instant noodles and flavoured snacks.',

    # ── Sweeteners E900–E999 (additions) ─────────────────────────────────────
    'dimethyl polysiloxane': """Dimethyl polysiloxane (PDMS / E900 / INS 900 / polydimethylsiloxane) is a linear silicone polymer — a chain of alternating silicon and oxygen atoms (the siloxane backbone) with two methyl groups on each silicon atom: [-Si(CH₃)₂-O-]ₙ. It belongs to the organosilicone family. Produced industrially by hydrolysis of dichlorodimethylsilane followed by polymerisation. Viscosity ranges from thin fluid (low molecular weight PDMS) to thick grease (high molecular weight); food-grade PDMS is typically the viscous 350–12,500 centistokes grade (INS 900a). In food: functions exclusively as an anti-foaming agent. Mechanism: PDMS is highly surface-active and spreads rapidly at air-liquid interfaces, displacing the surface-active molecules (proteins, phospholipids) that stabilise foam bubbles, causing the bubbles to coalesce and collapse. Even at very low concentrations (1–10 mg/kg), PDMS dramatically suppresses foam formation. Critical applications: (1) deep-frying oils — prevents boil-over and excessive foaming when water-containing food (potato, doughnuts, battered products) is placed in hot oil; used in commercial fryers at 1–10 ppm; (2) jam and jelly manufacture — prevents foam during cooking that would trap air bubbles in the final gel; (3) fruit juice processing — prevents foaming during extraction and clarification; (4) wine and cider production — during fermentation and bottling; (5) certain beverages and food-processing situations. Metabolism and safety: PDMS is not absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract — too large and hydrophobic to cross the gut epithelium; excreted unchanged in faeces. No metabolism occurs. Not mutagenic, not carcinogenic, not reproductive toxin. Does not accumulate in the body. In a 2019/2020 study observing cultured cells, PDMS showed some effects on cell-line behaviour at high concentrations — these findings have not been substantiated as relevant to food-additive exposure levels in real humans. JECFA ADI: not specified (acceptable at food use levels). EU maximum level for PDMS in cooking oils: 10 mg/kg. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E900), FDA (21 CFR 173.340, permitted in food processing) and CODEX. Not found in significant concentrations in the finished food — trace residues well below any health-relevant threshold.""",
    'e900': 'E900/INS 900 is Dimethylpolysiloxane (PDMS), an anti-foaming agent used in cooking oils and beverages. Safe at food use levels.',
    'acesulfame k': """Acesulfame-K (acesulfame potassium / E950/INS 950 / Ace-K / Sweet One) is a synthetic non-caloric sweetener — the potassium salt of 6-methyl-1,2,3-oxathiazine-4(3H)-one-2,2-dioxide — discovered in 1967 by Karl Clauss at Hoechst AG. Approximately 200 times sweeter than sucrose, with a clean, fast sweetness onset but a slight bitter or metallic aftertaste at higher concentrations; usually blended with other sweeteners (sucralose, aspartame) to mask this. Exceptional heat stability (stable up to 225°C), making it suitable for baking and pasteurisation unlike aspartame. Chemically inert in food — not metabolised by human enzymes; rapidly absorbed and excreted unchanged in urine. Zero calories and zero glycaemic impact. Used in: diet carbonated beverages (very commonly with sucralose), sugar-free confectionery, baked goods, tabletop sweeteners, pharmaceuticals, yogurt, ice cream and energy drinks. Safety: approved by FDA (1988), EU (E950), FSSAI, CODEX and globally. ADI: 9 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2000). Some older animal studies raised preliminary concerns about insulin response and gut microbiome effects, but comprehensive regulatory reviews have maintained the current approval. Does not cause dental caries. Suitable for PKU patients (unlike aspartame). Halal and kosher compatible.""",
    'acesulfame potassium': """Acesulfame potassium (acesulfame-K / E950/INS 950) is the same compound as acesulfame K — the potassium salt of an oxathiazinone dioxide ring structure. A heat-stable, zero-calorie, non-nutritive sweetener approximately 200 times sweeter than sucrose. Not metabolised or absorbed in significant amounts — excreted essentially unchanged in urine, contributing no calories or glycaemic response. Unlike aspartame, it is stable at high processing temperatures (up to 225°C) and over a wide pH range, so it survives baking, pasteurisation and UHT processing. Widely used in diet beverages (often paired with sucralose or aspartame for synergistic sweetness and improved flavour), sugar-free confectionery, chewing gum, tabletop sweeteners (Sunett, Sweet One), pharmaceutical syrups and flavoured dairy products. Regulatory status: FDA approved (1988, 2003 for additional uses), EU (E950), FSSAI and CODEX. ADI: 9 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA); 15 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Generally well tolerated; no substantiated evidence of carcinogenicity or reproductive toxicity at human exposure levels. Does not contribute to dental caries.""",
    'e950': 'E950/INS 950 is Acesulfame-K (Acesulfame Potassium), a zero-calorie sweetener 200× sweeter than sugar. Heat-stable; used in baked goods, diet drinks and sugar-free confectionery.',
    'e951': 'E951/INS 951 is Aspartame, a low-calorie sweetener 200× sweeter than sugar. Must be avoided by people with phenylketonuria (PKU). IARC classified it as "possibly carcinogenic" (Group 2B) in 2023 but FDA and EFSA maintain it is safe at approved intake levels.',
    'cyclamate': """A synthetic non-caloric sweetener (E952/INS 952), typically used as its sodium or calcium salt. Discovered accidentally in 1937 at the University of Illinois. Chemically it is cyclamic acid — N-cyclohexylsulfamic acid. About 30–50 times sweeter than sucrose, with a clean flavour profile; commonly blended with saccharin (10:1 ratio) to mask saccharin's bitterness and extend effective sweetness. Banned in the USA in 1969 after a study showed bladder tumours in rats fed cyclamate-saccharin mixtures; subsequent research established the tumorigenic effect was specific to male rats via a testosterone-dependent urinary crystalline mechanism not applicable to humans. Multiple petitions for US reinstatement have been filed; as of 2024 it remains banned in USA. Approved in India (FSSAI), EU (E952), Canada, Australia and over 100 countries. ADI set by JECFA/EFSA at 11 mg/kg body weight/day. About 20–40% of the population can metabolise cyclamate to cyclohexylamine — a bladder irritant in large amounts; this 'converter' trait is relevant only at very high intake. Not suitable for individuals with PKU. Zero calories; does not raise blood glucose.""",
    'sodium cyclamate': """The sodium salt of cyclamic acid (E952/INS 952), and the most widely used commercial form of cyclamate sweetener. White crystalline powder, freely soluble in water. 30–50 times sweeter than sucrose. Used in diet soft drinks, tabletop sweeteners, pharmaceuticals and bakery products, almost always blended with saccharin in a 10:1 ratio — this combination achieves sweetness 140–200 times that of sucrose with reduced bitterness. Banned in the USA (1969) following animal carcinogenicity studies, but approved in India (FSSAI), the EU (E952), Canada, Australia and most countries worldwide. JECFA ADI: 11 mg/kg body weight/day; EFSA 7 mg/kg/day. Typical intake from blended-sweetener soft drinks does not approach the ADI. A subset of people (20–40%) harbour gut bacteria that convert cyclamate to cyclohexylamine; at high exposures cyclohexylamine may irritate the bladder and affect testes in animal models, but this is not a concern at regulatory-permitted intake. Zero calories; does not raise blood glucose or insulin — useful for diabetics. People on sodium-restricted diets should be aware of sodium contribution from large amounts, though this is generally negligible at normal sweetener use levels.""",
    'e952': 'E952/INS 952 is Cyclamate (often as sodium cyclamate), a synthetic sweetener banned in USA but permitted in India and EU. Often used with saccharin in diet beverages.',
    'isomalt': """Isomalt (E953/INS 953 / palatinit) is a sugar alcohol produced industrially in a two-step process: sucrose is first enzymatically rearranged by sucrose isomerase (from Protaminobacter rubrum) to isomaltulose (palatinose — a disaccharide of glucose and fructose linked differently than in sucrose), then catalytically hydrogenated to produce the final isomalt — a mixture of approximately equal parts of alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-1,6-sorbitol (GPS) and alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-1,6-mannitol (GPM). Sweetness: approximately 45–65% that of sucrose. Caloric value: 2 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g for sucrose) — partially metabolised in the small intestine, with the remainder fermented by colonic bacteria. Applications: isomalt's low hygroscopicity (it absorbs very little moisture from the air, unlike sorbitol) makes it ideal for hard boiled sugar-free candies, panned confectionery, chocolate, medicated sweets and edible decorations — it remains clear and non-sticky at room temperature without special humidity control. Also used in sugar-free chewing gum, baked goods and breakfast cereals. Low glycaemic index — does not significantly raise blood glucose, suitable for diabetics. Dental friendly — poorly metabolised by Streptococcus mutans, the primary cavity-causing bacterium. Digestive tolerance: laxative threshold approximately 25–30 g/day in adults (lower in children and some sensitive individuals); above this, osmotic water retention in the colon causes flatulence, bloating and diarrhoea. EU mandatory labelling: 'Excessive consumption may have a laxative effect.' ADI: not specified (JECFA — considered safe). FSSAI, EU (E953), FDA and CODEX approved.""",
    'e953': 'E953/INS 953 is Isomalt, a sugar alcohol bulk sweetener used in sugar-free sweets and chocolate. Half the calories of sugar; may cause bloating in large amounts.',
    'saccharin': """Saccharin (E954/INS 954 / sodium saccharin / o-benzoic sulfimide) is the world's oldest artificial sweetener, discovered in 1879 by Constantin Fahlberg at Johns Hopkins University. Approximately 300–400 times sweeter than sucrose; a characteristic bitter or metallic aftertaste at higher concentrations is often masked by blending with cyclamate (10:1 cyclamate:saccharin ratio). Not metabolised — absorbed and excreted in urine completely unchanged; zero calories and zero glycaemic impact. Outstanding chemical stability — resists heat, acid and alkaline conditions. History of controversy: in 1977, FDA proposed a ban after high-dose saccharin caused bladder tumours in male rats. The mechanism was later established as rat-specific: a urinary protein (alpha2u-globulin) unique to male rats forms crystals with saccharin metabolites in rat urine, causing chronic bladder irritation and tumour promotion — this mechanism does not exist in humans. The ban was not enacted; USA required warning labels (removed in 2000 when saccharin was delisted from carcinogen lists). IARC: Group 3 (not classifiable as carcinogen to humans). FSSAI special requirement: labels on saccharin-containing foods in India must state "Not Recommended for Children" and "Contains Saccharin." ADI: 5 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). FDA, EU (E954), FSSAI and CODEX approved with mandatory labelling.""",
    'e954': 'E954/INS 954 is Saccharin, the oldest synthetic sweetener (discovered 1879). 300-400× sweeter than sugar; zero calories. Previously feared as a carcinogen but cleared by FDA and WHO.',
    'steviol glycosides': """Steviol glycosides (E960/INS 960) — see the primary 'steviol glycosides' entry above for full chemistry, safety and regulatory details. Natural, zero-calorie sweeteners 200–350× sweeter than sucrose, extracted from Stevia rebaudiana leaves. Key glycosides: rebaudioside A (cleanest flavour), stevioside (slight bitterness), Reb M (premium grade). Not metabolised — excreted as steviol glucuronide. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day as steviol equivalents (JECFA/EFSA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E960), FDA (GRAS for certain glycosides) and CODEX. Zero glycaemic impact; suitable for diabetics. No carcinogenicity or genotoxicity concerns in comprehensive safety studies.""",
    'stevia': """Stevia (E960/INS 960) — see the primary 'stevia' entry above for full details. A natural, plant-derived zero-calorie sweetener from Stevia rebaudiana. The purified steviol glycoside extracts are 200–350× sweeter than sugar. Not absorbed; excreted unchanged in urine. Zero blood glucose impact. FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Suitable for diabetics, keto diets and weight management. Some users perceive a slight bitterness — blending with erythritol improves the taste profile. Not the same as whole stevia leaf powder.""",
    'e960': 'E960/INS 960 is Steviol Glycosides (from stevia plant), a natural zero-calorie sweetener. 200-350× sweeter than sugar. Approved in India, EU and USA.',
    'neotame': 'Neotame (E961/INS 961) is a synthetic sweetener approximately 7,000–13,000× sweeter than sugar, requiring only tiny amounts. Unlike aspartame it is safe for PKU patients. Approved by FDA and EU.',
    'e961': 'E961/INS 961 is Neotame, an extremely potent synthetic sweetener (7,000-13,000× sweeter than sugar). Does not require PKU warning unlike aspartame. Approved by FDA.',
    'xylitol': """Xylitol (E967/INS 967 / wood sugar) is a five-carbon sugar alcohol (pentitol) found naturally in small amounts in birch wood fibre, corn cobs, plums, strawberries, cauliflower and many vegetables. Also naturally produced in the human body as an intermediate in pentose phosphate metabolism (~5–15 g/day endogenously). Commercial production: catalytic hydrogenation of xylose (a five-carbon sugar extracted from hemicellulose in birch wood chips, corn cobs or sugar cane bagasse). Sweetness approximately equal to sucrose; pleasant cooling sensation in the mouth due to negative heat of solution. Caloric value: 2.4 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g sucrose) — partially metabolised via the pentose phosphate pathway, partly fermented by colonic bacteria. Dental health — the most proven dietary benefit: xylitol is not fermented by Streptococcus mutans (the primary cariogenic bacterium); xylitol enters the bacteria via the fructose phosphotransferase system but is phosphorylated to xylitol-5-phosphate, which cannot be further metabolised, effectively creating a futile cycle that depletes bacterial energy and inhibits biofilm adhesion. Clinical evidence from multiple randomised controlled trials confirms xylitol reduces dental caries incidence and Streptococcus mutans counts. The Finnish dental research establishing xylitol's anticaries properties is the most cited evidence base. EU and international dental associations support xylitol's dental benefit at ≥5 g/day in 3–5 divided doses. Applications: chewing gum (the primary use globally), toothpaste, mouthwash, sugar-free mints and confectionery, paediatric liquid medicines and vitamins. Also used as a nasal spray ingredient for congestion relief. Critical safety warning: HIGHLY TOXIC TO DOGS — even small amounts (0.1 g/kg body weight) cause life-threatening hypoglycaemia (via massive insulin release) and liver failure in dogs and ferrets; xylitol-containing products (especially sugar-free gum, peanut butter and baked goods) must be kept out of reach of pets. No toxicity to cats or other common pets. Digestive tolerance in humans: laxative threshold approximately 30–40 g/day; higher doses cause osmotic diarrhoea. EU mandatory warning: "Excessive consumption may have a laxative effect." ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E967), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e967': 'E967/INS 967 is Xylitol, a natural sugar alcohol with dental benefits. Used in sugar-free gum, toothpaste and sweets. 40% fewer calories than sugar. Toxic to dogs.',
    'erythritol': 'Erythritol (E968/INS 968) is a sugar alcohol that occurs naturally in fermented foods. Nearly zero calories (0.2 kcal/g), 60–70% as sweet as sugar. Better tolerated than other sugar alcohols — less likely to cause digestive discomfort. Used in keto and diabetic-friendly products.',
    'e968': 'E968/INS 968 is Erythritol, a nearly zero-calorie sugar alcohol used in keto products, sugar-free gum and beverages. Better digestive tolerance than other sugar alcohols. Naturally occurring.',
    'sorbitol': """Sorbitol (E420/INS 420 / D-sorbitol / D-glucitol) is a six-carbon sugar alcohol (hexitol) found naturally in many fruits — apples (0.3–0.8%), pears (2–4%), plums, prunes, cherries, peaches and dried apricots. First isolated from mountain ash berries (Sorbus aucuparia) in 1872. Commercial production: catalytic hydrogenation of glucose (D-glucose is reduced to D-sorbitol using hydrogen gas and a nickel catalyst). Sweetness: approximately 60% that of sucrose. Caloric value: 2.6 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g sugar). Slowly absorbed from the small intestine (active transport through glucose transporter GLUT5, not SGLT1) — this slow absorption is responsible for both the low glycaemic index and the digestive concerns. Functions in food: (1) humectant — absorbs and retains moisture, preventing bakery products, confectionery and chocolates from drying out; (2) low-calorie sweetener and bulking agent in sugar-free confectionery, mints, chocolates, diet foods and pharmaceutical preparations; (3) plasticiser in food coatings and capsules; (4) cosmetic humectant — widely used in personal care products (toothpaste, mouthwash, lotions) to retain moisture. Applications: diabetic foods, sugar-free gum, hard and soft candy, chocolate coatings, bakery fillings, pharmaceutical tablet coatings and syrups, cosmetics and oral care. Osmotic laxative at high intake: sorbitol that reaches the colon draws water osmotically, causing flatulence, bloating and diarrhoea at ~25–50 g/day. The laxative effect of prunes and pears is due to their natural sorbitol content. EU mandatory label: "Excessive consumption may have a laxative effect." Fructose malabsorption patients often have concomitant sorbitol malabsorption (low FODMAP diets exclude high-sorbitol fruits). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E420), FSSAI and CODEX approved. Cosmetic use: see cosmetics category for skin-safe humectant data.""",
    'e420': 'E420/INS 420 is Sorbitol, a natural sugar alcohol from fruits. Used in sugar-free sweets and as a humectant in bakery products. Causes digestive discomfort if consumed excessively.',
    'mannitol': """Mannitol (E421/INS 421 / D-mannitol) is a six-carbon sugar alcohol (hexitol), the stereoisomer of sorbitol differing only in the configuration at C-2. Found naturally in many plants — brown seaweed (Laminaria species, up to 25% dry weight), mushrooms, olives, onions and sweet potato. Named after the manna ash tree (Fraxinus ornus), from which it was first isolated in 1806. Commercial production: either hydrogenation of mannose or fructose, or extracted from seaweed. Sweetness approximately 60% that of sucrose with a pronounced cooling sensation (highly negative heat of solution, similar to xylitol). Caloric value: 1.6 kcal/g — less than sorbitol because mannitol is poorly absorbed from the GI tract (only 25–30% absorbed in small intestine vs ~80% for sorbitol). Food uses: low-calorie sweetener and bulking agent in sugar-free confectionery, chewing gum coatings (powder coating on chewing gum sticks), chocolate and bakery; anti-caking agent in powdered foods (its low hygroscopicity makes it stable in moist environments). Pharmaceutical significance: extensively used as a diluent, tablet filler and coating agent in pharmaceutical formulations; used as an osmotic diuretic in hospitals to reduce intracranial pressure and in acute kidney failure management (IV mannitol). Mannitol does not raise blood glucose significantly — of limited concern in diabetics, though it does provide some calories. Dental friendly — not metabolised by cariogenic bacteria. Digestive tolerance: the low absorption means more reaches the colon; laxative effect can occur above 20 g/day — typically milder than sorbitol because colonic fermentation is slower. EU mandatory label warning required. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E421), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'maltitol': 'Maltitol (E965/INS 965) is a sugar alcohol produced from maltose. 75–90% as sweet as sugar with about half the calories. Widely used in sugar-free chocolate and confectionery. May cause digestive discomfort in large quantities.',
    'e965': 'E965/INS 965 is Maltitol, a sugar alcohol used in sugar-free chocolate and confectionery. About 75% as sweet as sugar with half the calories.',
    'lactitol': """Lactitol (E966/INS 966 / 4-O-β-D-galactopyranosyl-D-sorbitol) is a disaccharide sugar alcohol produced by catalytic hydrogenation of lactose — the reducing end of lactose (glucose) is reduced to sorbitol, while the non-reducing galactose end remains intact. Approximately 30–40% as sweet as sucrose — very low sweetness makes it primarily a bulk sweetener rather than an intense sweetener. Caloric value: approximately 2 kcal/g. Physical properties: white, crystalline, freely water-soluble, pleasant mildly sweet taste with no off-notes; similar bulk and mouthfeel to sugar, making it an excellent sugar replacer in formulations. Food applications: low-calorie chocolate and confectionery, sugar-free baked goods (similar water-binding properties to sucrose, important for biscuit and cake texture), medicated chewing gum, chewing gum coatings, dietary fibre supplements and prebiotic formulations. Medical/pharmaceutical use: lactitol monohydrate is an approved laxative treatment for chronic constipation — it reaches the colon largely unabsorbed, where Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus species ferment it to short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate), increasing osmotic pressure and stimulating peristalsis. Dose as laxative: 10–20 g/day. Prebiotic effect: lactitol selectively stimulates bifidobacterial growth in the colon more than other sugar alcohols — a genuine functional prebiotic benefit. This bifidogenic effect has been documented in clinical studies. Critical note: lactitol is derived from lactose (milk sugar) and theoretically retains the galactose moiety. People with severe lactose intolerance or galactosaemia should be cautious, though the galactose is not released during normal metabolism — it is fermented intact by gut bacteria. Not suitable for patients with galactosaemia. Digestive tolerance: at confectionery use levels (5–15 g), most adults tolerate it well; higher doses may cause flatulence and soft stools. EU label warning required. ADI: not specified (JECFA). EU (E966), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e966': 'E966/INS 966 is Lactitol, a sugar alcohol from lactose used in sugar-free sweets and as a prebiotic. Not suitable for lactose intolerant individuals.',

    # ── Common food ingredients (additions) ──────────────────────────────────
    'maltodextrin': """Maltodextrin — see the primary maltodextrin entry for full details. A refined carbohydrate powder from partial starch hydrolysis (corn, wheat or potato; DE 3–20), used as a bulking agent, carrier, texture modifier and spray-drying aid across a wide range of processed foods. Despite its starchy appearance, maltodextrin has a very high glycaemic index (GI ~85–110) — higher than table sugar — and raises blood glucose rapidly. Found in protein powders, meal replacements, instant beverage mixes, seasoning blends, snack coatings, infant formula, baked goods and pharmaceutical coatings. Wheat-derived maltodextrin must be labelled as containing wheat (a major allergen) in EU and Indian markets unless gluten removal is verified. People with diabetes, insulin resistance or following low-GI diets should monitor intake. Generally recognised as safe by FSSAI, FDA and CODEX at food use levels.""",
    'dextrose': """Dextrose (D-glucose / dextrose monohydrate / INS 296i) is the most fundamental monosaccharide in human metabolism — the primary energy currency of every cell in the human body and the exclusive fuel for red blood cells and the normal brain. Chemically it is D-(+)-glucose in its monohydrate crystalline form (96% glucose + 4% water). Commercial production: complete enzymatic hydrolysis of starch (primarily from corn/maize, also wheat and cassava) using alpha-amylase and glucoamylase enzymes, followed by purification and crystallisation. When complete hydrolysis is carried out, 100 DE (dextrose equivalent) is achieved — essentially pure glucose. Sweetness: approximately 70–80% that of sucrose. Glycaemic index: 100 (the international reference standard against which all other foods are measured). Applications: fermentation substrate (dextrose is the preferred sugar for yeast fermentation in bread baking, beer brewing and pharmaceutical fermentations); rapid-energy sports gels, energy drinks and rehydration solutions (oral rehydration therapy — glucose is co-transported with sodium via SGLT1 in the gut, enabling efficient water absorption); confectionery and chocolate (reduces crystallisation, lowers sweetness); cured meats (feeds fermenting bacteria, contributes to browning and flavour); pharmaceutical IV solutions (5% dextrose in water is a standard hospital IV fluid); anti-caking in salt substitutes. Metabolism: absorbed instantly via SGLT1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter) and GLUT2 in the small intestine; transported to the liver via portal vein; immediately available for glycolysis, glycogen synthesis or lipogenesis. High GI — raises blood glucose rapidly. Diabetics must monitor intake carefully. Of note: 'dextrose' on labels is simply glucose — the terms are interchangeable in food labelling contexts. ADI: not specified. Generally recognised as safe. FSSAI, FDA, EU and CODEX permit as a food ingredient.""",
    'glucose syrup': """Glucose syrup (E1405 equivalents in some uses / 'liquid glucose') is a purified, concentrated aqueous solution of glucose saccharides — obtained by partial enzymatic or acid hydrolysis of starch. The defining parameter is Dextrose Equivalent (DE): a scale from 0 (pure starch, no hydrolysis) to 100 (pure glucose). Glucose syrups used in food typically range DE 30–95, with the most common commercial grades being DE 42 and DE 63 syrups. At DE 42, approximately 20% of sugars are glucose, 14% maltose, 12% maltotriose and 54% longer oligosaccharides and dextrins — providing moderate sweetness with strong viscosity and excellent bodying properties. At DE 63 or higher, the proportion of free glucose and maltose increases, sweetness increases, and viscosity decreases. Sources: corn (maize) starch is global standard; wheat starch (common in Europe, relevant for gluten labelling); cassava and potato starch are also used. Applications: confectionery — prevents crystallisation of sucrose in hard candies, toffee, fudge and caramels (the mixed sugar chain lengths physically disrupt sucrose crystal packing); controls sweetness level; adds viscosity and chewiness to soft candy; extends shelf life. Baked goods: provides fermentable sugars for yeast, improves crust browning (Maillard reactions), retards staling by competing with starch retrogradation. Beverages: bodying agent in energy drinks. Pharmaceuticals: IV nutrition, oral rehydration formulas. Nutritional profile: a high-energy carbohydrate with high GI (GI 70–100 depending on DE) — raises blood glucose significantly. No nutritional benefit beyond rapid calories. People managing diabetes or metabolic syndrome should limit foods containing glucose syrup. EU allergen note: glucose syrup from wheat must declare "glucose syrup from wheat" when it cannot be guaranteed to contain <20 ppm gluten — relevant for coeliac disease sufferers. FSSAI, EU and CODEX approved as a food ingredient.""",
    'high fructose corn syrup': """High fructose corn syrup (HFCS / glucose-fructose syrup / isoglucose) is produced by enzymatic isomerisation of glucose syrup — the enzyme glucose isomerase converts a portion of the glucose in corn-derived glucose syrup to fructose. Two main commercial grades: HFCS-42 (42% fructose, 55% glucose, 3% other) and HFCS-55 (55% fructose, 42% glucose, 3% other — the standard formulation for cola beverages). Developed in the 1970s as a cheaper liquid alternative to sucrose. Compared to sucrose (which is 50% glucose/50% fructose as a disaccharide): HFCS-55 is compositionally very similar to sucrose but with free (unbound) monosaccharides rather than a disaccharide requiring hydrolysis — the metabolic difference is minimal in healthy individuals. The scientific debate on HFCS: epidemiological associations between HFCS consumption (particularly through soft drink consumption) and obesity, insulin resistance, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and metabolic syndrome are well-documented. The mechanism debated is fructose-specific: unlike glucose (metabolised by all tissues via insulin-dependent pathways), fructose is almost exclusively metabolised in the liver via fructokinase — this bypasses normal glycolytic regulation, promotes de novo lipogenesis (fat synthesis) and raises liver triglycerides and LDL. WHO and nutrition bodies recommend limiting free sugars — including HFCS — to <10% of total energy intake. The controversy is not that HFCS is uniquely toxic (sucrose has identical metabolic effects once digested), but that its prevalence in ultra-processed foods drives excessive free-sugar and free-fructose consumption. In India, labelled as 'glucose-fructose syrup', 'liquid glucose' or sometimes obscured as 'fructose syrup'. FSSAI permits. Not banned. Significant public health concern at the population level of consumption in processed foods.""",
    'hfcs': 'HFCS (High Fructose Corn Syrup) is a processed corn-derived sweetener. Linked in multiple studies to metabolic disorders, obesity and fatty liver disease at high consumption levels. Rarely labelled as such in India — may appear as "liquid glucose" or "glucose-fructose syrup".',
    'corn syrup': """Corn syrup (glucose syrup from corn / maize glucose syrup) is a viscous sweetener produced by the enzymatic or acid hydrolysis of corn (maize) starch. During hydrolysis, the starch polymer (a chain of glucose units) is broken down into shorter glucose oligomers, maltose and free glucose — the degree of hydrolysis is measured as Dextrose Equivalent (DE): low DE syrups (DE 20–40) are less sweet, more viscous and have more complex carbohydrates; high DE syrups (DE 60–90) are sweeter and richer in free glucose and maltose. Distinct from High-Fructose Corn Syrup (HFCS): regular corn syrup is primarily glucose (dextrose) — unlike HFCS which has been enzymatically treated to convert a proportion of glucose to fructose. In food manufacturing: corn syrup prevents crystallisation in candy (by disrupting sucrose crystal formation — essential for smooth caramels, fudge, fondant and hard candy), provides body and mouthfeel to beverages and bakery products, extends shelf life (osmotic pressure), adds mild sweetness and acts as a humectant (retains moisture). Used in: confectionery, frostings, caramel, ice cream (prevents ice crystal formation), glucose-based sports drinks, baked goods and infant formula in some markets. Glycaemic index: corn syrup has a high GI (85–100 depending on DE) — it raises blood glucose rapidly. High-GI sweeteners contribute to postprandial glucose spikes; of concern for people managing diabetes or metabolic syndrome. Compared to HFCS (which contains fructose — associated with hepatic fat accumulation), regular corn syrup is metabolised directly through the glycolytic pathway rather than hepatic fructose metabolism, but remains a refined, high-calorie, low-nutrition sweetener. Not approved as a health ingredient; limited nutritional value beyond calories. FSSAI permits glucose syrup as a food ingredient. Labelling: may appear as 'glucose syrup', 'liquid glucose' or 'corn syrup' on Indian product labels.""",
    'invert sugar': """Invert sugar (inverted sugar syrup / trimoline / intervertase product) is a mixture of approximately equal parts glucose and fructose produced by the hydrolysis (inversion) of sucrose — the glycosidic bond between glucose and fructose is cleaved by the enzyme invertase (sucrase / beta-fructofuranosidase), by dilute acid (hydrochloric or citric acid), or by heat. Called 'invert' because the optical rotation of the solution changes from dextrorotatory (+66.5° for sucrose) to levorotatory (−19.8° for the resulting glucose-fructose mixture) — the mixture 'inverts' the plane of polarised light. Composition: approximately 50% glucose + 50% fructose as free monosaccharides (rather than the disaccharide sucrose). Sweeter than sucrose (by about 10–15%) because fructose has higher sweetness than sucrose and is now free rather than bound. Physical properties: a thick, clear syrup; highly hygroscopic (moisture-absorbing) due to the free fructose component; freezing point is lower than sucrose solution of the same concentration (cryoscopic depression). Key functional properties: (1) moisture retention — hygroscopicity keeps baked goods (cakes, muffins, soft cookies) soft and moist for longer, significantly extending shelf life; (2) crystallisation prevention — the mixture of two monosaccharides physically prevents sucrose crystal formation, essential for fudges, fondants, soft-centred chocolates and confectionery; (3) freeze-point depression — used in ice cream to prevent the formation of large ice crystals, yielding a smoother texture; (4) flavour — the Maillard reaction proceeds faster with the free reducing sugars, promoting more intense browning in baked goods. Used in: bakeries, confectionery manufacturing, soft drinks, jams and preserves, processed meats (surface browning), alcoholic beverages and as a fermentation sugar. Metabolic profile: nutritionally equivalent to sucrose after digestion (sucrose is hydrolysed to glucose and fructose in the gut by sucrase anyway) — identical caloric value (~4 kcal/g) and GI. High fructose proportion means the same NAFLD risk concerns as for HFCS apply at high consumption. Not suitable for diabetics in significant quantities. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient. Safe at food use levels.""",
    'treacle': """Treacle is the British/Commonwealth term for molasses — the thick dark syrup produced as a by-product of sugar refining. There are two commercial varieties: black treacle (equivalent to molasses or blackstrap molasses) — very dark, intensely bitter-sweet, high in minerals; and golden syrup (light treacle) — lighter, sweeter, produced by acid hydrolysis and refinement of cane sugar syrups. Black treacle composition: approximately 73% total sugars, 4.7 mg/100g iron, 205 mg calcium, 350 mg potassium — a meaningful source of these minerals. Used in: traditional British baked goods (parkin, sticky toffee pudding, treacle tart, gingerbread), pickles, some confectionery and beverages. In India, blackstrap molasses is used in some traditional preparations and as animal feed. As a flavour ingredient, black treacle adds a distinctive bitter, complex depth — commonly used in rich fruit cakes and dark bread. Iron content of blackstrap molasses/treacle is notable — historically used as an iron tonic. Non-haem iron; absorption enhanced by vitamin C co-consumption. High sugar content limits use in diabetic diets. The caramel-melanoidin compounds contribute antioxidant activity. No allergen concerns. Generally recognised as safe as a food ingredient; no regulatory restrictions. FSSAI permits as a natural food ingredient.""",
    'molasses': """Molasses (treacle in the UK) is the thick, dark viscous by-product of sugar cane (or sugar beet) refining — the syrup that remains after crystalline sucrose has been extracted by centrifugation, typically after multiple crystallisation cycles. Blackstrap molasses (from the third crystallisation) contains the highest mineral and nutrient concentration. Composition per 100g of blackstrap molasses: approximately 74 g total carbohydrate (mixture of sucrose, glucose and fructose), 4.7 mg iron (~26% of RDA — one of the richest plant-based iron sources), 242 mg calcium, 365 mg potassium, 242 mg magnesium, plus B vitamins (particularly B6) and trace minerals. The dark colour comes from melanoidin compounds (Maillard reaction products) formed during sugar processing; the distinctive bittersweet flavour from organic acids and caramelised compounds. Light and dark molasses (from earlier crystallisation stages) have higher sugar content and lower mineral density. Used in: baking (gingerbread, dark rye bread, baked beans), rum and dark spirit production, licorice flavouring, condiments, animal feed and Indian jaggery-style products. Some use as a traditional iron supplement. Nutritional note: the iron in blackstrap molasses is a mixture of haem and non-haem forms; bioavailability is moderate but the combination with vitamin C (in food combinations) improves absorption. High sugar content means moderation is warranted for diabetics.""",
    'coconut sugar': """Coconut sugar (coconut palm sugar / coconut blossom sugar / gula kelapa) is produced by tapping the sap (nira) from the flower stalks (inflorescences) of the coconut palm (Cocos nucifera), then heating the sap in open pans to evaporate most of the water, leaving a brown, granular or paste sugar. It should not be confused with palm sugar (from other palm species). Composition: predominantly sucrose (~70–80%), with smaller amounts of free glucose and fructose; trace amounts of minerals including potassium (~1028 mg/100g — a notable amount), zinc (~2.1 mg/100g), iron (~4.1 mg/100g), calcium and magnesium; small amounts of B vitamins; and a variable amount of inulin (a prebiotic fibre), typically 2–3%. The inulin content is the basis for the lower glycaemic index (GI) claims. Glycaemic index: several studies report GI approximately 35–54 compared to ~65 for white sugar. The inulin slows glucose absorption to some degree. However, the GI can vary significantly based on the processing and inulin content of the specific batch — and is substantially higher than the GI of most non-sugar sweeteners. Flavour: brown, caramel-like, slightly complex — resembles jaggery or brown sugar in flavour profile. Nutritional perspective: coconut sugar is often marketed as a 'healthy' or 'natural' alternative to white sugar, and it does contain more trace minerals and slightly lower GI than refined sucrose — but it is still approximately 78% sucrose, contributing similar calories (~380 kcal/100g) and metabolic effects. The quantities of trace minerals from typical serving sizes (1–2 tsp) are nutritionally insignificant compared to dietary sources. WHO, FSSAI and nutrition bodies classify all added sugars — including coconut sugar — as free sugars, with the same recommendation to limit intake to <10% of total energy. Not appropriate for diabetics in more than very small amounts. The environmental and sustainability profile is often cited positively (coconut palms are not destroyed for sap harvest, unlike sugar cane). FSSAI classifies as a natural food ingredient. Safe, but not a superfood.""",
    'palm sugar': """Palm sugar is produced by collecting and evaporating the sap tapped from the flower stems of various palm trees — primarily date palm (Phoenix dactylifera), toddy palm (Borassus flabellifer), coconut palm (Cocos nucifera) and the sago palm. The sap is boiled until most water evaporates, leaving a thick, viscous sugar paste or solid block. Composition: predominantly sucrose (~70–80%), with smaller amounts of glucose and fructose; trace minerals including potassium, zinc, iron and B vitamins (particularly inositol). The mineral and trace element content reflects the tree's soil environment. Glycaemic Index: palm sugar has a reported GI of approximately 35–54 — lower than white sugar (GI ~65–70), often attributed to small amounts of inulin-type fibres in some palm sap preparations, though this varies significantly by source and processing. It is NOT a safe sweetener for diabetics in large quantities despite the lower GI claim — all forms of sugar should be consumed in moderation by diabetics. Traditional South Indian and Southeast Asian significance: palmyra palm sugar (karupatti in Tamil, karakkara palm jaggery) is a traditional ingredient in Tamil Nadu cooking, used in ritual sweets, chutneys and beverages. Also used in Indonesian, Thai and Sri Lankan cooking. Indonesian palm sugar (gula jawa/gula Jawa) has a distinctive caramel-coconut flavour. No food allergen. Unrefined; retains more trace minerals than white sugar. FSSAI classifies as a natural food ingredient. Recognised as safe.""",
    'jaggery': """Jaggery (gur / गुड़) is a traditional unrefined sweetener made from the concentrated juice of sugar cane (Saccharum officinarum) or palm sap, produced by boiling fresh-pressed juice in open iron pans until it solidifies — no chemical refining, bleaching or crystallisation. Composition: predominantly sucrose (~65–85%), with glucose (~10–15%), fructose (~5%), moisture (~3–5%) and importantly trace minerals — iron (~3–4 mg/100g, among the highest of common sweeteners), calcium (~40–100 mg/100g), magnesium (~70–90 mg/100g), potassium (~1050 mg/100g), phosphorus and zinc — plus small amounts of B vitamins retained from the cane juice. The dark colour and complex flavour come from Maillard reaction products and retained molasses. Glycaemic index: approximately 84 — lower than refined white sugar (~65) but not dramatically different. It is still ~75% sucrose and raises blood glucose significantly. Health claims: commonly marketed as a healthier alternative to refined sugar based on its mineral content and lower processing. While it does provide real trace minerals that white sugar lacks entirely, typical jaggery serving sizes (5–10 g) contribute modest mineral amounts relative to dietary requirements. WHO classifies all added sugars including jaggery as free sugars — limit to <10% total energy. Suitable for all ages in moderation. FSSAI regulates jaggery as a natural food. Not appropriate for diabetics in significant quantities despite the lower GI narrative.""",
    'stevia leaf extract': """Stevia leaf extract (steviol glycosides / E960/INS 960) is a natural, zero-calorie sweetener derived from the leaves of Stevia rebaudiana Bertoni, a herbaceous perennial plant native to Paraguay and Brazil. Stevia leaves have been used by the Guaraní people of South America for centuries. The sweetening compounds — steviol glycosides — are extracted from the dried leaves using hot water or food-grade solvents, then purified by recrystallisation or column chromatography. Key steviol glycosides and their sweetness: rebaudioside A (Reb A, ~200–250× sucrose, cleaner flavour), stevioside (~100–150× sucrose, slightly more bitter/liquorice aftertaste), rebaudioside M and D (highest purity glycosides — up to 350× sucrose with the cleanest flavour profile, used in premium products), rebaudioside C, dulcoside. Steviol glycosides are not absorbed in the small intestine; they are cleaved by gut bacteria in the large intestine to steviol aglycone, which is absorbed, metabolised in the liver and excreted in urine as steviol glucuronide — no calorie contribution. Zero glycaemic impact — does not raise blood glucose or insulin levels, making it valuable for diabetics and people managing weight. The FDA (GRAS, 2008) and EFSA approved purified steviol glycosides; FSSAI permits steviol glycosides (E960) in several food and beverage categories in India. ADI: 4 mg/kg body weight/day as steviol equivalents (JECFA/EFSA). Extensive safety data: no carcinogenicity, no genotoxicity, no reproductive toxicity. Two decades of safety research supports safety for the general population. Side effects: some people report a slight bitterness or liquorice-like aftertaste, especially from lower-purity extracts or stevioside-dominant products — blending with erythritol or other sweeteners is common in commercial formulations to improve taste profile. Not the same as unprocessed stevia leaves, which are not approved as food additives (because GRAS/E960 applies to purified extracts, not whole leaf).""",
    'canola oil': 'Canola oil is a refined vegetable oil from a cultivar of rapeseed, bred to be low in erucic acid (<2%). One of the healthiest cooking oils — high in monounsaturated oleic acid (~63%), omega-3 ALA (~9%) and low in saturated fat (~7%). High smoke point (~204°C). Widely used in Indian food manufacturing. See also the primary canola oil entry for full composition and health details.',
    'sunflower oil': 'Sunflower oil is a common cooking and frying oil high in polyunsaturated omega-6 linoleic acid (~65%) and Vitamin E (one of the richest sources). Used extensively in Indian snack food manufacturing. Excessive omega-6 intake relative to omega-3 may promote an inflammatory state. High smoke point (~230°C). See the primary sunflower oil entry for full details.',
    'soybean oil': """Soybean oil is the world's most produced vegetable oil, extracted from soybeans (Glycine max). Fatty acid composition: approximately 51% linoleic acid (omega-6), 23% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 7% ALA (omega-3) and 15% saturated fats. Rich in Vitamin E (predominantly gamma-tocopherol). Very high omega-6:omega-3 ratio (~7:1) — one of the factors contributing to high omega-6 diets globally. Widely used in cooking, margarine, salad dressings and as a base for processed foods. Most commercial soybean oil is from genetically modified (GMO) soybeans — highly refined oil contains negligible soy protein and is considered safe for soy-allergic individuals, though cold-pressed soybean oil may retain traces. Partially hydrogenated soybean oil (PHSO) was historically the dominant source of trans fats in the American diet — now largely phased out following FDA's 2015 determination that PHOs are no longer GRAS. Modern soybean oil formulations avoid partial hydrogenation. High-oleic soybean oil (HOSO) variants have been developed with ~78% oleic acid for improved oxidative stability. FSSAI permits soybean oil as a standard edible vegetable oil. Soy is a major allergen — highly refined oil is generally safe for soy-allergic individuals but cold-pressed oil is not.""",
    'cottonseed oil': """Cottonseed oil is a vegetable oil extracted from the seeds of the cotton plant (Gossypium hirsutum and related species) — a by-product of cotton fibre production. Refined cottonseed oil has a mild, neutral flavour with a relatively high smoke point (~220°C). Fatty acid composition: approximately 52% linoleic acid (omega-6, polyunsaturated), 18% oleic acid (monounsaturated), 26% saturated fats (primarily palmitic acid). The high linoleic acid content contributes to the high omega-6 intake when used extensively. Safety concern — gossypol: raw cottonseed contains gossypol, a phenolic toxin that is harmful to non-ruminants (infertility, cardiac toxicity in animals at high doses). However, commercial refining processes (including bleaching and deodorisation) reduce gossypol to negligible, safe levels in refined cottonseed oil. Refined cottonseed oil is used for commercial deep-frying of snack foods (chips, namkeen) and as a component of vegetable oil blends in India. Tocopherol (Vitamin E) content of refined oil provides oxidative stability. Not a major allergen. FSSAI permits refined cottonseed oil as an edible oil. High omega-6 content and relatively high saturated fat make it nutritionally inferior to canola or olive oil.""",
    'rice bran oil': 'Rice bran oil is extracted from the outer bran layer of rice. Used extensively in India for cooking and commercial food manufacturing. Contains oryzanol (a cholesterol-lowering phytosterol ester), gamma-tocopherol and other beneficial phytonutrients. High smoke point (~232°C). See the primary rice bran oil entry for full clinical details.',
    'groundnut oil': 'Groundnut oil (peanut oil) is a cooking oil pressed from groundnuts (peanuts). Widely used for frying in India. High in monounsaturated oleic acid (~48%) and relatively heat-stable. Peanut is one of the most severe food allergens globally — must be declared on all packaging. See the primary groundnut oil entry for full allergen and compositional details.',
    'sesame oil': 'Sesame oil is extracted from sesame seeds. Used as a flavouring oil in Asian cooking and Indian cuisine. Contains sesamin and sesamolin — natural antioxidants with cholesterol-lowering properties. Light sesame oil is used for cooking; dark toasted sesame oil as a flavour condiment. Sesame is a major food allergen requiring mandatory label declaration under FSSAI. See the primary sesame oil entry for full details.',
    'mustard oil': 'Mustard oil is pressed from mustard seeds and widely used in North and East India for cooking. Contains erucic acid (~40%) — high-erucic acid mustard oil is restricted outside India; FSSAI permits traditional culinary use under a long-standing exemption. Rich in ALA omega-3 (~14%) and allyl isothiocyanate (antimicrobial flavour compound). See the primary mustard oil entry for full regulatory and safety details.',
    'vanaspati': """Vanaspati (Hindi: वनस्पति, meaning "vegetable") is a South Asian term for a partially or fully hydrogenated vegetable fat — the Indian equivalent of vegetable shortening or margarine. Traditionally produced by bubbling hydrogen gas through liquid vegetable oils (groundnut, cottonseed, palm, rice bran) in the presence of a nickel catalyst at elevated temperatures, converting liquid oils into a solid or semi-solid fat with a high melting point and long shelf life. Historically vanaspati was a staple cooking and baking fat in India due to its low cost compared to ghee. Trans fat problem: partial hydrogenation generates industrial trans fatty acids (primarily elaidic acid), which raise LDL cholesterol, lower HDL cholesterol and significantly increase cardiovascular disease risk. Vanaspati was for decades one of the major sources of industrial trans fats in the Indian diet. FSSAI regulatory action: FSSAI reduced the maximum permissible trans fat content in vanaspati (and bakery shortenings) from 10% to 5% in 2018, then to ≤2% by 1 January 2022 — aligning with WHO's REPLACE initiative. Modern vanaspati uses interesterification or full hydrogenation followed by fractionation to achieve the desired solid fat without trans fats. However, older stocks and small-scale manufacturers may still produce high-trans vanaspati. Consumers and patients with cardiovascular disease should check for "partially hydrogenated" on labels and minimise consumption. FSSAI has mandatory trans fat labelling requirements for packaged fats.""",
    'shortening': """Shortening is a term for fats used in baking that "shorten" (interrupt and weaken) the gluten strands in dough, producing a tender, crumbly, flaky texture rather than a chewy one. The term comes from this "shortening" of gluten. Traditional vegetable shortening was produced by partial hydrogenation of soybean, cottonseed or palm oil — this provided the solid, plastic consistency needed at room temperature. The partial hydrogenation process produced industrial trans fatty acids (TFAs), which are now recognised as the most harmful dietary fats for cardiovascular health. Major regulatory changes: FDA ruled in 2015 that partially hydrogenated oils (the primary source of trans fats in shortening) are no longer GRAS, with final compliance dates by 2018. FSSAI reduced India's maximum trans fat limit to ≤2% in bakery shortenings. Modern shortenings: food manufacturers now use interesterified fats (where fatty acids are redistributed between triglycerides enzymatically), fully hydrogenated vegetable oils blended with liquid oils, fractionated palm fat (palm stearin), or shea butter derivatives to achieve similar texture and functionality without trans fats. Modern vegetable shortenings are generally trans-fat-free but often high in saturated fats (particularly if palm stearin-based). Animal-derived shortening (lard, beef tallow) is naturally trans-fat-free but high in saturated fats. Functional role in baked goods: creates flakiness in pie crusts, tenderness in cake crumb, and structure in cookies and biscuits by coating flour particles and preventing extended gluten development.""",
    'margarine': """Margarine is a butter substitute produced from vegetable oils processed to achieve a butter-like solid or semi-solid consistency at room temperature. Invented by French chemist Hippolyte Mège-Mouriès in 1869 in response to a prize offered by Emperor Napoleon III for a cheap butter substitute. Traditional manufacturing: liquid vegetable oils were partially hydrogenated to create a solid fat — this process generated industrial trans fatty acids that proved highly harmful to cardiovascular health (raise LDL, lower HDL). Modern margarine: the trans fat problem has been substantially addressed in most countries — modern margarines use interesterification, full hydrogenation followed by blending, or palm/shea fractionates to achieve solid consistency without trans fats. Hard margarines (stick form) generally contain more saturated fat and potentially more trans fats than soft tub margarines. Fatty acid profile varies greatly by brand and formulation. Regulatory additions: margarine is typically fortified with Vitamins A and D (replacing vitamins naturally present in butter). In India: FSSAI mandates that margarine must have a trans fat content ≤2% and requires clear labelling of vitamin fortification. Comparison with butter: both provide similar calories (~720 kcal/100g); butter is ~65% saturated fat; modern margarine varies from 15–30% saturated fat depending on formulation. Soft, non-hydrogenated margarines made from liquid oils (with added emulsifiers and water) can have a more favourable fatty acid profile than butter. People with dairy allergy or lactose intolerance may use margarine as a dairy-free butter substitute (confirm label for dairy derivatives).""",
    'ghee': """Ghee is clarified butter — produced by simmering unsalted butter (from cow's or buffalo's milk) to evaporate water, then filtering out milk solids (casein protein and lactose), leaving nearly pure anhydrous milk fat with a characteristic golden colour and nutty aroma. One of India's oldest and most culturally significant foods, used in Vedic rituals, Ayurvedic medicine and daily cooking for over 3,000 years. Composition: approximately 62% saturated fatty acids (primarily palmitic C16:0, stearic C18:0 and myristic C14:0), 29% monounsaturated oleic acid (C18:1), 4% polyunsaturated fatty acids and 4% short-chain fatty acids — importantly butyric acid (C4:0, ~3–4%). Distinctive nutrients: butyric acid is the primary energy substrate for colonocytes (gut lining cells) and has anti-inflammatory properties (HDAC inhibitor); conjugated linoleic acid (CLA) — an anticarcinogenic and anti-obesity fatty acid produced during rumination in cattle, present at ~5 mg/g; fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K; and oxidised cholesterol is very low if ghee is made at appropriate temperatures. High smoke point (~250°C) — one of the highest of any cooking fat, making it excellent for high-temperature Indian cooking (tadka, deep frying). Lactose and casein content: the clarification process removes virtually all lactose and most casein — many lactose-intolerant individuals tolerate ghee well. However, it is not dairy-free — people with severe milk protein allergy (casein allergy) should exercise caution. Ayurvedic tradition considers ghee a sattvic, digestive-enhancing and medicinal fat. Health balance: high saturated fat content means ghee should be consumed in moderation by those with cardiovascular risk factors; however, the short-chain fatty acid and CLA profile differentiates it from highly processed fats. FSSAI specifies purity standards for commercial ghee (including free fatty acid limits).""",
    'sodium bicarbonate': """Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃ / E500ii/INS 500ii / baking soda) — see the primary 'sodium bicarbonate' entry above for full chemistry, leavening mechanism, Indian culinary applications and pharmacological uses. A white alkaline crystalline salt produced commercially via the Solvay process. The core leavening agent in baking — produces CO₂ by reacting with acids (buttermilk, curd, lemon juice, cream of tartar) or by thermal decomposition. Also used as an antacid (neutralises excess gastric acid rapidly), sports ergogenic aid (sodium bicarbonate loading before intense exercise buffers lactic acid), acidity regulator in food processing and as a mild anti-caking agent. Notable sodium contribution: 1 tsp ≈ 600 mg sodium — relevant for sodium-restricted diets. People with hypertension, heart failure or chronic kidney disease should be aware of the sodium content at high doses. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E500), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'baking soda': """Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate / NaHCO₃ / bicarbonate of soda / E500ii) is the common household name for sodium bicarbonate — an alkaline white crystalline salt that is one of the most versatile substances in both cooking and personal care. See the sodium bicarbonate entry for the full scientific food-additive description (leavening mechanism, CO₂ release chemistry, India-specific culinary uses, sodium content, ADI and regulatory status). In baking chemistry: baking soda requires an acid ingredient to function as a leavening agent — without an acid, it decomposes at high temperatures releasing CO₂ but also leaving sodium carbonate residue that imparts a soapy, bitter aftertaste. When paired with buttermilk, curd/yogurt, lemon juice, vinegar, brown sugar, honey, tamarind or cocoa powder (natural/Dutch-processed), the acid reacts immediately with baking soda: one teaspoon of baking soda is typically paired with one cup of buttermilk or 1/2 teaspoon for each 1/2 cup of plain yogurt in Indian cooking. Distinct from baking powder: baking powder contains baking soda PLUS a dry acid (cream of tartar or sodium acid pyrophosphate) and is self-contained — no additional acid in the recipe is needed. Baking soda is approximately 3–4× stronger than baking powder by weight. In Indian cuisine: used to leaven idli/dosa batter (alongside natural fermentation), naan, bhatura (gives characteristic puffiness), chakli, laddoo batters, some halwa preparations and quick breads. Also used to tenderise meat (sodium bicarbonate solution briefly coats meat to raise surface pH, denaturing proteins and improving tenderness — a technique used in Chinese velvet chicken). Non-baking uses: antacid (one teaspoon in water neutralises gastric acid rapidly; active ingredient in Eno effervescent antacid); mild abrasive in toothpastes and whitening products; natural deodoriser (absorbs acidic odour molecules); cleaning agent. Sports medicine: sodium bicarbonate 'loading' (approximately 0.3 g/kg body weight, 1–2 hours before exercise) increases blood pH, buffering lactic acid produced during intense anaerobic exercise and improving short-to-medium duration high-intensity performance (e.g. 400–1500m running); GI side effects (nausea, diarrhoea) limit practical use. Caution: excessive antacid use can lead to alkalosis; people with high blood pressure should note the substantial sodium content (see sodium bicarbonate entry). Safe for normal culinary use.""",
    'baking powder': """Baking powder is a complete, self-contained chemical leavening system — a pre-mixed combination of: (1) a base — sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃, baking soda) as the CO₂-generating component; (2) one or two acidulants — the dry acids that react with bicarbonate to release CO₂; and (3) a starch filler (usually cornstarch) that absorbs moisture, prevents premature reaction during storage and standardises the blend. The fundamental chemistry: the acid reacts with sodium bicarbonate in the presence of water to produce CO₂ gas: NaHCO₃ + H⁺ → Na⁺ + H₂O + CO₂. The CO₂ gas forms bubbles in the batter, which expand during baking, producing a light, porous structure. Standard ('single-acting') baking powder reacts entirely with the water when first mixed — the baker must work quickly. Double-acting baking powder (now standard globally and in India) uses two acids with different reaction kinetics: (a) a fast-acting acid (monocalcium phosphate / E341i) that reacts immediately when wet — producing 'first rise' during mixing; (b) a slow-acting acid (sodium acid pyrophosphate / E450i, or sodium aluminium sulfate) that reacts mainly when heated in the oven — producing 'second rise' during baking. Double-acting powder gives the baker more flexibility and more total leavening. Composition: typically 21–28% sodium bicarbonate, 56–63% acidulant(s) and 14–21% cornstarch. Aluminium-free baking powder (commonly labelled "aluminium-free" or using only cream of tartar/SAPP as the acidulant, without aluminium sulfate) is preferred by consumers concerned about aluminium intake — though JECFA considers aluminium in baking powder to be of low risk at typical use. Substitution: 1 tsp baking powder ≈ 1/4 tsp baking soda + 1/2 tsp cream of tartar. Baking powder provides sodium from the bicarbonate component — one teaspoon contains approximately 300–490 mg sodium. People restricting sodium should note this. Approved and safe; no specific ADI concern. FSSAI, FDA and internationally approved.""",
    'cream of tartar': """Cream of tartar (potassium bitartrate / potassium hydrogen tartrate / E336i/INS 336i) is the naturally occurring acidic salt deposited on the inside of wine barrels during grape juice fermentation and wine cold-stabilisation. See the primary cream of tartar entry for the complete description covering its chemistry, leavening mechanism, egg-white stabilisation and sugar crystallisation inhibition. Key uses: (1) leavening acid in baking — reacts with sodium bicarbonate (baking soda) at oven temperatures to release CO₂, providing rise in meringues, snickerdoodles, angel food cake and some quick breads; (2) egg-white stabiliser — added before whipping to lower pH and create a more stable foam for meringues, soufflés and macarons; (3) confectionery — prevents crystallisation of sugar syrups in fudge and fondant by interfering with sucrose nucleation. Naturally derived; no synthetic steps; suitable for vegan, vegetarian and gluten-free diets. FSSAI, EU (E336i), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. Safe at all food use levels.""",
    'sodium acid pyrophosphate': """Sodium acid pyrophosphate (SAPP / sodium pyrophosphate monobasic / disodium dihydrogen pyrophosphate / E450i/INS 450i / Na₂H₂P₂O₇) is an inorganic salt — the monosodium hydrogen salt of pyrophosphoric acid. Produced by partial neutralisation of phosphoric acid or by heating monosodium phosphate to form the pyrophosphate. White crystalline powder, moderately soluble in water, with an acidic aqueous solution. Functions in food: (1) Leavening agent (acidulant) — primary use; reacts with sodium bicarbonate at different rates depending on SAPP grade (the reaction rate in baking is characterised by the "reaction rate" value, e.g., SAPP-28 reacts 28% before baking, 72% during baking; SAPP-40, SAPP-22 are other grades). This controlled reaction is what gives double-acting baking powder its two-stage leavening — gas produced during mixing (shaping) and then more gas during oven heating. Used in baking powder, self-raising flour, pancake mixes, cake mixes, biscuits and doughnuts. (2) Discolouration inhibitor in potatoes — SAPP chelates iron ions in raw and cooked potato, inhibiting the Maillard-like grey discolouration that occurs in cut or cooked potatoes and in canned potato products; also used in some canned fruits and vegetables. (3) Sequestrant in processed meats — chelates metal ions that catalyse lipid oxidation, extending shelf life of canned fish and processed meats; also contributes to water binding. (4) Emulsifying salt in processed cheese — interacts with casein proteins to improve meltability and uniformity. Phosphate safety: all phosphate food additives contribute to dietary phosphate intake. Excessive dietary phosphate (from phosphate additives in processed foods, not from natural food phosphorus) is a concern specifically for people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) — impaired phosphate excretion leads to hyperphosphataemia (elevated blood phosphate), which is strongly linked to cardiovascular calcification and mortality in CKD patients. FSSAI, EU (E450) and FDA (GRAS) approve SAPP at specified limits. ADI: not separately specified — part of the group ADI for phosphoric acid and phosphates set by JECFA (70 mg/kg body weight/day as phosphorus).""",
    'e450': 'E450/INS 450 is Sodium Acid Pyrophosphate (SAPP), a leavening agent and acidity regulator used in baking powder and processed potatoes.',
    'yeast extract': """Yeast extract — see the primary 'yeast extract' entry above for full production chemistry, composition and regulatory details. A flavour-rich concentrate of yeast cell contents: high in free glutamic acid (natural umami), 5'-nucleotides (IMP, GMP), B vitamins and amino acids. Used in soups, stocks, gravies, sauces, instant noodles and savoury seasonings. Clean-label umami enhancer. Gout caution (nucleotide content). No ADI specified. FSSAI and internationally approved.""",
    'autolysed yeast': """Autolysed yeast extract is produced when yeast cells (typically Saccharomyces cerevisiae) are induced to self-digest by activation of their own endogenous proteases and nucleases, typically at 45–55°C. This autolysis process breaks down proteins to free amino acids (rich in glutamic acid) and hydrolyses RNA to 5'-nucleotides (IMP and GMP). The soluble contents are then separated from the insoluble cell wall fraction and concentrated or spray-dried. The resulting ingredient is a natural-source umami flavour enhancer chemically similar in function to MSG — rich in free glutamate and nucleotides — but derived entirely from yeast fermentation. Used in savoury seasonings, soups, noodle flavour packets, bouillon cubes and savoury snack coatings. Provides B vitamins including riboflavin, niacin and B6. People with gout or hyperuricaemia should moderate intake (purine nucleotide content raises uric acid). Generally considered a natural, clean-label ingredient. FSSAI and internationally recognised as a food flavouring ingredient.""",
    'malt extract': """Malt extract is a concentrated, viscous syrup (or dried powder) produced from malted barley (Hordeum vulgare) that has been allowed to germinate, dried (kilned) to arrest germination, then mashed in water to activate enzymatic hydrolysis of starch and proteins, followed by filtration, concentration and optionally spray-drying. The malting and mashing process produces a rich mixture of fermentable and non-fermentable sugars (primarily maltose ~60%, maltotriose, glucose and dextrins), free amino acids from protein hydrolysis (especially glutamic acid and alanine — contributing umami and savoury notes), B vitamins (thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, B6 and folate), minerals (iron, zinc, potassium, magnesium) and melanoidins (Maillard reaction products giving the characteristic dark colour and caramel-toffee flavour). Flavour profile: distinctly sweet, rich, slightly toasted, complex — depending on kilning temperature (pale malt extract: lighter, sweeter; dark malt extract: stronger, more bitter, coffee-like). Food uses: natural sweetener and flavour enhancer in bread, biscuits, breakfast cereals (Horlicks, Ovaltine, Bournvita all use malted barley), beer and ales (the primary raw material), energy drinks, malted milk, confectionery, infant cereals and instant noodles (flavour contribution). Nutritional value: a meaningful source of B vitamins and absorbable iron; the maltose provides slower glycaemic release than pure glucose. Contains gluten — not suitable for coeliac disease or wheat/gluten allergy. FSSAI permits as a natural food ingredient.""",
    'whey powder': """Whey powder is produced by spray-drying or roller-drying liquid whey — the watery by-product remaining after milk has been coagulated and the curds (protein and fat) separated during cheese or casein production. Composition: approximately 11–15% protein (predominantly fast-digesting whey proteins: beta-lactoglobulin, alpha-lactalbumin, immunoglobulins and lactoferrin), 60–75% lactose, 2–4% fat, and minerals including calcium, phosphorus, sodium, potassium and zinc. The protein content is lower than whey protein concentrate or isolate (which have lactose partially or fully removed). Uses in food manufacturing: baked goods (browning via Maillard reaction between lactose and proteins; moisture retention), confectionery and chocolate (milk chocolate replaces some dairy fat with whey powder), ice cream (body and texture), processed meats, infant formula, soups and sauces. Nutritional value: a meaningful source of complete dairy protein and calcium. Functional properties: protein contributes to water-binding; lactose contributes to browning; minerals contribute to flavour. Dairy allergen — contains both casein and whey proteins: must be declared as a dairy allergen on food labels under FSSAI, EU and FDA regulations. Lactose content: significant — lactose-intolerant individuals will likely experience GI symptoms from products with substantial whey powder content. Not suitable for vegans. Generally safe for those without dairy allergy or lactose intolerance.""",
    'whey protein concentrate': 'Whey protein concentrate (WPC) is a processed form of whey retaining some lactose and fat. Used in protein supplements, sports nutrition and processed foods. High-quality complete protein.',
    'casein': """Casein is the dominant protein in bovine milk, comprising approximately 80% of total milk protein (the remaining 20% is whey proteins). Exists in milk as spherical colloidal aggregates called casein micelles — structures held together by calcium phosphate bridges and hydrophobic interactions between protein subunits (alpha-s1, alpha-s2, beta- and kappa-casein). Unique biochemical property: casein contains phosphoserine residues that form calcium-casein-phosphate complexes — this is why milk is an efficient vehicle for calcium delivery (casein chelates and stabilises calcium in a bioavailable form). Digestion: casein forms a semi-solid gel ("clot") in the acidic stomach, slowing gastric emptying and producing a sustained, slow release of amino acids into the bloodstream over 5–7 hours — contrasted with whey protein's fast absorption (peak at ~60–90 minutes). This "slow protein" property makes casein micellar protein a popular ingredient in overnight or pre-sleep protein supplements. It is also an endogenous source of bioactive peptides including casomorphins (opioid-like activity, possible effect on gut transit and appetite), casein phosphopeptides (CPPs, enhance calcium and iron absorption) and antihypertensive peptides. Used in: cheese-making (the primary structural protein of cheese), protein supplements (micellar casein, sodium caseinate), coffee whiteners, processed cheese (via sodium caseinate), infant formula, meat products and confectionery. Dairy allergen: casein is the primary milk allergen — IgE-mediated allergy to casein is the most common type of cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA). Must be declared on food labels. Not suitable for vegans or those with dairy allergy.""",
    'sodium caseinate': """Sodium caseinate is a soluble, highly functional form of casein produced by dispersing acid-precipitated casein (isoelectric casein, precipitated from skim milk at pH 4.6) in water and neutralising with sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to approximately pH 6.7. The alkaline conditions cause the casein micelles to dissociate into individual casein molecules that disperse homogeneously in water — unlike native casein micelles, sodium caseinate forms a stable, clear to slightly opalescent solution rather than a gel. This solubility enables uses where casein micelles would not be effective. Functions in food: (1) emulsifier — amphiphilic protein structure with hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions stabilises oil-water interfaces; used in coffee whiteners (non-dairy creamers), whipped toppings and emulsified sauces; (2) protein fortification — high-quality complete dairy protein in protein supplements; (3) fat replacer — improves mouthfeel and creaminess in reduced-fat products; (4) water-binding in processed meats (restructured ham, luncheon meat); (5) film-former in coatings. Nutritional value: ~90% protein by dry weight, all essential amino acids. As a casein form, it provides sustained amino acid release similar to micellar casein. Dairy allergen: sodium caseinate is derived from milk protein and must be declared as a milk/dairy allergen on food labels — it is not dairy-free. May contain trace lactose depending on manufacturing. Not suitable for vegans, dairy-allergic individuals or those following vegan/plant-based diets. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'skimmed milk powder': """Skimmed milk powder (SMP / non-fat dry milk / NFDM) is produced by removing nearly all fat from fresh cow's milk (to produce skim milk with <0.5% fat), then evaporating most of the water in a multi-effect evaporator, followed by spray-drying the concentrated milk into a fine powder. The drying conditions (outlet temperature typically ~80°C) affect protein denaturation and powder functionality. Composition per 100g: approximately 36% protein (both casein ~80% and whey proteins ~20%), 52% lactose, <1.5% fat, and rich in minerals — calcium (~1100 mg/100g, one of the highest dietary calcium densities), phosphorus, potassium, sodium and zinc — plus B vitamins including riboflavin, B12, B6, thiamine, niacin and pantothenic acid. Vitamin A and D (fat-soluble) are largely absent since the fat has been removed. Uses in food manufacturing: baked goods (protein, Maillard browning, moisture retention), confectionery and chocolate (milk flavour and protein without fat), ice cream, processed cheese, infant formula (as the primary protein source), nutritional beverages, soups and sauces, and as a cost-effective dairy fortifier. Dairy allergen: SMP contains both casein and whey proteins and is a major dairy allergen — mandatory declaration on food labels. Contains significant lactose (~52%) — most lactose-intolerant individuals will react to products with substantial SMP content. Not suitable for vegans. Excellent and economical source of dietary calcium, protein and B vitamins when tolerated.""",
    'full cream milk powder': """Full cream milk powder (FCMP / whole milk powder) is produced by evaporating moisture from whole cow's milk — typically by spray drying (hot air atomisation) or roller drying — to a moisture content of <5%. Retains all the major components of whole milk: protein (~26%), fat (~26–28%, primarily saturated: palmitic, stearic, myristic acids plus short-chain butyric acid), carbohydrate as lactose (~38%), calcium, phosphorus and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K. Reconstitutes with water to produce a milk equivalent; shelf-stable at room temperature (6–24 months). Widely used in: confectionery (chocolate, toffee, fudge — provides milk fat for texture and creaminess), beverages (instant milk tea, flavoured milk drinks), baked goods (enriches texture and flavour), infant formula (as part of the protein-fat-carbohydrate matrix), Indian mithai (khoya substitute) and processed foods. FSSAI mandates FCMP meet specific compositional standards for milk fat and protein content. Milk allergy considerations: contains all milk proteins — casein and whey (including beta-lactoglobulin and alpha-lactalbumin) — must be declared as a dairy allergen on food labels per FSSAI and global regulations. Lactose intolerant individuals will react to reconstituted FCMP. Adults of South Asian origin have a higher prevalence of lactose intolerance (~70–80%) and may experience bloating and GI symptoms. Good dietary source of calcium (~950 mg/100g), protein and Vitamin A.""",
    'condensed milk': """Condensed milk (sweetened condensed milk / SCM) is whole milk from which approximately 60% of the water has been removed by vacuum evaporation, with substantial sucrose added (typically achieving a final sugar content of ~45–55%) before canning or packaging. The high sugar content acts as a preservative by lowering water activity to levels that inhibit microbial growth, allowing ambient storage without refrigeration for 1–2 years. Composition per 100g: approximately 55 g carbohydrate (primarily sucrose + lactose), 8 g protein, 9 g fat, and significant calcium (~285 mg). Energy dense: approximately 330 kcal/100g. The Maillard reaction between lactose and milk proteins during evaporation and storage produces the characteristic golden-caramel colour and flavour. Used in: Indian sweets (kheer, halwa, barfi, peda), Western desserts (key lime pie, banoffee pie, fudge), coffee and tea beverages, ice cream, confectionery and baked goods. Extremely high sugar content makes it unsuitable as a regular beverage for children and diabetics. Must be declared as a dairy allergen (contains both casein and whey proteins). Lactose-intolerant individuals will react to sweetened condensed milk. Significantly different from evaporated milk (unsweetened condensed milk). Widely used across India in both home cooking and commercial sweet manufacturing.""",
    'inulin': 'Inulin is a naturally occurring fructan dietary fibre found in chicory root, garlic, onion and Jerusalem artichoke. Used as a prebiotic fibre supplement and fat replacer in low-calorie foods. Feeds beneficial gut bacteria.',
    'fructooligosaccharides': """Fructooligosaccharides (FOS) are a family of short-chain carbohydrates consisting of fructose units linked by β(2→1) glycosidic bonds, usually with a terminal glucose unit — classified as inulin-type fructans. They occur naturally in many common foods including onions, garlic, leeks, asparagus, banana, wheat, barley, artichoke, chicory root and honey. Commercially, FOS are produced either by enzymatic synthesis from sucrose (using fructosyltransferase from fungi like Aspergillus niger) or by enzymatic hydrolysis of longer-chain inulin (extracted from chicory root). Scylllo-inositol FOS (kestose, nystose, fructosylnystose) have chain lengths of 2–10 fructose units. Short-chain FOS (scFOS) typically have 2–4 units. Function in food: used as prebiotic dietary fibre (FDA permits the "dietary fibre" designation); as a low-calorie bulk sweetener (FOS provides approximately 1.5–2 kcal/g vs 4 kcal/g for sugar); as a fat replacer; and as a sweetness modifier (about 30–60% as sweet as sucrose, providing a pleasant mild sweetness). Health benefits — prebiotic action: FOS pass through the stomach and small intestine undigested (human gut lacks the β-fructosidase enzyme to hydrolyse β(2→1) bonds) and are selectively fermented in the large intestine by beneficial bacteria, particularly Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus species. Fermentation produces short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate) which acidify the colon, inhibit growth of pathogens, provide energy to colonocytes and have anti-inflammatory effects. Documented benefits: improved stool regularity, increased calcium and magnesium absorption, reduced triglycerides (in some studies), immune modulation and support for a balanced gut microbiome. Blood sugar: FOS are not significantly absorbed — minimal glycaemic impact; useful in diabetic-friendly products. Side effects: at higher doses (>10–15 g/day for most people), FOS fermentation produces gas (hydrogen, methane, CO₂) leading to bloating, flatulence and abdominal discomfort. People with IBS (especially diarrhoea-predominant IBS) or FODMAP sensitivity may react at lower doses. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA as dietary fibre/food ingredient. Vegan; commonly from chicory or from sucrose synthesis.""",
    'fos': """FOS (fructooligosaccharides / fructo-oligosaccharides / oligofructose) are a family of naturally occurring and commercially produced non-digestible oligosaccharides consisting of short chains of fructose units linked by β(2→1) glycosidic bonds, typically with a terminal glucose molecule. Three principal types: (1) short-chain FOS (scFOS) — fructose chains of 2–8 units; (2) oligofructose — slightly longer chains; (3) inulin-type fructans — longer chains (10–60 units, sometimes classified separately as inulin). Natural sources: FOS occur naturally in onions (2–6 g/100g), garlic (9–16 g/100g), Jerusalem artichokes (14–19 g/100g), leeks, asparagus, chicory root, bananas, wheat and tomatoes — in significant amounts that contribute prebiotically to the diet of people eating these foods regularly. Commercial production: either by enzymatic synthesis from sucrose (using fructosyltransferases) to produce short-chain FOS, or by partial hydrolysis of inulin extracted from chicory root (Cichorium intybus) to produce oligofructose. Prebiotic mechanism: FOS are resistant to human salivary and pancreatic amylases, brush-border disaccharidases, and other human digestive enzymes — they pass through the upper GI tract intact and reach the large intestine where they are selectively fermented by colonic bacteria — particularly Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus species. This selective fermentation produces: (1) short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) — primarily butyrate (feeds colonocytes, anti-inflammatory, reduces colon cancer risk), propionate (liver metabolism, appetite regulation) and acetate; (2) reduced colonic pH (inhibits pathogenic bacteria); (3) increased stool bulk and frequency. Clinical evidence: FOS supplementation at 5–10 g/day consistently increases Bifidobacterium counts in human clinical trials, the strongest effect of any prebiotic; reduces transit time; may reduce triglycerides; emerging evidence for immune modulation. Sweetness: approximately 30–50% of sucrose sweetness; slight sweetness allows use as a partial sugar replacer in functional foods. Used in: yogurt and fermented dairy, infant formula (FOS+GOS blend mimics human milk oligosaccharides), breakfast cereals, nutrition bars, dietary supplements, functional beverages, weight management and diabetic products. Caloric contribution: approximately 1.5–2 kcal/g (from SCFA production, not full glucose absorption). Glycaemic index: very low (GI ≈ 1–2) — does not raise blood glucose significantly. Side effects at high doses: abdominal cramping, bloating, flatulence and diarrhoea — typically at >15 g/day in adults; highly dose-dependent; gradually increasing intake allows adaptation. ADI: not specified (JECFA — food ingredient). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; plant-derived.""",
    'resistant starch': """Resistant starch (RS) is a collective term for the fraction of dietary starch that is not digested by human amylases and brush-border enzymes in the small intestine — instead, it passes intact to the large intestine where it is fermented by colonic bacteria. Classified into five types: RS1 (physically inaccessible starch — trapped inside intact cell walls or seed structures; found in whole grains, seeds and legumes); RS2 (raw, native starch granules with tightly packed crystalline structure — particularly high-amylose corn starch and raw banana starch; disrupted by cooking); RS3 (retrograded starch — formed when cooked starch cools and starch chains reassociate into crystalline structures resistant to amylase; found in cooked-then-cooled potatoes, rice, pasta and bread); RS4 (chemically modified starches — cross-linked, acetylated or otherwise treated to resist enzymatic hydrolysis; used as a food ingredient labelled as "resistant starch" or in modified starch declarations); RS5 (starch-lipid complexes — amylose-lipid inclusion complexes formed during cooking with fat). Common food sources of RS: unripe (green) banana (12–15 g RS/100g — one of the richest natural sources), raw oats, cooked-then-cooled potato (3.2 g/100g vs 0.6 g hot), cooked-then-cooled rice (about doubles RS on cooling), legumes and commercial Hi-maize resistant starch supplement. Prebiotic and health benefits: RS is selectively fermented by Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus and other beneficial bacteria producing short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) — especially butyrate, the preferred fuel for colonocytes. Documented benefits: increased stool bulk and frequency; butyrate supports colon health (anti-inflammatory, protective against colon cancer); improves insulin sensitivity in type 2 diabetes; promotes satiety; bifidogenic (prebiotic) effect; improves lipid profiles in some studies. Caloric contribution: approximately 2 kcal/g (from SCFA production in the colon) vs 4 kcal/g for digestible starch — useful in reduced-calorie formulations. Used as a food ingredient in high-fibre bread, pasta, snacks and dietary supplements. FSSAI and FDA classify RS as dietary fibre.""",
    'dextrin': 'Dextrin is a partially hydrolysed starch used as a thickener, binder and coating agent in foods, pharmaceuticals and adhesives. Used in breadcrumb coatings and encapsulation of flavours.',
    'modified corn starch': """Modified corn starch (INS 1400–1442 range, source: corn/maize) is a broad category name for corn starch (extracted from maize endosperm) that has been physically, enzymatically or chemically treated to alter its functional properties beyond those of native corn starch. Native corn starch gelatinises at 62–72°C, forms opaque, moderately viscous pastes, and retrogrades (synereses, becomes grainy) on cooling or freezing. These properties limit its usefulness in modern food processing — modified starches overcome these limitations. Key modification types applied to corn starch: (1) Cross-linking (E1412 distarch phosphate, E1413 phosphated distarch phosphate, E1414 acetylated distarch phosphate, E1421 starch acetate) — introduces chemical bridges (phosphate or adipate diesters) between starch chains, stabilising the granule against heat, acid and mechanical shear; used in acidic fruit fillings, retorted canned products, baby foods and ketchup; (2) Stabilisation/substitution (E1420 acetylated starch, E1440 hydroxypropyl starch) — introduces substituent groups that prevent retrogradation, giving freeze-thaw stable sauces; (3) Dual modification (E1442 hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate) — combines both cross-linking and substitution for maximum functional versatility. From waxy corn (waxy maize) starch — which lacks amylose entirely — modified waxy corn starch has unique clear gelling properties. Used in: soups, sauces, gravies, baby foods, ketchup, yogurt, frozen meals and baked goods. Digested to glucose (not classified as dietary fibre at normal modification levels). No significant allergen concerns (corn is not a regulated major allergen, though corn allergy exists). Wheat-derived modified starch (if present) would require allergen declaration. ADI: not specified (JECFA for individual modifications). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Generally safe.""",
    'tapioca starch': """Tapioca starch is extracted from the tuberous roots of cassava (Manihot esculenta), a tropical shrub native to South America and now the third-largest source of food carbohydrates in the tropics. Extraction: cassava roots are peeled, washed, grated, and the starch is separated from the fibrous pulp by water washing and settling, then dried. The resulting white powder has a neutral flavour, high clarity when cooked, and good thickening power. Properties: gelatinises at 59–70°C; produces clear, glossy, slightly chewy gels; good freeze-thaw stability compared to wheat starch; excellent neutral flavour profile that does not interfere with delicate tastes. Uses: thickener in soups, sauces, puddings, custards, pie fillings and gravies; binder in gluten-free baked goods; base for tapioca pearls (boba) in bubble tea; coating agent in fried foods (provides a crispy, light coating). A major ingredient in Indian sabudana (tapioca pearls), used in vrat (fasting) foods. Nutritionally: high in digestible starch, low in fibre, protein and fat. High glycaemic index — raises blood glucose rapidly. Gluten-free; one of the most hypoallergenic starches available. Important note: raw cassava contains cyanogenic glucosides (linamarin) — cassava must be properly processed to remove these; commercial tapioca starch is safe. No significant food safety concerns at retail product level. Generally recognised as safe.""",
    'arrowroot': """Arrowroot is a fine white starch extracted from the rhizomes of Maranta arundinacea, a tropical plant native to the Caribbean and now cultivated in India, Southeast Asia, West Africa and South America. Extraction involves washing, grating and filtering the rhizomes followed by drying. The resulting starch has very small, oval-shaped granules (15–70 μm) that swell at relatively low temperatures (60–65°C), producing a clear, odourless gel with minimal flavour masking — ideal for delicate sauces and glazes. Key properties: forms a clear (not cloudy) thickened product unlike cornstarch; freezes and thaws well without syneresis; tolerates acidic conditions reasonably well; gelatinises at a lower temperature than cornstarch. Uses: thickener in sauces, gravies, fruit puddings, baby foods, biscuits (arrowroot biscuits are a traditional Indian and British product) and in gluten-free baking. Highly digestible and gentle on the stomach — traditionally used for convalescents and infants with digestive issues. Nutritionally: primarily carbohydrate (starch), with very low protein, fat and fibre content. Low in residual antinutrients. Gluten-free; suitable for coeliac disease. No known allergen. Not a regulated food additive (it is a food ingredient); generally recognised as safe. FSSAI does not restrict its use. Widely used in India in biscuits, custards and infant foods.""",
    'corn flour': """Corn flour (maize flour / cornstarch depending on context) refers to two different products: (1) Cornstarch — fine white starch extracted from the endosperm of maize kernels by wet milling; the predominant thickening ingredient; contains primarily amylose and amylopectin with negligible protein; produces a semi-opaque, moderately viscous gel when cooked; (2) Corn flour (UK/India) or masa harina — wholegrain or refined ground maize flour retaining protein, fibre and germ; used in cooking. In Indian packaged food labelling, 'corn flour' typically means cornstarch. Properties of cornstarch: gelatinises at 62–72°C; produces an opaque, moderately firm gel; retrogrades and thickens on cooling (forms a paste on refrigeration). Widely used as a thickener in soups, sauces, gravies, custard powder, biscuits, confectionery coatings and as a coating for fried foods. Gluten-free — suitable for coeliac disease. Major industrial source (USA, China, India) is maize. High glycaemic index as pure starch; nutritionally not a significant source of micronutrients. Wholesome at normal dietary levels. Generally recognised as safe. Not a regulated food additive; a standard food ingredient. FSSAI permits use without restriction.""",
    'rice flour': """Rice flour is produced by finely grinding white or brown rice (Oryza sativa). White rice flour is milled from polished rice (endosperm only), producing a bland, fine-textured powder; brown rice flour retains the bran and germ, providing more fibre, vitamins and minerals. One of the most hypoallergenic flours available — suitable for people with gluten intolerance, coeliac disease, wheat allergy, nut allergies and multiple food sensitivities. Nutritional profile of white rice flour: primarily starch (~80%), low protein (~6%), minimal fat and fibre; moderate glycaemic index (GI ~70–80). Brown rice flour: higher fibre, B vitamins, magnesium and antioxidants; lower GI than white rice flour. Functional properties: relatively bland flavour; creates a slightly gritty texture in baked goods; does not form a gluten network and requires binders (xanthan gum, eggs) in gluten-free baking; excellent frying coating (produces a light, crispy crust in Japanese tempura and many Indian recipes). South Indian applications: fundamental ingredient in idli, dosa, appam, puttu and rice papad batters; fermented preparations improve digestibility and bioavailability of minerals. Pantry staple across India, Japan, Thailand and Southeast Asia. Naturally gluten-free — widely used in coeliac-safe products. No known allergen. Generally recognised as safe. FSSAI permits use without restriction.""",
    'chickpea flour': """Chickpea flour (besan / gram flour) is produced by grinding dried chickpeas (Cicer arietinum), usually the desi variety (small, dark seeds). A cornerstone ingredient in Indian cooking. Nutritional profile: approximately 20–22% protein (relatively complete legume protein, good lysine content), 50–60% carbohydrate (including resistant starch and dietary fibre ~5%), 5–6% fat and significant micronutrients — iron, folate, zinc, manganese and B vitamins. Naturally gluten-free. Resistant starch content contributes to a lower glycaemic index (GI ~35–40) compared to wheat flour — blood glucose rises more slowly. Functional properties: forms a sticky batter that adheres well to vegetables and meats (for frying); produces a dense, protein-rich dough; thickens gravies and kadhi (yoghurt-based sauce). Used in: pakoras, bhajis, kadhi, besan laddoo, sev, papad, boondi, missi roti, dhokla (with fermentation) and many other traditional Indian preparations. High prebiotic fibre and resistant starch support gut health. A common ingredient in gluten-free baked goods internationally. Allergen: chickpeas (legumes) can trigger allergic reactions in people with legume allergies; cross-reactivity with peanuts and other legumes is documented. FODMAPs (fermentable oligosaccharides) in chickpea flour may trigger symptoms in people with IBS. Widely recommended as a healthful high-protein flour alternative. No regulatory restrictions.""",
    'soy flour': """Soy flour is produced by grinding raw, heat-treated or defatted soybeans into a fine powder. Full-fat soy flour (from whole soybeans) retains approximately 20% fat; defatted soy flour (from solvent-extracted or expelled meal) contains <1% fat. Both forms are very high in protein (35–50%) and contain all essential amino acids, making soy a complete plant protein. Rich in isoflavones (genistein, daidzein) — phytoestrogens that bind oestrogen receptors with weak agonist/antagonist activity. Uses: extender in processed meats (patties, sausages); protein enrichment in bread and baked goods; base for textured soy protein (TSP/TVP); ingredient in health foods and vegan products. In India, used in multi-grain flour blends, namkeen and protein-enriched atta. Allergen: soy is one of the 8 major allergens in the USA and one of the 14 major allergens in EU — mandatory declaration required on all food labels in India (FSSAI), EU and USA. Cross-reactivity with other legumes is possible. Phytoestrogen consideration: at typical dietary levels in non-Asian populations, soy isoflavones are unlikely to cause hormonal effects; populations with high soy intake (Japan, China) have not shown adverse hormonal outcomes. Large amounts may affect thyroid function in hypothyroid individuals on thyroid medication — space soy consumption from levothyroxine by several hours. Generally recognised as safe.""",
    'vital wheat gluten': """Vital wheat gluten (VWG) is the concentrated insoluble protein fraction of wheat flour — produced by washing wheat flour dough with water to remove starch and water-soluble components, leaving behind a sticky, elastic protein mass that is then spray-dried or flash-dried to preserve its functionality (the "vital" quality). Protein content: 75–82% protein (approximately 50% of which is glutenin and 50% gliadin). These two protein types form the viscoelastic gluten network when hydrated. Functions in food: (1) Dough strengthening — added to flour blends to increase protein content and improve dough structure, rise and chew in bread, bagels, baguettes and pizza bases; particularly important in India where atta (whole wheat flour) protein content can vary. (2) Protein fortification — used in high-protein or low-carb bread, protein bars and plant-based meat products. (3) Seitan ("wheat meat") — vital wheat gluten is the base ingredient for seitan, a high-protein vegan meat alternative widely used in Chinese Buddhist cuisine and increasingly in Indian vegetarian/vegan cooking. (4) Meat products — acts as a binder in processed meat and vegetarian burgers. (5) Pet food — used in some commercial pet foods as a protein source (though implicated in the 2007 US pet food recall due to melamine contamination in imported wheat gluten — this was a contamination issue not intrinsic to VWG). Allergen: gluten/wheat is among the most important food allergens globally. People with coeliac disease (an autoimmune disorder triggered by gliadin) must strictly avoid vital wheat gluten — consuming it causes intestinal damage. People with non-coeliac wheat sensitivity (NCWS) and wheat allergy must also avoid. FSSAI mandatory allergen labelling: products containing wheat/gluten must declare this prominently on packaging. Not suitable for gluten-free diets.""",
    'pea protein': """Pea protein is a plant-based protein concentrate or isolate extracted from yellow split peas (Pisum sativum) by aqueous extraction and optional isoelectric precipitation or membrane filtration. Two main forms: pea protein concentrate (~75–80% protein, retains some fibre and starch) and pea protein isolate (~85–90% protein, more refined). Amino acid profile: high in arginine (~8%), lysine (~7% — better than most cereals), branched-chain amino acids (BCAA — leucine, isoleucine, valine) which are important for muscle protein synthesis; relatively low in methionine and cysteine — often combined with rice protein (high in these amino acids) to achieve a complete amino acid profile for supplemental use. PDCAAS: 0.82–0.93 for isolated pea protein (good digestibility following processing). Hypoallergenicity: peas are legumes and can theoretically cross-react with other legumes (soy, peanut, lentil) in people with legume allergy; however, pea protein is not classified as a top-8 or top-14 major allergen in the USA or EU — making it accessible to soy-allergic individuals in most cases. Functional properties in food: relatively neutral flavour (less "beany" than soy), good water binding, excellent foam formation and gelling — increasingly used in plant-based meat and dairy alternatives for its functional protein network-forming properties. Iron content: pea protein contributes plant-based iron, though bioavailability is limited by phytate. Used in: protein shakes and bars, plant-based burgers (Beyond Meat uses pea protein), plant-based milk, infant formula alternatives and bakery enrichment. Widely used in Indian protein supplement products. FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'soy protein isolate': """Soy protein isolate (SPI) is the most highly processed commercial form of soy protein, produced from defatted soybean flakes by alkaline extraction, filtration to remove fibre, acid precipitation of the protein at pH ~4.5, neutralisation and spray-drying — achieving ≥90% protein content by dry weight. Protein quality: PDCAAS = 1.0 (equivalent to milk and egg protein); contains all 9 essential amino acids with a good BCAA and lysine profile. Isoflavone content depends on processing — acid-precipitated SPI retains more genistein and daidzein (phytoestrogens). Used in: protein powders, meal replacement shakes, plant-based meat products (key structural protein in plant burgers and sausages — the protein matrix binds and texturises), soy-based infant formula (for cow's milk protein allergy), processed meats and bakery products. Allergen — CRITICAL: soy is among the major 8 (USA) and 14 (EU) food allergens; FSSAI mandates prominent allergen declaration. SPI contains concentrated soy protein epitopes and is more allergenic per gram than refined soy oil. Isoflavone consideration: at typical food use levels, isoflavone content is not associated with adverse hormonal effects in healthy adults or infants. People on thyroid medication should separate soy consumption from levothyroxine by at least 4 hours (soy can impair thyroxine absorption). FSSAI permits as a food ingredient.""",
    'modified starch': """Modified starch (INS 1400–1442 range) — see the primary 'modified starch' entry above for comprehensive details on chemical modification types, safety and approvals. A broad category for starches (corn, wheat, potato, tapioca, waxy varieties) treated physically, enzymatically or chemically to overcome the limitations of native starch (heat/acid/shear breakdown, retrogradation, freeze-thaw instability). The key modification types: cross-linking (E1412–1414) for heat and acid stability; substitution/acetylation (E1420–1422) for freeze-thaw stability; combination treatments (E1442 — the most widely used modified starch globally); and oxidised starch (E1404) for whitening and film-forming. Not to be confused with genetically modified crops — modification here refers to chemical treatment of the extracted starch. Fully digested to glucose (most types); not classified as dietary fibre at approved modification levels. No significant allergen concerns except wheat-derived modified starch (may contain trace gluten — relevant for coeliac disease sufferers; must be labelled). ADI: not specified (JECFA for individual approved modifications). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1400–E1442), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'vegetable oil': """Vegetable oil on food ingredient labels is a generic term that can legally refer to any refined plant-derived oil or a blend of several oils without requiring specific disclosure in all labelling systems. The most commonly used oils under this description are: refined palm oil (most prevalent in Indian packaged food manufacturing due to low cost and semi-solid consistency), soybean oil, sunflower oil, canola oil, cottonseed oil and rice bran oil — singly or in blends. The lack of specificity is a consumer transparency concern because the health profile, allergen relevance, environmental impact and saturated fat content differ substantially between these oils. FSSAI's Food Safety and Standards (Labelling and Display) Regulations 2020 require that when a specific oil is blended, the label must declare the specific vegetable oils used — generic "vegetable oil" is increasingly being required to disclose component oils. Palm oil dominance in Indian packaged foods: an estimated 60–70% of "vegetable oil" in Indian packaged foods is palm oil or palm olein, which has a high saturated fat content (~50%). Consumers with cardiovascular risk factors, food allergies (peanut, sesame, soy) or ethical concerns (palm oil deforestation) should seek products that specifically name the oil used.""",
    'hydrogenated vegetable oil': """Hydrogenated vegetable oil (HVO) is vegetable oil that has been subjected to catalytic hydrogenation — reacting liquid vegetable oil with hydrogen gas in the presence of a nickel catalyst at 150–200°C. The degree of hydrogenation determines the product: fully hydrogenated oil (complete saturation of double bonds) produces a hard, waxy fat with negligible trans fats — essentially a saturated fat; partially hydrogenated oil (incomplete reaction) produces a semi-solid fat with significant industrial trans fatty acids (TFAs) as by-products. Health impact distinction: fully hydrogenated vegetable oil contains minimal trans fats but high saturated fat (palmitic, stearic acids); partially hydrogenated vegetable oil contains industrial TFAs (primarily elaidic acid), the most cardiovascular-damaging dietary fat — raising LDL and lowering HDL simultaneously. Global regulatory action on TFAs: FDA ruled partially hydrogenated oils are no longer GRAS (2015); WHO REPLACE initiative (2018) aims to eliminate industrial trans fats globally; FSSAI reduced India's maximum trans fat limit in hydrogenated vegetable oil to ≤2% from 2022. Modern food manufacturers largely use fully hydrogenated fat (blended with liquid oil), interesterified fat, or palm stearin to replace partially hydrogenated oil. On Indian food labels, "hydrogenated vegetable oil" without specification of degree may still contain trans fats in legacy products. Consumers should check the trans fat content declaration on the Nutrition Facts panel.""",
    'interesterified fat': """Interesterified fat (IE fat / interesterified vegetable oil / structured lipid) is produced by enzymatic or chemical rearrangement of fatty acids within and between triglyceride molecules of a fat blend, without altering the overall fatty acid composition but changing where the fatty acids are positioned on the glycerol backbone (sn-1, sn-2, sn-3 positions). This rearrangement changes the physical properties (melting profile, plasticity, solid fat content) without creating trans fatty acids — making interesterified fats a trans-fat-free alternative to partially hydrogenated oils for producing solid or semi-solid fats for margarine, shortening and bakery applications. Production methods: (1) chemical interesterification — using sodium methoxide catalyst at high temperature, randomises all fatty acid positions; (2) enzymatic interesterification — using immobilised lipase (typically sn-1,3 specific lipase from Rhizomucor miehei), selectively re-esterifies positions 1 and 3, allowing more targeted structural lipid design. Concern: early research raised concerns that interesterified fats might raise blood glucose levels or affect LDL/HDL ratios unfavourably, but subsequent well-controlled studies have not confirmed this. Current evidence suggests IE fats are metabolically neutral compared to equivalent saturated fat content — neither as harmful as trans fats, nor as healthful as unsaturated oils. They are high in saturated fat when based on palm stearin or fully hydrogenated oil blends — this remains a cardiovascular consideration. FSSAI permits interesterified fats; they are recognised as a technological improvement over partially hydrogenated fats. Ongoing long-term epidemiological data are limited compared to trans fat and saturated fat research.""",
    'acidity regulator': """Acidity regulator is the functional class name on food ingredient labels under FSSAI, EU and CODEX Alimentarius labelling regulations — it describes a food additive whose primary purpose is to control and maintain the pH (hydrogen ion concentration) of food within a defined range for safety, preservation, taste balance or technical function. The FSSAI Food Safety and Standards (Labelling and Display) Regulations 2020 permit the generic declaration "acidity regulator" followed by either the common name or INS/E-number of the specific compound. When "acidity regulator" appears without the specific compound named, the consumer cannot independently identify which acid or base is used. Common acidity regulators include: citric acid (E330 — the most widely used acidulant; safe, derived from Aspergillus niger fermentation of sugars), lactic acid (E270 — from fermentation; mild, smooth tartness; also a natural fermentation product), acetic acid/vinegar (E260 — sharp sour taste; anti-mould), phosphoric acid (E338 — inorganic acid in cola drinks; concern for dental erosion and bone density), malic acid (E296 — fruity tartness), tartaric acid (E334 — grape-derived; used in confectionery and wine), fumaric acid (E297 — strongest acidulant per gram; used in dry mixes), sodium/potassium carbonates and bicarbonates (alkaline pH regulators in confectionery and noodles). The specific compound matters for: (1) health concerns — phosphoric acid has dental and bone density considerations; (2) dietary restrictions — some sources are animal-derived (L-lactic acid may be from dairy fermentation in some products); (3) taste — each acid imparts a distinct flavour profile. FSSAI requires disclosure of acidity regulators on processed food labels. ADI and safety profile depend entirely on the specific compound identified.""",
    'raising agent': """Raising agent (leavening agent / leaven) is a functional class name on food labels under FSSAI, EU and CODEX labelling regulations — it describes any substance that produces gas (almost always carbon dioxide, CO₂) in dough or batter to make the final baked product light, porous and with greater volume than would result from the unbaked dough alone. The FSSAI Food Safety and Standards (Labelling and Display) Regulations 2020 permit the generic declaration "raising agent" with the specific compound INS/E-number. Mechanism: CO₂ expands in the oven, leavening the structure; the protein network (gluten in wheat, or egg protein in egg-based batters) and starch gelatinisation provide the final rigid structure that traps the gas bubbles. Major raising agents: (1) Sodium bicarbonate (E500ii/INS 500ii / baking soda) — the most fundamental leavening agent; releases CO₂ when it contacts acid (buttermilk, lemon juice, curd) or when heated above ~60°C; requires an acid to function cleanly; (2) Baking powder — a pre-mixed combination of sodium bicarbonate + a dry acid (cream of tartar E336i, sodium acid pyrophosphate E450i, monocalcium phosphate E341) + a starch filler; self-contained; widely used in India and globally; (3) Ammonium bicarbonate (E503ii/INS 503ii / baker's ammonia) — decomposes completely to CO₂ + NH₃ + H₂O with no residue; ideal for thin, crispy biscuits (digestive biscuits, crackers) where ammonia can volatilise; unsuitable for thick products; (4) Potassium bicarbonate (E501ii) — sodium-free alternative to baking soda; used in low-sodium baked goods; (5) Yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) — biological leavening via fermentation of sugars; produces CO₂ + ethanol; most important in bread and doughs requiring fermentation time. Selection of leavening agent affects final flavour, texture and sodium/mineral content. Safety: all approved raising agents in FSSAI/EU/CODEX are safe at food use levels. ADI depends on the specific compound identified.""",
    'anti caking agent': """Anti-caking agent is a functional class name on food labels under FSSAI, EU and CODEX Alimentarius labelling regulations — it describes a food additive whose purpose is to prevent powdered or granulated food materials from agglomerating (forming lumps or caking) during storage, particularly in humid tropical climates. The FSSAI labelling regulations allow the generic declaration "anti-caking agent" followed by the specific compound INS/E-number. Mechanism of caking: powder particles form inter-particle bonds via: (1) capillary water bridges — moisture adsorbed from the air creates liquid bridges between particles; (2) crystalline bridges — dissolved minerals recrystallise linking particles; (3) electrostatic forces; (4) mechanical interlocking (irregular particles). Anti-caking agents work by: absorbing water preferentially (keeping it away from the powder particles), creating hydrophobic coatings on particle surfaces (preventing water bridges), or providing very fine particles that physically separate the food particles. Major approved anti-caking agents: silicon dioxide (E551 — most widely used; food-grade amorphous silica; very high surface area absorbs moisture; used in salt, spices, soup mixes, protein powders); calcium silicate (E552 — high surface area; used in salt); magnesium carbonate (E504 — used in salt, icing sugar, baking powder); magnesium stearate (E470b — hydrophobic fatty acid salt; used in pharmaceutical tablets and nutritional supplements; also used in confectionery); calcium silicate (E552 — insoluble mineral); sodium ferrocyanide (E535 — used specifically in table salt at very low concentrations, 20 mg/kg; despite the "cyanide" name, the cyanide is tightly bound and safe); tricalcium phosphate (E341iii — used in powdered beverages). In India: salt, masala powders, soup mixes, protein powders and many powdered processed foods widely contain anti-caking agents. Safety: all approved anti-caking agents undergo toxicological review and are considered safe at permitted levels. ADI depends on the specific compound.""",
    'humectant': """Humectant is the functional class name assigned to food and cosmetic additives that attract and retain moisture from their surroundings — preventing dehydration of the product and extending shelf life by maintaining soft texture and reducing water activity. The term "humectant" may appear on food or cosmetic labels under FSSAI, EU and CODEX labelling rules, followed by either the common name or INS/E-number of the specific substance. In food applications: humectants bind water in baked goods (cakes, muffins, bread — keeping them soft and moist), confectionery (soft caramels, fudge, marzipan, soft-centred chocolates), dried fruits, processed meats, energy bars and beverages. Mechanism: humectants have multiple hydroxyl (-OH) groups that form hydrogen bonds with water molecules, reducing the effective "free" water in the product while maintaining moisture sensation. Major food humectants: (1) Glycerol/glycerin (E422) — the safest and most widely used; a natural by-product of fat saponification; very well tolerated; minimal glycaemic impact; (2) Sorbitol (E420) — a sugar alcohol derived from glucose; 60% sweetness of sugar; low GI; laxative in doses >20 g/day; (3) Mannitol (E421) — similar to sorbitol; used in chewing gum and pharmaceutical tablets; laxative at high doses; (4) Propylene glycol (E1520) — a synthetic diol; effective humectant but more controversial; acceptable at food use levels but concerns about skin penetration enhancement in cosmetics at high concentrations; (5) Maltitol (E965) — from maltose hydrogenation; used in sugar-free confectionery; (6) Xylitol (E967) — birch-derived; antibacterial in dental context. In cosmetics: the same principle applies — glycerol, hyaluronic acid, urea, amino acids, lactic acid and panthenol are key cosmetic humectants. FSSAI mandates disclosure of functional class name and specific compound (INS number). ADI and safety profile depend on the specific humectant identified.""",
    'glycerol': """Glycerol (glycerin / propane-1,2,3-triol / E422/INS 422) is a three-carbon polyol with three hydroxyl groups — the structural backbone common to all triglycerides (fats and oils), phospholipids and glycolipids. One of the most biologically fundamental small molecules. Natural sources: all vegetable oils and animal fats contain glycerol — released by the body's own lipases during fat digestion and metabolism. Commercial production: (1) saponification by-product — the largest source globally; when fats are hydrolysed to make soap or fatty acids, glycerol is co-produced (~10% w/w of soap production yield); (2) biodiesel by-product — transesterification of vegetable oils to biodiesel produces glycerol as a by-product (approximately 100 kg glycerol per tonne of biodiesel); (3) chemical synthesis from propylene (propylene glycol route — less common). Properties: colourless, odourless, viscous, hygroscopic liquid; miscible with water and ethanol; boiling point 290°C; very sweet taste (approximately 60% sweetness of sucrose). Food functions: (1) humectant — absorbs moisture from the environment and retains it in the product, preventing baked goods, confectionery, dried fruits, processed meats and energy bars from drying out; (2) solvent — dissolves many flavour compounds, colours and extracts for uniform distribution; (3) plasticiser — keeps confectionery flexible (fondants, marzipan, rolled icing); (4) low-calorie sweetener — 4.3 kcal/g but absorbed and metabolised differently from sugar; minimally raises blood glucose; (5) preservative co-agent — raises water activity depression in some products; (6) anti-freeze — lowers the freezing point of product systems. Cosmetic function: one of the most effective and widely used skin humectants — draws water from the dermis into the stratum corneum (epidermis); strengthens the skin barrier by interacting with aquaporin water channels (AQP3). Metabolism: absorbed completely; enters glycolysis as dihydroxyacetone phosphate or converted to glucose via gluconeogenesis in the liver. A normal intermediate in fat and carbohydrate metabolism. Health: very safe. Oral doses up to 3 g/kg body weight/day show no adverse effects. Mild laxative effect at very high doses. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FDA (GRAS), EU (E422), FSSAI and CODEX approved.""",
    'e422': 'E422/INS 422 is Glycerol (Glycerin), a humectant used to retain moisture in foods. Naturally derived from fats. Safe.',
    'propylene glycol': 'Propylene Glycol (E1520/INS 1520) is used as a humectant, solvent and carrier for flavourings in foods. Generally regarded as safe in food use; different from ethylene glycol (antifreeze) which is toxic.',
    'e1520': 'E1520/INS 1520 is Propylene Glycol, a humectant and solvent used in flavourings and food coatings. Generally safe in food use levels.',
    'shellac': 'Shellac (E904/INS 904) is a natural resin secreted by the lac bug. Used as a glazing agent on confectionery, pills and fresh citrus fruit. Provides a shiny coating. Animal-derived; not vegan.',
    'e904': 'E904/INS 904 is Shellac, a natural animal-derived resin used as a glazing agent on sweets, chocolates and citrus fruit. Not vegan.',
    'beeswax': """Beeswax (E901/INS 901 — in food; also used in cosmetics as beeswax/Cera alba) is a natural wax produced by worker honeybees (Apis mellifera and other Apis species) from special wax glands on their abdomens. The bees use it to construct honeycomb cell walls. Composition: complex mixture of esters (~71% — primarily fatty acid esters of long-chain alcohols), hydrocarbons (~17%), free fatty acids (~12%) and minor compounds. Melting point: 62–65°C. White when purified ('white beeswax / Cera alba'); naturally yellow-brown from pollen pigments. In food (E901): glazing agent on confectionery (imparts shine and a slight moisture barrier), fresh fruit (food-grade coating reduces moisture loss in transit), chocolate confectionery coatings and pharmaceutical tablets. Animal-derived — not suitable for vegans; halal and kosher certification depends on source and processing. In cosmetics: one of the most versatile natural wax ingredients — used as a thickener, emulsifier and emollient in lipsticks, lip balms, mascara, eye shadow, creams, lotions and hair waxes. Provides structure, firmness, film-forming properties and moisture retention. Softening point and texture modify with blend of other waxes. Allergen note: occasional contact allergy to beeswax is reported — typically due to pollen or propolis contaminants rather than the wax itself. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E901), FDA (GRAS).""",
    'e901': 'E901/INS 901 is Beeswax, a natural glaze from honeybees used on sweets and fresh fruit. Not vegan.',
    'carnauba wax': """Carnauba wax (E903/INS 903 / Brazilian wax / palm wax) is a natural plant wax harvested from the leaves of the carnauba palm (Copernicia prunifera), a drought-resistant palm tree that grows almost exclusively in the semi-arid Caatinga region of northeastern Brazil (primarily the states of Ceará, Piauí and Rio Grande do Norte). The palm secretes wax on the surface of its leaves as an adaptation to prevent water loss during the harsh dry season — the waxy coating reflects intense sunlight and minimises evaporation from leaf surfaces. Harvesting: leaves are harvested (2–4 times per year), dried, threshed to dislodge wax powder, and the powder is then melted, filtered and refined into blocks or flakes. Brazil exports virtually the entire global supply; carnauba wax is a significant Brazilian agricultural export. Composition: complex mixture of aliphatic esters (~40%), diesters of 4-hydroxycinnamic acid (~20%), fatty alcohols (~12%), fatty acids (~3%), hydrocarbons (~1.5%) and water (~1%). Primary ester components are n-tetracosanyl n-octacosanoate and other C28–C34 ester waxes. Physical properties: melting point 78–85°C — the hardest and highest-melting-point of all natural waxes commercially used in food and cosmetics; hardness comparable to some plastics; extremely bright gloss-forming ability. Food applications: (1) glazing agent on confectionery — the characteristic hard, high-gloss coating on M&Ms, Skittles, jelly beans, hard-coated sugar confectionery and dragees; (2) glazing agent on fresh citrus fruit, apples and tomatoes — a thin carnauba wax coating replaces natural surface waxes removed during washing, extending shelf life by reducing moisture loss and preventing microbial entry; (3) tablet coating in pharmaceutical solid dosage forms; (4) chewing gum base component; (5) as a food-release agent. Cosmetic applications: hardener and gloss enhancer in lipstick, mascara and eyeliner; provides water resistance and durability; in hair styling waxes and pomades; in nail polish as a hardener; sunscreen formulations. Other: car wax ('Carnation' type), shoe polish, furniture polish, board wax, dental floss coating — carnauba wax appears in many non-food applications. Metabolism: poorly digested; passes through the GI tract largely intact; not absorbed; no caloric contribution. No known allergen. Vegan and plant-derived; suitable for all religious dietary requirements. No genotoxicity or carcinogenicity concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E903), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. One of the safest and most natural glazing agents in commercial use.""",
    'e903': 'E903/INS 903 is Carnauba Wax, a plant-based glazing agent used on sweets and fruit. Vegan-friendly.',
    'mineral oil': 'White mineral oil (E905/INS 905) is a highly refined petroleum-derived oil used as a glazing and coating agent on fresh fruit, dried fruit and confectionery. Concern exists around contamination with mineral oil aromatic hydrocarbons (MOAH) which are potentially carcinogenic.',
    'e905': 'E905/INS 905 is Mineral Oil (White Oil), a petroleum-derived glazing agent on fresh and dried fruit. Potential MOAH contamination concern.',
    'polysorbate 80': """Polysorbate 80 (Tween 80 / E433/INS 433) is a synthetic polyoxyethylene sorbitan monooleate emulsifier — produced by first making sorbitan monooleate (sorbitan esterified with oleic acid) then reacting with approximately 80 moles of ethylene oxide. High HLB value (~15) makes it a powerful oil-in-water emulsifier and solubiliser. Widely used in: ice cream (prevents fat coalescence, improves scoop properties), baked goods, salad dressings, vitamin/flavour emulsifications in beverages, pharmaceuticals (vaccine stabiliser, solubiliser for injectable drugs including some chemotherapy agents) and cosmetics (emulsifier, fragrance solubiliser). The 2015 Chassaing et al. (Nature) study found polysorbate 80 at 1% in drinking water promoted colitis and metabolic syndrome in mice via gut microbiome disruption and mucus layer thinning — a finding replicated in subsequent studies at high doses in rodents. Human relevance: food-use concentrations are typically 0.1–0.5% of the finished product (and the product is not consumed continuously), making direct extrapolation to 1% continuous dietary exposure problematic. FSSAI, EU (E433), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day combined polysorbates. Ethoxylation process may introduce trace 1,4-dioxane. Relevant consideration for individuals with IBD (inflammatory bowel disease).""",
    'e433': 'E433/INS 433 is Polysorbate 80, a synthetic emulsifier used in ice cream and pharmaceuticals. Generally safe; some research on gut microbiome effects at high animal doses.',
    'polysorbate 60': """Polysorbate 60 (Tween 60 / E435/INS 435) is produced by ethoxylating sorbitan monostearate with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. The stearic acid (C18) ester chain is more solid at room temperature than the oleic acid chain in polysorbate 80, giving polysorbate 60 slightly different functionality in food systems. Used as an emulsifier and stabiliser in: whipped toppings (provides aeration and stability without dairy fat), non-dairy coffee creamers, cake mixes, baked goods, shortenings, ice cream and confectionery fillings. In baking, it improves crumb structure, volume and shelf life. A versatile o/w emulsifier with HLB ~14.9. Same ethoxylation-related 1,4-dioxane trace contaminant concern as other polysorbates — quality-controlled manufacturers use vacuum stripping to remove residuals. 2015 animal studies (Chassaing et al., Nature) showed polysorbate 60 and 80 at concentrations of 1% (far above food use levels) disrupted the gut mucus layer and promoted low-grade inflammation in mice — relevance to typical human dietary exposure (where concentrations are orders of magnitude lower) is actively debated. FSSAI, EU (E435), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day combined polysorbates (JECFA).""",
    'e435': 'E435/INS 435 is Polysorbate 60, a synthetic emulsifier used in baked goods and whipped toppings.',
    'polysorbate 20': """Polysorbate 20 (Tween 20 / E432/INS 432) is a synthetic non-ionic emulsifier produced by ethoxylating sorbitan (derived from sorbitol) and esterifying with lauric acid (a C12 fatty acid) followed by polyethoxylation with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. The polyoxyethylene chains confer high hydrophilicity (HLB value ~16.7), making polysorbate 20 primarily an oil-in-water emulsifier and solubiliser. Used in: food (flavour solubilisation, cosmetic cream emulsification), beverages, confectionery coatings, and widely in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals as a mild surfactant and fragrance solubiliser. In personal care, it is used in facial cleansers, toners, lotions and baby products for its gentle surfactancy and emulsification. The ethoxylation process can introduce trace amounts of 1,4-dioxane (a possible carcinogen, IARC Group 2B) — manufacturers should use vacuum-stripping purification. EU (E432), FSSAI and CODEX approved in food at specified limits. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day for all polysorbates combined (JECFA). Animal studies at very high polysorbate doses showed gut microbiome disruption and mild intestinal inflammation; relevance to human food-additive exposure is considered minimal. Generally safe and mild.""",
    'e432': 'E432/INS 432 is Polysorbate 20, a mild synthetic emulsifier used in some foods and widely in cosmetics.',
    'sodium stearoyl lactylate': """Sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL / E481/INS 481) is an anionic emulsifier and dough conditioner produced by reacting stearic acid (an 18-carbon saturated fatty acid from vegetable or animal sources) with lactic acid to form stearoyl lactic acid, then neutralising with sodium hydroxide or sodium carbonate. The resulting compound has an oil-loving (lipophilic) stearic acid tail and a water-loving (hydrophilic) sodium lactate head group, with a moderate HLB value (~21) suitable for o/w emulsification and starch complexation. Mode of action in baked goods: (1) gluten strengthening — SSL interacts with gliadin and glutenin proteins in the gluten network, increasing gluten strength and elasticity, allowing the dough to retain more CO₂ gas from yeast/baking powder fermentation, producing better loaf volume and crumb structure; (2) starch complexation (anti-staling) — SSL forms helical inclusion complexes with amylose (linear starch) chains, dramatically slowing starch retrogradation and extending the soft texture shelf life of bread by 2–7 days; (3) emulsification — improves fat distribution throughout the dough. Applications: primarily white bread, whole-grain bread, hamburger buns, English muffins, tortillas, pasta, crackers and some cakes; also in coffee whiteners and whipped toppings as an oil-in-water stabiliser. Sodium content: SSL provides a small amount of sodium — not nutritionally relevant at food additive concentrations. Derived from lactic acid (typically fermentation-derived) and stearic acid (plant or animal origin — the specific source determines vegan/halal/kosher status; vegetable-sourced SSL is common). ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E481), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved at specified limits (typically 2–5 g/kg of flour weight in bread). Generally safe; no known allergen concerns.""",
    'calcium stearoyl lactylate': 'Calcium Stearoyl Lactylate (CSL/E482/INS 482) is similar to SSL but uses calcium instead of sodium. Used as a dough conditioner in bread and as an emulsifier in coffee whiteners.',
    'sorbitan monostearate': """Sorbitan monostearate (Span 60 / E491/INS 491) is a synthetic non-ionic emulsifier — produced by the esterification of sorbitol (a sugar alcohol from glucose reduction) with stearic acid (an 18-carbon saturated fatty acid, usually derived from plant or animal fats). During esterification, the sorbitol undergoes partial dehydration to form the sorbitan ring structure (1,4-anhydrosorbitol), which is then esterified at one hydroxyl group with one stearic acid molecule. The sorbitan esters (Span series) are lipophilic emulsifiers (HLB ~4.7 for Span 60) — they promote water-in-oil (W/O) emulsions or stabilise oil phases. They are commonly used in combination with polysorbate 60 (Tween 60 / E435 — a more hydrophilic version made by ethoxylating Span 60) to achieve balanced emulsification systems with a wider range of HLB values. Uses in food: emulsifier in whipped cream (improves foam stability and texture), cake mixes (improves crumb structure and moisture retention), coffee whiteners, peanut butter (prevents oil separation), shortenings, confectionery coatings, chocolate (anti-bloom alongside lecithin) and cream fillings for pastries. In non-food applications: the Span/Tween combination is used in pharmaceutical emulsions, cosmetics, topical creams and as a surfactant in veterinary and industrial applications. Safety: sorbitan esters are hydrolysed in the gut to sorbitol and stearic acid — both normal food components. Sorbitol provides minimal calories; stearic acid is a saturated fatty acid that has a neutral effect on serum cholesterol (unlike palmitic acid). No carcinogenicity, genotoxicity or reproductive toxicity. No sensitisation at food use levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E491), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Sorbitan monostearate from plant-derived stearic acid (palm kernel or shea) is suitable for vegans; from tallow-derived stearic acid it is not vegan.""",
    'e491': 'E491/INS 491 is Sorbitan Monostearate, an emulsifier used in baked goods and confectionery.',
    'quillaia extract': 'Quillaia extract (E999/INS 999) is a natural foaming agent from the bark of the soapbark tree, used in beverages (root beer, ginger beer) to create foam. Also used in frothy coffee drinks.',
    'e999': 'E999/INS 999 is Quillaia Extract, a natural foaming agent from soapbark tree bark. Used in beverages and cocktails.',

    # ── Common cosmetic & personal care additions ─────────────────────────────
    'dimethicone': 'Dimethicone is a silicone polymer widely used in hair conditioners, moisturisers, sunscreens and makeup primers. Creates a smooth, silky feel and forms a protective barrier on skin and hair. Non-comedogenic at standard concentrations. Considered safe by regulatory bodies.',
    'cyclomethicone': 'Cyclomethicone is a lightweight, volatile silicone used in hair care and skin care products. Evaporates after application, leaving no residue. Considered safe at current use levels.',
    'cyclopentasiloxane': 'Cyclopentasiloxane (D5) is a volatile silicone used in deodorants, hair care and skin care. The EU has restricted its use in rinse-off cosmetics citing environmental persistence concerns, though it is considered safe for human health.',
    'phenoxyethanol': 'Phenoxyethanol is a widely used preservative in cosmetics and personal care products, replacing parabens. Effective against bacteria and some fungi. Generally safe at ≤1%; high concentrations may irritate skin. EU and FDA permit use up to 1%.',
    'ethylhexylglycerin': """Ethylhexylglycerin (3-(2-ethylhexyloxy)propane-1,2-diol) is a synthetic glycerin-derived ingredient produced by etherification of glycerin with 2-ethyl-1-hexanol. Multifunctional in cosmetics: humectant (attracts and retains moisture to the skin via its hydroxyl groups); skin conditioner (improves skin feel and softness); mild antimicrobial agent and preservative booster (reduces surface tension at microbial cell membranes, enhancing the efficacy of conventional preservatives like phenoxyethanol, parabens or benzyl alcohol — allowing lower concentrations to be used). Commonly found in products labelled as 'paraben-free' or using natural-derived preservation systems, where it is paired with phenoxyethanol (at combined concentrations of 0.3% each). Used in facial moisturisers, sunscreens, body lotions, shampoos and conditioners. Well-tolerated by most skin types; a small number of contact allergy cases have been reported (patch-test confirmed) — relatively uncommon. EU Cosmetics Regulation does not restrict it; CIR Expert Panel has confirmed it safe for cosmetic use at current use concentrations. Non-comedogenic. Suitable for sensitive skin formulations where conventional preservatives cause reactions. Safe for adults; standard cosmetic caution for infant/very young child products.""",
    'caprylyl glycol': 'Caprylyl Glycol is a humectant and mild antimicrobial agent used in cosmetics as a preservative booster. Often paired with phenoxyethanol or parabens. Generally safe.',
    'sodium pca': 'Sodium PCA (Sodium Pyrrolidone Carboxylic Acid) is a natural humectant — one of the skin\'s natural moisturising factors (NMF). Used in moisturisers and hair conditioners to attract and retain water. Safe and skin-compatible.',
    'glycerin': 'Glycerin (Glycerol) is a natural humectant found in all fats and oils. Widely used in skincare to attract moisture from the air into the skin. Effective, safe and well-tolerated by all skin types.',
    'propanediol': 'Propanediol (1,3-Propanediol) is a plant-derived humectant and solvent used in cosmetics as an alternative to propylene glycol. Used in serums, moisturisers and sunscreens. Generally well-tolerated.',
    'butylene glycol': 'Butylene Glycol (1,3-Butanediol) is a humectant and solvent used in cosmetics. Helps other ingredients penetrate skin. Generally safe; occasionally causes contact dermatitis in sensitive individuals.',
    'pentylene glycol': 'Pentylene Glycol is a humectant and mild preservative used in skincare products. Provides moisture retention and antimicrobial properties. Generally safe and well-tolerated.',
    'octyldodecanol': """Octyldodecanol (2-octyldodecanol) is a long-chain, branched fatty alcohol (C20 total) produced by condensation of octanol and decanol. A liquid at room temperature despite its molecular weight, due to branching. In cosmetics: a versatile emollient and co-solvent — excellent for dissolving lipophilic active ingredients (UV filters, retinoids, vitamins) in cosmetic formulations; provides a smooth, elegant skin feel without the heaviness of linear fatty alcohols; good spreading properties; helps emulsify ingredients in anhydrous (water-free) formulations. Used in: lipsticks (provides a smooth application and film feel), foundations, sunscreens (dissolves UV filters such as avobenzone and octocrylene), hair conditioners, body lotions and facial creams. Compatible with silicones, oils and waxes. Not a surfactant — does not strip skin barrier. Non-comedogenic rating: low (0–1). Very good skin tolerability; extremely low sensitisation potential — rare sensitisation cases have been reported but are uncommon. EU Cosmetics Regulation: permitted; no restrictions. CIR Expert Panel: confirmed safe. Vegan; usually plant-derived. Suitable for sensitive skin and eye area products.""",
    'isopropyl myristate': 'Isopropyl Myristate is an ester of isopropanol and myristic acid used as an emollient in cosmetics. Provides a lightweight, non-greasy feel. Can be comedogenic for some people.',
    'caprylic capric triglyceride': """Caprylic/capric triglyceride (CCT / fractionated coconut oil) is produced by esterifying glycerol with caprylic acid (C8:0) and capric acid (C10:0) — the two medium-chain fatty acids — isolated from coconut oil or palm kernel oil by fractional distillation. The resulting triglyceride is a liquid at room temperature (unlike whole coconut oil which solidifies below 24°C), odourless, colourless and very stable. In cosmetics: a superior emollient and carrier oil with an extremely low comedogenicity rating (0–1 on a scale of 0–5); provides excellent skin feel — lightweight, silky, non-greasy and highly moisturising without occlusion; does not go rancid quickly (high oxidative stability from absence of polyunsaturated fatty acids); enhances the penetration of active ingredients by increasing their solubility in oil phase; widely used in facial serums, sunscreens, body oils, hair oils and lipsticks. One of the most compatible carrier oils with skin sebum composition. As a food ingredient: same as MCT oil — medium-chain triglycerides absorbed rapidly without requiring bile acid emulsification, bypassing lymphatic transport and going directly to the liver for energy; used in ketogenic diet supplements, clinical nutrition (for patients with fat malabsorption disorders) and sports nutrition. Well tolerated; very low sensitisation potential. CIR Expert Panel confirmed safe for cosmetic use. FDA GRAS. Vegan (plant-derived).""",
    'coco caprylate': """Coco-caprylate (coconut caprylate) is an ester produced by reacting coconut-derived fatty alcohols (primarily cetyl and stearyl alcohols from coconut oil) with caprylic acid (C8:0, also from coconut oil), creating a 100% coconut-derived emollient. A liquid to semi-liquid oil at room temperature, colourless and odourless. In cosmetics: primary function is as a lightweight, fast-spreading emollient — provides an excellent 'dry touch' skin feel with a silicone-like elegance but is entirely plant-derived and biodegradable. Extremely low in comedogenicity (0). Used as a silicone substitute in silicone-free formulations — increasingly sought as sustainable alternatives to dimethicone and cyclomethicone. Applications: sunscreens (provides excellent spreadability, increases SPF efficiency of UV filters), moisturisers, facial serums, hair care, colour cosmetics and lip products. Helps dissolve UV filters in sunscreen formulations, reducing the white cast of physical UV filters (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide). Excellent skin compatibility; very low sensitisation potential. Not greasy; absorbs quickly. EU Cosmetics Regulation: permitted; classified as an emollient. CIR Expert Panel: confirmed safe at cosmetic use concentrations. Vegan; coconut-derived. Biodegradable and considered an eco-friendly ingredient compared to silicones.""",
    'squalane': 'Squalane is a stable, saturated form of squalene used as an emollient in skincare. Derived from olive oil (plant-based) or shark liver oil (animal). Plant-based squalane is widely preferred. Non-comedogenic and excellent skin compatibility.',
    'squalene': 'Squalene is an unsaturated lipid naturally produced by the human body and found in olive oil, amaranth and shark liver. Used in cosmetics as a moisturiser; however, due to instability it is usually converted to squalane for use.',
    'jojoba oil': 'Jojoba oil is technically a liquid wax extracted from jojoba plant seeds. Closely resembles skin\'s natural sebum; used in moisturisers, hair care and lip products. Non-comedogenic and stable.',
    'argan oil': 'Argan oil is pressed from the kernels of the argan tree from Morocco. Rich in oleic acid, linoleic acid, tocopherols and polyphenols. Used in hair care and skincare for its nourishing and antioxidant properties.',
    'rosehip oil': 'Rosehip oil is cold-pressed from rosehip seeds. Rich in Vitamin A (as retinol precursor), Vitamin C, and linoleic acid. Used in anti-ageing skincare. May cause sensitivity in some individuals.',
    'marula oil': """Marula oil is cold-pressed from the kernels of the fruit of the marula tree (Sclerocarya birrea), a wild deciduous tree native to Southern and East Africa (South Africa, Zimbabwe, Mozambique, Namibia). Marula fruit pulp is consumed by both humans and the famed 'drunk elephants' (a popular, though partially exaggerated, wildlife story). The kernel oil has an exceptional fatty acid profile: ~78% oleic acid (C18:1, omega-9), ~6% palmitic acid, ~5% stearic acid, ~6% linoleic acid (C18:2), with the remaining fraction being beneficial unsaturated fats. Very high oleic acid content gives the oil oxidative stability, lightweight skin feel and rapid absorption without a greasy residue. Rich in antioxidant compounds including oleic acid, tocopherols (Vitamin E), sterols and flavonoids. In cosmetics: non-comedogenic (comedogenicity rating: 3–4, moderate), so more suitable for normal to dry skin than oily/acne-prone; provides exceptional skin softening and moisture retention; used in facial oils, hair oils, anti-ageing serums and lip balms. Absorbs quickly; compatible with most skin types including sensitive. Sustainability consideration: marula is wild-harvested in Southern Africa; several ethical brands work with local women's cooperatives and fair-trade sourcing. The Africare Foundation and similar organisations support sustainable marula harvesting. Generally safe; rare sensitisation possible. Not widely used in Indian food but gaining popularity in Indian premium cosmetics.""",
    'shea butter': 'Shea butter is a fat extracted from the nuts of the shea tree, native to Africa. Rich in oleic acid, stearic acid and various vitamins. Widely used in moisturisers, hair care and lip balms. Generally safe; tree nut allergy may be relevant for sensitive individuals.',
    'cocoa butter': """Cocoa butter (Theobroma oil / cacao butter) is the natural vegetable fat extracted from cocoa beans (Theobroma cacao) — obtained by pressing roasted, de-shelled cocoa nibs to separate the fat from the cocoa solids (the pressed cake becomes cocoa powder; the extracted fat is cocoa butter). Fatty acid composition: a distinctive and highly saturated profile for a plant fat — approximately 35% stearic acid (C18:0), 25% palmitic acid (C16:0), 35% oleic acid (C18:1) and 3% linoleic acid. Despite high saturated fat content, the dominant saturated acid (stearic acid) appears to have a neutral effect on serum LDL cholesterol (unlike palmitic acid), because stearic acid is efficiently desaturated to oleic acid in the liver. Also contains polyphenols (epicatechin, procyanidins), phytosterols (beta-sitosterol) and small amounts of fat-soluble vitamins. Physical properties: very narrow melting range (30–35°C) — solid at room temperature, melts just below body temperature (gives chocolate its "melt in the mouth" sensation). Oxidative stability is good (low polyunsaturated fat content, stearic acid resistance to oxidation). In food: the primary fat in chocolate manufacturing — responsible for chocolate's smooth texture, gloss, snap and melt behaviour; combined with cocoa solids, sugar and milk solids; also used in confectionery fillings, baked goods and as a natural fat for pharmaceutical tablets. In cosmetics: widely used as an emollient, occlusive skin moisturiser and lip product ingredient; melts on skin providing a protective barrier and reducing transepidermal water loss; used in lip balms, body butters, moisturisers and stretch mark creams. Comedogenicity: rated 4/5 on the comedogenicity scale — cocoa butter may clog pores and is not recommended for acne-prone skin. Allergen: cocoa is not among the top regulated allergens, but cocoa/chocolate allergy does exist (rare). FSSAI permits cocoa butter as a food ingredient.""",
    'cetyl alcohol': 'Cetyl alcohol is a fatty alcohol derived from coconut or palm oil. Used as an emulsifier, emollient and thickener in creams and lotions. Not drying like ethyl alcohol — fatty alcohols are beneficial in skincare.',
    'stearyl alcohol': 'Stearyl alcohol is a fatty alcohol used as an emollient and emulsifier in creams and lotions. Safe and non-irritating for most skin types.',
    'cetearyl alcohol': 'Cetearyl alcohol is a mixture of cetyl and stearyl fatty alcohols used as an emulsifier and emollient in skincare. Safe and beneficial for skin; not the same as drying alcohols.',
    'behenyl alcohol': """Behenyl alcohol (docosanol / 1-docosanol / C22 fatty alcohol) is a long-chain, straight-chain saturated fatty alcohol produced by reduction of behenic acid (docosanoic acid) — obtained from rapeseed (canola) oil, peanut oil or other plant oils. Solid white wax at room temperature. In cosmetics: emollient (softens and smooths skin); thickener and consistency regulator in creams and lotions (provides firmness and body without greasiness); emulsifier (aids formation of stable oil-in-water emulsions). Used in hair conditioners, deep conditioners, hair masks, facial moisturisers and body creams. As a hair conditioner: behenyl alcohol deposits on the hair shaft, smoothing the cuticle, reducing static, improving combability and adding shine — particularly useful for thick, coarse or curly hair types. Despite being an 'alcohol,' behenyl alcohol does not dry skin or hair — fatty alcohols are nourishing, not drying; this is a common source of consumer confusion. Antiviral application: docosanol (marketed as Abreva in USA) is FDA-approved as an OTC antiviral for recurrent herpes labialis (cold sores) at 10% concentration; it works by inhibiting viral envelope fusion with cell membranes. EU Cosmetics Regulation: permitted without restriction. CIR Expert Panel: confirmed safe. Very low sensitisation potential. Vegan (plant-derived from rapeseed or other plant oils).""",
    'stearic acid': """Stearic acid (octadecanoic acid / C18:0) is an 18-carbon saturated fatty acid found in cocoa butter (~35%), beef tallow (~25%), shea butter (~40%) and many other animal and plant fats. Unique among common saturated fatty acids: stearic acid does not raise LDL cholesterol — it is rapidly converted in the liver to oleic acid (a monounsaturated fat) by stearoyl-CoA desaturase, explaining its neutral cardiovascular profile. This is why dark chocolate (high in cocoa butter stearic acid) does not raise LDL cholesterol despite high saturated fat content. Metabolic behaviour: stearic acid raises HDL ('good') cholesterol slightly and is considered neutral or mildly beneficial regarding cardiovascular risk. In cosmetics and personal care: a widely used multi-functional ingredient — emulsifier, thickener, opacifier, and emollient. In creams and lotions, stearic acid (typically as stearic acid USP grade) forms the emulsified base when neutralised with triethanolamine (creating soap-in-place emulsification). Provides a silky, non-greasy texture. Used in lipsticks (body and structure), lotions, creams, sunscreens and bar soaps. In food: may be derived from animal (tallow) or vegetable sources (shea, cocoa butter); used in food-grade emulsifiers and confectionery coatings. The animal-derived form is not vegan. Generally recognised as safe for all applications. No significant allergen concerns.""",
    'palmitic acid': """Palmitic acid (hexadecanoic acid / C16:0) is the most abundant saturated fatty acid in both the animal and plant kingdoms. Produced naturally in all living organisms via fatty acid synthesis. Present in high concentrations in palm oil (~44%), meat (~20–25% of fat), dairy (~30% of fat), cocoa butter (~26%) and human breast milk (~25% of fatty acids). In food: used as an emollient, emulsifier and thickener in processed foods (mono and diglycerides often contain palmitic acid), and as a substrate for various food additives. As a dietary fatty acid: palmitic acid raises LDL ('bad') cholesterol similarly to lauric acid — epidemiological studies associate high saturated fat intake (particularly palmitic acid from processed foods and red meat) with increased cardiovascular disease risk. However, palmitic acid from whole food sources (cocoa, avocado, nuts) is consumed in different matrices with fibres and phytochemicals that modify its effects. Endogenous production: the body synthesises palmitic acid as the primary product of de novo lipogenesis (synthesis of fat from excess carbohydrates) — high sugar and refined carbohydrate diets can drive elevated plasma palmitate via this pathway. In cosmetics: excellent skin-compatible emollient (palmitic acid is a natural skin surface fatty acid); thickens creams and lotions; provides a stable, non-greasy feel. Generally recognised as safe at food use levels. Relevant for cardiovascular health management at high dietary intakes.""",
    'myristic acid': """Myristic acid (tetradecanoic acid / C14:0) is a saturated fatty acid naturally found in high concentrations in coconut oil (~18%), palm kernel oil (~16%), dairy fat (~10–12%), nutmeg (~75% of fat — hence 'myristicin' but also myristic acid), and human breast milk. One of the most widely distributed saturated fatty acids in nature. Dietary effects: of all common saturated fatty acids, myristic acid has the strongest LDL-cholesterol-raising effect per gram — more potent than palmitic or stearic acid. It is incorporated into LDL particles and impairs LDL receptor clearance. Populations with high coconut oil or dairy consumption tend to have higher myristic acid intakes. Despite this, studies in populations with traditional coconut-based diets (Pacific islanders, Kerala) have not consistently shown higher cardiovascular disease rates — the 'saturated fat paradox' remains a subject of active nutritional debate. Myristic acid is also used in protein N-myristoylation — an important post-translational modification that helps anchor proteins to cell membranes; this is an endogenous metabolic function not directly related to dietary intake. In cosmetics: C14 fatty acid used in soap formulations (myristyl myristate soaps), skin conditioners, thickeners and emulsifiers; skin-compatible and generally well-tolerated. In foods: not directly added but present in coconut oil, palm kernel oil and dairy-derived ingredients. Generally recognised as safe at food concentrations.""",
    'lauric acid': """Lauric acid (dodecanoic acid / C12:0) is a 12-carbon saturated fatty acid — the dominant fatty acid in coconut oil (~48%) and palm kernel oil (~46%), and present in meaningful amounts in human breast milk (~6%). A medium-chain fatty acid (MCFA) that is absorbed and metabolised differently from longer-chain saturated fats — transported via the portal vein directly to the liver rather than packaged into chylomicrons for lymphatic transport. Antimicrobial activity: lauric acid (and its monoglyceride form, monolaurin) has documented antimicrobial properties against certain bacteria, viruses and fungi by disrupting lipid membranes of pathogens. This has driven interest in coconut oil as a natural antimicrobial. In the body, lauric acid raises both LDL and HDL cholesterol; the net cardiovascular effect is debated — the ratio improvement (LDL:HDL unchanged or improved) may be more neutral than for longer-chain saturated fats. However, evidence is insufficient to make strong health claims. In personal care and cosmetics: lauric acid is a key component of soaps (produced by saponification of coconut oil); provides rich lather and antimicrobial properties in cleansers, shampoos and skin care. Monolaurin (glyceryl monolaurate) is used as an emulsifier in cosmetics. Generally regarded as safe at food use levels. Widely present in coconut-containing foods throughout India.""",
    'carbomer': 'Carbomer is a synthetic polymer used as a thickener and gelling agent in gels, creams and serums. Creates a clear, stable gel texture. Generally safe; may cause irritation in sensitive individuals.',
    'xanthan gum': 'Xanthan gum (E415/INS 415) is a natural polysaccharide produced by fermenting sugars with the bacterium Xanthomonas campestris. Widely used as a thickener and stabiliser in dressings, sauces, gluten-free baking and beverages. Safe.',
    'e415': 'E415/INS 415 is Xanthan Gum, a fermentation-derived polysaccharide thickener. Used in salad dressings, sauces and gluten-free products. Safe.',
    'gellan gum': 'Gellan gum (E418/INS 418) is a microbial polysaccharide used as a gelling and stabilising agent in plant-based milks, beverages and gel desserts. Generally safe.',
    'locust bean gum': 'Locust bean gum (Carob gum/E410/INS 410) is a natural thickener from carob seeds. Used in ice cream, cheese and infant formula to improve texture and prevent ice crystal formation.',
    'tara gum': 'Tara gum (E417/INS 417) is a natural thickener from tara tree seeds, related to locust bean gum. Used in processed meats, dairy products and baked goods.',
    'e417': 'E417/INS 417 is Tara Gum, a natural thickener from tara tree seeds. Used in processed meats and dairy.',
    'konjac gum': """Konjac gum (konjac glucomannan / KGM / E425/INS 425) is extracted from the dried and powdered corm (underground storage organ) of the konjac plant (Amorphophallus konjac, family Araceae), cultivated in China, Japan and Southeast Asia. The konjac corm contains approximately 40–60% konjac glucomannan on a dry basis, along with starch, protein, alkaloids (konjac alkaloid) and water. Chemical structure: a water-soluble β-1,4-linked glucomannan with a mannose:glucose ratio of approximately 1.6:1; about 1 in every 19 main-chain sugar units carries a β-1,3-linked branch point. The molecular weight is very high (~200,000–2,000,000 Da), which accounts for its exceptional viscosity — konjac gum forms the most viscous solutions of any known food polysaccharide at equivalent concentration (0.1% solution has viscosity >10,000 cP). KGM gels are thermoreversible unless treated with alkali (calcium hydroxide — creating irreversible konnyaku gels used in traditional Japanese tofu-like foods). Food applications: thickener and gelling agent in jelly products, noodles (shirataki noodles — essentially pure konjac, nearly zero calories), confectionery, meat products, gluten-free foods and dietary supplements. Dietary supplement use: konjac/glucomannan is one of the best-evidenced dietary supplements for weight management, cholesterol reduction and blood glucose control — 3–4 g/day before meals forms a viscous gel in the stomach, increasing satiety, slowing gastric emptying and reducing postprandial glucose spikes. EU authorised a health claim: 'Konjac glucomannan contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels.' FDA evaluating glucomannan health claims. Safety: safe for adults with adequate water (minimum 250 mL per dose of powder supplement); without water, the powder can expand and cause oesophageal or intestinal obstruction — several choking/obstruction cases reported with high-dose konjac supplements and some confectionery products. EU banned mini cup jelly products containing konjac due to choking hazard. ADI: not specified (JECFA). EU (E425), FSSAI and CODEX approved as food additive. Standard konjac gum at normal food additive concentrations is safe.""",
    'e425': 'E425/INS 425 is Konjac Gum (Glucomannan), a highly viscous natural thickener. Also used as a dietary fibre supplement for weight management.',
    'tragacanth': """Tragacanth (E413/INS 413 / gum tragacanth / goat's thorn gum) is one of the oldest natural food gums, harvested from the dried exudate that oozes from incisions made in the stems and branches of shrubby legumes of the genus Astragalus (particularly A. gummifer, A. microcephalus and related species), growing in the semi-arid mountain regions of Iran, Turkey, Syria and Afghanistan. Iran supplies most of the world's tragacanth. The dried exudate is hand-sorted and sold as ribbon-shaped flakes or processed into powder. Chemical composition: tragacanth is a complex heterogeneous polysaccharide consisting of two fractions: (1) Tragacanthin (~30–40%) — a highly branched, water-soluble arabinoxylan that hydrates readily in cold water to form a viscous colloidal sol; (2) Bassorin (~60–70%) — a methylated and acetylated pectin-like polymer, insoluble but water-absorbing — swells to form a gel. The unique combination of these two fractions gives tragacanth exceptional thickening properties across a wide pH range (2–10), which is its defining advantage over many other gums — it functions in highly acidic environments where other gums lose viscosity. Also notable for very high stability at elevated temperatures and excellent emulsion stability. Applications: confectionery (prevents crystallisation and provides smooth texture in cream and fondant fillings; essential in traditional 'gum tragacanth paste' used by pastry artists for modelling decorations); salad dressings and condiments (acid-stable thickener); pharmaceutical preparations (suspending agent for heavy insoluble powders in liquid medicines; tablet binder; dental impression paste); ice cream; baby foods; and cosmetics (thickener in creams). Gradually being replaced by xanthan gum in industrial applications due to xanthan's greater consistency and lower cost. Allergen note: tragacanth is a documented IgE-mediated allergen — it can cause occupational asthma in food and pharmaceutical workers exposed to dust; contact urticaria and anaphylaxis are documented. Cross-reactivity with other plant gums possible. Must be declared as an allergen in some regulations. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E413), FSSAI and CODEX. Generally safe at food use levels for non-sensitised individuals.""",
    'e413': 'E413/INS 413 is Tragacanth Gum, a natural stabiliser from Astragalus shrub. Used in confectionery and pharmaceuticals.',
    'modified potato starch': """Modified potato starch (E1404–1442 range, source: potato / Solanum tuberosum) refers to potato starch that has been subjected to physical, enzymatic or chemical modification to improve its functional properties beyond those of native potato starch. Native potato starch is particularly distinctive — it gelatinises at a relatively low temperature (56–67°C), produces very high peak viscosity pastes, has an extremely long potato flavour note in high concentrations, and exhibits significant retrogradation (syneresis) on cooling. The large granule size (~15–110 μm, largest of commercial food starches) and high phosphate monoester content of native potato starch contribute to its unique rheological properties. Modification types applied to potato starch: cross-linking (E1412/E1413/E1414) — reduces the extreme viscosity peak and improves stability under heat, acid and shear; substitution/hydroxypropylation (E1440) — reduces retrogradation and improves freeze-thaw stability; dual modification (E1442) — the most common industrial form; oxidisation (E1404) — reduces molecular weight, improves whiteness and clarity. Modified potato starch is particularly valued for: its clean, neutral flavour (especially when waxy potato varieties are used); high clarity pastes; and gel texture superior to corn starch in some applications. Used as a thickener and stabiliser in: ready meals and soups; sauces; baby foods (due to potato's natural potato flavour and clean profile); snack foods (potato crisps — modified potato starch binds reformed potato products); bakery. Generally digestible to glucose (not dietary fibre at standard modification levels). No allergen concerns. Potato is not a regulated major allergen in most jurisdictions. ADI: not specified for approved modification types (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA and CODEX for respective modification codes.""",
    'oxidised starch': """Oxidised starch (E1404/INS 1404) is produced by treating native starch (from corn, potato, wheat or tapioca) with a mild oxidising agent — most commonly sodium hypochlorite (bleach solution) or hydrogen peroxide under controlled conditions of pH, temperature and time. Oxidation introduces carbonyl (aldehyde, ketone) and carboxyl (carboxylic acid) groups along the starch chains, and also causes partial depolymerisation (reduces molecular weight). These chemical changes produce functional properties distinct from native starch and from other modified starches: (1) reduced viscosity and gelatinisation temperature — the carbonyl and carboxyl groups disrupt the ordered crystalline structure, allowing the starch to swell and gelatinise at lower temperatures; (2) improved whiteness/brightness — bleaching action; (3) improved paste clarity — oxidised starch gels are clearer than native starch; (4) reduced tendency to retrograde — carbonyl groups disrupt the starch chain reassociation; (5) improved film-forming properties — used extensively in paper and textile sizing, which is historically the primary industrial use. Food applications: oxidised starch is used in food systems primarily for its clarity, low viscosity and film-forming properties — in sugar-panned confectionery (as a moisture barrier and crystallisation inhibitor in the sugar coating process); in soups and sauces requiring a clear, fluid consistency; in baked goods and breakfast cereals. The level of oxidation (characterised by hypochlorite concentration and treatment conditions) is regulated to ensure the starch retains food-grade safety: maximum allowed active chlorine treatment is regulated by CODEX and EU. Metabolised: digested to glucose as regular starch. The introduced carboxyl groups are released as carboxylic acids during digestion — no safety concern at permitted levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1404), FDA and CODEX at regulated treatment levels.""",
    'e1404': 'E1404/INS 1404 is Oxidised Starch, a modified starch with reduced viscosity used in certain food applications.',
    'phosphoric acid': 'Phosphoric acid (E338/INS 338) is a mineral acid used to acidify beverages — especially colas — giving them their characteristic sharp taste. Also used in cheese processing. May affect tooth enamel and bone density with chronic high consumption.',
    'sodium hexametaphosphate': """Sodium hexametaphosphate (SHMP / E452/INS 452 / Calgon) is a polyphosphate salt — a cyclic or linear chain of six phosphate units — produced by heating sodium dihydrogen phosphate to high temperatures. A highly versatile sequestrant, emulsifying salt and texture modifier. Functions: chelates divalent metal ions (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺) — this is its primary mechanism; forms stable complexes that prevent these ions from causing turbidity, rancidity or precipitation; emulsifying salt in processed cheese — disperses casein micelles and fat globules creating a smooth, homogeneous melted texture (used alongside sodium citrate); prevents protein coagulation in canned seafood (prevents fish muscle proteins from coagulating on heat processing, maintaining texture); water softener in beverages; dispersant in seafood glazes (prevents oxidation); texture modifier in surimi (imitation crab), fish paste and minced fish products. Health concern: phosphate, unlike most food additives, is a biologically active molecule. High dietary phosphate intake is associated with increased cardiovascular disease risk and accelerated chronic kidney disease progression in individuals with pre-existing renal impairment. The kidneys regulate phosphate excretion; in CKD, phosphate accumulates and causes secondary hyperparathyroidism, vascular calcification. Processed food phosphate additives are absorbed at ~90% vs ~50% for organic food phosphate. ADI: not specified, but EFSA has noted cumulative phosphate intake from additives as a concern. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E452), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e452': 'E452/INS 452 is Sodium Hexametaphosphate, an emulsifier and sequestrant used in processed cheese and seafood products.',
    'potassium chloride': """Potassium chloride (KCl / E508/INS 508) — see the primary 'potassium chloride' entry above for comprehensive details on chemistry, physiology, health considerations and regulatory status. Used as a sodium-reduced salt substitute in low-sodium and reduced-sodium food products. Provides salty taste with potassium instead of sodium. Important for consumers monitoring sodium intake; also provides the essential mineral potassium (important for blood pressure and cardiovascular health — dietary potassium counteracts the blood pressure-raising effect of sodium). Critical caution for people with chronic kidney disease, those on potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors or ARBs — excess potassium can cause dangerous hyperkalaemia. FSSAI, EU (E508), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'acetylated starch': """Acetylated starch (E1420/INS 1420 / starch acetate) — see the primary 'acetylated starch' entry above for full chemical description, modification mechanism and regulatory details. A mildly modified starch produced by treating native starch with acetic anhydride to introduce acetyl groups (–OCOCH₃), reducing retrogradation (starch crystallisation), lowering gelatinisation temperature and improving paste clarity and freeze-thaw stability compared to native starch. Used as a thickener and stabiliser in sauces, salad dressings, dairy desserts and bakery products. Not classified as dietary fibre — digested normally to glucose. FSSAI, EU (E1420), FDA and CODEX approved. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Safe.""",
    'hydroxypropyl starch e1440': """Hydroxypropyl starch (E1440/INS 1440) — see 'hydroxypropyl starch' for full description. This is a chemically modified food starch produced by reacting native starch with propylene oxide to introduce hydroxypropyl groups that prevent retrogradation and provide outstanding freeze-thaw stability. A single modification (hydroxypropylation only, not cross-linked), making it less heat-shear stable than E1442 (hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate) but very effective for simple freeze-thaw applications. Used in frozen soups, dairy desserts, fruit preparations and salad dressings. EFSA re-evaluated in 2017 and confirmed ADI 'not specified.' Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1440), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'distarch phosphate': """Distarch phosphate (E1412/INS 1412 / cross-linked starch) is a chemically modified food starch produced by treating native starch (from corn, potato, wheat or tapioca) with phosphoric acid or phosphorus oxychloride, creating phosphate diester bonds that crosslink adjacent starch chains. This crosslinking is the defining modification: instead of individual starch granule chains swelling and breaking apart during cooking (leading to viscosity loss and texture breakdown), crosslinked starch chains are held together by the phosphate bridges, dramatically improving structural stability. The functional benefits: (1) heat stability — native starch pastes break down and thin out on prolonged heating; distarch phosphate maintains viscosity even after extended boiling or autoclaving (retort/sterilisation conditions in canned soups and sauces); (2) shear stability — withstands vigorous mixing, pumping and high-pressure processing without thinning; critical in continuous manufacturing; (3) acid stability — native starch pastes thin rapidly at low pH; distarch phosphate maintains viscosity in acidic products (tomato sauce, fruit fillings, vinegar-containing products); (4) freeze-thaw stability — without additional hydroxypropylation, distarch phosphate is less freeze-thaw stable than E1442 (hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate), but the crosslinking still improves stability compared to native starch. Used in: canned soups and stews, fruit pie fillings, gravies, sauces, ketchup, instant dry mixes, puddings and baby food. Distinguished from hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate (E1442): E1412 has only crosslinking modification; E1442 has both crosslinking (phosphate ester) and stabilisation (hydroxypropyl ether) modifications, giving superior freeze-thaw stability. In the body: distarch phosphate is hydrolysed by amylases in the gut to glucose and short dextrins — fully digestible; not a dietary fibre. No significant residual phosphate from crosslinking bonds at food use levels. Metabolism: converted to glucose. No significant allergen concerns from the starch itself (though gluten-containing cereals used as starch source could present concerns for coeliac patients — verify source starch). ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1412), FDA (GRAS for specific starches) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups including children and the elderly.""",
    'e1412': 'E1412/INS 1412 is Distarch Phosphate, a cross-linked modified starch thickener with high stability. Used in soups and canned foods.',


    # ── INS/E-Number entries added for searchability by number ────────────────

    # --- Raising Agents / Leavening Agents ---
    'e500': 'E500 / INS 500 — Sodium Carbonates. A group of leavening and pH-adjusting agents used in baking, confectionery and biscuits. Sodium bicarbonate (500ii) is common baking soda; sodium carbonate (500i) is washing soda. Permitted by FSSAI and Codex as a safe food additive.',
    'e500i': 'E500(i) / INS 500(i) — Sodium Carbonate (soda ash / washing soda). Used as a leavening agent, pH adjuster and firming agent in baked goods, cocoa powder and noodles. Safe at permitted food levels.',
    'e500ii': 'E500(ii) / INS 500(ii) — Sodium Bicarbonate (baking soda). The most widely used leavening agent in baking — reacts with acidic ingredients to produce CO₂ bubbles that make cakes, cookies and bread rise. Safe; the body handles it like ordinary sodium and CO₂.',
    'e500iii': 'E500(iii) / INS 500(iii) — Sodium Sesquicarbonate. A mixture of sodium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate used as a leavening and pH-control agent. Safe at food-use levels.',
    'e501': 'E501 / INS 501 — Potassium Carbonates. Used as leavening agents and acidity regulators in biscuits, cocoa and baked goods. Provide a milder alkalinity than sodium carbonates.',
    'e501i': 'E501(i) / INS 501(i) — Potassium Carbonate. Used as a leavening agent and pH adjuster in baked goods and cocoa products. Generally safe.',
    'e501ii': 'E501(ii) / INS 501(ii) — Potassium Bicarbonate. A leavening agent used in baking as a sodium-free alternative to baking soda. Safe.',
    'e503': 'E503 / INS 503 — Ammonium Carbonates. Leavening agents used in dry biscuits, crackers and cookies. At baking temperatures they fully decompose to CO₂, ammonia and water — no ammonia remains in the finished product.',
    'e503i': 'E503(i) / INS 503(i) — Ammonium Carbonate (bakers ammonia). Traditional leavening agent for crisp baked goods. Fully decomposes during baking; no ammonia residue in finished product.',
    'e503ii': 'E503(ii) / INS 503(ii) — Ammonium Bicarbonate. A leavening agent used in dry biscuits, crackers and cookies. Decomposes completely at baking temperatures into CO₂, water and ammonia gas — no ammonia remains in the baked product.',
    'e504': 'E504 / INS 504 — Magnesium Carbonates. Used as an anti-caking agent, colour retention agent and leavening agent. Also used as a dietary magnesium supplement.',
    'e504i': 'E504(i) / INS 504(i) — Magnesium Carbonate. Anti-caking and leavening agent; also used as a dietary magnesium source.',
    'e504ii': 'E504(ii) / INS 504(ii) — Magnesium Hydroxide Carbonate (magnesite). Used as an anti-caking agent and leavening agent. Safe.',
    'e450': 'E450 / INS 450 — Diphosphates (Pyrophosphates). A group of raising agents and emulsifying salts used in baking powder, processed meats and processed cheese. The most common is sodium acid pyrophosphate (SAPP/E450i). Excessive phosphate intake is a concern for kidney health.',
    'e450i': 'E450(i) / INS 450(i) — Disodium Diphosphate (SAPP). Used in baking powder as a slow-acting leavening acid, and in processed meats and seafood to retain moisture.',
    'e451': 'E451 / INS 451 — Triphosphates (Tripolyphosphates). Used as emulsifying salts in processed cheese and as water-retention agents in seafood and processed meats. High phosphate intake may affect kidney function.',
    'e451i': 'E451(i) / INS 451(i) — Pentasodium Triphosphate (STPP). Used in processed cheese, seafood and meat products to retain moisture and improve texture.',
    'e452': 'E452 / INS 452 — Polyphosphates. Used as emulsifying salts in processed cheese and as stabilisers and moisture-retention agents in meat and seafood products.',

    # --- Emulsifiers ---
    'e322': 'E322 / INS 322 — Lecithins. Natural emulsifiers extracted from soybean oil, sunflower or egg yolk. Widely used in chocolate, bread, margarine and baked goods to blend fat and water smoothly. The most commonly used food emulsifier globally. Important soy allergen if soy-derived.',
    'e322i': 'E322(i) / INS 322(i) — Lecithin. The most widely used natural emulsifier, typically from soybean or sunflower. Used in chocolate, baked goods, margarine and confectionery. Natural and well-tolerated; note soy allergen if soy-sourced.',
    'e322ii': 'E322(ii) / INS 322(ii) — Hydroxylated Lecithin. Chemically modified lecithin with improved emulsifying efficiency. Used in bread, margarine and confectionery. Same allergen profile as standard lecithin.',
    'e471': 'E471 / INS 471 — Mono- and Diglycerides of Fatty Acids. Emulsifiers produced from glycerol and fatty acids (from vegetable or animal fats). Widely used in bread, margarine, ice cream and baked goods to improve texture, volume and shelf life. May contain residual trans-fatty acids if made from partially hydrogenated oils; also contains glycidol fatty acid esters (GFAEs) at trace levels — the European Food Safety Authority has reviewed this and set limits.',
    'e472a': 'E472a / INS 472a — Acetic Acid Esters of Mono- and Diglycerides (ACETEM). Emulsifier used in bread and baked goods. Derived from E471 esterified with acetic acid. Generally safe.',
    'e472b': 'E472b / INS 472b — Lactic Acid Esters of Mono- and Diglycerides (LACTEM). Emulsifier used in bread, whipped products and dairy analogs. Improves aeration and shelf life. Generally safe.',
    'e472c': 'E472c / INS 472c — Citric Acid Esters of Mono- and Diglycerides (CITREM). Emulsifier used in margarine and processed foods as an antioxidant emulsifier. Generally safe.',
    'e472e': 'E472e / INS 472e — Diacetyl Tartaric Acid Esters of Mono- and Diglycerides (DATEM). A powerful dough emulsifier and gluten network strengthener used in bread, rolls and wraps to improve volume and crumb structure. Widely used in commercial bread-making. Generally safe.',
    'e473': 'E473 / INS 473 — Sucrose Esters of Fatty Acids. Emulsifiers made from sucrose and fatty acids, used in coffee creamers, baked goods and dairy products. Generally safe.',
    'e475': 'E475 / INS 475 — Polyglycerol Esters of Fatty Acids. Emulsifiers used in low-fat spreads, margarine and baked goods. Generally recognised as safe.',
    'e476': 'E476 / INS 476 — Polyglycerol Polyricinoleate (PGPR). A synthetic emulsifier made from glycerol and castor oil, primarily used in chocolate to improve flow and reduce cocoa butter usage. Generally safe at permitted levels.',
    'e481': 'E481 / INS 481 — Sodium Stearoyl Lactylate (SSL). An emulsifier and dough conditioner made from stearic acid and lactic acid. Strengthens gluten networks in bread, improves volume and extends shelf life. Widely used in commercial bread. Generally safe.',
    'e482': 'E482 / INS 482 — Calcium Stearoyl Lactylate (CSL). Similar to SSL but calcium-based. Used as a dough conditioner in bread and as an emulsifier in coffee whiteners and whipped toppings. Generally safe.',
    'e491': 'E491 / INS 491 — Sorbitan Monostearate (Span 60). An emulsifier used in baked goods, confectionery fillings and yeast-raised products. Generally safe.',
    'e442': 'E442 / INS 442 — Ammonium Phosphatides. An emulsifier from rapeseed oil used in chocolate as a lecithin alternative to improve flow properties. Generally safe.',

    # --- Acidity Regulators / Acidulants ---
    'e260': 'E260 / INS 260 — Acetic Acid (Vinegar acid). The acid responsible for the sour taste and preserving properties of vinegar. Used as an acidulant and preservative in pickles, sauces, condiments and baked goods. Safe.',
    'e270': 'E270 / INS 270 — Lactic Acid. A naturally occurring organic acid produced by fermentation, present in yoghurt, cheese, sauerkraut and sourdough. Used as an acidulant, pH adjuster and preservative in a wide range of foods. Safe.',
    'e296': 'E296 / INS 296 — Malic Acid. A naturally occurring fruit acid found in apples, pears and cherries. Used as an acidulant to give a smooth, tart flavour in beverages, confectionery and bakery products. Safe.',
    'e297': 'E297 / INS 297 — Fumaric Acid. A dicarboxylic acid used as an acidulant and leavening acid in baked goods, beverages and confectionery. Used as a natural alternative to citric acid. Safe.',
    'e325': 'E325 / INS 325 — Sodium Lactate. The sodium salt of lactic acid, used as a humectant, acidity regulator and preservative in meat products, confectionery and baked goods. Generally safe.',
    'e327': 'E327 / INS 327 — Calcium Lactate. The calcium salt of lactic acid, used as a firming agent, acidity regulator and calcium fortification agent. Generally safe.',
    'e330': 'E330 / INS 330 — Citric Acid. The most widely used acidulant in the food industry, naturally found in citrus fruits. Used in beverages, confectionery, jams and processed foods to add tartness and act as a pH adjuster and preservative. Safe.',
    'e331': 'E331 / INS 331 — Sodium Citrates. Sodium salts of citric acid used as acidity regulators, emulsifying salts and preservatives in beverages, processed cheese, confectionery and sausages. Safe.',
    'e332': 'E332 / INS 332 — Potassium Citrates. Potassium salts of citric acid used as acidity regulators and sequestrants in beverages and confectionery. Safe.',
    'e333': 'E333 / INS 333 — Calcium Citrates. Calcium salts of citric acid used as acidity regulators, firming agents and calcium supplements. Safe.',
    'e334': 'E334 / INS 334 — Tartaric Acid. A naturally occurring acid from grapes and other fruits, used as an acidulant and antioxidant in baking powder, wine, confectionery and beverages. Safe.',
    'e335': 'E335 / INS 335 — Sodium Tartrates. Sodium salts of tartaric acid used as acidity regulators and stabilisers in confectionery and processed foods. Safe.',
    'e336': 'E336 / INS 336 — Potassium Tartrates (Cream of Tartar). Potassium salt of tartaric acid used as a leavening agent with baking soda, a stabiliser for whipped egg whites and a wine stabiliser. Safe.',
    'e337': 'E337 / INS 337 — Potassium Sodium Tartrate (Rochelle Salt). A mixed tartrate salt used as an acidity regulator and sequestrant in confectionery and meat products. Safe.',
    'e339': 'E339 / INS 339 — Sodium Phosphates. A group of sodium salts of phosphoric acid used as emulsifying salts, acidity regulators and nutrient supplements. Used in processed cheese, meat products and cereals. High phosphate intake may affect kidney health with chronic excess.',
    'e340': 'E340 / INS 340 — Potassium Phosphates. Potassium salts of phosphoric acid used as acidity regulators, emulsifying salts and potassium supplements. Used in processed cheese and instant beverages.',
    'e341': 'E341 / INS 341 — Calcium Phosphates. Calcium salts of phosphoric acid used as a raising agent, firming agent, anti-caking agent and calcium supplement. Found in baking powder, flour treatment and cereals. Safe.',
    'e341i': 'E341(i) / INS 341(i) — Monocalcium Phosphate (MCP). A fast-acting leavening acid used in baking powder. Reacts with sodium bicarbonate during mixing to produce CO₂. Used in cakes, muffins and self-raising flour.',
    'e341ii': 'E341(ii) / INS 341(ii) — Dicalcium Phosphate (DCP). Used as a leavening agent, calcium supplement and anti-caking agent in flour and cereals. Safe.',
    'e341iii': 'E341(iii) / INS 341(iii) — Tricalcium Phosphate (TCP). Used as an anti-caking agent, calcium fortification agent and leavening agent in powdered foods, cereals and supplements. Safe.',

    # --- Antioxidants ---
    'e300': 'E300 / INS 300 — Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C). The most widely used food antioxidant — prevents browning, rancidity and vitamin degradation. Used in fruit juices, meat products, bread and cereals. Same as supplemental Vitamin C; safe and beneficial.',
    'e301': 'E301 / INS 301 — Sodium Ascorbate. The sodium salt of Vitamin C, used as an antioxidant preservative in processed meats, cured meats and bakery products. Provides the same antioxidant action as ascorbic acid. Safe.',
    'e302': 'E302 / INS 302 — Calcium Ascorbate. The calcium salt of Vitamin C, used as an antioxidant and calcium supplement. Safe.',
    'e304': 'E304 / INS 304 — Ascorbyl Palmitate / Ascorbyl Stearate. Fat-soluble esters of Vitamin C used to protect oils and fat-containing foods from oxidation. Used in frying oils, margarines and infant formula. Safe.',
    'e306': 'E306 / INS 306 — Mixed Tocopherols (Natural Vitamin E). A natural antioxidant extract from vegetable oils containing alpha, beta, gamma and delta tocopherols. Used to protect oils and fat-containing foods. Safe.',
    'e307': 'E307 / INS 307 — Alpha-Tocopherol (Vitamin E). The most biologically active form of Vitamin E, used as a natural antioxidant in oils, margarines and infant formula. Safe and nutritionally beneficial.',
    'e308': 'E308 / INS 308 — Gamma-Tocopherol. A form of Vitamin E used as an antioxidant in edible fats and oils. Safe.',
    'e309': 'E309 / INS 309 — Delta-Tocopherol. A form of Vitamin E used as an antioxidant. Safe.',
    'e315': 'E315 / INS 315 — Erythorbic Acid (Isoascorbic Acid). A stereoisomer of Vitamin C used as an antioxidant in cured meats and beverages. Not equivalent to Vitamin C nutritionally, but effective as an antioxidant preservative. Safe.',
    'e316': 'E316 / INS 316 — Sodium Erythorbate. The sodium salt of erythorbic acid, widely used in hot dogs, sausages and cured meats to preserve colour and prevent rancidity. Safe.',

    # --- Sweeteners (Sugar Alcohols and Novel) ---
    'e420': 'E420 / INS 420 — Sorbitol. A naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in fruits, also produced commercially. Used as a low-calorie sweetener, humectant and bulking agent in sugar-free confectionery, diabetic foods and cosmetics. May cause digestive upset at high doses (>50g). About 60% as sweet as sugar.',
    'e420i': 'E420(i) / INS 420(i) — Sorbitol. Sugar alcohol sweetener and humectant. See E420.',
    'e421': 'E421 / INS 421 — Mannitol. A naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in mushrooms, seaweed and sweet potatoes. Used as a low-calorie sweetener and anti-caking agent in sugar-free confectionery. Poorly absorbed; may cause laxative effect at high doses.',
    'e953': 'E953 / INS 953 — Isomalt. A sugar substitute derived from sucrose, approximately half as sweet. Used in sugar-free confectionery, chewing gum and diabetic foods. Low glycaemic index; may cause digestive discomfort in large quantities.',
    'e960': 'E960 / INS 960 — Steviol Glycosides (Stevia). Natural zero-calorie sweeteners extracted from the stevia plant (Stevia rebaudiana), 200–400 times sweeter than sugar. Used in beverages, dairy products and tabletop sweeteners. Approved by FSSAI, FDA and EU. Generally well-tolerated.',
    'e960a': 'E960a / INS 960a — Steviol Glycosides from Stevia. Natural high-intensity sweetener from stevia leaves. Zero calorie. Generally safe.',
    'e961': 'E961 / INS 961 — Neotame. A synthetic high-intensity sweetener (7,000–13,000 times sweeter than sugar) derived from aspartame but metabolised differently — safe for people with phenylketonuria. Used in tiny amounts in beverages and processed foods.',
    'e962': 'E962 / INS 962 — Aspartame-Acesulfame Salt. A blend of aspartame and acesulfame potassium in a 2:1 ratio, providing a more sugar-like taste profile than either alone. Used in beverages and sugar-free products. Contains phenylalanine (PKU warning).',
    'e965': 'E965 / INS 965 — Maltitol. A sugar alcohol approximately 75–90% as sweet as sugar, derived from maltose. Used in sugar-free chocolates, baked goods and confectionery. Low glycaemic impact; may cause laxative effects at high intakes.',
    'e966': 'E966 / INS 966 — Lactitol. A sugar alcohol made from lactose (milk sugar), approximately 40% as sweet as sugar. Used in sugar-free confectionery and as a bulk laxative in pharmaceutical use. Contains lactose backbone — relevant for lactose-intolerant individuals.',
    'e967': 'E967 / INS 967 — Xylitol. A naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in birch trees, plums and corn cobs. Approximately as sweet as sugar with 40% fewer calories. Used in sugar-free gum, confectionery and dental products — proven to reduce tooth decay. High doses cause laxative effects.',
    'e968': 'E968 / INS 968 — Erythritol. A naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in fermented foods. Zero calories, approximately 60–70% as sweet as sugar. Does not spike blood glucose or insulin. Best tolerated of all sugar alcohols; rarely causes digestive upset.',

    # --- Food Colours ---
    'e100': 'E100 / INS 100 — Curcumin. A natural bright-yellow pigment derived from turmeric (Curcuma longa) rhizomes. Used to colour mustard, dairy products, confectionery and sauces. Also used as a dietary supplement for its anti-inflammatory properties. Safe.',
    'e100i': 'E100(i) / INS 100(i) — Curcumin. Natural yellow pigment from turmeric. Permitted food colour. Safe.',
    'e101': 'E101 / INS 101 — Riboflavin (Vitamin B2). A natural yellow-orange pigment used as a food colour and nutritional supplement. Found naturally in milk, eggs, meat and leafy greens. Used to colour pasta, bread and dairy products. Safe and nutritionally beneficial.',
    'e101i': 'E101(i) / INS 101(i) — Riboflavin. Natural Vitamin B2 used as a yellow food colour and nutritional supplement. Safe.',
    'e120': 'E120 / INS 120 — Cochineal / Carmine / Carminic Acid. A red dye derived from dried female cochineal insects (Dactylopius coccus). Used to colour yoghurts, juices, confectionery and cosmetics. Animal-derived — not vegan. Can cause severe allergic reactions including anaphylaxis in susceptible individuals; FDA mandates explicit labelling.',
    'e140': 'E140 / INS 140 — Chlorophylls and Chlorophyllins are the natural green pigments responsible for the colour of all photosynthetic plants. There are two main forms: Chlorophyll a (blue-green) and Chlorophyll b (yellow-green), both porphyrin ring structures with a central magnesium atom and a phytol tail. For food colouring, chlorophylls are extracted from plant material (nettles, spinach, grass, alfalfa, lucerne) using solvents. Two main food-additive forms: (1) Chlorophylls (E140) — the natural lipid-soluble pigment; used in oils, fats, waxes and fat-based confectionery; somewhat less stable and gives a more muted, natural green; (2) Chlorophyllins (E140(ii)) — water-soluble sodium/potassium salts of hydrolysed chlorophylls; used in water-based products (beverages, sauces, jelly confectionery); brighter green but less "natural." Stability limitations: chlorophylls are sensitive to heat, acid and light — above pH 5 and at high temperatures, the central magnesium can be displaced by hydrogen ions (pheophytisation), turning the colour from bright green to olive drab brown. Copper complexes (E141) were developed to overcome this stability problem. Nutritional aspects: dietary chlorophylls have antioxidant properties; chlorophyllins have been studied for DNA protection (antimutagenic) effects in animal models. Safe; ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e141': 'E141 / INS 141 — Copper Complexes of Chlorophylls / Chlorophyllins. Copper-stabilised versions of chlorophyll used to create a more stable, brighter green colour in foods. Approved by FSSAI and EU. Safe at permitted levels.',
    'e142': 'E142 / INS 142 — Green S (Lissamine Green). A synthetic green dye used in canned vegetables, mint jelly and some confectionery. Banned in USA, Canada, Japan and Australia. Approved in EU and India with restrictions.',
    'e150a': 'E150a / INS 150a — Plain Caramel (Caramel I). Produced by heating carbohydrates without acids or alkalis. Used in spirits, vinegar and confectionery. Considered the safest class of caramel colour.',
    'e150b': 'E150b / INS 150b — Caustic Sulfite Caramel (Caramel II). Produced by reacting carbohydrates with sulfite compounds. Used in spirits. Rare in other foods.',
    'e150c': 'E150c / INS 150c — Ammonia Caramel (Caramel III). Produced by reacting carbohydrates with ammonium compounds. Used in beer and soy sauce. Contains 2-methylimidazole and 4-methylimidazole (4-MEI) as trace by-products — 4-MEI is listed as a possible carcinogen by IARC (Group 2B); California Prop 65 warning required.',
    'e150d': 'E150d / INS 150d — Sulfite Ammonia Caramel (Caramel IV). Produced with both sulfite and ammonium compounds. The most widely used caramel colour — in colas, soy sauce, beer and baked goods. Contains 4-MEI (possible carcinogen, IARC Group 2B); Coca-Cola reformulated in some markets due to Prop 65. FSSAI permits it; California requires a cancer warning label.',
    'e151': 'E151 / INS 151 — Brilliant Black BN (Black PN). A synthetic black azo dye used to colour confectionery, sauces and condiments. Banned in USA, Canada, Japan and Australia. Carries the EU hyperactivity warning label. Approved in EU and India with restrictions.',
    'e160b': 'E160b / INS 160b — Annatto (Bixin / Norbixin). A natural orange-yellow colour derived from the seeds of the annatto tree (Bixa orellana). Widely used in cheeses, butter, margarines, snacks and ice cream. Generally safe; occasional allergic reactions reported. No hyperactivity concerns.',
    'e160c': 'E160c / INS 160c — Paprika Extract / Capsanthin / Capsorubin. Natural orange-red colour from dried red peppers. Used in sausages, processed meats, sauces, snacks and dairy products. Safe.',
    'e162': 'E162 / INS 162 — Beetroot Red (Betanin). Natural red pigment extracted from red beetroot. Used in yoghurts, ice cream, confectionery and beverages. Safe. Not stable to heat; colour may fade in cooking.',
    'e163': 'E163 / INS 163 — Anthocyanins are a class of over 700 naturally occurring water-soluble plant pigments belonging to the flavonoid family, responsible for the red, purple and blue colours of berries (blueberries, blackberries, strawberries, cranberries), red/purple grapes, red cabbage, hibiscus flowers, elderberries, purple sweet potato, purple corn and numerous other plant foods. Chemically: glycosides of anthocyanidins (aglycones) — the most common being cyanidin, pelargonidin, delphinidin, malvidin, peonidin and petunidin. The colour is pH-dependent: red at low pH (<3), purple/mauve at neutral, blue/green at alkaline pH (>7) — this pH sensitivity is the principal challenge in formulating with anthocyanins. Commercial sources for food colouring: grape skin extract (concentrated from wine production by-products, the most common source), elderberry concentrate, red cabbage extract, black carrot extract, purple sweet potato extract. Applications: beverages, fruit yogurts, confectionery, jams, juices and dairy products. Health significance: anthocyanins are among the most potent dietary antioxidants — they scavenge free radicals, inhibit LDL oxidation, reduce inflammation, improve endothelial function and have been linked in epidemiological studies with reduced cardiovascular disease risk, improved cognitive function and reduced type 2 diabetes incidence. The effective dietary dose in human studies ranges from 50–600 mg/day — most food-additive uses provide moderate amounts. Safe; ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e172': 'E172 / INS 172 — Iron Oxides and Iron Hydroxides. Inorganic colouring agents (yellow, red, black) used in sugar coatings, chocolate casings, olives and pharmaceuticals. Also provide a small amount of dietary iron. Safe at food-use levels.',

    # --- Preservatives (additional) ---
    'e200': 'E200 / INS 200 — Sorbic Acid. A natural organic acid originally isolated from rowan berries, now produced synthetically. The parent compound of the sorbate preservatives (E202, E203). Used to inhibit mould and yeast in cheese, wine, dried fruits and baked goods. Safe.',
    'e201': 'E201 / INS 201 — Sodium Sorbate. The sodium salt of sorbic acid, used as a preservative. Inhibits mould and yeast in foods and beverages. Generally safe at permitted levels.',
    'e203': 'E203 / INS 203 — Calcium Sorbate. The calcium salt of sorbic acid, used as a preservative to inhibit mould and yeast. Provides a calcium source alongside the preservative function. Safe.',
    'e210': 'E210 / INS 210 — Benzoic Acid. The acid form of sodium benzoate (E211). Naturally present in cranberries, cinnamon and prunes. Used as a preservative in acidic foods and beverages. Same benzene-formation concern as E211 when combined with Vitamin C.',
    'e212': 'E212 / INS 212 — Potassium Benzoate. The potassium salt of benzoic acid, used as an antimicrobial preservative in foods and beverages. Same benzene-formation risk as sodium benzoate (E211) in combination with Vitamin C.',
    'e213': 'E213 / INS 213 — Calcium Benzoate. The calcium salt of benzoic acid used as a preservative. Similar properties and concerns as sodium benzoate (E211).',
    'e218': 'E218 / INS 218 — Methyl p-Hydroxybenzoate (Methylparaben). The INS/E-number code for methylparaben, a widely used preservative in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and foods. Hormone-disrupting paraben — same concerns as "methylparaben".',
    'e219': 'E219 / INS 219 — Sodium Methyl p-Hydroxybenzoate (Sodium Methylparaben). Sodium salt of methylparaben used as a preservative in cosmetics and some food products. Same endocrine disruption concerns as methylparaben.',
    'e221': 'E221 / INS 221 — Sodium Sulfite. A sulfite preservative used to prevent browning in dried fruits, potatoes and wines. Same allergen concerns as other sulfites — can trigger asthma attacks; must be declared on labels.',
    'e222': 'E222 / INS 222 — Sodium Bisulfite (Sodium Hydrogen Sulfite). A sulfite preservative used in winemaking, food processing and brewing. Asthma and allergy risk; mandatory label declaration for sulfite content.',
    'e224': 'E224 / INS 224 — Potassium Metabisulfite. A sulfite preservative used in winemaking, brewing and food processing. Same asthma and allergy risks as other sulfites; mandatory declaration.',
    'e225': 'E225 / INS 225 — Potassium Sulfite. A sulfite preservative with the same allergen/asthma profile as other sulfites. Mandatory declaration required.',
    'e234': 'E234 / INS 234 — Nisin. A natural antimicrobial peptide produced by the bacterium Lactococcus lactis during fermentation. Used to prevent growth of pathogenic bacteria (especially Listeria and Clostridium) in processed cheese, dairy products and canned goods. Considered safe.',
    'e235': 'E235 / INS 235 — Natamycin (Pimaricin). A natural antifungal agent produced by Streptomyces natalensis bacteria. Used as a surface treatment on hard cheeses, sausage casings and dried meats to prevent mould growth. Not absorbed through the gut. Safe.',
    'e280': 'E280 / INS 280 — Propionic Acid. A naturally occurring short-chain fatty acid produced during fermentation and digestion. Used as an antifungal preservative in bread, baked goods and dairy products to prevent mould growth. Safe.',
    'e281': 'E281 / INS 281 — Sodium Propionate. The sodium salt of propionic acid, used as an antifungal preservative primarily in bread and baked goods. Generally safe; some links to migraine in sensitive individuals.',
    'e282': 'E282 / INS 282 — Calcium Propionate. The calcium salt of propionic acid — the most widely used bread preservative in commercial baking. Inhibits mould and rope bacteria in bread. Provides small amount of dietary calcium. Generally safe; occasional migraine reports.',
    'e283': 'E283 / INS 283 — Potassium Propionate. The potassium salt of propionic acid used as a preservative in baked goods. Same function and safety profile as calcium propionate.',
    'e290': 'E290 / INS 290 — Carbon Dioxide. The natural gas used in carbonated beverages, beer, wine and modified atmosphere packaging to prevent oxidation and microbial growth. Safe.',

    # --- Thickeners / Stabilisers / Hydrocolloids ---
    'e400': 'E400 / INS 400 — Alginic Acid. A natural polysaccharide extracted from brown seaweed. Used as a thickener and gelling agent in ice cream, salad dressings and pharmaceuticals. Safe.',
    'e401': 'E401 / INS 401 — Sodium Alginate. The sodium salt of alginic acid, widely used as a thickener and gel-former in ice cream, sauces, bakery and restructured foods. Safe.',
    'e402': 'E402 / INS 402 — Potassium Alginate. Potassium salt of alginic acid, similar uses to sodium alginate. Safe.',
    'e404': 'E404 / INS 404 — Calcium Alginate. Calcium salt of alginic acid — forms firmer gels. Used in restructured fish and meat products and controlled-release pharmaceuticals. Safe.',
    'e406': 'E406 / INS 406 — Agar (Agar-agar). A natural gelling agent derived from red algae (primarily Gelidium species), used for centuries in Asian cuisine. Sets at lower concentrations than gelatin. Widely used in desserts, confectionery and as a vegetarian gelatin substitute. Safe.',
    'e410': 'E410 / INS 410 — Locust Bean Gum (Carob Gum). A natural thickener from the seeds of the carob tree. Used in ice cream, cheese, salad dressings and infant formula to improve texture and prevent ice crystal formation. Safe.',
    'e412': 'E412 / INS 412 — Guar Gum. A natural thickener and emulsifier ground from guar bean seeds, primarily grown in India and Pakistan. Widely used in ice cream, dairy products, sauces and gluten-free baked goods. One of the most widely used food hydrocolloids globally. Safe.',
    'e414': 'E414 / INS 414 — Acacia Gum (Gum Arabic). A natural gum from acacia trees, used as a thickener, stabiliser and encapsulant in confectionery, beverages, flavour emulsions and pharmaceutical coating. One of the oldest food additives, used for over 4,000 years. Also a prebiotic fibre. Safe.',
    'e440': 'E440 / INS 440 — Pectins. Natural gelling agents from citrus peel, apple pomace and sugar beet. Used to make jams, jellies and marmalades set; also used in confectionery, dairy and beverages. A source of soluble dietary fibre. Safe and beneficial.',
    'e440i': 'E440(i) / INS 440(i) — Pectin (amidated). Amidated pectin forms gels that are more tolerant to calcium levels and temperature. Used in jams, dairy products and low-sugar preserves. Safe.',
    'e460': 'E460 / INS 460 — Cellulose. The most abundant natural polymer — the structural component of plant cell walls. Used as a filler, anti-caking agent and dietary fibre in processed foods and pharmaceuticals. Not digestible; provides bulk. Safe.',
    'e460i': 'E460(i) / INS 460(i) — Microcrystalline Cellulose (MCC/Avicel). Refined cellulose used as an anti-caking agent, bulking agent, fat replacer and texturiser in processed foods and supplements. Safe.',
    'e461': 'E461 / INS 461 — Methyl Cellulose. A cellulose derivative used as a thickener, stabiliser and fat replacer in foods. Unique property: forms a gel when heated and dissolves when cooled (opposite of gelatin). Used in vegetarian burgers. Safe.',
    'e462': 'E462 / INS 462 — Ethyl Cellulose. A cellulose ether used as a film former and binder in pharmaceutical tablet coating and as an edible packaging material. Safe.',
    'e463': 'E463 / INS 463 — Hydroxypropyl Cellulose (HPC). A cellulose derivative used as a thickener, binder and film former in food products and pharmaceuticals. Safe.',
    'e464': 'E464 / INS 464 — Hydroxypropyl Methyl Cellulose (HPMC). A cellulose derivative used as a thickener, emulsifier and film former in gluten-free bread, sauces and pharmaceuticals. Safe.',
    'e466': 'E466 / INS 466 — Sodium Carboxymethyl Cellulose (CMC / Cellulose Gum). A cellulose derivative used as a thickener, stabiliser and water-retention agent in ice cream, baked goods and beverages. Also used in pharmaceutical tablets and personal care products. Some animal studies at very high doses showed effects; at food-use levels it is safe.',

    # --- Anti-caking Agents ---
    'e530': 'E530 / INS 530 — Magnesium Oxide. Used as an anti-caking agent in cocoa powder and as a dietary magnesium supplement. Safe.',
    'e551': 'E551 / INS 551 — Silicon Dioxide (Silica). An inorganic anti-caking agent used to prevent powdered foods (spices, instant soups, protein powders) from clumping. Not absorbed by the body. Safe. Note: nanoparticle forms are under review by regulators.',
    'e552': 'E552 / INS 552 — Calcium Silicate (CaSiO₃, calcium metasilicate) is an inorganic anti-caking agent — a white, amorphous powder produced by combining calcium oxide (lime) and silicon dioxide (silica). It functions by: (1) absorbing moisture at its enormous surface area, preventing it from bridging between powder particles; (2) providing physical separation between powder particles. Used in: table salt (prevents clumping in humid climates), baking powder, powdered sugar, powdered spices, coffee whitener powder, processed cheese powders and dietary supplement tablets. Also used as a food-grade release agent (prevents bread from sticking to baking tins). Bioavailability: calcium silicate is poorly soluble; it is not a significant dietary calcium source. Silicon from calcium silicate is excreted in urine as silicic acid — silicon is actually an essential micronutrient for bone health in trace amounts, though calcium silicate is not a clinically significant silicon source. No toxicological concerns; not significantly absorbed; no accumulation. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e553a': 'E553a / INS 553(i) and 553(ii) — Magnesium Silicates (synthetic magnesium silicate [553(i)] and trimagnesium disilicate [553(ii)]) are inorganic anti-caking agents — white powders with large surface areas that absorb moisture and prevent clumping in powdered foods. The 553(i) form is a synthetic amorphous silicate; 553(ii) is the tribasic form. Used in: spice blends, baking powder, powdered food mixes, sugar confectionery coatings and pharmaceutical tablet manufacture. The magnesium component is benign — magnesium is an essential mineral and any contribution from the anti-caking agent is modest but not harmful. Amorphous (non-crystalline) magnesium silicates have no asbestos-related concerns (unlike some crystalline forms of related minerals). Distinct from E553b (talc), which is a crystalline magnesium silicate with different structural properties. No significant absorption; no toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e553b': 'E553b / INS 553b — Talc (Magnesium Silicate, Mg₃Si₄O₁₀(OH)₂) is a naturally occurring soft mineral — the softest on the Mohs hardness scale (1). Used as an anti-caking agent and surface coating agent in: confectionery coating (polishing agent for sugar-coated tablets and sweets), chewing gum (anti-stick), rice (surface coating in some Asian rice-polishing practices), dietary supplements and food processing. SAFETY NOTE: the primary concern with talc is asbestos contamination — talc is a silicate mineral that occurs geologically in proximity to asbestiform fibres (tremolite, actinolite asbestos); inadequately separated talc can contain carcinogenic asbestos fibres. Food-grade and cosmetic-grade talc must comply with strict specifications for freedom from detectable asbestos. Historical talcum powder litigation (cosmetic use, not food) has raised public awareness; the FDA has worked to establish formal asbestos testing limits for cosmetic talc. Food-grade talc with confirmed asbestos-free specifications is approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA. ADI: not specified.',
    'e554': 'E554 / INS 554 — Sodium Aluminosilicate (Zeolite) is a synthetic crystalline aluminium silicate — a member of the zeolite mineral family characterised by their regular, porous three-dimensional framework structure that gives them enormous surface area and ion-exchange capacity. Used as an anti-caking agent in table salt, powdered mixes and some spices. The regular microporous structure (molecular-scale channels) absorbs moisture and traps water molecules, preventing particle-to-particle bridges that cause caking. Aluminium note: zeolites contain aluminium in a tetrahedrally coordinated form that is chemically stable and largely insoluble under normal food conditions; the aluminium is not in the ionic form that raises health concerns at high exposure. However, individuals with chronic kidney disease (CKD) should minimise all aluminium-containing food additive exposure, as the kidney is the primary route of aluminium elimination — impaired kidneys allow accumulation. For healthy individuals at food additive concentrations, JECFA and EFSA consider sodium aluminosilicate safe. ADI not specified by JECFA; EU aluminium additive group ADI applies.',
    'e559': 'E559 / INS 559 — Aluminium Silicate (Kaolin, China Clay, Al₂Si₂O₅(OH)₄) is a naturally occurring hydrated aluminium silicate clay mineral with a fine, white, non-abrasive texture. It is the mineral that gives white porcelain its colour and is used in paper coating to give glossy paper its smooth surface. Food applications: anti-caking agent in powdered foods; surface coating on some fresh fruits (provides a white bloom that serves as a physical pest deterrent in some countries); used in winemaking as a fining agent to clarify wine. Also used medicinally as an ingredient in some anti-diarrhoeal preparations (kaolin-pectin mixture). Aluminium note: kaolin is structurally stable; bioavailable aluminium from kaolin is very low at food use concentrations. As with all aluminium-containing additives, individuals with CKD should be aware. For healthy individuals at food use levels, no significant safety concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (limited uses).',

    # --- Flavour Enhancers (additional) ---
    'e620': 'E620 / INS 620 — Glutamic Acid. The amino acid form of MSG, found naturally in tomatoes, parmesan, mushrooms and soy sauce. Produced by fermentation. Used as a flavour enhancer to intensify umami taste. Safe.',
    'e622': 'E622 / INS 622 — Monopotassium Glutamate. The potassium salt of glutamic acid, used as a flavour enhancer similarly to MSG (E621). Generally safe.',
    'e623': 'E623 / INS 623 — Calcium Glutamate. The calcium salt of glutamic acid, used as a flavour enhancer. Generally safe.',
    'e624': 'E624 / INS 624 — Monoammonium Glutamate. The ammonium salt of glutamic acid, used as a flavour enhancer. Generally safe.',
    'e625': 'E625 / INS 625 — Magnesium Glutamate. The magnesium salt of glutamic acid, used as a flavour enhancer. Provides dietary magnesium alongside flavour-enhancing action. Safe.',
    'e626': 'E626 / INS 626 — Guanylic Acid. A ribonucleotide flavour enhancer found naturally in mushrooms, meat and fish. Used to amplify umami taste. Generally safe.',
    'e628': 'E628 / INS 628 — Dipotassium Guanylate. Potassium salt of guanylic acid, used as a flavour enhancer in chips and savoury snacks. Safe.',
    'e629': 'E629 / INS 629 — Calcium Guanylate. Calcium salt of guanylic acid, used as a flavour enhancer. Safe.',
    'e630': 'E630 / INS 630 — Inosinic Acid. A ribonucleotide found naturally in meat and fish, used as a flavour enhancer to boost umami taste. Usually used with MSG and guanylates. Safe.',
    'e632': 'E632 / INS 632 — Dipotassium Inosinate. Potassium salt of inosinic acid, used as a flavour enhancer. Often combined with MSG and disodium guanylate. Safe.',
    'e633': 'E633 / INS 633 — Calcium Inosinate. Calcium salt of inosinic acid, used as a flavour enhancer in processed foods. Safe.',
    'e635': 'E635 / INS 635 — Disodium 5\'\'-Ribonucleotides. A blend of disodium inosinate (E631) and disodium guanylate (E627) in an optimised ratio for maximum umami enhancement. Used in chips, instant noodles and savoury snacks. Safe at permitted levels. Not suitable for gout sufferers (high purine content).',

    # --- Modified Starches (additional E-numbers) ---
    'e1410': 'E1410 / INS 1410 — Monostarch Phosphate is a modified starch produced by esterification of native starch (from maize, wheat, potato or tapioca) with a phosphate salt (sodium tripolyphosphate or sodium orthophosphate), introducing a small number of phosphate mono-ester groups on the starch chains. The phosphate groups are negatively charged, creating electrostatic repulsion between starch chains that reduces retrogradation (the crystallisation of starch that causes gelling on cooling). This results in: (1) clearer, more stable pastes that do not set or gel as strongly on cooling; (2) improved tolerance to acidic conditions; (3) reduced syneresis (water expulsion). Used in: sauces, soups, pie fillings, gravies, dairy desserts and baby foods. The degree of phosphorylation is low (typically <0.5% phosphate groups), so the phosphate contribution to dietary phosphate intake is minimal. Safety: completely hydrolysed in the gut to glucose, phosphoric acid and starch — normal food components. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (Codex permitted uses).',
    'e1420': 'E1420 / INS 1420 — Acetylated Starch is a modified starch produced by reacting native starch with acetic anhydride or vinyl acetate, introducing acetyl groups (–COCH₃) on the hydroxyl groups of the starch chains at low degrees of substitution (DS <0.1 as permitted by Codex). The acetyl groups create steric hindrance that prevents starch chains from crystallising back together (retrograding) after gelatinisation — this is the primary cause of staling and texture changes in sauces, puddings and dairy products on refrigeration. Properties: (1) lower gelatinisation temperature than native starch — swells and thickens more readily; (2) reduced tendency to retrograde, gel or synerese on cooling; (3) clearer, smoother pastes; (4) improved freeze-thaw stability (though less than hydroxypropyl starch). Used in: salad dressings, sauces, custards, dairy desserts, instant soup powders and frozen foods. Safety: acetyl groups are hydrolysed in the gut to acetic acid (vinegar) — a normal metabolite. No safety concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e1422': 'E1422 / INS 1422 — Acetylated Distarch Adipate is a dual-modified starch produced by cross-linking with adipic anhydride (which bridges starch chains with adipate diester cross-links for heat and shear stability) AND acetylating with acetic anhydride (which reduces retrogradation and improves freeze-thaw stability). This combination of two chemical modifications gives E1422 superior functional properties compared to either modification alone: (1) exceptional stability under high temperature, high shear and acidic processing conditions (cross-linking provides this); (2) excellent freeze-thaw stability — can withstand multiple freeze-thaw cycles without syneresis or separation (acetylation provides this). Used in: frozen sauces and soups, retorted ready meals, canned baby foods, fruit pie fillings, salad dressings and culinary sauces. Safety: both adipate and acetate groups are hydrolysed in the gut to adipic acid and acetic acid — both normal food acids metabolised without concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e1440': 'E1440 / INS 1440 — Hydroxypropyl Starch is a modified starch produced by reacting native starch with propylene oxide — introducing hydroxypropyl ether groups (–CH₂-CHOH-CH₃) on the starch chains. The hydroxypropyl groups are bulky, hydrophilic substituents that: (1) significantly reduce the gelatinisation temperature (starch thickens at lower temperatures — useful for cold-process sauces); (2) dramatically inhibit retrogradation — the added bulk physically prevents starch chains from re-crystallising; (3) provide exceptional freeze-thaw stability — hydroxypropyl starch is the best-performing modified starch for products that will be frozen, thawed and reheated (sauces, frozen meals, dairy desserts); (4) produce clear, glossy, smooth-textured pastes. Used in: frozen soups, gravies, dairy desserts, fruit preparations, salad dressings and baby foods. Propylene oxide (the reagent) is a classified carcinogen, but the modification reaction is conducted under controlled conditions and the resulting hydroxypropyl starch is thoroughly analysed — residual propylene oxide in food-grade product is essentially zero. The hydroxypropyl groups are stable and not metabolised. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA with purity and maximum DS specifications.',
    'e1442': 'E1442 / INS 1442 — Hydroxypropyl Distarch Phosphate is the most extensively used modified starch in the food industry — it combines two types of chemical modification: (1) cross-linking with phosphorus oxychloride or sodium trimetaphosphate (distarch phosphate — phosphate bridges between starch chains), which provides thermal stability, acid stability and shear stability; (2) hydroxypropyl substitution (using propylene oxide), which provides freeze-thaw stability and reduced retrogradation. This dual modification gives E1442 the broadest functional versatility of any modified food starch: (1) withstands high-temperature processing (retorting, sterilisation, pasteurisation); (2) tolerates acidic pH conditions; (3) survives mechanical shear; (4) remains stable through freezing and thawing; (5) does not gel or synerese on refrigeration. This comprehensive stability profile makes E1442 the go-to starch for: ketchup, tomato-based sauces (acid + heat), baby foods (retorted at 121°C), mayonnaise, salad dressings, frozen ready meals, canned soups and fruit-filled pastries. One of the most safety-tested modified starches — reviewed by JECFA, EFSA and FDA. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e1201': 'E1201 / INS 1201 — Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP / Povidone). A synthetic polymer used as a clarifying agent in beer and wine, and as a binder in pharmaceutical tablets. Safe at food-use levels.',

    # ── New Cosmetic & Personal Care Ingredients ──────────────────────────────
    'cetyl esters': 'Cetyl Esters is a mixture of esters of saturated fatty alcohols and fatty acids, used as a waxy emollient in hair conditioners and skin creams. It gives a rich, creamy texture without greasiness and is a plant-derived alternative to spermaceti wax (historically from sperm whales). CIR Expert Panel has confirmed it is safe for cosmetic use.',
    'quaternium-33': 'Quaternium-33 is a quaternary ammonium conditioning polymer used in hair care products to reduce static and improve combability. It provides anti-static and softening effects by adsorbing onto the negatively charged hair surface. Generally considered safe at cosmetic use concentrations.',
    'hydroxypropyltrimonium hydrolyzed wheat protein': 'Hydroxypropyltrimonium Hydrolyzed Wheat Protein is a cationic (positively charged) derivative of hydrolyzed wheat protein. The quaternary ammonium group allows it to bond electrostatically to damaged, negatively charged hair, depositing protein to reinforce and strengthen the hair shaft. Effective for frizz control and damage repair. Safe at cosmetic concentrations. Note: contains wheat — relevant for those with wheat allergy using rinse-off products with prolonged contact.',
    'behentrimonium chloride': 'Behentrimonium Chloride (BTAC-22) is a quaternary ammonium salt derived from rapeseed or coconut oil, used as a conditioning agent in hair conditioners and treatments. It imparts smoothness, reduces frizz and improves detangling. EU Cosmetics Regulation restricts its use to 3% in rinse-off and 1% in leave-on products. Generally safe within these limits; higher concentrations can irritate skin and eyes.',
    'trideceth-6': 'Trideceth-6 is a non-ionic surfactant and emulsifier produced by ethoxylating tridecanol (a fatty alcohol) with approximately 6 moles of ethylene oxide. Used to emulsify oils in water in creams and conditioners. The ethoxylation process can introduce trace amounts of 1,4-dioxane, a possible carcinogen (IARC Group 2B) — manufacturers can minimise this through vacuum stripping. Generally considered safe within approved cosmetic use concentrations.',
    'chlorhexidine digluconate': 'Chlorhexidine Digluconate is a water-soluble salt of chlorhexidine — a potent broad-spectrum antiseptic effective against a wide range of bacteria, fungi and some viruses. Used in mouthwashes, wound care and surgical scrubs. EU Cosmetics Regulation restricts it to 0.3% in oral hygiene products and 0.5% in other applications. Rare but serious risk of severe allergic reactions (anaphylaxis) has been documented, primarily from medical-grade products. Ototoxic — must not contact the middle ear. Not recommended for daily home cosmetic use.',
    'limonene': 'Limonene is a naturally occurring cyclic terpene found in the peel of citrus fruits (lemons, oranges, limes) and in many plant essential oils. It is widely used as a fragrance ingredient and is one of the most prevalent terpenes in nature. However, it is also one of the most common causes of fragrance-related contact dermatitis. The EU Cosmetics Regulation requires individual declaration on labels when present above 0.001% in leave-on products or 0.01% in rinse-off products. Limonene can also auto-oxidise on storage to form more potent skin sensitisers.',
    'linalool': 'Linalool is a naturally occurring terpene alcohol found in lavender, coriander, rosewood and many other plants. It contributes to floral, woody scents in perfumes, personal care products and household products. Like limonene, linalool is an EU-mandated fragrance allergen that must be individually declared on cosmetic labels above threshold concentrations. Linalool can oxidise on storage to form linalool hydroperoxides, which are stronger skin sensitisers than the parent compound. A common trigger for fragrance-related contact dermatitis.',
    'benzyl salicylate': 'Benzyl Salicylate is a synthetic ester with a sweet, floral, balsamic fragrance, used in perfumes, shampoos and lotions. It is one of the 26 fragrance allergens required by EU Cosmetics Regulation to be individually declared on labels when present above threshold concentrations. Associated with contact allergic reactions in sensitised individuals, particularly those with fragrance allergy.',
    'hexyl cinnamal': 'Hexyl Cinnamal (Hexyl Cinnamaldehyde) is a synthetic aldehyde with a strong jasmine-like fragrance, widely used in personal care products, perfumes and household cleaners. It is one of the 26 EU-mandated fragrance allergens that must be individually declared on cosmetic labels. Can cause contact allergic dermatitis in sensitised individuals. One of the more common fragrance allergens identified in patch testing.',
    'isopropyl alcohol': 'Isopropyl Alcohol (IPA / Isopropanol) is a petroleum-derived short-chain alcohol used as a solvent, antiseptic and astringent in cosmetics, hand sanitisers, antiseptic wipes and pharmaceuticals. Effective at denaturing proteins and killing bacteria and viruses. However, frequent application is drying to the skin — it can strip natural oils and disrupt the skin barrier, leading to dryness, irritation and potential sensitisation. Not recommended for daily cosmetic use on the face or as a primary skincare ingredient.',
    'cetrimonium chloride': 'Cetrimonium Chloride (CTAC) is a quaternary ammonium salt used as a conditioning agent in hair rinses, conditioners and hair masks. It imparts smoothness and reduces static by coating the negatively charged hair surface. EU Cosmetics Regulation restricts it to 0.25% in rinse-off products due to its potential to cause skin and eye irritation at higher concentrations. Also used as a mild preservative at low concentrations. Toxic to aquatic organisms — environmental persistence noted.',
    'butylphenyl methylpropional': 'Butylphenyl Methylpropional (Lilial) is a synthetic fragrance ingredient with a floral, lily-like scent that was widely used in perfumes, shampoos, conditioners, lotions and household products for decades. It has been BANNED in the European Union since March 2022 under the EU Cosmetics Regulation (Regulation EC 1223/2009, Amendment 2021/1099) due to its classification as a Category 1B reproductive toxicant (toxic to reproduction). Studies demonstrated adverse effects on fertility in animal studies. Products containing Lilial can no longer be sold in the EU. Consumers should avoid products still listing this ingredient.',
    'arginine': """Arginine (L-arginine) is a semi-essential (conditionally essential) amino acid — the body can synthesise it but not always in sufficient quantities during rapid growth, illness or stress. Chemical structure: an aliphatic amino acid with a guanidinium side chain, giving it a strongly basic character (pKa ~12.5) and a net positive charge at physiological pH. Dietary sources: red meat, poultry, fish, dairy, nuts (walnuts, almonds), pumpkin seeds and legumes. In cosmetics and personal care: (1) Hair conditioning — arginine's positive charge at pH of haircare formulations allows it to bind to negatively charged damaged hair surfaces (damaged hair cuticle exposes the negatively charged cortex); it neutralises static and can help bond and fill damaged zones along the hair shaft, contributing to smoothness and reduced frizz; (2) Skin conditioning — functions as a humectant (water-attracting) component of the Natural Moisturising Factor (NMF) in skin; supports wound healing and skin barrier function, partly via its role as a precursor to nitric oxide (through nitric oxide synthase), which promotes angiogenesis and collagen synthesis in healing tissue; (3) pH adjuster — used as a mild alkaline pH adjuster in cosmetic formulations, replacing stronger alkaline agents. Nutritional/medical note: oral L-arginine supplements have been studied for cardiovascular benefit (vasodilation via NO production), erectile dysfunction and wound healing — evidence is mixed; not directly relevant to topical cosmetic use where systemic absorption is minimal. Safe at cosmetic concentrations; no known skin sensitisation; well tolerated by all skin types. Approved for use in EU Cosmetics Regulation (Annex III not restricted); FSSAI and FDA permit in food at normal dietary levels.""",
    'serine': """Serine (L-serine) is a non-essential aliphatic amino acid with a hydroxyl side chain (-CH₂OH), which makes it hydrophilic and gives it both its humectant properties and its role as a metabolic precursor. Biosynthesised in the body from 3-phosphoglycerate (a glycolysis intermediate) via the phosphoserine pathway. Dietary sources: soy, tofu, eggs, meat, dairy, nuts and lentils. Metabolic significance: serine is a precursor to cysteine, glycine, sphingolipids (the backbone of ceramides), phospholipids (via phosphatidylserine) and the neurotransmitter glycine; it is involved in one-carbon metabolism (folate cycle). In cosmetics and personal care: (1) Skin humectant — serine is one of the NMF (Natural Moisturising Factor) components naturally found in the stratum corneum. The NMF is a collection of water-soluble molecules (amino acids, PCA, urea, lactic acid, sugars) that are the primary hygroscopic agents keeping the uppermost skin layers hydrated. Serine contributes to water retention in the stratum corneum; (2) Skin-conditioning agent — replenishes depleted NMF in dry, compromised or ageing skin; (3) Hair care — as with other amino acids, helps improve moisture retention in the hair shaft. Used in premium moisturisers, serums, hair conditioners and anti-ageing formulations. Safety: non-irritating, non-sensitising, biocompatible — identical to a component of healthy skin. Well tolerated by all skin types including sensitive skin. EU Cosmetics Regulation does not restrict serine; FSSAI and FDA permit in food. No toxicological concerns at cosmetic or dietary concentrations.""",
    '2-oleamido-1,3-octadecanediol': '2-Oleamido-1,3-Octadecanediol is a synthetic ceramide analogue (a compound that structurally mimics natural skin ceramides). It contains both an oleic acid-derived amide group and a diol that replicate the sphingolipid backbone of endogenous ceramides. Used in advanced barrier-repair skin care formulations to restore the skin lipid lamellar structure, reduce transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and soothe dry, eczema-prone or compromised skin. Safe and well tolerated.',
    'glyceryl linoleate': 'Glyceryl Linoleate is a naturally occurring glyceryl ester of linoleic acid (omega-6 fatty acid). Found naturally in many vegetable oils including safflower and sunflower oil. Used in cosmetics as an emollient and skin-conditioning agent. Linoleic acid is an essential fatty acid for healthy skin barrier function — it is depleted in acne-prone and atopic skin. Restores the skin lipid barrier, reduces inflammation and supports the ceramide pathway. Safe and skin-compatible.',
    'glyceryl oleate': 'Glyceryl Oleate (Glyceryl Monooleate) is a natural emulsifier and emollient derived from oleic acid and glycerol. Found naturally in olive and sunflower oils. Used in skin and hair care as a gentle emulsifier, emollient and conditioner. Helps maintain the skin moisture barrier. Safe and well-tolerated.',
    'glyceryl linolenate': 'Glyceryl Linolenate is a glyceryl ester of alpha-linolenic acid (ALA, omega-3 fatty acid), found naturally in flaxseed, perilla and hemp oils. Used in cosmetics as an emollient and skin barrier-restoring ingredient. Omega-3 fatty acids have anti-inflammatory properties and support ceramide synthesis in skin. Safe and skin-compatible.',

    # ── EU Fragrance Allergens (additional) ──────────────────────────────────
    'eugenol': 'Eugenol is a naturally occurring phenol found abundantly in clove oil, cinnamon, basil and nutmeg. Used as a flavouring in food and as a fragrance ingredient in cosmetics. It is also used as a local anaesthetic and antiseptic in dentistry. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens that must be declared on cosmetic labels. Can cause contact allergic dermatitis in sensitised individuals.',
    'geraniol': 'Geraniol is a naturally occurring terpene alcohol found in geranium, rose, palmarosa and citronella essential oils. Used as a fragrance ingredient in cosmetics, personal care and household products. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens that must be individually declared. Can oxidise on storage to form stronger skin sensitisers. A common fragrance allergen in patch testing.',
    'coumarin': 'Coumarin is a naturally occurring lactone found in tonka beans, sweet clover, cassia cinnamon and many essential oils. Used as a fragrance ingredient in perfumes, cosmetics and formerly as a flavouring. EU Cosmetics Regulation requires individual declaration as an allergen. In food, EU restricts coumarin content due to hepatotoxicity at very high doses (studies in rodents at high doses showed liver damage). Safe at the very low amounts typical of cosmetic use.',
    'cinnamal': 'Cinnamal (Cinnamic Aldehyde) is the primary fragrance compound responsible for the characteristic scent of cinnamon. A potent skin sensitiser and one of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Must be declared individually on cosmetic labels. Can cause contact allergic dermatitis — particularly in occupational settings (bakers, confectioners) and in leave-on personal care products.',
    'benzyl alcohol': 'Benzyl Alcohol is a naturally occurring aromatic alcohol found in jasmine, hyacinth and many other plant essential oils; also produced synthetically. Used as a preservative, solvent and fragrance ingredient in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens — must be declared above threshold concentrations. At high concentrations, it metabolises to benzaldehyde and benzoic acid, which can be irritating. Critically, benzyl alcohol is toxic to neonates — it can cause fatal "gasping syndrome" in premature or newborn infants; never use products containing it on newborns.',
    'farnesol': 'Farnesol is a naturally occurring sesquiterpene alcohol found in ylang-ylang, rose, musk and many other essential oils. Used in fragrances and cosmetics. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Can cause contact allergic dermatitis in sensitised individuals. Also has antimicrobial properties at higher concentrations.',
    'citronellol': 'Citronellol is a natural terpene alcohol found in geranium, rose, citronella and lemon essential oils. Widely used in perfumes and cosmetics for its floral, rosy scent. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens — must be declared individually on cosmetic labels. Can cause contact allergy in sensitised individuals.',
    'hydroxycitronellal': 'Hydroxycitronellal is a synthetic aldehyde fragrance with a strong lily-of-the-valley and muguet scent. Used in perfumes and personal care products. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Can cause contact sensitisation and allergic reactions in some individuals.',
    'isoeugenol': 'Isoeugenol is a structural isomer of eugenol found in ylang-ylang, nutmeg and clove oils. Used as a fragrance ingredient. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Considered a stronger sensitiser than eugenol — it has been voluntarily restricted or banned in some cosmetic applications by the fragrance industry.',
    'benzyl benzoate': 'Benzyl Benzoate is a naturally occurring ester in Peru balsam and some essential oils; also produced synthetically. Used as a fragrance fixative and in cosmetics. Also used medically as an antiparasitic agent (scabies, lice treatment). One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Can cause contact allergy in sensitive individuals.',
    'benzyl cinnamate': 'Benzyl Cinnamate (Cinnamein) is a naturally occurring ester in Peru balsam, styrax and tolu balsam. Used as a fragrance fixative. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Found to cause contact sensitisation in some individuals.',
    'amyl cinnamal': 'Amyl Cinnamal (Amyl Cinnamic Aldehyde) is a synthetic fragrance with a jasmine-like floral scent. Used in perfumes and personal care products. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens that must be individually declared on cosmetic labels. Potential skin sensitiser in sensitive individuals.',
    'alpha-isomethyl ionone': 'Alpha-Isomethyl Ionone is a synthetic fragrance compound with a violet and iris-like floral scent. Widely used in fine fragrance, personal care and household products. One of the 26 EU mandatory fragrance allergens. Can cause contact allergic dermatitis in sensitised individuals.',

    # ── Quaternary Ammonium Compounds ────────────────────────────────────────
    'behentrimonium methosulfate': 'Behentrimonium Methosulfate (BTMS) is a cationic conditioning agent derived from rapeseed oil, widely used in hair conditioners and detangling products. Compared to behentrimonium chloride, it has a more neutral pH, can be used in emulsifier blends (BTMS-50), and is considered milder and less irritating. Popular in natural and "clean" formulation. Safe at cosmetic use concentrations.',
    'cetrimonium bromide': 'Cetrimonium Bromide (CTAB) is a quaternary ammonium surfactant and preservative used in hair rinses and conditioners. Has similar conditioning properties to cetrimonium chloride. Also used as an antimicrobial in laboratory settings. EU restricts its use in cosmetics. Potential irritant at higher concentrations.',
    'guar hydroxypropyltrimonium chloride': 'Guar Hydroxypropyltrimonium Chloride is a cationic guar gum derivative — natural guar gum modified with a quaternary ammonium group. Used as a conditioning polymer in shampoos and conditioners to provide detangling and softness. Better biodegradable profile than fully synthetic cationic polymers. Generally well tolerated.',
    'quaternium-18': 'Quaternium-18 (Dimethyldioctadecyl Ammonium Chloride) is a quaternary ammonium conditioning compound used in hair care. Provides conditioning and anti-static effects. Similar environmental persistence concerns as other quats; generally safe at cosmetic use concentrations.',

    # ── Cosmetic Skin-Brightening and Active Ingredients ─────────────────────
    'tranexamic acid': 'Tranexamic Acid is an amino acid derivative (a lysine analogue) clinically used as a haemostatic drug at high doses. In dermatology and cosmetics, it is used at much lower concentrations (2–5%) as a skin-brightening and anti-melasma active. Clinical studies have shown effectiveness in reducing hyperpigmentation. At cosmetic concentrations, it does not carry the systemic procoagulant risks of the pharmaceutical form. Safe and well-tolerated topically.',
    'tetrahexyldecyl ascorbate': 'Tetrahexyldecyl Ascorbate (THD Ascorbate) is an oil-soluble, lipid-stable form of Vitamin C. Unlike water-soluble ascorbic acid, it penetrates the lipid-rich stratum corneum more effectively and is significantly more stable against oxidation. Converts to ascorbic acid once inside the skin. Used in anti-ageing and brightening serums at concentrations of 10–30%. Safe and well-tolerated.',
    'ascorbyl glucoside': 'Ascorbyl Glucoside is a water-soluble glycoside of Vitamin C produced by bonding ascorbic acid to glucose. More stable than ascorbic acid; enzymes in the skin cleave the glucose to release free ascorbic acid. Used in brightening and antioxidant skincare at 2–3%. Gentler than ascorbic acid; well-tolerated.',
    'ethyl ascorbic acid': 'Ethyl Ascorbic Acid (3-O-Ethyl Ascorbic Acid) is a stable, water-soluble Vitamin C derivative that is both more stable and potentially more potent than ascorbyl glucoside. Converts to ascorbic acid in the skin. Used at 0.5–3% in brightening serums. Safe and generally well-tolerated.',
    'kojic dipalmitate': 'Kojic Dipalmitate is a more stable, oil-soluble form of kojic acid — a natural tyrosinase inhibitor produced by Aspergillus fermentation of rice and sake. Inhibits melanin production for skin brightening. Less irritating than kojic acid itself. Safe for cosmetic use within approved concentrations.',
    'resveratrol': 'Resveratrol is a naturally occurring polyphenolic stilbenoid found in grape skin, red wine, blueberries and Japanese knotweed. A potent antioxidant with studied anti-inflammatory and anti-ageing properties in laboratory research. Used in premium skincare serums and supplements. Safe for topical and oral use; oral bioavailability is limited — topical delivery is more direct. The evidence for anti-ageing effects is promising but requires further human clinical validation.',
    'ferulic acid': 'Ferulic Acid is a natural hydroxycinnamic acid found in the cell walls of rice, wheat and oat bran. A potent antioxidant that acts as a photoprotectant and synergises strongly with Vitamins C and E in skincare (stabilising them and enhancing their antioxidant effect). Used in cosmetics as an antioxidant active and food-grade preservative. Safe; well-tolerated.',

    # ── Additional Functional Cosmetic Ingredients ────────────────────────────
    'caprylyl methicone': 'Caprylyl Methicone is a lightweight, low-viscosity silicone fluid with an organic (caprylyl) modification that makes it more skin-compatible and with a lower environmental persistence than cyclic silicones. Used for a smooth, non-greasy skin feel. Safe for topical cosmetic use.',
    'trimethylsilylamodimethicone': 'Trimethylsilylamodimethicone is a non-cyclic, trimethylsilyl-terminated amino-functional silicone. Used in hair care products for long-lasting conditioning, anti-frizz effects and shine. The non-cyclic structure means it does not share the EU restrictions placed on cyclic silicones (D4, D5). Generally safe.',
    'sodium phytate': 'Sodium Phytate is the sodium salt of phytic acid, a natural compound found in rice bran, sesame and other grains. Used in cosmetics as a chelating agent (sequestering trace metal ions that could degrade products) and as a mild skin-brightening ingredient. Safe natural alternative to synthetic chelators like EDTA.',
    'phytic acid': """Phytic acid (inositol hexaphosphoric acid / IP6 / myo-inositol-1,2,3,4,5,6-hexakis dihydrogen phosphate) is the primary storage form of phosphorus in plant seeds, constituting 1–5% of the dry weight of cereals, legumes, nuts and oilseeds. Structurally, it is myo-inositol with all six hydroxyl groups esterified with phosphate groups, giving it a strong negative charge and powerful chelating ability. In plant foods: phytic acid is the antinutrient most responsible for reducing the bioavailability of divalent minerals (iron, zinc, calcium, magnesium) — it binds these minerals in insoluble phytate salts in the gut, preventing absorption. Soaking, sprouting, fermentation and cooking reduce phytate content significantly. People whose diets consist primarily of unfermented whole grains and legumes (common in parts of Africa and Asia) are at greater risk of iron and zinc deficiency partly due to high phytate intake. In cosmetics: (1) Chelating agent — phytic acid binds trace metal ions (iron, copper) in cosmetic formulations, preventing metal-catalysed oxidation of oils and active ingredients, improving product stability and shelf life. A natural, gentler alternative to synthetic EDTA; (2) Skin-brightening — phytic acid inhibits tyrosinase, the key enzyme in melanin biosynthesis, reducing pigmentation. Used in serums and creams for hyperpigmentation, dark spots and uneven skin tone; (3) Gentle chemical exfoliant — as a mild alpha-hydroxy acid analogue, it promotes gentle cell turnover; (4) Antioxidant — chelating iron prevents it from catalysing free radical (Fenton) reactions. Sourced from rice bran, corn, sesame or other oilseeds. Safety: safe for topical cosmetic use; non-irritating at typical concentrations (0.1–3%); rare contact sensitivity. No photosensitivity risk (unlike AHAs). Vegan and natural; approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation; FSSAI, FDA and CODEX permit in food.""",
    'gluconolactone': 'Gluconolactone is a polyhydroxy acid (PHA) — a gentler, larger-molecular relative of AHAs (alpha-hydroxy acids) like glycolic acid. Provides mild exfoliation without significant irritation, making it suitable for sensitive and post-procedure skin. Also acts as a humectant and antioxidant chelator. Safe and well-tolerated.',
    'lactobionic acid': 'Lactobionic Acid is a polyhydroxy acid (PHA) derived from lactose. Like gluconolactone, it is a larger molecule that exfoliates more gently than AHAs. Additionally acts as a strong humectant and antioxidant through its metal-chelating properties. Well-tolerated by sensitive skin. Safe.',
    'mandelic acid': 'Mandelic Acid is an alpha-hydroxy acid (AHA) derived from bitter almonds. Its larger molecular size (compared to glycolic acid) means it penetrates the skin more slowly, resulting in a gentler exfoliating action with fewer side effects. Also has antibacterial properties, making it useful for acne-prone skin. Photosensitising — SPF protection recommended. Safe at cosmetic concentrations.',
    'propanediol': 'Propanediol (1,3-Propanediol) is a naturally derived glycol produced by fermenting corn sugars, used as a humectant and solvent in cosmetics. It is a sustainable alternative to petroleum-derived propylene glycol, with a gentler skin feel and minimal irritation potential. FDA GRAS status. Very safe and well-tolerated.',
    'caprylyl glycol': 'Caprylyl Glycol (1,2-Octanediol) is an 8-carbon diol used in cosmetics both as a humectant and a preservative booster. It has intrinsic antimicrobial activity against Gram-positive bacteria and Candida, allowing it to enhance the efficacy of preservative systems. Safe and generally well-tolerated at typical cosmetic concentrations.',
    'pentylene glycol': 'Pentylene Glycol (1,5-Pentanediol) is a multifunctional glycol used as a humectant, solvent and mild preservative booster in cosmetics. It has good skin compatibility, provides moisture retention and enhances the penetration of other actives. Generally well-tolerated. Often used in natural-oriented formulations.',
    'betaine': 'Betaine (Trimethylglycine) is a naturally occurring compound derived from sugar beet processing. Used in cosmetics as a humectant, anti-irritant and osmoprotectant — it helps protect the skin from environmental stressors. Also used in hair care to provide gentle conditioning and reduce the irritation potential of surfactants. Safe and well-tolerated.',
    'colloidal oatmeal': 'Colloidal Oatmeal is finely milled whole oat grain (Avena sativa) that has been processed to remain suspended in water. FDA-approved as an OTC skin protectant at 0.5–2%. Rich in avenanthramides (unique oat polyphenols with potent anti-inflammatory properties), beta-glucan and natural oils. Clinical evidence supports its use for atopic dermatitis (eczema), dry skin and skin barrier repair. Excellent tolerability; safe. Possible concern only for individuals with severe oat or wheat allergy via topical route.',
    'saccharide isomerate': 'Saccharide Isomerate (trade name PENTAVITIN) is a carbohydrate complex derived from corn. It is claimed to form lasting covalent bonds with skin keratins, providing long-duration hydration effects. Used in premium moisturisers. Generally safe and well-tolerated.',
    'trehalose': """Trehalose (α,α-trehalose / alpha-D-glucopyranosyl alpha-D-glucopyranoside) is a non-reducing disaccharide of two glucose molecules linked by an α,α-1,1-glycosidic bond — a very stable linkage that makes trehalose chemically unreactive (non-reducing), resistant to acidic hydrolysis and exceptionally stable across a wide range of temperatures and pH levels. Produced commercially at scale by enzymatic conversion of starch (using trehalose synthase from Thermus thermophilus — a process developed by the Japanese company Hayashibara Co. Ltd.). Occurs naturally in trace amounts in mushrooms (particularly dried shiitake), yeasts, fungi, insects (bees, dragonflies — it is the primary blood sugar in most insects), shrimps, algae and notably in resurrection plants (Myrothamnus flabellifolius, Selaginella lepidophylla) — plants that can survive almost complete desiccation, reviving when wetted, using trehalose to protect their cell membranes and proteins against damage during drying. Mechanism of protection: trehalose preferentially replaces water molecules around proteins and membranes during desiccation, forming hydrogen bonds with polar groups and maintaining their three-dimensional structure — a process called vitrification (glass formation), where trehalose forms an amorphous glassy matrix around biological structures. This makes it invaluable for: pharmaceutical/biotech applications — preserving proteins, vaccines, live cells, blood products and tissue for storage and lyophilisation (freeze-drying); cosmetics — used as a cryoprotectant and humectant, stabilising formulation proteins and providing skin hydration; In food: used as a sweetener (approximately 45% sweetness of sucrose), bulking agent and moisture stabiliser in confectionery, baked goods, freeze-dried foods and some beverages; its stability makes it useful in products requiring long shelf life. As a food sweetener: trehalose is digested by intestinal trehalase (brush-border enzyme) to two glucose molecules — provides 4 kcal/g. Some individuals (rare — approximately 8% of adults in some populations, more common in Greenlandic Inuit populations) have congenital trehalase deficiency and cannot digest trehalose, experiencing bloating and diarrhoea. Blood glucose impact is similar to sucrose. FDA GRAS (2000); EFSA approved; FSSAI permits as a food ingredient. Safe for the general population. In cosmetics: well tolerated; safe for all skin types; no known sensitisation.""",

    # ── Isopropyl Alcohol ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'isopropanol': """Isopropanol (isopropyl alcohol / IPA / 2-propanol / propan-2-ol) is a simple secondary alcohol (CH₃CHOHCH₃) produced synthetically from propylene (a petroleum refinery by-product) by hydration. It is fully miscible with water and most organic solvents. Key properties: rapidly evaporating (boiling point 82°C), strong solvent for fats, oils, resins and most non-polar compounds; antimicrobial at 60–70% aqueous concentrations (higher concentrations are less effective as some water is needed for protein denaturation). Uses across sectors: (1) Antiseptic — standard skin antiseptic for medical procedures, injection sites and surgical hand rubs at 70% concentration; effective against bacteria, most viruses (including SARS-CoV-2) and fungi, but not bacterial spores; WHO-recommended hand sanitiser formulation includes 75% IPA; (2) Cosmetics — used as a solvent (to dissolve fragrances, resins, film-forming polymers), quick-drying agent (in antiperspirant sticks, perfumes, hair sprays, aftershaves, nail products, toners and astringent lotions); (3) Pharmaceutical — excipient in topical preparations, cleaning agent, manufacturing solvent; (4) Industrial cleaning and electronics. Skin concerns: short-chain alcohols including isopropanol denature proteins at the skin surface and solubilise the sebum lipid layer. Repeated or daily topical application to facial skin can: damage the stratum corneum lipid barrier, increase transepidermal water loss (TEWL), cause dryness, flaking, irritation and potentially exacerbate conditions like eczema and rosacea. For this reason, dermatologists advise against using products with high alcohol content on sensitive, dry or eczema-prone facial skin. As an astringent or antiseptic in a toner or acne product (used occasionally), the risk is lower. As a solvent in a rinsed-off product (shampoo), risk is minimal. Inhalation of high vapour concentrations causes CNS depression — relevant for industrial exposure, not cosmetic use. Not classified as carcinogenic or genotoxic. EU Cosmetics Regulation permits isopropanol in cosmetics; FSSAI does not permit in food; approved as disinfectant by WHO.""",

    # ── Ceramide Analogues ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'ceramide np': 'Ceramide NP (N-Palmitoyl Sphinganine) is the most abundant ceramide type in human stratum corneum, making up approximately 20% of total skin ceramides. Critical for maintaining the lamellar lipid barrier of the skin. Used in barrier-repair moisturisers for dry skin, eczema and aged skin. Safe and bioidentical to skin lipids.',
    'ceramide ap': 'Ceramide AP (N-Palmitoyldihydrosphingosine) is another important skin ceramide type. Works alongside ceramide NP and other ceramides to form the intercellular lipid matrix of the stratum corneum. Used in barrier-repair formulations. Safe.',
    'phytosphingosine': 'Phytosphingosine is a sphingoid base (the backbone component of ceramides) naturally found in yeast and skin. It has intrinsic anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial properties in addition to its role as a ceramide precursor. Used in acne-prone and sensitive skin formulations. Safe.',

    # ── Butylphenyl Methylpropional ───────────────────────────────────────────
    'lilial': 'Lilial (Butylphenyl Methylpropional / p-t-Bucinal) is a synthetic fragrance ingredient formerly used for its fresh, lily-like floral scent in numerous cosmetics and personal care products. It has been BANNED in the European Union since 1 March 2022, classified as a Category 1B reproductive toxicant under CLP Regulation — animal studies demonstrated adverse effects on fertility. Products containing Lilial cannot be placed on the EU market. Consumers should avoid any product still listing this ingredient.',

    # ══ Cosmetic Polymers / Film-formers ══════════════════════════════════════
    'polyethylene': 'Polyethylene (PE) is a synthetic thermoplastic polymer used in cosmetics as a texture modifier, thickener and emollient in anhydrous formulations like lipsticks, foundations and mascaras. High-molecular-weight polyethylene wax is safe for topical use. Note: low-molecular-weight PE microbeads have been banned in rinse-off products in many countries (UK, USA, EU) due to marine microplastic pollution — but the wax form used in leave-on cosmetics is different and not banned.',
    'polysilicone-11': 'Polysilicone-11 is a cross-linked dimethicone silicone elastomer used in long-wear makeup formulations (foundations, eye shadows, lipsticks) as a film-former and suspension agent. It provides a smooth, non-tacky film and excellent wear-resistance. Not absorbed through the skin; safe for topical use.',
    'nylon-12': 'Nylon-12 (Polyamide-12) is a synthetic polyamide polymer used as a texture agent and film-former in cosmetics including foundations, eyeshadows and mascara. It produces a smooth, soft-focus finish and improves long-wear properties. Safe for topical cosmetic use; not absorbed.',
    'methyl methacrylate crosspolymer': 'Methyl Methacrylate Crosspolymer is a cross-linked acrylic polymer used as a soft-focus agent, mattifying powder and film-former in foundations, primers and skincare products. Creates a blurring effect by scattering light. Safe for topical use; not absorbed.',

    # ══ Cosmetic Emollients / Esters ══════════════════════════════════════════
    'caprylic/capric triglyceride': 'Caprylic/Capric Triglyceride (CCT) is a light, non-greasy emollient derived from coconut oil by esterifying glycerol with caprylic (C8) and capric (C10) fatty acids. It is one of the most widely used cosmetic emollients — hypoallergenic, non-comedogenic, and exceptionally well-tolerated by all skin types including sensitive and acne-prone skin. Also functions as an excellent carrier oil for active ingredients. Safe.',
    'polyhydroxystearic acid': 'Polyhydroxystearic Acid (PHSA) is a branched, polymeric ester of 12-hydroxystearic acid used as an emulsifier and dispersing agent, particularly to stabilise inorganic UV filters (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) in sunscreens and mineral makeup. It prevents nanoparticle clumping and ensures even distribution. Safe for topical cosmetic use.',
    'pentaerythrityl tetra-di-t-butyl hydroxyhydrocinnamate': 'Pentaerythrityl Tetra-di-t-butyl Hydroxyhydrocinnamate (trade name Irganox 1010) is a synthetic hindered phenol antioxidant used at trace levels in cosmetics to prevent oxidation of oils, fats and polymers during manufacturing and storage. It is not a skin-active ingredient — it protects the formula, not the skin. Safe at the trace concentrations used.',
    'tocopheryl acetate': 'Tocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E Acetate) is the most widely used esterified form of Vitamin E in cosmetics. More stable against oxidation than tocopherol itself. It acts as an antioxidant, protects the skin lipid barrier from free-radical damage, and is used as a skin conditioner in moisturisers, sunscreens and anti-ageing products. Converts to free tocopherol on the skin. Safe and very well-tolerated.',
    'cholesterol': """Cholesterol (cholest-5-en-3β-ol) is a 27-carbon sterol — a waxy, amphiphilic lipid molecule consisting of four fused rings (the sterane/gonane ring system) with a hydroxyl group at C-3, a double bond at C-5,6 and an isooctyl side chain. It is essential to all animal cell membranes — intercalated between phospholipid bilayer molecules, it modulates membrane fluidity, forming specialised lipid raft microdomains. It is also the biosynthetic precursor to all steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone, oestrogen, progesterone), bile acids, and Vitamin D. In the skin specifically: cholesterol is one of the three major lipid classes of the stratum corneum lamellar bodies (the other two being ceramides and free fatty acids). These three lipids assemble in an approximately 1:1:1 molar ratio into a highly ordered lamellar structure that forms the primary permeability barrier of the skin — critical for preventing transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and protecting against pathogen entry. Skin conditions including atopic dermatitis (eczema) and psoriasis are characterised by disrupted stratum corneum lipid composition, including altered cholesterol:ceramide ratios. In cosmetics: cholesterol is used in barrier-repair formulations (moisturisers, eczema creams, lipid-replacement products) where it replenishes the stratum corneum lipid pool. Sources: typically lanolin-derived (from sheep wool) or synthesised from plant sources (phytosterol conversion). The topically applied cholesterol does not contribute to serum cholesterol levels — dietary cholesterol and topical cholesterol are metabolised completely differently. Vegan concern: lanolin-derived cholesterol is animal-derived (sheep wool grease). Safety: fully bioidentical to skin lipids; non-irritating; non-sensitising; extensively studied. Safe for all skin types. EU Cosmetics Regulation does not restrict cholesterol in topical products.""",

    # ══ Cosmetic Minerals / Clays ══════════════════════════════════════════════
    'mica': 'Mica is a group of naturally occurring phyllosilicate minerals (muscovite, phlogopite, lepidolite) that split into thin, shiny, reflective sheets. Widely used in makeup (eyeshadows, highlighters, foundations, lip products) for shimmer, sparkle and light-reflective effects. Safe for topical cosmetic use. The primary concern with mica is ethical rather than toxicological — large-scale mica mining in India (Jharkhand, Rajasthan) and Madagascar has been linked to child labour, and reputable brands now use responsibly sourced or synthetic mica.',
    'dicalcium phosphate': """Dicalcium Phosphate (DCP / calcium hydrogen phosphate / CaHPO₄ / E341ii) is a calcium salt of phosphoric acid in which two calcium atoms share one phosphate group. Available as the dihydrate form (CaHPO₄·2H₂O — brushite) and the anhydrous form. A white, odourless, slightly alkaline powder. Applications across sectors: (1) Toothpaste abrasive — DCP is a mild, water-insoluble polishing agent that provides mechanical cleaning action to remove plaque and surface stains from tooth enamel without excessive abrasion of the enamel surface. Relative Dentine Abrasion (RDA) value ~40–50 — considered mild. Must be formulated carefully to avoid compatibility issues with fluoride (phosphate can interfere with fluoride deposition on enamel in some formulations); (2) Pharmaceutical excipient — tablet filler, binder and direct compression agent in tablets and capsules, particularly calcium supplement tablets where it provides both the filler and the active calcium mineral; (3) Food ingredient (E341ii — dicalcium phosphate) — used as a leavening agent (reacts with sodium bicarbonate to release CO₂), calcium fortification agent (dietary calcium supplement), dough conditioner (strengthens gluten network in bread dough), and emulsifying salt in processed cheese; (4) Animal feed supplement. Nutritional value: bioavailable calcium and phosphorus source. A single 500 mg tablet of DCP provides approximately 115 mg elemental calcium (23% of the 500 mg tablet weight). Health caution: people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) must limit phosphate intake — the kidneys regulate serum phosphate, and impaired kidneys allow dangerous phosphate accumulation (hyperphosphataemia), leading to secondary hyperparathyroidism, cardiovascular calcification and renal bone disease. CKD patients should avoid phosphate food additives. For healthy individuals, DCP is safe and nutritionally beneficial. FSSAI, FDA (GRAS) and EU (E341) approved.""",
    'stearalkonium bentonite': 'Stearalkonium Bentonite is a clay mineral (bentonite) that has been organically modified with stearalkonium chloride (a quaternary ammonium salt) to become compatible with anhydrous (non-water) formulations. Used as a rheology modifier — it thickens and stabilises anhydrous cosmetic formulations such as foundations, lipsticks and eyeshadows, suspending pigments evenly. Safe for topical cosmetic use.',

    # ══ UV Filters / Sunscreen Actives ════════════════════════════════════════
    'ethylhexyl salicylate': 'Ethylhexyl Salicylate (Octyl Salicylate / 2-Ethylhexyl Salicylate) is an organic UVB-absorbing sunscreen active that absorbs UV radiation in the 295–315nm range. Used at concentrations up to 5% (EU) to protect skin from UVB-induced sunburn. Generally well-tolerated; low sensitisation risk. Some in-vitro endocrine activity detected at high concentrations but not considered clinically relevant at sunscreen-use levels. Safe and approved by EU, FDA (as Octyl Salicylate) and CDSCO.',
    'phenylbenzimidazole sulfonic acid': 'Phenylbenzimidazole Sulfonic Acid (Ensulizole / PBSA) is a water-soluble UVB sunscreen filter approved by the EU, FDA and many other regulatory bodies. Absorbs UVB radiation in the 290–320nm range. The water-soluble nature makes it suitable for lightweight, non-greasy sunscreen formulations. Safe at approved concentrations (up to 8% EU, 4% FDA).',
    'butyl methoxydibenzoylmethane': 'Butyl Methoxydibenzoylmethane (Avobenzone / Parsol 1789) is the most widely used broad-spectrum UVA filter, absorbing UVA1 radiation in the 320–400nm range. A key ingredient in most broad-spectrum sunscreens globally. Its main limitation is photostability — it degrades in sunlight when used alone. Combined with photostabilisers (Octocrylene, Tinosorb S) it remains effective. Safe at approved concentrations (up to 5% EU, 3% FDA). Some studies showed skin penetration and trace detection in blood — FDA has requested additional data, but current evidence does not indicate a safety concern.',
    'octocrylene food': 'Octocrylene is both a UVB/UVA2 sunscreen filter and a photostabiliser for unstable UV filters like Avobenzone. See the main "Octocrylene" entry for full details.',

    # ══ Cosmetic Surfactants / Emulsifiers ════════════════════════════════════
    'laureth-12': 'Laureth-12 is a non-ionic surfactant and emulsifier produced by ethoxylating lauryl alcohol with 12 ethylene oxide units. Like all ethoxylated ingredients, manufacturing may leave trace amounts of 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B possible carcinogen) — reputable manufacturers vacuum-strip this contaminant to well below safe limits. Used in cleansers and emulsions. Safe within approved limits; the 1,4-dioxane concern is a manufacturing quality issue.',
    'steareth-21': 'Steareth-21 is a non-ionic emulsifier produced by ethoxylating stearyl alcohol with 21 ethylene oxide units. Used as an O/W emulsifier in lotions, creams and conditioners. Ethoxylation may leave trace 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B) — manufacturers control this via vacuum stripping. Safe within approved limits.',
    'steareth-2': 'Steareth-2 is a non-ionic emulsifier produced by ethoxylating stearyl alcohol with 2 ethylene oxide units. Used alongside Steareth-21 in classic emulsion systems. Same trace 1,4-dioxane concern as other ethoxylates. Safe within approved limits.',
    'sodium lauroyl lactylate': """Sodium lauroyl lactylate (SLL) is an anionic surfactant and emulsifier produced by esterifying lauric acid (a C12 fatty acid from coconut or palm kernel oil) with lactic acid to form lauroyl lactic acid, which is then neutralised with sodium hydroxide to yield the sodium salt. Similar in chemical structure to sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL, E481) used in baking — but using lauric acid (C12) instead of stearic acid (C18), giving a significantly different functional and safety profile. Chemical nature: amphiphilic molecule with a moderately water-soluble sodium lactate head group and a lipophilic lauric acid tail, providing an HLB value that suits both surfactancy and emulsification in aqueous systems. Applications in cosmetics: (1) mild facial and body cleansers — SLL is a considerably milder anionic surfactant compared to sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS) or sodium laureth sulfate (SLES), with lower skin irritation potential; provides gentle cleansing and lather without stripping the lipid barrier; (2) emulsifier in moisturisers, serums and creams — stabilises oil-in-water emulsions; (3) hair care — mild scalp cleansing in sensitive-scalp shampoos; (4) baby products — where gentleness is paramount. Biodegradability: being a fatty acid-lactic acid ester, SLL degrades readily in the environment — a green chemistry advantage. Safety: very well tolerated by most skin types; significantly less irritating than SLS in standard patch testing; no known significant allergenicity; not a known sensitiser at cosmetic use concentrations; CIR Expert Panel confirms safety. In food: SLL has similar emulsifying properties to SSL and is approved in some jurisdictions as a food emulsifier (E481 class), though SSL is more commonly used in food. Biodegradable; can be derived from renewable plant-based sources (coconut + fermented lactic acid). FSSAI permits as a cosmetic ingredient; EU and FDA approved for cosmetic use. Safe for adults and children in rinse-off and leave-on products at standard use concentrations.""",

    # ══ Conditioning / Quat Agents ════════════════════════════════════════════
    'linoleamidopropyl pg-dimonium chloride phosphate': 'Linoleamidopropyl PG-Dimonium Chloride Phosphate is a quaternary ammonium conditioning agent derived from linoleic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid). Used as an antistatic conditioning agent in hair care and skincare products. The quaternary ammonium group improves hair manageability and reduces static. Safe at cosmetic-use concentrations; biodegradable.',

    # ══ Hyaluronic Acid Variants ══════════════════════════════════════════════
    'sodium acetylated hyaluronate': 'Sodium Acetylated Hyaluronate is a modified form of sodium hyaluronate in which acetyl groups are attached to the hyaluronic acid polymer. The acetylation makes it more lipophilic (fat-attracting), improving its affinity for the skin surface and enabling longer-lasting moisture retention compared to standard sodium hyaluronate. Safe and very well-tolerated.',
    'sodium hyaluronate crosspolymer': 'Sodium Hyaluronate Crosspolymer is a cross-linked, high-molecular-weight form of sodium hyaluronate. The cross-linking increases its molecular size so it remains on the skin surface, providing a long-lasting film that continuously releases moisture. Used in premium anti-ageing and hydrating formulations. Safe.',
    'hydrolyzed sodium hyaluronate': 'Hydrolyzed Sodium Hyaluronate consists of very small fragments of sodium hyaluronate produced by enzymatic hydrolysis. The small molecular size (typically under 10 kDa) allows deeper penetration into the upper layers of the stratum corneum compared to high-molecular-weight HA. Used in serums and moisturisers for deeper hydration. Safe.',

    # ══ Cosmetic Polymers / Rheology Modifiers ════════════════════════════════
    'acrylates/c10-30 alkyl acrylate crosspolymer': 'Acrylates/C10-30 Alkyl Acrylate Crosspolymer is a synthetic thickener and stabiliser related to Carbomer but with added long-chain alkyl groups, making it more effective at stabilising emulsions with oil droplets. Widely used in sunscreens, moisturisers and gel formulations. Safe for topical use.',
    'propylene carbonate': 'Propylene Carbonate is an organic solvent and plasticiser used in cosmetics to dissolve and stabilise ingredients, improve spreadability and act as a coupling agent. Also used in pharmaceutical formulations. Safe at cosmetic-use concentrations; good skin tolerability.',

    # ══ Cosmetic Colourants / Pigments ════════════════════════════════════════
    'red 7 lake': 'Red 7 Lake (D&C Red 7 Lake / CI 15850:1 / Lithol Rubine BCA) is a synthetic azo dye lake used in cosmetics (lipsticks, blushers, eyeshadows) for its bright, vivid red-pink colour. "Lake" pigments are precipitated onto a substrate (usually aluminium), making them more opaque and bleed-resistant than their soluble counterparts. EU and FDA approved for cosmetic use within specified limits. Safe for external cosmetic use.',
    'red 6': 'Red 6 (D&C Red 6 / CI 15850 / Lithol Rubine B) is a synthetic azo dye used as a cosmetic colorant in lipsticks, blushers and other makeup products. The non-lake (soluble salt) form. FDA approved for cosmetic use; EU permitted in cosmetics. Safe for external cosmetic application.',
    'red 28 lake': 'Red 28 Lake (D&C Red 28 Lake / CI 45410 / Phloxine B Lake) is a synthetic xanthene dye lake used as a cosmetic colorant in lipsticks and other makeup. Approved by FDA for cosmetic use (not for use around the eyes in the US). EU permitted at specified limits. Generally safe for topical cosmetic use; some photosensitisation concern at high concentrations.',
    'ci 15850': 'CI 15850 is the Colour Index number for Lithol Rubine, a synthetic azo dye. Used in cosmetics (Red 6, Red 7 and their lake forms) for red-pink shades in lipsticks and blushers. EU and FDA approved for cosmetic use.',
    'ci 45410': 'CI 45410 is the Colour Index number for Phloxine B (Red 28), a synthetic xanthene dye used as a cosmetic colorant. FDA approved for lipsticks; EU permitted in cosmetics at specified limits.',
    'ci 77491': 'CI 77491 is the Colour Index number for Red Iron Oxide (ferric oxide, Fe2O3). A natural inorganic mineral pigment providing red-brown shades in foundations, eyeshadows and lipsticks. Very safe; chemically inert; approved globally.',
    'ci 77492': 'CI 77492 is the Colour Index number for Yellow Iron Oxide (goethite, FeOOH). A natural inorganic mineral pigment providing yellow-gold shades in foundations and makeup. Very safe; chemically inert; approved globally.',

    # ══ Bis-PEG Silicone Compounds ════════════════════════════════════════════
    'bis-peg-12 dimethicone beeswax': 'Bis-PEG-12 Dimethicone Beeswax is a PEG-modified dimethicone-based wax used as an emulsifier and skin conditioner in anhydrous cosmetic formulations. The PEG modification makes the silicone wax water-dispersible. Contains a PEG group (ethylene oxide-based) — ethoxylation may introduce trace 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B possible carcinogen); reputable manufacturers minimise this. Also contains a beeswax-derived component — not suitable for strict vegans. Safe at cosmetic-use concentrations within approved limits.',

    # ══ Ceramide EOP ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    'ceramide eop': 'Ceramide EOP (Ceramide 1 / N-[(1R,2R)-2-Hydroxy-1-(hydroxymethyl)heptadecyl]-... ester) is one of the nine ceramide types identified in human skin. It is unique among ceramides for having an ester-linked omega-hydroxy fatty acid — it forms the structural backbone of the lamellar lipid sheets in the stratum corneum. Critically important for maintaining the skin\'s water-permeability barrier. Used in barrier-repair and eczema-care formulations alongside other ceramides (NP, AP). Safe; bioidentical to skin lipids.',

    # ══ Food Additive E Numbers / INS Numbers (comprehensive) ══════════════════

    # ── Colours ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'curcumin': """Curcumin (E100/INS 100) is the primary bioactive polyphenol and yellow pigment of turmeric (Curcuma longa), a member of the ginger family cultivated in South Asia for over 4,000 years. India produces ~80% of the world's turmeric. The commercial extract contains three curcuminoids: curcumin (60–70%), demethoxycurcumin (~20%) and bisdemethoxycurcumin (~10%). Chemically curcumin is (1E,6E)-1,7-bis(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)hepta-1,6-diene-3,5-dione — two feruloyl groups joined by a methylene bridge through a beta-diketone. The vivid yellow-orange colour is pH-sensitive: bright yellow at neutral and acidic pH, shifts to red-brown above pH 8. Solubility: highly lipophilic — poorly water-soluble in its natural form; water-dispersible forms for food use are prepared using emulsification (polysorbate 80, lecithin) or cyclodextrin complexation. As a food colour: used in mustard, butter, margarine, dairy products, confectionery, rice dishes, instant noodles, snack seasonings, beverages and soups. Also used as a natural alternative to sunset yellow and tartrazine. As a bioactive ingredient: curcumin has been studied in >16,000 published papers for anti-inflammatory (inhibits NF-κB transcription factor and COX-2), antioxidant (scavenges reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, chelates metal ions), and potential anticancer activities in cell and animal models. Human clinical trials have explored curcumin for osteoarthritis, IBD, metabolic syndrome, depression and cancer prevention — results are promising but inconsistent due to its very poor oral bioavailability: rapid metabolism (glucuronidation, sulfation), poor water solubility and rapid elimination mean only trace amounts reach systemic circulation from standard curcumin preparations. Piperine from black pepper increases bioavailability ~20-fold by inhibiting glucuronidation. Modern bioavailability-enhanced forms (phytosome, nanoformulations, PLGA microspheres) achieve better absorption. At food colour doses (far below supplement doses), curcumin intake from products is modest. No genotoxicity; no carcinogenicity. ADI: 0–3 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E100), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Natural; plant-derived.""",
    'e100': 'E100 is the EU/INS code for Curcumin. Natural turmeric yellow food colour. Safe and beneficial.',
    'annatto': """Annatto (E160b/INS 160b) is a natural orange-yellow colourant derived from the seed coat (aril) of the achiote tree (Bixa orellana), a tropical shrub native to Central and South America, now cultivated extensively in India, Brazil, Peru and Africa. The principal pigment compounds are bixin (fat-soluble, accounts for ~80% of colour) and norbixin (water-soluble, the de-esterified saponified form). Bixin-based preparations colour fat-containing foods — butter, margarine, cheese rinds, cream cheeses, full-fat crisps — while norbixin-based preparations (water-dispersible sodium or potassium norbixin) are used in beverages, processed cheese slices and water-based sauces. Produces warm yellow to deep orange-red colours depending on concentration and the preparation form. Used extensively in: Cheddar and Red Leicester cheese (the characteristic orange colour), butter, margarine, custard powder, rice dishes, bakery products, smoked fish, processed meats, snack seasonings and some beverages. Traditional uses in India include use in marinades, curries and rice preparations. Chemically, bixin and norbixin are carotenoids with unique structural characteristics — they are the only commercially used carboxylic acid-bearing carotenoids. Not provitamin A sources. Colour is generally stable in fat-based products but moderately sensitive to light and heat in water-based systems. Allergen note: annatto is the most commonly reported natural food colour allergen — case reports of anaphylaxis, urticaria, angioedema, dermatitis and IBS-like symptoms have been published. Unlike azo dye reactions (which affect aspirin-sensitive individuals via cyclooxygenase inhibition), annatto allergy is IgE-mediated. ADI: 0–6 mg/kg body weight/day (JECFA 2016 for bixin and norbixin combined). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160b), FDA (GRAS as annatto extract) and CODEX. Vegan; natural.""",
    'e160b': 'E160b is the EU/INS code for Annatto (Bixin/Norbixin). Natural orange-red colour. Safe.',
    'paprika extract': """Paprika extract (E160c/INS 160c / oleoresin paprika / capsanthin-capsorubin extract) is a concentrated natural colourant and mild flavour ingredient obtained by solvent extraction (typically hexane, acetone or supercritical CO₂) of dried red Capsicum annuum peppers. The primary pigments are capsanthin (~65% of carotenoids) and capsorubin, along with beta-carotene, zeaxanthin, beta-cryptoxanthin and other carotenoids. Capsanthin and capsorubin are xanthophyll (oxygenated) carotenoids unique to red pepper — not found in any other commercial natural source. These give paprika extract its characteristic warm red-orange colour that is more heat-stable than beta-carotene and resistant to typical food processing. Unlike raw paprika spice, commercial paprika extract for colouring is typically deodorised and demineralised, so it contributes minimal heat (capsaicin may be largely removed in some grades). Produces warm orange-red to deep red colours highly soluble in oils; water-dispersible forms are prepared by emulsification. Used widely in: snack seasonings (namkeen, crisps, extruded snacks), processed meats and sausages, soups, spice blends, dairy products and poultry colouring. In India, ubiquitous in namkeen, masala snack coatings and many ready-to-eat products. Provides some provitamin A (from beta-carotene fraction) and dietary xanthophyll antioxidants. No azo dye — not linked to hyperactivity or aspirin cross-reactivity. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160c), FDA (GRAS as oleoresin paprika) and CODEX. Natural; plant-derived.""",
    'e160c': 'E160c is the EU/INS code for Paprika Extract. Natural red-orange colour from capsicum. Safe.',
    'lycopene': """Lycopene (E160d/INS 160d) is a naturally occurring acyclic carotenoid (C₄₀H₅₆) — the pigment responsible for the red colour of tomatoes, watermelon, guava, pink grapefruit, papaya and rose hips. It is the most potent singlet oxygen quencher among common dietary carotenoids — approximately twice as effective as beta-carotene and ten times more effective than alpha-tocopherol. Commercial production: extracted from tomato processing by-products (tomato paste, tomato skin waste) using solvent extraction followed by purification, OR biosynthesised by fermentation of the filamentous fungus Blakeslea trispora. Tomato-derived lycopene is EU-approved; Blakeslea-derived lycopene received EU approval in 2006 (E160d). As a food colour: provides red to deep red colouration in juices, soups, sauces and beverages. Fat-soluble; requires emulsification (with polysorbates or lecithin) for use in water-based products. Bioavailability: lycopene from processed or cooked tomatoes (tomato paste, tomato sauce, puree) is significantly more bioavailable than from raw tomatoes — thermal processing breaks down cell walls, releasing lycopene from chromoplasts; the presence of fat (olive oil) further increases absorption by 2–4 fold. Not a provitamin A source. Health significance: epidemiological studies have consistently associated higher lycopene intake with reduced prostate cancer risk — the evidence is sufficiently strong that in 2005, FDA authorised a qualified health claim for tomato products and reduced prostate cancer risk (with the caveat that evidence is limited and not conclusive). Meta-analyses also link lycopene intake with reduced cardiovascular disease risk, reduced LDL oxidation and lower blood pressure. IARC notes this as promising but not definitive. No significant toxicological concerns; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E160d), FDA (GRAS for tomato-sourced lycopene) and CODEX. Natural, plant-derived.""",
    'e160d': 'E160d is the EU/INS code for Lycopene. Natural red colour from tomatoes. Safe.',
    'lutein': """Lutein (E161b/INS 161b) is a xanthophyll carotenoid (C₄₀H₅₆O₂ — a dihydroxy carotene) found in marigold flowers (Tagetes erecta), egg yolks, kale, spinach, corn, peas and many other plants. Commercial food-grade lutein is primarily extracted from dried marigold flower petals using food-grade solvents (hexane, ethanol) followed by saponification and crystallisation. Two commercial forms: free lutein (oil-suspended, fat-soluble) and lutein esters (the natural form in marigolds — more stable; cleaved to free lutein in the gut). Produces yellow to golden-yellow colours. Used as a food colour in beverages, dairy products, yoghurt, confectionery, bakery products, egg products, infant formula and dietary supplements. Exceptionally important nutritional role: lutein and its structural isomer zeaxanthin are selectively and uniquely concentrated in the macular pigment of the human retina. The macula is the region of highest visual acuity — the fovea and macula centralis. Macular pigment composed of lutein and zeaxanthin functions as an internal optical filter (absorbing damaging blue/HEV light before it can damage photoreceptors) and as an antioxidant (quenching reactive oxygen species generated in the high-metabolic-rate photoreceptors). Evidence base: the AREDS2 randomised controlled trial (Age-Related Eye Disease Study 2, involving 4,203 participants) demonstrated that supplementation with 10 mg lutein + 2 mg zeaxanthin daily reduced the risk of progression to advanced age-related macular degeneration (AMD) by ~26% compared to placebo in people with intermediate AMD. This is clinically validated evidence at an unusually high level of rigour for a dietary supplement. Additional evidence supports lutein for reducing glare sensitivity and improving contrast sensitivity in the general population. Regular dietary lutein from leafy vegetables provides 1–4 mg/day in most diets; supplemental lutein provides 5–20 mg. Dietary fat enhances absorption significantly. No safety concerns; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E161b), FDA (GRAS for specific applications) and CODEX. Natural; plant-derived.""",
    'e161b': 'E161b is the EU/INS code for Lutein. Natural yellow colour with eye health benefits. Safe.',
    'beetroot red': """Beetroot red (E162/INS 162 / betanin) is the natural crimson-red pigment extracted from red beetroot (Beta vulgaris var. rubra). Produced commercially from concentrated beetroot juice, beetroot powder or by water extraction of the fresh or dried root. The colour is derived primarily from betanin, with smaller contributions from isobetanin and prebetanin (all betacyanins). pH-sensitive: vivid red-violet at pH 4–6 (ideal for yoghurt, dairy and confectionery), yellowing at pH < 3.5, and shifting to blue-violet above pH 7. Heat-sensitive and light-sensitive — challenging to use in products requiring pasteurisation or UHT treatment; best suited for refrigerated or frozen products. More stable in combination with antioxidants (ascorbic acid, erythorbic acid), reduced oxygen headspace and microencapsulation. Widely used in: yoghurt (both plain pink and strawberry-raspberry flavoured), dairy desserts, ice cream, confectionery coatings, juices, smoothies and clean-label bakery products where synthetic reds are undesirable. Common occurrence: eating significant quantities of beetroot causes beeturia (pink/red urine) in approximately 10–14% of the population — a harmless variation caused by incomplete metabolism of betanin; not an indication of toxicity. Blood in urine (haematuria) must be distinguished from beeturia — beeturia should clear within 24–48 hours after stopping beetroot consumption. Nutritional contribution: contains betalain antioxidants; beetroot products also contribute nitrates (converted to nitric oxide in the body, with well-documented cardiovascular benefits). No safety concerns; no ADI specified by JECFA or EFSA. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E162), FDA (permitted as a colourant in yoghurts and certain foods) and CODEX.""",
    'betanin': """Betanin (E162/INS 162 / beetroot red) is the principal red-violet pigment of red beetroot (Beta vulgaris var. rubra). Chemically it is a betalain — specifically a betacyanin — consisting of a cyclo-DOPA unit linked via an aldimine bond to betalamic acid. Extracted commercially from beetroot juice concentrate by cold filtration, ultrafiltration or spray-drying. Produces a vivid crimson-red to purple-red colour at neutral pH (pH 4–6); shifts to yellow-orange below pH 3.5 and to blue-violet above pH 7. Heat and light sensitivity: betanin degrades faster than most synthetic dyes during pasteurisation, UHT treatment and storage under light — degradation is catalysed by oxygen, heat, metal ions and low pH. This limits its use in heat-processed beverages and ambient-shelf products but it is excellent in yoghurt, dairy desserts, ice cream and confectionery. Commercially stabilised by antioxidants (ascorbic acid), chelating agents, reduced oxygen headspace and encapsulation. Used as a natural alternative to synthetic azo reds (allura red, carmoisine) in health-conscious and clean-label formulations. Dietary intake: regular beetroot consumption causes 'beeturia' (pink/red urine) and red stools in some individuals — this is a harmless metabolic variation affecting 10–14% of people depending on gut iron status; not a sign of toxicity. Antioxidant activity: betanin has documented free-radical scavenging and anti-inflammatory activity; preliminary studies suggest possible anticarcinogenic and cardioprotective properties, though human clinical evidence remains limited. No safety concerns; no ADI specified by JECFA or EFSA. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E162), FDA (GRAS for certain applications) and CODEX. Vegan, natural, non-GMO.""",
    'e162': 'E162 is the EU/INS code for Beetroot Red (Betanin). Natural red-purple colour. Safe.',
    'anthocyanins': """Anthocyanins (E163/INS 163) are a family of over 700 water-soluble flavonoid pigments responsible for the red, purple and blue colours of berries (blueberries, blackcurrants, elderberries, strawberries), red grapes, red cabbage, purple corn, purple sweet potato and hibiscus flowers. Chemically they are glycosides of anthocyanidins — the common aglycones being cyanidin, pelargonidin, delphinidin, malvidin, peonidin and petunidin. The colour is strongly pH-dependent: vivid red at pH < 3 (common in acidic beverages), purple at neutral pH, and blue-green at alkaline pH — limiting their use in alkaline or neutral products. Thermal stability varies by source: acylated anthocyanins (from red cabbage, purple sweet potato, purple carrot) are considerably more heat-stable than simple anthocyanins (from grape, elderberry), making source selection critical for processed foods. Commercial sources: grape skin extract (wine by-product, rich in malvidin glycosides), elderberry, black carrot, red cabbage and purple sweet potato. Used in beverages, fruit yogurts, confectionery, jams and ice cream as clean-label alternatives to synthetic azo reds. As dietary antioxidants: extensively studied — free radical scavenging, LDL oxidation inhibition, anti-inflammatory (NF-κB pathway), cardiovascular-protective (improved endothelial function in clinical trials) and potential neuroprotective effects. Regular dietary anthocyanin intake from whole fruits and vegetables is epidemiologically associated with reduced cardiovascular disease risk and slower cognitive decline with aging. No safety concerns; no ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E163), FDA (as natural colouring agents from fruit/vegetable sources) and CODEX. Natural, plant-derived; antioxidant-rich.""",
    'e163': 'E163 is the EU/INS code for Anthocyanins. Natural plant pigments from berries. Safe.',
    'calcium carbonate food': """Calcium carbonate (E170/INS 170 / CaCO₃) is the most abundant calcium mineral on Earth — the primary constituent of chalk, limestone and marble. Food-grade calcium carbonate is produced by precipitation from calcium chloride and sodium carbonate solutions, or by grinding natural limestone. Functions in food: white colorant (provides an opaque white colour in confectionery coatings and gum products); anti-caking agent in salt, baking powder and powdered mixes; calcium supplement in fortified foods, plant-based milks, infant formula, vitamins and pharmaceutical tablets; acidity regulator (neutralises excess acidity); firming agent; and dough conditioner in bread. Calcium content: approximately 40% elemental calcium by weight — the highest of any common calcium supplement. Bioavailability: calcium carbonate requires stomach acid for absorption; best absorbed when taken with food (food stimulates gastric acid production). Absorption is reduced in individuals with low stomach acid (achlorhydria, people taking proton pump inhibitors). Provides a highly cost-effective dietary calcium source. Excessive supplemental calcium intake (>2,500 mg/day elemental calcium) has been associated with kidney stones in susceptible individuals and, controversially, with cardiovascular calcification in some meta-analyses — applies to high-dose supplements, not food fortification levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E170), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e170': 'E170 is the EU/INS code for Calcium Carbonate. White colour and calcium supplement. Safe.',
    'iron oxides': """Iron oxides (E172/INS 172) are a group of inorganic mineral pigments — naturally occurring compounds of iron and oxygen (and hydroxyl groups) — produced synthetically for food and cosmetic use to ensure purity and freedom from heavy metal contaminants. Three approved forms: iron oxide red (Fe₂O₃, haematite, CI 77491 — warm brick red to brown-red), iron oxide yellow (FeOOH, goethite, CI 77492 — golden yellow to ochre), and iron oxide black (Fe₃O₄, magnetite, CI 77499 — jet black). These three can be blended in any ratio to create brown, skin-tone and all intermediate shades. Commercially produced by controlled precipitation of ferrous/ferric sulfate solutions at specific pH and temperature — the synthetic product is chemically identical to the natural mineral but is produced under pharmaceutical-grade quality control to ensure very low heavy metal impurities (arsenic, lead, cadmium). Food applications (E172): primarily used as surface colourants for olives (black iron oxide to fix the black colour in lye-cured olives), tablet coatings on pharmaceutical supplements, confectionery sugar coatings, sugarcraft and some surface colour treatments. Cosmetics applications (CI 77491, 77492, 77499): the most widely used inorganic pigments in makeup — foundations, concealers, blush, bronzers, eyeshadows, lipsticks and mineral sunscreens. Iron oxides in sunscreens additionally provide protection against HEV (high-energy visible light, 400–450 nm) — beneficial for patients with melasma, as HEV light triggers melanin production. Non-allergenic, chemically inert and do not penetrate the skin barrier. No ADI specified (JECFA — safe at food use levels). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E172), FDA (for cosmetics and limited food applications) and CODEX.""",
    'e172': 'E172 is the EU/INS code for Iron Oxides and Hydroxides. Mineral food colours. Safe at approved levels.',
    'e174': 'E174 is the EU code for Silver. Metallic surface decoration on confectionery. Chemically inert; safe in decorative amounts.',
    'e175': 'E175 is the EU code for Gold. Metallic food decoration. Chemically inert; safe.',
    'e140': 'E140 is the EU/INS code for Chlorophylls. Natural green plant pigments. Safe.',
    'e141': 'E141 is the EU/INS code for Copper Complexes of Chlorophylls. Stabilised natural green colours. Safe at permitted levels.',
    'e150a': 'E150a is Plain Caramel (Class I). Produced without ammonia or sulphites. Used in spirits. Safest caramel class.',
    'e150b': 'E150b is Caustic Sulphite Caramel (Class II). Used in cognac and sherry. Safe.',
    'e150c': 'E150c is Ammonia Caramel (Class III). Used in beer and sauces. Safe at typical food levels.',

    # ── Acidity regulators ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    'acetic acid': 'Acetic Acid (E260/INS 260, ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH) — the defining acid of vinegar; natural fermentation product; normal human metabolic intermediate. See INS 260 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e260': 'E260 / INS 260 — Acetic Acid (ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH). The defining compound of vinegar; naturally produced by Acetobacter fermentation of ethanol or by Aspergillus niger fermentation; a normal human metabolic intermediate (acetate is produced in every cell during carbohydrate and fat catabolism). See INS 260 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'potassium acetate': """Potassium acetate (E261/INS 261 / CH₃COOK) is the potassium salt of acetic acid — produced by neutralising acetic acid with potassium hydroxide or potassium carbonate. A white crystalline, hygroscopic solid that is freely soluble in water. In food: used as an acidity regulator (provides acetic acid's tartness while minimising the sharp, astringent taste of pure acetic acid), as a mild preservative (acetic acid released in solution has antimicrobial activity — inhibits mould and many bacteria) and as a salt substitute (provides the salty mouthfeel sensation of potassium rather than sodium chloride). Also used to adjust pH in food systems. Found in: some cheeses, bread, confectionery, sauces, pickled vegetables, processed meats and certain seafood products. As a salt substitute: potassium acetate delivers potassium ions (like all potassium-based salt substitutes) and is used in reduced-sodium food formulations — particularly useful for people on sodium-restricted diets or managing hypertension. Unlike potassium chloride (the most common sodium substitute), potassium acetate has a slightly milder flavour and less metallic aftertaste. Safety: acetic acid and acetate are normal intermediates in human metabolism (acetyl-CoA is a universal metabolic hub). Potassium acetate at food use concentrations presents no toxicological concern. However: (1) People with renal impairment (kidney disease) must restrict potassium intake from all sources — potassium acetate, potassium chloride and other potassium-based food additives contribute to total potassium load and can cause hyperkalaemia (dangerous elevations in blood potassium) in people with CKD. (2) Very high doses (pharmacological, not food): IV potassium acetate is used medically as a potassium replacement — misuse can cause cardiac arrhythmia. At normal food additive concentrations, this is irrelevant. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E261), FSSAI and CODEX.""",
    'e261': 'E261 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Acetate. Acidity regulator and salt substitute. Safe.',
    'sodium acetate': 'Sodium Acetate (E262/INS 262, CH₃COONa) — the sodium salt of acetic acid (vinegar acid). Functions as an acidity regulator (buffers pH in the 4.5–6.5 range), mild preservative, and flavouring agent. Responsible for the characteristic "salt and vinegar" flavour of crisps and snacks when used in combination with citric acid. In food processing, sodium acetate is a safer, less volatile form of acetic acid for use in neutral or mildly acidic products where liquid acetic acid would be difficult to handle. Also used in baking and poultry products to extend shelf life by inhibiting Listeria and other pathogens. Completely metabolised as acetate (enters Krebs cycle). No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e262': 'E262 / INS 262 — Sodium Acetates (Monosodium Acetate, Sodium Diacetate). Sodium salts of acetic acid used as acidity regulators and mild preservatives in crisps, bread, meat and vegetable products. Responsible for "salt-and-vinegar" flavour. See sodium acetate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e270': 'E270 is the EU/INS code for Lactic Acid. Natural fermentation acid found in yoghurt and pickles. Safe.',
    'propionic acid': 'Propionic Acid (E280/INS 280, propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH) — natural 3-carbon short-chain fatty acid from fermentation; principal anti-mould agent in bread; a normal human metabolite produced daily by gut bacteria fermenting dietary fibre. See E280 for full description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e280': 'E280 / INS 280 — Propionic Acid (propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH). Three-carbon short-chain fatty acid naturally produced in Swiss Emmental cheese (by Propionibacterium shermanii — the same bacteria responsible for the characteristic holes) and in the human colon during dietary fibre fermentation. Used as an anti-mould and anti-rope preservative in bread and baked goods. Mechanism: undissociated propionic acid penetrates fungal membranes and disrupts intracellular pH and energy metabolism, halting mould growth. Works synergistically with reduced water activity (< aw 0.95) in packaged sliced bread. Metabolically: propionate is a completely normal substrate in human metabolism — transported from the gut to the liver via portal circulation and converted to succinyl-CoA for use in the Krebs cycle (gluconeogenic precursor). No accumulation, no toxic metabolites. Some individuals report propionic acid-triggered migraines (propionates appear in some older lists of migraine dietary triggers) — but controlled evidence for this effect is weak and inconsistent. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'sodium propionate': 'Sodium Propionate (E281/INS 281) — the sodium salt of propionic acid; same anti-mould function as propionic acid in bread. See E280 and E281 for full descriptions. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e281': 'E281 / INS 281 — Sodium Propionate. Sodium salt of propionic acid; primary form used in bread as neutral pH mould inhibitor (the free acid form is slightly acidic and may affect bread flavour); provides the same anti-mould activity as propionic acid by releasing propionate in the dough matrix. Metabolised as a normal fatty acid. Some migraine sensitivity reports (weak evidence). JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'calcium propionate': """Calcium propionate (E282/INS 282) is the calcium salt of propionic acid (CH₃CH₂COOH), a short-chain fatty acid naturally produced in Swiss Emmental cheese by Propionibacterium freudenreichii (the same bacteria responsible for the characteristic holes/eyes) and in the human colon during dietary fibre fermentation. Commercially produced by neutralising propionic acid with calcium hydroxide. Calcium propionate is the single most widely used bread preservative globally — present in virtually all commercially sliced bread, sandwich loaves and packaged baked goods. Antimicrobial mechanism: at the slightly acidic pH of bread dough (pH 5.5–6.0), a proportion of calcium propionate remains in the undissociated free acid form; propionate as a lipophilic weak acid permeates fungal and bacterial cell membranes, disrupting intracellular pH homeostasis and inhibiting key metabolic enzymes (particularly pyruvate dehydrogenase), preventing energy production. Most effective against Aspergillus and Rhizopus mould species and the 'rope'-forming Bacillus subtilis and B. licheniformis bacteria. Does not inhibit baker's yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) at food use concentrations — critical, as yeast must continue to leaven the dough. As an antifungal: calcium propionate extends the shelf life of sliced bread by several days in ambient conditions; at typical bread use levels (1,500–3,000 mg/kg), it is highly effective in tropical climates where bread moulds rapidly. Additional calcium benefit: provides approximately 21% elemental calcium by weight; at food use concentrations the calcium contribution per slice is modest but meaningful. Metabolically: propionate is a completely normal human metabolite — gut bacteria produce ~500–1,500 mg propionate per day from fibre fermentation; absorbed propionate is transported to the liver and converted to succinyl-CoA in the Krebs cycle (gluconeogenic). Safety controversy: a 2002 Australian double-blind crossover study in children (Dengate & Ruben) reported that calcium propionate at levels typical of bread consumption worsened irritability, restlessness, inattention and sleep disruption — this has not been consistently replicated but is taken seriously enough by some parents to choose 'preservative-free bread.' The systematic evidence base remains limited; the conservative view is that children showing unexplained behaviour issues might benefit from a trial elimination of propionate-containing bread. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E282), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Generally considered safe for the broad population.""",
    'e282': 'E282 / INS 282 — Calcium Propionate. Most widely used bread mould inhibitor; also provides calcium; metabolised as normal fatty acid. See calcium propionate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'potassium propionate': """Potassium propionate (E283/INS 283) is the potassium salt of propionic acid, a naturally occurring short-chain fatty acid. Functionally identical to calcium propionate (E282) and sodium propionate (E281) as an antimould and antimicrobial preservative — used in bread, baked goods, flour tortillas, pastries and certain cheese products. Mechanism: undissociated propionic acid at the slightly acidic dough/food pH disrupts fungal cell metabolism by collapsing intracellular pH gradients, halting ATP production. Does not affect Saccharomyces cerevisiae at typical food use concentrations. Advantage over calcium propionate: in formulations where calcium content must be controlled (e.g., where calcium might interfere with gel formation, or in products already high in calcium), potassium propionate or sodium propionate provides the same antimould function without adding calcium. Advantage over sodium propionate: provides potassium (an essential mineral that helps counterbalance sodium's blood pressure effects) rather than sodium; useful in reduced-sodium product formulations. Metabolically: propionate is a normal metabolite of gut bacterial fermentation; fully converted in the liver to succinyl-CoA; no accumulation or toxic metabolites. Same potential behaviour concern as calcium propionate in children applies (see calcium propionate entry) — the propionic acid released is the active species regardless of counterion. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by EU (E283), CODEX and FSSAI. Used less widely than E282 but functionally equivalent.""",
    'e283': 'E283 / INS 283 — Potassium Propionate. Potassium salt of propionic acid; antimould preservative in baked goods; provides potassium rather than sodium or calcium. See potassium propionate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'e290': 'E290 is the EU/INS code for Carbon Dioxide. Used to carbonate drinks and preserve freshness. Safe -- the gas we exhale.',
    'malic acid': """Malic acid (E296/INS 296 / 2-hydroxybutanedioic acid / hydroxysuccinic acid / C₄H₆O₅) is a naturally occurring dicarboxylic acid and a key intermediate in the Krebs (citric acid) cycle — all aerobic organisms produce and metabolise malic acid continuously. Natural sources: the highest concentrations are found in green apples (the name derives from 'malum' — Latin for apple), cherries, grapes, rhubarb, quinces and many other fruits. Green apples can contain up to 15 g/L of malic acid, which gives them their characteristic sharp, tart taste. Two enantiomers exist: L-malic acid (the natural, biologically active form) and D-malic acid (rare in nature); commercial food-grade malic acid is typically a racemic mixture (DL-malic acid) produced by hydration of maleic or fumaric acid, or it may be the pure L-form from enzymatic bioconversion. Food functions: (1) acidulant and acidity regulator — provides a smooth, lingering tartness with less sharpness than citric acid; preferred in apple, cherry, watermelon, grape and berry-flavoured products where it closely matches the natural flavour profile; (2) flavour enhancer — rounds and enhances other flavour notes; widely used in hard candies, boiled sweets, chewing gum, soft drinks, fruit juices, yogurt and jellies; (3) flavouring ingredient in confectionery (the sour coating on sour jelly sweets is typically malic acid); (4) mild preservative — acidic pH inhibits microbial growth; (5) antioxidant synergist — chelates metal ions; (6) pH adjustment in winemaking (acidification of low-acid wines). India context: one of the most commonly used acids in confectionery (Gems, Hajmola, flavoured candies) and fruit beverages. Safety: L-malic acid is directly metabolised in the Krebs cycle — completely safe. D-malic acid (present in racemic commercial grade) is metabolised to a lesser extent. At high concentrations (as in very sour confectionery) it can contribute to dental enamel erosion — the pH depression from concentrated malic acid on tooth surfaces is comparable to citric acid. People with sensitive teeth or erosive enamel history should avoid frequent consumption of very sour sweets coated with concentrated malic acid. No systemic toxicity. ADI: not specified (JECFA — normal food constituent at approved uses). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E296), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan.""",
    'e296': 'E296 / INS 296 — Malic Acid. Natural apple and Krebs cycle acid; smooth, lingering tartness in beverages and confectionery; dental erosion risk with high-concentration sour coatings. See malic acid entry for full details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'fumaric acid': 'Fumaric Acid (E297/INS 297, trans-butenedioic acid) — see E297 below for full scientific description. Natural Krebs cycle acid; the most potent food acidulant per gram; non-hygroscopic (ideal for dry mixes); Krebs cycle intermediate. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e297': 'E297 / INS 297 — Fumaric Acid (trans-butenedioic acid, HOOC-CH=CH-COOH) is a naturally occurring unsaturated dicarboxylic acid and a key Krebs cycle intermediate (fumarate is produced from succinate and converted to malate). Naturally present in mushrooms, lichen and many plants. Commercially produced by fungal fermentation or from maleic acid. Outstanding properties as a food acidulant: (1) most acidic food-grade acid by weight — approximately 1.5× stronger than citric acid per gram, enabling significantly lower usage levels; (2) non-hygroscopic — unlike citric acid, fumaric acid does not absorb moisture from the air, making it ideal for dry mixes, drink powders and baking mixes that must remain free-flowing in humidity; (3) slow solubility — releases acidity slowly during baking (double-acting leavening behaviour); (4) mould inhibitor — at acidic pH it inhibits many spoilage organisms. Applications: dry beverage powders, baking mixes, leavening systems (combined with bicarbonate), confectionery, gelatine desserts and processed meats. Metabolically, fumarate is directly incorporated into the Krebs cycle. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',

    # ── Antioxidants (E300-E329) ─────────────────────────────────────────
    'e300': 'E300 is the EU/INS code for Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C). Essential vitamin and food antioxidant. Safe and nutritionally beneficial.',
    'sodium ascorbate': """Sodium ascorbate (E301/INS 301) is the sodium salt of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C), produced by reacting L-ascorbic acid with sodium hydroxide or sodium bicarbonate. It provides the full antioxidant activity of Vitamin C without the acidity of ascorbic acid itself — pH of a 5% aqueous solution is approximately 7.5 (slightly alkaline), making it far less tooth-erosive and stomach-irritating than ascorbic acid. Functions in food: (1) antioxidant preservative — prevents oxidative browning in cured meats, prevents colour fading in beverages and inhibits lipid oxidation in prepared foods; (2) curing accelerator in processed meats — rapidly reduces metmyoglobin and promotes nitric oxide availability, accelerating the development of the characteristic pink cured meat colour; works synergistically with sodium nitrite; (3) flour treatment agent — oxidises thiol groups in gluten proteins; (4) vitamin C fortification — provides dietary vitamin C in fortified foods and beverages where the acidity of ascorbic acid would be undesirable or affect flavour. Sodium content: provides approximately 12% sodium by weight; at typical food use concentrations (50–500 mg/kg), the sodium contribution is negligible. As a vitamin C supplement: equivalent in biological activity to ascorbic acid at equivalent molar doses; well-tolerated as a buffered supplement for people with GI sensitivity to ascorbic acid. The body treats ascorbate anions identically regardless of whether they are the free acid or sodium salt form. ADI: not separately specified (same as ascorbic acid, JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E301), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Generally very safe; no known adverse effects at food use or supplemental levels.""",
    'e301': 'E301/INS 301 is sodium ascorbate, the sodium salt of Vitamin C. Non-acidic antioxidant preservative used in cured meats, beverages and fortified foods. See the sodium ascorbate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'calcium ascorbate': """Calcium ascorbate (E302/INS 302) is the calcium salt of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C), formed by reacting ascorbic acid with calcium carbonate or calcium hydroxide. A white to slightly off-white crystalline powder with neutral pH (~6–7 in solution), making it the gentlest form of Vitamin C supplement — essentially non-acidic and non-tooth-erosive. Dual function as both a vitamin C source and a well-absorbed calcium supplement. Elemental calcium content: approximately 11% by weight; provides a modest but meaningful calcium contribution at supplement doses. Vitamin C bioavailability: calcium ascorbate delivers ascorbate anion with identical bioavailability to ascorbic acid; the calcium form is particularly useful for people with sensitive stomachs or erosion-prone tooth enamel who cannot tolerate standard ascorbic acid supplements. In food: functions as an antioxidant preservative, curing aid in processed meats, and vitamin C fortification agent — the same roles as other ascorbate salts. Preferred over sodium ascorbate in formulations where sodium reduction is a goal, or where the additional calcium is nutritionally desirable. Stability: somewhat more stable than ascorbic acid to oxidation under neutral and slightly alkaline pH conditions. Safe at food use and supplemental doses. No significant adverse effects. ADI: not separately specified (same as ascorbic acid). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E302), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e302': 'E302/INS 302 is calcium ascorbate, a buffered, non-acidic form of Vitamin C. Antioxidant preservative and calcium supplement. See calcium ascorbate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'ascorbyl palmitate': """Ascorbyl palmitate (E304i/INS 304 / ascorbyl-6-palmitate / L-ascorbic acid 6-palmitate) is a fat-soluble ester of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) and palmitic acid (a C16 saturated fatty acid), produced by esterification at the 6-hydroxyl position of ascorbic acid. Unlike water-soluble vitamin C, ascorbyl palmitate is lipophilic and dissolves readily in oils and fats, allowing it to function as an antioxidant within the fat phase of food products and at oil-water interfaces — where water-soluble ascorbic acid cannot reach effectively. Mechanism: the ascorbate moiety donates electrons to lipid peroxyl radicals and singlet oxygen species, halting the oxidative chain reaction. Particularly effective at protecting polyunsaturated fatty acids in cooking oils, margarines, infant formula fats and bakery shortenings. Used in: refined cooking oils, margarines, infant formula (fat fraction), bakery products with high fat content, powdered soups and cosmetic products (oil-phase antioxidant and skin-conditioning agent). Typical food use concentration: 50–200 mg/kg fat. As a vitamin C source: ascorbyl palmitate is partially hydrolysed in the gastrointestinal tract to free ascorbic acid and palmitic acid, providing some (but not full equivalent) vitamin C activity. Palmitic acid is a common saturated fatty acid with minimal cardiovascular impact at normal intake levels. Stability: significantly more stable in fatty food matrices than water-soluble ascorbic acid, which is prone to oxidative degradation in aqueous systems. No significant toxicological concerns — both hydrolysis products (ascorbic acid and palmitic acid) are normal food constituents. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E304), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; no animal-derived components.""",
    'e304': 'E304/INS 304 is ascorbyl palmitate, a fat-soluble ester of Vitamin C. Antioxidant in oils, fats and infant formula. See ascorbyl palmitate entry for full details. Safe.',
    'e306': 'E306 is the EU/INS code for Tocopherol-rich extract (Vitamin E). Natural antioxidant. Safe and nutritionally beneficial.',
    'e307': 'E307 / INS 307 — Alpha-Tocopherol (2R,4\'R,8\'R-alpha-tocopherol) is the most biologically active form of Vitamin E — the form preferentially retained, transported and maintained in human plasma and tissues. It is produced by methylation of gamma-tocopherol and occurs naturally in high-oleic sunflower oil, wheat germ oil, almond oil and olive oil. As a food antioxidant, alpha-tocopherol donates a hydrogen atom from its phenolic hydroxyl group to peroxyl radicals generated during lipid oxidation, terminating the oxidative chain reaction and preventing rancidity in oils and fatty foods. Nutritional significance: alpha-tocopherol is the sole form of Vitamin E that the liver preferentially incorporates into VLDL for re-secretion into plasma via the alpha-tocopherol transfer protein (alpha-TTP). Functions include: (1) membrane antioxidant — protects polyunsaturated fatty acids in cell membranes from oxidative damage; (2) immune modulator — optimises T-cell function; (3) anti-inflammatory — inhibits protein kinase C and platelet aggregation; (4) neuroprotective — Vitamin E deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy and ataxia; (5) skin protection — topical application provides some photoprotection against UV-induced lipid peroxidation. Used in infant formula, edible oils, margarines, meat products and nutritional supplements. Safe; ADI not specified as a food additive. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e308': 'E308 / INS 308 — Gamma-Tocopherol (gamma-tocopherol, C₂₈H₄₈O₂) is a natural form of Vitamin E found in abundance in soybean oil (~55–65% of tocopherols), corn oil (~80% of tocopherols), rapeseed/canola oil and sesame oil. Unlike alpha-tocopherol, which is primarily the biological Vitamin E form in human plasma, gamma-tocopherol excels as a chemical antioxidant in protecting food oils from rancidity — particularly against reactive nitrogen species (RNS) generated during processing, since gamma-tocopherol can trap peroxynitrite (a powerful oxidant) by electrophilic nitrosation at C-5 of its chromanol ring (alpha-tocopherol cannot, due to its extra methyl group blocking this position). Recent nutritional research suggests gamma-tocopherol may have unique biological activities: (1) anti-inflammatory — natriuretic activity in the kidney through inhibition of prostaglandin PGE₂ and leukotriene LTB₄ synthesis; (2) anticarcinogenic — epidemiological data tentatively link higher gamma-tocopherol intake with reduced prostate and colorectal cancer risk; (3) cardiovascular — inhibits platelet aggregation via distinct mechanisms from alpha-tocopherol. Commonly used in food manufacturing as a natural antioxidant in soy-derived and corn-derived oils and food products. Safe; ADI not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e309': 'E309 / INS 309 — Delta-Tocopherol (delta-tocopherol, C₂₇H₄₆O₂) is the Vitamin E form with the least methylation of the chromanol ring head group (only one methyl group, at position 8). This structural simplicity gives delta-tocopherol the weakest biological Vitamin E activity of the four tocopherol forms in the classic DPPPH radical assay — but paradoxically, it is the most potent radical-trapping antioxidant in lipid systems and the best inhibitor of lipid oxidation chain reactions under food processing conditions. This is because the unmethylated positions on the chromanol ring make it more reactive towards the peroxyl radicals that initiate rancidity. Delta-tocopherol is particularly abundant in soybean oil and palm oil. In food antioxidant applications, it is often the preferred form for protecting highly unsaturated oils (containing DHA, EPA, linolenic acid) because of its superior chemical antioxidant activity. It is frequently used in combination with rosemary extract and ascorbyl palmitate (mixed antioxidant systems provide superior protection through synergy). Emerging research suggests delta-tocopherol may have anti-proliferative activity against certain cancer cell lines — but evidence is preliminary. Safe; ADI not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'propyl gallate': 'Propyl Gallate (E310/INS 310) is a synthetic antioxidant in fats and oils. Associated with tumour promotion in animal studies; banned in baby foods in the EU; under EFSA review. Approved with quantity limits for general food use.',
    'e310': 'E310 is the EU/INS code for Propyl Gallate. Synthetic antioxidant under regulatory review; banned in baby foods in EU.',
    'octyl gallate': """Octyl gallate (E311/INS 311 / n-octyl gallate / octyl 3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoate) is a synthetic antioxidant — the n-octyl ester of gallic acid (3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoic acid), a naturally occurring polyphenolic acid found in many plant foods. Produced by esterification of gallic acid with n-octanol. Part of the gallate antioxidant family along with propyl gallate (E310) and dodecyl gallate (E312), differing in the chain length of the alcohol ester group, which determines lipophilicity and thus distribution within fatty food matrices. Mechanism: the trihydroxybenzene (pyrogallol) ring donates hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxyl radicals, terminating the oxidative chain reaction; also chelates pro-oxidant metal ions (Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺) via the hydroxyl groups. Used in: edible fats and oils, lard, margarine, shortenings, biscuits, crackers and other fat-containing products. Typically used at 50–200 mg/kg fat. Gallates (E310-E312) share potential cross-reactivity concerns: rare cases of contact allergy and pseudo-allergic reactions (urticaria, asthma) have been reported, primarily to propyl gallate; octyl gallate may cross-react in sensitised individuals. EFSA safety review (2014): EFSA established ADIs for propyl gallate (0.07 mg/kg bw/day) and lowered the group ADI for gallates; octyl gallate was removed from the EU positive list in 2016 as EFSA could not confirm safety at the then-maximum use levels due to data gaps — it is no longer approved in the EU but remains approved in CODEX, India (FSSAI) and some other jurisdictions. People with aspirin sensitivity or salicylate sensitivity should avoid gallate antioxidants. Ban from baby foods in EU. JECFA ADI: 0.5 mg/kg bw/day (ocytl and dodecyl gallate combined). Check current regulatory status in your jurisdiction.""",
    'e311': 'E311 / INS 311 — Octyl Gallate. Synthetic gallate antioxidant; removed from EU positive list in 2016 due to safety data gaps; still permitted in some jurisdictions including India. See octyl gallate entry for full details.',
    'dodecyl gallate': """Dodecyl gallate (E312/INS 312 / lauryl gallate / n-dodecyl gallate / dodecyl 3,4,5-trihydroxybenzoate) is a synthetic antioxidant — the n-dodecyl (lauryl, C12) ester of gallic acid. The longest chain in the food-approved gallate series (propyl = C3, octyl = C8, dodecyl = C12), making it the most lipophilic of the three and the most soluble in oils and fats. Mechanism: same as propyl gallate and octyl gallate — the trihydroxybenzene ring donates hydrogen atoms to lipid peroxyl radicals and chelates pro-oxidant metals. The greater lipophilicity of dodecyl gallate means it partitions strongly into the fat phase, making it particularly effective in deep frying oils and fat-rich products. Used in: edible fats, refined cooking oils, lard, dripping, margarine, fat-containing biscuits, crackers and snacks. Also used in cosmetic and pharmaceutical preparations as an antioxidant stabiliser. Similar safety concerns as octyl gallate (E311): EFSA 2014 safety review found data gaps; dodecyl gallate was also removed from the EU permitted list; remains approved by CODEX and FSSAI in India and other jurisdictions. Potential for cross-allergic reactions in gallate-sensitive individuals. People with aspirin sensitivity or salicylate sensitivity should avoid gallate antioxidants. Ban from baby foods (EU). JECFA ADI: 0.5 mg/kg bw/day for octyl + dodecyl gallate combined. Declining commercial use as manufacturers switch to tocopherols and rosemary extract for clean-label positioning.""",
    'e312': 'E312 / INS 312 — Dodecyl Gallate (Lauryl Gallate). Synthetic antioxidant; most lipophilic gallate; removed from EU approved list due to data gaps; still permitted in India and other jurisdictions. See dodecyl gallate entry for full details.',
    'sodium lactate': 'Sodium Lactate (E325/INS 325) is the sodium salt of L-lactic acid — a clear, slightly viscous, hygroscopic liquid produced by neutralising fermentation-derived lactic acid with sodium hydroxide. In food manufacturing it functions as: (1) humectant — strongly hygroscopic; retains moisture in baked goods, confectionery and meat products, extending shelf life and maintaining texture; (2) acidity regulator and pH buffer — buffering capacity in the pH 4.8–6.2 range; (3) antimicrobial synergist — enhances the effectiveness of other preservatives (sodium diacetate, nisin) in cooked meats; (4) flavour enhancer — imparts a mild, clean, meaty flavour note in processed meats; (5) low-sodium alternative component. Well tolerated; metabolised identically to L-lactate (Krebs cycle). ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e325': 'E325 / INS 325 — Sodium Lactate. Fermentation-derived sodium salt of lactic acid; humectant, pH buffer and antimicrobial synergist in meats and bakery products. See sodium lactate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'potassium lactate': 'Potassium Lactate (E326/INS 326) is the potassium salt of L-lactic acid — functions similarly to sodium lactate as a humectant, pH buffer and antimicrobial synergist in processed meats and poultry, with the advantage of contributing potassium rather than sodium. Particularly useful in reduced-sodium meat product formulations where sodium replacement with potassium is desired. Potassium is an essential mineral that counterbalances the blood-pressure effects of sodium. Metabolised as L-lactate — normal Krebs cycle substrate. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e326': 'E326 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Lactate. Antimicrobial in processed meats. Safe.',
    'calcium lactate': 'Calcium Lactate (E327/INS 327, Ca(C₃H₅O₃)₂·5H₂O) is the calcium salt of lactic acid — a white crystalline powder that is highly water-soluble and readily releases calcium ions. As a food additive it functions as: (1) firming agent in fresh-cut fruits and vegetables — calcium ions cross-link pectin in plant cell walls, maintaining firmness and preventing mushiness during storage (particularly important in bagged salads, fresh-cut melon and ready-to-eat produce); (2) calcium fortification — an exceptionally well-absorbed, stomach-friendly calcium source used in fortified beverages, infant formula, calcium-fortified juices and vitamin gummies; (3) bread dough conditioner — calcium strengthens gluten; (4) anti-caking and stabiliser in dry mixes. Compared to calcium carbonate (poorly soluble, can cause constipation) and calcium citrate (well-absorbed but lower calcium content), calcium lactate offers excellent solubility and bioavailability (~32% elemental calcium by molecular weight) with good GI tolerability. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e327': 'E327 / INS 327 — Calcium Lactate. Well-absorbed calcium supplement and firming agent in fresh produce and fortified foods. See calcium lactate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'tartaric acid': 'Tartaric Acid (E334/INS 334, 2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acid, C₄H₆O₆) is a naturally occurring dicarboxylic acid — the primary organic acid of grapes (0.5–1% of grape juice weight), tamarind, and bananas. It exists as three stereoisomers: L-(+)-tartaric acid (natural, levorotatory — the most common food form), D-(-)-tartaric acid (rare), and meso-tartaric acid (achiral). L-tartaric acid is responsible for the characteristic crisp tartness of dry wines and the acidity of tamarind. Production: extracted from wine production by-products (cream of tartar, wine lees) or synthetically from malic acid. Food functions: (1) acidity regulator — clean, tart taste; (2) baking powder component — reacts with bicarbonate to produce CO₂; (3) confectionery — grape flavour in jellies, candies; (4) beverages — acidulant in grape drinks; (5) antioxidant synergy — chelates metal ions; (6) cream of tartar (potassium hydrogen tartrate) stabilises egg white foams. Metabolically, L-tartaric acid is largely unmetabolised in humans (minimal enzymatic capacity) and is excreted unchanged in urine. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e334': 'E334 / INS 334 — Tartaric Acid. Natural grape and tamarind acid; clean, crisp acidity; baking powder component. See tartaric acid for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e335': 'E335 / INS 335 — Sodium Tartrates (Monosodium L-tartrate, E335(i); Disodium L-tartrate, E335(ii)) are sodium salts of tartaric acid. Used as acidity regulators and buffering agents in food and pharmaceutical manufacturing. The disodium salt is less acidic than tartaric acid itself and provides pH-buffering in the pH 4–6 range. Applications: pH adjustment in beverages and confectionery; stabiliser in cream fillings; pharmaceutical excipient. Safety: metabolised similarly to tartaric acid (largely excreted unchanged). No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e336': 'E336 / INS 336 — Potassium Tartrates (Cream of Tartar / Potassium Bitartrate, E336(i); Dipotassium Tartrate, E336(ii)) are potassium salts of tartaric acid, the most important of which is potassium bitartrate (KHC₄H₄O₆), universally known as "cream of tartar." Cream of tartar is a by-product of wine making — it crystallises from fermenting grape juice and is purified from wine lees. Culinary functions: (1) egg white stabiliser — prevents overcoagulation of proteins during beating, enabling meringues and soufflés to maintain volume; (2) leavening acid — reacts with sodium bicarbonate at room temperature to release CO₂ immediately (fast-acting baking powder component); (3) prevents sugar crystallisation in caramel and candy; (4) copper bowl alternative — prevents overbeating of egg whites. Also used to stabilise tartaric acid solutions in acidic beverages. Safe; potassium source. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e337': 'E337 is the EU/INS code for Sodium Potassium Tartrate (Rochelle Salt). Acidity regulator. Safe.',
    'e340': 'E340 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Phosphates. Acidity regulators. Safe at approved levels.',
    'calcium phosphate': """Calcium phosphates (E341/INS 341) comprise a group of calcium salts of phosphoric acid used as food additives — primarily monocalcium phosphate (E341i / MCP / Ca(H₂PO₄)₂), dicalcium phosphate (E341ii / DCP / CaHPO₄) and tricalcium phosphate (E341iii / TCP / Ca₃(PO₄)₂). Each has different solubility and function. MCP (monocalcium phosphate): the primary leavening acid in baking powder — reacts with sodium bicarbonate in two stages (double-acting) to release CO₂, responsible for rise in cakes, biscuits, muffins and self-raising flour; very soluble; fast-acting in dry mixing, slower in the oven. DCP (dicalcium phosphate): less soluble; used as a slow-acting leavening acid, calcium supplement in fortified foods, mineral supplement in infant formula, mild abrasive in toothpaste and as an anti-caking agent. TCP (tricalcium phosphate): essentially insoluble in water; used primarily as an anti-caking and flow agent in powdered foods, salt, seasoning blends and spice powders; also as a calcium supplement (provides calcium in a form absorbed in the gut via vitamin D-dependent mechanism). All three forms are significant sources of dietary calcium and phosphorus. Nutritional benefits: calcium is essential for bone density, muscle contraction, nerve transmission and blood coagulation. Foods fortified with calcium phosphate (infant formula, breakfast cereals, flour) make meaningful contributions to daily calcium intake — particularly important in India where dairy consumption varies widely. Phosphate concern: the same concern applies as for other phosphate additives — people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) have impaired phosphate excretion and must restrict total phosphate intake from all sources; food-additive phosphates (highly bioavailable inorganic phosphate) contribute more to serum phosphate than natural food phosphorus (organic phosphate, lower bioavailability). ADI: not specified (JECFA; included in group ADI for phosphoric acid and phosphates, 70 mg/kg body weight/day as phosphorus). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E341), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e341': 'E341 is the EU/INS code for Calcium Phosphates. Raising agents and calcium supplements. Safe.',
    'adipic acid': 'Adipic Acid (E355/INS 355, hexanedioic acid, HOOC-(CH₂)₄-COOH) is the most commercially important dicarboxylic acid — an industrial-scale chemical produced in vast quantities globally, primarily as a monomer for nylon (polyamide) production. The food-grade fraction is used as an acidity regulator and leavening acid in baked goods, confectionery, gelatine desserts and beverages. Properties as a food acidulant: non-hygroscopic solid with excellent chemical stability; slightly less tart than citric acid; provides a "clean" acid taste without metallic or astringent notes. Key advantage in baking powder formulations: adipic acid reacts with sodium bicarbonate slowly at room temperature but rapidly at oven temperature (double-acting behaviour) — particularly useful in cake mixes and muffins where a longer mixing-to-baking window is needed. Metabolically, adipic acid enters the Krebs cycle as a six-carbon dicarboxylic acid substrate and is oxidised to CO₂ and water. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e355': 'E355 / INS 355 — Adipic Acid. Clean, non-hygroscopic acidulant and slow-acting baking powder leavening acid. See adipic acid for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'succinic acid': 'Succinic Acid (E363/INS 363, butanedioic acid, HOOC-CH₂-CH₂-COOH) is a four-carbon dicarboxylic acid that is one of the key intermediates of the Krebs cycle — every aerobic cell in every organism (bacteria, fungi, plants, humans) continually produces and consumes succinic acid in mitochondrial energy metabolism. It is found naturally in unripe fruits, amber (fossilised tree resin — the Latin name succinum means amber), wine and fermented foods. Commercially produced by microbial fermentation (bio-based, using engineered Escherichia coli or Basfia succiniciproducens) or petrochemically. Food functions: acidulant (mild sourness); pH buffer; flavour enhancer (imparts a characteristic meaty, brothy umami note — one of the acids responsible for the complex taste of fermented soy products, wine and aged cheese); sequestrant. Used in: dry beverages, confectionery, condiments and flavour preparations. Excellent safety profile — a normal cellular metabolite; completely metabolised. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e363': 'E363 / INS 363 — Succinic Acid. Natural Krebs cycle acid with mild sourness and flavour-enhancing properties. See succinic acid for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'gluconic acid': 'Gluconic Acid (E574/INS 574, D-gluconic acid, C₆H₁₂O₇) is a mild organic acid produced by oxidation of the aldehyde group of glucose to a carboxyl group — a reaction performed industrially by Aspergillus niger or Gluconobacter oxidans fermentation of glucose solutions. It is naturally present in honey (~0.5–1%), grape juice, wine, beer, miso and many fermented foods. Properties: clean, mild, non-bitter, non-astringent sourness — often described as "round" and "pleasant" compared to the sharp tartness of citric or malic acid; pH ~3.7 as a 10% solution. Food functions: (1) acidulant and pH adjuster — frequently preferred in products where a smooth, understated acidity is desired (dairy, beverages, mild sauces); (2) sequestrant — gluconate anions chelate divalent and trivalent metal ions (calcium, iron, magnesium), preventing metal-catalysed oxidation; (3) dispersant for calcium salts (calcium gluconate is exceptionally soluble). Its lactone (cyclised form), glucono delta-lactone (GDL, E575), is a more practically useful food additive (see GDL). Metabolically: gluconic acid is metabolised via the pentose phosphate pathway without any metabolic concerns. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e574': 'E574 / INS 574 — Gluconic Acid. Mild, clean food acid from fermentation; sequestrant; precursor to GDL. See gluconic acid for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'glucono delta-lactone': """Glucono delta-lactone (GDL / δ-gluconolactone / E575/INS 575) is the cyclic ester (lactone) of D-gluconic acid, produced industrially by the controlled oxidation of glucose using Aspergillus niger fermentation or glucose oxidase enzyme, followed by crystallisation. It occurs naturally in honey, fruit juices and wine at low concentrations. Chemical nature: a six-membered ring (δ-lactone) that hydrolyses slowly and spontaneously in aqueous solution to gluconic acid — a mild organic acid (pKa ~3.7) — this hydrolysis rate is temperature-dependent (faster when warm), giving GDL a unique property: it is a controlled-release acidifier. The delayed, progressive acidification distinguishes it from direct food acids such as citric acid or lactic acid, which acidify immediately. Food applications: (1) silken tofu and bean curd — GDL is the standard coagulant for silken-style tofu (kinugoshi tofu); it releases acid slowly within the hot soybean milk, denaturing and coagulating soy proteins uniformly without forming a grainy, lumpy texture (unlike calcium sulfate, which coagulates faster and produces a firmer, more porous curd); (2) fermented-style processed sausages (mettwurst, salami) — GDL accelerates the acidification of meat emulsions to a target pH of 4.8–5.2 without requiring prolonged bacterial fermentation, improving both safety and consistency; (3) leavening agent in baked goods — slow CO₂ release from sodium bicarbonate when used with GDL; (4) cheese curd acidification; (5) cold-set meat and surimi products. Health: gluconic acid is a normal human metabolite — produced by glucose metabolism and present in blood at low concentrations. GDL is completely hydrolysed in the GI tract to gluconic acid and then fermented by gut bacteria or absorbed and excreted. No toxicity, mutagenicity or carcinogenicity concerns. Suitable for vegans and vegetarians. ADI: not specified (JECFA). FSSAI, EU (E575), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved.""",
    'e575': 'E575 / INS 575 — Glucono Delta-Lactone (GDL). See INS 575 for full scientific description — a natural cyclic ester of gluconic acid that hydrolyses slowly in water to release a mild acid; key in silken tofu production and controlled meat acidification. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'sodium gluconate': 'Sodium Gluconate (E576/INS 576) is the sodium salt of gluconic acid. It is an efficient, non-toxic sequestrant (chelating agent) that forms stable water-soluble complexes with calcium, iron, copper, aluminium and other multivalent metal ions. This chelation: (1) prevents metal-catalysed oxidation reactions that cause off-flavours, rancidity and colour deterioration in food; (2) softens hard water in industrial cleaning and food processing equipment (better than EDTA in highly alkaline conditions). Used in: food processing as a sequestrant; cleaning and descaling formulations in food processing plants; pharmaceutical as an excipient; concrete retarder and plasticiser in construction (one of the largest industrial applications). In food products: inhibits the calcium-bridging that can cause undesirable gelling or precipitation in dairy drinks and beverages. Safety: rapidly metabolised via the pentose phosphate pathway. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e576': 'E576 / INS 576 — Sodium Gluconate. Efficient sequestrant and metal chelator; see sodium gluconate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'potassium gluconate': 'Potassium Gluconate (E577/INS 577) is the potassium salt of gluconic acid — a highly soluble, easily absorbed organic potassium salt. In food and dietary supplements, potassium gluconate is preferred over inorganic potassium chloride as a potassium supplement because: (1) excellent water solubility; (2) mild, non-bitter taste at supplemental doses; (3) well-tolerated gastrointestinal absorption without the metallic/bitter off-taste of KCl; (4) acid-base neutral (gluconate is metabolised to CO₂ and water, producing a mildly alkalinising effect in the kidney unlike KCl). Used in: dietary supplements (electrolyte tablets, sports nutrition), low-sodium salt replacement formulations, and as a food-grade sequestrant similar to sodium gluconate. Potassium is an essential mineral — important for nerve transmission, muscle function, cardiac function and blood pressure regulation; recommended daily intake 3,500–4,700 mg/day. Potassium gluconate is an excellent, well-tolerated form of this essential nutrient. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e577': 'E577 / INS 577 — Potassium Gluconate. Well-tolerated potassium supplement and food-grade sequestrant. See potassium gluconate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'calcium gluconate': 'Calcium Gluconate (E578/INS 578, Ca(C₆H₁₁O₇)₂) is the calcium salt of gluconic acid — one of the most soluble and best-tolerated calcium salts available. Medical significance: calcium gluconate injection (10% solution) is the first-line emergency treatment for severe hyperkalaemia (life-threatening high blood potassium that threatens cardiac arrhythmia) and for calcium-channel blocker or hydrofluoric acid toxicity — it acts within minutes to stabilise cardiac membranes. Also used as: (1) food calcium fortification — highly bioavailable calcium source added to plant milks (oat, almond, soy), fortified juices and infant formula; (2) food processing aid — firming agent in canned vegetables and fruits (calcium ions cross-link pectin); (3) sequestrant — chelates metal ions; (4) pharmaceutical — calcium supplement tablets (well-tolerated, high calcium content ~9% elemental calcium). Compared to calcium carbonate (40% calcium) or calcium citrate (21% calcium), calcium gluconate (9% calcium) requires larger tablet sizes for equivalent calcium delivery but is exceptionally well-absorbed and causes no constipation. No toxicological concerns at food/supplement levels. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e578': 'E578 / INS 578 — Calcium Gluconate. Highly soluble, well-tolerated calcium supplement and firming agent; also an emergency cardiac medicine. See calcium gluconate for details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'ferrous gluconate': """Ferrous gluconate (E579/INS 579) is the ferrous (Fe²⁺) iron salt of gluconic acid — an organic iron compound produced by reacting ferrous sulfate with gluconic acid or glucono delta-lactone. A green-grey to yellowish-grey powder with a slight characteristic odour. In food, it has two quite different applications: (1) Colour fixative for black olives — ferrous gluconate is applied to lye-cured ripe (californian-style) black olives. Lye curing produces a mottled brown-green-black colour; treatment with ferrous gluconate produces a uniformly jet-black colour by chelating with tannins and phenolic compounds in the olive flesh to form stable iron-phenolate complexes. This is a purely cosmetic application that makes the product commercially more appealing; European black olive varieties (e.g. Kalamata, Niçoise) do not require this treatment as they are naturally black. (2) Iron supplement and fortification ingredient — ferrous gluconate has moderate bioavailability as an iron source (~12% elemental iron by weight, absorbed at ~7–12% of dose) and is generally well-tolerated with less GI irritation than ferrous sulfate, though slightly less bioavailable. Used to fortify breakfast cereals, infant formula, salt and cooking oils (particularly in FSSAI iron fortification programmes). It imparts minimal off-taste at fortification levels. WHO/FAD and FSSAI include ferrous gluconate in food fortification guidelines for addressing iron deficiency anaemia — a major public health concern in India, where ~50% of women and ~58% of children under 5 are anaemic. Health note: the elemental iron in food contributes meaningfully to daily iron intake; those with haemochromatosis (iron overload disorder) should note iron from fortified foods. No toxicity at approved levels. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E579), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e579': 'E579/INS 579 is ferrous gluconate, an organic iron salt used to colour black olives and as an iron fortification ingredient. See ferrous gluconate entry for full details. Safe.',

    # ── Emulsifiers / Hydrocolloids (E400-E499) ──────────────────────────
    'alginic acid': 'Alginic Acid (E400/INS 400) — see INS 400 for full scientific description. Natural brown seaweed cell-wall polysaccharide; basis for all alginate food additives; soluble dietary fibre with cholesterol-lowering and prebiotic properties. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e400': 'E400 / INS 400 — Alginic Acid. Natural polysaccharide from brown kelp seaweed; linear copolymer of mannuronic and guluronic acid; basis for the entire alginate family. See INS 400 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'sodium alginate': """Sodium alginate (E401/INS 401) is the sodium salt of alginic acid, a natural linear polysaccharide extracted from the cell walls of brown seaweed species including Laminaria hyperborea, Macrocystis pyrifera and Ascophyllum nodosum. Alginic acid is a block copolymer of two uronic acid monomers: D-mannuronic acid (M) and L-guluronic acid (G), arranged in homopolymeric blocks (poly-M, poly-G) and alternating MG sequences. The M:G ratio varies by species and determines gel properties — high-G alginates form firm, brittle, heat-stable gels; high-M alginates form softer, more elastic gels that are more freeze-thaw stable. Sodium alginate dissolves freely in cold or warm water to form viscous, pseudoplastic solutions. Gelation is induced by calcium ions (Ca²⁺), which ionotropically crosslink adjacent poly-G sequences through 'egg-box' coordination — this is the basis of calcium alginate spherification in molecular gastronomy (the Ferran Adrià-popularised technique). Food uses: stabiliser and thickener in ice cream (controls ice crystal formation), salad dressings, bakery fillings, restructured fish and meat products (forms a heat-stable gel binding), instant noodles, cream cheese and processed foods. Also used in pharmaceutical tablet coatings and wound dressings. As a dietary fibre: sodium alginate is a viscous soluble fibre that binds bile acids in the intestine, reducing cholesterol reabsorption; clinical trials show 4–7 g/day sodium alginate supplementation reduces LDL cholesterol by 5–7%. Also has prebiotic properties. Very low allergen risk. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E401), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; sustainably sourced from seaweed.""",
    'e401': 'E401/INS 401 is sodium alginate, a seaweed-derived thickener and gelling agent. See sodium alginate entry for full details. Safe; prebiotic dietary fibre.',
    'potassium alginate': """Potassium alginate (E402/INS 402) is the potassium salt of alginic acid from brown seaweed (Laminaria, Macrocystis and related species). Produced by extraction of alginic acid and neutralisation with potassium hydroxide rather than sodium hydroxide (as in sodium alginate production). Chemically and functionally very similar to sodium alginate — both are soluble in cold water, forming viscous solutions that gel in the presence of calcium ions via the same egg-box ionic crosslinking mechanism. Key distinction: potassium alginate is the preferred choice in formulations targeting sodium reduction — it provides all the functional properties of sodium alginate (thickening, gelling, stabilising, emulsion stabilisation, encapsulation) without contributing dietary sodium. Used in: dairy products, beverages, ice cream, bakery fillings, pharmaceutical tablet coatings and some dietary supplement capsules where low sodium is required. Also relevant for people on strict sodium-restricted diets (heart failure, hypertension, chronic kidney disease). Provides a modest dietary potassium contribution. Non-digestible soluble fibre with mild cholesterol-reducing and prebiotic effects similar to sodium alginate. No significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E402), CODEX and FSSAI. Vegan; seaweed-derived.""",
    'e402': 'E402 / INS 402 — Potassium Alginate is the potassium salt of alginic acid from brown seaweed. Functionally similar to sodium alginate (E401) but preferred when sodium reduction is a formulation goal. In the presence of calcium ions it forms the same ionic cross-linked gel as sodium alginate. Used in dairy products, beverages and dietary supplements. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'e403': 'E403 / INS 403 — Ammonium Alginate is the ammonium salt of alginic acid from brown seaweed. Functions as a thickener, stabiliser and gelling agent in food applications. The ammonium moiety is metabolised normally via the urea cycle. Less commonly used than sodium or potassium alginate; occasionally preferred for its slightly different solubility and rheological profile. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'calcium alginate': 'Calcium Alginate (E404/INS 404) is the calcium salt of alginic acid — unlike sodium alginate (which is freely water-soluble), calcium alginate is water-insoluble and forms solid gels spontaneously. This insolubility is both its strength and its primary food-use limitation. Key applications: (1) wound dressings — calcium alginate fibres are the most widely used dressing for highly exuding wounds; they absorb large amounts of wound fluid, forming a soft gel that maintains a moist wound environment and can be removed without damaging healing tissue; (2) molecular gastronomy spherification — the reverse spherification technique places an alginate-containing ingredient into a calcium chloride bath, forming a gel skin around a liquid core; (3) food restructuring — used to form "reformed" fruit or vegetable pieces; (4) dental impressions (in combination with other alginates). ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'propylene glycol alginate': """Propylene glycol alginate (PGA / E405/INS 405) is a partial ester of alginic acid (extracted from brown seaweed) with propylene glycol, produced by reacting alginic acid with propylene oxide. The propylene glycol ester groups (typically 50–85% esterification) interrupt the carboxylate groups on the alginate backbone that would otherwise form gels with calcium or precipitate at low pH. This makes PGA uniquely functional in acidic beverages and high-calcium systems where unmodified sodium alginate (E401) would precipitate or form unwanted gels. Unique functional advantage: PGA is the only alginate that is both acid-stable (pH 2–7 without precipitation) and calcium-tolerant — making it the alginate of choice for acid beverages and some dairy applications. Functions: emulsifier, thickener, stabiliser and foam stabiliser in: (1) salad dressings and mayonnaise-style products (acid-stable thickening); (2) beer and ale foam stabilisation — PGA adsorbs at the interface of CO₂ bubbles and stabilises the beer head/foam, one of its most distinctive applications; (3) fruit-flavoured beverages and fruit drink concentrates; (4) ice cream and frozen desserts; (5) bakery cream fillings. Metabolism: ester bonds are hydrolysed in the gut releasing alginic acid (non-digestible dietary fibre, excreted) and propylene glycol (absorbed, metabolised to lactic acid). Propylene glycol from food-grade PGA at typical use concentrations (0.1–0.5%) is considered safe — JECFA ADI for propylene glycol from all food sources is 25 mg/kg body weight/day. Approved by EU (E405), CODEX, FSSAI and most markets. USA: FDA limits its use in specific applications (21 CFR 172.858) rather than broad GRAS approval. Generally safe at food use concentrations.""",
    'e405': 'E405/INS 405 is Propylene Glycol Alginate (PGA), an acid-stable seaweed-derived emulsifier and thickener. See propylene glycol alginate for full details. Safe at approved levels.',
    'agar': """Agar (E406/INS 406 / agar-agar / kanten) is a natural polysaccharide extracted from the cell walls of red algae, primarily Gelidium amansii, Gracilaria and related species. It consists of two fractions: agarose (the gelling fraction — approximately 70%; a linear polymer of alternating 3,6-anhydro-L-galactopyranose and D-galactopyranose units linked by alternating α-1,3 and β-1,4 glycosidic bonds) and agaropectin (the non-gelling sulfonated fraction — approximately 30%). Agar has been used in East Asian cuisine for centuries: Japan ('kanten'), Indonesia, and India (used in traditional sweets and jellies). Unique thermal behaviour: agar gels form (set) on cooling below 32–40°C, but the gels do not melt again until heated to 85–95°C — a temperature hysteresis of over 50°C. This means agar gels are stable at room temperature and body temperature, unlike gelatine (which melts at ~35°C in the mouth and at ambient temperatures in warm climates). This makes agar uniquely suited for tropical countries like India where ambient temperatures regularly exceed 30°C. Gels are firm, brittle, clear and have a clean break. Sets at lower concentrations than most natural gels: 0.5–1% agar forms a firm gel; lower concentrations give soft, fluid-gel textures. Food uses: (1) gelling agent in confectionery, jellies and fruit preparations; (2) vegan/vegetarian gelatine substitute in desserts, aspic, cheesecakes and panna cotta; (3) thickener and stabiliser in ice cream, cream cheese, processed cheese and canned meat; (4) traditional Asian desserts — Japanese mitsumame, yokan and kanten sweets; Indian agar-based halwa and sweet preparations; (5) microbiological culture medium (LB agar, blood agar, MacConkey agar) — the most widely used solidifying agent in microbiology. Dietary properties: agar is a non-digestible dietary fibre — not cleaved by human intestinal enzymes (though partially fermented by colonic bacteria). Used as a bulk laxative at 4–16 g/day doses. Some studies suggest agar consumption may reduce postprandial glucose and cholesterol levels. Zero calories. No significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E406), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; kosher; halal.""",
    'e406': 'E406/INS 406 is agar (agar-agar), a natural red algae polysaccharide and the classic vegetarian/vegan alternative to gelatine. See agar entry for full details. Safe.',
    'locust bean gum': """Locust bean gum (LBG / carob bean gum / E410/INS 410) is extracted from the endosperm of carob tree (Ceratonia siliqua) seeds. The carob tree is an evergreen native to the Mediterranean; its pods have been used as food for over 4,000 years (possibly the biblical 'locusts' of John the Baptist). Processing: carob pods are cracked, the germ (which contains bitter tannins) is removed, and the starchy endosperm is milled into a fine powder. LBG is a galactomannan: a linear mannose backbone (β-1,4-linked D-mannopyranose) with D-galactopyranose side chains attached at every fourth mannose unit — mannose:galactose ratio approximately 4:1. This structure is critical to its functional properties. Cold-water hydration: unlike guar gum (which hydrates in cold water), LBG requires heating to 75–85°C for full solubilisation and viscosity development, then cooling for maximum thickness. At low temperatures (below 50°C), LBG forms weak gels due to partial association of unsubstituted mannose segments. Synergistic gelation: LBG's most important and unique functional property is its ability to form strong gels with kappa-carrageenan and xanthan gum at concentrations where neither alone would gel. The galactose-free ('smooth') sections of the LBG mannan chain interdigitate with the double helix structures of carrageenan or xanthan, forming supramolecular crosslinks. This synergy is exploited commercially to: (1) reduce the amount of expensive carrageenan needed; (2) produce elastic, freeze-thaw stable gels (more like pectin gels) vs the brittle, fragile gels of carrageenan alone; (3) create gels with zero or minimal syneresis. Critical application in ice cream: LBG delays ice crystal growth (recrystallisation) during freeze-thaw cycling, preventing the coarse icy texture of poorly formulated ice cream. Also prevents whey separation in processed cheese and improves sliceability in sausages and deli meats. Other uses: infant formula (thickener for reflux management — one of the most studied uses in preterm and at-risk infants), salad dressings and sauces. Dietary fibre: non-digestible, fermented slowly by colonic bacteria; prebiotic properties. Very safe; no significant allergen concerns. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E410), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan.""",
    'e410': 'E410/INS 410 is locust bean gum (carob bean gum), a natural galactomannan from carob seeds. See locust bean gum entry for full details. Safe; dietary fibre.',
    'tragacanth': 'Tragacanth (E413/INS 413) — one of the oldest food gums known, from the Astragalus shrub of Iran and Turkey. Uniquely acid-stable (pH 2–10) and very high viscosity. See E413 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e413': 'E413 / INS 413 — Tragacanth. Ancient natural plant gum exudate from Astragalus shrubs; one of the oldest and most acid-stable food gums; exceptionally high viscosity; used in specialty confectionery and pharmaceutical emulsions. See tragacanth entry above for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'gum arabic': """Gum arabic (E414/INS 414 / acacia gum / Senegal gum) is the dried, purified exudate gum obtained primarily from Acacia senegal and Acacia seyal trees — thorny shrub-to-tree legumes native to the semi-arid Sahel region of sub-Saharan Africa (Sudan, Nigeria, Chad, Senegal). The gum exudes naturally from bark wounds (tapping) as a clear amber nodule that hardens on exposure to air. Sudan and Chad together produce approximately 70–80% of the world's supply; Sudan historically produced the highest-quality 'hard grades.' Chemically, gum arabic is a highly complex, branched arabinogalactan polysaccharide covalently linked to protein (~2% protein content). The backbone consists of β-1,3-linked D-galactopyranose units with side chains of arabinose, rhamnose, glucuronic acid and 4-O-methylglucuronic acid. Its molecular weight is variable (200,000–2,000,000 Da depending on source) and the protein component confers amphiphilic (surface-active) character critical to its unique emulsification ability. Unique functional properties: (1) Highest solubility among all food gums — dissolves up to 50% w/v in water to form only mildly viscous solutions, allowing very high levels to be used without thickening the product. (2) Superior emulsification — the protein-polysaccharide complex adsorbs strongly at oil-water interfaces, stabilising flavour oil-in-water emulsions in beverages (essential for cloud beverages and flavoured sodas). (3) Encapsulant — gum arabic is the gold standard spray-drying encapsulant for flavour oils; forms a continuous, oxygen-impermeable film around oil droplets during spray drying, protecting volatile aroma compounds. (4) Thickener and binder in confectionery (gumdrop sugar-starch blends, panning). (5) Wine and beer fining agent (precipitates tannins). (6) Film-forming — used in confectionery panning as a glaze. Prebiotic dietary fibre: gum arabic is fermented by colonic bacteria to produce short-chain fatty acids (butyrate, propionate, acetate), with documented prebiotic benefits at 10–30 g/day — it has among the best-studied prebiotic evidence of any natural gum. Clinical trials show selective fermentation by Bifidobacterium, improved bowel regularity and modest cholesterol-lowering. Very mild adverse effects even at high doses — possible bloating at >30 g/day. No allergenicity (protein content is insufficient to trigger IgE-mediated reactions in most individuals, though very rare occupational asthma in Arabic gum handlers has been reported). No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E414), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan.""",
    'acacia gum': """Acacia gum (E414/INS 414) is the same substance as gum arabic — the natural polysaccharide exudate from Acacia senegal and Acacia seyal trees in Africa. A uniquely multifunctional natural ingredient: as an emulsifier, stabilises oil-in-water emulsions in beverages and flavour emulsions; as a thickener, provides low viscosity at high concentrations compared to other gums; as an encapsulant, forms a flexible film around spray-dried flavour oils; as a prebiotic dietary fibre, it ferments slowly in the colon to produce short-chain fatty acids. Exceptional solubility (up to 50% w/v in water) without high viscosity makes it uniquely useful. Used in confectionery coatings, soft drink flavour emulsions, wine fining, pharmaceutical tablet binders and cosmetics. Clinically studied prebiotic — trials show beneficial effects on gut microbiome diversity and bowel regularity at 10–30 g/day. Well tolerated; may cause mild flatulence at high doses. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E414), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe and beneficial.""",
    'e414': 'E414 is the EU/INS code for Gum Arabic (Acacia Gum). World\'s most used food gum. Safe.',
    'karaya gum': """Karaya gum (E416/INS 416 / sterculia gum / Indian tragacanth) is a natural polysaccharide exudate from the Sterculia urens tree, a medium-sized deciduous tree native to the hilly terrain of central and southern India (Madhya Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Chhattisgarh). Collected by tapping bark incisions, it dries into irregular, translucent to opaque lumps of pale yellow-white colour. Chemically: partially acetylated polysaccharide composed of D-galacturonic acid, L-rhamnose, D-galactose and L-fructose — different from most other gums, which are neutral or only weakly acidic. Swells in water to form viscous, thick dispersions (not true solutions) at concentrations of 1–3%. Key properties: high acid stability; strong water-binding and swelling capacity; good emulsification. Food uses: stabiliser in salad dressings, ice cream, sherbet, meringues and some cheeses; moisture retainer in processed meats. Non-food uses: bulk laxative in pharmaceuticals (similar mechanism to psyllium); dental adhesive in denture fixatives; wound ostomy care (karaya seals are used around stomas). Sustainability significance: karaya gum tapping provides rural income to tribal communities in central India. Occasional cases of contact allergy and occupational asthma in handlers. No ADI specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E416), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e416': 'E416 / INS 416 — Karaya Gum (Sterculia Gum, Indian Tragacanth) is a natural plant exudate gum obtained from the Sterculia urens tree — a medium-sized deciduous tree native to the hilly, rocky terrain of India (particularly Madhya Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Chhattisgarh and Orissa). The gum exudes naturally from bark wounds or incisions in the trunk. Chemically, karaya gum is a partially acetylated polysaccharide composed of D-galacturonic acid, L-rhamnose, D-galactose and L-fructose residues. It swells in water to form viscous, gel-like dispersions rather than true solutions (poorly soluble). Key properties: (1) acid-stable — more resistant to hydrolysis at low pH than most other natural gums; (2) strong laxative and bulking effect — used as a bulk laxative in pharmaceutical preparations; (3) adhesive and mucilage properties — used in denture adhesives and wound dressings (karaya forms a protective gel that absorbs wound exudate). Food applications: stabiliser in salad dressings, ice cream and meringues; used for its strong water-binding properties. Occasional mild hypersensitivity reactions reported (contact allergy). ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Karaya gum harvesting provides income for tribal communities in central India.',
    'tara gum': 'Tara Gum (E417/INS 417) is a natural galactomannan polysaccharide extracted from the pods of the tara tree (Caesalpinia spinosa), a tree native to Peru and the Andean regions of South America. Chemically, it is a linear mannose backbone with galactose side branches in approximately a 3:1 mannose-to-galactose ratio — intermediate between guar gum (2:1) and locust bean gum (4:1). This intermediate galactose substitution gives tara gum properties between its two counterparts: (1) greater water solubility than locust bean gum; (2) somewhat lower cold viscosity than guar gum; (3) shows synergy with xanthan gum (similar to locust bean gum but less pronounced); (4) heat stability; (5) clean, neutral flavour profile. Used as a thickener and stabiliser in ice cream, dairy products, dressings, sauces and bakery products. As a galactomannan soluble fibre, tara gum contributes prebiotic properties. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'e417': 'E417 / INS 417 — Tara Gum. Natural galactomannan from South American tara tree pods; thickener and stabiliser with properties between guar and locust bean gum. See tara gum for full description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'gellan gum': 'Gellan Gum (E418/INS 418) is a microbial polysaccharide produced by fermentation with the bacterium Sphingomonas elodea (formerly Pseudomonas elodea). It consists of a tetrasaccharide repeating unit: two glucose residues, one glucuronic acid and one rhamnose, with acyl substituents (L-glycerate and acetate) in the native (high-acyl) form. Two commercial forms exist: (1) High-acyl gellan gum (native) — produces soft, elastic, fluid gels with a clean flavour release; used in fluid gels, pourable jellies and some beverages; (2) Low-acyl gellan gum (deacylated with alkali) — produces firm, brittle, clear gels at very low concentrations (0.1–0.5%); heat-stable gels that melt at much higher temperatures than agar; used in plant-based milks to suspend particles without sedimentation, in confectionery jellies, desserts and pharmaceutical suspensions. Key advantage: gellan gum works at very low concentrations (typically 0.1–0.3%), significantly lower than agar or carrageenan; produces exceptionally clear, glossy gels; excellent flavour release. Widely used in plant milks (oat milk, almond milk) to stabilise the emulsion. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'e418': 'E418 / INS 418 — Gellan Gum. Microbial exopolysaccharide from Sphingomonas elodea; creates clear, stable gels at very low concentrations; key stabiliser in plant-based milks. See gellan gum for full description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e420': 'E420 is the EU/INS code for Sorbitol. Sugar alcohol sweetener from fruits; laxative effects at >50g/day; label warning required.',
    'e421': 'E421 is the EU/INS code for Mannitol. Natural sugar alcohol; laxative effects; label warning required.',
    'e422': 'E422 is the EU/INS code for Glycerol (Glycerine). Natural humectant from fats. Safe.',
    'konjac': """Konjac (E425/INS 425 / konjac glucomannan / KGM) — see the primary 'konjac gum' entry above for full chemistry, applications and safety details. Konjac is derived from the corm of Amorphophallus konjac and is one of the most viscous food hydrocolloids known. Functions as a thickener, gelling agent and dietary fibre supplement. Used in traditional Japanese konnyaku, shirataki noodles (nearly zero calories), konjac jelly, and as a dietary supplement for weight management, cholesterol reduction and blood glucose control. EU health claim: glucomannan contributes to maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels (requires 4 g/day of glucomannan as 3 doses of at least 1 g with adequate water). EU bans certain firm mini konjac jelly formats for children due to choking risk — this restriction applies to specific product formats, not konjac as an ingredient. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E425), FSSAI and CODEX. Safe for adults with adequate fluid intake.""",
    'e425': 'E425 is the EU/INS code for Konjac (Konjac Gum). High-fibre gelling agent; EU restricts certain children\'s jelly formats.',
    'e440': 'E440 is the EU/INS code for Pectins. Natural gelling agent from citrus peel. Safe; soluble dietary fibre.',
    'e441': 'E441 is the EU/INS code for Gelatin. Animal-derived gelling protein. Not suitable for vegetarians, vegans or halal/kosher.',
    'ammonium phosphatides': """Ammonium phosphatides (E442/INS 442 / AYP / ammonium salts of phosphorylated glycerides) are a group of synthetic emulsifiers produced by esterification of vegetable oils (predominantly rapeseed/canola oil) with phosphoric acid, then partial neutralisation with ammonium hydroxide. Developed specifically as a cost-effective alternative to lecithin (E322) in chocolate and compound chocolate coatings. Function in chocolate: in molten chocolate, the cocoa particle and sugar crystal surfaces are coated with fat, and the viscosity of the chocolate melt depends on how easily these coated particles flow past each other. Ammonium phosphatides adsorb onto the hydrophilic sugar crystal surfaces, reducing the effective friction between particles and enabling smoother flow — reducing both Casson plastic viscosity and Casson yield value (the force needed to initiate flow). This allows chocolate manufacturers to use less cocoa butter (expensive) while maintaining the required flow properties for enrobing, moulding and coating. Used at approximately 0.1–0.5% in milk chocolate, dark chocolate, compound coatings and carob-based products. EFSA 2012 safety evaluation: confirmed safety with ADI 0–25 mg/kg body weight/day; no concerns at food use levels. The ammonium ions are a normal metabolic compound, rapidly converted to urea in the liver. Metabolically, the phosphorylated glyceride backbone is hydrolysed to fatty acids, glycerol and phosphate — all normal food metabolites. No carcinogenicity, no reproductive toxicity, no genotoxicity. No significant allergen concerns (rapeseed-derived; rapeseed allergy is rare). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E442), FDA (GRAS as 'ammonium phosphatide') and CODEX. Vegan when made from vegetable fats.""",
    'e442': 'E442 / INS 442 — Ammonium Phosphatides (AYP, Ammonium Salts of Phosphorylated Glycerides) are a group of synthetic emulsifiers produced by esterifying vegetable oils (most commonly rapeseed/canola oil) with phosphoric acid and then partially neutralising with ammonium hydroxide. They were developed specifically as a more economical alternative to soy lecithin (E322) for the cocoa and chocolate industry. Mechanism in chocolate: AYP reduces the plastic viscosity (yield value) and Casson viscosity of chocolate melt — i.e., how easily it flows under shear — by adsorbing onto the surface of cocoa particles and sugar crystals, lubricating their movement past each other. Unlike PGPR (E476), which primarily reduces Casson yield value (the minimum force to initiate flow), AYP acts more like lecithin in reducing both types of viscosity. This makes it particularly useful in milk chocolate and compound chocolate. EFSA safety review (2012): EFSA assessed AYP thoroughly and established an ADI of 0–25 mg/kg body weight/day — well above the maximum estimated dietary intake from chocolate consumption. No genotoxicity, no reproductive toxicity, no carcinogenicity concerns at food use levels. The ammonium component is rapidly metabolised in the liver via the urea cycle. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA; Codex Alimentarius standard for chocolate allows its use.',
    'sucrose esters': """Sucrose esters of fatty acids (E473/INS 473) are a family of synthetic non-ionic emulsifiers produced by esterifying sucrose (table sugar) with fatty acids — typically stearic, palmitic, oleic or lauric acid derived from vegetable fats (often from palm oil). The degree of esterification varies: mono-esters (one fatty acid), di-esters (two fatty acids) or poly-esters (three to eight fatty acids per sucrose molecule). This allows manufacturers to tune the hydrophilic-lipophilic balance (HLB) of the emulsifier: sucrose monostearate (HLB ~15, very hydrophilic — O/W emulsions), sucrose distearate (HLB ~8, more lipophilic), sucrose tristearate (HLB ~5, lipophilic — W/O emulsions). This versatility makes sucrose esters useful across a wide range of food applications. Distinct from sucrose polyesters (Olestra/Olean) — those are poly-esters designed to be non-absorbable fat replacers (different product, different regulatory status, not the same as E473). Functions and applications: emulsifier and stabiliser in ice cream (improves overrun and resistance to heat shock), whipped toppings, margarine, peanut butter (prevents oil separation), confectionery coatings, bakery products (improves loaf volume, crumb softness, shelf life), dairy beverages, coffee creamers, spreads and sugar confectionery. Also used in cosmetics as emulsifiers. Safety: sucrose esters are hydrolysed in the small intestine to sucrose (further digested to glucose and fructose), sorbitol (none — no sorbitol in sucrose esters) and fatty acids — all normal food components. Mono- and di-esters are well absorbed; tri- and higher-esters are progressively less absorbed (similar to dietary fat). No toxicological concerns — no carcinogenicity, no genotoxicity, no reproductive toxicity. No sensitisation. ADI: not specified (JECFA; previously had a group ADI of 0–30 mg/kg with emulsifiers that was later not further specified). Approved by EU (E473), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS for specific grades) and CODEX. Typically vegan when made from plant-derived fatty acids — check source; some may use animal tallow.""",
    'e473': 'E473 / INS 473 — Sucrose Esters of Fatty Acids (SEFAs) are a family of emulsifiers produced by transesterification of sucrose with methyl esters of edible fatty acids (palmitic, stearic, oleic acids from palm oil or other vegetable fats). They are characterised by their wide range of hydrophilic-lipophilic balance (HLB) values (1–16), achievable by controlling the number of fatty acid ester groups per sucrose molecule (mono, di, tri, poly esters) — this makes them highly versatile: low-HLB SEFAs stabilise water-in-oil emulsions (margarine) while high-HLB SEFAs stabilise oil-in-water emulsions (coffee whiteners, dairy drinks, ice cream). Applications: (1) ice cream — reduce fat globule size, improve whipping, control ice crystal growth; (2) coffee whiteners — prevent feathering (flocculation when added to hot acidic coffee); (3) bread and bakery — anti-staling effect similar to SSL and DATEM; (4) meat products — water retention; (5) confectionery — bloom prevention in chocolate. HLB values printed on commercial SEFAs (S-1, S-3, S-7, S-11, S-15) correspond to monoester content — higher number = higher HLB = more water-loving. Safety: SEFAs are completely hydrolysed in the gut to sucrose and fatty acids — normal food components. JECFA ADI 0–10 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e475': 'E475 / INS 475 — Polyglycerol Esters of Fatty Acids (PGEs) are emulsifiers produced by esterifying polyglycerol (chains of glycerol units, typically 2–10) with fatty acids from edible vegetable oils. The balance between the hydrophilic polyglycerol backbone and the lipophilic fatty acid chain(s) can be tuned by varying: (1) the chain length of the polyglycerol (number of glycerol units); (2) the fatty acid composition; (3) the degree of esterification. This tuneability gives PGEs an HLB range of 3–16. Applications: (1) low-fat spreads and margarines — stabilise water-in-oil emulsions at lower fat contents than achievable with mono/diglycerides alone; (2) cake batter — improve aeration and foam stability; (3) bread — anti-staling; (4) confectionery — control sugar crystallisation; (5) chewing gum base — softener. Safety: PGEs are hydrolysed in the gut to polyglycerol and fatty acids; polyglycerol is water-soluble and rapidly excreted in urine. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'polysorbate 20': """Polysorbate 20 (Tween 20 / E432/INS 432 / polyoxyethylene (20) sorbitan monolaurate) is a synthetic non-ionic emulsifier produced by esterifying sorbitol with lauric acid (C12, a medium-chain saturated fatty acid from coconut/palm kernel oil) to form sorbitan monolaurate (Span 20), then ethoxylating with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. The resulting molecule has the lauric acid ester as its lipophilic tail and a large, flexible polyoxyethylene chain as its hydrophilic head — HLB value approximately 16.7, one of the most hydrophilic polysorbates. Functions: mild, effective oil-in-water emulsifier and solubiliser; stabilises fine flavour oil emulsions in beverages; solubilises hydrophobic vitamins (A, D, E, K) and active pharmaceutical ingredients in aqueous systems; used in ice cream and frozen desserts as a surface-active emulsifier. In cosmetics and personal care: widely used in facial moisturisers, facial cleansers, body washes, toners and baby products as an emulsifier, fragrance solubiliser and mild surfactant — considered particularly gentle and suitable for sensitive skin formulations. In pharmaceuticals: used in injectable formulations and oral solutions to solubilise hydrophobic drugs. The lauric acid chain (C12) gives polysorbate 20 mild antimicrobial properties at higher concentrations. Ethoxylation concern: the manufacturing process (ethylene oxide addition) can generate trace 1,4-dioxane as a by-product — an IARC Group 2B possible carcinogen. Quality-controlled manufacturers use vacuum-stripping to remove 1,4-dioxane to <1–10 ppm; most regulatory agencies consider these residual levels acceptable. EU (E432), FSSAI, FDA and CODEX approved. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day for all polysorbates combined (JECFA). Generally well tolerated; rare contact allergy cases reported. The gut microbiome studies (Chassaing 2015) specifically used polysorbate 80 at 1% — polysorbate 20 at food use concentrations has no established gut microbiome concern.""",
    'e432': 'E432 / INS 432 — Polysorbate 20. Mild non-ionic ethoxylated emulsifier with lauric acid chain (C12); HLB ~16.7; trace 1,4-dioxane from ethoxylation (manage via manufacturer purification). See polysorbate 20 entry for full details. ADI: 25 mg/kg bw/day combined polysorbates. Safe within approved limits.',
    'polysorbate 80': """Polysorbate 80 (Tween 80 / E433/INS 433 / polyoxyethylene (20) sorbitan monooleate) is a synthetic non-ionic emulsifier produced by esterifying sorbitol with oleic acid (C18:1, monounsaturated fatty acid — usually from vegetable oils) to form sorbitan monooleate (Span 80), then ethoxylating with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. A pale yellow oily liquid with a mild characteristic odour; HLB value approximately 15 — very hydrophilic. The oleic acid chain (liquid at room temperature, unlike stearic acid) gives polysorbate 80 a liquid consistency, making it easier to handle and dispense in manufacturing. Most widely used polysorbate globally. Functions in food: emulsifier in ice cream (interacts with milk fat globule membranes during freezing, controlling fat coalescence and improving texture, scoopability and heat-shock resistance), salad dressings, pickles, chewing gum, condiments, coffee creamers and vitamin E solubilisation in beverages. Functions in pharmaceuticals: critical excipient in injectable chemotherapy drugs (paclitaxel/Taxol — solubilised in polysorbate 80 + dehydrated ethanol), erythropoietin biologics, monoclonal antibody formulations and vaccine adjuvants — one of the most important pharmaceutical solubilisers. In cosmetics: emulsifier and fragrance solubiliser in creams, lotions, serums and makeup. Gut microbiome research: the landmark Chassaing et al. 2015 (Nature) study fed mice polysorbate 80 and polysorbate 60 at 1% of total fluid intake (continuous exposure at ~10 g/day for a 10 kg human equivalent) and found disruption of the mucus layer and promotion of low-grade intestinal inflammation. These findings have been reproduced in mice and some cell systems. However, the concentrations used far exceed typical human food-additive exposure (food use ~0.5–1 g/day from all sources combined). Human dietary epidemiology and clinical trial data are insufficient to confirm this risk at food-additive levels. The finding remains under regulatory review. Ethoxylation concern: trace 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B) from manufacturing — managed by vacuum-stripping. EU (E433), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS for specific applications) and CODEX approved. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day combined polysorbates (JECFA). People with IBD may consider avoiding high-polysorbate food choices as a precaution.""",
    'e433': 'E433 / INS 433 — Polysorbate 80. Most widely used polysorbate globally; food and pharmaceutical emulsifier; gut microbiome research at high animal doses is a concern; trace 1,4-dioxane from ethoxylation. See polysorbate 80 for full details. ADI: 25 mg/kg bw/day combined polysorbates. Safe within approved limits.',
    'polysorbate 60': """Polysorbate 60 (Tween 60 / E435/INS 435 / polyoxyethylene (20) sorbitan monostearate) is produced by esterifying sorbitol with stearic acid (C18, saturated fatty acid — from vegetable stearic acid or tallow), forming sorbitan monostearate (Span 60), followed by ethoxylation with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. HLB value approximately 14.9 — very hydrophilic. The stearic acid chain (solid at room temperature, unlike oleic acid) gives polysorbate 60 slightly different functional properties from polysorbate 80 in fat-containing food systems. Functions: oil-in-water emulsifier in non-dairy whipped toppings (provides aeration and structure to fat-stabilised foams without dairy fat), non-dairy coffee creamers (prevents feathering in hot acidic coffee), cake mixes (improves crumb structure, moisture retention, shelf life), baked goods, shortenings, confectionery coatings and ice cream. Particularly useful in whipped cream alternatives where the solid stearate chain stabilises the crystalline fat network that supports the foam structure. Same ethoxylation processing concern as polysorbate 80: trace 1,4-dioxane possible; quality-controlled production vacuum-strips to safe levels. Chassaing 2015 mouse study used polysorbate 60 as one of two tested emulsifiers and found effects similar to polysorbate 80 at 1% continuous intake — same caveat about concentration far exceeding food-additive exposure applies. EU (E435), FSSAI, FDA (GRAS) and CODEX approved. ADI: 25 mg/kg body weight/day combined polysorbates (JECFA). Generally well tolerated at food use levels; rare contact allergy possible.""",
    'e435': 'E435 / INS 435 — Polysorbate 60. Non-ionic stearate-based polysorbate emulsifier; key in non-dairy whipped toppings; trace 1,4-dioxane from ethoxylation; see polysorbate 60 for full details. ADI: 25 mg/kg bw/day combined polysorbates. Safe within approved limits.',
    'polysorbate 65': """Polysorbate 65 (Tween 65 / E436/INS 436 / polyoxyethylene (20) sorbitan tristearate) is a non-ionic synthetic emulsifier — produced by esterifying sorbitol with three stearic acid molecules to form sorbitan tristearate (Span 65), then ethoxylating it with approximately 20 moles of ethylene oxide. The result is a much more hydrophilic molecule than Span 65, with an HLB value of approximately 10.5 — in between polysorbate 60 (HLB ~14.9) and sorbitan esters (HLB ~3–5). This intermediate hydrophilicity makes polysorbate 65 suitable for emulsifying water-in-oil or complex emulsion systems. Food uses: ice cream and frozen desserts (emulsifier that interacts with milk fat globule membranes, promotes partial fat coalescence and improves the overrun/air incorporation, heat shock resistance and creaminess of the product), chocolate coatings and compound coatings (reduces viscosity, improves enrobing), whipped toppings and cream liqueurs. Like all polysorbates, polysorbate 65 is produced via ethoxylation with ethylene oxide, meaning trace levels of 1,4-dioxane (IARC Group 2B probable carcinogen) may be present as a manufacturing by-product — reputable manufacturers vacuum-strip to remove residuals; EU limits 1,4-dioxane in cosmetics to <10 ppm (food limits are set nationally). Polysorbate 65 itself is not carcinogenic. It is metabolised to stearic acid, sorbitol and polyethylene glycol — all safe food components. No sensitisation at food use levels. The same mouse gut microbiome research that flagged polysorbate 80 (Chassaing et al., 2015) used P-80 specifically; the extent to which those findings generalise to polysorbate 65 at food-additive concentrations is not established. ADI: 0–25 mg/kg body weight/day for polysorbates combined (JECFA). Approved by EU (E436), FSSAI and CODEX. Not commonly found on Indian product labels (less widely used than P-20 or P-80).""",
    'e436': 'E436 is the EU/INS code for Polysorbate 65. Emulsifier. Safe within limits.',
    'e460': 'E460 is the EU/INS code for Microcrystalline Cellulose. Insoluble dietary fibre; anti-caking agent. Safe.',
    'e461': 'E461 is the EU/INS code for Methyl Cellulose. Heat-set modified cellulose in plant-based burgers. Safe.',
    'e464': 'E464 is the EU/INS code for Hydroxypropyl Methyl Cellulose (HPMC). Used in vegetarian capsules and gluten-free baking. Safe.',
    'datem': """DATEM (diacetyl tartaric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides / E472e/INS 472e) is a synthetic complex emulsifier produced by reacting mono- and diglycerides (from edible oils) with diacetyl tartaric acid (from tartaric acid and acetic anhydride). The most powerful bread dough strengthener among commercially used emulsifiers. Mechanism in bread: DATEM intercalates between gluten protein strands, displacing lipids from gliadins and glutenins, allowing the glutenin long chains to form stronger intermolecular bonding networks — this translates to enhanced dough strength, improved gas retention, greater tolerance to mixing variation and up to 25% greater loaf volume. Also used in croissants, Danish pastry, pizza bases and laminated dough. Metabolised to tartaric acid, acetic acid and fatty acids — all normal food constituents. JECFA ADI: not specified (JECFA considers it safe). May be animal fat-derived or vegetable fat-derived — vegans, vegetarians and halal/kosher consumers should check source. Approved by FSSAI, EU (E472e), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Very well tolerated; no known adverse effects at food use levels.""",
    'e472e': 'E472e / INS 472e — DATEM (Diacetyl Tartaric Acid Esters of Mono- and Diglycerides of Fatty Acids) is a synthetic, complex emulsifier produced in a multi-step process: (1) fatty acids from palm oil or other edible fats are esterified with glycerol to form mono- and diglycerides; (2) the free hydroxyl groups of these are then esterified with tartaric acid and acetic acid (diacetyl tartaric acid). The resulting DATEM molecule has a hydrophilic head (tartaric and acetic acid moiety) and a lipophilic tail (fatty acid chain). In breadmaking, DATEM is the most powerful dough-strengthening emulsifier available: (1) gluten network enhancement — DATEM interacts with gluten proteins (specifically, displaces lipids from the hydrophobic surface of gliadins and glutenins, enabling tighter protein-protein cross-linking); this significantly increases dough extensibility, gas retention and tolerance to mechanical mixing; (2) oven spring — products containing DATEM show 10–25% greater loaf volume; (3) crumb texture — finer, more uniform cell structure; (4) machinability — dough is more tolerant to variations in mixing and fermentation time. Used in commercial bread, rolls, croissants, Danish pastry, pizza bases and bagels. Safety: DATEM is hydrolysed in the gut to its component molecules (glycerol, fatty acids, tartaric acid, acetic acid) — all normal food metabolites. JECFA ADI 0–50 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'sodium stearoyl lactylate': """Sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL / E481/INS 481) is an anionic food emulsifier produced by esterification of stearic acid (C18 saturated fatty acid) with lactic acid — typically forming a lactyl lactate oligomer — then neutralised with sodium hydroxide to form the sodium salt. A pale ivory to cream-coloured powder with a mild dairy odour. Multifunctional dough conditioner and emulsifier: (1) Gluten strengthening — SSL forms lipid-protein complexes with glutenins, increasing dough extensibility, gas retention and tolerance to over-mixing or under-fermentation; produces improved loaf volume and finer crumb structure; (2) Starch complexation — SSL forms helical inclusion complexes with amylose starch chains (similar to alpha-amylase starch substrate interactions), which retards starch retrogradation and maintains crumb softness — extending the shelf life of bread from 2–3 days to 5–7 days without visible staling. Both functions together make SSL the most commonly used emulsifier in commercial bread globally. Used at 0.1–0.5% of flour weight. Also used as an aerating emulsifier in non-dairy whipped toppings, coffee creamers and dehydrated potato products. Metabolically: hydrolysed in the gut to stearic acid and lactic acid — both completely normal dietary components. Stearic acid has a neutral effect on plasma cholesterol. Lactic acid is a normal metabolic intermediate. May be derived from animal tallow or vegetable oils — vegans and halal/kosher consumers should verify source with the manufacturer. No genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity, no reproductive toxicity. No significant sensitisation. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E481), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. One of the most comprehensively safety-studied food emulsifiers.""",
    'e481': 'E481 / INS 481 — Sodium Stearoyl Lactylate (SSL) — see INS 481 for full description. An efficient, multifunctional bread emulsifier that forms gluten-starch complexes to extend softness and improve volume. JECFA ADI 0–20 mg/kg bw/day. Safe.',
    'calcium stearoyl lactylate': 'Calcium Stearoyl Lactylate (CSL/E482/INS 482) is the calcium salt of stearoyl-2-lactylate — functionally very similar to sodium stearoyl lactylate (SSL, E481) with the same production method (esterification of stearic acid with lactic acid), but neutralised with calcium hydroxide instead of sodium hydroxide. CSL produces slightly firmer dough strengthening than SSL. Used in bread, tortillas, specialty bakery products and as an emulsifier in coffee whiteners and whipped toppings. Preferred over SSL where reduced sodium is desired (the calcium salt contributes calcium instead of sodium). Like SSL, CSL is hydrolysed in the gut to stearic acid and lactic acid. JECFA ADI 0–20 mg/kg bw/day (combined with SSL). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e482': 'E482 / INS 482 — Calcium Stearoyl Lactylate (CSL). Bread emulsifier and dough conditioner. See calcium stearoyl lactylate for details. Safe.',
    'e491': 'E491 / INS 491 — Sorbitan Monostearate (Span 60) is a non-ionic emulsifier — an ester of sorbitol (a sugar alcohol) and stearic acid (C18 saturated fatty acid), produced by partial dehydration of sorbitol followed by esterification with stearic acid. It belongs to the Span family of sorbitan esters (named for their use in the industrial emulsifier trade). Sorbitan esters are lipophilic (low HLB ~4.7) and are used primarily in water-in-oil applications. Used in: bakery products (yeast food, anti-staling), ice cream (controls fat coalescence and improves texture), chocolate products, whipped toppings, confectionery coatings. Span 60 is frequently used in combination with Tween 60 (polyoxyethylene sorbitan monostearate, Polysorbate 60) to achieve intermediate HLB values and optimise emulsion stability. Safety: sorbitan esters are poorly absorbed in the gut; partially hydrolysed to sorbitol and stearic acid; no significant systemic effects. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (total sorbitan esters). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e492': 'E492 / INS 492 — Sorbitan Tristearate (Span 65) is a non-ionic lipophilic emulsifier — a triester of sorbitol and stearic acid with a very low HLB (~2.1). It is particularly used in confectionery (chocolate) as a crystal modifier: sorbitan tristearate promotes the formation of stable beta\' and beta crystal forms in cocoa butter and compound fats, reducing bloom (the white-grey fat crystal rearrangement on chocolate surfaces that makes chocolate look stale without affecting taste). It also improves the snap and gloss of chocolate coating. Used in chocolate products, compound coatings and cocoa-based confectionery. Safety: same class as other sorbitan esters — poorly absorbed, partially hydrolysed to fatty acids and sorbitol. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (combined sorbitan esters). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e493': 'E493 / INS 493 — Sorbitan Monolaurate (Span 20) is a non-ionic emulsifier — an ester of sorbitol and lauric acid (C12). With a slightly higher HLB (~8.6) than other sorbitan esters due to the shorter lauric acid chain, Span 20 is less lipophilic and more useful in oil-in-water applications (or as a co-emulsifier to adjust HLB in complex formulation systems). Used in food processing and in cosmetics. In food: emulsifier in bakery products, confectionery. More common in cosmetics (where it stabilises oil-in-water creams). Safety: same sorbitan ester class — poorly absorbed; no significant systemic effects. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (combined). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e494': 'E494 / INS 494 — Sorbitan Monooleate (Span 80) is a non-ionic emulsifier — an ester of sorbitol and oleic acid (C18:1 monounsaturated fatty acid). HLB value ~4.3. The oleic acid chain makes Span 80 a liquid at room temperature (unlike the solid sorbitan stearate esters), making it easy to handle and incorporate into formulations. Applications: food emulsifier in baked goods, yeasted products, ice cream, confectionery and coatings; also used as a processing aid in chocolate manufacture; commonly used in pharmaceutical injectable formulations and as a vaccine adjuvant stabiliser. Safety: sorbitan ester class — poorly absorbed; metabolised to oleic acid and sorbitol. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (combined). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e495': 'E495 / INS 495 — Sorbitan Monopalmitate (Span 40) is a non-ionic emulsifier — an ester of sorbitol and palmitic acid (C16 saturated fatty acid). HLB ~6.7 — intermediate between sorbitan monostearate (Span 60) and sorbitan monolaurate (Span 20). Used in bakery products, confectionery and chocolate as an emulsifier and crystal modifier. Less commonly used than Span 60 or Span 80. Safety: sorbitan ester class — same safety profile. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (combined). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'diphosphates': """Diphosphates (E450/INS 450 — pyrophosphates) are a subgroup of the phosphate food additive family — salts of pyrophosphoric acid (H₄P₂O₇), formed by condensation of two phosphate units. The main forms: sodium acid pyrophosphate (SAPP / E450i / Na₂H₂P₂O₇), disodium diphosphate; tetrasodium pyrophosphate (TSPP / E450iii / Na₄P₂O₇); disodium diphosphate; tetrapotassium pyrophosphate (TKPP); calcium diphosphate. Each has different solubility, pH and functional properties. Functions in food: (1) Leavening acidulants — SAPP (E450i) is the principal leavening acid in baking powder and self-raising flour (see sodium acid pyrophosphate entry for detail). (2) Emulsifying salts in processed cheese (TSPP, E450iii) — pyrophosphates interact with the protein casein in cheese, breaking up the protein calcium network and allowing the cheese to melt smoothly and uniformly without oil separation; essential for processed cheese slices, spreads and cheese sauces. (3) Sequestrants — chelate trace metal ions (iron, copper) in potato products (SAPP inhibits the enzymatic browning in cut potato and the greying in cooked potato / canned potatoes and seafood). (4) Protein-modifying agents — improve water-holding capacity and texture of restructured meat products and seafood. (5) Retort aid — stabilise pH during high-temperature sterilisation of canned products. Health concern — phosphate: all phosphate food additives (E450, E451, E452) add inorganic phosphate to the diet. Inorganic food-additive phosphate is more readily absorbed (bioavailability ~90–100%) than naturally occurring organic phosphate in food proteins (bioavailability ~60–70%). For the general healthy population at normal dietary exposure, this is not a concern — kidneys efficiently excrete excess phosphate. For people with chronic kidney disease (CKD, stages 3–5), impaired phosphate excretion leads to hyperphosphataemia → elevated fibroblast growth factor-23 (FGF-23) → secondary hyperparathyroidism → renal osteodystrophy and cardiovascular calcification (a leading cause of mortality in CKD). CKD patients must minimise phosphate additive intake, which is challenging because food-additive phosphate is ubiquitous in processed foods and not always listed individually (often listed generically as 'raising agent' or 'emulsifying salt'). ADI: group ADI 70 mg/kg body weight/day as phosphorus (JECFA). Approved by EU (E450), FSSAI and CODEX.""",
    'e450': 'E450 is the EU/INS code for Diphosphates (Pyrophosphates). Emulsifying salts in processed cheese. Safe at approved levels.',
    'e451': 'E451 is the EU/INS code for Triphosphates. Moisture-retaining agent in processed meats. Safe.',
    'e452': 'E452 / INS 452 — Polyphosphates (Sodium Polyphosphate, Potassium Polyphosphate, Sodium Calcium Polyphosphate) are a group of condensed phosphate salts with more than two phosphate units in a chain. They are produced by high-temperature dehydration of diphosphates (pyrophosphates) to form longer chains. Longer-chain polyphosphates (typically 12–20 units) have stronger chelating, dispersing and water-sequestering properties than diphosphates. Key functions: (1) emulsifying salts in processed cheese — polyphosphates sequester calcium from casein micelles more effectively than diphosphates, enabling smooth melting and emulsification of cheese proteins; (2) moisture retention in processed meats and seafood — polyphosphates bind water molecules in muscle tissue, improving yield and texture in cooked hams, chicken fillets and shrimp; (3) sequestrants — chelate divalent metal ions preventing discolouration and rancidity; (4) anti-tartar agents in toothpaste — prevent calcium phosphate scale. Phosphate accumulation concern: as with all phosphate additives, excessive total dietary phosphate from combined sources (cola beverages, processed meats, cheese products, polyphosphate-treated seafood) can impair kidney function and reduce bone density — particularly problematic in chronic kidney disease patients. For healthy individuals at food use levels, EFSA considers polyphosphates safe (group ADI 40 mg/kg/day as phosphorus). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',

    # ── Raising agents / minerals (E500-E599) ────────────────────────────
    'e500': 'E500 is the EU/INS code for Sodium Carbonates (including Baking Soda). Most widely used raising agent. Safe.',
    'sodium bicarbonate': """Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃ / baking soda / bicarbonate of soda / E500ii/INS 500ii) is a naturally occurring white crystalline salt found as the mineral nahcolite, commercially mined in Colorado (USA) and produced industrially by the Solvay process (reacting sodium chloride brine with ammonia and carbon dioxide). One of the most ancient and universally used food ingredients. In food as a leavening agent: sodium bicarbonate alone releases CO₂ only weakly (at high temperatures, ~70°C, or in the presence of moisture) via thermal decomposition: 2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂. In acidic dough or batter (with buttermilk, yogurt, lemon juice, vinegar, brown sugar, honey, cocoa), it reacts immediately to release CO₂: NaHCO₃ + acid → salt + H₂O + CO₂. This reaction is used in: traditional Indian recipes (idli/dosa batter, where naturally fermented acids trigger rise; naan and some roti preparations; khamiri roti; chakli, murukkus), Western baking (Irish soda bread, pancakes, muffins, scones — always with an acidic ingredient), and is the active leavening component of baking powder (combined with cream of tartar or sodium acid pyrophosphate). As an acidity regulator: neutralises excess acidity in jams, tomato products and beverages. As an anti-caking agent: small amounts in salt prevent clumping. Medical uses: antacid (neutralises gastric HCl; fast-acting; used in Eno and similar Indian antacid preparations); alkalinising agent (IV sodium bicarbonate used in hospitals for metabolic acidosis and to alkalinise urine during acute aspirin or methotrexate toxicity). Culinary chemistry: in baked goods, sodium carbonate (the product of baking soda decomposition) can leave an alkaline, slightly soapy taste if not fully reacted — proper acid-base balance in the recipe prevents this. Soda in chocolate cake: an interesting effect — baking soda raises pH during baking, causing anthocyanins in natural cocoa to turn from reddish to brown/black, deepening colour. In red velvet cake, vinegar and buttermilk preserve the red colour by keeping pH acidic. Sodium content: sodium bicarbonate provides ~27% sodium by weight; one teaspoon (~4.6 g) provides ~1,260 mg sodium — significant for people on sodium-restricted diets. ADI: not specified (JECFA — normal food ingredient). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E500ii), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Essentially safe for the general population at food use levels.""",
    'e501': 'E501 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Carbonates. Raising agent in cocoa and noodles. Safe.',
    'ammonium carbonate': """Ammonium carbonate (E503/INS 503 / bakers' ammonia / hartshorn / sal volatile / (NH₄)₂CO₃) is a leavening agent historically used before modern baking powder was invented. Traditional preparation: produced by dry distillation of nitrogenous organic material (hence 'hartshorn' — deer antler horn was a historical source). Modern production: by reacting ammonia with carbon dioxide. Decomposes readily to release both CO₂ (the leavening gas) and ammonia gas: (NH₄)₂CO₃ → 2NH₃ + H₂O + CO₂. Critical characteristic: unlike sodium bicarbonate (which leaves sodium carbonate as a non-volatile residue), ammonium carbonate decomposes completely into gases — CO₂, ammonia and water vapour — leaving absolutely no non-volatile residue in the baked product. This makes it particularly suited to thin, flat baked goods where complete gas release ensures a very light, crisp texture without any residual alkaline taste. Applications: traditional European biscuits (Spritzgebäck, Lebkuchen, Speculaas), crackers, Danish cookies, Indian cream crackers and digestive biscuits; used in bakeries specialising in crisp, flat products. The ammonia smell is very evident in raw dough but fully dissipates during baking at temperatures above 60°C — the final product has no ammonia taste or smell. If the bakery product is thick, ammonia may not fully escape, leaving an ammonium bicarbonate residue; for this reason, ammonium carbonate is NOT used in thick cakes or bread. Compared to baking powder: ammonium carbonate produces very light, crispy textures difficult to achieve with baking powder. Currently declining in use as many bakers switch to baking powder for convenience. Safe for the general population; not a concern for people with phenylketonuria at food use levels (very different from dietary phenylalanine). ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E503), FDA and CODEX.""",
    'e503': 'E503 is the EU/INS code for Ammonium Carbonates. Traditional raising agent. Ammonia bakes out. Safe.',
    'magnesium carbonate': 'Magnesium Carbonate (E504/INS 504) is used as an anti-caking agent. Provides dietary magnesium. Safe.',
    'e504': 'E504 is the EU/INS code for Magnesium Carbonates. Anti-caking agent; provides magnesium. Safe.',
    'potassium chloride food': """Potassium chloride (KCl / E508/INS 508 / potassium salt) is an inorganic salt naturally occurring in seawater, certain mineral deposits and all living organisms. Commercially produced by mining potash deposits or through solar evaporation of brines. In food: (1) Low-sodium salt substitute — widely used to partially or fully replace sodium chloride (NaCl) in reduced-sodium products. At 1:1 ratio, potassium chloride is saltier-tasting than table salt, but with a metallic or bitter off-note at higher concentrations; typically blended with NaCl (e.g., 50/50) for best flavour. This application is of significant public health importance: high sodium intake is a leading risk factor for hypertension and cardiovascular disease; FSSAI, WHO and public health agencies globally encourage sodium reduction; potassium chloride is the principal tool. (2) Firming agent — used in tofu (interchangeable with calcium sulphate as a coagulant for soy milk), canned vegetables and fruit to maintain texture. (3) pH adjuster and sequestrant — minor role in some food systems. (4) Mineral supplement — potassium is an essential macronutrient required for nerve transmission, muscle contraction (including heart muscle), fluid balance and blood pressure regulation. The dietary reference intake for potassium for adults is 3,500–4,700 mg/day (FSSAI/WHO); most Indians are potassium-deficient due to low fruit and vegetable intake. Additional dietary potassium from salt substitutes may be beneficial for most healthy people. Safety concerns: potassium toxicity at normal dietary levels is extremely rare in people with healthy kidneys — excess potassium is efficiently excreted in urine. However, people with chronic kidney disease (CKD), type 1 or type 2 diabetes with diabetic nephropathy, adrenal insufficiency, or people taking ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) or potassium-sparing diuretics must carefully monitor potassium intake, as these conditions impair potassium excretion and can lead to dangerous hyperkalaemia (elevated blood potassium → cardiac arrhythmias). These individuals should consult their doctor before switching to potassium-based salt substitutes. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E508), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan, halal, kosher.""",
    'e508': 'E508 / INS 508 — Potassium Chloride (KCl). Essential mineral salt used as a sodium-reduction tool and firming agent. See INS 508 for full scientific description. Note for kidney disease patients: consult physician before regular use. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'calcium chloride food': """Calcium chloride (E509/INS 509 / CaCl₂) is an inorganic calcium salt — a white, highly hygroscopic solid that readily dissolves in water with significant heat of dissolution (exothermic). Produced by the Solvay process (as a by-product of soda ash production) or by neutralising calcium carbonate with hydrochloric acid. Functions in food: firming agent — calcium ions crosslink pectin chains in plant cell walls (calcium-pectin cross-links), maintaining firmness in canned vegetables, sliced apples, maraschino cherries, processed tomatoes and olives; tofu coagulant — calcium ions precipitate soy proteins, producing a firm tofu curd with high calcium content; cheese making — added to milk to compensate for calcium lost during pasteurisation, improving curd formation; brine for pickling — provides a salty solution without adding sodium. Calcium content: provides approximately 36% elemental calcium by weight — one of the best food-grade calcium sources. Bioavailability of calcium chloride is good. Provides meaningful dietary calcium in calcium-set tofu (up to 200–300 mg calcium per 100g tofu). No significant toxicological concerns at food use levels. Very high doses (industrial applications) can irritate mucous membranes. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E509), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX.""",
    'e509': 'E509 / INS 509 — Calcium Chloride. Inorganic calcium salt; firming agent and tofu coagulant; calcium source. See INS 509 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'magnesium chloride food': """Magnesium chloride (MgCl₂ / E511/INS 511) is an inorganic salt naturally found in seawater and mineral brines. Commercially produced by evaporating seawater or from salt lake brines. The food-grade anhydrous or hexahydrate form is a white, crystalline, hygroscopic solid. In Japan, it is known as "nigari" (bittern — the concentrated mineral solution remaining after sodium chloride is removed from seawater) and is the traditional coagulant used to make tofu. Mechanism of tofu making: when added to hot soy milk (70–80°C), magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) interact with the negatively charged protein molecules (mainly 11S globulin glycinin and 7S beta-conglycinin), neutralising their surface charges and causing the proteins to coagulate and precipitate — forming the tofu curd. Nigari (magnesium chloride) produces a slightly firmer, more flavourful tofu compared to calcium sulphate, which produces a smoother, silkier texture. Food uses beyond tofu: firming agent (maintains firmness in canned vegetables and fruit), acidity regulator (provides magnesium and chloride ions to adjust pH), mineral supplement (added to mineral water and sports drinks for magnesium content), anti-caking agent (hygroscopic properties can actually make it an anti-caking agent in some powders or a humectant) and food-grade dust suppression. Nutrition: magnesium is an essential macromineral required for 300+ enzymatic reactions, muscle and nerve function, blood pressure regulation, bone mineralisation and glucose metabolism. The RDA for adults is 310–420 mg/day; many Indians are subclinically magnesium-deficient. Magnesium chloride is one of the most bioavailable forms of supplemental magnesium. Side effects: excess magnesium from food sources causes osmotic diarrhoea — magnesium is used as a laxative at high doses (magnesium citrate, magnesium oxide). At normal food additive concentrations no adverse effects. Caution in severe renal disease (magnesium excretion is impaired). ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E511), FSSAI and CODEX. Vegan; derived from seawater.""",
    'e511': 'E511 / INS 511 — Magnesium Chloride (Nigari). Traditional Japanese tofu coagulant; essential magnesium source; the reason traditional tofu is high in magnesium. See INS 511 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'calcium sulphate food': """Calcium sulphate (E516/INS 516 / CaSO₄ / food-grade gypsum) is a naturally occurring mineral that has been used for over 2,000 years in Chinese tofu production. Slightly soluble white powder. Functions in food: tofu coagulant — slowly dissolves to release calcium ions which precipitate soy protein curds; produces a firm, smooth tofu with high calcium content (calcium-set tofu is among the best dietary calcium sources — up to 350 mg per 100g, comparable to milk); firming agent in canned vegetables, fruit products and beer; leavening agent in bread and baked goods (reacts with sodium bicarbonate for slow leavening); acidity regulator; calcium supplement in fortified foods and beverages. Calcium content: approximately 29% elemental calcium by weight. Bioavailability from calcium sulphate is moderate — slightly lower than calcium chloride but better than calcium carbonate in some studies. Historical significance: the traditional Chinese preparation of doufu (tofu) using 'plaster of Paris' (gypsum) coagulation is one of the oldest examples of food technology. Widely used in Indian soy-based products, processed cheese and bakery. No genotoxicity or toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E516), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Safe for all populations including infants.""",
    'e516': 'E516 / INS 516 — Calcium Sulphate (food-grade gypsum). Ancient tofu coagulant and significant dietary calcium source. See INS 516 for full scientific description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e524': 'E524 is the EU/INS code for Sodium Hydroxide (Lye). Processing aid for pretzels and olives; neutralised in final product. Safe.',
    'e525': 'E525 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Hydroxide. Processing aid in cocoa; neutralised in final product. Safe.',
    'e526': 'E526 is the EU/INS code for Calcium Hydroxide (Slaked Lime). Used in traditional nixtamalisation of corn; provides calcium. Safe.',
    'e530': 'E530 is the EU/INS code for Magnesium Oxide. Anti-caking agent; provides magnesium. Safe.',
    'silicon dioxide food': """Silicon dioxide (amorphous silica / E551/INS 551) is produced for food use by controlled precipitation or pyrogenic processes, yielding an ultra-fine white powder with enormous surface area (up to 700 m²/g). Distinct from crystalline silica (quartz, cristobalite) which is an occupational carcinogen via inhalation — food-grade amorphous silica has a different structure and safety profile. Mechanism: enormous surface area absorbs moisture, preventing hygroscopic food powders from absorbing water vapour and clumping. Used in: table salt, powdered spices, instant soup mixes, coffee whitener, seasoning blends, protein powders and pharmaceutical tablets. Also a clarification aid in beer and wine. Nanoparticle concern: EFSA 2018 and 2021 evaluations concluded that food-grade E551 may contain nano-sized particles (<100 nm) and that genotoxicity risk from nanoparticle SiO₂ could not be fully excluded based on available data — though no human harm has been confirmed. The EU retained approval while monitoring; FDA maintains GRAS status. FSSAI, EU (E551) and CODEX approved. At normal food additive levels, amorphous silica is essentially non-absorbed (excreted in urine as soluble silicic acid — silicon is actually an essential trace nutrient for bone health). Generally safe for all consumers at food use levels.""",
    'e551': 'E551 / INS 551 — Silicon Dioxide (Amorphous Silica, SiO₂). Inorganic anti-caking agent with enormous surface area (up to 300 m²/g) — physically absorbs moisture and separates powder particles to prevent clumping. Biologically inert; not absorbed; excreted in urine as silicic acid. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e552': 'E552 / INS 552 — Calcium Silicate. Inorganic anti-caking agent used in baking powder, table salt and powdered mixes. See full description in the anti-caking agents section above. Poor solubility; not significantly absorbed. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e553a': 'E553a / INS 553a — Magnesium Silicates (synthetic amorphous and trimagnesium disilicate). Inorganic anti-caking agents in powdered foods, spice blends and baking powder. See full description above. Safe.',
    'e572': 'E572 / INS 572 — Magnesium Stearate (Mg(C₁₈H₃₅O₂)₂) is the magnesium salt of stearic acid — a fine white powder with a characteristic waxy feel. Used as: (1) an anti-caking agent and flow improver in powdered foods, spice blends and dietary supplements — its waxy surface coating on particles prevents clumping and improves flow through manufacturing equipment; (2) a tablet lubricant in the pharmaceutical industry — by far its most important application, used in >90% of tablet formulations to prevent tablets from sticking to production tooling; (3) a release agent in confectionery moulds. At food and supplement use levels, magnesium stearate is essentially non-toxic: it is hydrolysed in the gut to stearic acid (a common saturated fatty acid, also found in cocoa butter) and magnesium ions — both of which are normal dietary components. There is ongoing debate in the supplement industry about whether magnesium stearate affects bioavailability of the active ingredients in capsules (the "biofilm" hypothesis) — but scientific evidence does not support this concern at typical supplement manufacturing concentrations. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'sodium ferrocyanide': 'Sodium Ferrocyanide (E535/INS 535) prevents table salt from clumping. Despite the name, the cyanide is firmly bound to iron and not released at food-use conditions. Safe in the tiny concentrations used in salt.',
    'e535': 'E535 is the EU/INS code for Sodium Ferrocyanide. Anti-caking agent in salt. Cyanide firmly bound -- safe at approved levels.',
    'e536': 'E536 is the EU/INS code for Potassium Ferrocyanide. Anti-caking agent in salt. Stable; safe.',
    'e558': 'E558 is the EU/INS code for Bentonite. Natural clay used to clarify wine; filtered out. Safe.',
    'e559': 'E559 is the EU/INS code for Kaolin (China Clay). Anti-caking agent. Safe.',

    # ── Flavour enhancers (E620-E650) ────────────────────────────────────
    'glutamic acid food': 'Glutamic Acid (E620/INS 620) is the naturally occurring amino acid responsible for umami taste in meat, cheese, tomatoes and mushrooms. Safe -- one of the most abundant amino acids in the diet.',
    'e620': 'E620 is the EU/INS code for Glutamic Acid. Natural amino acid responsible for umami taste. Safe.',
    'e622': 'E622 / INS 622 — Monopotassium Glutamate (MPG) is the potassium salt of glutamic acid — a direct functional equivalent of MSG (monosodium glutamate) but replacing sodium with potassium. It provides the same umami taste enhancement as MSG (activating the T1R1/T1R3 glutamate receptor) while contributing potassium rather than sodium — making it preferred in low-sodium formulations for people managing hypertension or sodium intake. Potassium is an essential mineral that counterbalances the blood-pressure-raising effects of sodium. Used in soups, sauces, seasonings and reduced-sodium food products. Safety: glutamate is the most abundant amino acid in the diet, essential for metabolism; MPG is metabolised identically to MSG. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e623': 'E623 / INS 623 — Calcium Diglutamate is the calcium salt of glutamic acid — a flavour enhancer that provides both umami taste enhancement and dietary calcium in a single additive. Like all glutamate salts, it activates the T1R1/T1R3 umami taste receptor. It provides approximately 13% calcium by weight — a modest but real calcium contribution in food products. Used in sauces, soups, seasonings and dairy-based products where calcium enhancement is desirable. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'e624': 'E624 / INS 624 — Monoammonium Glutamate (MAG) is the ammonium salt of glutamic acid, used as a flavour enhancer with the same umami-activating mechanism as MSG. The ammonium moiety is rapidly metabolised in the liver via the urea cycle and excreted as urea — a normal metabolic process at food use concentrations. Less commonly used than MSG, MPG or calcium diglutamate; sometimes preferred where neither sodium nor potassium content is desired. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'e625': 'E625 / INS 625 — Magnesium Diglutamate is the magnesium salt of glutamic acid — a flavour enhancer that provides both umami taste enhancement and dietary magnesium. Magnesium is an essential mineral required for over 300 enzymatic reactions; deficiency is widespread in India and globally. E625 provides approximately 10% magnesium by weight. Used in soups, seasonings and functional food products where magnesium fortification and flavour enhancement are both desired. JECFA ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. Safe.',
    'guanylic acid': """Guanylic acid (GMP / guanosine monophosphate / guanosine 5'-monophosphate / E626/INS 626) is a nucleotide — composed of the purine nucleobase guanine, the sugar ribose and a phosphate group. It occurs naturally in all living organisms as a cellular metabolite (component of RNA, GTP energy carrier, signalling molecules). Commercially, guanylic acid and its salts are produced by enzymatic hydrolysis of RNA from yeast extract or microbial fermentation, or by extraction from fish (sardines, anchovies) and dried mushrooms (especially shiitake / Lentinula edodes — which contain particularly high levels of guanylic acid and inosinic acid combined, explaining the intense umami of dried shiitake in Japanese dashi broth). It is a powerful umami synergist: guanylic acid and inosinic acid (E630) act synergistically with monosodium glutamate (E621) at a molecular level — they bind adjacent to glutamate at the metabotropic glutamate receptor (mGluR4), dramatically amplifying perceived umami intensity. A mixture of 0.5% GMP + 0.5% IMP can produce the same umami intensity as 2.5% MSG — this allows manufacturers to reduce MSG content while maintaining flavour intensity. Used in: instant noodles, chips, snack seasonings, soups, bouillon cubes, seasoning powders and ready meals. The salts used commercially: disodium guanylate (E627/INS 627) and dipotassium guanylate (E628/INS 628) are more soluble and commonly used than the free acid. Safety: guanylic acid is metabolised to uric acid via the purine catabolism pathway. High purine intake raises serum uric acid levels — people with gout (hyperuricaemia with urate crystal deposition in joints) should restrict all purine-rich foods including nucleotide flavour enhancers. Also avoid in patients with Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (HGPRT deficiency). Safe for healthy individuals at food use concentrations. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E626), FSSAI, FDA and CODEX. Source transparency: manufacturers should indicate whether derived from fish/animal (not vegan) or microbial fermentation (vegan).""",
    'e626': 'E626 is the EU/INS code for Guanylic Acid. Nucleotide umami enhancer; high purine -- avoid with gout.',
    'e628': 'E628 is the EU/INS code for Dipotassium Guanylate. Nucleotide flavour enhancer; avoid with gout.',
    'e629': 'E629 is the EU/INS code for Calcium Guanylate. Nucleotide flavour enhancer; avoid with gout.',
    'inosinic acid': """Inosinic acid (IMP / inosine monophosphate / inosine 5'-monophosphate / E630/INS 630) is a nucleotide composed of the purine nucleobase hypoxanthine, the sugar ribose and a phosphate group. Like guanylic acid (GMP), IMP is a normal metabolic intermediate present in all living cells — formed from AMP (adenosine monophosphate) by deamination and as an intermediate in purine synthesis. Natural sources: inosinic acid is found in high concentrations in meat (especially pork and beef), fish (sardines, mackerel, tuna, anchovies), poultry and dried fish products — explaining the intense umami flavour of high-quality meat stocks and Japanese dashi (where katsuobushi/dried bonito flakes are rich in IMP). Commercially: produced by enzymatic hydrolysis of RNA from yeast extract or by microbial fermentation; the disodium salt (disodium inosinate / E631/INS 631) is the commercially used form. Umami synergism: IMP acts synergistically with glutamate (MSG, E621) — binds to the flavour-1 site on the taste receptor TAS1R1/TAS1R3 complex, amplifying glutamate's umami signal 7–15 fold depending on concentration ratio. This allows significant reduction of MSG content in formulations while maintaining or increasing umami intensity. Used in: instant noodles (ubiquitous), flavoured crisps and chips, soup powders, seasonings, ready meals, bouillon, sauces and processed meats. Frequently listed with disodium guanylate (E627) as a combined enhancer (E635 / INS 635 = ribonucleotides mixture). Safety: metabolised to uric acid via purine catabolism. Like all purine-rich compounds, high intake raises serum uric acid levels. People with gout, hyperuricaemia, kidney stones (urate type) or Lesch-Nyhan syndrome must restrict intake. Animal-derived unless the manufacturing source is microbial fermentation — vegetarians and vegans should check source disclosure. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by EU (E630), FSSAI, FDA and CODEX. Standard Indian snack products (Lay's, Uncle Chips, etc.) commonly contain E631 + E627 combination.""",
    'e630': 'E630 is the EU/INS code for Inosinic Acid. Nucleotide umami enhancer; avoid with gout.',
    'e632': 'E632 is the EU/INS code for Dipotassium Inosinate. Nucleotide flavour enhancer; avoid with gout.',
    'e633': 'E633 is the EU/INS code for Calcium Inosinate. Nucleotide flavour enhancer; avoid with gout.',
    'glycine food': """Glycine (E640/INS 640 / aminoacetic acid / H₂N-CH₂-COOH) is the simplest and only achiral amino acid — consisting of just an amino group, a carboxyl group and a hydrogen atom at the alpha carbon. It is a proteinogenic amino acid — found in all proteins, with particularly high concentrations in collagen (the most abundant protein in the body, where glycine constitutes ~33% of all residues as the repeating sequence Gly-Pro-Hyp). Non-essential — the body synthesises sufficient glycine from serine and threonine. Naturally abundant in: gelatin, bone broth, collagen supplements, meat, poultry, fish, soybeans, legumes and many other protein-containing foods. Used as a food additive (E640/INS 640) as: a mild sweetener (glycine has a pleasant, slightly sweet taste — approximately 0.7× the sweetness of sucrose, with a clean profile that can mask bitter notes); a flavour enhancer (rounds and improves overall flavour in savoury applications without the pronounced umami of MSG); a bitter masker (used to mask bitterness from caffeine, artificial sweeteners, minerals or pharmaceutical compounds); an acidulant and buffering agent (acts as a mild acid and buffer); an anti-crystal agent in confectionery. Food applications: soy sauce and fermented food seasoning, instant beverages (masks caffeine bitterness), pharmaceutical tablets (tablet binder and taste masker), food supplements, Japanese-style sweets (wagashi) where glycine is used as a conventional preservative and sweetener. Pharmacological uses at higher doses: glycine is an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem; research interest in supplemental glycine for sleep quality, schizophrenia (as an adjunct to antipsychotics), metabolic syndrome and gut health. Safety: no known toxicity at food additive or supplemental doses. ADI: not specified (JECFA — acceptable for use in food). Approved by EU (E640), FSSAI and CODEX. Vegan if synthetic (produced from chloroacetic acid and ammonia); non-vegan if derived from animal collagen/gelatin hydrolysis.""",
    'e640': 'E640 is the EU/INS code for Glycine and its Sodium Salt. Amino acid flavour enhancer. Safe.',

    # ── Glazing agents / waxes / gases (E900-E999) ───────────────────────
    'dimethyl polysiloxane': 'Dimethyl Polysiloxane (PDMS/E900/INS 900) is a silicone polymer anti-foaming agent in frying oils and beverages. Not absorbed by the body. Safe.',
    'e900': 'E900 is the EU/INS code for Dimethyl Polysiloxane. Silicone anti-foaming agent. Safe.',
    'beeswax food': 'Beeswax (E901/INS 901) is a natural wax secreted by worker honeybees (Apis mellifera and related species) from specialised wax-producing glands on their abdomen — used by bees to construct the hexagonal honeycomb cells of the hive. It is harvested by melting and purifying the empty honeycomb. Composition: complex mixture of monoesters of fatty acids and fatty alcohols (~35%), diesters (~14%), saturated and unsaturated fatty acids (~12%), hydrocarbons (~14%), triesters (~3%) and other minor components. Melting point: ~62–65°C. Food applications: (1) glazing agent on confectionery (hard-coated sweets, chocolate products, jelly beans — gives the characteristic high-gloss finish); (2) coatings on fresh fruits (citrus, apples) to replace natural waxes removed during washing and extend shelf life; (3) pharmaceutical tablet coating; (4) cheese rind coating. Texture and flavour: beeswax imparts a very mild, barely perceptible wax note at food application levels. Animal-derived — NOT vegan; may also have religious dietary considerations. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e901': 'E901 / INS 901 — Beeswax. Natural honeybee-derived glazing agent used in confectionery coatings and fresh fruit waxing. See beeswax food for full description. Not vegan. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'candelilla wax': 'Candelilla Wax (E902/INS 902) is a natural plant-derived wax obtained from the hard wax-coated stems of the candelilla shrub (Euphorbia antisyphilitica and related Euphorbia species), native to the Chihuahuan Desert of northern Mexico and the Big Bend region of southwest Texas. The plant coats its stems with wax as an adaptation to prevent desiccation in the arid desert climate. Harvested by boiling the plant stems in acidified water (with sulphuric acid to break the wax-plant bond), skimming the floating wax layer, and purifying it. Composition: high in hydrocarbons (paraffin waxes, ~50% alkanes), with esters (~28%), alcohols (~11%) and resins (~9%). Melting point: ~68–72°C — harder than beeswax but softer than carnauba wax. Requires a higher concentration than carnauba wax to achieve equivalent hardness in formulations. Applications: (1) glazing agent on confectionery (chewing gum, sweets, chocolate products); (2) lipstick, lip balm and cosmetics — the primary plant-based wax for lip products; (3) pharmaceutical tablet coatings; (4) vegan beeswax substitute. Candelilla wax is a key ingredient in the Mexican wax industry. VEGAN — plant-derived; suitable for vegetarians and vegans. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e902': 'E902 / INS 902 — Candelilla Wax. Plant-derived, vegan glazing agent from the candelilla shrub of the Chihuahuan Desert. See candelilla wax for full description. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'carnauba wax food': 'Carnauba Wax (E903/INS 903) — the hardest natural wax known, from Brazilian carnauba palm leaves (Copernicia prunifera). See INS 903 for full scientific description. Vegan; high melting point (~80–86°C); exceptional gloss and moisture resistance. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e903': 'E903 is the EU/INS code for Carnauba Wax. The shine on hard-coated confectionery. Safe.',
    'shellac food': 'Shellac (E904/INS 904, Laccifer lacca resin) is a natural resin secreted by the female lac insect (Kerria lacca, formerly Laccifer lacca) — a scale insect that lives on the sap of specific host trees (Butea monosperma, Schleichera oleosa and others) in tropical Asia. The resin is secreted as a protective coating around the insect colonies. India and Thailand are the world\'s two largest producers, and India (particularly Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and West Bengal) historically produced the majority of global shellac supply — making shellac an important Indian forest industry product. Composition: shellac is a complex polymer of aleuritic acid and shellolic acid esters; approximately 65–90% resin, 3–5% wax and 6–7% water. Soluble in alcohol (ethanol) but not in water. Exceptionally hard and high-gloss forming. Applications: (1) pharmaceutical — coating for tablet time-release (enteric coating) and confectionery pills; (2) confectionery — chocolate products (notably "confectioner\'s glaze" on M&Ms and similar coated chocolates), jelly beans, other hard-coated confectionery; (3) fresh citrus fruit coatings (orange, lemon, tangerine) to extend shelf life; (4) wood and instrument varnish (non-food). ANIMAL-DERIVED — NOT VEGAN: shellac is harvested from insect secretions; vegans and some Jain communities avoid it. Halal status: scholars differ — some consider shellac halal (insect origin, impurity questions); others consider it haram. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'e904': 'E904 / INS 904 — Shellac. Insect-derived natural resin glazing agent from the lac insect; India is a major producer. See shellac food for full scientific description. Not vegan; check halal status. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'e920': 'E920 is the EU/INS code for L-Cysteine. Dough conditioner amino acid. Often from hair/feathers -- check vegan status. Safe.',
    'e941': 'E941 is the EU/INS code for Nitrogen. Inert packaging gas. Safe -- makes up 78% of air.',
    'e942': 'E942 is the EU/INS code for Nitrous Oxide. Whipped cream propellant. Safe in food; dangerous recreationally.',

    # ── Sweeteners (E950-E968) ───────────────────────────────────────────
    'steviol glycosides': 'Steviol Glycosides (Stevia/E960/INS 960) are naturally sweet compounds from the stevia plant -- 200-400 times sweeter than sugar. Zero calories. Approved by FSSAI, FDA, EU and Codex. Safe; suitable for diabetics.',
    'e960': 'E960 is the EU/INS code for Steviol Glycosides (Stevia). Natural zero-calorie sweetener. Safe; suitable for diabetics.',
    'thaumatin': """Thaumatin (E957/INS 957) is a natural intensely sweet-tasting protein extracted from the arils (the fleshy seed coverings) of the katemfe fruit (Thaumatococcus daniellii), a plant native to the tropical rainforests of West Africa (Ghana, Côte d'Ivoire, Cameroon). The protein consists of two forms (thaumatin I and thaumatin II) — both approximately 207 amino acids long with a molecular weight of ~22,000 Da. Thaumatin is approximately 2,000–3,000 times sweeter than sucrose on a weight basis; however, because it is active at such tiny concentrations (0.5–5 ppm), its caloric contribution is essentially zero in practical use. The onset of sweetness is slower than sugar (takes a second to develop), and it lingers longer — the "taste profile" has a liquorice-like, or sometimes menthol-like character at higher concentrations. This temporal profile makes it difficult to use as a direct 1:1 sugar substitute in most applications. Primary uses: (1) Flavour enhancer and sweetness modifier — at very low sub-sweet concentrations (0.1–1 ppm), thaumatin modifies and enhances other flavours (vanilla, mint, citrus, fruity notes) — this flavour-modifying property is arguably its most commercially valuable application. Used in chewing gum, oral hygiene products (toothpastes, mouthwash), dairy desserts, dietary supplements and tabletop sweeteners. (2) High-intensity sweetener — at higher concentrations (>3 ppm) delivers intense sweetness with calorie-free or negligible calorie contribution; used in combination with other sweeteners. (3) Flavour masking — masks bitterness of active pharmaceutical ingredients and bitterness in protein-fortified products. As a protein, thaumatin is digested and absorbed like any dietary protein — metabolised to amino acids. No evidence of allergenicity. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. No effect on blood glucose or insulin. Approved by EU (E957), FSSAI (permitted as a sweetener), FDA (GRAS), Australia/NZ (Code A13) and CODEX. ADI: not specified (JECFA — considered acceptable when used as a food additive). Vegan; plant-derived protein.""",
    'e957': 'E957 is the EU/INS code for Thaumatin. Natural sweet protein; ~2,500x sweeter than sugar. Safe.',
    'neotame': """Neotame (E961/INS 961) is a synthetic ultra-high-intensity non-caloric sweetener and a structural derivative of aspartame — made by reacting aspartame with 3,3-dimethylbutyraldehyde to add a 3,3-dimethylbutyl group to the nitrogen of the aspartate moiety. This single structural modification dramatically increases sweetness potency (approximately 7,000–13,000 times sweeter than sucrose, compared to aspartame's 200× — making neotame one of the two or three most potent approved sweeteners) and eliminates the phenylalanine metabolite concern. Why safe for phenylketonurics: in standard aspartame, the phenylalanyl ester bond is hydrolysed in the gut releasing free phenylalanine — problematic for PKU patients who cannot metabolise phenylalanine. In neotame, the bulky 3,3-dimethylbutyl group sterically blocks the same enzymatic hydrolysis — only trace amounts of phenylalanine are released (well below any PKU-relevant threshold), so neotame does NOT require a phenylalanine warning on product labels. Potency comparison: while aspartame requires approximately 0.5 g to sweeten a litre of beverage, neotame requires only ~0.04 g — meaning trace amounts provide commercial sweetening. Heat stability: neotame is significantly more heat-stable than aspartame — can be used in baked goods where aspartame degrades. pH stability: moderate — best performance at acidic to neutral pH. Applications: carbonated and non-carbonated beverages, dairy products, frozen desserts, baked goods, chewing gum, tabletop sweeteners and health foods. Metabolism: the small amount of neotame absorbed is rapidly and completely metabolised — converted to N-[N-(3,3-dimethylbutyl)-L-α-aspartyl]-L-phenylalanine (de-esterified neotame) and excreted primarily in urine; no accumulation. Comprehensive safety testing in multiple animal species showed no carcinogenicity, mutagenicity, developmental toxicity or neurological effects. Does not raise blood glucose. No bitter aftertaste at typical use levels (slight aftertaste at very high concentrations). FDA approved in 2002; EU approved 2010; FSSAI approved; CODEX approved. JECFA ADI: 2 mg/kg body weight/day. One of the most thoroughly tested sweeteners despite limited consumer awareness in India.""",
    'e961': 'E961 / INS 961 — Neotame. Ultra-high-potency synthetic sweetener (7,000–13,000× sweeter than sugar); derivative of aspartame but does not release phenylalanine — safe for PKU patients. See neotame entry for full details. ADI: 2 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FDA, EU and FSSAI.',
    'e953': 'E953 is the EU/INS code for Isomalt. Sugar alcohol from sucrose; laxative at high intake; label warning required.',
    'e965': 'E965 is the EU/INS code for Maltitol. Sugar alcohol sweetener; laxative at >40g/day; label warning required.',
    'e966': 'E966 is the EU/INS code for Lactitol. Sugar alcohol; laxative at high intake; not for lactose-intolerant individuals.',
    'e967': 'E967 is the EU/INS code for Xylitol. Natural sugar alcohol with dental benefits. CAUTION -- toxic to dogs. Laxative effects in humans at high doses.',
    'erythritol': """Erythritol (E968/INS 968 / 1,2,3,4-butanetetraol / meso-erythritol) is a four-carbon sugar alcohol (polyol) that occurs naturally in small amounts in grapes, pears, watermelon, mushrooms, fermented beverages (wine, beer, sake) and fermented foods (miso, soy sauce). Commercially produced by fermentation — specifically by Moniliella pollinis, Trichosporonoides megachiliensis or other osmophilic yeasts fermenting glucose or sucrose from wheat or corn starch. Produced in large quantities primarily in Japan and South Korea. Structural uniqueness: erythritol's four-carbon symmetrical structure (meso compound) makes it completely unable to be fermented by human gut bacteria — unlike sorbitol, mannitol, maltitol and xylitol (all longer chain or with branching that gut bacteria can ferment, causing the gas/diarrhoea associated with excess sugar alcohol intake). Sweetness: approximately 60–70% as sweet as sucrose, with a clean, non-bitter profile and a characteristic slight cooling sensation on the tongue (due to negative enthalpy of dissolution — it absorbs heat as it dissolves in saliva). Caloric value: officially zero to 0.2 kcal/g (EU/FDA: 0 kcal/g; some national authorities allow 0.2 kcal/g) — erythritol is absorbed rapidly and efficiently in the small intestine (~90%) but passes unchanged into blood and is excreted unmetabolised in urine within 24 hours; the body cannot metabolise the erythritol molecule (no erythritol dehydrogenase enzyme). Glycaemic index: zero — does not raise blood glucose or insulin levels. Suitable for diabetics and people following ketogenic/low-carb diets. Dental benefits: erythritol is non-cariogenic (does not feed caries-causing bacteria) and may actually reduce Streptococcus mutans colonisation at 10–20 g/day (better evidence than xylitol on this specific endpoint). Digestive tolerance: as the only sugar alcohol almost completely absorbed before reaching the colon, erythritol has the best digestive tolerance of all polyol sweeteners — the threshold for GI effects (gas, diarrhoea) is approximately 50 g per day in adults, compared to 10–20 g for sorbitol and 20–30 g for xylitol. Applications: zero-calorie beverages, protein bars, keto-friendly chocolates, chewing gum, tabletop sweeteners, baked goods, candy and frozen desserts. Widely used in India in diabetic-friendly and weight-management products. Safety: extensively studied; no carcinogenicity, no reproductive toxicity, no genotoxicity. 2023 observational study (Hazen et al.) associated high erythritol plasma levels with cardiovascular events — this was a correlational study (erythritol was already elevated in high-cardiometabolic-risk patients who consumed it) and does not establish causation; multiple expert agencies maintain erythritol's safety approval. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E968), FDA (GRAS) and CODEX. Vegan; from plant fermentation.""",
    'e968': 'E968 / INS 968 — Erythritol. Natural four-carbon sugar alcohol; zero-calorie; best GI tolerance of all polyols (nearly all absorbed before colon); does not raise blood glucose. See erythritol entry for full details. ADI: not specified. Safe.',
    'cyclamate': """Cyclamate (E952/INS 952 / cyclamic acid / N-cyclohexylsulfamic acid) is a synthetic non-nutritive sweetener — approximately 30–50 times sweeter than sucrose at use concentrations, with a clean, mellow sweetness profile very similar to sugar, lacking the bitter aftertaste of saccharin. Discovered accidentally in 1937 at the University of Illinois (Michael Sveda left a cigarette on a laboratory bench contaminated with cyclamate, noticed it tasted sweet when putting it in his mouth). Used commercially as its sodium salt (sodium cyclamate) or calcium salt (calcium cyclamate). Commonly blended with saccharin in a 10:1 (cyclamate:saccharin) ratio to mask saccharin's bitterness and extend effective sweetness with a more sugar-like profile. Regulatory controversy: in 1969, the US FDA banned cyclamate after a study reported bladder tumours in rats fed a 10:1 mixture of cyclamate + saccharin at very high doses (the equivalent of drinking 350+ cans of diet soda per day). Subsequent investigation found: (1) the carcinogenic effect in that study was attributed primarily to saccharin, not cyclamate; (2) a specific testosterone-dependent mechanism in male rats converts cyclohexylamine (a cyclamate metabolite) to cyclohexane-sulfamic acid crystalline deposits in the bladder, creating a physical irritant — this mechanism does not apply to humans (different urinary composition, different pH). Multiple scientific reviews since 1969 have not confirmed a carcinogenic risk for cyclamate in humans. Cyclamate remains banned in the USA despite at least two FDA reinstatement petitions (1982, 2002) — the regulatory position is political/procedural rather than purely scientific. Also banned or restricted in the UK. Approved in: India (FSSAI — permitted in table-top sweeteners, beverages and certain food categories at specified limits), EU (E952, with ADI-based limits), Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and over 100 countries worldwide. WHO/JECFA ADI: 11 mg/kg body weight/day. Approximately 20–40% of people can metabolise cyclamate to cyclohexylamine — those individuals ('converters') produce higher cyclohexylamine levels from equivalent cyclamate intake, which is relevant for people at very high intakes. Zero calories; does not raise blood glucose; suitable for diabetics.""",
    'e952': 'E952 / INS 952 — Cyclamate (sodium or calcium cyclamate). Non-caloric synthetic sweetener; 30–50× sweeter than sucrose. See cyclamate entry for full regulatory history. Banned in USA; approved in EU, India and over 100 countries. ADI: 11 mg/kg bw/day.',
    'sodium cyclamate': """Sodium cyclamate (E952/INS 952) is the sodium salt form of cyclamic acid (N-cyclohexylsulfamic acid) — the most commercially important form of cyclamate used in food and beverage products. Highly water-soluble white crystalline powder; stable in acidic conditions and at high temperatures (heat-stable up to 200°C), making it suitable for cooked or processed food applications unlike aspartame. Approximately 30–50 times sweeter than sucrose at use concentrations; clean sweetness profile closest to sucrose of any high-intensity sweetener; frequently combined with saccharin at 10:1 ratio (cyclamate:saccharin) to achieve synergistic sweetening with minimal bitterness. Applications: tabletop sweeteners (sachets, liquid drops), sugar-free beverages (soft drinks, fruit juices, cordials), dairy products (yogurt, ice cream), desserts, jams, confectionery, baked goods, pharmaceuticals (syrups, chewable tablets) and canned fruit. The sodium content at food use concentrations is extremely small and nutritionally irrelevant. Banned in USA (1969, based on disputed rat bladder cancer study); banned in UK. Approved in India under FSSAI, EU (E952), Canada, Australia/NZ, WHO/JECFA and over 100 countries. JECFA ADI: 11 mg/kg body weight/day. Zero calories; GI: effectively 0 (not metabolised to glucose). Suitable for diabetics, people on ketogenic diets and weight management. See cyclamate entry for full scientific and regulatory details.""",

    # ── Modified starches (E1400-E1599) ──────────────────────────────────
    'e1404': 'E1404 is the EU/INS code for Oxidised Starch. Modified starch for confectionery coatings. Safe.',
    'e1410': 'E1410 is the EU/INS code for Monostarch Phosphate. Modified starch thickener. Safe.',
    'e1412': 'E1412 is the EU/INS code for Distarch Phosphate. Cross-linked modified starch. Safe.',
    'e1420': 'E1420 is the EU/INS code for Acetylated Starch. Modified starch. Safe.',
    'e1422': 'E1422 is the EU/INS code for Acetylated Distarch Adipate. Modified starch in soups and frozen foods. Safe.',
    'e1440': 'E1440 is the EU/INS code for Hydroxypropyl Starch. Modified starch. Safe.',
    'e1442': 'E1442 is the EU/INS code for Hydroxypropyl Distarch Phosphate. Widely used modified starch in ketchup, mayo and baby food. Safe.',
    'e1450': 'E1450 is the EU/INS code for Starch Sodium Octenyl Succinate. Emulsifying modified starch for encapsulating flavours. Safe.',
    'hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate': """Hydroxypropyl distarch phosphate (E1442/INS 1442) is a dual-chemically modified starch with both phosphate crosslinking (provides heat, acid and shear stability) and hydroxypropylation (prevents retrogradation, provides freeze-thaw stability). The combination makes it the most functionally stable and versatile modified starch commercially available. Superior to distarch phosphate (E1412) in freeze-thaw performance and superior to hydroxypropyl starch (E1440) in heat-acid stability. Used in: frozen ready meals and soups, ketchup, tomato sauce, mayonnaise, baby food, fruit pie fillings, chocolate puddings, instant noodle sauces and dairy desserts. Fully digestible to glucose in the gut. Not a dietary fibre. ADI: not specified (JECFA). Approved by FSSAI, EU (E1442), FDA (GRAS for specified sources) and CODEX. Safe for all population groups.""",

    # ── INS number lookup entries ─────────────────────────────────────────
    'ins 100': 'INS 100 / E100 — Curcumin (Turmeric Yellow, diferuloylmethane, C₂₁H₂₀O₆) is the principal bioactive polyphenol and natural yellow-orange pigment of turmeric (Curcuma longa) rhizomes — the spice that gives Indian curry, mustard and golden milk their characteristic colour. Curcumin is responsible for approximately 80% of turmeric\'s colour and a substantial portion of its therapeutic properties. As a food colour, curcumin is extracted from turmeric rhizomes using solvents (ethanol, acetone) and the resulting extract is standardised to curcuminoid content. Colour: vivid yellow-orange at neutral pH; orange-red in alkaline conditions; used in cheese, butter, ice cream, mustard, confectionery, beverages and snacks. Scientific interest: curcumin is one of the most extensively researched phytochemicals — over 10,000 peer-reviewed studies. Documented properties include: anti-inflammatory (inhibits NF-κB signalling and COX-2 enzyme), antioxidant (scavenges free radicals and induces antioxidant enzymes via Nrf2 pathway), antimicrobial, anti-proliferative (multiple cancer cell lines in-vitro), and neuroprotective. Bioavailability limitation: curcumin is poorly water-soluble and rapidly metabolised/eliminated — oral bioavailability is low. Piperine (black pepper extract), lipid formulations and nanoparticle delivery significantly enhance absorption. Safety: curcumin has an excellent safety profile at food-use levels; JECFA ADI 0–3 mg/kg bw/day. At very high supplemental doses, mild GI discomfort may occur. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. One of the most important Indian natural food ingredients.',
    'ins 101': 'INS 101 / E101 — Riboflavin (Vitamin B2, 7,8-dimethyl-10-ribitylisoalloxazine, C₁₇H₂₀N₄O₆) is a water-soluble B-vitamin that serves both as an essential human micronutrient and as a natural yellow food colour. As a yellow pigment, riboflavin imparts a characteristic bright yellow-green fluorescent colour (it absorbs blue-violet light and emits yellow-green fluorescence — this is why riboflavin-containing beverages and preparations glow under UV light). Used to colour beverages, energy drinks, infant formula, nutritional supplements, dairy products and yellow-hued confectionery. Sources: riboflavin is naturally abundant in organ meats (liver, kidneys), dairy products, eggs, almonds and certain leafy vegetables; commercially produced by microbial fermentation (Bacillus subtilis or Ashbya gossypii) for both nutritional supplementation and food colouring. Physiological roles: riboflavin is a precursor for the essential coenzymes FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide) and FMN (flavin mononucleotide), which are critical electron carriers in the mitochondrial electron transport chain (energy production), beta-oxidation of fatty acids, amino acid catabolism and many oxidoreductase reactions. Deficiency (ariboflavinosis) causes cheilosis (cracked corners of mouth), angular stomatitis, glossitis and corneal vascularisation. Adding riboflavin as a food colour also fortifies the product with Vitamin B2 — uniquely, the colour and nutrition functions are combined. ADI: not specified (essential nutrient). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 102': 'INS 102 / E102 -- Tartrazine. Synthetic yellow azo dye. EU warning label required. See tartrazine.',
    'ins 110': 'INS 110 / E110 -- Sunset Yellow FCF. Synthetic orange azo dye. EU warning label required.',
    'ins 129': 'INS 129 / E129 -- Allura Red AC (Red 40). Synthetic red azo dye. EU warning label required.',
    'ins 150d': 'INS 150d / E150d -- Sulphite Ammonia Caramel. Contains 4-MEI (IARC Group 2B). See e150d.',
    'ins 160a': 'INS 160a / E160a -- Beta-Carotene. Natural orange-yellow colour; Vitamin A precursor. Safe.',
    'ins 160b': 'INS 160b / E160b — Annatto (Bixin / Norbixin) is a natural orange-yellow to orange-red carotenoid pigment extracted from the seeds of the achiote tree (Bixa orellana), native to tropical America and widely cultivated in India. The two main pigment compounds are bixin (oil-soluble, orange) and norbixin (water-soluble, yellow-orange). Used to colour dairy products (butter, cheese, ghee), rice, breakfast cereals, snack foods and beverages. It is one of the oldest and most widely used natural food colours globally. Safety: EFSA 2016 re-evaluation set an ADI of 0.1 mg/kg bw/day based on developmental toxicity data in rats at high doses — significantly lower than the previous ADI; at typical food exposure levels most consumers stay below this. Allergy note: annatto is a relatively common cause of non-immunoglobulin-E-mediated pseudo-allergic reactions (urticaria, angioedema) — it is the most frequent natural food colour implicated in colour-related hypersensitivity reactions in Europe. If you have unexplained chronic urticaria, annatto is worth eliminating from the diet. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA; GRAS status USA.',
    'ins 162': 'INS 162 / E162 — Beetroot Red (Betanin / Betacyanin) is a natural reddish-purple pigment extracted from the roots of red beetroot (Beta vulgaris). The main pigment is betanin — a betalain compound (betacyanin) with the molecular formula C₂₄H₂₆N₂O₁₃. Used as a clean-label natural food colour in yogurt, ice cream, soft drinks, confectionery, breakfast cereals and meat alternatives. Heat stability is limited — betanin degrades above ~50°C, making it unsuitable for baked goods; it is also pH-sensitive (best stability at pH 3–7), light-sensitive and oxygen-sensitive. Despite these limitations, betanin has gained significant commercial importance as consumers and manufacturers shift away from synthetic dyes. Health benefits: red beetroot is one of the richest dietary sources of nitrates, betaine and antioxidant betacyanins; in concentrated forms, these have demonstrated blood-pressure-lowering, anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects in clinical studies. As a food colour extract at typical doses, health benefit effects are minimal. Completely safe; no ADI set (acceptable intake not limited). Approved by FSSAI, EU and FDA.',
    'ins 171': 'INS 171 / E171 — Titanium Dioxide (TiO₂) is an inorganic white mineral pigment — the whitest, most opaque food colour additive ever used. It is naturally occurring in the minerals rutile and anatase, mined globally and refined to food-grade purity. Used in confectionery (chewing gum, candy coatings), baked goods and dairy products. SAFETY CONCERN AND BAN: In May 2021, EFSA (European Food Safety Authority) published a comprehensive safety assessment concluding that titanium dioxide could no longer be considered safe as a food additive, primarily because: (1) it cannot be ruled out that TiO₂ nanoparticles (which constitute a significant fraction of food-grade TiO₂) are genotoxic — capable of damaging DNA; (2) concerns about accumulation in tissues (liver, spleen, kidneys) with chronic exposure; (3) incomplete data on absorption of nano-sized particles in the gut. The EU formally banned E171 in food from August 2022 (EU Regulation 2022/63). It remains permitted in the USA (FDA GRAS), India (FSSAI permits it), and many other markets. If you see "INS 171" or "Titanium Dioxide" in an Indian product, know it is banned in the EU for food use. For cosmetics and sunscreen, TiO₂ remains widely used and is well-tolerated at the skin surface (does not penetrate intact skin at relevant particle sizes).',
    'ins 200': 'INS 200 / E200 — Sorbic Acid (2,4-hexadienoic acid, CH₃-CH=CH-CH=CH-COOH) is a naturally occurring polyunsaturated fatty acid first isolated from the unripe berries of the rowan tree (Sorbus aucuparia) in 1859. It is a broad-spectrum antimicrobial preservative effective against yeasts, moulds and many bacteria, working by inhibiting dehydrogenase enzymes in microbial cells — disrupting their energy metabolism without affecting eukaryotic cells. Used in cheese, baked goods, dried fruits, wines, syrups and cosmetics. pH-sensitive: active primarily below pH 6.5 (most effective at pH 4–5); its potassium salt (INS 202) is preferred in water-based products for better solubility. Metabolically: sorbic acid is metabolised in mammals through the same beta-oxidation pathway as dietary fatty acids and is detoxified to carbon dioxide and water — no accumulation, no toxic metabolites. Extensive safety data: JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day; EFSA reconfirmed safety in 2015. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS). No genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity. The most toxicologically benign class of food preservatives.',
    'ins 202': 'INS 202 / E202 — Potassium Sorbate (potassium 2,4-hexadienoate, C₆H₇KO₂) is the potassium salt of sorbic acid — the most widely used food and cosmetic preservative in the world. It offers all the antimicrobial benefits of sorbic acid (INS 200) with significantly better water solubility, making it practical for liquid, semi-solid and water-based formulations. Mechanism: in acidic environments (pH <6.5) the potassium sorbate dissociates to release the active sorbate anion, which penetrates microbial cell membranes and inhibits key enzymatic processes, halting the growth of moulds, yeasts and many bacteria. Used to preserve bread, cheese, wine, juices, jams, dried fruits, yogurt, sauces and countless personal care products. Safety profile: metabolised identically to sorbic acid — completely converted to CO₂ and water via normal fatty acid beta-oxidation. JECFA ADI 0–25 mg/kg bw/day (as sorbic acid equivalent). EFSA and FDA classify as safe. One of the most comprehensively safety-tested food additives: negative in genotoxicity, carcinogenicity and reproductive toxicity studies. No cumulative or tissue accumulation. Occasional mild skin sensitisation in topical cosmetics reported at high concentrations; in food at permitted levels, no allergenic concern. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA, Codex Alimentarius.',
    'ins 211': 'INS 211 / E211 -- Sodium Benzoate. Preservative; forms benzene with Vitamin C. See sodium benzoate.',
    'ins 220': 'INS 220 / E220 -- Sulfur Dioxide. Preservative; triggers asthma in sensitive individuals. See sulfur dioxide.',
    'ins 250': 'INS 250 / E250 -- Sodium Nitrite. Meat curing agent; forms nitrosamines when heated. See sodium nitrite.',
    'ins 260': 'INS 260 / E260 — Acetic Acid (ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH) is the defining organic acid of vinegar — the compound that gives vinegar its sharp, sour taste and pungent smell. In food manufacturing it functions as an acidity regulator, flavouring, solvent and mild preservative (antimicrobial at concentrations above ~0.5%). At pH <5, undissociated acetic acid crosses microbial cell membranes and disrupts intracellular pH, inhibiting bacterial and mould growth — the principle behind vinegar-based pickling and preservation. Naturally produced by acetic acid bacteria (Acetobacter, Gluconobacter) fermenting ethanol, or by petrochemical synthesis (methanol carbonylation). Food grade and fermentation-derived acetic acid are chemically identical. Metabolically: acetic acid (as acetate) is a normal intermediate in human metabolism — a key product of carbohydrate, fat and amino acid catabolism — and is completely and harmlessly oxidised to CO₂ and water. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified (intake not limited). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS). Also has antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory and blood-glucose-modulating properties in research studies on higher doses.',
    'ins 270': 'INS 270 / E270 — Lactic Acid (2-hydroxypropanoic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH) is one of the most ancient and ubiquitous organic acids in nature — found in sour milk, yogurt, cheese, sourdough, sauerkraut, kimchi, wine and pickles. It is produced by lactic acid bacteria (LAB) fermenting lactose and other sugars, or synthetically from petrochemical precursors (though fermentation-derived is predominant in food applications). In the body, lactic acid is a normal metabolite of anaerobic glycolysis — produced in muscles during intense exercise and completely metabolised by the liver, heart and skeletal muscle via the Cori cycle. Food functions: acidulant (sharpening sourness without astringency), pH buffer, mild preservative (antimicrobial at pH <5), calcium-chelator in mineral fortification (calcium lactate), texture modifier in dairy and bakery. Unlike citric acid (which can have a harsh, astringent sourness), lactic acid imparts a clean, mellow, dairy-like acidity — preferred in fermented dairy, beverages and infant formula where clean flavour is important. The L(+) form (naturally produced by LAB fermentation) is generally preferred over the DL-racemic mix (synthetic) as L-lactate is preferentially metabolised. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS).',
    'ins 296': 'INS 296 / E296 — Malic Acid (2-hydroxybutanedioic acid, HOOCCH₂CH(OH)COOH) is a naturally occurring dicarboxylic acid — the primary organic acid in apples, giving them their characteristic sharp-tart flavour (from which the name derives: Latin "malum" = apple). Also found in grapes, cherries, tomatoes and other fruits. Biochemically, malic acid is a key intermediate in the Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) — the central metabolic pathway of cellular energy production in all aerobic organisms. Food applications: acidulant in confectionery, beverages, fruit preparations and baked goods; often preferred over citric acid because malic acid provides a smoother, more persistent, fruit-like sourness that builds gradually and lingers, rather than the rapid sharp sourness of citric acid; flavour enhancer — at sub-threshold concentrations, enhances perception of fruity and apple notes; pH regulation and buffering; sequestrant (chelating agent). The L(-)-malic acid form (naturally occurring) is identical to the body\'s own metabolic intermediate and presents no concerns; the D(+)-malic acid (synthetic racemic mixture contains both) is also cleared by the kidneys without issues. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 300': 'INS 300 / E300 — Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C, L-ascorbic acid, C₆H₈O₆) is a water-soluble essential vitamin and one of the most important antioxidants in human biology. Humans, unlike most other mammals, lack the enzyme L-gulonolactone oxidase and cannot synthesise ascorbic acid — making dietary intake essential. Physiological roles: (1) cofactor for collagen synthesis — required for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase enzymes that cross-link collagen fibres; deficiency causes scurvy; (2) antioxidant — quenches reactive oxygen species (ROS) and regenerates vitamin E from its oxidised form; (3) enhances non-haem iron absorption — reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ in the gut, dramatically increasing uptake; (4) immune function — supports leucocyte migration and phagocytic activity; (5) neurotransmitter synthesis — cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase (noradrenaline synthesis). Food industry uses: antioxidant preservative in packaged meats, beverages, baby foods, cereals — prevents oxidative rancidity and colour changes; dough improver in breadmaking (oxidises glutathione, strengthening gluten network); vitamin fortification (added to juices, infant formula, breakfast cereals). Both naturally occurring and synthetic L-ascorbic acid are chemically and biologically identical. No toxicological concerns at food use levels; ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS).',
    'ins 306': 'INS 306 / E306 — Tocopherol-rich Extract (Mixed Tocopherols) is a natural antioxidant extracted from vegetable oils (soybean, sunflower, rapeseed, palm, wheatgerm) by vacuum distillation. It is a mixture of the four natural vitamin E forms: alpha-tocopherol (highest vitamin E activity), beta-tocopherol, gamma-tocopherol and delta-tocopherol, in proportions varying by source oil. Mechanism: tocopherols are chain-breaking antioxidants — they donate a hydrogen atom to peroxyl radicals generated during lipid oxidation, interrupting the oxidative chain reaction and preventing rancidity. Delta-tocopherol and gamma-tocopherol have superior antioxidant function (better radical-trapping) while alpha-tocopherol has superior biological vitamin E activity. Used to preserve edible oils, fats, fried snacks, bakery products, meat products, infant formula and pet food. Vitamin E is also one of the key fat-soluble antioxidants in human biology — protecting cell membranes from oxidative damage, supporting immune function and preventing LDL oxidation relevant to cardiovascular disease. At food use concentrations, tocopherol extracts contribute to dietary vitamin E intake. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. GRAS (FDA); approved by FSSAI and EU. Unlike BHA and BHT (synthetic antioxidants, INS 320/321), tocopherols are widely accepted and preferred in natural and clean-label formulations.',
    'ins 320': 'INS 320 / E320 — BHA (Butylated Hydroxyanisole, 2-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol + 3-tert-butyl-4-methoxyphenol) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant added to fats, oils, fried snacks, bakery products, meat products and packaging materials to prevent oxidative rancidity. Mechanism: BHA donates a phenolic hydrogen to peroxyl radicals, interrupting the lipid oxidation chain reaction — similar mechanism to natural tocopherols but with greater stability at high temperatures. SAFETY CONCERN: BHA is classified by the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) as a Group 2B carcinogen (possibly carcinogenic to humans) based on studies in rodents showing dose-dependent forestomach papillomas and carcinomas at high dietary doses. Note that rodents have a forestomach (a distinct anatomical structure) that humans lack — making the direct relevance to human cancer debated among toxicologists. BHA also shows estrogenic activity in some in-vitro studies, raising endocrine disruption concerns. The US National Toxicology Program lists BHA as "reasonably anticipated to be a human carcinogen." At current permitted food use levels (typically ≤200 mg/kg in regulated markets), most regulatory bodies (including EFSA and FSSAI) consider the risk low — EFSA reconfirmed ADI 0.5 mg/kg bw/day in 2012. However, natural alternatives (tocopherols, rosemary extract) are widely available and preferred. A precautionary choice would be to avoid products heavily reliant on BHA.',
    'ins 321': 'INS 321 / E321 — BHT (Butylated Hydroxytoluene, 2,6-di-tert-butyl-4-methylphenol) is a synthetic phenolic antioxidant with the same primary food uses as BHA (INS 320): preserving fats, oils, fried snacks, bakery goods, breakfast cereals and edible fats from oxidative rancidity. Mechanism: chain-breaking radical scavenger — similar to BHA. SAFETY CONCERN: BHT has a complex and still-debated toxicological profile. Carcinogenicity findings in rodent studies are inconsistent and context-dependent: some studies show tumour promotion in rat liver and lung, while others show tumour-inhibition effects; the IARC does not formally classify BHT as a carcinogen. Thyroid effects: BHT has demonstrated thyroid hormone disruption (dose-dependent reduction in thyroxine levels) in rat studies. Coagulation effects: high-dose BHT impaired blood clotting in some rodent studies. Reproductive toxicity: mixed findings. At low food-use concentrations, current regulatory bodies (EFSA, FDA, FSSAI) consider BHT safe with ADI 0.25 mg/kg bw/day. However, the combination of: synthetic origin, debated toxicology, and ready availability of natural alternatives (tocopherols, rosemary extract, ascorbyl palmitate) means BHT has been voluntarily removed from many clean-label and premium food formulations. California\'s Proposition 65 lists BHT as a chemical known to the state to cause cancer or reproductive toxicity. Precautionary avoidance is reasonable.',
    'ins 330': 'INS 330 / E330 — Citric Acid (2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid, C₆H₈O₇) is the most widely used food acidulant in the world. It occurs naturally in citrus fruits (lemon juice is ~8% citric acid, lime juice ~7%) and in smaller amounts in all living cells as a central intermediate of the Krebs cycle (TCA cycle) — the fundamental metabolic pathway for cellular energy production in every aerobic organism. Commercially produced almost entirely by large-scale fermentation of molasses or glucose by Aspergillus niger fungus — identical in purity to naturally extracted citric acid. Food functions: (1) acidulant — imparts a clean, sharp, well-rounded tartness to beverages, confectionery, jams, dairy products, canned goods and baked products; (2) pH buffering — maintains consistent acidity that protects flavour, colour and product safety; (3) preservation — inhibits browning (chelates metal ions that catalyse oxidation), preserves colour and prevents microbial growth; (4) emulsification — chelates calcium in processed cheese and dairy spreads; (5) flavour enhancer — at sub-acidic concentrations enhances perception of fruit flavours; (6) raising agent component — in combination with bicarbonate (as in baking powder) releases CO₂ for leavening. Metabolically: citric acid is immediately incorporated into the Krebs cycle and fully oxidised to CO₂ and water. Exceptionally safe. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS). Dental note: frequent consumption of highly citric-acid-containing beverages (sodas, energy drinks, citrus juices) does contribute to dental enamel erosion — a concern about the delivery format, not the additive in isolation.',
    'ins 338': 'INS 338 / E338 -- Phosphoric Acid. Gives cola drinks their tang; erodes tooth enamel with heavy use.',
    'ins 400': 'INS 400 / E400 — Alginic Acid is a natural polysaccharide (anionic linear polymer) extracted from the cell walls of brown seaweeds (kelp — primarily Macrocystis pyrifera, Ascophyllum nodosum and Laminaria species). Chemically, it is a copolymer of D-mannuronic acid (M blocks) and L-guluronic acid (G blocks); the ratio and distribution of M and G blocks determines the physical properties of the resulting gel — high G-block alginates form firm, brittle gels while high M-block alginates form softer, more elastic gels. Alginic acid itself is poorly water-soluble (unlike its salts); it functions primarily as a gelling agent in acid conditions (forms gels when protonated by stomach acid, which is the basis of some alginate-based antacid/reflux treatments like Gaviscon). In food manufacturing, its sodium, potassium, ammonium and calcium salts (INS 401–404) are the commercially used forms. Dietary fibre properties: alginic acid is a soluble dietary fibre; resistant to human digestive enzymes; metabolised by colonic microbiota; associated with improved lipid profiles, slower glucose absorption and prebiotic effects. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 401': 'INS 401 / E401 — Sodium Alginate is the sodium salt of alginic acid — the most commercially important alginate, produced by reacting alginic acid extracted from brown kelp seaweed with sodium carbonate or sodium hydroxide. It is the primary food-grade alginate used as a thickener, gelling agent, stabiliser and emulsifier across a wide range of applications: ice cream (controls ice crystal formation), dairy (yogurt, cheese processing), sauces, dressings, bakery products, meat products and jelly confectionery. Gel-formation mechanism: sodium alginate solutions form gels when exposed to calcium ions (Ca²⁺) through ionic cross-linking of guluronic acid residues — this is the basis of the culinary "spherification" technique (alginate + calcium chloride bath). The resulting gels are heat-stable (unlike gelatin or agar gels, alginate gels do not melt when reheated), making them uniquely useful in baked goods and retorted products. Medical applications: sodium alginate is also used in wound dressings (calcium alginate absorbs exudate), dental impressions and the antacid-reflux treatment Gaviscon (forms a physical gel raft over stomach contents to prevent acid reflux). Excellent safety profile — classified as soluble dietary fibre; no significant absorption; no accumulation; no toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 406': 'INS 406 / E406 — Agar (Agar-agar, Kanten) is a natural polysaccharide extracted from the cell walls of red algae (Rhodophyta) — primarily Gelidium and Gracilaria species. It is composed of two fractions: agarose (the gelling fraction, a neutral polymer of alternating D-galactose and 3,6-anhydro-L-galactose units) and agaropectin (the non-gelling fraction, a sulphated heteropolymer). Agar is the strongest natural hydrocolloid gel-former: concentrations as low as 0.5–1% form firm, heat-stable gels that melt at 85–95°C but set at only 32–40°C — this wide hysteresis gap (large difference between melting and setting temperatures) is unique to agar and makes it invaluable in confectionery (jellies that withstand warm ambient temperatures), desserts, processed cheese, noodles, ice cream and canned meats. Cultural significance in India and Japan: agar is used in traditional Indian sweets (mishti doi stabilisation, agar-based kanten sweets), and is the defining ingredient of the Japanese dessert Anmitsu. Vegetarian/vegan gelatin substitute: agar sets firmer than gelatin and is heat-stable; texturally different from gelatin but accepted as the principal vegan gelatin alternative in food, pharmaceutical capsule and laboratory microbiology (culture plate) applications. Dietary fibre: agar is entirely non-digestible and acts as a bulk-forming dietary fibre; has demonstrated mild cholesterol-lowering and satiety-enhancing effects. No toxicological concerns; ADI not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 407': 'INS 407 / E407 -- Carrageenan. Seaweed stabiliser. Gut inflammation concerns; banned in EU infant formula.',
    'ins 410': 'INS 410 / E410 — Locust Bean Gum (Carob Gum, St John\'s Bread Gum) is a natural galactomannan polysaccharide extracted from the endosperm (seed kernel) of the carob tree (Ceratonia siliqua), native to the Mediterranean and Middle East. Chemically, it is a linear chain of mannose units with galactose side branches in a 4:1 mannose-to-galactose ratio — this relatively low galactose content means it does not form gels alone, but it synergistically enhances the gels of other hydrocolloids (particularly xanthan gum and carrageenan). Carob bean itself is the precursor to carob powder — a chocolate substitute widely used in health foods. LBG functions: (1) viscosity builder and stabiliser in sauces, soups and dressings; (2) ice cream — prevents large ice crystal formation and improves texture and melt resistance; (3) synergistic gelling with xanthan gum — together they form strong, elastic gels despite neither gelling alone; (4) cheese processing — controls moisture; (5) meat products — water retention and texture control. Dietary fibre properties: galactomannans are soluble dietary fibres with prebiotic effects on gut microbiota; demonstrated in clinical studies to reduce postprandial blood glucose and cholesterol levels at effective doses. Excellent safety profile; ADI: not specified; approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 412': 'INS 412 / E412 — Guar Gum (Guaran) is a natural galactomannan polysaccharide extracted from the endosperm of guar seeds (Cyamopsis tetragonoloba), an annual legume crop of significant agricultural importance in India — India and Pakistan together produce over 80% of the global guar supply, with Rajasthan being the primary producing state. Chemically, it is a linear mannose backbone with galactose side branches in a 2:1 mannose-to-galactose ratio — more densely branched than locust bean gum, which makes guar gum fully water-soluble and an excellent viscosity builder without requiring heat. Food functions: (1) efficient, cost-effective thickener — produces 5–8× more viscosity per gram than cornstarch; used in dairy products, sauces, soups, gravies, dressings, bakery products, processed meats and ready meals; (2) ice cream — stabilises by preventing ice crystal growth; (3) gluten-free baking — provides structure and moisture retention to replace gluten; (4) noodles and pasta — improves texture; (5) dietary supplement industry — used as a fibre supplement for satiety and blood glucose management. Nutritional significance: guar gum is a soluble dietary fibre. At doses of 10–20 g/day (much higher than food additive use), clinical studies show significant reductions in fasting blood glucose, postprandial glucose spikes, total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol. As a food additive, prebiotic effects on gut microbiota are likely even at lower doses. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 414': 'INS 414 / E414 — Gum Arabic (Acacia Gum, E414) is a natural complex polysaccharide with associated protein (arabinogalactan-protein complex) exuded from the stems and branches of Acacia senegal and Acacia seyal trees — hardy acacia species that grow in the Sahel region of sub-Saharan Africa (Sudan produces the majority of global supply). It is one of the oldest food additives known to humankind, used since ancient Egypt as a food binding agent and medicine. Chemically complex: it is a heterogeneous mixture of highly branched polysaccharides composed of arabinose, galactose, rhamnose and glucuronic acid residues, with associated protein moieties (~2–3%) — the protein component gives gum arabic unique emulsification and film-forming properties. Unique among food gums: (1) high water solubility at high concentrations (up to 50% solutions possible) at relatively low viscosity — enabling products with high gum levels without excessive thickening; (2) exceptional emulsifier — encapsulates and stabilises oil droplets and flavour compounds; used to microencapsulate flavours and aromas in spray-dried powders; (3) film-former — coats confectionery, pills and candies; (4) glazing agent; (5) stabiliser in beverages (citrus oils, Cola beverages). Dietary fibre properties: gum arabic is a highly fermentable soluble dietary fibre — consistently prebiotic in human trials; increases Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus; reduces total and LDL cholesterol; may have beneficial effects on satiety and blood glucose management. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 415': 'INS 415 / E415 — Xanthan Gum is a microbial exopolysaccharide produced by the bacterium Xanthomonas campestris (a Gram-negative plant pathogen) through aerobic fermentation of glucose or sucrose. It was developed by the USDA Northern Regional Research Laboratory in the 1950s-60s and patented in 1969. Chemically, it has a cellulose-like (beta-1,4-glucan) backbone with charged trisaccharide side chains on alternating glucose units — the side chains contain mannose, glucuronic acid and pyruvate residues. These anionic side chains create a molecular structure that is extraordinarily resistant to shear, temperature and pH changes. Rheological properties: xanthan gum is a pseudoplastic (shear-thinning) hydrocolloid — at rest it forms a thick gel, but under shear (stirring, pumping, chewing) it flows easily, then re-thickens immediately. This behaviour is ideal for: (1) sauces and dressings — thick at rest (good coating on food) but pourable when shaken; (2) gluten-free baking — mimics the viscoelastic network of gluten, trapping gas bubbles to give bread and cake rise and structure (the single most critical ingredient in gluten-free baking formulations); (3) ice cream — exceptional ice crystal control at very low concentrations; (4) ready meals — freeze-thaw stable; (5) personal care — stabilises emulsions, provides gel texture in creams. Synergy: forms strong elastic gels with locust bean gum at lower concentrations than either alone. Dietary fibre: non-digestible; mild prebiotic. Safety: no absorption through the gut; approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA; GRAS.',
    'ins 420': 'INS 420 / E420 — Sorbitol (D-glucitol, C₆H₁₄O₆) is a naturally occurring sugar alcohol found in many fruits including apples, pears, peaches, prunes and cherries (prunes are particularly high — ~6.1 g per 100 g — which is the well-known reason prunes have laxative effects). Commercially produced by catalytic hydrogenation of glucose (the carbonyl group of glucose is reduced to a hydroxyl group). Properties: (1) sweetness ~60% of sucrose; slowly absorbed in the small intestine (partially passive, partially active transport) leading to a lower glycaemic response than sucrose — peak blood glucose rise ~50% lower; (2) metabolised independently of insulin by the liver via sorbitol dehydrogenase (to fructose); historically used in diabetic foods (its insulin-independent metabolism was seen as advantageous) but the current view is that the fructose produced may have its own metabolic concerns; (3) humectant — strongly hygroscopic; retains moisture in baked goods, confectionery and cosmetics; (4) texture enhancer in confectionery — used to control crystallisation, provide smooth mouthfeel; (5) bulking agent and sweetener in sugar-free confectionery, chewing gum, diabetic foods, medicines and cosmetics; (6) at >50 g/day: osmotic laxative effect — unabsorbed sorbitol draws water into the colon, causing bloating, flatulence, cramping and loose stools. EU mandatory labelling above 10 g sorbitol per serving: "excessive consumption may produce laxative effects." Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 422': 'INS 422 / E422 — Glycerol (Glycerine, Propane-1,2,3-triol, C₃H₈O₃) is a simple trihydric alcohol — a colourless, odourless, viscous, sweet-tasting liquid that is produced as a by-product of soap manufacturing (saponification of fats and oils) and biodiesel production (transesterification), or by synthetic petrochemical routes. It is found naturally in all fats and oils as the backbone molecule of triglycerides. In human metabolism, glycerol is released from fat stores during fasting and exercise (lipolysis of triglycerides) and is a gluconeogenic substrate — the liver uses it to produce glucose during prolonged fasting. Food functions: (1) humectant — most widely used humectant in food and cosmetics; prevents desiccation, maintains softness and moisture; (2) solvent — dissolves flavourings, colours and extracts for use in food and beverages; (3) sweetener — ~60% as sweet as sucrose, 0 glycaemic index (not metabolised as carbohydrate in the conventional sense); (4) thickener — increases viscosity; (5) emulsifier in combination with fatty acids; (6) plasticiser in edible coatings and packaging. Used in baked goods, confectionery, dried fruits, ice cream, beverages, dairy products and cosmetics. Excellent safety profile — a normal metabolic intermediate with no known toxicity at food use levels. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 433': 'INS 433 / E433 -- Polysorbate 80. Emulsifier; trace 1,4-dioxane possible. Safe within limits.',
    'ins 440': 'INS 440 / E440 — Pectin is a complex structural polysaccharide naturally present in the primary cell walls and middle lamella of all higher plants. It is most concentrated in citrus peel (15–30% of dry weight), apple pomace and sugar beet pulp — the commercial sources from which pectin is extracted using hot dilute acid followed by precipitation with alcohol. Chemically, pectin is primarily composed of D-galacturonic acid units (alpha-1,4-linked) which may be partially methyl-esterified; the degree of esterification (DE) is the most important commercial parameter: (1) High-methoxyl (HM) pectin (DE >50%): gels in the presence of sugar (>55% soluble solids) and acid (pH 2.8–3.5); the classic jam and marmalade ingredient; (2) Low-methoxyl (LM) pectin (DE <50%): gels in the presence of calcium ions regardless of sugar concentration — used in low-sugar, no-sugar, dairy-based and high-protein products. Applications: gelling agent in jams, jellies, marmalades, confectionery (jelly beans, wine gums); stabiliser in yogurt, milkshakes and fruit drinks (prevents protein aggregation at low pH); thickener in sauces and dressings; fat replacer in reduced-fat spreads. Nutritional significance: pectin is a highly fermentable soluble dietary fibre — the most effective dietary fibre at lowering serum LDL cholesterol (by binding bile acids in the gut and preventing their reabsorption); slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption (reduces postprandial glucose spikes); strongly prebiotic. Excellent safety profile. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 450': 'INS 450 / E450 — Diphosphates (Pyrophosphates) are a group of salts derived from pyrophosphoric acid (H₄P₂O₇) — the condensation product of two phosphoric acid molecules. The common diphosphate salts in food are sodium acid pyrophosphate (SAPP, E450(i)), disodium diphosphate, tetrasodium diphosphate, tetrapotassium diphosphate and dicalcium diphosphate. They function as: (1) leavening acids — SAPP reacts with sodium bicarbonate in baking powder to release CO₂ (two-stage: fast at room temperature when wet, slower in the oven); (2) emulsifying salts in processed cheese — sequester calcium from casein micelles, reducing their size and enabling a smooth melt; (3) water-binding agents in cooked meats — improve texture, moisture retention and yield; (4) sequestrants — chelate metal ions (iron, copper) that would otherwise catalyse oxidation and discolouration; (5) acidulants. As phosphorus compounds: excessive phosphate intake (from combined sources: cola drinks, processed meats, baked goods, cheese products) is associated in epidemiological studies with reduced bone density and impaired kidney function, particularly in individuals with pre-existing chronic kidney disease. At food additive use levels in isolation, EFSA considers diphosphates safe (ADI 40 mg/kg bw/day as phosphorus, combined with all phosphate additives). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 471': 'INS 471 / E471 — Mono- and Diglycerides of Fatty Acids (MDG) are the most widely used emulsifiers in the global food industry — present in bread, margarine, bakery products, ice cream, chocolate, coffee whiteners, instant noodles and countless processed foods. They are produced by partial glycerolysis: reacting triglycerides (fats/oils) with glycerol in the presence of an alkaline catalyst at high temperature, which rearranges the fatty acid distribution so some glycerol molecules carry only one (monoglyceride) or two (diglyceride) fatty acid chains instead of three. They are technically incomplete fats — the same building blocks as natural food fats. Emulsification mechanism: MDGs are amphiphilic (hydrophilic glycerol head + lipophilic fatty acid tail), sitting at the oil-water interface and reducing interfacial tension. Applications: (1) bread — prevents staling by complexing with starch; extends shelf life by 2–3 days; improves loaf volume; (2) margarine — stabilises the water-in-oil emulsion; (3) ice cream — controls fat agglomeration, improves texture; (4) chocolate — reduces viscosity; (5) coffee whiteners — stabilises oil droplets. Fatty acid composition mirrors the source fat (palm, soy, sunflower, animal fat) — source often not specified on label. Palm-derived MDGs carry palm oil sustainability concerns. Trans fat note: partial hydrogenation creates trans MDGs — fully saturated or high-oleic sources avoid this. Safety: chemically, MDGs are essentially fat molecules; metabolised identically to triglycerides. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 476': 'INS 476 / E476 — PGPR (Polyglycerol Polyricinoleate) is a complex synthetic emulsifier produced by reacting polyglycerol (glycerol oligomers with 2–10 glycerol units) with condensed ricinoleic acid polyesters derived from castor oil (from the seeds of Ricinus communis). The molecule has a polyglycerol hydrophilic head and long polyricinoleate lipophilic tails — making it highly surface-active at the water-fat interface. Primary application: chocolate manufacturing — PGPR dramatically reduces the viscosity (plastic and Casson viscosity) of chocolate melt at concentrations of 0.1–0.3%. This allows manufacturers to use less cocoa butter (which is expensive) to achieve the same fluidity and moulding properties. PGPR is therefore a direct cost-reduction tool for chocolate manufacturers, and its increasing use since the 2000s has enabled cheaper products with less cocoa butter. Safety evaluation: the main concern arose from rat studies at very high doses (≥2.5% of diet, far exceeding any realistic human exposure) that showed enlarged livers. At food use concentrations (typically 2–5 g/kg chocolate), EFSA concluded in 2017 that PGPR does not raise safety concerns, with an ADI of 7.5 mg/kg bw/day. No genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity at realistic exposures. However, PGPR\'s primary function is as a cheap substitute for cocoa butter — its presence in chocolate often signals a product with reduced premium fat content. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 481': 'INS 481 / E481 — Sodium Stearoyl Lactylate (SSL) is a multifunctional food emulsifier produced by esterification of stearic acid (a saturated C18 fatty acid) with lactic acid, followed by sodium neutralisation. It was originally developed in the 1940s specifically for breadmaking. Primary function in baking: (1) dough strengthener — SSL forms strong complexes with gluten proteins, increasing the extensibility and gas retention of dough, resulting in better loaf volume, finer crumb structure and improved oven spring; (2) starch complexer — SSL complexes with amylose chains in starch, significantly slowing the retrogradation (re-crystallisation) of starch that causes bread to stale; bread containing SSL stays soft 30–50% longer than non-emulsified bread; (3) gluten-free baking — partially compensates for the absence of gluten network in gluten-free products. Secondary applications: stabiliser in whipped toppings and non-dairy creamers (improves foam stability and texture); cake batter stabiliser; tortilla and flatbread conditioner (prevents drying and cracking). Calcium stearoyl lactylate (CSL, E482) is the calcium salt — functionally similar but with slightly different applications. Safety: SSL is partially hydrolysed in the gut to stearic acid, lactic acid and glycerol — all normal food components metabolised without concerns. Extensive safety data; JECFA ADI 0–20 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. One of the most useful and well-characterised bread improver additives.',
    'ins 500': 'INS 500 / E500 -- Sodium Carbonates (Baking Soda). Most widely used raising agent. Safe.',
    'ins 508': 'INS 508 / E508 — Potassium Chloride (KCl) is an inorganic mineral salt — the potassium analogue of table salt (sodium chloride). It is the most widely used sodium reduction technology in the food industry, partially or fully replacing NaCl in low-sodium products. Potassium is an essential mineral (recommended daily intake: 3,500–4,700 mg/day for adults) crucial for: nerve impulse transmission (sodium-potassium ATPase pump), muscle contraction including cardiac function, blood pressure regulation, and fluid balance. The DASH diet and WHO both recommend increased potassium intake as a dietary strategy to lower blood pressure. Taste properties: at low concentrations, KCl closely mimics the saltiness of NaCl; at higher concentrations it can impart a bitter or metallic off-taste — typically addressed by using KCl in combination with NaCl (50:50 or 75:25 NaCl:KCl) or with salt taste enhancers. Applications: low-sodium bread, cured meats, cheese, snacks and ready meals; sports drinks (electrolyte replacement); pharmaceutical tablets (direct compression). Medical note: individuals with kidney disease, on ACE inhibitors or potassium-sparing diuretics should not consume high amounts of KCl without medical supervision — these conditions impair potassium excretion and excess supplementation may cause hyperkalaemia (dangerous elevated blood potassium). For healthy individuals, KCl in food is safe and often beneficial. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 509': 'INS 509 / E509 — Calcium Chloride (CaCl₂) is an inorganic salt of calcium and hydrochloric acid — highly water-soluble, releasing calcium ions (Ca²⁺) and chloride ions in solution. In the food industry it functions primarily as a firming agent: calcium ions cross-link pectin molecules in plant cell walls and form ionic bridges in protein gels, maintaining texture during processing. Applications: (1) pickles and canned vegetables — maintains crispness and firmness of cucumbers, tomatoes and other vegetables when processed at high temperatures; (2) cheese making — added to pasteurised milk to restore calcium lost during heat treatment, improving rennet coagulation; (3) tofu — used as a coagulant (alongside or as an alternative to magnesium chloride/nigari and calcium sulphate/gypsum) to set soy milk into tofu; (4) fresh-cut produce — post-harvest treatment maintains firmness of cut fruits and vegetables; (5) molecular gastronomy — spherification agent in combination with sodium alginate; (6) brewing — water treatment to adjust mineral profile; (7) electrolyte replacement in sports products. Nutritional: each gram of CaCl₂ provides ~360 mg calcium — a meaningful source of calcium in tofu. Safety: calcium chloride is a normal food mineral. At high oral doses, concentrated solutions are irritating; at food use levels, completely safe. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 511': 'INS 511 / E511 — Magnesium Chloride (Nigari, MgCl₂) is an inorganic magnesium salt. Nigari (from Japanese "nigai" = bitter) is the traditional Japanese name for the magnesium chloride-rich brine remaining after sodium chloride crystallises from seawater — it has been used for over 1,000 years as the traditional tofu coagulant in Japan. Commercially produced either by evaporation of seawater brines or from mineral deposits. Mechanism of tofu coagulation: magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) released in solution interact with negatively charged soy proteins (primarily beta-conglycinin and glycinin), cross-linking the proteins and causing them to aggregate into a gel curd — the basis of tofu. Different coagulants (nigari/MgCl₂, CaSO₄, CaCl₂, GDL) produce tofu with different textures: nigari produces firmer, more elastic tofu with a subtle sea-mineral flavour (prized in Japanese premium tofu); calcium sulphate produces softer, silkier tofu. Magnesium is an essential mineral: involved in over 300 enzymatic reactions; required for ATP production, DNA synthesis, protein synthesis, muscle and nerve function and blood glucose regulation; the majority of Indians are estimated to have below-recommended magnesium intake (400 mg/day for men, 310 mg/day for women). Tofu coagulated with nigari provides a meaningful dietary magnesium source. No toxicological concerns at food use levels. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 516': 'INS 516 / E516 — Calcium Sulphate (Food-grade Gypsum, CaSO₄·2H₂O in hydrated form, or anhydrous CaSO₄) is an inorganic calcium salt — one of the most ancient food additives used by humans; calcium sulphate is the "plaster of Paris" mineral used in traditional Chinese and Japanese tofu making for over 2,000 years. Functions: (1) tofu coagulant — calcium ions slowly released from poorly-soluble CaSO₄ gently and uniformly coagulate soy protein, producing the characteristic soft, silky-smooth, high-moisture tofu (silken and soft tofu specifically); the slow release prevents the abrupt rapid coagulation of nigari (MgCl₂), which can cause irregular texture; (2) dough conditioner / flour treatment agent — calcium sulphate strengthens the gluten network and improves yeast performance; used in breadmaking as a yeast food and dough conditioner; (3) firming agent in canned vegetables, similar to calcium chloride; (4) acidity regulator and pH buffer; (5) calcium fortification — adds calcium to bread, flour, tofu and other foods; food-grade CaSO₄ is ~30% calcium by weight. Nutritional: the calcium in calcium sulphate is well-absorbed (comparable to calcium from dairy). This is the primary reason traditional tofu is a significant dietary calcium source (~350 mg per 100 g in some preparations). No toxicological concerns; approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 551': 'INS 551 / E551 — Silicon Dioxide (Silica, SiO₂) is an inorganic compound — the most abundant mineral in the Earth\'s crust; the same material as sand, quartz and many gemstones, but produced to food-grade amorphous (non-crystalline) purity for food use. As a food additive, it is used primarily as an anti-caking agent: silicon dioxide particles have enormous surface area (up to 300 m² per gram in amorphous fumed or precipitated forms) that absorbs surface moisture and creates a physical barrier between the particles of a powdered ingredient, preventing clumping and maintaining free-flowing properties. Applications: powdered spices, salt, sugar, powdered milk, coffee powder, soup mixes, pharmaceutical tablets and capsules, baking powder. It is also used to clarify beer and wine (the silica adsorbs proteins that would otherwise cause haze). Safety: amorphous silicon dioxide is essentially biologically inert — it is not absorbed by the gut, passes through completely in faeces, and has no known systemic toxicity. Importantly, distinction must be made between amorphous silica (safe) and crystalline silica (quartz dust from occupational inhalation causes silicosis — an occupational lung disease). Food-grade amorphous SiO₂ does not carry this respiratory hazard. No toxicological concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 575': 'INS 575 / E575 — Glucono Delta-Lactone (GDL, D-Glucono-1,5-lactone, C₆H₁₀O₆) is a naturally occurring cyclic ester of gluconic acid, found in honey, grape juice, wine and corn steep liquor. Commercially produced by enzymatic or microbial oxidation of glucose. GDL is a unique food acidulant: unlike mineral acids or citric/lactic/acetic acids which work immediately, GDL hydrolyses slowly and progressively in water to release gluconic acid — the hydrolysis rate is temperature-dependent (faster when warm), allowing formulated products to remain neutral during mixing and then acidify in situ during heating or over time. This controlled slow acidification enables: (1) tofu production — GDL is used as the coagulant in silken (kinugoshi) tofu and convenient "instant" tofu kits; the slow acid release ensures uniform, smooth coagulation of soy protein throughout the product; (2) cold-cut and luncheon meats — GDL initiates protein gelation and colour fixation (works with nitrites in cured meat chemistry); (3) sourdough bread acidification — contributes a mild, clean acidity without sourness; (4) dairy — acidifies cream cheese and quark preparations; (5) fermented sausages — initiates pH drop for safety and texture. GDL produces a mild, clean, dairy-like acidity (gluconic acid taste is less sharp than citric or lactic acids). Metabolically, gluconic acid is fully metabolised through the pentose phosphate pathway — no concerns. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 621': 'INS 621 / E621 — Monosodium Glutamate (MSG, sodium glutamate, C₅H₈NNaO₄) is the sodium salt of glutamic acid — the most abundant naturally occurring amino acid in human biology and in many foods. Discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Professor Kikunae Ikeda while studying why kelp broth (kombu dashi) had such an intensely savoury, satisfying taste distinct from the four known tastes — he isolated glutamic acid as the responsible compound and named the sensation "umami" (the fifth basic taste). Commercially produced by fermentation of molasses or starch using Corynebacterium glutamicum — a natural process (not a synthetic chemical). Natural umami sources: tomatoes (~2,400 mg glutamate per 100 g dry weight), parmesan cheese (~12,000 mg/100 g), soy sauce (~7,400 mg/100 g), kombu seaweed (~3,400 mg/100 g), dried shiitake mushrooms, anchovies, miso. MSG provides exactly the same glutamate molecule as these natural sources. "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" (CRS): historically, MSG was blamed for symptoms (headache, flushing, numbness) after Chinese restaurant meals — coining the term. Controlled double-blind clinical studies since the 1980s have consistently failed to reproduce any adverse effects when subjects were given MSG at realistic food concentrations without their knowledge. The scientific consensus is that MSG is not a cause of CRS at normal dietary concentrations. JECFA ADI: not specified (MSG is not limited, as glutamate is a normal metabolite). Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS). Note: MSG adds sodium — in very high-sodium diets, overall sodium reduction, not MSG avoidance, is the relevant concern.',
    'ins 627': 'INS 627 / E627 — Disodium Guanylate (Disodium 5\'-Guanylate, GMP) is a nucleotide flavour enhancer — a disodium salt of guanosine monophosphate (GMP), one of the four nucleotides that form RNA. It is commercially produced by enzymatic hydrolysis of yeast RNA or from the fermentation of microbial cultures. Mechanism of flavour enhancement: GMP activates the umami taste receptors (specifically the heterodimeric receptor T1R1/T1R3, particularly the T1R1 subunit) on the tongue with extraordinary potency. Critically, GMP acts synergistically with MSG (monosodium glutamate, INS 621) — a mixture of GMP and MSG produces umami intensity far exceeding the additive sum of each alone; this synergy enables manufacturers to achieve the desired umami taste with very low concentrations of each enhancer. GMP is typically found in combination with IMP (disodium inosinate, INS 631) as "I+G" (INS 635), which is the most common commercial nucleotide enhancer blend. Found naturally in: dried mushrooms (especially shiitake, reishi), dried fish and seafood, Parmesan cheese, and fermented products. CAUTION: purines (guanosine, inosine) are metabolised in humans to uric acid via xanthine oxidase. Excess uric acid (hyperuricaemia) causes gout — painful crystalline deposits in joints. Individuals with gout, hyperuricaemia or a family history of gout should limit consumption of nucleotide enhancers and purine-rich foods. For healthy individuals, typical food additive exposure is not a concern. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 631': 'INS 631 / E631 — Disodium Inosinate (Disodium 5\'-Inosinate, IMP) is a nucleotide flavour enhancer — the disodium salt of inosine monophosphate (IMP), a purine nucleotide naturally found in all living cells as an intermediate in purine biosynthesis. Produced commercially by enzymatic degradation of yeast RNA, or by microbial fermentation. Mechanism: like GMP (INS 627), IMP activates the T1R1/T1R3 umami receptor and shows profound synergy with glutamates — the presence of IMP reduces the MSG threshold for umami perception by 5–30×, enabling significant MSG reduction when IMP is co-used. IMP is found at high concentrations naturally in: sardines, mackerel, tuna, pork, chicken, beef, fish sauce and dried bonito (dashi stock) — IMP is one of the primary reasons these foods taste so intensely savoury. In instant noodles, chips, snacks, soups and processed foods, IMP is typically combined with GMP as "I+G" (INS 635, disodium ribonucleotides). Derivation note: IMP in food products is frequently animal-derived (from fish or pork/chicken nucleotides) — relevant for vegetarians, vegans and those with religious dietary restrictions. Yeast-derived IMP is available but manufacturers may not specify the source. CAUTION: same as GMP — purines are metabolised to uric acid; individuals with gout, hyperuricaemia or kidney stones should be cautious. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 635': 'INS 635 / E635 — Disodium 5\'-Ribonucleotides (I+G) is a commercial blend of disodium inosinate (IMP, INS 631) and disodium guanylate (GMP, INS 627), typically in a 1:1 ratio. It is the most widely used nucleotide flavour enhancer formulation in the processed food industry because: (1) the combination achieves synergy — IMP and GMP together are substantially more potent umami enhancers than either alone; (2) practical — using a single I+G blend is simpler than formulating with two separate nucleotides. The synergy with glutamates (MSG or naturally occurring glutamates) is even more marked: at typical food additive concentrations, I+G reduces the amount of MSG needed to achieve the same taste intensity by 5–10×, allowing manufacturers to label products with lower MSG levels while achieving equivalent or greater flavour impact. Found in: instant noodles, chips, crisps, soups, bouillon cubes, gravies, sauces, seasoning mixes, flavoured nuts. Animal derivation concern: I+G is frequently produced from fish or pork/chicken sources — strictly vegetarian and halal-certified alternatives (yeast-derived) exist but the label rarely specifies. CAUTION: gout and hyperuricaemia — same as INS 627 and INS 631 individually. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 900': 'INS 900 / E900 — Dimethyl Polysiloxane (PDMS, Polydimethylsiloxane, Dimethicone) is a silicon-based synthetic polymer — a member of the silicone family of materials. Chemically, it is a repeating chain of -Si(CH₃)₂-O- units, with the chain length (n, number of repeating units) determining viscosity. It is a colourless, odourless, chemically inert liquid or semi-solid with exceptional surface-active properties. Food function: anti-foaming agent — PDMS works by dramatically reducing surface tension at air-water interfaces, causing foam bubbles to coalesce and collapse. Used at very low concentrations (2–10 mg/kg) in: frying oils (prevents overflow of oil during high-temperature frying — a safety and process efficiency function; used in McDonald\'s fryers), pineapple juice, beverages, confectionery. Also: release agent in baking (prevents sticking). Pharmaceutical use: simethicone (medical anti-gas agent for bloating) is a mixture of PDMS with SiO₂. Non-food uses: as "dimethicone" it is the primary silicone conditioning agent in hair conditioners and skin moisturisers. Safety: PDMS is biologically inert — essentially no absorption through the gut or skin; passes through the digestive system unchanged; no metabolites; no accumulation; no known toxicity at food use levels. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA (GRAS).',
    'ins 903': 'INS 903 / E903 — Carnauba Wax (Brazil Wax, Palm Wax) is a natural wax obtained by harvesting, drying and beating the leaves of the carnauba palm tree (Copernicia prunifera / Copernicia cerifera), which grows exclusively in the semi-arid northeastern states of Brazil (Piauí, Ceará, Maranhão, Rio Grande do Norte). The wax is scraped from the dried leaf surface. It is the hardest, highest-melting natural wax known (~80–86°C melting point), harder than beeswax, with an outstanding natural gloss and resistance to moisture. Composition: predominantly esters of fatty acids (C24–C32) and fatty alcohols (C32–C34) — highly chemically complex. Food applications: (1) glazing agent — coating of fruits (apples, citrus, cucumbers) to replace natural waxes removed during washing, prevent moisture loss and improve appearance; (2) confectionery coating — chocolate-covered candies, hard-shelled sweets, chewing gum pellets; (3) pharmaceutical tablet coating — provides gloss, moisture barrier and smooth swallowing; (4) cheese rind coating. Carnauba wax is vegan (plant-derived), unlike shellac or beeswax. It is one of the most desirable natural waxes in both food and cosmetics. Safety: essentially not absorbed; passes through the digestive system unchanged; no known toxicity. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA. The carnauba palm leaf harvest is a significant economic activity for rural communities in Brazil\'s northeast.',
    'ins 951': 'INS 951 / E951 — Aspartame (L-aspartyl-L-phenylalanine methyl ester, C₁₄H₁₈N₂O₅) is a dipeptide methyl ester artificial sweetener approximately 180–200× sweeter than sucrose. It was accidentally discovered by James Schlatter (G.D. Searle & Company) in 1965. Hydrolysis in the gut yields aspartic acid, phenylalanine and methanol — all present in normal food (a glass of tomato juice contains more methanol than an aspartame-sweetened drink). CRITICAL NOTE — IARC Group 2B (2023): In July 2023, IARC classified aspartame as a Group 2B carcinogen ("possibly carcinogenic to humans"), based on limited evidence from three observational studies (including the EPIC cohort) suggesting association with hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer) at habitual high consumption. JECFA simultaneously reviewed the same data and maintained the ADI at 40 mg/kg body weight/day, concluding the studies were not sufficient to change safety conclusions. The two organisations reached different conclusions because IARC identifies hazard (CAN this cause harm?) while JECFA assesses risk (DOES it cause harm at typical exposures?). PHENYLKETONURIA (PKU) MANDATORY WARNING: products containing aspartame must legally carry the warning "Contains a source of phenylalanine" in India, EU and USA. PKU is a rare genetic disorder (phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency) causing toxic phenylalanine accumulation — patients must strictly limit aspartame. For general consumers at typical use levels, JECFA considers risk low; the IARC Group 2B classification indicates some scientific concern worth being aware of. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 954': 'INS 954 / E954 — Saccharin (benzoic sulphimide, C₇H₅NO₃S) is the oldest artificial sweetener — accidentally discovered by Ira Remsen and Constantine Fahlberg at Johns Hopkins University in 1878, and used commercially since the early 1900s. It is approximately 300–500× sweeter than sucrose and has no caloric value as it is not metabolised. Has a characteristic bitter or metallic aftertaste at higher concentrations. Safety history: the 1970s were saccharin\'s most controversial period — a Canadian study showed that saccharin caused bladder tumours in male rats, leading the US FDA to attempt a ban (blocked by Congress). The mechanistic reason was subsequently identified: the bladder tumours in male rats result from a species-specific precipitation of saccharin with alpha-2u-globulin protein (a protein unique to male rats) in urine, causing chronic bladder irritation and subsequent tumour formation. This mechanism does not apply to humans (who lack alpha-2u-globulin in urine). In 2000, saccharin was removed from the US National Toxicology Program\'s list of anticipated carcinogens. However, it remains a CQ (commonly questioned) ingredient because: (1) the historical concern has not been fully dispelled in public perception; (2) some newer observational data suggest possible associations with Type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome (though causality is debated); (3) microbiome studies show saccharin may alter gut bacteria in ways that impair glucose tolerance. JECFA ADI: 0–5 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA but rarely used in modern formulations due to taste quality and perception concerns.',
    'ins 955': 'INS 955 / E955 — Sucralose (4,1\',6\'-trichlorogalactosucrose, C₁₂H₁₉Cl₃O₈) is a chlorinated disaccharide sweetener approximately 400–700× sweeter than sucrose, discovered at Queen Elizabeth College London in 1976. It is produced by selectively replacing three hydroxyl groups of sucrose with chlorine atoms — a chemical modification that makes it non-caloric (not recognised by metabolic enzymes) while dramatically increasing sweetness potency. Properties: (1) heat-stable — unlike aspartame, sucralose withstands high baking and cooking temperatures; (2) pH-stable across a wide range; (3) excellent water solubility; (4) clean sweet taste with minimal bitterness or aftertaste; (5) bulked with maltodextrin or dextrose in granulated forms (Splenda). Historical safety view: sucralose was considered extremely safe — absorbed minimally (<15%), mostly excreted unchanged; no caloric contribution; approved by FDA in 1998 and marketed as "made from sugar, so it tastes like sugar." Recent concerns (2022–2023): (1) A 2023 study published in the Journal of Toxicology and Environmental Health found sucralose-6-acetate — a minor metabolite produced during sucralose digestion and also a trace impurity in commercial sucralose — was genotoxic in in-vitro assays and caused intestinal barrier damage. The study prompted increased regulatory scrutiny; (2) Microbiome effects — multiple rodent and some human studies suggest sucralose may reduce Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium populations and impair gut barrier integrity at habitual-use concentrations. IARC has not classified sucralose; JECFA ADI: 0–15 mg/kg bw/day. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA — but the emerging data on genotoxicity and microbiome effects merits monitoring.',
    'ins 960': 'INS 960 / E960 — Steviol Glycosides (Stevia) are a class of diterpene glycosides extracted from the leaves of Stevia rebaudiana Bertoni, a perennial herb native to the Amambay region of Paraguay, where indigenous Guaraní people used the leaves as a sweetener and medicine for centuries. Over 40 steviol glycosides have been identified; the most commercially important are stevioside (~5–10% of dry leaf weight), rebaudioside A (Reb A, ~2–4%) and rebaudioside M (Reb M). Sweetness: 200–350× that of sucrose (rebaudioside A) to over 300× (rebaudioside M). Mechanism: steviol glycosides bind to the same bitter and sweet taste receptors — the glycoside portion primarily interacts with sweet receptors while the steviol aglycone backbone can weakly activate bitter receptors (T2R4, T2R14), accounting for the characteristic liquorice-like or minty-bitter aftertaste of less-purified steviol glycoside preparations. Higher-purity Reb A and Reb M preparations have significantly reduced aftertaste. Metabolism: the glycoside portion is removed by gut bacteria; the resulting steviol aglycone is absorbed, glucuronidated in the liver, and excreted in urine — zero caloric contribution. Blood glucose and insulin: multiple clinical studies confirm no effect on blood glucose or insulin levels; accepted as safe for diabetics. Blood pressure: evidence from some studies suggests mild antihypertensive effects. Safety: JECFA ADI 0–4 mg/kg bw/day (as steviol equivalents); approved by FSSAI, EU (since 2011), FDA (GRAS). One of the most thoroughly safety-tested natural sweeteners.',
    'ins 967': 'INS 967 / E967 — Xylitol (C₅H₁₂O₅) is a five-carbon sugar alcohol (pentitol) naturally found in many fruits and vegetables (plums, strawberries, raspberries, cauliflower, birch tree bark) and produced endogenously in the human body as a normal intermediate of glucose metabolism in the pentose phosphate pathway. Commercially produced by hydrogenation of xylose from hemicellulose hydrolysate (from birch wood, corn cobs or other lignocellulosic sources). Sweetness approximately equal to sucrose; caloric value ~2.4 kcal/g (40% less than sucrose). Dental health: xylitol\'s most significant evidence-based benefit — it is not fermented by Streptococcus mutans and other cariogenic oral bacteria (which lack xylulose kinase to metabolise it); it actually actively inhibits S. mutans growth and adhesion; causes a "xylitol futile cycle" in oral bacteria that depletes their ATP; significantly reduces dental caries incidence. Multiple Cochrane-level meta-analyses support xylitol\'s caries-preventive effects, particularly in chewing gum (2–3 pieces after meals). Glycaemic: low glycaemic index (~7–13 vs 65 for glucose); metabolism is insulin-independent. Laxative effect: at >15–20 g/day, osmotic laxative effect occurs (less severe than sorbitol). CRITICAL SAFETY WARNING FOR DOGS: xylitol is acutely toxic to dogs — causes rapid, severe insulin release leading to hypoglycaemia (blood sugar crash), and liver failure even at doses as low as 0.1 g/kg body weight. Keep xylitol-containing products completely away from dogs. In humans, xylitol at food use levels is safe. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 968': 'INS 968 / E968 — Erythritol (meso-erythritol, C₄H₁₀O₄) is a four-carbon sugar alcohol (tetritol) naturally found in small amounts in fruits (grapes, pears, melons), fermented foods (soy sauce, wine, beer, miso, cheese) and produced endogenously in small amounts by the human pentose phosphate pathway. Commercially produced by large-scale fungal fermentation (using Torula yeast or Aureobasidium pullulans) of glucose or sucrose — this makes commercial erythritol a biotechnology product from natural fermentation. Properties: (1) sweetness ~60–80% of sucrose; (2) caloric value ≈0.2 kcal/g (essentially zero calories — FDA approved zero-calorie labelling); (3) non-glycaemic — not metabolised by humans; does not raise blood glucose or insulin; (4) excellent absorption: unlike other sugar alcohols, approximately 90% of ingested erythritol is absorbed in the small intestine unchanged and rapidly excreted unchanged in the urine — this absorption prevents the colonic fermentation that causes gas, bloating and laxative effects with other polyols; minimal laxative effect even at high doses; (5) dental: not fermented by oral bacteria, so caries-neutral. Used in confectionery, beverages, bakery goods, protein bars and low-carbohydrate / ketogenic diet products. Safety: long considered one of the best-tolerated sugar substitutes. EMERGING CONCERN (2023): A major study published in Nature Medicine (Hazen et al., Cleveland Clinic) found that blood erythritol levels were independently associated with increased risk of major cardiovascular events (heart attack, stroke) in a large prospective human cohort, and animal studies suggested erythritol enhanced platelet aggregation (blood clotting tendency). The dose context: blood erythritol rose dramatically after consumption of a single erythritol-sweetened beverage and remained elevated for hours. The scientific community considers these findings preliminary but important and worthy of further investigation. ADI: not specified. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA.',
    'ins 1442': 'INS 1442 / E1442 -- Hydroxypropyl Distarch Phosphate. Modified starch in ketchup and baby food. Safe.',

    # ══ Soap Surfactants (Saponified Salts) ═══════════════════════════════════
    'sodium palmate': 'Sodium Palmate is the sodium salt of the fatty acids of palm oil, produced by saponification of palm oil with sodium hydroxide (lye). It is the primary cleansing and lathering ingredient in most commercial bar soaps. Chemically, it is a mixture of sodium palmitate (C16) and sodium stearate (C18) — the two dominant fatty acids in palm oil. While highly effective at removing oils, dirt and bacteria, its alkaline pH (~9–10) disrupts the skin\'s natural acid mantle (pH 4.5–5.5), which can cause transient barrier dysfunction, dryness and irritation particularly in individuals with eczema, sensitive skin or psoriasis. Environmental concern: palm oil is one of the most controversial agricultural products globally — large-scale palm cultivation is responsible for extensive tropical deforestation, peatland destruction, significant greenhouse gas emissions, and habitat loss for orangutans, pygmy elephants and Sumatran tigers. RSPO (Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil) certification is the primary standard for responsible sourcing, though compliance is inconsistent. For consumers with dry or sensitive skin, syndet (synthetic detergent) bars containing sodium cocoyl isethionate are a significantly milder alternative.',
    'sodium palm kernelate': 'Sodium Palm Kernelate is the sodium salt of palm kernel oil fatty acids, produced by saponification of palm kernel oil — the oil extracted from the seed (kernel) of the oil palm fruit, distinctly different from palm (fruit pulp) oil. Palm kernel oil has a significantly different fatty acid profile from palm oil: it is rich in lauric acid (C12, ~45–50%) and myristic acid (C14, ~15%), making its composition closer to coconut oil. Consequently, sodium palm kernelate produces a harder bar soap with denser, more stable, longer-lasting lather compared to sodium palmate — the lauric acid component is responsible for excellent foam generation. It is somewhat less drying than sodium palmate but still alkaline (pH ~9) and may cause barrier disruption in sensitive individuals. It carries the same environmental concerns as all palm-derived ingredients (deforestation, habitat loss, biodiversity impact). RSPO certification should be verified for palm kernel oil sourcing.',

    # ══ Natural Butters and Plant Oils ════════════════════════════════════════
    'kokum butter': 'Kokum Butter is a hard, white vegetable fat extracted from the seeds of Garcinia indica (the kokum tree), a plant native to the Western Ghats of India — particularly coastal Karnataka, Goa and Maharashtra. It is obtained by cold-pressing or solvent extraction of the dried kokum seeds. Fatty acid composition: exceptionally high in stearic acid (~55–60%) and oleic acid (~35–40%), with minimal polyunsaturated fats — making it one of the most oxidatively stable natural butters. Key properties: (1) Non-comedogenic — rated 0 on the comedogenic scale, uniquely suitable for oily and acne-prone skin unlike shea or cocoa butter; (2) deeply emollient without greasiness — melts at skin temperature and absorbs quickly without occlusive residue; (3) healing and regenerative — used traditionally in Ayurveda for cracked heels, chapped lips, dry scalp, sunburn and wound healing; (4) anti-inflammatory — Garcinia indica extracts have demonstrated anti-inflammatory activity in multiple in-vitro studies, attributed to garcinol and hydroxycitric acid; (5) antioxidant — garcinol, a polyisoprenylated benzophenone, shows significant free-radical scavenging activity; (6) promotes skin elasticity — stearic acid content supports skin lipid barrier integrity. In hair care, used to nourish dry, brittle hair and seal the cuticle. One of the most traditional and well-tolerated Indian cosmetic ingredients. Safe; no known allergenicity. An excellent choice for dry, very dry, or eczema-prone skin.',
    'oenocarpus bataua fruit oil': 'Oenocarpus Bataua Fruit Oil (patauá oil / patuá oil) is extracted from the fruits of the patauá palm (Oenocarpus bataua, syn. Jessenia bataua), a tall palm tree indigenous to the Amazon rainforest and Orinoco basin of South America, harvested by indigenous Amazonian communities using traditional extraction methods. Fatty acid composition: exceptionally high in oleic acid (C18:1, ~72–80% — rivalling and often exceeding argan and olive oils), with palmitic acid (~11%) and small amounts of linoleic acid (~3–5%). This makes patauá oil one of the richest natural sources of oleic acid available in cosmetics. Properties: (1) exceptional hair conditioning — oleic acid oils have a well-established affinity for the hair cortex; research has shown that C18:1-rich oils penetrate the hair fibre, reduce hygral fatigue (the swelling and shrinkage damage from repeated wetting) and restore the hair\'s natural lipid content after chemical or heat treatment; (2) emollient — quickly absorbed, non-greasy skin feel; (3) anti-inflammatory potential — patauá oil extracts have shown anti-inflammatory properties in studies by Brazilian and French research groups; (4) antioxidant — naturally rich in tocopherols (Vitamin E) and phytosterols; (5) sustainability credentials — patauá oil is harvested from wild-growing Amazonian palms, incentivising forest preservation over slash-and-burn agriculture; featured as a key ingredient in sustainable Amazonian bioeconomy initiatives. Safe; well-tolerated; no identified allergenicity at cosmetic use levels.',
    'hydrolyzed plukenetia volubilis seed extract': 'Hydrolyzed Plukenetia Volubilis Seed Extract is produced from the seeds of Plukenetia volubilis — the sacha inchi plant (Inca peanut, mountain peanut), a vine indigenous to the Peruvian and Brazilian Amazon, now cultivated in tropical regions for its exceptionally nutritious seeds. Sacha inchi seeds are among the richest known plant sources of polyunsaturated fatty acids: alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3, ~45–53%), linoleic acid (omega-6, ~33–38%), and oleic acid (omega-9, ~8%), with 25–30% of the seed being high-quality complete protein containing all essential amino acids. Hydrolysis (by enzymatic or mild acid treatment) breaks the seed protein into short peptides and free amino acids, and the oil fraction into smaller components — making the extract water-compatible and able to penetrate the hair fibre and skin surface. Properties in hair care: (1) conditioning and repair — peptides from sacha inchi protein temporarily fill structural gaps in the hair cuticle, reducing breakage and improving tensile strength; (2) hydration — amino acids act as natural moisturising factors (NMFs), attracting and retaining water in the hair cortex; (3) antioxidant protection — the polyphenol and tocopherol fraction protects hair from UV and oxidative damage; (4) scalp nourishment — the omega-3 component may reduce scalp inflammation and support healthy hair follicles. The extract\'s compatibility with rinse-off formulations (unlike the oil itself) makes it practical in shampoos and conditioners. Safe; no significant allergenicity identified in available literature. Well-tolerated; environmentally sustainable source.',

    # ══ Mild Surfactants / Cleansing Agents ══════════════════════════════════
    'sodium cocoyl isethionate': 'Sodium Cocoyl Isethionate (SCI) is a mild anionic surfactant produced by reacting coconut-derived fatty acids (primarily lauric acid, C12) with isethionic acid (2-hydroxyethanesulfonic acid), followed by sodium hydroxide neutralisation. It is the defining ingredient of "syndet bars" (synthetic detergent bars) — bar soap alternatives specifically formulated for sensitive, dry and eczema-prone skin. Key properties: (1) skin pH-compatible — SCI produces a lather with a pH of approximately 5.5–6.5, substantially closer to the skin\'s natural acid mantle (pH 4.5–5.5) than traditional soap (pH 9–10); this is clinically significant because maintaining acid mantle pH preserves barrier enzyme function and microbiome health; (2) exceptionally mild — multiple clinical studies comparing surfactant mildness consistently rank SCI among the lowest skin irritation scores, significantly below SLS, SLES and even cocamidopropyl betaine; (3) non-stripping — maintains stratum corneum lipid integrity better than conventional surfactants; (4) conditioning — leaves a slight conditioning feel; (5) rich, creamy, stable foam; (6) biodegradable; (7) compatible with ECOCERT/COSMOS natural cosmetics certification. Approved by FDA, EU Cosmetics Regulation, FSSAI and all major regulatory bodies. No significant allergenicity identified. The preferred surfactant for dermatologically-tested sensitive skin formulations endorsed by dermatology associations.',
    'sodium lauroyl sarcosinate': 'Sodium Lauroyl Sarcosinate (SLS — not to be confused with Sodium Lauryl Sulfate, also abbreviated SLS) is a mild anionic surfactant derived from sarcosine — a naturally occurring amino acid (N-methylglycine found in human muscle tissue, cranberry juice and starfish) — and coconut-derived lauric acid (C12 fatty acid). It produces excellent, creamy foam with good conditioning properties and is significantly milder on skin and mucous membranes than sodium lauryl sulfate. Used in facial cleansers, shampoos, body washes and toothpaste. Notable property in oral care: sodium lauroyl sarcosinate has demonstrated inhibition of Streptococcus mutans and other cariogenic bacteria in multiple in-vitro and clinical studies, contributing to anti-cavity and anti-plaque effects in toothpaste beyond mechanical cleaning. SCCS (EU Scientific Committee on Consumer Safety) review assessed sodium lauroyl sarcosinate as safe in cosmetics at typical use concentrations. The primary safety note in the scientific literature is the theoretical potential for N-nitrosoarcosine formation — a nitrosamine — if the ingredient contacts strong nitrosating agents (such as certain nitrogen-containing preservatives like sodium nitrite). Well-formulated products specifically avoid such combinations. No significant allergenicity; approved by FDA, EU Cosmetics Regulation, FSSAI.',
    'capryloyl/caproyl methyl glucamide': 'Capryloyl/Caproyl Methyl Glucamide is a mild, biodegradable amphoteric-like surfactant from the glucamide family, produced by the condensation reaction of N-methyl glucamine (from glucose) with a blend of caprylic acid (C8) and caproic acid (C6) — both fatty acids typically derived from coconut oil. It belongs to a category of "green chemistry" surfactants designed to be both highly effective and environmentally responsible. Properties: (1) mild and gentle — very low irritation potential; suitable for baby products, sensitive skin and atopic dermatitis formulations; (2) creamy, stable foam compatible with other mild surfactants; (3) rapidly biodegradable — the glucamide backbone degrades efficiently in wastewater treatment; (4) derived from renewable resources — glucose and coconut oil; (5) broad pH stability; (6) good compatibility with both anionic and amphoteric surfactants. Often used as the primary or co-surfactant in sulphate-free cleansing formulations. No significant allergenicity or regulatory concerns identified. Approved for cosmetic use globally.',
    'lauroyl/myristoyl methyl glucamide': 'Lauroyl/Myristoyl Methyl Glucamide is a mild surfactant from the glucamide family, made from N-methyl glucamine and a blend of lauric acid (C12) and myristic acid (C14) fatty acids from coconut and palm kernel oil. The longer carbon chain lengths (C12–C14) compared to capryloyl/caproyl methyl glucamide provide enhanced cleansing performance (better oil removal) alongside good conditioning properties, while maintaining the excellent skin tolerability characteristic of the glucamide family. Properties: (1) mild with excellent skin compatibility; (2) denser, more abundant foam than the C8/C6 variant — suitable for both facial and body cleansing; (3) conditioning after-feel — skin feels smooth and non-stripped after rinsing; (4) biodegradable; (5) compatible with natural cosmetic certification standards (ECOCERT-approved surfactant). Commonly paired with its C8/C6 counterpart (Capryloyl/Caproyl Methyl Glucamide) and Coco-Glucoside in sulphate-free "natural" formulations. Safe; no known allergenicity or regulatory concerns.',
    'coco-glucoside': 'Coco-Glucoside is a mild, non-ionic surfactant produced by condensation reaction of glucose (from corn starch or other starch sources) with coconut-derived fatty alcohols (primarily lauryl alcohol C12 and myristyl alcohol C14). It belongs to the alkyl glucoside (APG) surfactant family — a group recognised for being derived entirely from renewable plant-based raw materials and for having excellent biodegradability and skin tolerability. Properties: (1) exceptionally gentle — multiple clinical studies consistently show very low primary skin irritation scores; eye-safe; used in ophthalmologically and dermatologically tested baby products; (2) biodegradable — readily and rapidly biodegrades; one of the most environmentally favourable surfactant classes; (3) non-ionic — compatible with all other surfactant types (anionic, cationic, amphoteric); (4) skin-compatible pH ~11–12 as supplied, but in formulations buffered to normal cosmetic pH, excellent tolerability; (5) ECOCERT/COSMOS approved — frequently featured in certified natural cosmetics; (6) no ethoxylation — unlike SLES or PEG ingredients, alkyl glucosides do not carry 1,4-dioxane contamination risk; (7) good foam quality. Widely used in baby shampoos, sensitive skin cleansers, natural formulations and micellar waters. Approved globally. Safe.',
    'glycol distearate': 'Glycol Distearate (Ethylene Glycol Distearate / EGDS) is an ester produced by reacting ethylene glycol with two molecules of stearic acid (a saturated C18 fatty acid typically derived from vegetable or animal fat). It is the ingredient responsible for the distinctive pearl shimmer or "nacreous" appearance in shampoos, conditioners and body washes — the white, opalescent, light-reflecting quality of many liquid personal care products. Mechanism of pearlising action: glycol distearate forms microscopic crystalline platelets within the aqueous product matrix, which scatter incident light and produce the characteristic mother-of-pearl visual effect. Properties: (1) pearlising and opacifying — cosmetic aesthetic role; (2) mild emollient and skin-conditioning effect at the hair and skin surface; (3) mild thickener at higher concentrations. Safety: well-tolerated; patch test studies show low sensitisation potential; non-irritating to eyes at typical concentrations in rinse-off products; approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation, FDA and FSSAI. The ethylene glycol moiety in the molecule is part of the ester bond and is not the same as free ethylene glycol (which is toxic); the ester form is considered safe at cosmetic use levels. Safe.',
    'peg-150 distearate': 'PEG-150 Distearate is a high-molecular-weight polyethylene glycol (PEG) ester, produced by reacting polyethylene glycol with an average chain length of 150 ethylene oxide units (hence "PEG-150") with stearic acid. Used as a primary viscosity-building (thickening) agent in shampoos, conditioners and body washes — it is what gives these liquid products their characteristic thick, gel-like consistency without leaving a heavy or greasy feel. Properties: (1) efficient thickener at low concentrations; (2) the very high molecular weight (PEG-150 ≈ average MW ~6,600 Da) means essentially zero dermal penetration; (3) compatible with most surfactants and cosmetic ingredients. Safety concern: PEG-150 Distearate is manufactured via ethoxylation — a reaction of stearic acid with ethylene oxide that may generate 1,4-dioxane as a process by-product. The European Chemicals Agency (ECHA), FDA and Health Canada have all identified 1,4-dioxane as a potential carcinogen (IARC Group 2B — possibly carcinogenic to humans) and an environmental concern. The concern is a manufacturing quality and purity issue — the FDA recommends manufacturers minimise 1,4-dioxane content in cosmetic products to below 10 ppm; vacuum-stripping during production removes the vast majority of residual 1,4-dioxane to well below safety thresholds in quality-controlled manufacturing. Approved for cosmetic use; safe within applicable purity specifications.',
    'stearamidopropyl dimethylamine': 'Stearamidopropyl Dimethylamine (SAPDMA) is a fatty acid amide tertiary amine — a synthetic conditioning agent derived from stearic acid (C18 saturated fatty acid from vegetable or animal tallow) and 3-(dimethylamino)propylamine. In conditioner formulations maintained at slightly acidic pH (typically 4.5–5.5), SAPDMA undergoes protonation — its tertiary amine nitrogen gains a positive charge — which provides strong substantivity (adhesion) to the negatively charged surface of hair fibres. This electrostatic attraction is the basis for its conditioning efficacy. Physiological function: (1) detangling — significantly reduces hair-to-hair friction and improves wet and dry combability; (2) anti-static control — neutralises static charge that causes frizz and flyaways in low-humidity conditions; (3) smoothing — fills gaps in the hair cuticle; (4) improved hair feel. Safety: SCCS (EU Scientific Committee on Consumer Safety) reviewed stearamidopropyl dimethylamine and assessed it as safe for use in rinse-off hair care products at typical concentrations. A concern noted in the scientific literature: tertiary amines can theoretically form nitrosamines (N-nitroso compounds, which are IARC carcinogens) in the presence of nitrosating agents. EU Cosmetics Regulation Article 27 restricts amines from being formulated with nitrosating agents for this reason. Well-formulated products comply with this requirement, making the risk in compliant products very low. Approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation, FDA and FSSAI.',

    # ══ Optical Brightening Agent ═════════════════════════════════════════════
    'disodium distyrylbiphenyl disulfonate': 'Disodium Distyrylbiphenyl Disulfonate (CAS 27344-41-8; trade names Tinopal CBS, FWA-351, Fluorescent Brightening Agent 351) is a stilbene-type fluorescent whitening agent (FWA) or optical brightening agent (OBA). It works by absorbing ultraviolet radiation (UVA, 300–400 nm) and re-emitting it as visible blue-white light (emission peak ~430–440 nm), making white and pale-coloured products appear brighter, whiter and more visually appealing. In bar soaps, it deposits onto fabric and skin surfaces during washing, making clothing appear whiter — a purely cosmetic function with no therapeutic or hygiene benefit. Safety concerns: (1) photoallergy potential — fluorescent brighteners can cause photocontact allergic reactions in susceptible individuals (skin sensitisation followed by UV-triggered immune response); case reports of photoallergic contact dermatitis to Tinopal CBS have been documented in the dermatology literature; (2) environmental persistence — stilbene-type optical brighteners are poorly biodegradable under standard conditions and accumulate in aquatic environments; ECHA classifies some OBAs as potentially persistent, bioaccumulative and toxic (PBT); (3) aquatic toxicity — some studies report toxicity to aquatic organisms; (4) in-vitro genotoxicity concerns have been raised for stilbene OBAs under UV irradiation conditions in some studies, though in-vivo significance at cosmetic exposure levels remains unclear. Not approved in EU rinse-off products at certain concentrations. Added solely for aesthetic whitening of the product with no skin health benefit.',

    # ══ Conditioning / Quaternary Ammonium Polymers ════════════════════════════
    'polyquaternium-113': 'Polyquaternium-113 (PQ-113) is a high-molecular-weight cationic conditioning polymer based on a modified polysaccharide backbone (typically hydroxypropyl guar or similar natural biopolymer) that has been quaternized with trimethyl ammonium functional groups. It belongs to the polyquaternium family of hair conditioning polymers — a broad group of positively charged polymers used in shampoos and conditioners. Properties: (1) conditioning — the permanent positive charge (quaternary nitrogen) is electrostatically attracted to the negatively charged hair cuticle surface; adsorbs substantively from rinse-off products, providing detangling, smoothing and frizz control; (2) good film-forming on hair — reduces split ends, improves hair elasticity; (3) low build-up potential compared to many conditioning agents; (4) compatible with anionic surfactants — can be used directly in shampoo formulations; (5) the polysaccharide backbone confers improved biodegradability compared to fully synthetic polyquaterniums such as PQ-7 or PQ-11. Safety: no significant adverse effects at cosmetic use concentrations; well-tolerated. The primary concern for quaternary ammonium polymers as a class is environmental persistence — quats have antimicrobial activity that affects beneficial environmental microorganisms, and accumulate in water systems. PQ-113\'s natural polysaccharide backbone partially mitigates this environmental concern. Approved for cosmetic use.',
    'quaternium-98': 'Quaternium-98 is a quaternary ammonium salt conditioning agent — a member of the broad quaternary ammonium compound (quat) family used in personal care products, particularly in combined shampoo-conditioner formulations. Quaternary ammonium compounds carry a permanent positive charge on the nitrogen atom, enabling strong electrostatic adsorption to the negatively charged surface of wet hair fibres. This substantivity provides: (1) conditioning and detangling — reduces combing force on wet and dry hair; (2) anti-static control — neutralises charge build-up; (3) smooth, silky after-feel; (4) film formation on hair cuticle. Safety profile for quaternary ammonium compounds as a class: (1) contact allergy — quats are a recognised cause of contact allergic and irritant contact dermatitis, particularly in individuals with occupational exposure (hairdressers, healthcare workers) or pre-existing sensitive skin; (2) eye irritation — concentrated quat solutions are irritating to eyes; in rinse-off products at cosmetic concentrations, risk is low; (3) environmental concerns — quaternary ammonium compounds are generally poorly biodegradable, accumulate in wastewater treatment sludge and aquatic environments, exhibit aquatic toxicity, and have demonstrated disruption of beneficial environmental microbial communities at sub-lethal concentrations; (4) antimicrobial resistance — chronic sub-lethal exposure to quats in the environment may contribute to cross-resistance with antibiotics in some bacterial species. At cosmetic concentrations in rinse-off products, individual consumer risk is low. Environmental persistence is the more significant concern.',

    # ══ Mineral / Petrochemical Emollients ════════════════════════════════════
    'light liquid paraffin': 'Light Liquid Paraffin (also known as light mineral oil, light white oil, liquid paraffin light grade) is a highly refined, low-viscosity grade of mineral oil — a petroleum-derived mixture of saturated aliphatic (paraffin) and alicyclic hydrocarbon chains. The "light" designation refers to its lower density and viscosity (~20–30 cP) compared to heavy liquid paraffin. It is chemically equivalent to Paraffinum Liquidum (the INCI name used in cosmetic formulations). Highly refined to pharmaceutical or USP grade by processes including hydrotreatment, hydrocracking or acid-clay treatment to remove impurities. Properties: (1) excellent occlusive emollient — forms a physical barrier on skin surface that slows transepidermal water loss (TEWL); (2) inert — does not react with skin components; (3) hypoallergenic in pure form; (4) widely used in baby products, pharmaceutical preparations and cosmetics. Concerns: (1) Mineral Oil Aromatic Hydrocarbons (MOAH) — petrochemical refining can leave trace contamination with polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAH), some of which are classified IARC Group 1 carcinogens. EFSA has expressed concern about MOAH in food-contact applications; in cosmetics, EU requires safety data demonstrating MOAH levels below the threshold of concern (typically <2 ppm); (2) occlusive nature — while beneficial for xerosis, complete occlusion traps bacteria, sebum and heat, potentially aggravating acne-prone skin with regular use; (3) inhibits cell desquamation and skin breathing with heavy long-term use; (4) petroleum-derived; non-renewable origin; (5) environmental persistence. Approved for cosmetic use in EU, FDA and FSSAI jurisdictions at appropriate purity grades.',

    # ══ Skincare Actives ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    'panthenol': 'Panthenol (D-panthenol, dexpanthenol, Pro-Vitamin B5) is the alcohol precursor of pantothenic acid (Vitamin B5), an essential water-soluble B vitamin required by every living cell for the synthesis of coenzyme A and acyl-carrier protein. In the skin and hair, panthenol is enzymatically oxidised to pantothenic acid, which is then incorporated into cellular metabolism. Properties: (1) deep penetration — owing to its small molecular weight (~205 Da) and amphiphilic nature, panthenol is one of the very few cosmetic ingredients capable of penetrating into the hair cortex and deeper epidermal layers; (2) humectant — attracts and retains atmospheric moisture; clinical studies demonstrate significant improvements in skin and hair water content; (3) wound healing and anti-inflammatory — panthenol/dexpanthenol has demonstrated acceleration of epithelial cell proliferation and anti-inflammatory properties in multiple double-blind clinical trials; used therapeutically in wound-healing creams, nappy rash preparations and post-laser/post-peeling skincare; (4) improved skin elasticity — Ebner et al. (2002) demonstrated improved stratum corneum water-binding capacity and elasticity in a double-blind trial; (5) hair conditioning and repair — reduces hair shaft brittleness, improves wet combability, reduces breakage by temporarily swelling and strengthening the hair fibre; (6) exceptional tolerability — extremely low sensitisation rate; broad compatibility across cosmetic and pharmaceutical formulations. EU Cosmetics Regulation approved at relevant concentrations; FDA recognised as safe; FSSAI approved. One of the most evidence-based and well-tolerated cosmetic and pharmaceutical actives globally.',

    'tranexamic acid': 'Tranexamic Acid (TXA, trans-4-(aminomethyl)cyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid) is a synthetic lysine analogue and antifibrinolytic drug originally developed in 1962 by Japanese scientists Shosuke and Utako Okamoto, now listed on the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines for control of haemorrhage in surgery and trauma. Its mechanism in skin brightening is distinct from most other depigmenting agents: (1) TXA inhibits the plasminogen–plasmin pathway in keratinocytes — plasmin normally activates arachidonic acid release and melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH) production, which directly stimulates melanin synthesis; TXA blocks this cascade at its initiation; (2) evidence also supports direct inhibition of tyrosinase and DOPA oxidase activities; (3) anti-inflammatory effects reduce post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH). Clinical evidence: multiple randomised double-blind controlled trials — including studies in the Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology, Journal of Dermatological Treatment and Indian dermatology journals — demonstrate topical TXA at 2–5% achieving statistically significant improvements in melasma, PIH and overall hyperpigmentation within 8–12 weeks. Crucially, it does not photosensitise the skin (unlike AHAs), is safe across all Fitzpatrick skin types (I–VI), and is particularly valued by Indian dermatologists for managing melasma in darker South Asian skin where photosensitising actives risk worsening pigmentation. Oral TXA is prescription-only; topical TXA in cosmetic concentrations (typically 2–5%) is over-the-counter. Safety: well-tolerated topically; no systemic procoagulant risk at cosmetic concentrations; no significant allergenicity or reproductive toxicity identified. Approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation, FDA and FSSAI.',

    'mandelic acid': 'Mandelic Acid ((R)-mandelic acid, phenylglycolic acid, α-hydroxyphenylacetic acid) is an alpha hydroxy acid (AHA) derived by the hydrolysis of amygdalin — a glycoside extracted from bitter almonds (Prunus dulcis var. amara). It has a molecular weight of 152 Da — approximately 3.5 times larger than glycolic acid (76 Da) — which means significantly slower percutaneous absorption and gentler action despite equivalent solution pH. Properties: (1) chemical exfoliation — disrupts desmosomes between corneocytes and accelerates desquamation, improving skin texture, surface roughness and mild photodamage; (2) acne treatment — mandelic acid has demonstrated in-vitro antibacterial activity against Cutibacterium acnes and Staphylococcus aureus; its keratolytic effect reduces comedonal plugging; sebostatic effects reduce sebum production; multiple clinical studies confirm efficacy in inflammatory and non-inflammatory acne, particularly in Fitzpatrick skin types III–V where faster AHAs like glycolic acid risk triggering PIH; (3) hyperpigmentation — inhibits tyrosinase at moderate concentrations; improves uneven skin tone, melasma and PIH; (4) anti-ageing — stimulates dermal fibroblasts and collagen synthesis; (5) one of the preferred AHAs for South Asian and Middle Eastern skin types due to its lower PIH risk and excellent tolerability. EU Cosmetics Regulation approved at up to 25% (pH ≥3.5 for consumer products); FDA approved for cosmetic use. Photosensitising — daily SPF 30+ is mandatory during use.',

    'caprylic/capric triglyceride': 'Caprylic/Capric Triglyceride (INCI: Caprylic/Capric Triglyceride; also Fractionated Coconut Oil or Medium-Chain Triglyceride Oil, MCT) is produced by fractionation of coconut oil or palm kernel oil to isolate the medium-chain fatty acid fraction — primarily caprylic acid (C8:0, ~65–75%) and capric acid (C10:0, ~25–35%) — followed by re-esterification with glycerol. The result is a highly purified, colourless, odourless triglyceride ester with a much simpler and more uniform composition than the source oil. Properties: (1) lightweight, dry skin feel — absorbs rapidly leaving no greasy or heavy residue; rated non-comedogenic (comedogenic score 1 or 0 depending on the source); suitable for all skin types including oily, combination and acne-prone; (2) excellent oxidative stability — near-complete saturation makes it highly resistant to rancidity; shelf life is substantially longer than most plant oils; (3) carrier and penetration enhancer — widely used as a delivery vehicle for lipophilic actives (retinoids, Vitamin C esters, botanical extracts) in serums, concentrates and sunscreens; (4) occlusive emollient — at higher concentrations reduces transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and supports skin barrier; (5) hair care — lightweight cuticle-smoothing and conditioning oil with no heavy build-up; (6) ingestible-grade MCT oil is also widely used as a nutritional supplement (ketogenic and endurance sport applications); (7) derived from renewable plant-based resources; biodegradable. Safety: FDA GRAS for food use; approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation and FSSAI; very low sensitisation potential — one of the best-tolerated cosmetic emollients identified in the CIR safety database.',

    'jojoba oil': 'Simmondsia Chinensis Seed Oil (INCI) — commonly known as jojoba oil — is obtained by cold-pressing the seeds of the jojoba plant (Simmondsia chinensis), a desert shrub native to the Sonoran Desert (southern Arizona, southern California and northern Mexico) and now cultivated across arid and semi-arid regions worldwide. Jojoba is botanically unique because its "oil" is not a triglyceride oil but a liquid wax — composed predominantly of long-chain monounsaturated wax esters (C20:1–C22:1 fatty alcohol esters of C20–C22 fatty acids), accounting for ~97% of its composition. This wax-ester structure is structurally analogous to the wax esters in human sebum and skin surface lipids, making jojoba bio-compatible and skin-identical in a way that conventional triglyceride oils are not. Properties: (1) sebum-mimicking — incorporates harmoniously into the skin lipid film without disrupting sebum regulation; (2) non-comedogenic — wax esters do not oxidise, polymerise or solidify within hair follicles, making jojoba one of the most widely recommended oils for oily and acne-prone skin by dermatologists; (3) emollient and occlusive — forms a breathable, semi-occlusive film that reduces TEWL while allowing skin respiration; (4) anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial — oleic acid, myristic acid and long-chain alcohols have demonstrated in-vitro anti-inflammatory and mild antimicrobial activity; (5) exceptional oxidative stability — liquid wax esters are far more resistant to thermal and photochemical degradation than triglycerides; jojoba oil stored correctly remains stable for over five years; (6) hair care — penetrates the hair shaft, significantly reduces hygral fatigue (repetitive water-swelling damage), conditions the cuticle and reduces breakage; a Robbins research group study demonstrated penetration into the hair cortex after heat treatment; (7) sustainable — jojoba is a drought-resistant, nitrogen-fixing desert perennial that regenerates desertified land. No significant allergenicity at cosmetic concentrations. Approved globally.',

    'shea butter': 'Butyrospermum Parkii Butter (INCI) — universally known as shea butter — is a naturally occurring fat extracted from the nuts of the African shea tree (Vitellaria paradoxa, formerly Butyrospermum parkii), native to the dry savanna belt spanning West and Central Africa from Senegal to Uganda (Sahel region). It has been used as a food and cosmetic ingredient in Africa for over 3,000 years, with major commercial production in Ghana, Burkina Faso and Mali. Fatty acid composition: stearic acid (C18:0, ~40–50%), oleic acid (C18:1, ~35–45%), linoleic acid (C18:2, ~5–8%), palmitic acid (C16:0, ~4–7%). The high stearic and oleic acid content gives shea butter its semi-solid consistency at room temperature (melting point ~32–38°C) and its barrier-repairing properties. Bioactive unsaponifiable fraction (~4–9%): triterpene alcohols (lupeol, butyrospermol, α-amyrin), phenolic compounds (catechins, epicatechins, quercetin), cinnamic acid esters (lupeol cinnamate, butyrospermol cinnamate), Vitamin E (tocopherols and tocotrienols). Clinical properties: (1) intensive emollient and skin barrier repair — stearic and oleic acids are skin-identical lipids that replenish stratum corneum lipid content; one of the most evidence-based emollients in dermatological practice for xerosis, eczema (atopic dermatitis), ichthyosis and dry skin conditions; (2) anti-inflammatory — lupeol cinnamate and triterpene fraction inhibit 5-lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase pathways; clinical studies demonstrate reduction of eczema and scalp inflammation; (3) UV protection — cinnamic acid esters absorb UV-B (provides approximately SPF 2–4); not a substitute for sunscreen but offers supplementary protection; (4) wound healing — traditional and evidence-based clinical reports support faster healing of minor cuts, abrasions and burns; (5) anti-ageing — improves skin elasticity, hydration and collagen synthesis. Safety: comedogenic score 0–2 depending on refinement; tree nut-derived but IgE cross-reactivity with latex or peanut is uncommon; documented contact allergy to shea butter is rare but exists. Approved for cosmetic and food use globally.',

    'pantothenic acid': 'Pantothenic Acid (Vitamin B5, calcium pantothenate in supplement form) is an essential water-soluble B vitamin named from the Greek pantothen ("from everywhere") because of its ubiquity in food. It is found in virtually all plant and animal foods, with particularly high concentrations in liver (~6 mg/100g), sunflower seeds (~7 mg/100g), shiitake mushrooms (~3.6 mg/100g), egg yolk (~3.5 mg/100g), avocado (~1.4 mg/100g) and legumes. Physiological function: pantothenic acid is the metabolic precursor of coenzyme A (CoA) — arguably the most central cofactor in human metabolism. CoA is required for the Krebs cycle (acetyl-CoA), fatty acid synthesis and oxidation, synthesis of steroid hormones, synthesis of acetylcholine (neurotransmitter), synthesis of haem, and hundreds of other biochemical reactions. Deficiency is extremely rare due to the ubiquity of dietary sources; when it does occur (typically during severe global malnutrition), it causes burning feet syndrome, fatigue and dermatitis. Used in food fortification and dietary supplements as calcium D-pantothenate (the stable calcium salt). ICMR RDA is 5 mg/day for adults. FDA GRAS; approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX. Safe at normal supplement doses; no upper tolerable intake level established due to absence of toxicity even at high doses.',

    'vitamin d3': 'Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is the form of Vitamin D synthesised photochemically in human skin upon UVB irradiation (wavelength 280–315 nm) — 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin is converted to previtamin D3, then thermally isomerises to Vitamin D3 within minutes. Dietary sources: fatty fish (salmon ~400–1000 IU per 100g), fish liver oils (cod liver oil ~10,000 IU per 100ml), egg yolk (~40 IU), and fortified foods. Cholecalciferol undergoes hepatic hydroxylation to 25(OH)D (calcidiol) — the storage and clinically measured form — and then renal 1α-hydroxylation to calcitriol (1,25(OH)₂D), the biologically active hormone. Functions: (1) calcium and phosphorus homeostasis — directly regulates intestinal absorption of dietary calcium (up to 30–40% with adequate D vs <10–15% without) and renal reabsorption; essential for bone mineralisation; deficiency in children causes rickets, in adults causes osteomalacia; (2) immunomodulation — Vitamin D receptors (VDRs) are expressed on virtually all immune cells; significant evidence for roles in innate immunity (macrophage antimicrobial activity), modulation of autoimmune responses and respiratory infection resistance; (3) muscle function; (4) cardiovascular and metabolic health — under active investigation. Vitamin D deficiency is pandemic, disproportionately affecting South Asians — an ICMR multicentre study (2010) found 65–70% of urban Indians are deficient (<20 ng/ml 25(OH)D) due to indoor lifestyles, low dietary intake and, paradoxically, melanin reducing UVB synthesis in sunny India. ICMR and IAP recommend supplementation (400–1000 IU/day for children, 600–2000 IU/day for adults depending on baseline levels). Safe at recommended doses; toxicity threshold is >4000 IU/day sustained long-term.',

    'vitamin b12': 'Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12, cobalamin) is the most widely used and most stable supplemental form of Vitamin B12 — a water-soluble, cobalt-containing vitamin found exclusively in animal-derived foods (meat, poultry, fish, eggs, dairy) and not produced by plants. Cyanocobalamin contains a cyanide ligand (CN⁻) attached to the cobalt centre, present in quantities far too small to cause toxicity (and rapidly replaced by methyl or adenosyl groups in cellular metabolism). In the body, cyanocobalamin is converted to active coenzyme forms — methylcobalamin (required in the cytoplasm) and adenosylcobalamin (required in mitochondria). Functions: (1) cofactor for methionine synthase — methylcobalamin is essential for homocysteine remethylation to methionine and for 5-methyltetrahydrofolate recycling to tetrahydrofolate; deficiency impairs DNA synthesis and causes megaloblastic anaemia and impaired cell division; (2) cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase — adenosylcobalamin is required for odd-chain fatty acid and branched-chain amino acid catabolism; deficiency causes accumulation of methylmalonic acid (MMA), a clinical biomarker of B12 status; (3) myelin synthesis and neurological function — B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord: progressive demyelination resulting in paraesthesia, weakness, sensory ataxia and dementia, which can be irreversible if untreated; (4) red blood cell maturation — B12 deficiency leads to large, immature megaloblastic red cells. Critical public health context for India: vegetarians (particularly Gujarati, Jain and Brahmin communities) and vegans have no reliable dietary source of B12 and are at high risk — AIIMS and CMC Vellore studies consistently report B12 deficiency in 50–80% of strict vegetarians in India. ICMR RDA is 1 mcg/day; therapeutic supplementation is 500–1000 mcg/day. Added to protein powders, energy drinks, cereals and health supplements for fortification. Safe; no toxicity identified even at very high supplemental doses (water-soluble, renally cleared).',

    'folic acid': 'Folic Acid (pteroylmonoglutamic acid, synthetic Vitamin B9) is the synthetic, oxidised and most bioavailable form of folate — a water-soluble B vitamin. Naturally occurring food folates are found in dark leafy greens (spinach ~194 mcg/100g, mustard greens, fenugreek), legumes (lentils ~358 mcg/100g, chickpeas, black-eyed peas), liver (~290 mcg/100g), eggs and avocado. Synthetic folic acid is 1.7 times more bioavailable than food folates (due to the absence of polyglutamate tail processing). Functions: (1) DNA synthesis and repair — folic acid (as 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate) is required for thymidylate synthesis (dTMP from dUMP), a rate-limiting step in DNA replication; all rapidly dividing cells (red blood cell precursors, intestinal epithelium, foetal cells) are highly dependent on adequate folate; (2) prevention of neural tube defects (NTDs) — periconceptional supplementation of 400–800 mcg/day, starting at least one month before conception and continuing through the first trimester, reduces the risk of spina bifida by 50–70% and anencephaly by ~70%; WHO, ICMR and Indian National Health Mission mandate folic acid supplementation in all pregnancies from preconception through the first trimester; (3) megaloblastic anaemia — folate deficiency causes large, immature red blood cells; particularly important in pregnancy; (4) homocysteine metabolism — reduces cardiovascular risk; (5) critical in early foetal neurodevelopment and organogenesis. ICMR RDA: 200 mcg/day for adults, 500 mcg/day in pregnancy. Added to flour, cereals, infant formula and women\'s health supplements for fortification. Note: supplementation above 1 mg/day in older adults may mask underlying B12 deficiency (both deficiencies cause macrocytic anaemia, but only B12 deficiency causes neurological damage). Safe and essential.',

    'cyanocobalamin': """Cyanocobalamin is the most common synthetic supplemental form of Vitamin B12 (cobalamin), a water-soluble B-vitamin. It is characterised by a cyanide ligand coordinated to the central cobalt atom of the corrin ring — a chemically stable form that does not occur naturally to any meaningful extent in food (the natural forms in food are methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin, the two active coenzyme forms). Why cyanocobalamin is used: (1) exceptional chemical and thermal stability — survives manufacturing, encapsulation, pasteurisation and storage conditions that would degrade the more reactive natural forms; (2) low cost — economical synthesis from Streptomyces fermentation; (3) effectively converted in the body — after absorption via intrinsic factor (IF) in the ileum, cyanocobalamin is stripped of the cyanide group and converted to methylcobalamin (in the cytoplasm) and adenosylcobalamin (in mitochondria) — the two enzymatically active forms. The cyanide released is a trace, physiologically irrelevant amount (micrograms) — well within safe limits, even for daily use. Exception: individuals with impaired cyanide detoxification (e.g., tobacco amblyopia, Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy) are sometimes advised to use methylcobalamin instead. Functions: Vitamin B12 is essential for (1) DNA synthesis — cofactor for methionine synthase (converts homocysteine to methionine, regenerating the methyl donor S-adenosylmethionine); (2) fatty acid metabolism — adenosylcobalamin is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (catabolism of odd-chain fatty acids and branched-chain amino acids); (3) red blood cell maturation — B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia; (4) myelin synthesis and neurological function — deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. Deficiency risk groups: strict vegans and vegetarians (B12 found only in animal-derived foods), elderly (reduced gastric acid and intrinsic factor production), people on metformin (long-term use depletes B12), individuals with pernicious anaemia (autoimmune destruction of intrinsic factor). FSSAI mandates Vitamin B12 fortification in select foods. Safe at standard supplemental doses; no established UL (Tolerable Upper Intake Level) — excess is excreted in urine. Approved by FSSAI, FDA, EU and CODEX for food fortification and supplementation.""",

    'cholecalciferol': 'Cholecalciferol is the chemical name for Vitamin D3 — the natural form of Vitamin D produced photosynthetically in human skin and present in fatty fish and fish liver oils. See vitamin d3 entry. In fortified foods, infant formula and supplements, cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is the preferred form due to its superior bioavailability compared to ergocalciferol (Vitamin D2). FSSAI, ICMR and WHO endorse cholecalciferol supplementation in Vitamin D-deficient populations.',

    # ══ Hair Care / Conditioning Agents ══════════════════════════════════════
    'cetearyl alcohol': 'Cetearyl Alcohol is a cosmetic-grade waxy mixture of cetyl alcohol (C16:0, 1-hexadecanol) and stearyl alcohol (C18:0, 1-octadecanol) in typical ratios of approximately 30:70 or 50:50 depending on grade. These are fatty alcohols — high-carbon-chain solid alcohols derived from vegetable oils (palm oil, coconut oil, palm kernel oil) or by chemical reduction of stearic and palmitic acids. Fatty alcohols are categorically different from short-chain alcohols like ethanol: they are non-volatile, non-drying, waxy solids that moisturise rather than dehydrate. Functions: (1) primary emulsifier — one of the most versatile and widely used cosmetic emulsifiers, enabling stable oil-in-water and water-in-oil emulsions in creams and lotions; forms liquid crystalline lamellar structures in cosmetic emulsions that mimic the skin lipid bilayer structure; (2) rheology modifier and thickener — provides body, creaminess and viscosity to emulsions; (3) emollient — deposits a thin conditioning film on skin that reduces transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and improves skin softness; (4) hair conditioning — substantively adsorbs to hair cuticle from conditioner formulations, improving combability and smoothing the cuticle surface; (5) long stability — prolongs product shelf life. Safety: CIR (Cosmetic Ingredient Review) Expert Panel confirmed safety in 1988 and reaffirmed in subsequent reviews; non-irritating at cosmetic concentrations; documented contact allergy to cetearyl alcohol exists but is rare (IgE-mediated Type I reactions to the stearyl alcohol component have been described); EU Cosmetics Regulation approved; FDA approved; FSSAI approved. One of the best-studied and most trusted cosmetic ingredients.',

    'cetyl alcohol': 'Cetyl Alcohol (1-hexadecanol, palmityl alcohol) is a saturated C16 fatty alcohol first isolated in 1817 by the French chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul from the head cavity of sperm whales (hence "cetyl," from the Latin cetus — whale). Today it is produced industrially by reduction of palmitic acid from vegetable sources (palm oil or palm kernel oil). Functions: (1) emulsifier — stabilises oil-in-water emulsions; (2) emollient — deposits a conditioning, moisturising film on skin; (3) thickener and opacifier — gives creams and lotions body; (4) hair conditioning — smooths the cuticle and improves hair combability; (5) co-emulsifier — frequently used in combination with cetearyl alcohol and emulsifying waxes. Safety: well-studied; CIR Expert Panel confirmed safety; very rare contact allergy documented (less than cetearyl alcohol); FDA approved; EU Cosmetics Regulation approved. A classic, well-tolerated cosmetic ingredient.',

    'stearyl alcohol': 'Stearyl Alcohol (1-octadecanol) is a saturated C18 fatty alcohol produced industrially by hydrogenation of stearic acid (from vegetable or animal tallow) or by reduction of methyl stearate. Functions and safety profile essentially identical to cetyl alcohol and cetearyl alcohol: emulsifier, emollient, thickener, hair conditioner. CIR confirmed safety. Very rare contact allergy documented. Approved by EU Cosmetics Regulation, FDA and FSSAI.',

    'cetrimonium chloride': 'Cetrimonium Chloride (CTAC, cetyltrimethylammonium chloride) is a quaternary ammonium cationic surfactant — the trimethylated quaternary ammonium chloride salt of cetyl alcohol (C16). It was among the first quat conditioning agents introduced to hair care formulations in the 1950s. Mechanism: the permanent, pH-independent positive charge on the quaternary nitrogen atom is strongly attracted to the overall negative surface charge of keratin-rich hair fibres (particularly at the damaged cuticle surface), enabling substantive adsorption even after rinsing. Properties: (1) hair detangling — significantly reduces wet and dry combing forces; (2) anti-static — eliminates static charge that causes frizz and flyaways; (3) cuticle smoothing; (4) mild antimicrobial activity (used at higher concentrations as a disinfectant); (5) used as primary cationic agent in rinse-off conditioners and co-washes at 0.1–0.5%. Safety considerations: EU Cosmetics Regulation restricts cetrimonium chloride to ≤0.25% in rinse-off products (except hair care products) and ≤0.1% in leave-on products; contact allergy is documented (particularly in hairdressers with occupational exposure); irritant to eyes at concentrated levels; quaternary ammonium compounds as a class exhibit poor biodegradability and are toxic to aquatic organisms — environmental persistence is the primary ecological concern. Approved for cosmetic use globally at specified concentrations.',

    'behentrimonium chloride': 'Behentrimonium Chloride (BTAC, docosyltrimethylammonium chloride, N,N,N-trimethyl-1-docosanaminium chloride) is a long-chain quaternary ammonium cationic surfactant derived from behenic acid (C22:0, a very-long-chain saturated fatty acid found at ~1–4% in rapeseed oil, peanut oil and arachis oil). The C22 carbon chain is significantly longer than cetrimonium chloride (C16), which confers: (1) higher substantivity to hair surfaces — deeper and more durable adsorption from rinse-off formulations; (2) enhanced conditioning performance — more detangling, greater softness and silky feel; (3) significantly lower irritation potential than shorter-chain quat surfactants — longer chain length reduces CMC (critical micelle concentration) which is associated with reduced irritation; (4) particularly effective in deep conditioning treatments, hair masks and professional salon conditioners. EU Cosmetics Regulation restricts BTAC to ≤3% in rinse-off products; lower irritation profile than CTAC. Environmental profile: like all quat surfactants, behentrimonium chloride is poorly biodegradable and accumulates in aquatic environments. Contact allergy is possible but less frequent than with shorter-chain quats at cosmetic use concentrations. Approved for cosmetic use globally.',

    'behentrimonium methosulfate': 'Behentrimonium Methosulfate (BTMS) is a quaternary ammonium conditioning emulsifier that is the methosulfate salt (rather than chloride salt) of behentrimonium — typically supplied as a blend with cetyl alcohol (BTMS-25, BTMS-50) as a complete conditioning emulsifier system for leave-on and rinse-off hair care products. The methosulfate counter-ion (CH₃SO₄⁻) confers significantly better biodegradability compared to chloride-based quats — behentrimonium methosulfate is classified as "readily biodegradable" under OECD 301B testing, unlike most quaternary ammonium chlorides. Properties: (1) cationic conditioning — same substantivity to negatively charged hair as BTAC; (2) self-emulsifying — the BTMS/cetyl alcohol combination forms stable emulsions without additional emulsifiers; (3) excellent conditioning, detangling and hair softness; (4) significantly better environmental profile than BTAC. Safety: generally well-tolerated; lower irritation potential; EU Cosmetics Regulation approved. One of the more environmentally-responsible quat conditioning agents available.',

    'hydrolyzed keratin': 'Hydrolyzed Keratin is produced by hydrolysis of keratin protein — predominantly sourced from wool, feathers, horns, hooves or human hair — using acid, alkaline or enzymatic processes that break the protein backbone into smaller peptide fragments and free amino acids of varying molecular weights (typically 500–20,000 Da, with ultrafiltration possible to sub-200 Da). Keratin is the fibrous structural protein that constitutes approximately 95% of the hair cortex, 90% of the nail plate and the outer epidermal cornified layer. Properties in hair care: (1) targeted repair — keratin peptides have specific affinity for damaged hair keratin at sites of cuticle disruption and cortical depletion; they adsorb into structural gaps, temporarily filling defects and restoring tensile properties; Robbins and colleagues demonstrated in cosmetic science journals that repeated keratin peptide treatments measurably reduced combing force and hair breakage; (2) moisture binding — as natural moisturising factor (NMF) contributors, keratin-derived amino acids (particularly cysteine, serine, glutamic acid and glycine) attract and retain water within the hair fibre; (3) anti-frizz and smoothing — surface cuticle irregularity filling reduces light scattering and frizz; (4) improved elasticity and flexibility — allows hair to return to normal position after deformation without breaking; (5) in skin care — larger-MW fragments improve stratum corneum water-binding; lower-MW fragments may modestly support epidermal cell function. Safety: well-tolerated; hydrolysis breaks down the intact protein structure, reducing or eliminating the allergen epitopes of the parent protein; approved for cosmetic use globally. Vegan-certified alternative: wheat hydrolyzed proteins mimic some properties but differ in amino acid composition from animal keratin.',

    'hydrolyzed silk': 'Hydrolyzed Silk is produced by partial hydrolysis of silk fibroin — the structural protein of silk produced by the Bombyx mori silkworm — using enzymatic, acidic or alkaline processes that break the protein into silk peptides and free amino acids. Silk fibroin has an exceptional amino acid composition: ~46% glycine (the simplest amino acid), ~30% alanine, ~12% serine, with smaller amounts of tyrosine and valine — differing fundamentally from keratin (which is rich in cysteine). Properties: (1) hair conditioning — silk peptides adsorb to the hair surface with strong affinity, forming a thin protective film that increases combability, adds shine and improves tensile properties; (2) hygroscopic humectant — the small peptides act as natural humectants, attracting atmospheric moisture; (3) film-forming — creates a fine, non-greasy film on skin and hair that improves texture and smoothness; (4) antistatic; (5) in skin care — silk amino acids improve hydration and tactile smoothness. Safety: well-tolerated; low allergenicity (hydrolysis disrupts IgE binding epitopes); FDA approved; EU Cosmetics Regulation approved. Vegan-certified silk alternatives derived from fermentation or plant sources are emerging.',

    'hydrolyzed collagen': 'Hydrolyzed Collagen (also called collagen peptides or collagen hydrolysate) is produced by hydrolysis of collagen protein — predominantly bovine (hide, bone) or marine (fish scales, skin) in origin — into shorter peptide chains and free amino acids. Collagen is the most abundant structural protein in the human body (~30% of total protein mass), forming the scaffolding of skin, bone, cartilage, tendons and blood vessels. It is rich in glycine (~33%), proline (~12%), hydroxyproline (~10%) and lysine. Properties in cosmetics: (1) skin conditioning and film-forming — collagen peptides form a thin moisturising and smoothing film on the skin surface; (2) humectant — hygroscopic amino acids attract water; (3) hair conditioning — silk-like smoothing effect on cuticle. Oral hydrolyzed collagen supplements: clinical evidence from multiple double-blind, placebo-controlled trials (including Shaw et al. Journal of International Society of Sports Nutrition 2017, Proksch et al. Skin Pharmacology and Physiology 2014, Asserin et al. 2015) demonstrates that daily supplementation of 2.5–10g of hydrolyzed collagen improves skin hydration, elasticity and reduces wrinkle depth; clinical evidence for joint pain reduction in athletic populations; mechanism involves dipeptide absorption and fibroblast stimulation. Safety: generally non-allergenic (bovine-derived — relevant for those with beef allergy, rare); fish-derived variants carry fish allergy risk; approved for food and cosmetic use globally.',

    'sodium pca': 'Sodium PCA (Sodium L-pyroglutamate, sodium 2-pyrrolidone-5-carboxylate) is the sodium salt of pyroglutamic acid (PCA), a naturally occurring cyclic amino acid that constitutes one of the primary components of the skin\'s natural moisturising factor (NMF). The NMF is the collection of hygroscopic compounds in the stratum corneum that are responsible for maintaining skin hydration without occlusives — it includes free amino acids, PCA (~12%), lactate (12%), urea (7%), sugars and organic acids. Sodium PCA in the skin is formed from the proteolytic degradation of filaggrin (a protein in the stratum corneum) and subsequent cyclisation of glutamine residues. Properties: (1) powerful humectant — sodium PCA is one of the most hygroscopic substances found in nature; it binds and retains significantly more water than glycerin or hyaluronic acid at equivalent concentrations under dry conditions; (2) skin-identical moisturiser — as a natural component of human skin, it is exceptionally biocompatible and non-irritating; (3) conditioning for hair — also a natural component of hair NMF, sodium PCA supplementation from conditioning products prevents hair dehydration and brittleness; (4) non-occlusive — provides humectant moisture retention without creating an occlusive barrier (complementary to, not replacing, occlusives). Safety: well-tolerated; non-sensitising; EU Cosmetics Regulation approved; FDA approved. One of the most skin-compatible moisturising agents available.',

    'betaine': 'Betaine (trimethylglycine, TMG, Nε-trimethylglycine) is a naturally occurring amino acid derivative (zwitterionic compound) found in high concentrations in sugar beet (Beta vulgaris — hence the name), spinach, quinoa, wheat germ and shellfish. It is a methylation donor in biological systems (as part of the betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase pathway) that helps maintain normal blood homocysteine levels. In cosmetics, betaine functions as: (1) humectant — very effective water-binding agent; (2) osmoprotectant — protects cells from osmotic stress and thermal denaturation; (3) co-formulant that reduces the skin irritation of surfactants — betaine is a well-established "mildness amplifier" added to surfactant systems to reduce surfactant-induced transepidermal water loss and sting; clinical studies consistently demonstrate reduced Draize scores (irritation scoring) when betaine is added to SLS or SLES formulations; (4) conditioning for skin and hair; (5) anti-inflammatory. Note: INCI "Betaine" in cosmetics is trimethylglycine (the simple amino acid derivative), distinctly different from "Cocamidopropyl Betaine" which is an amphoteric surfactant. Safety: TMG betaine is exceptional tolerated — it is a natural food constituent and is safe for cosmetic, food and supplement use. FDA GRAS; EU Cosmetics Regulation approved.',

    'carbomer': 'Carbomer (Carbopol, polyacrylic acid cross-linked polymers) is a family of high-molecular-weight synthetic polymers of acrylic acid, cross-linked with allyl sucrose or allyl pentaerythritol. First synthesised by BF Goodrich in the 1950s, carbomers are the most widely used rheology modifiers in the pharmaceutical and cosmetic industry globally. When dispersed in water and neutralised (typically with sodium hydroxide or triethanolamine), carbomer forms a clear, high-viscosity gel at extremely low concentrations (~0.1–1.0%). Properties: (1) exceptional rheology control — produces stable, transparent, high-viscosity gels; (2) suspension agent — maintains active ingredients uniformly distributed in formulations; (3) film-forming — provides a light, non-greasy skin feel; (4) pH-sensitive — gel viscosity is pH-dependent (maximum at pH 6–11); (5) compatible with a wide range of cosmetic ingredients. Safety: CIR Expert Panel reviewed and confirmed safety; not absorbed through intact skin; no reproductive or developmental toxicity identified; rare contact allergy documented; eye irritation risk is minimal in formulated (neutralised) products; very slightly irritating to mucous membranes at high concentrations when unneutralized. Manufacturing note: acrylic acid monomer (petroleum-derived) is a reactive starting material; trace monomer is regulated and typically below safety thresholds in finished carbomers. EU Cosmetics Regulation approved; FDA approved. Widely used in pharmaceutical gels, sunscreens, moisturisers and hand sanitisers.',

    'isopropyl myristate': 'Isopropyl Myristate (IPM) is an ester produced by reacting isopropanol with myristic acid (C14:0 saturated fatty acid, commonly derived from coconut or palm kernel oil). It is a clear, colourless, very lightweight, non-greasy liquid widely used as an emollient and skin-feel modifier in cosmetics and pharmaceuticals. Properties: (1) dry, lightweight skin feel — provides slip, spreadability and lubrication without the heavy or greasy feel of mineral oil or triglyceride oils; (2) penetration enhancer — IPM significantly increases the dermal penetration of other ingredients by disrupting the intercellular lipid structure of the stratum corneum; used clinically in transdermal drug delivery for this reason; (3) carrier and solvent — dissolves many poorly water-soluble cosmetic actives; (4) good spreadability in sunscreens, foundations and skin care. Important concern: Isopropyl myristate is a known comedogen — it has one of the highest comedogenic ratings (4–5 out of 5) in the dermatology comedogenicity testing system developed by Fulton et al. This means it is documented to cause or worsen acne when applied to the skin repeatedly. While the clinical relevance of rabbit-ear comedogenicity tests to human skin is debated, IPM is consistently recommended to be avoided by those with acne-prone skin, and many acne-focused dermatologists advise against cosmetics containing IPM for acne patients. EU Cosmetics Regulation approved; FDA approved. Generally safe for non-acne-prone individuals; problematic for acne-prone skin.',

    'butylene glycol': 'Butylene Glycol (1,3-butanediol, BG) is a small synthetic diol (two hydroxyl groups) with a molecular formula of C₄H₁₀O₂, produced by industrial fermentation of sugars or by petrochemical processes (reduction of acetaldehyde). It is a clear, viscous, odourless liquid widely used in cosmetics and food. Functions: (1) humectant — attracts and retains atmospheric moisture; less hygroscopic than glycerin but with a lighter, less sticky skin feel; (2) solvent — dissolves many cosmetic actives, fragrance components and water-insoluble ingredients; aids ingredient delivery; (3) viscosity modifier — thin formulas and improve the spreadability; (4) preservative booster — antimicrobial synergy; reduces required concentrations of primary preservatives; (5) anti-freeze agent — prevents formulations from freezing in cold storage. The 1,3-butanediol isomer is used in cosmetics; the 1,4-butanediol isomer has significantly different and concerning pharmacology (metabolises to gamma-hydroxybutyrate, GHB — a controlled substance), but the 1,3 isomer used in cosmetics does not share this metabolic pathway. Safety: CIR Expert Panel confirmed safety; low sensitisation potential; non-irritating at cosmetic concentrations; generally well-tolerated; approved for food and cosmetic use globally. Note on "petroleum-derived" consumer concern: bio-based 1,3-butanediol from fermentation is increasingly available and functionally identical.',

    'natural flavour': 'Natural Flavour (or Natural Flavoring) refers to a flavouring substance derived from a natural source — including spices, fruits or fruit juices, vegetables, edible yeast, herbs, bark, buds, roots, leaves, meat, seafood, poultry, eggs, dairy products or fermentation products of these — whose characteristic flavour component is obtained by physical, microbiological or enzymatic processes. The FDA definition (21 CFR §101.22) and EU Regulation EC 1334/2008 both specify that the flavour-characterising component must originate from a natural biological source. Important consumer awareness: despite the "natural" designation, natural flavours can be highly complex proprietary mixtures containing dozens to hundreds of individual chemical compounds (esters, terpenes, lactones, aldehydes, alcohols, acids) extracted, concentrated and blended from their natural source matrices. Individual chemical components are not required to be disclosed on food labels, as the proprietary formula is legally protected. The chemical composition of some "natural flavours" may be structurally identical or similar to their synthetic counterparts. FSSAI (India), EU and FDA all permit "natural flavour" as a blanket label term protecting proprietary formulas. Allergen note: natural flavours derived from the 14 EU major allergens or the 8 FDA major allergens (milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, wheat, soy, fish, shellfish) must be specifically identified on the label.',

    'natural flavoring': """Natural Flavoring (US FDA spelling of "natural flavour" / "natural flavouring") is a flavour ingredient defined by the US FDA (21 CFR §101.22) as a substance derived from specified natural biological sources — spices, fruits, fruit juice, vegetables, vegetable juice, edible yeast, herbs, bark, buds, roots, leaves, any animal (meat, seafood, poultry, eggs, dairy) or fermentation products of these — whose characteristic flavour component originates from the biological source through physical, microbiological or enzymatic processes. Under EU Regulation 1334/2008, the equivalent term is "natural flavouring substance." Key regulatory nuance: the "natural" designation does not require the flavour to be obtained simply or minimally — it can involve extensive chemical extraction, distillation, concentration, hydrolysis, fermentation and blending, producing a complex proprietary mixture of hundreds of individual organic compounds (esters, aldehydes, ketones, terpenes, lactones, alcohols). The biological origin of the characterising component must come from the named source. Critical consumer transparency issue: neither FDA nor FSSAI requires manufacturers to disclose the individual component chemicals of natural flavors on labels, as the proprietary formula is a trade secret. A product can legally label the ingredient as "natural flavor" or "natural flavoring" without disclosing the dozens of specific chemical compounds used. Allergen concern: natural flavoring derived from the 8 major US allergens (milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, soy) must be disclosed — e.g., "natural flavor (milk)" or "contains milk." However, flavoring from non-major allergens (sesame seed, etc.) may not be declared. Safety: individual natural flavour compounds are regulated and assessed by FSSAI, FDA, EU and JECFA; the regulatory presumption is safe at food use levels. Indian context: FSSAI Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) regulations govern flavouring use; FSSAI has largely adopted CODEX and EU standards for flavouring safety.""",

    'dextrin': 'Dextrin is a group of low-molecular-weight carbohydrates produced by controlled hydrolysis of starch (from maize, potato, wheat or cassava) using heat, acid, alkali or enzymatic hydrolysis — a process that partially breaks the 1,4-α-glycosidic bonds in starch without reaching complete hydrolysis to glucose. Dextrins have intermediate chain lengths between the long polymer chains of native starch and the short dimers of maltose. They naturally occur in the browned crust of bread and toasted foods (via Maillard reaction and pyrolysis). Industrial uses: (1) binder in tablet coatings and powdered foods; (2) encapsulant for spray-dried flavours, vitamins and nutritional supplements; (3) film former and coating agent for snacks; (4) thickener in sauces and soups; (5) replacer of fat in reduced-calorie products; (6) ingredient in instant food products. The glycaemic index of dextrin varies significantly with chain length and degree of branching — short-chain dextrins are rapidly hydrolysed by α-amylase and raise blood glucose quickly; long-chain dextrins are more slowly digested. Resistant dextrins (e.g., Fibersol-2) are a specific category of non-digestible dextrins used as soluble fibre. FDA GRAS; approved by FSSAI, EU and CODEX.',

    'maltitol': 'Maltitol (E965/INS 965) is a sugar alcohol (polyol) produced by catalytic hydrogenation of maltose — a disaccharide derived from the enzymatic hydrolysis of corn starch (maltose = two glucose units linked α-1,4). Maltitol has approximately 75–90% the sweetness of sucrose, a similar bulk and mouthfeel to sucrose, and 2.1 kcal/g (vs 4 kcal/g for sucrose). It is widely used in "sugar-free" and "no added sugar" confectionery, chocolate, chewing gum and diabetic food products. Critical scientific context: despite its widespread use in products marketed to people with diabetes and weight concerns, maltitol has a significantly higher glycaemic index (GI = 35–52, depending on study design — compared to sucrose GI ~65, glucose GI = 100) than most other sugar alcohols. For comparison: erythritol GI ≈ 0, xylitol GI ≈ 7, sorbitol GI ≈ 9. This means maltitol causes a measurable rise in blood glucose, insulin secretion and blood glucose area under the curve — making it an inappropriate "safe" sweetener choice for people with Type 1 or Type 2 diabetes. Multiple glycaemia studies in T2DM patients confirm this clinical effect. Additionally, like all polyols: laxative effect occurs at intakes exceeding 20–25g/day; bloating and diarrhoea are common side effects. EU labelling regulations require "excessive consumption may produce laxative effects" on products containing >10% added polyols. FSSAI, EU and CODEX approved at quantum satis in confectionery and bakery products.',

    'iodised salt': 'Iodised Salt (Iodized Salt) is ordinary sodium chloride (NaCl) to which trace amounts of potassium iodide (KI, 30 ppm) or potassium iodate (KIO₃, 20–30 ppm) have been added during or after processing to deliver a defined concentration of iodine (typically 15–30 ppm iodine by weight of salt). Iodisation of salt for human consumption is mandated by law in India under FSSAI Food Safety and Standards Regulations and the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, as well as being a WHO-endorsed strategy to combat Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD). Scientific basis: iodine is an essential trace mineral required exclusively for the biosynthesis of thyroid hormones — thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3). These hormones regulate basal metabolic rate, cardiac output, protein synthesis, nervous system development and maturation, and temperature regulation. Iodine deficiency consequences: goitre (thyroid gland enlargement), hypothyroidism, impaired cognitive development, increased risk of miscarriage and stillbirth; severe maternal deficiency causes cretinism — the most common preventable cause of intellectual disability worldwide. WHO estimates 35% of the global population (approximately 2.6 billion people) has insufficient iodine intake. India\'s iodisation mandate has substantially reduced goitre prevalence from ~35% in the 1950s to below 10% today. The amount of iodine delivered by iodised salt at recommended daily salt intake (5–10g) is well within the WHO-recommended iodine intake range of 150–250 mcg/day for adults. Safe and beneficial at regulated levels; excess iodine (>1100 mcg/day upper tolerable intake) can cause thyroid dysfunction — not achievable from iodised salt at normal dietary use.',

    'iodized salt': """Iodized salt (iodised salt — US/Indian spelling) is sodium chloride (NaCl) fortified with a measured trace amount of iodine — typically in the form of potassium iodate (KIO₃ at 20–30 mg iodine per kg salt, i.e., 20–30 ppm) or potassium iodide (KI). See the primary 'iodised salt' entry for comprehensive details. Iodisation of all packaged edible salt is mandated by Indian law under FSSAI regulations because iodine deficiency is widespread across the Indian subcontinent (due to iodine-poor soils and crops). Iodine is an essential trace mineral required solely for thyroid hormone synthesis (thyroxine T4 and triiodothyronine T3), which regulate metabolism, growth, cardiac function and — critically — fetal and infant brain development. India's Salt Iodisation Programme has dramatically reduced goitre and iodine deficiency disorders (IDD) since the 1980s. Recommended iodine intake: 150 mcg/day for adults, 250 mcg/day for pregnant and lactating women. The typical 5 g/day salt intake from iodised salt (at 15 ppm iodine) provides approximately 75 mcg iodine — about half the adult requirement from this source alone (other dietary iodine comes from milk, seafood and iodine-containing soil in some regions). Health notes: iodised salt retains all the sodium of regular salt (38.5% sodium) — the same cardiovascular and hypertension considerations apply. Using iodised salt does not increase sodium intake compared to regular salt. The standard WHO recommendation is <5 g total salt/day. Safe when consumed at normal dietary levels. Excessive supplemental iodine (>1100 mcg/day) can cause thyroid dysfunction.""",

    'milk solids': 'Milk Solids (Milk Solids Non-Fat/MSNF, skimmed milk powder/SMP, non-fat dry milk) are the dried, water-free components of skimmed milk (milk with fat removed). Composition: approximately 35–37% protein (primarily casein micelles ~80% and whey proteins ~20%), 49–53% lactose, 8–9% minerals (calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, potassium), 1% water, trace B vitamins (riboflavin, B12, pantothenic acid). Commercial production involves pasteurisation, evaporation and spray-drying of liquid skim milk to produce a fine powder. Used widely as a functional ingredient in: baked goods (Maillard browning, structure, moisture retention), confectionery and chocolate (improving flavour, texture and mouthfeel), dairy beverages, infant formula, ice cream, soups and sauces. Nutritional contribution: high-quality complete protein (casein and whey are both complete proteins with all essential amino acids); excellent dietary calcium source. Allergen: milk is one of the 14 EU major allergens and 8 FDA major allergens — milk solids must be clearly declared on product labels. Relevant also for lactose intolerance (the lactose in milk solids may cause digestive symptoms in lactose-intolerant individuals).',

    'skimmed milk powder': 'Skimmed Milk Powder (SMP) — see milk solids entry. The dried form of skimmed milk with fat content below 1.5%. Used as a food ingredient for protein enrichment, Maillard browning and dairy flavour. Dairy allergen and lactose source.',

    'whole milk powder': 'Whole Milk Powder is produced by spray-drying whole milk (with fat), yielding a powder with approximately 26–40% fat, 24–27% protein, 36–38% lactose and 5–7% minerals. Used in confectionery (milk chocolate, cream-filled biscuits), bakery and dairy products. Provides all-in-one dairy nutrition (protein, fat-soluble vitamins, calcium). Dairy allergen — must be declared. Shelf life is shorter than skimmed milk powder due to fat content.',

    'whey protein concentrate': 'Whey Protein Concentrate (WPC) is produced from the liquid whey byproduct of cheese or casein manufacturing by ultrafiltration or microfiltration to concentrate the whey protein fraction. WPC typically contains 25–89% protein by dry weight (the remainder being lactose, fat and minerals). The protein composition is mainly beta-lactoglobulin (~65%), alpha-lactalbumin (~25%), immunoglobulins, lactoferrin and glycomacropeptide. Whey is a "fast-digesting" complete protein with an exceptionally high BCAA content (branched-chain amino acids, particularly leucine ~10–11%) — properties that make it the most widely studied and used protein supplement in sports nutrition. Clinical evidence: dozens of randomised controlled trials consistently demonstrate that whey protein supplementation combined with resistance training produces greater increases in muscle mass and strength than resistance training alone; the leucine content activates mTORC1 signalling (the primary anabolic signal for muscle protein synthesis). Whey also has high biological value (BV=104) and protein digestibility-corrected amino acid score (PDCAAS=1.0). Dairy allergen (milk protein) — must be declared. Lactose content varies by grade (higher in WPC65, very low in WPC90 and whey protein isolate WPI). Safe for adults; high intakes do not harm healthy kidneys (contrary to older concerns).',

    'calcium caseinate': 'Calcium Caseinate is the calcium salt of casein — produced by acidifying skim milk to precipitate casein, followed by neutralisation with calcium hydroxide. It forms a soluble, stable powder used in protein supplements, meal replacements, sports nutrition, infant formula and processed foods. Casein is a slow-digesting complete protein: it forms gels in the stomach (due to the acidic pH) that release amino acids gradually over 4–7 hours, providing sustained amino acid availability. This makes calcium caseinate the preferred protein for overnight (pre-sleep) use in sports nutrition — a 2012 Maastricht University study (Res et al., Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise) demonstrated that 40g casein before sleep significantly increased muscle protein synthesis overnight. Dairy allergen — must be declared.',

    # ══ Baking / Flour Improvers ══════════════════════════════════════════════
    'ins 500(ii)': 'INS 500(ii) / E500(ii) — Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate (Sodium Bicarbonate, Baking Soda). The most widely used raising agent in baked goods, crackers and bread. Releases CO₂ on contact with acid or heat, causing dough to rise. Fully decomposes during baking — no residue. See sodium bicarbonate entry.',

    'e1101': 'INS 1101 / E1101 — Proteases (Proteinases) are naturally occurring protein-cleaving enzymes used as flour treatment and dough improver agents in baking. They partially hydrolyse gluten proteins, reducing dough tenacity and improving extensibility — essential in biscuit, cracker and wafer production where extensible (non-elastic) dough is required. Common sources: papain (papaya, INS 1101(i)), bromelain (pineapple, INS 1101(ii)), ficin (fig latex, INS 1101(iii)), bacterial proteases (Aspergillus oryzae). Safety: proteases are processing aids — they are heat-inactivated during baking and no active enzyme remains in the finished product. FDA GRAS; EU Regulation (EC) No 1332/2008 approved; FSSAI approved. No health concerns at bakery use levels.',

    'e1101i': 'INS 1101(i) / E1101(i) — Papain is a natural proteolytic enzyme extracted from papaya (Carica papaya) latex. It is a widely used protease flour improver, cleaving gluten proteins to reduce dough resistance. Also used as a meat tenderiser and in dietary supplements. Safety: inactivated by baking heat — negligible residual activity in finished goods. FDA GRAS; EU Regulation (EC) 1332/2008 approved; FSSAI approved. Well-tolerated at food use levels.',

    'fungal alpha amylase': 'Fungal Alpha-Amylase (INS 1100(i), Aspergillus oryzae alpha-amylase) is an enzyme produced from fermentation of the food-grade mould Aspergillus oryzae. It breaks down starch in wheat flour into fermentable sugars (maltose, glucose), improving bread volume, softening the crumb, enhancing crust colour and extending freshness. Used at very low concentrations (5–50 ppm of flour weight). Safety: completely inactivated by oven temperatures — no active enzyme in finished bread. FDA GRAS; EU Food Enzyme Regulation (EC) 1332/2008 approved; FSSAI and CODEX approved. No health concerns at bakery use levels.',

    'emulsifier 442': 'Emulsifier 442 (INS 442, E442, Ammonium Phosphatides, AYP) is a synthetic emulsifier produced from ammonium salts of phosphorylated mono- and di-glycerides from vegetable oils. Used primarily in chocolate and confectionery as a lecithin alternative — it reduces chocolate viscosity more efficiently at lower dosage levels (0.2–0.5% of chocolate mass), improving flow and preventing fat bloom. Safety: EFSA evaluated ammonium phosphatides and established an ADI of 25 mg/kg body weight/day — not exceeded at typical use concentrations. EU E442 approved; Codex Alimentarius permitted; FSSAI approved. Limited long-term data compared to lecithin.',

    'ins 442': 'INS 442 — Ammonium Phosphatides (E442). See emulsifier 442 entry. Used primarily as a chocolate emulsifier and viscosity reducer.',

    'ins 322': 'INS 322 — Lecithin. See lecithin entry. INS 322 is the international additive number for lecithin, a natural phospholipid emulsifier derived from soy, sunflower seed or egg yolk. One of the most widely used and well-studied food emulsifiers globally. Approved by FSSAI, EU, FDA and CODEX.',

    'pgpr': 'PGPR (Polyglycerol Polyricinoleate, INS 476, E476) — a synthetic emulsifier made from glycerol and ricinoleic acid (derived from castor oil). Used in chocolate and confectionery to reduce viscosity, improve flow and allow reduction of cocoa butter content. See polyglycerol polyricinoleate entry for detailed description.',

    'liquid glucose': 'Liquid Glucose (Glucose Syrup) is a concentrated solution of glucose and maltose produced by partial hydrolysis of starch (corn, wheat or potato starch). Unlike High Fructose Corn Syrup (HFCS), regular liquid glucose is primarily dextrose/glucose with no significant fructose fraction. Uses: confectionery (prevents sugar crystallisation in toffees, fondants, hard candy); bakery (moisture retention, shelf life extension); ice cream (controls ice crystal size). Glycaemic index ≈ 85–100 (same range as pure glucose) — causes rapid blood sugar spikes. High caloric density; no micronutrient value. Regular overconsumption linked to obesity, dental caries, metabolic syndrome and insulin resistance. FSSAI, EU and FDA GRAS approved. Should be used in moderation.',

    'cheesecake flavor': 'Cheesecake Flavor (Cheesecake Flavouring) is a proprietary blended flavouring preparation designed to impart the characteristic taste and aroma of cheesecake. The specific chemical compounds are not disclosed on labels. Typical cheesecake flavour compounds may include dairy-derived components (cream cheese, butter notes), lactones (gamma-decalactone — fruity dairy note), diacetyl (buttery note), and various aldehyde and ester compounds. Consumers with dairy allergies should note that dairy-derived natural flavouring compounds may be present even when not separately declared. Individual flavouring compounds are FSSAI and FDA-regulated, but without specific disclosure, full allergen and safety assessment from the label alone is not possible.',

    'cheesecake flavour': """Cheesecake Flavour (UK/Australian spelling of cheesecake flavor) is a proprietary multi-component flavouring preparation formulated to replicate the complex sensory profile of baked cheesecake — combining the creamy richness of cream cheese, the buttery note of biscuit base, subtle vanilla and lactic overtones, and the slightly sweet-acidic balance characteristic of the dessert. See the cheesecake flavor entry for full details. Key flavour chemistry: cheesecake aroma complexity typically derives from: (1) lactones — particularly gamma-decalactone and delta-decalactone (peach-cream notes), delta-dodecalactone (creamy-coconut note), found naturally in dairy fats; (2) diacetyl (2,3-butanedione) — the primary butter/cream flavour compound, produced naturally by lactic acid bacteria; note: diacetyl is associated with "popcorn lung" (bronchiolitis obliterans) in occupationally exposed workers who inhale concentrated vapours during flavour manufacturing — not a consumer safety concern at food use concentrations when the finished product is eaten normally; (3) free fatty acid compounds — propionic, butyric and caproic acids from dairy; (4) methyl and ethyl esters of fatty acids — fruity, milky notes; (5) vanilla-related compounds (vanillin, ethyl vanillin) for base sweetness; (6) 2-heptanone and 2-nonanone for blue-cheese character. Allergen concern: dairy-derived flavour compounds may be present — people with milk allergies should look for specific allergen disclosure. The exact compound composition is a proprietary trade secret and is not disclosed on product labels. FSSAI, EU (flavouring regulation EC 1334/2008) and FDA regulate individual flavour compounds; manufacturers are responsible for compound safety assessment. Safe at food use concentrations for the general population.""",

    'whole wheat flour': 'Whole Wheat Flour (Atta) is produced by milling the entire wheat grain — bran, germ and endosperm — retaining nearly all of the grain\'s original nutritional value. Significantly more nutritious than refined wheat flour (maida): contains 10–13g dietary fibre per 100g (vs 2–3g for maida), B vitamins (thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, B6, folate), vitamin E, iron, magnesium, phosphorus, zinc and phenolic antioxidants. Glycaemic index ≈ 69 (medium GI), considerably lower than refined wheat flour (GI ≈ 85). Regular whole grain consumption is associated in large prospective studies with reduced risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and colorectal cancer. Contains gluten — not suitable for coeliac disease or gluten sensitivity. FSSAI, EU and FDA GRAS. The nutritionally superior choice over refined flour for everyday Indian cooking.',

    'emulsifiers': 'Emulsifiers (undisclosed) — when a product label states only "emulsifiers" without identifying the specific compound(s), the consumer cannot assess safety. Individual emulsifiers vary widely: safe options include soy lecithin (E322), sunflower lecithin, mono- and di-glycerides; more concerning options include carrageenan (E407 — banned in EU infant formula, linked to gut inflammation), polysorbate 80 (animal studies: gut microbiome disruption), and mono- and di-glycerides from hydrogenated sources (potential trans fat). Without the specific names, full safety assessment is not possible. FSSAI, EU and FDA all require specific additive names or E-numbers on ingredient lists, but some product labels only use the category name.',

    'stabilizers': 'Stabilizers (undisclosed) — when a product label states only "stabilizers" without identifying the specific compound(s), safety cannot be fully assessed. Common food stabilizers range from safe (guar gum E412, locust bean gum E410, xanthan gum E415, pectin E440) to more concerning options (carrageenan E407 — banned in EU infant formula; carboxymethylcellulose/CMC E466 — animal studies suggest gut microbiome disruption at high doses). Without specific disclosure, consumers cannot determine if higher-concern stabilizers are present.',

    'stabilisers': 'Stabilisers — UK/Australian spelling of stabilizers. Same concern: specific compounds not named on label, preventing individual safety assessment. See stabilizers entry.',

    'sugandhit dravya': 'Sugandhit Dravya (सुगंधित द्रव्य — Hindi/Sanskrit for "aromatic substance/fragrance") is the term used on Indian product labels — particularly on packaged foods, cosmetics, Ayurvedic formulations and personal care products — as a generic collective term for fragrance ingredients, permissible under FSSAI and Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) labelling regulations. The specific aromatic chemicals that form the fragrance blend are NOT disclosed on the label; only this umbrella term appears. A single "Sugandhit Dravya" declaration may conceal dozens to over a hundred distinct chemical compounds, including: synthetic aroma chemicals (aromatic esters, aldehydes, ketones, terpenes, lactones), natural essential oil components (themselves containing multiple active chemicals — e.g., linalool, citral, geraniol, eugenol, limonene), solvent carriers and fixative materials. Concerns: (1) Allergenicity — fragrance is the #1 cause of allergic contact dermatitis from cosmetics globally; several potent fragrance allergens (limonene, linalool, citral, cinnamic aldehyde, isoeugenol, oak moss, tree moss) are regulated in the EU but not individually mandated for disclosure in India; (2) Respiratory sensitisation — relevant in sprays, incense sticks, room fresheners and powder products; (3) Phthalates — certain synthetic musks and fragrance compounds are formulated in phthalate diluents (DEP, DEHP), known endocrine disruptors; (4) Synthetic nitromusks and polycyclic musks — several are banned in EU for bioaccumulation and hormone-disruption concerns; (5) Endocrine-disrupting fragrance chemicals — some synthetic fragrance compounds (benzophenone, diethyl phthalate, certain musks) have demonstrated estrogenic or anti-androgenic activity in cell-based assays. Recommendation: if you have fragrance allergy, asthma, eczema or sensitive skin, treat "Sugandhit Dravya" as a caution flag — contact the manufacturer for the full fragrance ingredient list, or choose fragrance-free alternatives.',

    'excipients': 'Excipients (also listed as "Excipients q.s." — quantum sufficit, meaning "as much as needed") are the inactive, non-therapeutic ingredients in tablets, capsules, liquid medicines, dietary supplements, Ayurvedic preparations and fortified foods. They serve essential manufacturing and delivery functions: binders (microcrystalline cellulose, PVP — hold the tablet together), fillers/diluents (lactose, dicalcium phosphate, maize starch — add bulk), disintegrants (crospovidone, sodium starch glycolate — help tablets dissolve), lubricants (magnesium stearate, stearic acid — prevent tablets sticking to machinery), glidants (colloidal silicon dioxide — improve powder flow), coating agents (HPMC, shellac, carnauba wax — protect or mask taste), colourants (synthetic dyes, titanium dioxide — product identification), sweeteners (sucrose, sorbitol, saccharin in syrups), preservatives (sodium benzoate, methylparaben in liquids) and stabilisers. CONCERN — NOT DISCLOSED BY BRAND: when a product label simply states "Excipients" without listing them individually, consumers cannot determine: (1) allergen presence — lactose (lactose intolerant), wheat starch (coeliac disease), egg-derived stearic acid, shellac coating (vegans/Jains/certain halal interpretations); (2) which synthetic colourants or preservatives are present and at what levels; (3) sugar content — relevant for diabetics if glucose, lactose or sucrose fillers are used; (4) source of animal-derived excipients (gelatin capsules, lactose, stearic acid from tallow — concern for vegans, vegetarians, halal and kosher observance); (5) potentially allergenic excipients like groundnut oil (peanut oil) used as a lubricant or carrier. FSSAI and Indian drug regulations require excipients to be safe and approved, but individual disclosure is not always mandated for supplements and Ayurvedic products as strictly as for Schedule H pharmaceuticals. Choose brands that list each excipient individually for full transparency.',

    'flour treatment agent': 'Flour Treatment Agent (undisclosed) — a generic descriptor covering a wide range of additives used to improve flour baking performance. Includes oxidising agents (potassium bromate INS 924 — BANNED in EU, UK, Canada, Australia and China as a carcinogen; ascorbic acid E300 — safe; azodicarbonamide E927a — banned in EU, UK, Australia, Singapore), reducing agents (L-cysteine E920 — safe), bleaching agents (chlorine dioxide — banned in EU), and enzyme preparations (alpha-amylase, protease — all safe). When the specific agent is not disclosed, consumers cannot determine whether banned or concerning agents like potassium bromate are present. India\'s FSSAI permits specific flour treatment agents under Schedule of Permitted Preservatives/Additives — disclosure of the exact compound would allow proper assessment.',

    'calcium sulphate': 'Calcium Sulphate (INS 516, E516, Food-grade Gypsum, CaSO₄) is a naturally occurring mineral salt used in food processing as a coagulant (traditional tofu setting agent — provides bioavailable calcium), firming agent, yeast nutrient (brewer\'s yeast health), flour treatment agent (strengthens gluten), and water-hardness adjuster in brewing. As a tofu coagulant, it is one of the oldest food uses of calcium sulphate — "nigari-free" or calcium-set tofu is made with INS 516, which also significantly increases the calcium content (100–350 mg/100g, comparable to dairy). Safety: EFSA and FDA classify calcium sulphate as GRAS at food use levels; provides dietary calcium; no adverse health effects at normal consumption. FSSAI approved.',

    # ══ Cosmetic / Personal-Care Ingredients ══════════════════════════════════

    'c12-15 alkyl benzoate': 'C12-15 Alkyl Benzoate (INCI: C12-15 Alkyl Benzoate) is a lightweight synthetic ester produced by esterification of benzoic acid with a mixture of C12 (lauryl) and C15 (pentadecyl) linear fatty alcohols. It is one of the most widely used emollients and texture-enhancing agents in sunscreens, moisturisers, foundations and body lotions. Function: acts as an emollient (fills spaces between skin cells, reducing trans-epidermal water loss), a spreading agent (lowers surface tension, improving spreadability of sunscreen formulas), and an excellent solvent for lipophilic UV filters — it dissolves oil-soluble sunscreen actives (ethylhexyl methoxycinnamate, avobenzone, octocrylene) uniformly into formulations, preventing recrystallisation and ensuring even UV protection. Skin feel: leaves an ultra-light, dry, non-greasy skin feel superior to heavier mineral oils; non-comedogenic (does not clog pores) in standard rabbit-ear assay models. Effect on body: topically applied, C12-15 alkyl benzoate is poorly absorbed through intact skin; the ester bond is hydrolysed slowly by skin esterases to benzoic acid and the corresponding fatty alcohol, both of which are naturally present in the body. Benzoic acid at low dermal levels is quickly conjugated with glycine in the liver and excreted as hippuric acid — no systemic accumulation. Safety: rated as safe at concentrations up to 10% by the Cosmetic Ingredient Review (CIR, 2012); not a known skin sensitiser; non-irritating at use concentrations; EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex-compliant; FDA-approved inactive ingredient in OTC drug products; rated 1–2 (low hazard) by EWG Skin Deep. Biodegradation: the ester is readily biodegradable under aerobic conditions. No reproductive, developmental or endocrine-disruption concerns identified at cosmetic use levels.',

    'methylene bis-benzotriazolyl tetramethylbutylphenol': 'Methylene Bis-Benzotriazolyl Tetramethylbutylphenol (MBBT; trade name Tinosorb M; INCI: Methylene Bis-Benzotriazolyl Tetramethylbutylphenol) is a broad-spectrum UV filter and one of the most photostable sunscreen actives available. It is a bis-benzotriazolyl compound — essentially two benzotriazole UV-absorbing chromophores linked by a methylene bridge, combined with two 4-(1,1,3,3-tetramethylbutyl)phenol groups for lipophilicity. UV coverage: uniquely covers both UVB (290–320 nm, peak ~305 nm) AND UVA (320–400 nm, peak ~360 nm) in a single molecule with a very high molar extinction coefficient; prevents UVA-induced photoageing, hyperpigmentation and DNA damage in addition to UVB-induced sunburn. Photostability: Tinosorb M is exceptionally photostable — it does not degrade or lose UV-absorbing capacity on sun exposure, unlike avobenzone (which degrades ~50% in 1 hour); also photostabilises other less-stable UV filters in combination products. Physical form: used as a micronised aqueous dispersion (particle size ~200 nm); the particle-based nature means it primarily sits on top of the skin and does not significantly penetrate the stratum corneum. Regulatory status: approved in the EU (Annex VI, up to 10%), Australia, Japan, ASEAN, Canada and India (CDSCO); NOT yet approved by the US FDA (pending NDA review under the Sunscreen Innovation Act since 2014 — regulatory backlog, not a safety concern). Safety: SCCS Opinion (2005, reaffirmed 2020) — no safety concern at ≤10% in sunscreen products; minimal systemic absorption due to large molecular weight (659 g/mol) and particle-based application; no endocrine disruption, genotoxicity, carcinogenicity or reproductive toxicity identified in in vitro or animal studies; aquatic toxicity low due to particle form. Effect on body: negligible dermal penetration; considered essentially non-systemic. Highly recommended ingredient in broad-spectrum sunscreens.',

    'decyl glucoside': 'Decyl Glucoside (INCI: Decyl Glucoside; CAS 68515-73-1) is a non-ionic alkyl polyglycoside (APG) surfactant produced by the Fischer glycosidation of glucose (from corn starch) with decanol (a 10-carbon fatty alcohol from coconut or palm kernel oil). It belongs to the green chemistry family of sugar-based surfactants widely used in mild cleansers, baby products, sensitive-skin formulations and certified-organic cosmetics. Function: as a surfactant, it reduces surface tension to remove sebum, dirt, make-up and environmental pollutants from the skin surface. Mildness profile: decyl glucoside is consistently rated among the mildest surfactants in cosmetics — it does not disrupt the skin\'s natural lipid barrier to the extent that sodium lauryl sulphate (SLS) or sodium laureth sulphate (SLES) do; rated non-irritating in repeat insult patch tests and cumulative irritation assays; suitable for atopic dermatitis, rosacea and infant skin; does not compromise tight junction proteins at normal use concentrations. Foaming: produces a soft, creamy, modest foam — less lather than SLS-based cleansers (lather ≠ cleaning efficacy). Biodegradability: readily biodegradable (>90% in 28 days by OECD 301 B test); derived from renewable plant-derived raw materials; approved for use in certified-organic and natural cosmetics (COSMOS, NATRUE, ECOCERT). pH compatibility: stable and effective across a broad pH range (3–9). Safety: CIR concluded safe as used (2019); EU Cosmetics Regulation compliant; no sensitisation, phototoxicity or endocrine-disruption concerns; EWG rating 1 (very low hazard). Effect on body: works solely on the skin surface; rapidly biodegraded by skin microflora post-rinse; no systemic absorption of concern.',

    'diethylamino hydroxybenzoyl hexyl benzoate': 'Diethylamino Hydroxybenzoyl Hexyl Benzoate (DHHB; trade name Uvinul A Plus; INCI: Diethylamino Hydroxybenzoyl Hexyl Benzoate) is a lipophilic UVA-absorbing organic UV filter introduced to cosmetic sunscreens as a photostable alternative to avobenzone (butyl methoxydibenzoylmethane). UV coverage: selective long-wave UVA filter with maximum absorption at ~354 nm (UVA I range, 340–400 nm) — the most biologically damaging UVA sub-range responsible for photoageing (collagen/elastin degradation), immunosuppression and indirect DNA damage; does not meaningfully absorb UVB. Photostability: DHHB is intrinsically photostable — its chromophore undergoes efficient excited-state intramolecular proton transfer (ESIPT) after UV absorption, rapidly returning to the ground state without degradation; this distinguishes it from avobenzone, which photolyses unless stabilised by photostabilisers. Lipophilicity: log Kow ≈ 5.3 — highly lipophilic; formulates well into oil phases; good compatibility with other oil-soluble UV filters. Regulatory status: approved in the EU (Annex VI, up to 10%), Australia, Japan, ASEAN and India; NOT FDA-approved in the USA (pending). Safety: SCCS Opinion (2008, updated 2015) — safe at ≤10%; in vitro and in vivo skin penetration studies show very low dermal absorption (<0.3% of applied dose after 24 hours on intact human skin); high molecular weight (397 g/mol) and lipophilicity limit systemic exposure; no genotoxicity, no reproductive toxicity, no endocrine disruption identified; aquatic toxicity classified as harmful to aquatic organisms, so care advised with wash-off products. Effect on body: due to negligible skin penetration, systemic levels after topical use are pharmacologically insignificant; considered safe for use in sunscreens applied to normal intact adult skin; use on broken, damaged or infant skin not recommended as a general sunscreen precaution.',

    'ethylhexyl triazone': 'Ethylhexyl Triazone (EHT; trade name Uvinul T 150; INCI: Ethylhexyl Triazone; CAS 88122-99-0) is a highly efficient UVB-absorbing organic UV filter with one of the highest molar extinction coefficients of any sunscreen active, making it possible to achieve high SPF with relatively low concentrations. UV coverage: absorbs primarily UVB radiation with a peak at ~314 nm; excellent protection against UV-B-induced erythema (sunburn), thymine dimer formation (UVB-specific DNA damage) and photocarcinogenesis; does not cover UVA. Photostability: Ethylhexyl Triazone is photostable — it does not degrade on prolonged UV exposure; this is a significant advantage over some other UVB filters (e.g., PABA, padimate O); also acts as a photostabiliser for less-stable filters in combination sunscreen formulations. Molecular structure: triazine core with three 4-aminobenzoate branches esterified with 2-ethylhexanol; the triazine scaffold confers molecular rigidity and high UV absorption cross-section (ε ~150,000 L/mol/cm). Regulatory status: approved in the EU (up to 5%), Australia, Japan, ASEAN and India; NOT FDA-approved in the USA. Safety: SCCS Opinion (1999, 2003) — safe at ≤5%; negligible skin penetration due to large molecular size (823 g/mol — one of the largest UV filter molecules) and high lipophilicity; no sensitisation, genotoxicity, reproductive toxicity or endocrine disruption identified; no significant systemic absorption in human in vivo studies. Effect on body: essentially non-systemic at cosmetic use levels; sits primarily in the stratum corneum layer and does not reach viable epidermis in meaningful quantities; very well-tolerated in clinical use across diverse skin types.',

    'ceteareth-20': 'Ceteareth-20 (INCI: Ceteareth-20; CAS 68439-49-6) is a polyethylene glycol ether of cetearyl alcohol (a blend of cetyl C16 and stearyl C18 fatty alcohols) with an average of 20 ethylene oxide (EO) units per molecule. It belongs to the PEG (polyethylene glycol) ether surfactant family and is widely used as an emulsifier and solubiliser in creams, lotions, ointments and conditioners. Function: the molecule has a long hydrophobic fatty alcohol tail and a long hydrophilic PEG chain — this amphiphilic structure allows it to sit at oil-water interfaces in emulsions, reducing interfacial tension and stabilising oil-in-water (O/W) emulsions long-term; also increases viscosity and improves skin feel. Effect on skin barrier: at low concentrations used in cosmetics (0.5–5%), ceteareth-20 may temporarily disrupt the intercellular lipid lamellae of the stratum corneum, slightly increasing skin permeability — this can enhance penetration of actives (both beneficial and concerning depending on what else is in the formulation). Key concern — 1,4-dioxane contamination: the ethoxylation manufacturing process (reacting cetearyl alcohol with ethylene oxide) can leave residual 1,4-dioxane, a probable human carcinogen (IARC Group 2B, EPA Group B2). 1,4-dioxane is NOT listed on the label as an ingredient because it is a manufacturing by-product, not intentionally added; it is detectable in trace amounts in PEG-containing cosmetics. The EU Cosmetics Regulation requires 1,4-dioxane to be minimised to the lowest technically achievable level and sets a threshold ≤10 ppm; responsible manufacturers use vacuum stripping to reduce 1,4-dioxane below 1 ppm. Safety: the CIR concluded ceteareth-20 is safe as used in rinse-off and leave-on cosmetics (provided 1,4-dioxane levels are controlled); FDA allows use in OTC drug products; EU Annex-compliant; EWG rating 1–2 (low hazard) but flags 1,4-dioxane concern. Effect on body: the parent molecule is not significantly absorbed; the main concern is the potential 1,4-dioxane contaminant. Consumers who wish to avoid PEG-derived ingredients can look for "PEG-free" or certified organic products.',

    'sodium acrylate/acryloyldimethyltaurate/dimethylacrylamide crosspolymer': 'Sodium Acrylate/Acryloyldimethyltaurate/Dimethylacrylamide Crosspolymer (trade name Sepineo P 600; INCI: Sodium Acrylate/Acryloyldimethyl Taurate/Dimethylacrylamide Crosspolymer; also referred to as polyacrylamide copolymer or carbomer-adjacent polymer) is a high molecular weight, crosslinked synthetic anionic polymer used as a rheology modifier, thickener and emulsion stabiliser in sunscreens, moisturisers and fluid cosmetics. It belongs to the family of acrylic acid / acrylamide copolymers. Function: when hydrated in aqueous systems, the crosslinked polymer network swells to form a three-dimensional gel that dramatically increases the viscosity and yield stress of formulations; provides elegant light-gel textures preferred in modern sunscreens; stabilises oil droplets in emulsions by providing steric and electrostatic barriers; imparts suspension capacity for insoluble UV filter particles; delivers excellent sensory properties (non-sticky, non-greasy, fast-spreading). Composition: the three monomer units — sodium acrylate (anionic, hydrophilic), 2-acrylamido-2-methylpropanesulphonic acid (AMPS/taurate, anionic), and dimethylacrylamide (non-ionic) — combined in a crosslinked network give broad pH stability (pH 3–8) and excellent compatibility with electrolytes and cationic ingredients unlike traditional carbomers. Residual acrylamide concern: acrylamide (CH₂=CHCONH₂) is a known neurotoxin and probable carcinogen (IARC Group 2A); it is the monomer used to make polyacrylamide polymers. In the final crosslinked polymer, residual unreacted acrylamide monomer can be present as a trace impurity. EU Cosmetics Regulation limits acrylamide monomer in cosmetic leave-on products to ≤0.1 ppm and rinse-off to ≤0.5 ppm; responsible manufacturers control this to below 0.05 ppm. At regulated trace levels, the systemic dose is pharmacologically negligible. The polymer itself is not absorbed through skin — its large molecular weight prevents dermal penetration. Safety: SCCS considers polyacrylamide-based polymers safe in cosmetics when acrylamide monomer content is within regulatory limits; FDA approved as inactive ingredient; EWG rating 1 (low hazard when monomer controlled); no sensitisation or reproductive toxicity from the polymer itself.',

    'isohexadecane': 'Isohexadecane (INCI: Isohexadecane; CAS 4390-04-9; trade names Permethyl 101A, Panalane L-14E) is a highly branched, synthetic C16 isoparaffinic hydrocarbon (a 16-carbon alkane with multiple methyl side-branches) derived from petroleum or, in some formulations, from synthetic isoparaffin processing. It is a clear, colourless, odourless, low-viscosity liquid used extensively in cosmetics as a lightweight emollient, solvent for silicones and UV filters, and skin-feel enhancer. Function: acts as an emollient by forming an occlusive thin film on the skin surface that reduces trans-epidermal water loss (TEWL) without the heavy, greasy feel of mineral oil or petrolatum; its branched structure prevents close molecular packing, resulting in a very light, volatile-like skin feel with low spreading drag; used as a carrier solvent for silicone fluids, cyclopentasiloxane and certain UV filters; provides gloss and slip in makeup formulations. Skin feel: characterized as "dry" and "silky" — preferred in primers, lightweight foundations and sports sunscreens; often blended with silicones to combine surface smoothing with occlusivity. Comedogenicity: isohexadecane is rated 1 out of 5 on the comedogenicity scale (1 = very unlikely to clog pores) — considerably less comedogenic than mineral oil (rated 0–1) or isopropyl myristate (rated 3–5); however, individual skin response varies. Penetration: the highly branched structure and relatively large molecular size limit dermal absorption; absorption through intact skin is low; the absorbed fraction is metabolised via normal fatty acid β-oxidation pathways and exhaled as CO₂. Safety: CIR concluded safe as used in cosmetic formulations (2003, reaffirmed 2013); EU Cosmetics Regulation compliant; FDA permitted as inactive ingredient; non-sensitising, non-phototoxic; no reproductive or endocrine-disruption concerns at cosmetic use levels; EWG rating 1. Source concern: petroleum-derived; consumers seeking bio-based formulas can note that some brands use plant-derived isoparaffin alternatives.',

    'octenidine hcl': 'Octenidine Dihydrochloride (OCT; INCI: Octenidine HCl; systematic name: N,N\'-(1,10-decanediyldi-1(4H)-pyridinyl-4-ylidene)bis[1-octanamine] dihydrochloride; CAS 70775-75-6) is a broad-spectrum cationic bis-pyridinium antimicrobial agent widely used as a preservative in cosmetics, antiseptic wound cleansers and mucous membrane disinfectants in European and Asian clinical practice. Mechanism of action: octenidine is a membrane-active biocide — it inserts into the lipopolysaccharide (Gram-negative) and lipoteichoic acid (Gram-positive) layers of bacterial cell membranes, disrupting membrane integrity and causing leakage of intracellular contents; also disrupts fungal cell membranes. Spectrum: active against Gram-positive bacteria (Staphylococcus aureus including MRSA, Streptococcus spp.), Gram-negative bacteria (E. coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa), yeasts (Candida albicans), enveloped viruses (HIV, HSV) and dermatophytes; efficacy is rapid (bactericidal within 30 seconds at use concentrations). Use in cosmetics: at very low concentrations (0.01–0.1% w/w), used as a preservative in leave-on and rinse-off cosmetics to prevent microbial contamination during product use; the concentration in cosmetics is far below antiseptic use levels (0.1–1%). Clinical antiseptic use: in European medicine (Germany, Switzerland, Austria, Poland, India), octenidine solutions (Octenisept®, Octenilin®) are standard-of-care skin and wound antiseptics; unlike chlorhexidine, they can be used in mucous membranes, wound cavities and around the eyes (at antiseptic concentrations). Safety at cosmetic use levels: not significantly absorbed through intact skin at cosmetic concentrations; non-sensitising (unlike some other preservatives — no cross-reactivity with common fragrance or preservative allergens); not a known human carcinogen; not listed as an endocrine disruptor; EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex V approved preservative (up to 0.1% in rinse-off, 0.1% leave-on); very low EWG hazard rating (1–2). Important warning at clinical concentrations: octenidine-based antiseptic solutions must NOT be injected or used under pressure in deep wounds or body cavities in clinical settings — cases of severe tissue damage and necrosis have been reported when clinical-grade solutions were used incorrectly under pressure irrigation (the surfactant co-formulation, not octenidine itself, is implicated); this is irrelevant for cosmetic product use.',

    'glyceryl monostearate': 'Glyceryl Monostearate (GMS; INCI: Glyceryl Stearate; CAS 31566-31-1) is a monoglyceride — the monoester of glycerol and stearic acid (C18 saturated fatty acid). It is one of the oldest and most widely used cosmetic and food emulsifiers, occurring naturally in small amounts in animal and plant fats as a product of partial lipolysis. In personal care, it is found in virtually every cream, lotion, moisturiser, sunscreen and conditioner formulation. Function: (1) emulsifier — GMS has an HLB (Hydrophile-Lipophile Balance) of approximately 3.8, classifying it as a lipophilic W/O emulsifier; used in combination with higher-HLB emulsifiers (like ceteareth-20) to create stable O/W emulsions; (2) co-emulsifier and stabiliser — thickens the oil phase, stabilises the lamellar liquid crystalline structure at the oil-water interface that gives creams their rich texture; (3) emollient — the stearate chain lubricates and softens the skin surface; (4) opacifier — at higher concentrations, imparts a pearlescent, opaque appearance to lotions. Skin compatibility: glyceryl monostearate is non-irritating, non-sensitising and rated as non-comedogenic at typical use concentrations (1–10%); it mimics naturally occurring skin lipids (skin produces its own monoglycerides via enzymatic hydrolysis of sebum triglycerides); well-tolerated by all skin types including sensitive and acne-prone. Food use: also used as INS 471 (food-grade GMS) in bakery products, dairy, confectionery and margarine — one of the safest food additives with ADI "not specified" by JECFA. Safety: CIR and EFSA classify GMS as safe at cosmetic and food use levels; no sensitisation, phototoxicity, mutagenicity or reproductive toxicity; FDA GRAS; EU Annex-compliant; EWG rating 1–2. Biodegradation: readily biodegradable; hydrolysed by lipases to glycerol and stearic acid, both normal metabolites.',

    'polymethylsilsesquioxane': 'Polymethylsilsesquioxane (PMSQ; INCI: Polymethylsilsesquioxane; CAS 68554-70-1; trade names Tospearl, KSP-100) is a crosslinked silicone resin in the form of spherical, monodisperse microparticles (typical particle size 2–20 µm). The name reflects its chemical structure: a three-dimensional crosslinked network (sesqui = 1.5 oxygen bridges per silicon) of methyl-substituted silicon atoms — intermediate in crosslinking density between linear silicone fluids (D₄, D₅) and fully crosslinked silica. It is used in cosmetics as a soft-focus powder agent, mattifying agent, sensory modifier and oil-absorption agent. Function: (1) soft-focus / optical diffusion — the perfectly spherical particles scatter light in multiple directions, reducing the appearance of pores, fine lines and skin texture ("optical blurring" effect) in foundations, primers and BB creams; (2) mattification — absorbs sebum and controls surface shine; (3) sensory modification — imparts a smooth, silky, powdery skin feel superior to talc or silica; reduces drag and improves spreadability; (4) anti-caking — prevents powder cosmetic agglomeration. Physical inertness: PMSQ particles are chemically inert, do not react with skin, actives or other formulation ingredients; thermally stable up to ~300°C; water-repellent (hydrophobic surface). Penetration: particle size (5–15 µm) is far larger than the width of skin surface fissures and stratum corneum intercellular spaces — dermal penetration is physically impossible; particles remain entirely on the skin surface and are removed by washing. Safety: CIR Expert Panel concluded safe as used in cosmetics (2003, reaffirmed 2018); SCCS no safety concern in cosmetic products; not absorbed, not metabolised, not toxic by any route at cosmetic use levels; no sensitisation, genotoxicity, reproductive toxicity or environmental bioaccumulation concerns (unlike cyclic siloxane fluids D4/D5); EWG rating 1. Distinct from cyclosiloxanes: PMSQ does not share the endocrine-disruption, persistence or bioaccumulation concerns associated with volatile cyclic siloxanes (D4, D5) — it is a solid particle, not a volatile fluid.',

    'd-panthenol': 'D-Panthenol (INCI: Panthenol; also Dexpanthenol, D-Pantothenyl Alcohol; CAS 81-13-0) is the biologically active D-isomer of panthenol and the provitamin (immediate precursor) of pantothenic acid (Vitamin B5). It is produced commercially by reduction of D-pantolactone with beta-aminopropionic acid derivatives or by enzymatic synthesis. It is one of the most comprehensively researched and evidence-based moisturising agents in dermatology and cosmetology. Mechanism of action: after topical application, D-panthenol rapidly penetrates the stratum corneum and is converted to pantothenic acid by ubiquitous alcohol oxidase enzymes in keratinocytes; pantothenic acid is incorporated into Coenzyme A (CoA), which is indispensable for fatty acid synthesis, energy metabolism, cell membrane lipid production and protein acetylation. The pantothenic acid/CoA pathway stimulates keratinocyte proliferation and migration — key to wound healing. Effects on skin: (1) Moisturisation — hygroscopic; attracts and holds water molecules within the stratum corneum (humectant action); significantly reduces trans-epidermal water loss (TEWL); clinical studies show 30–35% TEWL reduction versus untreated skin; (2) Wound healing — accelerates re-epithelialisation; clinical studies demonstrate faster healing of surgical wounds, minor burns, skin abrasions and irritant contact dermatitis with 5% dexpanthenol formulations; (3) Anti-inflammatory — reduces erythema and pruritus (itching) in irritated skin; inhibits inflammatory cytokine release (IL-1β, TNF-α) from keratinocytes; useful in post-laser, post-waxing and after-sun formulations; (4) Skin barrier repair — stimulates synthesis of stratum corneum ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids — the three key lipids of the skin barrier; (5) Hair — in hair-care products, panthenol coats the hair shaft, filling micro-cracks in the cuticle, increasing hair diameter, reducing breakage and adding shine; (6) Scalp — reduces scalp irritation associated with frequent washing and chemical treatments. Concentration: effective at 0.5–5% in leave-on formulations; commonly 1–2% in moisturisers, 0.5–1% in rinse-off; therapeutic wound preparations use 5% (Bepanthen, Bepanthol). Safety: panthenol is extraordinarily safe — it is endogenous to human metabolism as a vitamin precursor; no sensitisation, no phototoxicity, no mutagenicity, no reproductive toxicity; safe for infants, pregnant and nursing mothers; EWG rating 1; CIR safe as used; EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex-compliant; FDA inactive ingredient approved; regarded as a "gold standard" moisturising and skin-repair active.',

    # ══ Synthetic Dyes / Colorants ════════════════════════════════════════════
    'ci 11680': 'CI 11680 (Colour Index Number 11680) is a synthetic monoazo dye known as Yellow AB (Aniline Yellow) or Fat Yellow 3. It produces a yellow to orange-yellow coloration and has been used in some personal care and cosmetic products. Regulatory status is highly restrictive: CI 11680 (Yellow AB) is NOT listed in Annex IV of the EU Cosmetics Regulation as an approved cosmetic colorant — its use in cosmetics is therefore not permitted in the European Union. In the USA, the FDA does not include CI 11680 in its list of approved cosmetic color additives for general use. Toxicology: as a monoazo dye, CI 11680 contains an azo group (-N=N-) that can be reductively cleaved by azo-reductase enzymes (present in intestinal bacteria and skin microflora under anaerobic conditions) to release aromatic amines. Several aromatic amines are classified as human carcinogens (IARC Group 1 or 2A). While the carcinogenic risk from azo dye reduction is primarily of concern with oral ingestion (food dyes) rather than topical application, the dermal absorption and metabolic activation potential of this specific compound warrants attention. The absence of regulatory approval in major cosmetic jurisdictions (EU, USA) is the primary concern. Its use in an Indian market product should be evaluated against FSSAI/BIS cosmetic colour regulations.',
    'ci 12120': 'CI 12120 (Colour Index Number 12120) is a synthetic monoazo dye — also referenced in some databases as Solvent Red 1 or related structures. It is used to impart red to orange-red coloration in personal care formulations. Regulatory status: CI 12120 is NOT listed in the EU Cosmetics Regulation Annex IV as an approved cosmetic colorant and is therefore not permitted for cosmetic use in the European Union. The FDA does not list CI 12120 as a permitted cosmetic color additive. Toxicological considerations: as a member of the azo dye class, CI 12120 contains an azo linkage susceptible to reductive cleavage by bacterial azo-reductases, potentially releasing aromatic amines — compounds for which various IARC carcinogenicity classifications exist depending on structure. The significance for topical rinse-off use (soap) is primarily the extent of dermal absorption, which is generally lower than for oral routes. However, the fundamental issue remains: this dye lacks regulatory approval for cosmetic use in major jurisdictions, meaning its safety has not been formally reviewed and established by the EU SCCS or FDA. Consumers have no assurance of independent safety evaluation. Its presence in a product sold in India should be verified against FSSAI Schedule IX cosmetic colorant regulations.',

}


